window.QUESTIONS=[{"id":"P1","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Definition","weight":"critical","q":"Does the definition still call frontmatter a markdown editor, or does the noun change?","lede":"\"Editor\" names the one part we did not write, and every page and price hangs on it.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Owned","items":["src/modules/mdmax: 3,614 lines, 13 files","19 construct detectors, the splice writer"],"side":"1 symbol reaches product code","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Unbuilt","items":[".frontmatter/review.jsonl","per-span hash, reviewed-by, reviewed-at"],"side":"the pilot","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Forked","items":["src/modules/editor, src/modules/app-shell","41 of 43 files byte-identical to sgnk-md"],"side":"0 files unique to us","tone":"sunk"}],"note":"Counts from the plan's fix-first table.","caption":"What the product owns, bottom-up. The noun names the top layer."},"stakes":"With the wrong noun, every artefact points at a forked shell we do not own.","state":["41 of 43 editor and app-shell files are byte-identical to sgnk-md.","One symbol of our 13 `mdmax` files reaches product code."],"tension":["\"Editor\" promises quality a fork cannot guarantee, and omits the engine.","Changing it re-points every screen and price; \"review layer\" has no search demand."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the codebase owns, re-counted in the tree 2026-09-09","items":[["Editor + app-shell files byte-identical to sgnk-md","41 of 43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223"],["Editor + app-shell files unique to frontmatter","0","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223"],["Files across src/ already diverged from the sibling","25 of 226 (181 still identical)","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223"],["Lines in src/modules/mdmax, the only owned module","3,614 across 13 files","verified: find src/modules/mdmax -name '*.ts' | wc -l → 13; cat | wc -l → 3614"],["Files outside mdmax that import it","2","verified: grep -rn 'modules/mdmax' src/ excluding the module itself"],["Distinct mdmax symbols reaching product code","1 (decodeStrict)","src/modules/vault/application/get-snapshot.ts:13 and src/modules/vault/infrastructure/search-index.ts:16"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The definition sentence, four versions in eleven days","cols":["Where","The noun and the claim","Status"],"rows":[["docs/DECIDE.md:59","\"a markdown editor for people who build software with AI\" (byte-exact editing)","Superseded; byte-exactness refuted as a pitch, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:174"],["docs/BRIEF.md:125","\"a markdown editor that knows which bytes a machine wrote\"","Superseded; authorship did not sell as a consumer feature, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175"],["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","\"a markdown editor for repositories whose documents are written by agents\"","Current, and labelled a failing hypothesis on line 22"],["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:27","\"nobody persists review state across people and across tools on a working tree with no pull request\"","What survived the attack; not yet the headline"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep \"markdown editor\" and budget the fork tax as a line item","gains":["Familiar noun; the free-editor funnel survives","No rename cost: every current screen, price and doc keeps its noun."],"costs":["Unestimated fork tax on every table-stakes feature","41 of 43 editor and app-shell files are already byte-identical to sgnk-md."],"system":"Recurring merge against sgnk-md.","screens":"None.","money":"Unpriced time on the shell.","what":"The landing page keeps saying editor. Every table-stakes feature is then paid for twice, once by the sibling and once here, and nobody has estimated that."},{"k":"b","label":"Change the noun to the engine and the state, shipped inside an editor","what":"The sentence becomes: where you write, review and keep current the files your agents obey. The editor is the container it happens in, not the claim.","gains":["Names the only code we own","The 13 mdmax files hold 3,614 owned lines against a codebase with 0 files unique elsewhere."],"costs":["A category with no search demand","Only one symbol (decodeStrict) from that owned code reaches product code today."],"system":"The review file becomes the headline object.","screens":"First-run and landing copy rewritten.","money":"Slower organic search."},{"k":"c","label":"Merge sgnk-md and frontmatter into one codebase, then write the sentence","gains":["Ends the fork tax at source","Removes the reason 25 of 226 files have already diverged from the sibling."],"costs":["Unestimated, at 1.21 engineering days a week","A merge this size has no engineering estimate anywhere in the corpus."],"system":"One codebase; also fixes the desktop app shipping as sgnk-md.","screens":"None yet.","money":"Unknown weeks before anything ships.","what":"One codebase, then the sentence. It ends the fork at source, and nobody has costed it against one person at 1.21 engineering days a week."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer the noun until the form-factor decision resolves","gains":["Form factor genuinely decides the noun","Costs nothing today; the noun has already changed four times in eleven days without one."],"costs":["Weeks 1 to 2 pages need copy now","The landing pages for that same fortnight are already scheduled to ship."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Wait for the surface decision. Honest, except Phase 0's landing page needs copy in its first week and cannot wait."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Name what we own: the engine and the review file, not the shell.","The editor stays as the container, so the fork finding does not break it."],"flip":"If option c's merge is genuinely one week, do it first and \"editor\" is honest.","linked":["P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:27","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:557","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48","docs/DECIDE.md:59","docs/BRIEF.md:125","src/modules/vault/application/get-snapshot.ts:13","src/modules/vault/infrastructure/search-index.ts:16","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:217","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan says the editor is the product. Confirm the noun before any copy or landing page is written.","recWhy":"The noun is what a stranger repeats to a colleague, so it has to name something we own and something they cannot get free elsewhere. Editor fails both. It is the one part we did not write, with 202 of 228 files identical to sgnk-md, and it is the one part nobody in this market charges for. This weekend moved the sentence again: not a review layer, which four free tools now ship, but the files your agents obey, which 60,000 projects carry and nobody helps maintain. Option b names that and keeps the editor as the container, so the fork finding does not break the free front door. Option c is cleaner and would be the right call if the merge is genuinely a week, which nobody has measured. Option d costs Phase 0 its landing copy for a decision the research has already made.","dupes":{"dropped":["G13"],"areas":["Go-to-market & channels"],"why":"Go-to-market & channels asked whether to call it a markdown editor in public, and recommended a split no option here offers: keep 'markdown editor' as the internal definition, never in public copy. Its option to rename the category matches option b here.","agree":false}},{"id":"P2","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Definition","weight":"critical","q":"Does review state lead, narrow to per-span state in the repo, or move onto the files agents obey?","lede":"Three of the four claims became table stakes in nine weeks, and the company that sold the fourth one shut down.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"See what the agent changed","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Know what nobody reviewed","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Which text the agent wrote","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Who ships it free already","cells":["Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex, VS Code","VS Code, per file, merged 2026-07-03","Nobody ships it"]},{"label":"When they shipped it","cells":["v2.1.260, published 2026-09-03","Milestone 1.128.0, undocumented","One extension, 88 installs, no date given"]},{"label":"Who tried to sell it","cells":["petergyang/human-review: 1,241 stars in 6 weeks","Almanac sold it, raised $45M, shut 2025-01-31","iA Writer 8: typed versus pasted, personal docs"]},{"label":"What is left for us","cells":["Nothing is left here","Per-span, in the repo, across tools and machines","The whole claim, and we cannot deliver it yet"]}],"note":"Almanac totals are from a podcast summary, not the founder's own writing.","caption":"Where each of the four claims stood on 9 September 2026"},"stakes":"Lead with a sentence four free tools satisfy and the room refutes it live.","state":["The plan leads with review state (who read which passages); its own test went against it.","Four tools show what the agent changed, free; the newest shipped 2026-09-03.","VS Code merged per-file review/unreview 2026-07-03 (1.128.0), behind a setting."],"tension":["Almanac shipped the general form, raised $45M, shut down 2025-01-31.","13 markdown-review extensions total 1,217 installs; the best has 400."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the attack returned, 2026-09-08","items":[["Reactions on the request cited as the demand signal","266","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23, anthropics/claude-code#33932"],["Of its 36 texts, how many ask for anything to persist","0","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23"],["Of its 36 texts, how many name a tool that already does it","18","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23"],["Times \"markdown\" appears in those 36 texts","1","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23"],["Purpose-built markdown-review VS Code extensions that already exist","13, summing to 1,217 installs, ceiling 400","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["Installs of mddiff, which is this sentence shipped 2026-06-03","74","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["Corpus discrepancy to settle before quoting the reaction count","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23 says 266; docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:21 says 262 for the same issue","Pick one and re-check the issue before it goes on a slide"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Obsidian forum, one population, one metric, read 2026-09-08","note":"The closest existing request to review state, against the rendering bug the plan treats as a week-2 channel. docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25 and :410.","data":[["Review state request (likes)",0],["\"Tracking changes within notes\" (likes)",1],["\"Suggestion mode like Google Docs\" (likes)",3],["Nested-construct rendering bug (likes)",501]],"unit":"forum likes"},{"type":"table","title":"The arms and what already stands behind each","cols":["Arm","Supporting evidence","Contradicting evidence"],"rows":[["See what the AI wrote","None found for consumers","iA Writer 7 shipped it 2023; 208-download Obsidian port; best VS Code extension 366 installs; 0 asks in the top twenty Reddit posts (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175, :296)"],["See what the agent changed without asking","The upvoted pain matches it (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296)","The 266-reaction request asks for a per-session panel; 18 of 36 texts name a tool that already does it (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23)"],["Know what nobody has reviewed yet","The upvoted pain matches it; it is the narrowed survivor (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:27)","Obsidian forum: closest request 0 likes, 1 post in nearly three years (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410)"],["Your checklist is green because the agent ticked it","354 of 355 boxes ticked by the agent across a 20-file sample; population 49,664; reviewer-owned checklist 11× rarer","Untested as a headline (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the weeks 1 to 2 tests currently specify","items":[["Developers shown the review mock in both framings","10","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Landing pages","3","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Team conversations on seats and gating","20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["People shown the editor screenshot for five seconds","5","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Kill signal stated for the product","Under 4 of 10 unprompted","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321"],["Kill signal stated per arm","None","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Confirm review state as the headline and test it unchanged","gains":["Every downstream artefact stays usable","No rebuild of the pilot, the pitch or the tier table."],"costs":["Page one of the plan refutes itself","The Obsidian population it would serve gave the idea 0 likes against 501 for a rendering bug."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the headline as written and test it in the pilot. Page one of the plan then refutes itself, because four free tools already satisfy the sentence."},{"k":"b","label":"Narrow it: per-span state kept in the repo, across people, tools and machines","gains":["True, checkable, unoccupied by any incumbent","The 13 competing extensions total 1,217 installs with a ceiling of 400; nobody has won this yet."],"costs":["Invisible without a second person and tool","A solo user sees nothing different from what four free tools already ship."],"system":"The review file must survive out-of-band writes.","screens":"A cross-person list, not a diff.","money":"What a team seat sells.","what":"Narrow to per-span state that lives in the repo and travels across people, tools and machines. True and unoccupied, and invisible until a second person and tool exist."},{"k":"c","label":"Lead with attribution: which text the agent wrote","gains":["The only claim of the four nobody shipped","Google already sells the same idea at its top two tiers, so the shelf exists."],"costs":["Prompts need the MCP server, due after the pilot, untested","Without a prompt, attribution shows only what a commit trailer carried, which is often nothing."],"system":"Pulls the MCP server and trailer readers forward.","screens":"The hover card becomes primary.","money":"A live paid precedent behind the tier.","what":"Lead with which text the agent wrote. Nobody has shipped it at span level, and the prompt half needs the MCP server, which is untested and due after the pilot."},{"k":"d","label":"Scope it to the instruction files agents obey, and drop the repo-wide claim","gains":["Answers a question nobody else is answering, on files that are obeyed rather than read","Stops competing with the four tools that shipped the working-tree half free"],"costs":["Only matters to people running agents, which is a narrower audience","Ties the headline to F35, so both stand or fall together"],"system":"Review state keyed to instruction files rather than to every file in the tree.","screens":"An unreviewed marker in the instruction tree, not a repo percentage.","money":"None directly.","what":"Not what share of the repo is unreviewed, which four free tools now answer. Whether a person has read the AGENTS.md an agent is about to follow."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The repo-wide version lost to four free tools in nine weeks, and the one company that sold it shut down.","Scoped to files an agent obeys, the claim is narrow, testable and nobody has taken it."],"flip":"If the twenty hand made kits show people do not maintain instruction files, this collapses back to (b). The repo-wide claim then has to be defended on its own.","linked":["F3","F35","G1","MK27","P11","P24"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:22","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:23","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:27","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:363","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:21","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:124","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:138","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22","merged: P22","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anthropics/claude-code/main/CHANGELOG.md","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward","https://api.github.com/repos/petergyang/human-review","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:148","docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan moves review state to after the pilot. This is the reset, so it needs saying out loud.","dependsOn":["P1"],"recWhy":"The repo-wide version was the headline, and in nine weeks Claude Code, Codex and VS Code all shipped a way to see what an agent changed, and VS Code shipped per-file review state behind a setting. Almanac, which sold the general form, raised $45M and shut on 31 January 2025. That is three of four claims gone table stakes and the fourth dead. Scoping to instruction files changes what the claim competes with. Not what share of a repo is unreviewed, which is now free, but whether a person has read the AGENTS.md this agent is about to obey. Those files are maintained, 77 commits in openai/codex; large, 6,100 words in apache/airflow; and they drift, LibreChat's two copies 3x apart. Nobody tools that. Tying the headline to F35 is the point, not a cost. If the twenty show nobody maintains these files, fall back to b.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK1"],"areas":["Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition asked the same question with near-identical options and recommended the same answer, option b here: narrow the headline to per-span review state kept in the repo. Its only different option was to decide nothing until the weeks 1-2 tests run.","agree":true}},{"id":"P26","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"THE WORKSHOP","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The AIOS is the founder's most-built thing. Whether it is the product or a source of templates decides the surface count.","q":"Does the AIOS ship as a product, or do three things from it ship as templates while the rest stays private?","lede":"82 rules, 128 skills, 31 hooks and 156 scripts make the founder's agent good. Nobody else can install it in a day.","stakes":"Ship it and the product is two products on one person's pace. Extract from it and frontmatter gets the three ideas that matter without the workshop.","state":["Counted 2026-09-14: 82 learned rules, 128 skills, 31 hooks, 156 scripts, a 10,033 word CLAUDE.md.","43 of the 128 skills are about running the system itself.","The preference log the loop learns from holds zero pairs; aios-site has no commits since it was made."],"tension":["It is why the founder's output is better than most people's.","A workshop tuned to one person's failures is not a kit anyone else adopts."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship three templates: rule format, file gate, skill shape. The rest stays private","gains":["The ideas that matter, without the surface","Fits one person's pace"],"costs":["The orchestrator's value is never sold","43 of the 128 skills only run the system itself, so even a generous slice sells a fraction of the whole."],"system":"Three templates in the product.","screens":"A template picker.","money":"None.","what":"The learned-rule format, the file gate and the skill file shape ship inside frontmatter as templates a stranger can use in a minute. The orchestrator, the 128 skills and the 156 scripts stay yours."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the AIOS as the product and frontmatter as its editor","gains":["The founder's strongest asset becomes the offer","Counted 14 September: 82 rules, 128 skills, 31 hooks, a 10,033-word CLAUDE.md, all built already."],"costs":["Two products on 1.21 days a week","Nobody installs 82 rules and 31 hooks on day one"],"system":"A second product with its own installer.","screens":"Its own surface.","money":"Its own support.","what":"The AIOS is the product and frontmatter is its editor. Two products on one person at 1.21 days a week, and nobody installs 82 rules and 31 hooks on their first day."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship the AIOS as a paid add-on to the Team tier, after the pilot","gains":["A later reason to pay","Needs no new build before launch; the add-on ships only once there is a base product to attach it to."],"costs":["Promises a second product before the first has a user","The preference log the system is meant to learn from currently holds zero pairs."],"system":"No change in the pilot.","screens":"None.","money":"A future price with no costing.","what":"The AIOS becomes a paid add-on to the Team tier after the pilot. A later reason to pay, promised before the first product has a single user."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep all of it private and ship nothing from it","gains":["Nothing to explain","Zero risk of shipping a workshop tuned to one person's failures and calling it a product for others."],"costs":["Loses the learned-rule format, which is the one pattern nobody has","The rule format is what the tension line calls the reason the founder's own output already outperforms."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Nothing ships from it. Nothing to explain, and the learned-rule format, the one pattern nobody has that answers a failure the vendor documents, stays invisible."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The learned-rule format is the one pattern inside the AIOS that answers a documented failure, and it ships as a template.","Everything else in it is tooling for the workshop, which one person cannot carry as a second product."],"flip":"If a team asks to buy the orchestration itself rather than the templates, (c) becomes worth costing.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"What the AIOS is, counted, and what could ship from it","cols":["In the workshop","Ships as a template","Stays private"],"rows":[{"label":"Learned rules","cells":["82","the format","the 82"]},{"label":"Skills","cells":["128","the file shape","the 128"]},{"label":"Hooks and gates","cells":["31 and 69","the file gate","the rest"]},{"label":"Scripts","cells":["156","none","all"]},{"label":"Installable by a stranger in a day","cells":["no","yes","not needed"]}],"note":"Counted from ~/.claude and ~/.sgnk on 2026-09-14. Skills about running the system itself: 43 of 128.","caption":"The workshop against what leaves it"},"sources":["docs/AUDIT-EVERYTHING-2026-09-14-0238.md:1","docs/WHAT-TO-SHIP-2026-09-14-0229.md:1"],"linked":["F39","FF8","P15"],"recWhy":"The AIOS is the best thing on this machine and it is not a product, and both halves matter. Every one of the 82 rules was paid for by a real failure, which is why the agent output here is better than most people's. But 43 of the 128 skills exist to run the system itself, the preference log the loop was built to learn from holds zero pairs, and aios-site has not had a commit since it was made. A workshop tuned to one person's scars is not a kit. Inside it is one pattern that is a product: an instruction file that grows as rules with a reason and a date, so it stays small enough to be obeyed. The vendor's own manual lists too large as a documented failure, and the founder's CLAUDE.md is 10,033 words, which is the proof and the customer in one. Ship that format, the gate that checks a file, and the skill shape. Keep the rest."},{"id":"P15","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Surface budget","weight":"medium","q":"Which concept and control budget governs, and what is displaced to fit inside it?","lede":"Two documents set different limits on what a new user meets first, and neither is enforced.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"The record's definition of simple","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"The thesis surface budget","tone":"accent"},{"title":"The current plan","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Concepts","cells":["3","4","\"seven\", never enumerated"]},{"label":"The nouns","cells":["file, folder, view","plus prompt, paid for in full","at least 10 introduced"]},{"label":"Controls at rest","cells":["9, enumerated","9, unchanged","tabs, 4 modes, counter, bars"]},{"label":"Enforced by","cells":["A CI DOM assertion","The same","Nothing: no .github directory"]}],"note":"From the record, the thesis surface budget and the current plan.","caption":"Three budgets in three documents, none of them enforced."},"stakes":"An untraced, unenforced budget stops nothing, and feature accretion is the failure the plan names.","state":["The phrase \"seven-concept budget\" occurs once in the corpus.","The record sets three first-session nouns, later raised to four: file, folder, view, prompt.","The record also caps controls at rest at nine, with a CI DOM assertion."],"tension":["The highlight and counter have no slot left after the editor's tabs and modes.","Nothing is enforced: the repo has no `.github` directory (verified 2026-09-09)."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three different budgets in three documents","cols":["Document","Number","The nouns","Citation"],"rows":[["Record §18.2","C ≤ 3","file, folder, view","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1848"],["THESIS §103.4","4","file, folder, view, prompt; the fourth bought and paid for at :864","docs/THESIS.md:862"],["Plan v15 §17","\"seven-concept\"","Not enumerated anywhere; the phrase occurs once in the whole corpus","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429"],["What v15 actually introduces","At least 10","file, folder, mode/view, span, review state, review file, attribution, proposal, scan, key","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20, :55 to 62, :135"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the budget rests on, and what it does not","items":[["NN/g progressive disclosure","Bounds disclosure DEPTH, not breadth: \"beyond 2 disclosure levels typically have low usability\"","docs/THESIS.md:872"],["Feature Fatigue (Thompson, Hamilton & Rust, JMR 2005)","Gives a direction for subscription businesses, not a count; Crossref metadata only, the paper is paywalled and was not opened","docs/THESIS.md:873"],["Arc, first-party adoption data","5.52% of DAUs use more than one Space; 4.17% Live Folders; 0.4% Calendar Preview on Hover","docs/THESIS.md:874"],["Note-app abandonment studies found","None; the circulated Obsidian claim is refuted at source","docs/THESIS.md:875, docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1867"],["Hick's law","Not cited: the attached DOI resolves to Hyman 1953, a reaction-time study, not adoption","docs/THESIS.md:877"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The nine at rest, and what the current plan wants to add","cols":["Slot","Record's at-rest list (docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1883 to 1891)","Current-plan addition"],"rows":[["1 to 2","The text, full-bleed; file tree collapsed to a side panel, folder names only","Tree gains unreviewed bars (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409)"],["3","Breadcrumb path, also the rename affordance","Tab strip (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55)"],["4","One mode control: three-state segment Edit · Read · Split","Four modes: Live, Edit, Split, Read (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55)"],["5","One view control: projection selector","none"],["6","Save/sync state as text, three states only","none"],["7","Word count","Review counter, top-left of the panel (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409)"],["8","Search entry point","none"],["9","Right-pane toggle that cycles","Properties panel for frontmatter (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55)"],["+","Explicitly not visible at rest: no AI button, no export button, no share button","ShareMenu, AIMenu and ExportMenu are all wired into the editor header today (src/modules/editor/presentation/EditorPane.tsx:905 to 907)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The counters, and what the code shows today","items":[["V0 target","≤ 9, pinned by a DOM assertion in CI","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1845"],["Repo has no CI at all","Verified 2026-09-09: no .github directory exists","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216 records \"No CI; four gates have reported green while blind\""],["Palette commands in the record","14","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1865"],["Palette commands counted in the tree today","13 fixed, plus dynamic \"Insert template: …\" entries per template file","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/CommandPalette.tsx"],["Slash commands today","14, capped at 16","src/modules/editor/presentation/slash-commands.ts:34 to 51; docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1901"],["Buttons wired into the editor header today","ShareMenu, AIMenu, ExportMenu: all three on the record's not-visible-at-rest list","src/modules/editor/presentation/EditorPane.tsx:905 to 907"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Four concepts, nine controls, and displace to fit","gains":["Both numbers traceable; displacement makes it real"],"costs":["No tabs, only quick-switch on mod+k"],"system":"Build the DOM assertion; there is no CI.","screens":"The editor loses its tab strip; Live folds into modes.","money":"None.","what":"Four nouns a new user meets: file, folder, view, prompt. Nine controls at rest, checked by a DOM assertion in CI. Anything else has to displace something to get in."},{"k":"b","label":"Raise both numbers and pin them with the assertion","what":"Five concepts, eleven controls.","gains":["The headline can be named on first run"],"costs":["Concedes the argument the budget exists to win"],"system":"The assertion pins the new numbers.","screens":"The editor keeps tabs and gains the counter.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep nine for the document surface and give the review panel its own budget","gains":["Precedent: mobile has its own budget"],"costs":["Unlike mobile, the panel shares the screen"],"system":"Two assertions, scoped by region.","screens":"The panel grows independently.","money":"None.","what":"Nine for the document and a separate budget for the review panel. Mobile has a precedent for its own budget; unlike mobile, the panel shares the screen."},{"k":"d","label":"Delete the counters and review the at-rest screenshot instead","gains":["Stops citing a number with no source"],"costs":["Loses the only enforceable check on surface growth"],"system":"No assertion.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Stop citing a number and review the at-rest screenshot instead. Removes a figure with no source, and removes the only enforceable check on surface growth."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Four is the only concept count anyone actually argued for. The thesis admits it was chosen, not calculated from any study.","Strike the unsupported \"seven-concept\" line from the plan this week, then build the automated check that enforces nine controls."],"flip":"If a plain five-second glance shows people need a label to notice the highlight, buy that slot under option b and enforce the new count the same way.","linked":[],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1848","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1867","docs/THESIS.md:862","docs/THESIS.md:864","docs/THESIS.md:872","docs/THESIS.md:873","docs/THESIS.md:874","docs/THESIS.md:877","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1845","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1865","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1881","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1901","docs/THESIS.md:843","docs/THESIS.md:905","docs/CRITIQUE.md:612","docs/CRITIQUE.md:614","src/modules/editor/presentation/EditorPane.tsx:905","merged: P16"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A bare bones editor is a budget claim. Say how many concepts and controls the first build may carry.","recWhy":"An unenforced budget is a sentence, not a constraint, and feature accretion is the failure the plan says it fears most. Four is the only concept count anyone actually argued for: file, folder, view, and now prompt. The plan's own \"seven-concept\" figure appears exactly once, with no list of what the seven are. Nine controls, checked by an automated test that fails the build if a tenth appears, turns the budget into something that holds even when nobody is watching, which matters because one person is building this alone with no CI today. Anything new, a highlight, a counter, an AI button, has to displace something already on the list, not just get added on top of it. That displacement is the actual discipline this card enforces, not the number four itself."},{"id":"P11","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Demo","weight":"critical","q":"Which demo do we build, and does it run on a repository the viewer chose?","lede":"The plan and the critique specify different demos. The current one opens a file we generated.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Fifteen-second highlight","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Rejection, byte-identical","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Their own repo","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Whose file","cells":["One we generated moments before","One we prepared","Theirs, unstaged"]},{"label":"What it proves","cells":["Review state exists","A rejected hunk is byte-identical","Their folder has named problems"]},{"label":"Cost","cells":["18 days: store, render, revert, panel","0 for us; Zed ships it","No new capability needed"]},{"label":"Who already ships it","cells":["Four free agent tools show the diff","Zed, per hunk, documented","Nobody"]},{"label":"Survives the falsifier","cells":["Unknown","No","It is the falsifier"]}],"note":"Costs from the plan's feature table; the third demo from the critique and the product document.","caption":"Three demos, and the only one nobody else can run."},"stakes":"A demo that works only on prepared files fails at week 12.","state":["The plan's demo starts \"Run the agent's spec command\": we generate the file.","Weeks 8 to 9, after 18 days of store, rendering, revert and panel.","The alternative: open the prospect's repo, print what will not survive."],"tension":["The difference shows only on a file built to break a competitor.","The exit test uses ten strangers' own repos, no prepared files."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three demos, and what each proves","cols":["Demo","Where specified","What it proves","What it costs"],"rows":[["Fifteen-second highlight","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:45","That review state exists on a spec the demo just generated","Store 6d, rendering 5d, revert 3d, panel 4d = 18d (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56)"],["Rejection / byte-identical","docs/DECIDE.md:75","That a rejected hunk is byte-identical","Shipped by Zed verbatim; \"0 days for the demo\" for them (docs/CRITIQUE.md:408)"],["Import / verification report on the prospect's own repo","docs/CRITIQUE.md:715, docs/PRODUCT.md:900","That their existing folder has named, countable problems","Needs the left-edge list bug fix (the splice return shape and a padEnd fix); no new capability, per docs/PRODUCT.md:900"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the demo has to overcome","items":[["Purpose-built markdown-review extensions already shipping the highlight idea","13, summing to 1,217 installs, ceiling 400","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["Zed's shipped per-hunk accept/reject","\"accept or reject each individual change hunk\"","docs/CRITIQUE.md:377"],["git add -p installs inside Obsidian alone","3,075,875","docs/CRITIQUE.md:379"],["Falsifier already written","≥6 of 10 strangers notice a difference on their own repo, unstaged, no CRLF setup","docs/CRITIQUE.md:386"],["Files the byte claim is verified against","8,513, of which one wiki is 77%","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296, :514"],["The counter-argument the corpus states against itself","Deterministic projections out-install every AI capability combined by 7.25×, and the import-report cut puts boards and calendars fourth","docs/PRODUCT.md:902"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The falsifier already written into the corpus, and when it is scheduled","items":[["The test","Hand ten strangers their own real repository and the binary, unstaged, with no CRLF setup","docs/CRITIQUE.md:386"],["Pass condition","≥6 of 10 spontaneously notice a difference from their current tool, without being shown the corrupted-file comparison","docs/CRITIQUE.md:386"],["Where it currently sits","DECIDE's \"What would tell us we are wrong\" day-41 test; the critique says run it at day 5 with a prototype instead","docs/CRITIQUE.md:386"],["Where the plan puts the equivalent","Weeks 10 to 12, ten strangers, 6 of 10 by the week-0 measure","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357"],["What the pilot's ten users bring","Their own repos and their own agents: no prepared files exist","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231"],["Corpus the fidelity claims rest on","Seven Obsidian vaults; one wiki is 77% of 8,513 files","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each demo needs from the file, and whether the pilot supplies it","cols":["Demo","File it needs to land","Does a stranger's repo supply it?"],"rows":[["Fifteen-second highlight (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:45)","A spec the agent just generated, in our repo","No: the stranger's agent must have written something they have not read"],["Rejection / byte-identical (docs/DECIDE.md:75)","Any file, but the difference is invisible unless it is CRLF or deeply nested","Sometimes, and only 83% of foreign vaults hit a refusal at all (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214)"],["Repo report (docs/CRITIQUE.md:715)","Any existing folder of markdown","Yes: that is the whole design"],["Any of them, on a file we prepared","A file crafted to break the competitor","Not applicable, and docs/CRITIQUE.md:382 says the room knows"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build the fifteen-second highlight demo as specified","gains":["Shows the headline; the only match if review state stays","Fits the arm the plan currently leads with, so no other messaging has to change."],"costs":["18 days, on our file, in our repo","13 competing extensions already ship a version of this same highlight idea."],"system":"Needs store, rendering, revert, panel.","screens":"The highlight and the review panel.","money":"None.","what":"The fifteen-second highlight demo on a file we prepared, in our repo. Eighteen days of build and it only shows the headline if review state stays the headline, which it did not."},{"k":"b","label":"Build the repo report first, on the viewer's own folder, and adopt it as a rule","what":"Prints what is stale, broken, missing or renders differently.","gains":["Checkable in five seconds on a folder they own","Needs only the left-edge list fix already scheduled, no new capability."],"costs":["Demos the scan, not the headline","Says nothing about review state, which is still what the plan leads with."],"system":"Fix the bug where a list starting at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file; make refusals say why; fix `padEnd`.","screens":"The repo report becomes the cold open.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Build both: the report as cold open, the highlight as the second beat","gains":["Attention and claim in one sitting","Covers both the pain that is measured, their own repo, and the claim that is not yet, the highlight."],"costs":["Largest scope, at 1.21 engineering days a week","Combines an 18-day build with a second, separately scoped demo."],"system":"Both paths finished.","screens":"The repo report, then the editor.","money":"None.","what":"Both: the repo report as the cold open, the highlight as the second beat. The most convincing sitting and the largest scope, at 1.21 engineering days a week."},{"k":"d","label":"Build no demo until weeks 1 to 2 pick the headline; test on mocks","gains":["No demo built for a headline that may fall","Avoids spending the 18-day store, rendering, revert and panel build before the headline is chosen."],"costs":["Defers the decision; the demo is week 8 to 9 anyway","Mocks cannot show a stranger their own repository, which is the one test with a written pass rate."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Mocks only.","money":"None.","what":"No demo until the headline is chosen; test on mocks. Defers a decision the research has now made, and the demo lands in week 8 or 9 either way."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The one demo nobody else can run says something unflattering about their own repo.","Two reviews argued for it independently; it needs no new capability."],"flip":"If review state stays the headline unchanged, the demo must show it and c becomes required.","linked":["F7","P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:45","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:356","docs/DECIDE.md:75","docs/CRITIQUE.md:377","docs/CRITIQUE.md:386","docs/CRITIQUE.md:408","docs/CRITIQUE.md:715","docs/PRODUCT.md:900","docs/PRODUCT.md:902","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/CRITIQUE.md:382","merged: P12","docs/ENGINE.md:1004"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The demo shows a finished loop. Nothing to demo until the editor and the kit both exist.","recWhy":"A demo on a file we prepared proves we can prepare a file. A demo on a folder the viewer chose, that prints what is stale, broken or missing in their own repository, proves something about them, and nobody else can run it because nobody else has the engine. Two independent reviews argued for it and it needs no capability we do not have. After this weekend it is also exactly Phase 1's screen: point it at their repo, show every instruction file, which one wins for a path, and what an agent changed that nobody read. The report and the product are the same view. Option c is the better show and the worse schedule; take it only if the pilot ends with time to spare, which the measured pace says it will not."},{"id":"P13","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Projection law","weight":"critical","q":"Does the projection law survive a review file beside the document, and how many stores outside the file are allowed?","lede":"The founding law says views own no state. The review file beside the document is a store, and the law never allowed one.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Contradicted","items":["shares store: firestore.rules, public route"],"side":"delete under the publishing decision","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Ours","items":["control plane: identity, teams, entitlements, billing"],"side":"one","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Theirs, in the repo","items":[".frontmatter/review.jsonl · usage.jsonl",".frontmatter/state.json · comments in a separate file"],"side":"one directory","tone":"accent"},{"label":"The file","items":["the document bytes, the only source of truth"],"side":"the law","tone":"sunk"}],"note":"Introduced in the current plan, the week-zero decisions and the thesis.","caption":"Every store outside the document, counted together for the first time."},"stakes":"By our own admission gate, the headline feature fails the law we sell.","state":["Every view is a projection \"owning no state of its own\".","Our second feature gate fails anything stored outside the file, and review state is.","Six stores exist across plan and code, each added separately, never totalled."],"tension":["In-file state is ruled out: annotation renders as garbage on GitHub.","The separate file is the point: state in the repo, not the editor."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The law, the gate, and the feature","cols":["Artefact","What it says","Citation"],"rows":[["The law","Every app-like thing is a projection \"owning no state of its own\"","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:267"],["The entailment gate","Fails if it \"needs a sidecar, an index, a `.base`-equivalent, or a row in the control plane\"","docs/THESIS.md:820"],["Dashboards, refused under the law","\"a view with no file behind it — the exact thing the projection law forbids\"","docs/THESIS.md:857"],["Saved searches, redesigned by the entailment gate","Admitted only because the saved search is itself a .md file whose frontmatter holds the query","docs/THESIS.md:836"],["Sort/group on the tree, admitted anyway","Passes only as palette plus `.frontmatter/state.json`, a review file already conceded once","docs/THESIS.md:837"],["Review state","Per-span hashes in `.frontmatter/review.jsonl` beside the files","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Why not in the file","\"every in-file syntax renders as garbage on GitHub\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:64"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the review file is and what is still unknown about it","items":[["Format","One JSON line per span: path, byte start, byte end, content hash, reviewed-by, reviewed-at, and when known author, model, prompt","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Anchor","Byte range plus content hash, relocated by the splice locator on every edit","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Why the journal cannot be the anchor","\"git, other editors and agents bypass it\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Nearest shipped pattern","SpecKit Companion's .spec-context.json, 9,367 installs","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Whether the anchor survives an out-of-band write","Still open after round 2: \"the only scenario the product exists for\"","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Every store outside the document, as currently planned or shipped","cols":["Store","What it holds","Where introduced","Status"],"rows":[[".frontmatter/review.jsonl","Per-span hash, reviewed-by, reviewed-at, and when known author, model, prompt","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","Planned for the pilot, unbuilt"],[".frontmatter/usage.jsonl","Days opened, docs opened, spans reviewed, proposals accepted","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","Drafted, week 0"],["Comments review file","Comments anchored to spans, never in the file","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:64","Planned for the build after the pilot"],[".frontmatter/state.json","Sort and group state on the tree","docs/THESIS.md:837","Ratified in the record, absent from the plan"],["Control plane","Identity, team membership, entitlements, billing, GitHub App installation id","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133; docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:49","Planned"],["shares / public slug","Slug, title, content snapshot, visibility","firestore.rules:78; src/app/(public)/[slug]/page.tsx","Rules committed, route wired and live"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the architecture claims, against what the tree does","items":[["Plan's claim","\"The control plane holds… no document bytes\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133"],["Week-0 draft's claim","\"No document byte ever passes through a Zephyrus server\"","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35"],["Shipped reality","Public page reads the repo server-side with one operator token","src/container/dependency-container.ts:140, src/config/env.ts:85"],["Stores counted together anywhere in the plan","0: no exhibit lists them","verified: no such exhibit in docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Amend the law and cap the stores at two: one repo directory, one control plane","gains":["Legalises the headline under a bounded exception","Caps the exception at two stores, so it cannot grow the way six ungoverned stores already have."],"costs":["The clause is what a competitor quotes back","It concedes in writing that the law, as stated, is not literally true today."],"system":"A CI check: no store outside the two.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Amend the law with a bounded exception. Two stores outside the file: one directory in the repo, one control plane for identity and billing. Nothing else, ever."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the law absolute and put review state in the file","gains":["The law stays as written","No new clause for a competitor to quote."],"costs":["Ruled out: renders as garbage on GitHub","Every in-file syntax tested for this renders as literal text on the one platform that matters."],"system":"An in-file syntax the engine writes and strips.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the law absolute by writing review state into the document. Ruled out already, because an annotation in the file renders as garbage on GitHub and in every other tool."},{"k":"c","label":"Drop the projection law from the product's language; keep it as engineering","gains":["No engineering; the contradiction disappears","Removes a sentence from the pitch rather than a store from the codebase."],"costs":["Loses the category sentence","\"Owning no state of its own\" is the line that currently makes the file-is-truth claim legible."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Retire the law from the product's language and keep it as an engineering rule. The contradiction disappears, and so does the one sentence that puts us in a category."},{"k":"d","label":"Re-test whether review state needs storage outside the file, and publish either way","gains":["The record's own process, settled on the record","Follows the same entailment-gate test already used to admit or refuse every other feature."],"costs":["Review state fails the gate as written, leaving no headline","The gate already fails anything needing \"a sidecar, an index, or a row in the control plane.\""],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Re-test whether review state needs a store outside the file at all, and publish the result either way. Honest, and as written the feature fails the gate and there is no headline."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The law is the only sentence that puts the product in a category.","Two stays true once billing exists; the record already conceded one separate state file."],"flip":"If the anchor cannot survive an out-of-band write, the review file projects nothing and c is honest.","linked":["F1","L1","P2"],"sources":["docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:267","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:297","docs/THESIS.md:820","docs/THESIS.md:836","docs/THESIS.md:837","docs/THESIS.md:857","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:64","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:527","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:49","firestore.rules:78","src/config/env.ts:85","src/container/dependency-container.ts:140","merged: P14"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The review file is the thing being deferred, so the law it bends waits with it.","recWhy":"The projection law is the sentence that makes this product a category rather than a feature: the file on disk is the only truth and every view is a projection of it. The review file beside the document breaks that as written, and it has to, because state inside the file renders as garbage everywhere else. So the choice is between amending the law and losing it. Option a amends it with a cap that stays true after billing exists: one directory in the repo that travels with the code, one control plane that holds identity and money and never a byte of a document. Two is a number a competitor can quote, and it is also a number we can defend, which is more than the six uncounted stores in the code today. On instruction files the same law holds: the review file records who read AGENTS.md, and AGENTS.md itself is untouched."},{"id":"P25","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Scope","weight":"critical","q":"Does the AIOS fusion, a compiler that drives Framer and heals its own code, belong in frontmatter or a separate product?","lede":"The largest thing dictated in six weeks sits in no plan, card or feature row, and keeps resurfacing.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Today's plan","items":["Splice engine","Review state","Editor"],"tone":"ok"},{"label":"Dictated, unplanned","items":["MDX compiler","YAML tokens to CSS","Live Framer render"],"tone":"warn"},{"label":"Dictated, unplanned","items":["Self-healing code","Headless test loop","Agent repair"],"tone":"warn"}],"note":"Bottom two layers exist only in a transcript.","caption":"What the plan scopes, against what was asked for"},"stakes":"Unplaced, it returns every planning round. Folded in unscoped, it doubles the build.","state":["Absent from the plan and every feature row; recovered from the second handover answers.","Two mechanisms named: a compiler driving Framer from YAML tokens; self-healing code.","Raised at least three times in six weeks, dropped each time."],"tension":["Rendering live components is a different product from a review-state editor.","The settled verdict already says build a compiler, so this is scope, not direction."],"evidence":[{"type":"quote","title":"What was actually asked for, 2026-07-25 to 2026-09-06","items":[["The fusion","'our AI OS, this front matter, this markdown capability … fuse them into generating something crazy'","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:593"],["The compiler","'our compiler directly trigger into the rapid web building platforms like framer … the entire live application updates automatically'","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:600"],["Self-healing","'change a database schema in one section … the frontend component in another section breaks … the error is piped into your local Claude code'","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:606"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Separate product, named and parked","gains":["Keeps the 90-day plan buildable; the idea gets an owner","The idea has surfaced at least three times in six weeks and been dropped each time; naming it stops that."],"costs":["Two products from one consulting income","Splits founder attention that the 90-day plan already prices at 1.21 engineering days a week."],"system":"No change to frontmatter.","screens":"None.","money":"None now.","what":"A separate product with a name and an owner, parked. The 90-day plan stays buildable and the idea stops returning every planning round because it finally has somewhere to sit."},{"k":"b","label":"One product, compiler in v2","gains":["One brand, one codebase; the engine is a compiler front end","Matches the settled verdict that the engine is a compiler, not a new framework."],"costs":["v2 unbounded until Framer and healing are specified","Neither mechanism, the Framer driver or the self-healing loop, has a written specification yet."],"system":"The engine gains a token pass and render target.","screens":"Adds a live-preview surface.","money":"Raises the v2 build cost materially.","what":"One product, the compiler in version two. One brand and one codebase, and version two is unbounded until Framer and self-healing are specified, which they are not."},{"k":"c","label":"Fold only the token pass into v1","what":"YAML tokens compile to CSS variables; no Framer, no healing.","gains":["Smallest slice that makes the vision visible","Needs no Framer integration or healing loop to demonstrate the underlying token pass."],"costs":["Still needs a render surface to be worth anything","A token pass with nothing rendering from it proves less than the full idea promises."],"system":"One new pass over frontmatter values.","screens":"Adds an inline preview.","money":"Days, not weeks."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The 90-day plan already carries 41.5 days of debt before the headline starts.","Nothing is decidable until headline and form factor are. A named owner stops the resurfacing."],"flip":"If the compiler is what a buyer actually wants, the editor is the side project.","linked":["FF1","P1","P2"],"sources":["docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:593","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:600","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:606","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:332","CLAUDE.md:74"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Fusing with the studio AI system is the opposite of narrowing. Revisit once the pilot has a result.","recWhy":"The compiler that drives Framer and heals its own code is the largest thing dictated in six weeks and it appears in no plan, card or feature row, which is why it keeps resurfacing. Rendering live components is a different product from an editor for the files agents obey, with a different buyer and a different surface, and the 90-day plan already carries 41.5 days of debt before its headline starts. Naming it and parking it is not a rejection; it gives the idea an owner so it stops being raised and dropped. This is also the P26 answer applied one level up: the AIOS is a workshop, and the compiler is the largest room in it. Option c, tokens to CSS variables only, is the smallest honest slice and still needs a render surface to be worth anything."},{"id":"P3","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Abandoned headlines","weight":"high","q":"Does byte-exactness return to the pitch, stay a silent mechanism, or leave the copy?","lede":"It is the only claim a stranger checks in five seconds, and no vendor has taught buyers to want it.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"A tool that reformats on write","lines":[{"t":"line 12: the requested change","mark":"="},{"t":"line 40: blank line doubled","mark":"+"},{"t":"every line: carriage return destroyed","mark":"-"}]},"after":{"title":"A byte-range splice","lines":[{"t":"line 12: the requested change","mark":"="},{"t":"every other byte identical","mark":"="}]},"via":"splice, or refuse","note":"Cursor staff call the left-hand column a known issue they are tracking.","caption":"Real, invisible on an ordinary file, and below the category's floor."},"stakes":"Promote it and the demo needs a file built to break a competitor.","state":["The plan ruled it \"Mechanism, not pitch\" on 4 complaints in 12,556.","No redlining vendor sells below word level; `--color-words` has been free since 2006."],"tension":["Real defect, admitted by an incumbent's staff; demand is 0.03%.","Invisible in a demo unless the file is adversarial.","Any fidelity number drags the 83% refusal rate with it."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Demand for byte-exactness, counted","items":[["Complaints about mangled formatting in editor comments","4 of 12,556","docs/DECIDE.md:27"],["In launch comments","1 of 3,749","docs/DECIDE.md:27"],["\"line endings\" in GitHub issue titles","1 of 43,656","docs/DECIDE.md:27"],["Formatting-caused switches found in eleven years","1","docs/DECIDE.md:27"],["Files the byte-exactness claim is verified on each release","8,513, of which one wiki is 77%","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296, :514"],["The incumbents' admitted defect","Cursor staff: doubled blank lines and destroyed CRs on CRLF, \"a known issue we're tracking\"","docs/DECIDE.md:35"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where the mechanism is visible and where it is not","cols":["Session-one file","What frontmatter shows","What the user already has"],"rows":[["LF, ASCII, flat, 2 hunks","a reviewable diff","Zed: accept or reject each individual change hunk (docs/CRITIQUE.md:377)"],["Same, inside Obsidian","nothing new","4,165,249 cumulative downloads across four AI plugins (docs/CRITIQUE.md:378)"],["Same, in a git repo","a byte-identical rejected file","git diff / git add -p, installed 3,075,875 times inside Obsidian alone (docs/CRITIQUE.md:379)"],["CRLF, or nested fence-in-callout-in-list, or a 6-file change","a real, unmatched difference","nothing equivalent (docs/CRITIQUE.md:380)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep it where it is: one proof line under the headline, never the headline","gains":["Free; matches the demand evidence exactly","Complaints about mangled formatting are 4 in 12,556; the mechanism does not need to be sold."],"costs":["A proof line nobody reads does no work","It sits under the headline, not in it, so it never gets tested as a claim on its own."],"system":"No change.","screens":"One landing-page line.","money":"None.","what":"One line under the headline that says bytes you did not touch are never changed. Costs nothing and matches a demand signal of 4 complaints in 12,556."},{"k":"b","label":"Promote it to a co-headline for the technical audience","gains":["A claim no competitor can make","Cursor's own staff call the defect it fixes a known issue they are tracking."],"costs":["A defect claim about named companies","Demand for the fix it demonstrates is one formatting-caused switch found in eleven years."],"system":"No change.","screens":"A second, technical landing page.","money":"None.","what":"Make it a headline for the technical reader. It is a claim about named competitors' defects, and it drags the 83% refusal rate into every conversation."},{"k":"c","label":"Remove it from user-facing copy; keep it as a constraint and a test","gains":["Shorter pitch, no fidelity number to defend","Removes the one figure that drags the 83 percent refusal rate into the same sentence."],"costs":["The demo loses its only checkable claim","Every other line left in the pitch is then a subjective preference, not a fact."],"system":"The corpus test stays in CI.","screens":"One line removed.","money":"None.","what":"Take it off the page and keep it as an engine test. Shorter pitch, and the demo loses the one thing a stranger can check on their own folder in five seconds."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep it, and publish fidelity and refusal rate as one fraction","gains":["Defensible, and the plan requires it anyway","The corpus already ties every fidelity figure to a refusal rate; this just publishes the pairing."],"costs":["An 83% refusal rate beside a fidelity claim","The refusal rate is measured on 8,513 files where one wiki alone is 77 percent of the count."],"system":"The corpus run emits both numbers.","screens":"A methodology note beside it.","money":"None.","what":"Publish fidelity and refusal as one fraction. Defensible and required by the plan anyway, and an 83% refusal figure sits beside the claim."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The measurement is real but demand is 4 in 12,556, so the plan's slot is right.","One sentence keeps the only claim checkable against their own folder."],"flip":"If every headline variant fails in weeks 1 to 2 and people react to corruption, take b.","linked":["E5","P11"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:174","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:516","docs/DECIDE.md:27","docs/DECIDE.md:35","docs/DECIDE.md:59","docs/CRITIQUE.md:377","docs/CRITIQUE.md:382","https://www.diffchecker.com/pricing/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Byte exactness is engine copy. The pitch is the editor and the kit now.","recWhy":"Byte-exactness is real, measured, and admitted as a defect by an incumbent's own staff. It is also wanted by almost nobody: four complaints in a corpus of 12,556, and no vendor has taught buyers to want it because none of them can make the claim. That is the shape of a proof line, not a headline. Phase 0 puts that line under the sentence on the landing page, which is why E5, the one engine file that refutes it, moved into the floor this weekend. A proof line that is false in one line of code is worse than no line. Option a keeps the only claim checkable against their own folder without spending the pitch on it. Promote it and the demo has to be built on a file designed to break a competitor, which is a demo about them rather than about us."},{"id":"P7","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Boundary","weight":"high","q":"Which refusal list is the public one, and does each refusal carry a published reversal price?","lede":"Refusals are the positioning. The plan's list and the record's list differ and were never compared.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"The plan's not-in-product line","tone":"warn"},{"title":"The record's refusal list","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Plugins, code execution","cells":["Refused, no price","Refused; reverses at 1,000 paying users"]},{"label":"Project management","cells":["Refused, no price","Refused; nothing reverses it"]},{"label":"Chat sidebar","cells":["Refused, no price","Refused; under 20% acceptance at 90 days"]},{"label":"Hosting user content","cells":["Refused","Not listed, and a public route ships today"]},{"label":"Live cursors, multiplayer","cells":["Missing","Refused; 3 of first 20 teams cancel on presence"]},{"label":"Mobile editor","cells":["Refused in v1","Admitted, with its own budget of 4 controls"]}],"note":"Record entries come from the thesis; mobile is admitted in its ten-feature test.","caption":"Two lists, one contradiction, and four missing reversal prices."},"stakes":"A refusal announced and then quietly reversed costs more than never announcing it.","state":["The plan lists six refusals and makes them the positioning.","The record lists seven, each with a reversal condition.","Mobile: refused by the plan, budgeted by the record."],"tension":["Some entries are identity, some roadmap; the list presents one kind.","A published reversal price is a number a competitor quotes back."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two refusal lists, compared","cols":["Refusal","In the plan's Definition section, line 43","In the record's refusal list, section 103.3","Reversal condition on record"],"rows":[["Plugins / code execution","Yes","Yes (:853)","Capability model with no fs/network/eval, plus a published audit; not before 1,000 paying users"],["Project management","Yes","Yes (:852)","\"Nothing reverses it\" (a company-identity constraint, to be said publicly)"],["Chat sidebar","Yes","Yes (:855)","<20% strong acceptance across all verbs at 90 days"],["Hosting user content","Yes","Not listed","None written, and the code ships a wired public route (the document-hosting decision)"],["Vendor database of document bytes","Yes","Not listed","None written, and firestore.rules designs one (the same hosting decision)"],["Mobile editor in v1","Yes","Admitted, not refused (:843: ADMIT with its own V0 ≤ 4)","Direct conflict between the two documents"],["Live cursors / real-time multiplayer","Missing","Yes (:854)","≥3 of the first 20 paying teams cancel citing presence by name"],["Template galleries","Missing","Yes (:856)","\"Where do I get templates\" a top-3 support string for two quarters, and even then the answer is a git repo"],["Dashboards","Missing","Yes (:857)","Never as a screen; a dashboard file is already legal today"],["Anything with its own configuration surface","Missing","Yes (:858)","Only a default wrong for a majority cohort"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What one refusal is worth as deflection, measured on the closest analogue","items":[["Obsidian forum topics tagged to the plugin surface","7,076 of 31,048 tagged topics = 22.79%","docs/BUSINESS.md:808"],["Just dataview + templater, the two that require code execution","4,243 topics = 13.67%","docs/BUSINESS.md:809"],["Support load per install, code-execution plugins vs ecosystem average","2.78×","docs/BUSINESS.md:811"],["Warning attached to that number by its own author","Do not quote 22.79% as a share of our future tickets: it establishes the class exists and is large, not its size for us","docs/BUSINESS.md:819"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Each refusal, what it costs us, and what would reverse it","cols":["Refusal","Measured cost of keeping it","Written reversal condition"],"rows":[["Plugins / code execution","Forecloses the category's only demonstrated community moat; the competitor's registry is 143,290,675 downloads deep","Capability model with no fs/network/eval plus a published audit; not before 1,000 paying users (docs/THESIS.md:853)"],["Project management","Refuses a whole adjacent category","\"Nothing reverses it\" (a company-identity constraint, to be stated publicly; docs/THESIS.md:852)"],["Chat sidebar","Refuses the surface every AI product ships","<20% strong acceptance across all verbs at 90 days (docs/THESIS.md:855)"],["Live cursors / multiplayer","HackMD and Docmost lead with it","≥3 of the first 20 paying teams cancel citing presence by name (docs/THESIS.md:854)"],["Hosting user content","\"a byte-preserving engine whose output nobody can see is a claim without a demonstration\"","None written; PRD v2 recommends shipping it (docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4206)"],["Mobile editor in v1","About 15% of complaints","No condition: the record simply ADMITS mobile with its own V0 ≤ 4 budget (docs/THESIS.md:843, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:539)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the strongest refusal buys, and what the weakest one costs","items":[["Share of Obsidian tagged forum topics touching the plugin surface","22.79%, but explicitly not to be quoted as a share of our tickets","docs/BUSINESS.md:808, :819"],["Support load per install for code-execution plugins","2.78× the ecosystem average","docs/BUSINESS.md:811"],["Support cost at 10,000 users, already modelled","about 90 founder-hours a month","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137"],["Permanent duty publishing creates","24-hour acknowledgement, no size floor, personal, never ends","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4208"],["Incumbents that shipped a hide-all-AI switch only after backlash","Zed (412 reactions), VS Code (30), Telegram (181)","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One reconciled list, with each entry's reversal price published","what":"The record's seven plus hosting and the vendor database.","gains":["One artefact; principle told apart from roadmap","Reconciles two lists that already disagree on three entries the plan calls final."],"costs":["Waits on the hosting decision for two entries","Hosting user content is refused in one document and shipped, wired, in the code."],"system":"The list becomes the admission gate.","screens":"A refusals section, site and pitch.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the plan's six as public copy, the record's seven as the internal gate","gains":["Short copy, strict gate, nothing to publish","Costs nothing to write; the internal gate already exists in the record."],"costs":["Nobody reading both can tell which governs","The plan lists six refusals, the record seven, and mobile is refused by one and budgeted by the other."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"The plan's six stay as public copy and the record's seven stay as the internal gate. Short, strict, and nobody reading both can tell which one governs."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish only the three that are identity, and treat the rest as roadmap","what":"No plugins, no code execution, no project management, no chat sidebar.","gains":["Refuses only what never reverses, so it holds","Plugins, project management and the chat sidebar are the three the record calls hardest to reverse."],"costs":["Loses \"we never hold your files\"","That line is the one the shipped public route already contradicts."],"system":"No change.","screens":"A shorter positioning line.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Stop publishing a refusal list; keep it as the internal admission gate","gains":["No visible reversal risk; the gate still works","Keeps the deflection value measured on Obsidian, 22.79 percent of tagged forum topics, unpublished."],"costs":["Loses the only copy needing no unproven feature","A reader gets no way to check which refusals the product actually keeps."],"system":"No change.","screens":"The positioning line goes.","money":"None.","what":"Stop publishing refusals and keep them as the admission gate. No reversal can embarrass us, and the copy loses the only lines that promise nothing unproven."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One list, or the documents keep diverging; they already disagree on mobile.","Priced reversals make the permanent refusals read stronger by contrast."],"flip":"If the founders will not publicly refuse multiplayer, galleries and dashboards, take c.","linked":["P10"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/THESIS.md:843","docs/THESIS.md:852","docs/THESIS.md:853","docs/THESIS.md:854","docs/THESIS.md:855","docs/THESIS.md:856","docs/THESIS.md:857","docs/THESIS.md:858","docs/BUSINESS.md:808","docs/BUSINESS.md:819","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:539","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4206","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4208","merged: P17"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The refusal list is positioning for a product with users. None yet.","recWhy":"Refusals are the positioning, and two lists that disagree, already on mobile, mean the positioning is not written down. One reconciled list fixes that, and this weekend added two entries it has to carry: no live collaborative editing, and no writes to a customer's repository, both refused with reasons in the research. A published reversal price is the part that worries people, because a competitor can quote it. It is also what makes the permanent refusals credible by contrast: no plugins, no code execution, no held documents read as identity when the roadmap items beside them carry a price. Option c keeps only the three that never reverse and loses the promise that files stay yours, which is the one buyers check.","dupes":{"dropped":["G14"],"areas":["Go-to-market & channels"],"why":"Go-to-market & channels asked which refusals may be stated in public and recommended publishing only the three that are true and differentiating today, closest to option c here. This card recommends one reconciled list with a reversal price on each entry.","agree":false}},{"id":"P10","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Boundary","weight":"high","q":"Does the plugin ban stay absolute, or does the record's data-only exception go into the product?","lede":"The ban leads the positioning. The record found it forecloses the only community moat this category has shown.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Banned outright","items":["third-party code in our process","eval, filesystem, network"],"side":"the ban","tone":"stop"},{"label":"The record's exception","items":["declarative render profiles","out-of-process import and export adapters"],"side":"unbuilt","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Already planned","items":["MCP server: agents write through the engine"],"side":"the build after the pilot","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"The real boundary","items":["the bytes on disk, not the process"],"side":"the security argument","tone":"accent"}],"note":"A sandbox protects the machine and does nothing for byte preservation.","caption":"What a ban on the process protects, and what it does not."},"stakes":"Claim a community moat while banning its only mechanism and informed readers stop believing.","state":["The plan refuses plugins and code execution outright, and again as a security rule.","The record keeps the ban with one exception: data-only, no code.","The record finds no durable community moat here without third-party code."],"tension":["What fails is the moat claim on top; dropping it is cheapest.","A declarative surface grows conditionals and becomes a language."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the ban costs, measured 2026-08-30","items":[["Obsidian community registry, cumulative downloads","143,290,675","docs/CRITIQUE.md:350"],["Registry entries","7,139 plugins (docs/CRITIQUE.md:350). Note docs/BUSINESS.md:827 counts 7,119 on the same date; reconcile before quoting","plus 717 themes, docs/BUSINESS.md:827"],["Cumulative downloads across four AI plugins inside Obsidian","4,165,249","docs/CRITIQUE.md:378"],["Claudian: one developer, elapsed time, stars","269 days, 15,064 stars, no CI, no PRD, no corpus","docs/CRITIQUE.md:409"],["VS Code Marketplace extensions","135,646","docs/BUSINESS.md:825"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the ban buys, and what the exception would cost","cols":["Measure","Value","Source"],"rows":[["Share of Obsidian tagged topics touching the plugin surface","22.79% (7,076 of 31,048)","docs/BUSINESS.md:808"],["Support load per install, code-execution plugins vs average","2.78×","docs/BUSINESS.md:811"],["Top 20 Obsidian plugins that are core roadmap, not code engines","18 of 20","docs/BUSINESS.md:864"],["Templater's irreducible arbitrary-code share of active installs","0.701%","docs/BUSINESS.md:864"],["Plugins whose best-ever single version never passed 1,000 downloads","4,763 of 7,085 = 67.2%","docs/BUSINESS.md:857"],["Predicted failure of a declarative surface","It grows conditionals, then loops, then an escape hatch: Dataview shipped DQL then dataviewjs","docs/BUSINESS.md:914"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the ban absolute and say so in the positioning","gains":["Simplest to defend; the largest support deflection","Matches a support load measured at 2.78 times the ecosystem average for code-execution plugins."],"costs":["Forecloses the only demonstrated community moat","Obsidian's registry alone has moved 143 million cumulative downloads on this surface."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No third-party code, ever, and say so on the page. Simplest to defend and the largest support deflection; it also rules out the one community moat this category has shown."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the exception: declarative render profiles and out-of-process adapters","gains":["Contribution without touching the byte guarantee","18 of the 20 most popular Obsidian plugins are declarative, not code engines, so the shape has precedent."],"costs":["Becomes a programming language","Dataview's own history is the warning: it shipped a query language, then dataviewjs, an escape hatch."],"system":"Profile format, conformance runner, registry.","screens":"A profiles list in settings.","money":"Support load with no revenue.","what":"Allow declarative render profiles and out-of-process adapters. Contribution without touching the bytes, until the profile grammar grows conditionals and becomes a language."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the ban and demote community from moat number one","gains":["A writing fix; nothing breaks","Costs one sentence in the positioning deck, and the code-execution ban itself is untouched."],"costs":["Every deck loses a moat claim with no replacement","The 22.79 percent Obsidian figure it drops is explicitly not to be quoted as our own future number."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the ban exactly as written and stop calling community the first moat. A documentation change; every deck loses a claim it could not back."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the ban and make the MCP server the sanctioned third-party surface","gains":["Already planned after the pilot; no sandbox needed","Reuses a build that has to exist for other reasons, rather than adding a new one."],"costs":["Untested whether agents route writes through it","Nothing in the corpus has measured whether an agent would actually use this path over a direct write."],"system":"The MCP server gains a published contract.","screens":"An integrations page.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the ban and route third parties through the MCP server, which is planned after the pilot. No sandbox needed; untested whether agents will route writes through it."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The ban is correct: the security boundary is the bytes, and sandboxes do not protect bytes.","Change the moat claim and keep the ban; the record calls that a documentation fix."],"flip":"If \"I need X and you won't build it\" tops 15% of tickets a quarter, b is forced.","linked":["FF12","P7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:551","docs/BUSINESS.md:825","docs/BUSINESS.md:827","docs/BUSINESS.md:846","docs/BUSINESS.md:902","docs/BUSINESS.md:906","docs/BUSINESS.md:914","docs/BUSINESS.md:916","docs/BUSINESS.md:922","docs/CRITIQUE.md:350","docs/CRITIQUE.md:398","docs/CRITIQUE.md:409"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"No plugin surface ships before the pilot, so the ban has nothing to govern.","recWhy":"The ban is correct for a reason that does not move: the security boundary in this product is the bytes on disk, and a sandbox protects the process, not the bytes. Every plugin system that ever mattered eventually wrote to the file. What fails is the sentence on top, that community is the first moat, because the record could not find a durable community moat in this category without third-party code. Dropping the claim costs a paragraph in some decks. Dropping the ban costs the promise the whole product rests on. Option d is where this goes later: an MCP surface lets an agent act through us without running inside us, which is the only third-party model that keeps the boundary. It is after the pilot and untested, so it is not the answer today.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK9"],"areas":["Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition recommended allowing out-of-process extension only, and saying so explicitly, which is the out-of-process half of option b here. This card recommends keeping the ban and no longer calling community the first moat.","agree":false}},{"id":"P24","cat":"Product & definition","sub":"Definition","weight":"high","q":"Is the claim that review catches defects, or that it tells you what changed?","lede":"The plan never says which job the review surface does.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":160,"cols":[{"title":"Catch what the agent broke","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Know what changed","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"What the evidence says","cells":["Defects were 78 of 570 review comments, 14%","Reviewers told the origin validated better"]},{"label":"What we must prove","cells":["That our surface finds errors","That a person can see the change at all"]},{"label":"Failure mode","cells":["Missed one, so the tool failed","Ignored it, so the tool is noise"]},{"label":"Precedent","cells":["Clinical alerts: 55 to 98% override","GitHub Viewed, Google's blue dot"]},{"label":"Demo","cells":["Needs a planted defect","Opens their own repository"]}],"note":"Microsoft's 14% is from ICSE 2013; the override range is 34 drug-interaction studies. Both are transferred from other domains.","caption":"Two jobs, one surface, and only one of them is defensible."},"stakes":"Promise defects and the first miss is our fault; promise orientation and people must want it daily.","state":["Changed spans are highlighted; what the reading is for is never said.","Reviewers told which code was machine-written validated it better.","No evidence exists on reviewing AI prose; all of it is transferred."],"tension":["Defects were 78 of 570 review comments, fourth of nine categories.","One missed error falsifies a defect claim; the dead review tools made it."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the review and alerting literature measures","items":[["Defects as a share of code review comments","78 of 570 (14%), fourth of nine categories","sback.it ICSE 2013"],["Drug-interaction alert override rate across 34 studies","55 to 98%","pubmed 35673040"],["One hospital: alerts overridden, and reasons that were meaningless","12,659 of 14,612 (86.6%); 88.3%","pubmed 38658357"],["The one system that achieved acceptance","31 rules firing in 0.19% of 500,274 sessions, 57.5% accepted","pubmed 34450669"],["Developer review budget, and median change size","3.2 h/week; 24 lines","sback.it ICSE 2018 SEIP"],["Evidence about reviewing AI-written prose","None found","self-declared gap"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Claim defect detection: the tool finds what the agent got wrong","gains":["The strongest promise, and the easiest to sell","Matches what a buyer instinctively wants review software to do."],"costs":["Falsified by one missed defect","Across 570 review comments in the closest study, defects were 14 percent, the fourth largest category."],"system":"Needs a judgement layer we do not have.","screens":"A ranked findings list, not a change list.","money":"Higher price, and a support obligation.","what":"Promise that review finds what the agent got wrong. The strongest sentence in a sale, falsified by the first defect it misses, which every dead review tool learned."},{"k":"b","label":"Claim orientation: you can see what changed and who wrote it","gains":["The one measured job; no missed error falsifies it","Matches what GitHub, Reviewable and the Claude Code extension already track: state, not verdicts."],"costs":["Weaker than a buyer wants to hear","Says nothing about whether what was written is any good, only that someone has seen it."],"system":"No judgement layer; the review file is enough.","screens":"An exhaustible change list and the highlight.","money":"Prices like a utility.","what":"Promise that you can see what changed and who wrote it. The one job with a measurement behind it: reviewers told which text was machine-written validated it better."},{"k":"c","label":"Claim the record: a durable ledger for whoever is answerable","gains":["Fits the regulated buyer; needs no attention","A durable ledger survives even if nobody ever opens the review panel."],"costs":["No regulation opened requires per-span review","Every regulation checked, pharma and aviation included, approves at the whole-document level."],"system":"Keeping review-file history becomes a feature nothing builds.","screens":"An export of who read what, when.","money":"Team tier only.","what":"Promise a durable record of who was answerable for what. Fits a regulated buyer and needs no attention; no regulation opened requires review below the whole document."},{"k":"d","label":"Claim no job; show the state and let people decide what it is for","gains":["Nothing to be wrong about","The safest claim available, and it costs nothing to defend in a room."],"costs":["A pitch with no verb dies in one meeting","Every real alerting system studied needed a stated job to earn any acceptance at all."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Promise nothing and show the state. Cannot be wrong, and a pitch with no verb does not survive a meeting."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Orientation is the only job with a measurement behind it.","Put c under b as the team framing; nobody buys a record for themselves."],"flip":"If the five who bill for documents need errors caught, c is the sale.","linked":["P11","P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:427","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13","https://sback.it/publications/icse2013.pdf","https://sback.it/publications/icse2018seip.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35673040/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34450669/","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:408"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Both readings describe review state, which is deferred.","recWhy":"There are two jobs a review surface can claim and only one of them can be measured. Defect detection is the sentence a buyer wants to hear and it is falsified by a single miss, which is how the dead tools in this category went. Orientation, seeing what changed and who wrote it, is the job with evidence behind it. Reviewers told which text was machine-written validated it better, and defects were 78 of 570 review comments, fourth of nine categories, so review was never mostly about errors. On instruction files orientation is the entire feature: what did an agent change in the file other agents obey, and has a person looked. Claim b, and put c underneath it as the team framing, because a record is bought by whoever is answerable and nobody buys one for themselves."},{"id":"FF1","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Core choice","weight":"critical","q":"Standalone editor, VS Code extension, or one core behind both, and which ships first?","lede":"Every estimate prices a standalone shell. No line anywhere prices an extension.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":144,"cols":[{"title":"Standalone shell","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"VS Code extension","tone":"accent"},{"title":"One core, both","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Population in reach","cells":["Show HN median 3 points across 386 launches","24.89M Claude Code installs in that store","Both, in sequence"]},{"label":"Review niche there","cells":["Not applicable","13 extensions, 1,217 installs, ceiling 400","Same 400 ceiling"]},{"label":"Live mode","cells":["Yes","No view-zone API, so no","Two diverging feature sets"]},{"label":"Table stakes","cells":["14.5 to 20.5 days, unbudgeted","Host supplies about 6.5 of 17.5 days","Paid once, on the shell"]},{"label":"The name","cells":["Free","Owned by an 82,265-install incumbent","Two names to explain"]},{"label":"Time to the pilot","cells":["57.9 weeks at 1.21 days a week","No estimate exists","No estimate exists"]}],"note":"Rows read from the plan and re-measured 2026-09-09.","caption":"Three surfaces, and the row nobody has costed"},"stakes":"Choosing the shell commits 58 calendar weeks before a stranger is asked anything.","state":["The plan's form-factor decision offers three options and carries an estimate for none.","The 2026-09-08 research round measured byte-exact extension writes twice: 94/94 and 156/156 bytes.","GitHub's Spec Kit does prompt-to-spec free and open source: 136,339 stars, 12,249 forks, pushed 2026-09-12. Kiro from AWS does it paid. Fetched 2026-09-14."],"tension":["It shipped the per-file half of our review marker itself on 2026-07-03.","Nobody ships review state, who read which span, in the repo across machines.","Generating the kit is the half two of the largest players give away or subsidise. Reading, editing and keeping it current is the half nobody has taken."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What round 2 actually measured","items":[["Extension byte-exact write, harness 1","94 / 94 bytes","VS Code 1.135.0 (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364)"],["Extension byte-exact write, harness 2","156 / 156 bytes","independent second harness (GAPS:125)"],["Engine that would port","3,614 lines, 13 files, no DOM","re-verified in src/modules/mdmax, 2026-09-09"],["Engine symbols reaching product code today","1 symbol from 1 of 13 files","decodeStrict, exactly 2 import sites, re-verified"],["Extension engineering estimate in the plan","none","no line in Exhibit 1 or §9"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Population against niche","cols":["Surface","Installs","Source"],"rows":[["Copilot Chat","78.06M","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48"],["Claude Code VS Code extension","24.89M","GAPS:48; PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:150"],["markdown-all-in-one (10.6x co-recommendation lift with Copilot)","14.45M","GAPS:48"],["git-ai","61,517","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:151"],["SpecKit Companion (the closest comparable tool)","9,367","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:152"],["All 13 markdown-review extensions, combined","1,217","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9, :24"],["Best of those 13, the ceiling","400","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["mddiff, our own sentence, shipped 2026-06-03","74","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Standalone editor only, as the plan is written","gains":["Keeps Live, the free-editor story, the Obsidian audience","Keeps Live rendering, which VS Code cannot host at all: it has no view-zone API."],"costs":["57.9 calendar weeks to the pilot","Table stakes paid on sgnk-md's 41 of 43 files"],"system":"No change.","screens":"All nine prototype screens survive.","money":"$99 Apple, $150-400 Windows.","what":"The standalone editor and nothing else, as the plan is written. Keeps Live and the free-editor story, and pays every table-stakes day on a shell that is 202 of 228 files sgnk-md's."},{"k":"b","label":"VS Code extension only","gains":["Meets the population already running the agents","The extension write path is already measured twice: 94 of 94 and 156 of 156 bytes exact."],"costs":["No view-zone API, so no Live mode","A niche of 1,217 installs, ceiling 400"],"system":"Engine ports; shell and Tauri abandoned.","screens":"Launcher, editor tabs and settings screen die.","money":"No certificates, no store fee.","what":"An extension only. Meets the people already running agents, inside the window they work in, with no Live mode and a niche whose best of thirteen has 400 installs."},{"k":"c","label":"One core, extension first, standalone shell second","gains":["A real population sees the highlight inside the test window","A stranger inside the extension sees the highlight while the standalone shell is still building."],"costs":["Two shells to maintain later","The extension is still unestimated"],"system":"Engine becomes a package: two hosts.","screens":"Editor and review panel ship twice, one spec.","money":"Unpriced.","what":"One engine, extension first, a standalone shell second. A real population sees it early; two shells to keep later, and the extension has never been estimated."},{"k":"d","label":"One core, standalone first, extension in the build after the pilot","gains":["No re-planning; every estimate stays valid","Every fix-first estimate in the plan is already costed against the standalone shell."],"costs":["Defers the population question 57.9 weeks","VS Code may document its July review feature first"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"One engine, the web editor first at /, the extension in the build after the pilot. Every estimate stays valid and the front door a stranger meets is the one we can judge in four weeks."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Q3 settles the web editor at / first. The extension is the one surface with no estimate and a niche whose best entry has 400 installs.","The core is already portable, so the extension is not foreclosed. It is sequenced after the pilot, where its cost can be measured against a real ask."],"flip":"If VS Code loses a byte when an agent writes while you type, the extension never ships. If the twenty all live inside VS Code, it moves up.","linked":["F5","FF11","FF12","FF3","FF4","FL3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:217","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:125","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141","docs/CRITIQUE.md:441","src/modules/mdmax","https://api.github.com/repos/microsoft/vscode/pulls/324218","docs/PRODUCT-SHAPE-2026-09-14-0219.md:1"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan proposes standalone web first. It is the shape of everything built in phase one.","recWhy":"The engine is the only thing here that is ours, 3,614 lines with no DOM and measured as portable, so one core behind more than one surface is right and was never in doubt. The order is what changed. The extension route looked like the fast way to a real population, and it still has no estimate, no Live mode, and a niche where the best of thirteen listings has 400 installs. Meanwhile the tools a developer reaches for to do one quick markdown job, dillinger and StackEdit and Excalidraw, show zero sign-in words and an editor on arrival, and our home page is a login screen. Phase 0 fixes that on the web in four weeks, on code that exists. Kiro from AWS is now fighting Cursor for the IDE window, which is one more reason not to open there first. The extension waits, unforeclosed, until someone in the twenty asks for it.","dupes":{"dropped":["FF16"],"areas":[],"why":"Both ask what gets built and on which surface. FF16's 'one portable core, both surfaces' is FF1's option c word for word, so the two were the same decision written twice.","agree":true}},{"id":"FF3","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"The fork","weight":"critical","q":"The shell is a fork of sgnk-md. Merge back into it, freeze it, hard-fork it, or delete it?","lede":"The plan lists the same fork once as a strength and once as a liability.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Splice engine, src/modules/mdmax","items":["3,614 lines","13 files, no DOM","1 symbol reaches product code"],"side":"ours","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Editor and app shell","items":["41 of 43 files byte-identical","0 unique to frontmatter"],"side":"forked","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"The rest of src/","items":["202 identical, 25 diverged","20 unique, 13 of them the engine"],"side":"forked","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"src-tauri desktop shell","items":["productName sgnk-md","identifier ai.sgnk.md"],"side":"sibling's","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Re-measured 2026-09-09 against the sibling repo.","caption":"What this product owns, by layer: 14.2% of 25,407 lines"},"stakes":"Every unbudgeted table-stakes day is work done twice, or a merge nobody scheduled.","state":["The plan's fix list calls the shell a fork of sgnk-md, not an asset.","Its closing status table lists the same fact as a strength.","Its whole-repo counts disagree with each other: 181 of 226 files, and 202 of 228."],"tension":["Merging needs sgnk-md's user count, which nobody has asked for yet.","Hard-forking commits 14.5-20.5 days that 1.21 engineering days a week cannot pay.","Deleting is right only if the form-factor decision picks VS Code."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Re-measured 2026-09-09 against the sibling repo at ~/Desktop/GitHub/md","cols":["Scope","Byte-identical","Diverged","Unique to frontmatter"],"rows":[["src/modules/editor + src/modules/app-shell (all files)","41","2","0"],["src/ (all files, including non-code)","202","26 (one is .DS_Store)","20"],["src/ (.ts, .tsx, .css only)","183","25","20"],["Of those 20 unique files","none","none","13 engine, 5 Firebase auth, 1 DI container, 1 splice-frontmatter"],["The plan says, at line 223","181 of 226","25","0 in editor + app-shell"],["The plan also says, at line 479","202 of 228","none","none"]],"note":"The 41-of-43 editor figure reproduces exactly. The whole-repo figure does not: line 223 and line 479 disagree with each other and both disagree with measurement. On like-for-like code files the divergence count is still 25, not 26 (the extra file is a macOS .DS_Store artifact), so nothing has actually drifted since the plan was written."},{"type":"stat","title":"What is actually owned","items":[["Total source, .ts and .tsx","25,407 lines","re-measured 2026-09-09; matches CRITIQUE.md:52"],["Engine","3,614 lines = 14.2%","re-measured, 13 files"],["Everything else","21,793 lines","CRITIQUE.md:52 calls it an Obsidian clone"],["Engine reaching product code","1 symbol, 2 import sites","decodeStrict from shape-gate.ts"],["src-tauri identity","productName sgnk-md, id ai.sgnk.md","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3, :5"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Merge back: one codebase, frontmatter as a mode of sgnk-md","gains":["Ends the divergence cost outright","25,407 total lines against 3,614 owned means the shell is 85.8 percent someone else's code either way."],"costs":["Needs a user count nobody has asked for","The two counts of shared files in the plan itself disagree, 181 of 226 versus 202 of 228."],"system":"One tree, one build, a feature flag.","screens":"The launcher becomes a mode switch.","money":"One set of certificates, not two.","what":"Merge frontmatter back into sgnk-md as a mode of it. Ends the divergence at source, and it needs to know how many people use sgnk-md, which nobody has asked."},{"k":"b","label":"Freeze the shell: new work goes to the engine and one surface","gains":["Needs no answer we do not already have","Costs nothing today and defers the choice until the form-factor decision is actually made."],"costs":["The shell stays at current quality","Eight table-stakes gaps stay open"],"system":"No upstream tracking. Engine work continues.","screens":"Existing screens stop improving.","money":"None.","what":"Freeze the shell where it is. New work goes into the engine and one surface; the shell stays at today's quality with eight table-stakes gaps open."},{"k":"c","label":"Hard-fork and own it: stop tracking the sibling, budget the table stakes","gains":["Honest about what maintaining an editor costs","Makes visible what maintaining an editor actually costs, rather than inheriting it silently."],"costs":["14.5-20.5 days before any novel feature","The measured rate cannot pay it"],"system":"The shell becomes first-class, with tests.","screens":"Table editing, paste-to-markdown, a keymap.","money":"Four months of the measured rate.","what":"Hard-fork and own it. Honest about what maintaining an editor costs, which is 14.5 to 20.5 days before any new feature, at a rate that cannot pay it."},{"k":"d","label":"Delete the shell; ship the engine inside someone else's shell","gains":["Keeps only the 14.2% that is ours","Keeps only the 3,614 lines and one symbol, decodeStrict, that reach product code today."],"costs":["Loses 21,793 lines and the free-editor story","Live mode goes too"],"system":"Engine becomes a package. Nothing to sign.","screens":"Launcher, editor and settings retired.","money":"No certificates, no hosting.","what":"Delete the shell and ship the engine inside someone else's editor. Keeps the 14.2 percent that is ours and loses 21,793 lines, the free-editor story and Live mode."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Freezing is the only option that needs no answer we do not have today.","Merging needs a user count, and hard-forking needs days the measured rate cannot pay."],"flip":"If sgnk-md has ten reachable users, merging is the best deal here.","linked":["FF1","FF8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:479","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:557","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:105","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141","docs/CRITIQUE.md:52","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:5","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v1-table-stakes.md:87"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The MVP keeps the sibling look, so whether we merge, freeze or own that shell decides week one.","dependsOn":["FF1"],"recWhy":"The plan calls the same fork a strength on one page and a liability on another, and both are true, which is why this is a decision rather than a fix. Merging is the best deal if sgnk-md has reachable users, because one codebase ends the divergence cost for good, and the honest state is that nobody has asked whether those users exist. That is question 3 of the facts only you know. Hard-forking is the honest cost of owning an editor, and 14.5 to 20.5 days is not payable at 1.21 days a week before anything new ships. Deleting is right only if the surface goes to VS Code, and Q3 just said web first. Freezing is the one answer that needs nothing we do not already have: the shell stays as it is, new work goes to the engine and the one surface, and the merge question waits for the user count.","dupes":{"dropped":["B11","PL11"],"areas":["Business & operations","Plan, scope & sequencing"],"why":"Business & operations recommended owning the fork outright, option c here: absorb the shell and keep sgnk-md parked. Plan, scope & sequencing recommended pinning the shell as a versioned internal package, closest to freezing it, option b. Whether the product is still called a markdown editor is a separate Product & definition question.","agree":false}},{"id":"FF8","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Web vs desktop","weight":"critical","q":"The desktop app in the plan does not exist. Build it, ship web-only, or make it paid?","lede":"Every desktop-only promise rests on a Tauri shell that holds no filesystem permission.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":157,"cols":[{"title":"Web app today","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Desktop shell today","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Desktop as promised","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Open a local file","cells":["Chrome and Edge, per folder","No fs permission at all","Any folder, full read and write"]},{"label":"MCP server","cells":["No","No","Yes, a local process"]},{"label":"Offline","cells":["Cached open files","Nothing; it loads a remote URL","Everything except the model"]},{"label":"Signing","cells":["Not applicable","signingIdentity null; dmg and app","Notarised dmg, signed exe, AppImage"]},{"label":"Identity","cells":["frontmatter","productName sgnk-md, id ai.sgnk.md","frontmatter"]},{"label":"Cost to close the gap","cells":["Built","Plan budgets 1 day","Measured at 4 to 8 weeks"]}],"note":"Right column is the plan's platform table. Middle column is the committed config, read 2026-09-09.","caption":"What the plan promises on desktop against what is committed"},"stakes":"Four to eight weeks of shell work buys the ten pilot strangers nothing.","state":["The plan describes a Tauri app holding the editor, engine, git, MCP server and search.","Its fix list budgets one day, to relabel the shell's identity.","The committed config loads md.sgnk.ai, sgnk-md's site, and declares no filesystem permission."],"tension":["The plan's what-works-where table promises desktop features from a shell that cannot open a file.","The File System Access API is Chrome and Edge only, refusing about one visitor in five.","An MCP server needs a local process, so putting it in the pilot makes desktop mandatory."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"src-tauri/tauri.conf.json and capabilities/default.json, read 2026-09-09","cols":["What the plan says","What the committed config says"],"rows":[["Contains the editor, engine, git client, MCP server, search index (line 104)","window url, devUrl and frontendDist all = https://md.sgnk.ai (the sibling product's website)"],["Full local read/write; offline everything except the model (the plan's platform table)","zero fs permissions. The set is: core, event, window, webview, shell, os, process, clipboard read/write, dialog"],[".dmg notarised, Windows .exe code-signed, Linux AppImage (line 104)","targets: [dmg, app]; signingIdentity: null; entitlements: null"],["Product identity re-labelled in 1 day (fix #9)","productName sgnk-md, identifier ai.sgnk.md, against a measured 4-8 weeks to go from remote wrapper to real (a research report, section 91.2, option B)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What each route costs and forecloses","items":[["File System Access API support","desktop Chrome and Edge only","none of Firefox, Safari, iOS, Android (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:102, caniuse 2026-09-08)"],["Users refused on a non-Chromium browser","about 1 in 5 desktop visitors","r19-p2 §91.3, explicitly tagged [derived], not measured"],["Desktop certificates","$99/yr Apple; $150-400/yr Windows plus a hardware token","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104, :231"],["Real Tauri build","4-8 weeks","r19-p2 §91.2"],["Measured engineering rate","1.21 days per calendar week","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227, independently reproduced"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build the real Tauri app now","gains":["Delivers MCP, local git, the file watcher, offline","The only route that lets an MCP server run at all, since that needs a local process."],"costs":["Four to eight weeks, two certificates, a per-OS matrix","Two certificates and a per-OS matrix is real, ongoing maintenance, not a one-time cost."],"system":"Scoped filesystem access, bundled frontend, local git.","screens":"No new screens; the desktop promises become true.","money":"$99/yr Apple, $150-400/yr Windows.","what":"Build the real Tauri app now: MCP, local git, the file watcher, offline. Four to eight weeks, two signing certificates, a per-OS build matrix, before the first stranger."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship web-only for the pilot; rewrite the plan's platform table to match what exists","gains":["The pilot runs on what is already built","Matches what is actually shippable today: the committed config has zero filesystem permissions."],"costs":["Loses MCP, local git, the file watcher, offline","Refuses about one desktop visitor in five"],"system":"No Tauri work; file access stays browser-only.","screens":"The what-works-where table loses its desktop column.","money":"None.","what":"Ship web only for the pilot and rewrite the platform table to match what exists. The pilot runs on code that is built; MCP, local git and the file watcher wait."},{"k":"c","label":"Web-only free; the desktop app becomes the paid tier","gains":["Gives the paid tier a concrete offer","Lets the pilot ship on the web now while pricing evidence accumulates for the paid build later."],"costs":["Prices an unbuilt product, and the weeks come before the first rupee","Nobody has paid for anything yet, so this prices a product with no revenue signal behind it."],"system":"Entitlement check in the desktop build.","screens":"An upgrade path in settings.","money":"A price on an unbuilt artefact.","what":"Web free, desktop as the paid tier. Gives the paid tier something concrete, and prices a product that does not exist yet, with the weeks arriving before the first rupee."},{"k":"d","label":"Desktop primary, web as a read-only viewer","gains":["Best offline and legal posture of any option researched","Desktop-first avoids the File System Access API gap that refuses roughly one visitor in five on the web."],"costs":["An install step before a 1.26M-visitor funnel","Every desktop cost arrives at once"],"system":"Two builds, sharply different capabilities.","screens":"The web app loses editing.","money":"Certificates and an update server first.","what":"Desktop first, web as a read-only viewer. The best offline and legal posture of any option, and an install step in front of the funnel, with every desktop cost at once."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The exit test is ten strangers in twelve weeks, and shell work buys them nothing.","In the plan, the web app already covers the eight pilot features on Chrome and Edge."],"flip":"If the MCP server moves into the pilot, desktop becomes mandatory, so set its tier alongside.","linked":["AC12","AC19","AC8","FF3","FF7","FF9"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:92","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:102","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:222","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3","src-tauri/capabilities/default.json","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md:11","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md:18","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md:67","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md:78"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan says web only. Saying so closes the desktop work the old plan still implies.","recWhy":"The shell in the repository loads md.sgnk.ai and declares no filesystem permission, so every desktop promise in the plan rests on an app that cannot open a file. A .dmg was built on 27 May and it exists; it is not the product the plan describes. Phase 0 is four weeks and twenty people, and shell work buys those people nothing they can judge. The web app already covers the pilot's features on Chrome and Edge, and the front-door research says the web is where a stranger meets us and where zero sign-in tools win. So web only for the pilot, honestly labelled, with the platform table corrected so nobody reads a promise the code cannot keep. Desktop comes back the moment there is a paying reason, and the one tree builds both. If the MCP server moves into the pilot, desktop becomes mandatory and this card reopens.","dupes":{"dropped":["FF14"],"areas":[],"why":"FF14 asks how many surfaces one person can carry and FF8 asks whether the desktop app is one of them. Answering FF14 with 'one surface for the pilot' names the web app and settles FF8 in the same breath.","agree":true}},{"id":"FF2","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"The rendering bet","weight":"high","q":"Do we chase rendering fidelity, meaning Live mode in the pilot and an Obsidian surface, or cut both?","lede":"The only demand signal with a vote behind it points at a feature VS Code cannot host.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"501 likes, 117 posts","note":"Obsidian's nested-construct render bug","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Fix needs live preview","note":"nested constructs inside list items","tone":"neutral","edge":"needs"},{"label":"Obsidian plugin is cut","note":"the Live Preview tree is depth-1","tone":"stop","edge":"so"},{"label":"Only route left is Live mode","note":"inside our own shell","tone":"warn","edge":"then"},{"label":"No view-zone API in VS Code","note":"Live is standalone-only","tone":"stop","edge":"but"},{"label":"Standalone shell has to go first","note":"57.9 weeks at 1.21 days a week","tone":"stop","edge":"so"},{"label":"Or cut Live and Obsidian","note":"one feature set on either surface","tone":"accent","edge":"or"}],"note":"Every step was measured. Only the last is a choice.","caption":"How one forum vote forces the most expensive surface"},"stakes":"Keeping Live forces the standalone shell first and rules out VS Code.","state":["The plan ships Live, Edit, Split and Read in the pilot.","VS Code has no view-zone API, so Live mode is standalone-only.","The Obsidian plugin is cut in the plan's channel table and still referenced six times."],"tension":["On Obsidian's forum, the only voting population, rendering beats review state (what you have read) 501 likes to 0.","A plugin cannot deliver that fix, and what it can deliver scored zero.","Obsidian itself shipped the neighbouring nested-in-list fix twice in 2026."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The vote, and the window","items":[["Obsidian forum, nested-construct rendering bug","501 likes, 117 posts, 18,331 views","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25"],["Obsidian forum, closest review-state request","0 likes, 251 views, 1 post in three years","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25, :410"],["Obsidian releases 2025-26 mentioning live preview","51 hits across 142 releases","CRITIQUE.md:362 (M13)"],["Obsidian shipped the neighbouring nested-in-list fix","twice in 2026","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304"],["View-zone API in VS Code","none: Live is standalone-only","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where the cut plugin still lives in the same document","cols":["Location","Line","What it still says"],"rows":[["Exhibit 17, the cut","304","CUT: the parse is unreachable, 115 installs against a 200 kill line"],["Exhibit 6, MVP-0 channels","204","\"Free Obsidian live-preview plugin, week 2; kill: under 200 installs in 14 days\""],["§14 ninety-day flowchart, the no-go branch","338","\"Stop: free plugin, services, engine\""],["Exhibit 24, retention lever","428","\"The plugin ... stays installed in a vault the user already opens daily\""],["§19 family diagram","464","\"Chrome extension · Obsidian plugin\""],["Exhibit 26, family members","477","\"Free; channels — MVP-1, week 2\""],["§21 rendering rules","524","the 501-like fix \"shipped first as the free plugin\""]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What that population votes for","items":[["Nested-construct rendering bug","501 likes","the thing a plugin cannot fix (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25)"],["Closest review-state request","0 likes, 1 post in three years","the thing a plugin could ship (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25)"],["Closest measured analogue, first 14 days","115 installs","against our own 200-install kill line (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)"],["Extensions and plugins already sitting in this niche","13 VS Code, 7 Obsidian, none above 1,540 downloads","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep Live in the pilot and ship the additive Obsidian review-state plugin","gains":["Keeps the only feature with a 501-vote signal","Obsidian shipped the neighbouring nested-list fix twice in 2026, so the demand is durable, not a fluke."],"costs":["Forces the standalone shell first","Ships that population what it scored 0 on"],"system":"Live-preview renderer plus a decoration layer.","screens":"Editor keeps four mode tabs; adds a settings pane.","money":"None. Both surfaces are free.","what":"Keep Live in the pilot and ship the additive Obsidian review plugin. Keeps the one feature with a 501-vote signal, forces the standalone shell, and ships that population the thing it scored zero."},{"k":"b","label":"Cut Live from the pilot, cut Obsidian, delete the six stale references","gains":["One feature set on both surfaces","Removes six stale references to a channel already cut from the plan's own table."],"costs":["Drops the only feature with measured demand","501 likes on one Obsidian thread is the single largest demand signal found anywhere in the corpus."],"system":"No live-preview renderer, no CodeMirror build.","screens":"Editor loses the Live tab.","money":"None.","what":"Cut Live and cut Obsidian, and delete the six stale references. One feature set on both surfaces, and the only feature with measured demand goes with it."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep Live in the standalone shell only; no Obsidian surface","gains":["Leaves the form-factor choice open","VS Code's missing view-zone API already rules Live out there, so nothing is lost on that surface."],"costs":["Two feature sets to document and support","Still forces the shell if rendering wins"],"system":"One engine, two renderers, one review file.","screens":"Editor differs by surface, unlike the prototype.","money":"None.","what":"Keep Live in the standalone web shell and ship no Obsidian surface. Two feature sets if an extension ever comes; the form-factor choice stays open."},{"k":"d","label":"Hold until the five-second screenshot test in weeks 1-2","gains":["Cheap, and already scheduled for weeks 1-2","The five-second test is already scheduled and costs nothing extra to also answer this."],"costs":["Blocks the form factor two weeks","It measures the highlight, not Live"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Wait for the five-second screenshot test. Cheap, already scheduled, and it measures the highlight rather than Live, so it answers a different question."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["D8 in the MVP set keeps Live and cuts Split, and the MVP decision governs. Live stays in the standalone web shell.","Obsidian and the six stale references still go; the parse was verified unreachable twice."],"flip":"If Live's cost blocks Phase 0's four weeks, cut it there and say so; the Obsidian cut does not move either way.","linked":["D8","FF1","G2"],"sources":["merged: FF6","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:25","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:338","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:464","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127","docs/CRITIQUE.md:362"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Rendering fidelity and an Obsidian surface are both outside the first build.","recWhy":"Live mode and the Obsidian surface were bundled into one question because both are about rendering fidelity, and they need separating. D8 in the MVP set keeps Live and cuts Split, and an MVP decision governs a card staged after the pilot, so Live stays in the web shell. It is also the one feature with a counted vote behind it, 501 likes against 0 for our old headline, on the only forum that votes. Obsidian is different. The parse the plugin would need was verified unreachable twice, and what a plugin could deliver scored zero with that audience, so the plugin stays cut and its six stale references go with it. That leaves c: Live in the shell we ship, nothing in a shell we do not. If Live's cost threatens Phase 0's four weeks, cut it there and say so on the card."},{"id":"FF4","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Gates before the choice","weight":"high","q":"Which measurements must land before the surface is chosen, and with what pre-registered kill lines?","lede":"Neither surface has been tested with an agent writing while you type.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":236,"cols":[{"title":"Standalone shell","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"VS Code extension","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Quiet write, nobody typing","cells":["0 throws across 1,354 blocks that proceeded","94/94 and 156/156 bytes, two harnesses"]},{"label":"Agent writes while a human types","cells":["unmeasured","unmeasured, named as a data-loss path"]},{"label":"Anchor after an out-of-band write","cells":["unmeasured","unmeasured"]},{"label":"Host save on a dirty buffer","cells":["not applicable","rewrites the file"]},{"label":"Refusal rate on Obsidian vaults","cells":["83.01% of 7,969 blocks","same engine, same number"]},{"label":"Refusal rate on repo markdown","cells":["unmeasured","unmeasured"]}],"note":"Cells read 2026-09-09 from the plan and the round-2 harnesses.","caption":"Six scenarios, two surfaces, five cells nobody has filled"},"stakes":"Choosing before either test runs means finding out after the build.","state":["The plan records an untested data-loss path when the extension bypasses VS Code's save.","Still open: an agent writing mid-typing, and whether review marks survive an outside write.","The test corpus is seven Obsidian vaults; one wiki is 77% of 8,513 files."],"tension":["Most of the 83.01% refusal, 6,613 blocks, is one Obsidian habit: lists starting at the left edge.","If the buyer is a repo developer, the four-day fix for that refusal repairs nothing.","Each test delays the form-factor decision; three delays are already queued."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What has been tested, per surface","cols":["Scenario","Standalone shell","VS Code extension","Status"],"rows":[["Quiet write, no human in the buffer","0 throws, 0 corruptions across the 1,354 frontmatter blocks that proceeded of 7,969 (ENGINE.md:146)","94/94 and 156/156 bytes","measured"],["Agent writes while a human is typing","unmeasured","unmeasured: named as a data-loss path","GAPS:146"],["Review anchor survives an out-of-band write","unmeasured","unmeasured","GAPS:146"],["Host save pipeline on a dirty buffer","not applicable","rewrites the file","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Corpus composition, test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json, read 2026-09-09","note":"Seven Obsidian vaults, 8,513 markdown files, pinned 2026-08-29. Zero repo-shaped sources: no spec-kit specs, no docs/ folders, no agent-written specs. File counts reproduce exactly against the manifest.","unit":"markdown files","data":[["community-archive/obsidian-hub",6586],["oldwinter/knowledge-garden",961],["quanru/obsidian-example-lifeos",540],["s-blu/obsidian_dataview_example_vault",264],["kepano/kepano-obsidian",103],["erazlogo/obsidian-history-vault",40],["CyanVoxel/Obsidian-Vault-Template",19]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What that corpus produced","items":[["Splice writer refusals","6,615 of 7,969 frontmatter blocks = 83.01%","ENGINE.md:146"],["Of those, one cause","6,613, a `-` item at column zero","ENGINE.md:146, :51"],["the left-edge list bug budget, first item in the fix list","4 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214"],["Unaddressable top-level keys (the spaced-key bug)","2,447 of 29,288 across 937 of 7,969 files","ENGINE.md:213 (includes CJK keys from the same vaults)"],["Refusal rate on repo-shaped markdown","unmeasured","no repo source is pinned"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Decide the form factor at the meeting; run neither test","gains":["Unblocks every downstream decision today","Removes the two-test delay entirely, at the cost of deciding on what is measured today."],"costs":["Chooses without testing agent writes during typing","The four-day refusal fix may be misaimed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Decide the surface at the meeting and run neither test. Unblocks everything today, and nobody has tested an agent writing while a person types, which is the product's whole claim."},{"k":"b","label":"Run the contention test only, with a one-byte kill written first","gains":["Covers the only scenario the product exists for","The one-byte kill line means this test can fail fast rather than run indefinitely."],"costs":["Leaves the headline refusal number measured on vaults","Leaves the 83.01 percent refusal figure resting on seven Obsidian vaults, not a repo-shaped corpus."],"system":"A harness where an agent writes mid-typing, on both surfaces.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Run the contention test only, with a one-byte kill line written first. Covers the scenario the product exists for and leaves the refusal number measured on Obsidian vaults."},{"k":"c","label":"Run both: contention test and a repo-shaped corpus, kills written first","gains":["Answers if the surface is safe and the refusal fix aimed right","Answers both open questions the corpus itself names as unmeasured before committing to a surface."],"costs":["Delays the form-factor decision by two tests","Pinning a corpus is real work"],"system":"That harness plus a pinned repo-markdown corpus.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Run both: the contention test and a repo-shaped corpus, kill lines written first. Answers whether the surface is safe and whether the four-day refusal fix is aimed at real users."},{"k":"d","label":"Run both, but decide the surface anyway; results feed build order","gains":["Keeps momentum and still produces the evidence","Keeps the build moving instead of stalling on a decision the tests will inform either way."],"costs":["The wrong surface shows after the build","A late result gets re-argued"],"system":"Same two harnesses, no gate.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Run both but decide the surface anyway, and let the results set build order. Keeps momentum, and a late bad result gets re-argued after the build has started."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The whole claim, bytes surviving an agent writing while a person reads, is untested.","One repo-markdown run shows whether the left-edge list fix, four days and first, matters."],"flip":"If both tests together cost over a week, run the contention test alone.","linked":["FF1","FF11"],"sources":["merged: FF10","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:125","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/ENGINE.md:51","docs/ENGINE.md:146","docs/ENGINE.md:213","test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The surface is chosen by the plan, so the measurements that would choose it are moot.","recWhy":"The surface is now settled by Q3, which makes the first clause of this question moot and leaves the part that matters. Nothing has ever tested bytes surviving an agent writing while a person reads, and that is the entire claim. The contention test with a one-byte kill line written before it runs is the cheapest way to find out. The repo-shaped corpus is the second half: 83 percent of today's refusals are one Obsidian habit, lists at the left edge, and if the buyer is a developer with a repository that four-day fix may repair nothing they have. Both tests are days, not weeks, and both belong at the start of Phase 1 before the instruction-file view is built on top of the engine. If together they cost more than a week, run the contention test alone."},{"id":"FF12","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Package and CLI","weight":"high","q":"Do we add a fourth form-factor option, the engine as npm package, MCP server and plugin, and run it first?","lede":"The repo's own critique recommends a form factor the plan's decision does not list, at about a week.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Splice engine, 3,614 lines","note":"14.2% of source, the only thing owned","tone":"accent"},{"label":"npm package","note":"mdmax is free; frontmatter is not","tone":"accent","edge":"ship as"},{"label":"MCP server","note":"agents write through the engine","tone":"accent","edge":"and"},{"label":"Agent plugin","note":"runs inside Claude Code or Codex","tone":"accent","edge":"and"},{"label":"Thirty days of installs","note":"about one week of work to get here","tone":"neutral","edge":"then"},{"label":"Under 100 installs, stop","note":"kill number written before the run","tone":"stop","edge":"if"}],"note":"The critique's own recommendation, absent from the plan's form-factor decision.","caption":"One week and one number, before 58 weeks go into a shell"},"stakes":"Committing 58 weeks to a shell without running the one-week test that predicts whether it is wanted.","state":["The plan's form-factor decision offers exactly three options and no package route.","`mdmax cert` exits 1 today and is not among the repo's 26 npm scripts.","The critique recommends the package route outright, at a week."],"tension":["The research record's strongest recommendation is missing from the decision put to the founders.","dealfluence/adeu already ships the same refuse-when-ambiguous engine for .docx, MIT, 149 stars.","Giving the engine away under MIT forecloses selling it as a library."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The unlisted fourth option","items":[["Engine share of the codebase","3,614 of 25,407 lines = 14.2%","re-measured 2026-09-09"],["Cost of the package + MCP + plugin route","about one week, against a 240-day plan","CRITIQUE.md:163"],["Pre-registered kill number","fewer than 100 installs in 30 days","CRITIQUE.md:163"],["Information yield, per the critique","\"higher than rounds 21-25 combined\"","CRITIQUE.md:163"],["gray-matter, the substrate","about 35.8M npm downloads a month","RECORD:9297, read 2026-08-30"],["npm name `mdmax`","free (HTTP 404)","RECORD:1403"],["Competitor already shipping this shape","dealfluence/adeu, MIT, 149 stars, pushed 2026-08-30","CRITIQUE.md:33, :1700 (for .docx)"],["`mdmax cert` today","exits 1, ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND; not among the 26 scripts","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224, re-verified"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Add it as a fourth option and run it first, with the 100-install kill","gains":["One week buys an installs number before 58 weeks","A free MIT competitor, dealfluence/adeu, already ships this shape for .docx, so the window is closing."],"costs":["Gives the engine away, foreclosing a sale","A free comparator holds that attention"],"system":"Engine extracted, CLI fixed, MCP server built.","screens":"None. This surface has no screens.","money":"None in, a week out.","what":"Add the engine as an npm package, MCP server and plugin as a fourth surface and run it first, with a 100-install kill line. One week for a number, and it gives the engine away."},{"k":"b","label":"Fix the CLI only, one day, and keep it internal","gains":["Makes `mdmax cert` actually run, closing that fix","The fix is one day and finally makes `mdmax cert` runnable, which today exits 1 on every call."],"costs":["No external signal and no installs","Leaves the fourth option unmodelled"],"system":"Import extensions and the `@/` alias fixed; `cert` script added.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fix the CLI only, one day, and keep the engine internal. mdmax cert finally runs, which closes a fix; no external signal and no installs."},{"k":"c","label":"Leave the package surface unmodelled, as the plan has it","gains":["Three choices, no licence decision, nothing given away","Commits to nothing and gives away nothing while the licence question stays open."],"costs":["Leaves the cheapest test unrun","The strongest recommendation stays untested"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave the package route unmodelled, as the plan has it. Three choices, no licence decision, nothing given away, and the cheapest test stays unrun."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship the package after the pilot exits","gains":["No new work before the pilot","Keeps founder attention on the 240-day plan rather than a one-week detour before any pilot user exists."],"costs":["If the pilot fails, the one-week test never ran","An MIT competitor is already moving"],"system":"Deferred.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the package after the pilot exits, kill line intact. No new work before the pilot; if the pilot fails the one-week test never ran, and an MIT competitor is already moving."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Q3 settles one surface first, the web editor at /. An npm probe run first is a second surface before the first has a user.","The probe is still cheap and its 100-install kill line travels with it; it runs after the pilot exits."],"flip":"If a maintainer asks for the engine as a package during the pilot, the probe moves up and the kill line stays.","linked":["E24","F26","FF1","FF11","FF5","G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:462","docs/CRITIQUE.md:33","docs/CRITIQUE.md:163","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1700","docs/ENGINE.md:899","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1403","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9297","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9439","https://api.github.com/repos/petergyang/human-review","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:516"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The plan puts the engine package after the pilot.","recWhy":"The critique's strongest recommendation, the engine as a package with a kill line, was missing from the decision put to the founders, and this card put it back. The recheck against Q3 changed only its timing. One surface first means the web editor gets a user before anything else does, and an npm probe run first is a second surface however small. The probe is still cheap, its 100-install kill line still travels with it, and a maintainer asking for the engine during the pilot would move it up. What it does not change is the licence question underneath: publishing forecloses selling the engine as a library, and dealfluence/adeu already ships a refuse-when-ambiguous engine for .docx under MIT with 149 stars, so the field is not empty. Decide the licence on E24 before the package ships, not after.","dupes":{"dropped":["E31","F14","PL14","MK11"],"areas":["Engine & technical","Features","Plan, scope & sequencing","Market & competition"],"why":"Engine & technical, Features, Plan, scope & sequencing and Market & competition all recommended shipping the package early as a probe, option a here. Engine & technical would first fix the left-edge list bug and the spaced-key bug; Market & competition would make the release the launch. Licence and package shape are decided separately.","agree":true}},{"id":"FF5","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Name and store","weight":"high","q":"Does the marketplace name collision veto the extension, or does the extension ship under the engine's name?","lede":"If form factor resolves toward VS Code, the name is already owned in that store.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Keep frontmatter","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"mdmax in the store","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Rename the product","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"VS Code store name","cells":["Second to an 82,265-install incumbent","Free","Free"]},{"label":"npm name","cells":["Taken; @frontmatter is theirs","mdmax is free, HTTP 404","Depends on the new name"]},{"label":"Cost in conversation","cells":["\"Not the VS Code one\", forever","Two names to explain","One name, once"]},{"label":"What it unblocks","cells":["Nothing","The engine's npm package","Every surface, later"]},{"label":"What it costs","cells":["A permanent qualifier","A two-name story","Every internal reference"]}],"note":"Store and npm states read from the record, 2026-08-29 to 2026-09-07.","caption":"Three name strategies against the store, npm and the conversation"},"stakes":"Under a taken name, we rank second to the incumbent in every search.","state":["The plan lists the collision in its key figures and its form-factor decision, still unresolved.","The incumbent, Front Matter CMS, is bigger than recorded: 82,265 installs, alive at v10.12.0.","On npm, `mdmax` is free (HTTP 404) and the `@frontmatter` scope is the incumbent's."],"tension":["The collision also hits npm, which constrains the package route.","Renaming now is one decision; renaming later means changing every internal reference.","An EU word mark was reported but not reproduced; five registries blocked the check."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The incumbent, and what names are left","items":[["Front Matter CMS, VS Code installs","82,265","GAPS:132, alive at v10.12.0 on 2026-08-21"],["Same, read nine days earlier","80,605","RECORD:4799, read 2026-08-29"],["npm `frontmatter`","taken (HTTP 200): a dead 2022 0.0.3 YAML parser","RECORD:1403, :4827"],["npm `@frontmatter` scope","the incumbent's","GAPS:132"],["npm `mdmax`","free (HTTP 404)","RECORD:1403"],["npm `frontmatter-cert`","free (HTTP 404)","RECORD:1403"],["\"Frontmatter Studio\"","taken 2026-09-07","GAPS:132"],["gray-matter, the generic-name substrate","about 35.8M downloads a month (derived: 8,352,860 weekly x 4.28)","RECORD:9297, read 2026-08-30; CRITIQUE.md:1728 gives 35,124,859 for 2026-08-31 (same order, different day)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Rename the whole product before any store listing","gains":["Removes the name problem on every surface at once","Ends the two-name story cleanly, since the collision hits the extension, the app and npm alike."],"costs":["Discards what the name has built","Every document and repo reference changes"],"system":"Package, repo and bundle identifier change.","screens":"Every title bar and the wordmark.","money":"A domain, and five registries re-checked.","what":"Rename the whole product before any store listing. Removes the collision on every surface at once, and every document, repository and reference changes with it."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the extension as mdmax; keep frontmatter for the app","gains":["The one name free on npm and in the store","mdmax is free on npm (HTTP 404) and unclaimed in either extension marketplace today."],"costs":["A two-name story to explain","Search splits between two words"],"system":"Published as mdmax; the app keeps its identifier.","screens":"A different wordmark on the store page.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the extension under the engine's name, mdmax, and keep frontmatter for the app. The one name free on npm and in the store; a two-name story to tell."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the name everywhere and pay the tax","gains":["One brand everywhere, and no rework","No rework and no new name to teach anyone who already knows the product."],"costs":["A permanent qualifier against the incumbent","Seven years of search history is theirs"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Paid in acquisition, not cash.","what":"Keep frontmatter everywhere and pay the tax: a permanent qualifier against Front Matter CMS, 82,265 installs and seven years of search history that are theirs."},{"k":"d","label":"Stay out of the marketplace; the collision becomes a search problem","gains":["Sidesteps the store collision entirely","Removes the store collision without touching the product name anywhere else."],"costs":["Rules out the extension option being weighed","Leaves the npm collision for the package decision"],"system":"No extension build.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Stay out of the marketplace entirely. Sidesteps the store collision, rules out the extension being weighed, and leaves the npm collision for the package decision."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The engine is already called mdmax, and mdmax is free where frontmatter is not.","It frees the npm-package route, which needs a free name and has only this one."],"flip":"If the founders want one brand on every surface, this becomes a rename.","linked":["FF1","FF11","FF12"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:13","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:50","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:1403","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4799","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4827","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5632","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9297","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"No extension ships before the pilot, so the store name cannot veto anything yet.","recWhy":"This only fires if an extension ships, which Q3 puts after the pilot, so it is not now. When it does fire the facts are fixed: Front Matter CMS is bigger than the record thought, 82,265 installs and alive, and it owns the @frontmatter scope on npm as well as the store listing. mdmax is free in both places and it is already the engine's name. Shipping the extension as mdmax costs a two-name story, which Q16 may resolve anyway if the product name is coined. Renaming the whole product to dodge one store is the tail wagging the dog, and paying the qualifier tax forever is worse than either. The one thing this card should not do is decide the product's name; that is Q16's, and this waits for it.","dupes":{"dropped":["G21"],"areas":["Go-to-market & channels"],"why":"Go-to-market & channels recommended renaming the whole product first and then shipping the extension under the new name, option a here, not shipping it as mdmax. The naming itself is decided in Name & identity.","agree":false}},{"id":"FF11","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Extension scope","weight":"high","q":"If an extension ships, what is inside it, where is it published, and does it carry its own key?","lede":"The plan names an extension without saying what is in it, and scope moves cost tenfold.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"The extension inside VS Code","panes":[{"label":"Host supplies","w":32,"rows":[{"label":"Explorer, palette, keymap, outline","meta":"free","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Model picker and MCP registration","meta":"unverified on forks","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Multi-file diff, review and unreview","meta":"merged 2026-07-03","tone":"warn"}]},{"label":"Thin version adds","w":38,"rows":[{"label":"Span highlights from a separate file","meta":"our claim","tone":"accent"},{"label":"One-key revert","meta":"byte range","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Review panel, state kept in the repo","meta":"the differentiator","tone":"accent"}]},{"label":"Only if thick","w":30,"rows":[{"label":"Idea mode and decision flow","meta":"","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Repo docs scan","meta":"","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Table editing","meta":"5-8 d, host gives none","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"The host now ships a per-file reviewed marker of its own, gated and undocumented.","caption":"What the host gives, what thin adds, what only thick adds"},"stakes":"A thick extension is a second product with no estimate, at 1.21 days a week.","state":["The plan's form-factor decision names a VS Code extension with no scope and no estimate.","VS Code's model picker would save three days of bring-your-own-key work.","Whether Cursor and other VS Code forks honour the model APIs is still open."],"tension":["A thin extension lands in a niche where the best of 13 has 400 installs.","VS Code now ships a per-file reviewed marker, behind a setting, undocumented.","Not shipped: review state, who read which span, stored in the repo across machines."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"IDE usage, Stack Overflow 2025 (n=49k+, CRITIQUE.md:363 and :431)","cols":["Editor","Usage","Store it pulls from"],"rows":[["VS Code","75.9%","Microsoft Marketplace"],["Cursor","17.9%","not established in our record"],["Sublime Text","10.5%","not a VS Code host"],["Claude Code","9.7%","runs inside a host"],["Zed","7.3%","not a VS Code host"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What rides on the unverified claim","items":[["Extensions in the Microsoft Marketplace","135,646","RECORD:9176, read 2026-08-30"],["BYO key, fix #6","3 engineering days (\"deleted\" by the host model picker)","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:219, :364"],["Model-picker support on Cursor and other forks","unmeasured","GAPS:146"],["Which registry the forks serve extensions from","not established in the corpus","only open-vsx.org as a probed host, COMPLETE-RECORD:20787"],["Consequence if absent","no AI on about a quarter of the host population, or fix #6 comes back","derived from the two rows above"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Table-stakes gaps in the standalone shell, and whether the host supplies them (a research report, eight ranked gaps)","cols":["Gap","Cost","Host supplies it?"],"rows":[["Heading hotkeys, link, list/quote/task toggles, move line","0.5 d","Yes (markdown-all-in-one, 14.45M installs)"],["Outline hotkey and fuzzy heading jump","0.5 d","Yes"],["Command palette that indexes everything","1-2 d","Yes"],["Quick-switcher deltas","0.5 d","Yes"],["HTML paste to markdown","2-3 d","Partly"],["Table editing: Tab nav, add/remove row and column, alignment","5-8 d","No"],["Splice-backed table writes","+2 d","No"],["Customisable keymap","3-4 d","Yes"]],"note":"Sums to 14.5-20.5 days; mid-point 17.5. The five host-supplied rows are about 6.5 of those days at their mid-points."},{"type":"stat","title":"The niche the thin version enters","items":[["All 13 markdown-review extensions combined","1,217 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9, :24"],["Ceiling of the best one","400 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["mddiff, our sentence shipped 2026-06-03","74 installs in three months","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["The closest comparable tool, SpecKit Companion","9,367 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:152"],["git-ai, commit-level attribution","61,517 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:151"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Thin, on the host model picker, Microsoft Marketplace only","gains":["Cheapest test of the one surviving claim","Uses the host's own model picker, which is exactly the three days fix #6 would otherwise cost."],"costs":["No AI on forks that ignore the model API","Cursor, at 17.9 percent of the IDE population measured, is not confirmed to honour that API."],"system":"No key UI, no keychain, no registry.","screens":"The settings key field never ships.","money":"Saves 3 days if the assumption holds.","what":"A thin extension on the host's own model picker, in the Microsoft store only. The cheapest test of the one surviving claim, and no AI on any fork that ignores the host API."},{"k":"b","label":"Thin first, own key, both stores, widening line written before publishing","gains":["AI works on every host, using key work already needed","AI works on every host by construction, since the key is not borrowed from any one vendor's picker."],"costs":["Spends the three days the model picker saved","A second registry, unconfirmed"],"system":"Bring-your-own key and keychain, shared with the shell.","screens":"The settings key field ships on both.","money":"3 engineering days, never wasted.","what":"Thin first, with its own key, in both stores, and the line at which it widens written before publishing. AI works on every host, using key work the shell needs anyway."},{"k":"c","label":"Thick: the first eight features, editor through file-scoped AI, inside the host","gains":["Competes above the niche, where git-ai sits at 61,517","Aims at a larger population: git-ai alone, at 61,517 installs, is fifty times the whole niche's total."],"costs":["A second product, unestimated","The host supplies none of the table days"],"system":"Every feature ported to the host's APIs.","screens":"All nine screens rebuilt in the host idiom.","money":"Unpriced.","what":"Thick: the first eight features inside the host, editor through file-scoped AI. Competes above the niche, and it is a second product with no estimate."},{"k":"d","label":"No extension","gains":["One surface, no store, no host API, no collision","No store, no marketplace review, and no dependency on VS Code or any fork's own API choices."],"costs":["Removes the only measured route to the population","Removes the only measured route into a population as large as 135,646 marketplace extensions."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"No extension. One surface, no store, no host API, no name collision, and the only measured route to the population running agents is gone."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The saving protected is three days; the risk is AI failing silently on a quarter of hosts.","The key work is what the shell needs anyway, so it is never wasted."],"flip":"If Cursor and other forks prove to honour the model API, drop the own-key fallback.","linked":["FF1","FF12","FF5"],"sources":["merged: FF13","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:151","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:152","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:219","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:125","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/CRITIQUE.md:363","docs/CRITIQUE.md:431","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9176","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:20787","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v1-table-stakes.md:87","https://api.github.com/repos/microsoft/vscode/pulls/324218"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"Nothing is inside an extension that is not being built.","recWhy":"Not now, because Q3 puts any extension after the pilot. When it comes, the shape is mostly forced by one fact: whether Cursor and the other VS Code forks honour the model API is still an open question, and the saving from relying on it is three days. A thin extension that carries its own key spends those three days and works on every host, and the key work is what the web shell needs anyway, so nothing is wasted. Thick is a second product with no estimate. None is a surface count kept at one, and it gives up the only measured route to the people already running agents. The widening line has to be written before the first publish, because scope moves after a store listing cost tenfold."},{"id":"FF9","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Mobile","weight":"medium","q":"Is mobile a stated permanent non-goal, a read-only reviewer in the build after the pilot, or left undecided?","lede":"The plan cuts mobile from every tier while its critique calls it a basic expectation.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"A read-only mobile reviewer, if it ships","panes":[{"label":"Unreviewed","w":38,"rows":[{"label":"specs/auth.md, 4 spans","meta":"agent, 2h ago","mark":"4","tone":"warn"},{"label":"docs/AGENTS.md, 1 span","meta":"agent, today","mark":"1","tone":"warn"},{"label":"README.md","meta":"clear","mark":"","tone":"good"}]},{"label":"Span","w":62,"rows":[{"label":"Highlighted paragraph, rendered read-only","meta":"hash matched","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Mark read","meta":"one tap","tone":"good"},{"label":"Revert","meta":"not offered on mobile","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Attribution: prompt not recorded","meta":"from the commit trailer","tone":"neutral"}]}],"note":"Reading and marking only. No editing, no revert, no file tree.","caption":"What a mobile reviewer would contain, and what it refuses"},"stakes":"If review state, who has read what, is the product, the reviewer is the one away from a laptop.","state":["The plan's operating rules say mobile is about 15% of complaints, not in v1, later a reviewer.","Its tier table marks mobile absent from the pilot and the build after it.","Its what-works-where table gives mobile repo connect only, so the three disagree."],"tension":["The critique classes it Kano Must-be and the top churn-risk gap.","Surface count is the binding constraint, and this adds a surface.","The counter-evidence, the 1/32 and 7:1 ratios, is inference, not measurement."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Two sources pulling opposite ways","items":[["Kano class","MUST-BE","CRITIQUE.md:203"],["Ranked churn risk","#1 gap, cut from every stage","CRITIQUE.md:680; RECORD:10836"],["The record's own verdict on sequencing","\"an MVP-2 item that will feel like an MVP-1 item\"","RECORD:10836"],["Tiers mobile appears in","0 of 3","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205"],["Markdown-native mobile vs the OS notes app","about 1/32 the size (tagged [inference] in the record, not measured)","RECORD:2067"],["Capture-shaped vs authoring-shaped markdown apps","about 7:1 (same [inference] tag)","RECORD:2067"],["Named mobile complaint in that thread","\"Make Obsidian Sync work in background (on Mobile)\", 124 posts","RECORD:2067"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"State mobile as a permanent non-goal, on the page","gains":["Cheap, honest, and the plan then says one thing","Matches the operating rules' own figure: mobile complaints are about 15 percent, not a majority."],"costs":["Goes without a Must-be the whole category has","Kano classes this a Must-be, the category people expect regardless of what else ships."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Mobile leaves the what-works-where table.","money":"None.","what":"Say on the page that mobile is a permanent non-goal. Cheap and honest, and it means going without something the whole category treats as expected."},{"k":"b","label":"Non-goal for the pilot; a read-only mobile reviewer in the build after the pilot","what":"Shows unreviewed spans and lets a person mark them read. No editing.","gains":["Matches the thesis: the reviewer is mobile","A read-only reviewer is the cheapest version of the thesis that the reviewer belongs on mobile."],"costs":["Adds a surface when count already binds","Marking read on a phone is weak review"],"system":"Responsive layout plus a mobile write path.","screens":"One narrow screen; the review panel becomes a list.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Leave it undecided","what":"Keep the plan's three conflicting statements as they are.","gains":["Nothing committed, nothing foreclosed","Nothing is spent and nothing is promised while the surface count question is still open elsewhere."],"costs":["The plan keeps saying three things","A Must-be gap stays unowned"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Mobile-first review, desktop-first editing","gains":["The strongest expression of the review-state thesis","The strongest possible version of the review-state thesis: reviewed anywhere, written with intent."],"costs":["Doubles surface count before anyone is asked","Two design systems on 1.21 days a week"],"system":"Two layouts and two interaction models.","screens":"A full mobile set beside the desktop nine.","money":"None.","what":"Review on mobile first, edit on desktop first. The strongest form of the review thesis, and it doubles the surface count before anyone has been asked."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Reviewing is the one job here that genuinely happens away from a laptop.","The web app already renders on mobile, so this is scope, not a build."],"flip":"If the twenty team conversations say reviewers work at their desks, (a) wins.","linked":["FF8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:92","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:539","docs/CRITIQUE.md:203","docs/CRITIQUE.md:680","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:2067","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:10836"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"Mobile is outside the first build in the plan.","recWhy":"Three documents say three different things about mobile, and surface count is the constraint that binds everything else here, so the answer has to add no surface now and not foreclose one later. Reviewing is the one job in this product that genuinely happens away from a desk: reading what an agent changed in an instruction file is a phone-shaped task in a way editing never is. The web app already renders on a phone, which makes a read-only reviewer after the pilot a scope decision rather than a build. Stating mobile as a permanent non-goal saves a sentence and goes without something the critique classes as expected. Building mobile-first review doubles the surfaces on 1.21 days a week. Not now, then a reader, is the line that keeps the plan saying one thing.","dupes":{"dropped":["F28"],"areas":["Features"],"why":"Features recommended a cheaper route no option here names: make the web app responsive enough to read and mark reviewed on a phone, rather than a separate mobile reviewer with its own budget and date.","agree":false}},{"id":"FF7","cat":"Form factor & surfaces","sub":"Browser","weight":"medium","q":"Does the `?src=` URL parameter ship in the pilot, and do the three stale Chrome-extension references get deleted?","lede":"The cheapest surface costs hours and appears only in the row that cuts the Chrome extension.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"A public .md URL","note":"raw.githubusercontent.com","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"access-control-allow-origin: *","note":"no extension needed to read it","tone":"good","edge":"sends"},{"label":"?src=<raw url>","note":"one parameter on the web app","tone":"accent","edge":"so"},{"label":"Rendered, with review state","note":"the reader installs nothing","tone":"accent","edge":"gives"},{"label":"Chrome extension","note":"store listing, Manifest V3, already cut","tone":"stop","edge":"instead of"}],"note":"The CORS header is what killed the extension.","caption":"The cut surface, and the parameter that replaces it in hours"},"stakes":"The one distribution surface that costs only hours is unscheduled, and nobody chose that.","state":["The plan's channel table cuts the Chrome extension and names `?src=` as its replacement.","The tier table, the product-family diagram and the family table still list the extension.","`?src=` appears once in the plan, in no tier and no sequence."],"tension":["Three places still promise a surface that one place cut.","The replacement is measured in hours and has no owner and no tier.","If desktop goes first, `?src=` becomes a viewer feature, not a channel."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The cut, and what replaces it","items":[["Claimed browser reader population","1.5M","refuted: two products summed, one a clipper"],["Real reading population","about 647,000","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"],["Market leader's last store build","over two years ago","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"],["raw.githubusercontent.com CORS header","access-control-allow-origin: *","no extension needed (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["Cost of the `?src=` replacement","hours","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"],["Times `?src=` appears in the plan","1 (line 310 only)","verified 2026-09-09"],["Places the cut extension still appears","3 (lines 203, 464, 477)","verified 2026-09-09"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship `?src=` in the pilot and delete the Chrome extension from all three places","gains":["A shareable entry point before the first Show HN"],"costs":["Adds a surface one person cannot carry","Read-only until a repo connects"],"system":"A query parameter and a fetch path.","screens":"A read-only editor: no file tree, no tabs.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship `?src=` in the build after the pilot, as the plan's channel table implies","gains":["Nothing changes in the ninety-day sequence"],"costs":["Misses the ten-stranger window","Three stale references stay in the plan"],"system":"Deferred.","screens":"Deferred.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Reinstate the Chrome extension","gains":["A store listing carries its own search"],"costs":["Overturns two measured findings","The market leader has not shipped in two years"],"system":"A Manifest V3 build and store review.","screens":"A popup nobody has designed.","money":"A one-off registration fee."},{"k":"d","label":"No browser-link surface at all","what":"Neither the extension nor the parameter. Repos and folders only.","gains":["Simplest to maintain and to document"],"costs":["Nothing for a visitor who installed nothing","Contradicts the plan's own channel table"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["It costs hours, needs no store, and is the only path for someone who installed nothing.","The three stale references cost nothing to delete, alongside the six Obsidian-plugin ones."],"flip":"If desktop goes first, `?src=` becomes a viewer feature and its priority drops.","linked":["FF2","FF8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:464","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:134"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"Deleting three stale references to a cut extension is a chore, not a choice.","dupes":{"dropped":["G25"],"areas":["Go-to-market & channels"],"why":"Go-to-market & channels also recommended building it early, but as the demo surface for the three landing pages, not as a channel. On shipping it, that is option a here; whether it is ever counted as a channel follows from this card.","agree":true}},{"id":"F10","cat":"Features","sub":"AI panel","weight":"critical","q":"Is bring-your-own key a hard gate before any stranger touches the product?","lede":"Every AI request in the shipped code bills the operator, and ten strangers arrive in weeks 10 to 12.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"User's machine","items":["Editor","Splice engine","No key field yet"],"tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Zephyrus server","items":["Six live /api/ai routes","Five operator keys read at request time","Vercel AI Gateway fallback"],"side":"billed to us","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Model provider","items":["Google, Groq, Cerebras, Mistral, OpenRouter"],"tone":"sunk"}],"note":"The plan says the request goes from the user's machine to their provider.","caption":"The middle layer the architecture claims does not exist"},"stakes":"An unbounded model bill owed by a company funded from consulting hours.","state":["The plan's fix list: no bring-your-own key yet, so every AI request bills the operator.","The gateway reads five operator keys, falling back to Vercel.","The cost rules assume model cost is zero, on the user's key."],"tension":["Three days sit in front of the schedule, unsequenced against generation and the panel.","A stranger with no key sees a smaller product than the plan describes.","A cap in policy is not a cap; in code it is another build."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The current AI cost path","items":[["Bring-your-own key","Not built","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:219; fix #6, 3 days"],["Operator keys read at request time","5 providers: Google, Groq, Cerebras, Mistral, OpenRouter","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:34-53"],["Fallback when none set","Vercel AI Gateway, credit-gated","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:66"],["Live AI routes exposed","6","measured: src/app/api/ai/"],["Model cost the plan assumes","Zero: all AI on the user's key","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231"],["Strangers scheduled","10, weeks 10 to 12","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Features that cannot be shown to a stranger without it","cols":["Feature","Needs a model","Blocked without BYO key"],"rows":[["Idea mode","Yes","Yes"],["Decision flow","Yes","Yes"],["Repo docs scan generation half","Yes","Yes, the scan itself is model-free"],["File-scoped AI panel","Yes","Yes"],["Ghost text (shipped, off by default)","Yes","Yes"],["Review state","No","No"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Hard gate: no AI route is reachable by a non-founder until the key UI ships","what":"Keychain and key field land before the pilot; unkeyed users get the editor and highlight.","gains":["Bounded spend, and the architecture claim becomes true","Three days inside a 24-day block that exists"],"costs":["Strangers without a key see a smaller product","Key entry is friction in the first session"],"system":"Key UI, keychain, routes reading the user's key.","screens":"Settings gains the key field.","money":"Model cost goes to zero, as assumed."},{"k":"b","label":"Run the pilot on operator keys with a per-user cap enforced in code","gains":["Ten strangers see the whole product immediately"],"costs":["Contradicts the plan's own cost rule and risk response","The cap is unbudgeted and must be right first time"],"system":"Metering, ceilings, a refusal at the ceiling.","screens":"A quota state nobody has drawn.","money":"A real bill, bounded by unwritten code.","what":"Run the pilot on the operator's keys with a per-user cap enforced in code. Strangers see the whole product at once, against the plan's own cost rule, and the cap has to be right first time."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship BYO key and keep a metered operator fallback as paid hosted AI","gains":["The upsell moment is real and observable"],"costs":["Hosted AI was planned later, so cost and legal widen early","The one path where document text reaches a model"],"system":"Key UI plus metering plus billing.","screens":"Settings key field plus usage.","money":"Revenue and cost start before a paying tier.","what":"Ship the user's own key and keep a metered operator fallback as paid hosted AI. The upsell is observable, and this is the one path where document text reaches a model through us."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut every model-dependent feature from the pilot and run it on review state alone","gains":["No key problem, no spend, no metering"],"costs":["Removes generation and the panel from the demo","A first-time user has nothing to do"],"system":"Routes unreachable behind a build flag.","screens":"Settings loses the AI section.","money":"None.","what":"Cut every model feature from the pilot and run it on review state alone. No key, no spend, no metering, and a first-time user has nothing to do."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The plan's own risk table says bring-your-own key before any stranger.","Three days inside a 24-day fix-first block, against an unbounded bill."],"flip":"Option b is acceptable only with a spend cap enforced in code, not in policy.","linked":["AC13","F27","F5","F8","L10"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:219","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:328","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:34"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Strangers use the AI features on day one. Whose key pays is a gate before anyone signs up.","recWhy":"Every AI request in the shipped code bills the operator through five keys with a Vercel fallback, and the cost model assumes model cost is zero because it sits on the user's key. Both cannot be true when strangers arrive. A hard gate, no AI route reachable by a non-founder until the key field ships, makes the architecture claim true and bounds the bill to nothing, and it is three days inside a fix-first block that already exists. Q10 carries the same position: the user's key, in session memory on the web and the keychain on desktop, no local model. A stranger without a key sees a smaller product, and that is the honest product; the editor, the highlight and export all work with AI hidden. A cap in policy is not a cap, and a cap in code is another build."},{"id":"F19","cat":"Features","sub":"Share","weight":"critical","q":"Does the shipped public-share page stay, get gated, or get deleted before anyone outside sees the build?","lede":"The plan says frontmatter hosts nothing. The code serves any shared note to anonymous readers today.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Anonymous visitor","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"/p/[slug] on our server","note":"unauthenticated server component","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Server reads the note from GitHub","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Rendered page served to anyone","tone":"stop"},{"label":"public_slug spliced into the file","note":"907 of 907 files byte-identical","tone":"sunk"}],"note":"The plan's definition lists no hosting of user content among the things not in the product.","caption":"The path the plan says does not exist, live in the tree"},"stakes":"A public route serving user content confers intermediary status and the obligations that follow.","state":["The plan's definition and tier table both put hosting outside the product.","The share module is 1,407 lines and serves `/p/[slug]` without sign-in.","Its write path is measured clean: 907 of 907 files byte-identical."],"tension":["The share decision, hosted viewer or permalink plus export, is set for week 2 and written as though no code exists.","No operating rule can be written while three committed files disagree.","A build flag defers the decision, and flags tend to ship on."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What is actually shipped","items":[["Share module","1,407 lines across 16 files","measured: src/modules/share/"],["Public route","/p/[slug], unauthenticated Server Component","src/app/(public)/p/[slug]/page.tsx"],["Byte path","Server reads the note from GitHub and renders it","src/modules/share/application/resolve-public-note.ts:36"],["What is written into the user's file","public_slug, spliced into the YAML frontmatter","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts"],["Splice fidelity, measured on the pinned corpus","907 of 907 files byte-identical after a no-op publish/unpublish cycle; the regenerating path left 33 of 907","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:22"],["Founder-hours the PRD prices for shipping it safely","74","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Four artefacts, three positions","cols":["Source","Position on holding documents"],"rows":[["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:43","No hosting of user content"],["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","Hosting absent from the pilot"],["FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4295 and :4204","We hold user documents; ship publishing in v1, narrow surface, 74 founder-hours of obligations first"],["firestore.rules, committed, 419 lines","900,000-byte note bodies, revisions with allow delete: if false, and UNAUTHENTICATED GET for visibility==public (line 83)"],["src/app/(public)/p/[slug]","Shipped and serving"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete the share module and the public route before any external build","gains":["The legal section can be finalised this month"],"costs":["Deletes the only way to show a document to a non-user","Deletes a write path measured clean on 907 files"],"system":"Removes a module, a route and a writer.","screens":"Share control and public page go.","money":"None.","what":"Delete the share module and the public route before any build leaves the studio. The legal section closes this month; the only way to show a document to a non-user goes with it."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep it and rewrite the operating rules around hosting","gains":["Publishing is a distribution surface nothing else replaces"],"costs":["Every legal rule rewritten around intermediary status","Grievance, takedown and abuse become live work"],"system":"No code change; policy and process work.","screens":"The public page becomes supported.","money":"74 founder-hours by the PRD's estimate.","what":"Keep the page and rewrite every operating rule around hosting. A distribution surface nothing else replaces, and grievance, takedown and abuse become live work today."},{"k":"c","label":"Disable the route in the build, keep the code, decide in week 2","gains":["One build-flag change, reversible either way"],"costs":["A flag defers the decision, and flags tend to ship on","The contradiction stays in the tree another fortnight"],"system":"A build flag over the public route.","screens":"Share control hidden.","money":"None.","what":"Disable the route behind a build flag, keep the code, decide in week two. One reversible change; a flag defers the decision and flags tend to ship on."},{"k":"d","label":"Replace it with permalink plus export","what":"A link opens the file in the reader's own frontmatter; we host nothing.","gains":["Matches the promise and the architecture"],"costs":["Does nothing for a private repo or plain folder","Requires the reader to have the product"],"system":"A URL parameter over the web app.","screens":"Share becomes a copy-link action.","money":"None."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The hosting decision governs this, and it is already scheduled for week 2.","What can be settled today: nothing serves user content while that is open."],"flip":"If the founders confirm we never hold documents, delete the module rather than flagging it.","linked":["L1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:367","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:142","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194","firestore.rules:83","src/modules/share/application/resolve-public-note.ts:36","src/modules/share/application/resolve-public-note.ts","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:22"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The public share page is replaced by unlisted kit links, so its fate has to be settled.","dependsOn":["L6"],"recWhy":"The plan says frontmatter hosts nothing, and the code serves any shared note to an anonymous reader today, 1,407 lines with a write path measured clean on 907 files. Three committed artefacts disagree and no operating rule can be written while they do. The hosting decision itself, L1, is question one in the reading order and it is not this card's to make. What this card can settle is that nothing serves user content while that decision is open, which one build flag does, reversibly. Q11 carries it that way: route disabled, decided in week two, and if publishing returns it returns gated to the founder with noindex and system slugs. If L1 confirms we hold nothing, delete the module rather than leaving a flag that will one day ship on.","dupes":{"dropped":["G10","MK18"],"areas":["Go-to-market & channels","Market & competition"],"why":"Go-to-market & channels recommended keeping the page live, rendered from the repo with no stored snapshot and noindex by default, closest to option b here. Market & competition recommended option d, permalink plus export. If the page survives, who may publish and whether it is indexed is a separate Legal, privacy & data decision.","agree":false}},{"id":"F21","cat":"Features","sub":"Cut and inherited","weight":"critical","q":"Do we delete the table editor and ghost text this week, given each refutes a claim the pitch rests on?","lede":"Two inherited surfaces in the code break byte-exact editing and the promise of no document bytes on our server.","visual":{"kind":"ba","via":"table-edit.ts","before":{"title":"A 4-line CRLF table, before one cell edit","lines":[{"t":"100 bytes","mark":"kept"},{"t":"4 carriage returns","mark":"kept"},{"t":"5 lines untouched","mark":"kept"}]},"after":{"title":"After the shipped table editor","lines":[{"t":"84 bytes","mark":"lost"},{"t":"1 carriage return","mark":"lost"},{"t":"3 of 5 lines rewritten","mark":"lost"}]},"note":"Ghost text is the same class: it posts the prefix to our own route on a timer.","caption":"The counterexample to the pitch, measured in our own code"},"stakes":"A prospect who edits a table, or turns ghost text on, finds the counterexample in seconds.","state":["One cell edit in a 4-line CRLF table destroys 3 carriage returns.","A 2026-08-31 decision already said: delete the table UI and keep the measurement.","Ghost text sends the text before the cursor to `/api/ai/complete`. It is off by default."],"tension":["Table editing is table stakes, and a byte-exact replacement is unbudgeted.","Ghost text is off by default, which mitigates but does not resolve it.","Deleting is half a day; rebuilding table editing is a week or more."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the table editor does to a file","items":[["Lines rewritten on a one-cell edit","3 of 5","r26-c4-features.md finding R3, module executed in Node"],["Bytes","100 to 84","r26-c4-features.md finding R3"],["Carriage returns on a CRLF file","4 to 1 (3 destroyed)","DECIDE.md:216; r26-c4-features.md finding R3"],["Status in the decision layer","Dead code, already slated for deletion","DECIDE.md:216, dated 2026-08-31"],["Status in the tree","Present, 190 lines","src/modules/preview/presentation/table-edit.ts"],["Contrast: the share writer's splice","907 of 907 files byte-identical; the regenerating path left 33 of 907","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:22"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Ghost text against three stated rules","items":[["Rule 1","No document byte passes through a Zephyrus server","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35"],["Rule 2","The AI panel is one collapsed line; nothing runs until asked","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247"],["Rule 3","The AI request goes from the user's machine to their provider","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133"],["What ghost text does","POSTs the whole prefix before the cursor to /api/ai/complete after 650 ms idle","src/modules/editor/presentation/ghost-text.ts:121, :133"],["Default","Off","src/modules/editor/presentation/editor-settings.ts:41"],["In the feature exhibit","Absent","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55-67"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete both this week and keep the table measurement as a regression test","gains":["Turns the worst artefact in the repo into the most persuasive"],"costs":["Inline table editing goes until a byte-exact version exists","One of six live AI routes goes with it"],"system":"Removes 190 lines, a CodeMirror compartment, a route.","screens":"Preview table editing and ghost text go.","money":"None.","what":"Delete the table editor and ghost text this week and keep the CRLF table measurement as a regression test. The worst artefact in the repo becomes the most persuasive."},{"k":"b","label":"Rebuild both correctly before deleting anything","what":"Table writes become byte-exact, and completion calls the user's provider directly.","gains":["No capability lost"],"costs":["5 to 8 days plus 2, none of it budgeted","The counterexample ships until the rebuild lands"],"system":"Byte-exact table writes; direct-to-provider completion.","screens":"Same screens, different behaviour.","money":"Removes a route we pay for."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep both and disclose the limitations in the UI","gains":["Zero work"],"costs":["A note saying this control rewrites your file is worse than none","A written admission that the pitch is conditional"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Two disclosure strings.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both and disclose the limits in the interface. Zero work, and a note saying this control rewrites your file is worse than no control at all."},{"k":"d","label":"Make tables read-only in preview and gate ghost text behind the hide-all switch","gains":["Cheapest way to make the byte claim true"],"costs":["Editing a table in source is the friction editors remove","The contradiction persists for everyone who leaves AI on"],"system":"A read-only flag and a switch dependency.","screens":"Tables read-only in the preview pane.","money":"None.","what":"Tables read-only in preview, ghost text behind the hide-all switch. The cheapest way to make the byte claim true, and the contradiction persists for anyone who leaves AI on."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The table decision was made on 2026-08-31 and not executed. Only the date is open.","Keeping the measurement as a regression test turns the counterexample into the demonstration."],"flip":"If completion is wanted as a differentiator, ghost text must call the user's provider directly.","linked":["F18","F9"],"sources":["docs/DECIDE.md:216","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c4-features.md","src/modules/preview/presentation/table-edit.ts","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:22","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247","src/modules/editor/presentation/ghost-text.ts:133","src/modules/editor/presentation/editor-settings.ts:41","merged: F22"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Two shipped surfaces contradict claims the product makes. Keep or cut them before strangers see them.","recWhy":"One cell edit in a four-line table with Windows line endings destroys three carriage returns, which refutes byte-exact editing in one line. And ghost text sends the text before the cursor to our server, which refutes no document bytes on our side. Two inherited surfaces, each disproving a claim the pitch rests on. The decision to delete the table editor was made on 31 August and never executed, so the only open question was the date. Deleting is half a day; rebuilding table editing byte-exactly is a week or more and unbudgeted. Keeping the CRLF measurement as a regression test is the move that matters, because the same file that proves the defect then proves it fixed, in the test suite, forever. Q5 carries this as deleted. If completion is wanted later as a differentiator, ghost text has to call the user's provider directly, which is a different feature."},{"id":"F34","cat":"Features","sub":"KIT CONSISTENCY","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The kit is the funnel. Thirty-three files that contradict each other are worse than none, and nothing checks them.","q":"Who checks that the generated files agree with each other, and does that check ship with the kit?","lede":"Any model writes thirty-three files. Getting the API reference to match the data model is the whole job.","stakes":"A user hands a contradictory kit to their agent, the agent builds the contradiction, and it surfaces weeks later.","state":["The kit in the plan is a prompt, a handful of questions and a set of files at an unlisted link.","Nothing in the plan describes a check that the files are consistent with each other.","The failure is silent: a contradiction reads as ordinary prose in either file on its own."],"tension":["Generation is commodity and every agent can do it. The check is the part that is ours.","A check that refuses is a product that sometimes says no, which is the engine's own posture already."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Generate only, no check. Ship it and let the user read the kit","gains":["Fastest to build","No refusal to explain"],"costs":["The contradiction lands in their codebase, not in our product, so we never see it","No defensible part"],"system":"Generation only.","screens":"None.","money":"Lowest token cost.","what":"Generate the files and ship them; the user reads the kit. Fastest, no refusal to explain, and a contradiction lands in their codebase where we never see it."},{"k":"b","label":"A names-and-claims pass across the kit, reported to the user before the link is made","gains":["Catches the failure where it is cheap, which is before the agent runs","This is the part a competitor cannot copy from a prompt"],"costs":["A second model pass on every kit, so roughly double the tokens","A report the user has to read"],"system":"A consistency stage between generation and publication.","screens":"A findings list before the link.","money":"Roughly double the per-kit token cost.","what":"A names-and-claims pass across every file before the link is made, with the report shown to the user. Catches the failure before an agent runs, at roughly double the tokens."},{"k":"c","label":"Generate the kit from one structured answer set, so the files cannot disagree","gains":["The contradiction becomes impossible rather than detected","One source, many projections, which is the projection law again"],"costs":["Much harder to build and slower to change","Files read as templated, which buyers notice"],"system":"An intermediate model of the project, then rendering.","screens":"None.","money":"Lower tokens, higher build.","what":"Generate from one structured answer set so the files cannot disagree. The contradiction becomes impossible; much harder to build, and the output reads as templated."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship the check as a separate paid step after the free kit","gains":["A clear thing to charge for","Free tier stays cheap"],"costs":["The free kit is the one that reaches the agent, so the damage still happens","Splits one product into two"],"system":"A paid stage.","screens":"An upsell.","money":"A second price point.","what":"Ship the check as a paid step after the free kit. Something clear to charge for, and the free kit is the one that reaches the agent, so the damage still happens."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The check is the only part of the kit a competitor cannot reproduce with a better prompt.","It catches the failure before the agent runs, which is the only moment it is cheap."],"flip":"If the twenty hand made kits show contradictions are rare, the check becomes a warning rather than a stage.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Where a contradiction gets caught, and what it costs there","nodes":[{"label":"Answers","meta":"5 to 8 questions"},{"label":"Generate","meta":"33 files"},{"label":"Consistency pass","meta":"names and claims","mark":true},{"label":"Unlisted link","meta":"the kit"},{"label":"Their agent","meta":"builds from it"},{"label":"Three weeks later","meta":"the contradiction ships","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Caught at the third step it is a line in a report. Caught at the last it is a rewrite.","caption":"The cost of a contradiction, by where it is found"},"sources":["docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1","docs/GENERATION-SURFACE-2026-09-13-2113.md:1","docs/13-TECH-DEBT.md:1"],"linked":["F5","PR7","R1"],"recWhy":"Any model writes thirty-three files, and Spec Kit does it free with 136,339 stars, so generation is not the part that is ours. What no generator does is check that 04-API-REFERENCE describes the endpoints 03-DATA-MODEL implies, with the same names, and a kit whose files disagree is worse than no kit, because the user hands it to an agent and the agent builds the contradiction without noticing. That failure is silent and lands weeks later. A names-and-claims pass before the link is made catches it at the only moment it is cheap, and it is the half a competitor cannot copy from a better prompt. Generating from one structured source, c, is the right end state and too large to start with; the check is the road to it. Q12 carries this. If the twenty hand-made kits show contradictions are rare, the stage becomes a warning."},{"id":"F35","cat":"Features","sub":"INSTRUCTION FILES","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"This is the clearest fit between what we already are and something many people are short of. It changes what the MVP is for.","q":"Do we build authoring for the instruction files agents read, and is that the MVP's reason to exist rather than a later feature?","lede":"`AGENTS.md` is a Linux Foundation standard used by over 60,000 projects, and people write it by hand in a code editor.","stakes":"If this is the wedge, the MVP is an editor for agent instructions that also does documents. If it is a feature, it waits and somebody else takes it.","state":["agents.md, fetched 2026-09-13: over 60,000 projects, stewarded by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation.","Sampled the same day: 77 commits to the file in `openai/codex`, 64 in `apache/airflow`, 35 in `vercel/next.js`.","`apache/airflow` and `vercel/next.js` both symlink `CLAUDE.md` to `AGENTS.md`. LibreChat keeps two and they have drifted."],"tension":["Roughly two dozen agents read one format, so the work is portable in a way almost nothing else here is.","It is a new surface on a plan that already has more surfaces than one person can carry."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Make it the MVP's point: an editor for the files agents obey","gains":["A reason to exist that no markdown editor has taken","Review state gets a real job, on files that are obeyed"],"costs":["Reframes the landing page, the kit and the pricing together","Narrower audience than everyone who writes markdown"],"system":"Hierarchy resolution, size reporting, a which-file-wins view.","screens":"A tree of instruction files, and a resolved view for a path.","money":"None directly. It is the reason somebody arrives.","what":"Make it the MVP's reason to exist: an editor for the files agents obey. Review state gets a real job on files that are obeyed; the landing page, the kit and the pricing reframe together."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship it as one feature inside the document kit, after the kit works","gains":["No reframing, and the kit stays the funnel","Lower risk if the standard fades"],"costs":["The best-founded thing in the research sits behind the least-founded","A well funded competitor can take it in a quarter"],"system":"A generator template.","screens":"One more kit output.","money":"None.","what":"Ship it as one feature inside the kit, after the kit works. No reframing, and the best-founded thing in the research sits behind the least-founded."},{"k":"c","label":"Generate the files only, and never edit or check them","gains":["Days of work, not weeks","Fits the kit exactly as planned"],"costs":["Generation is the commodity half, so nothing here is defensible","Does not touch any of the three documented failures"],"system":"One more file in the kit.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Generate the instruction files and never edit or check them. Days of work, and it touches none of the three failures the vendor documents."},{"k":"d","label":"Not now. Decide after the twenty hand made kits","gains":["Keeps the surface count where the pace can carry it"],"costs":["The evidence is already in and waiting does not add to it","Adoption is growing while we wait"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Not now; decide after the twenty hand-made kits. The surface count stays where the pace can carry it, and the evidence is already in."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["It is the only item in the research with measured adoption, a governed standard and failure modes the vendor documents itself.","It gives review state a job that is not the claim four free tools already took apart."],"flip":"If a wider random sample shows most instruction files were written once and abandoned, this is a one-off generator and (c) is right.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"The three things the vendor's own manual says go wrong","cols":["Written by hand today","What an editor could do","Ours?"],"rows":[{"label":"Agent does not follow it","cells":["no way to tell","show what was obeyed","review state"]},{"label":"File is too large","cells":["no size signal","report the size","yes"]},{"label":"Lost after compaction","cells":["invisible","flag what dropped","yes"]},{"label":"88 files, which one wins","cells":["read the tree yourself","resolve for a path","yes"]},{"label":"Portable across agents","cells":["one format, two dozen agents","same","same"]}],"note":"Failure list quoted from the Claude Code memory doc, fetched 2026-09-13. File count from agents.md.","caption":"What breaks today, and whether an editor can see it"},"sources":["docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1","AGENTS.md:1","CLAUDE.md:1"],"linked":["F34","P2","PR24"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What was fetched on 2026-09-13","note":"Both pages were opened and the figures read off them. Neither number is derived.","items":[["Open-source projects using AGENTS.md","over 60,000","agents.md, fetched 2026-09-13"],["Who stewards the format","Agentic AI Foundation, Linux Foundation","agents.md"],["AGENTS.md files in the main OpenAI repository","88","agents.md"],["Scopes a CLAUDE.md can live at","4: managed policy, user, project, local","Claude Code memory doc"],["Failure modes in the vendor's own troubleshooting","3","Claude Code memory doc"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Sampled on GitHub, 2026-09-13: are these files maintained, and how big do they get","note":"A convenience sample of eight repositories named on agents.md or known to carry the file, read through the GitHub API. Biased towards large active projects, so it says what happens in a live repository and not what the median project does.","items":[["openai/codex, commits touching AGENTS.md","77","GitHub API, repos/openai/codex/commits?path=AGENTS.md"],["apache/airflow, commits touching AGENTS.md","64","GitHub API"],["vercel/next.js, commits touching AGENTS.md","35","GitHub API"],["Largest file in the sample","apache/airflow, 36,577 bytes, roughly 6,100 words","GitHub API contents"],["apache/airflow and vercel/next.js CLAUDE.md","git mode 120000, a symlink to AGENTS.md","GitHub API git/trees"],["danny-avila/LibreChat, two real files","AGENTS.md 7,415 bytes against CLAUDE.md 23,170","GitHub API contents"]]}],"recWhy":"This is the strongest signal found all week and the only one with adoption, governance and failure modes all measured. AGENTS.md is used by over 60,000 open-source projects and stewarded by the Agentic AI Foundation under the Linux Foundation. Roughly two dozen agents read it. Files nest and the nearest one wins, and the main OpenAI repository holds 88 of them. They are maintained, 77 commits in openai/codex; they are large, 6,100 words in apache/airflow; and they drift, LibreChat's two copies 3x apart while airflow resorted to a symlink. The vendor's own manual lists too large, not followed and lost after compaction as known failures, and the founder's CLAUDE.md is 10,033 words. Spec Kit and Kiro generate and leave. Nobody stands where a person reads, edits and keeps these files current. That is an editor problem, and it is ours."},{"id":"F36","cat":"Features","sub":"SHARING","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Sharing is the free tier hook in the 13 September direction, and the shape decides months of engine work.","q":"Is sharing a live editing session, or a link somebody reads and comments on?","lede":"Live editing means operational transforms or CRDTs, and this repo settled against CRDTs on evidence.","stakes":"Live editing is a multi-month lane against Google Docs. Read and comment is weeks and fits what a reviewed kit needs.","state":["The direction given on 13 September proposed live sharing, free for up to two people, with Google login.","`CLAUDE.md` records sync as git merge plus a splice journal and CAS, never a CRDT.","A kit is reviewed before it goes to an agent, which is asynchronous work by nature."],"tension":["Two people in one document at once is what people picture when they hear sharing.","Almost nobody edits a specification simultaneously. They read it and leave remarks."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Read and comment on a link, with suggestions the owner accepts or refuses","gains":["Weeks rather than months, and no settled position reopened","Matches how a document actually gets reviewed"],"costs":["Looks less impressive in a demo than two cursors","Somebody will ask for live editing anyway"],"system":"Comment threads against a span, and a suggestion queue.","screens":"A shared read view with a margin, and a review inbox.","money":"Storage for comments. Small.","what":"A link somebody reads and comments on, with suggestions the owner accepts or refuses. Weeks rather than months, and it matches how a document actually gets reviewed."},{"k":"b","label":"Live editing for two on free, as the direction proposed","gains":["The strongest free tier hook on the list","Demonstrates well"],"costs":["Reopens the CRDT decision the repo settled on evidence","Competes with Google Docs on their single strongest axis"],"system":"A realtime transport and a merge strategy we have argued against.","screens":"Presence, cursors, conflict states.","money":"A realtime bill per connected pair.","what":"Live editing for two people on the free tier, as the direction proposed. The strongest hook on the list, and it reopens the sync decision and competes with Google Docs where they are strongest."},{"k":"c","label":"A read-only link and nothing else","gains":["Days of work, and the public share route already exists"],"costs":["No reason for the second person to come back","Gives the reviewer nowhere to put a remark"],"system":"Reuse the share module.","screens":"One.","money":"None.","what":"A read-only link and nothing else. Days of work on a route that exists, and the second person has no reason to return and nowhere to put a remark."},{"k":"d","label":"Read and comment now, live editing priced into the top tier later","gains":["Keeps the door open without paying for it now"],"costs":["Promises something the settled architecture makes expensive","A promise on a page is a commitment"],"system":"As (a), plus a stated roadmap.","screens":"As (a).","money":"A future price with no costing behind it.","what":"Read and comment now, live editing priced into the top tier later. Keeps the door open, and a promise on a pricing page is a commitment the architecture makes expensive."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Asynchronous review is what a document kit needs before it reaches an agent, and it costs a fraction of live editing.","Live editing would reopen a position this repo settled with reasons that have not changed."],"flip":"If the twenty hand made kits show people sit together and edit at the same time, the cost of (b) has to be faced.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"What the two shapes actually cost","cols":["Read and comment","Live editing","Read only"],"rows":[{"label":"Build","cells":["weeks","months","days"]},{"label":"Reopens the sync decision","cells":["no","yes","no"]},{"label":"Competes with","cells":["nothing directly","Google Docs, Notion","nothing"]},{"label":"Fits reviewing a kit","cells":["yes","not really","no place for remarks"]},{"label":"Free tier hook","cells":["two readers","two editors","a link"]}],"note":"Sync position quoted from CLAUDE.md: git merge plus a splice journal and CAS, never a CRDT.","caption":"Three shapes of sharing, against what a kit review needs"},"sources":["CLAUDE.md:1","docs/GENERATION-SURFACE-2026-09-13-2113.md:1","src/app/api/share/route.ts:31","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1"],"linked":["F19","L6","PR24"],"recWhy":"Two people editing one document at the same time needs operational transforms or CRDTs, and this repository settled against CRDTs on evidence that has not changed. They converge on byte-identical garbage for prose, and the sync position is git merge plus a write log. Building live multiplayer reopens that, costs months, and lands in direct competition with Google Docs on the one axis where they are strongest. What a document kit actually needs is different. A kit is reviewed before it goes to an agent, which is asynchronous by nature: someone reads it, leaves remarks, suggests a change, the owner accepts or refuses. Read and comment delivers that in weeks, and it is the free-tier hook in a shape we can afford. Q11 carries it. If the twenty show people genuinely sit together and edit at once, b's cost has to be faced honestly."},{"id":"F5","cat":"Features","sub":"Generation","weight":"high","q":"Does generation ship in the pilot on founder evidence that has never been attached?","lede":"Idea mode and the decision flow are eight days between them, both resting on one statement in chat.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"spec-kit, Kiro, plan mode"},{"title":"Standalone PRD tools","tone":"sunk"},{"title":"Idea mode + decision flow","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Asks the clarifying questions","cells":["Yes, all three","No","Yes, from the LLM port"]},{"label":"Where the answers land","cells":["In chat, or outside the repo","n/a","Frontmatter keys in the file"]},{"label":"Price","cells":["Free","Free","Free, 8 engineering days"]},{"label":"Traction","cells":["The toolchains people already run","Top repo 57 stars of 512","Founder test, no notes attached"]},{"label":"Survives with AI hidden","cells":["n/a","n/a","No: needs a key and a network"]}],"note":"Claude Code plan mode writes to ~/.claude/plans and deletes after 30 days.","caption":"The clarify-then-generate step, and who already performs it free"},"stakes":"Eight days and an evidence label both go if the founder test does not replicate.","state":["Idea mode exists as code; the decision flow, which asks the open questions before generating, adds 4 days.","Both are blocked because the model interface returns a bare string, not structured output.","The gap register rates the evidence High severity: a chat statement, no notes, no protocol."],"tension":["The stated difference is where the answers land. Asserted, never measured.","Kiro already stages line comments at the point of need.","Without generation, a first-time user has nothing to do in an empty folder."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Idea mode's evidence and cost","items":[["Evidence class","Founder user test, reported","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59"],["Notes, participants, protocol attached","None","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23, severity High"],["Public evidence, standalone PRD generators","512 repos; top repo 57 stars","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59"],["Code that exists","generate-document, 5 prompt kinds, /api/ai/generate-doc","src/modules/ai/application/doc-prompts.ts:10"],["Remaining build","2d structured output + 2d from-repo input","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59"],["Blocked by","LLM port returns a bare string; JSON is sliced between brackets","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:220"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The five document kinds already coded","cols":["Kind","Sections the prompt fixes"],"rows":[["prd","Overview / Problem, Goals and non-goals, Target users, User stories, Functional requirements, UX and flows, Success metrics, Risks and open questions, Milestones"],["frd","Functional requirements document"],["brd","Business requirements document"],["product-note","TL;DR, Problem, Proposed solution, Why now, Open questions, Next steps"],["spec","Summary, Goals and non-goals, Architecture, Data model, API, Key flows, Edge cases and failure modes, Testing plan, Rollout, Open questions"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Who already asks the clarifying questions","cols":["Tool","The step","Where the answer lives","Price"],"rows":[["spec-kit","/speckit.clarify","In chat","Free"],["Kiro","Spec clarification at phase checkpoints","In the tool, as line comments at the point of need","Free"],["Claude Code plan mode","Plan mode questions","~/.claude/plans/<slug>.md, outside the repo, deleted after 30 days","Free"],["frontmatter, decision flow","Structured question list from the LLM port","Frontmatter keys in the user's file","Free, 4 engineering days"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Decision flow's dependencies and class","items":[["Build","4 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:60"],["Blocked by","Idea mode structured output; the LLM port returns a bare string today","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:220"],["Web without install","Yes, with the user's key","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:60"],["Desktop offline","No","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:60"],["Evidence class","Founder user test, reported","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:187"],["Where the incumbents' files land","Verified on a scratch repo in round 2","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:126"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship both in the pilot as planned, eight days","what":"Ship idea mode and the decision flow in the pilot, eight days. The founders' strongest-evidenced feature, and four of the eight days repeat a step three free tools perform.","gains":["The strongest founder-evidenced feature is in the pilot","Most of idea mode is already written"],"costs":["Four of eight days duplicate a step three free tools perform","Doubles exposure to one unattached claim"],"system":"Structured output plus a question-list contract.","screens":"Launcher cards and the generation flow.","money":"Needs a key, so bring-your-own key first."},{"k":"b","label":"Attach the founder notes first, then ship both","gains":["One founder hour closes a High-severity gap"],"costs":["If no notes exist, the label changes before the meeting","Blocks eight days behind a founder-only task"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Attach the founder test notes first, then ship. One hour closes the evidence gap; if the notes do not exist the label changes before anything is built."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship idea mode, defer the decision flow to the build after the pilot","gains":["Halves the exposure and buys four days"],"costs":["A one-shot generator has no public traction","Drops the part the founders say they tested"],"system":"Structured output only; no question contract.","screens":"Launcher cards without a question step.","money":"None.","what":"Ship idea mode, defer the decision flow. Halves the exposure and buys four days; drops the part the founders say they watched someone use."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut both and let the user's agent generate","what":"Cut both and let the user's own agent generate. Zero build, and the empty folder stays empty on first run; the only two features with user contact go.","gains":["Zero build, and it matches the reading-surface story"],"costs":["The empty folder stays empty on first run","Removes the only two features with user contact"],"system":"Deletes the generate routes and prompt kinds.","screens":"The launcher becomes blank plus templates.","money":"None."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The founders either have notes or they do not, and one hour settles a High-severity gap. Attach them first.","Q12 then puts generation in Phase 2 as start from here, after twenty kits made by hand, never as the claim. Not the pilot."],"flip":"If no notes exist, relabel both untested; idea mode alone in Phase 2 is option c's shape. If the twenty ask for generation unprompted, it moves up.","linked":["F10","F27","F7","FF1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:69","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:220","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:325","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:52","src/modules/ai/application/doc-prompts.ts:10","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:60","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:187","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:126","merged: F6"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Generation is the funnel now, so whether it ships is no longer optional.","dependsOn":["R17"],"recWhy":"Two features rest on a sentence in a chat window that says founders tested them, and the notes the sentence cites are not attached. That is cheap to fix and it has to be fixed first, because an evidence column reading founder user test with nothing behind it is the kind of thing a reviewer finds in a minute. After that the research moved the timing. GitHub's Spec Kit does prompt-to-spec free with 136,339 stars and Kiro does it paid, so generation cannot be what a stranger meets first. Q12 puts it in Phase 2 as start from here, after twenty kits have been made by hand, with a consistency check that is the defensible half. So attach the notes, then ship in Phase 2, and if the notes turn out not to exist, say so on the card and let idea mode go alone.","dupes":{"dropped":["P6","PL1"],"areas":["Product & definition","Plan, scope & sequencing"],"why":"Product & definition recommended the same as option b here: ship both and attach the founder test notes this week. Plan, scope & sequencing recommended cutting both, option d, along with the file-scoped AI, keeping only the scan with no model. Its option to settle the form factor first is carried by the form-factor card.","agree":false}},{"id":"F8","cat":"Features","sub":"AI panel","weight":"high","q":"Does the AI panel ship the two verbs the plan specifies, or the eight the code already has?","lede":"The plan and the shipped product disagree about how much AI is in the editor, fourfold.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"The AI surface, plan against code","panes":[{"label":"At rest","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"One collapsed line, nothing runs until asked","tone":"accent"}]},{"label":"Menu in the code","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Refine selection / note","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Summarize note","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Expand, Shorten","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Professional tone, Casual tone, Translate","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Suggest wikilinks","tone":"warn"}]},{"label":"Menu in the plan","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Fix this selection","tone":"good"},{"label":"Critique this selection","meta":"does not exist","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Summarise this file","tone":"good"}]}],"note":"The plan's feature table prices this row at 3 days for the key UI and keychain only.","caption":"Eight shipped verbs, three specified, one of them unbuilt"},"stakes":"Either working code gets deleted or the positioning's restraint stops being true.","state":["The plan's feature table: two verbs, collapsed by default, plus a hide-all switch.","The shipped menu has eight items, none of them critique.","Six AI routes are live: complete, generate-doc, link-doctor, refine, suggest-links, summarize."],"tension":["Critique is the only verb on either list belonging to a review product.","Deleting working code costs days that buy no capability.","Five palette entries are still five findable things, so demotion is not deletion."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Plan against code","cols":["Verb","In the plan (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62)","In the code (AIMenu.tsx:248-255)"],"rows":[["Fix this selection","Yes","Yes, refine selection or note"],["Critique this selection","Yes","No"],["Summarise this file","Yes","Yes, summarise note"],["Expand","No","Yes"],["Shorten","No","Yes"],["Change tone: professional","No","Yes"],["Change tone: casual","No","Yes"],["Translate to English","No","Yes"],["Suggest wikilinks","No","Yes"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What file-scoped AI is priced at, and what exists","items":[["File-scoped AI build in the plan's feature build-cost table","3 days: bring-your-own-key UI plus keychain","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62"],["Verbs in the shipped menu","8","src/modules/editor/presentation/AIMenu.tsx:248-255"],["Live AI routes","6: complete, generate-doc, link-doctor, refine, suggest-links, summarize","measured: src/app/api/ai/"],["Ghost text, handled separately","Shipped, off by default, absent from the plan's feature build-cost table","see F22"],["Public evidence cited for two verbs","Editing supplied text is 10.6% of ChatGPT messages","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Cut to the two specified verbs and write critique","gains":["The product matches the plan and the positioning"],"costs":["Deletes working code and adds an unbudgeted build","Six deletions become merge conflicts with the parent repo, sgnk-md"],"system":"Removes five route entry points; adds one.","screens":"The AI menu shrinks to three.","money":"None.","what":"Cut to the two verbs the plan specifies and build critique. The product matches the positioning; working code is deleted and six deletions become merge conflicts with sgnk-md."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the eight that exist and drop the two-verb line from the plan","gains":["Zero build"],"costs":["The largest surface in a product leading with restraint","Eight verbs is the writing assistant the pitch disclaims"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the eight verbs the code already has and drop the two-verb line. Zero build, and the largest surface in a product that leads with restraint."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship three verbs; move the other five behind the palette","what":"Refine, summarise and a new critique on the menu; the rest by search only.","gains":["One verb to build, five to demote rather than delete"],"costs":["Five palette entries are still five findable features","Critique is unbudgeted work in a three-day row"],"system":"Routing change plus one prompt kind.","screens":"Menu of three; palette gains five.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship two by promoting refine and summarise, and defer critique","gains":["No build at all"],"costs":["Loses the verb that fits a review product best","Still leaves five inherited verbs undecided"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Menu of two.","money":"None.","what":"Ship two by promoting refine and summarise, and defer critique. No build, and the one verb that belongs to a review product is the one left out."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Critique is the one verb that belongs to a review product, so build it.","Demoting five working routes to the palette costs an afternoon against deleting them."],"flip":"If the five-second screenshot test shows people reading the panel before the highlight, cut to two.","linked":["F10","F21","F9"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:202","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:381","src/modules/editor/presentation/AIMenu.tsx:248"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The rail carries AI edit. Two verbs or eight decides what that rail looks like.","recWhy":"The plan says two AI verbs and the shipped menu has eight, none of them critique, which is the only verb on either list that belongs in a product about reviewing what a machine wrote. Deleting six working routes costs days and buys no capability, and every deletion becomes a merge conflict with the parent repository. Demoting them to the palette costs an afternoon, keeps them findable by search, and clears the menu to three: refine, summarise, and a critique that has to be built. Q5 in the final set carries this as three verbs on the rail. On instruction files critique is the whole point of the panel, because the question is whether the rule you just wrote will be obeyed. If the five-second test shows people reading the panel before the highlight, cut to two."},{"id":"F39","cat":"Features","sub":"VOICE","weight":"high","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The gate exists and runs on real text. Whether it is a feature decides one rail item in Phase 1.","q":"Does the writing gate ship as a feature, telling the reader a paragraph drifted and naming the tell?","lede":"A gate measured against 196,522 of the founder's own words already runs on documents. No editor offers it.","stakes":"Ship it and the product has one feature nobody else can copy. Skip it and the most unusual asset here stays a private tool.","state":["bands.json is derived from 2,710 dictation entries and 196,522 cleaned words, audited 2026-09-11.","gate.py, patterns.py and style_score.py run today and caught the author's own replies three times on 2026-09-13.","The corpus is one person's instruction register, not composed prose, and the audit says so."],"tension":["A feature that says your writing drifted is a feature some people will resent.","The bands are one person's. A second user needs their own corpus or a generic band, and neither exists yet."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship in Phase 1 with the founder's bands; each user grows their own from their edits","gains":["The one feature no editor has","The corpus mechanism already exists"],"costs":["First-run bands are somebody else's voice","Needs a per-user corpus store, which is bytes held"],"system":"A per-document gate run, with bands per user.","screens":"A rail item that names the tell.","money":"None until per-user corpora need storage.","what":"Ship in Phase 1 with the founder's bands and let each user grow their own from their edits. The one feature no editor has; first-run bands are someone else's voice and need a store."},{"k":"b","label":"Generic tells only: dashes, stock phrases, three constructions. No personal bands","gains":["Works for everyone on day one","Holds no personal data"],"costs":["Loses the reads-like-you claim, which is the point"],"system":"The gate with bands.json's banned list only.","screens":"Same rail item, weaker.","money":"None.","what":"The generic tells only: dashes, stock phrases, the three constructions. Works for a stranger on the first paste and holds no personal data; the reads-like-you claim waits."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep it private, as a tool the founders use on their own documents","gains":["Nothing to build or explain"],"costs":["The most unusual asset stays invisible"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep it private, a tool the founders run on their own documents. Nothing to build or explain, and the most unusual asset on this machine stays invisible."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship it after the pilot, once a second corpus exists to prove the bands generalise","gains":["Evidence before a claim"],"costs":["The pilot loses its one unusual feature"],"system":"No change in the pilot.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship after the pilot, once a second corpus proves the bands generalise. Evidence before a claim, and the pilot loses its one unusual feature."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The generic tells are measured, hold no personal data, and work for a stranger on the first paste.","Personal bands can follow once a second corpus shows the mechanism generalises past one voice."],"flip":"If three of the twenty ask for their own voice rather than the generic tells, (a) is right and the per-user store is worth its bytes.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"What the gate can say about a paragraph, and to whom","cols":["Generic tells","Founder's bands","Per-user bands"],"rows":[{"label":"Works for a stranger on first paste","cells":["yes","no, wrong voice","after a corpus"]},{"label":"Holds personal data","cells":["no","no","yes"]},{"label":"Makes the reads-like-you claim","cells":["no","only for one person","yes"]},{"label":"Exists today","cells":["yes","yes","no"]}],"note":"Bands derived from a 196,522 word corpus audited 2026-09-11, in gate/bands.json.","caption":"Three ways to ship the gate, by who it works for"},"sources":["docs/AUDIT-EVERYTHING-2026-09-14-0238.md:1","docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1"],"linked":["F35","P26"],"recWhy":"A gate that reads a document and names the tell, this paragraph drifted into machine cadence here, exists today and no editor offers it. It is measured against 2,710 dictation entries and 196,522 of one person's words, and it caught that person's own agent three times in one evening. The corpus is one voice, which is the honest limit: the personal bands would tell a stranger their writing does not sound like Sagnik, which is true and useless. The generic tells are different. Dashes above a measured rate, forty-two stock phrases, three constructions, all derived from the same audit, all voice-neutral, all things a stranger recognises in their own draft. Ship those in Phase 1, hold no personal data, and let personal bands follow when a second corpus shows the mechanism generalises. If three of the twenty ask for their own voice, a is right and the store is worth its bytes."},{"id":"F9","cat":"Features","sub":"AI panel","weight":"medium","q":"What does the hide-all-AI switch actually hide, and does it ship on day one as the positioning promises?","lede":"The positioning leads with the switch as a refusal, and no code for it exists.","visual":{"kind":"ba","via":"one switch","before":{"title":"Today: no switch at all","lines":[{"t":"AI menu, eight verbs","mark":"on"},{"t":"Ghost text on a 650 ms idle timer","mark":"on"},{"t":"Idea mode in the launcher","mark":"on"},{"t":"Generate half of the docs scan","mark":"on"}]},"after":{"title":"Switch on, all or nothing","lines":[{"t":"Every AI surface","mark":"off"},{"t":"Editor, tree, modes","mark":"on"},{"t":"Review state and revert","mark":"on"},{"t":"First run from a generated document","mark":"off"}]},"note":"Five booleans exist in settings today; none of them is a global AI switch.","caption":"What is left when the switch is on: the editor and the highlight"},"stakes":"A switch that leaves three AI surfaces running is the kind of thing that gets posted.","state":["The plan puts the switch in settings on day one.","Shipped settings hold five booleans and no global AI switch.","Nobody has written down what the switch hides."],"tension":["Hide everything and a first-run user cannot start from a generated document.","Hide only the panel and the promise is thinner than the positioning implies.","Per-surface toggles are a settings surface, which the plan's five-gate feature test fails outright."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Reactions on the issue that forced each incumbent's off switch","note":"PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382. Each switch shipped after the issue, not before.","data":[["Zed disable_ai",412],["Telegram AI button",181],["VS Code chat.disableAIFeatures",30]],"unit":"reactions"},{"type":"stat","title":"The settings surface that exists today","items":[["Booleans in the shipped settings","5: vimMode, lineNumbers, spellcheck, focusMode, aiGhostText","src/modules/editor/presentation/editor-settings.ts:12-16"],["Global AI switch","Not present","measured: grep for hideAI / disableAI / hideAllAI over src/ returns nothing"],["Where the plan puts it","The settings screen, day one, named in the positioning","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382"],["Features that would have to respect it","idea mode, decision flow, repo docs scan generation, file-scoped AI, ghost text","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:200-202"],["Admission-test constraint","A configuration surface is an automatic fail, not a trade-off, under the one-person-operability gate","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.1"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One switch that hides every AI surface","what":"Panel, ghost text, idea mode, decision flow and the scan's generate half all go.","gains":["Strongest version of the promise, stateable in one line"],"costs":["A first-run user with it on cannot generate","With it on, the product is the editor plus the highlight"],"system":"One flag, checked in five places.","screens":"Settings gains a row; the launcher loses generator cards.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Hide the panel and ghost text only; generation stays reachable","gains":["Keeps the launcher useful with AI mostly off"],"costs":["The switch does not mean what the positioning implies","That gap is exactly what gets screenshotted"],"system":"One setting, narrower scope.","screens":"A settings row with a qualifier.","money":"None.","what":"Hide the panel and ghost text only; generation stays reachable. The launcher stays useful, and the switch does not mean what the positioning says it means."},{"k":"c","label":"Per-surface toggles instead of one switch","gains":["Precise control for the user who wants some"],"costs":["Five booleans become ten, an outright fail on the feature test","No single sentence for the positioning"],"system":"Five settings and their persistence.","screens":"Settings grows a section.","money":"None.","what":"A toggle per surface instead of one switch. Precise, and five booleans become ten, which the plan's feature test fails outright, with no sentence for the page."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop the switch from the positioning until it is built","gains":["Nothing false on the page"],"costs":["Gives up the refusal three incumbents proved people demand","The lead-with-refusals section loses its sharpest item"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Take the switch off the page until it is built. Nothing false in the copy, and the sharpest refusal in the positioning, one three incumbents proved people want, goes quiet."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["All-or-nothing is cheapest to build and the only version stateable in one line.","Its scope shrinks if ghost text is deleted, so settle that deletion first."],"flip":"If first-run users reach idea mode before anything else, the switch must spare the launcher.","linked":["F21","F27","F8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:202","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382","src/modules/editor/presentation/editor-settings.ts:12","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q6-feature-discipline.md"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A switch that hides AI is a promise made in the positioning. Say what it hides.","recWhy":"The positioning leads with a switch that turns every AI surface off, and no code for it exists, and nobody has written down what off means. All or nothing is the only version that can be said in one sentence, which is the only version a positioning line can carry, and it is also the cheapest to build. With it on, the product is the editor and the highlight, which is exactly the Phase 0 front door: paste, clean up, export, nothing sent anywhere. Its scope shrinks if ghost text is deleted under F21, so settle that first. Per-surface toggles are a settings surface the plan's own five-gate test fails. Hiding only the panel leaves a gap between the switch and the promise, and that gap is what gets screenshotted."},{"id":"F18","cat":"Features","sub":"Cut and inherited","weight":"medium","q":"Do bookmarks and graph view get deleted, demoted to the palette, or left as they are?","lede":"Both are shipped, both unchosen, and one holds a third of the product's global hotkeys.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Inherited","meta":"41 of 43 editor files","via":"fork","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Visible now","meta":"bookmark control, mod+g","via":"no decision","tone":"warn","current":true},{"label":"Palette only","meta":"no pixel, no hotkey","via":"demote","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Deleted","meta":"685 + 33 lines","via":"fork call","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"The plan tells a reader bookmarks do not exist; the status bar disagrees.","caption":"The four states an inherited surface can be in"},"stakes":"The published plan describes a product that differs from the build in reachable controls.","state":["The gap register lists bookmarks under what the product should not have.","`useBookmarks` is wired into the editor pane.","Graph view is 685 lines and holds one of three global hotkeys."],"tension":["Deleting working code to satisfy a one-word entry on a refusal list.","A visible control and a global hotkey compete for the attention the highlight needs.","Every deletion is a future merge conflict with the parent repo, sgnk-md."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Bookmarks: plan against code","items":[["Plan's position","Should not have, confidence E","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:81"],["Code","useBookmarks, 33 lines, wired into EditorPane","src/modules/editor/presentation/bookmarks.ts; EditorPane.tsx:53"],["Storage key","sgnk-md-bookmarks","src/modules/editor/presentation/bookmarks.ts:31"],["Same leak elsewhere","sgnk-md-editor-settings; src-tauri productName sgnk-md, id ai.sgnk.md","editor-settings.ts:46; PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:222"],["Evidence in the earlier inventory","Bear pin-notes [fetched]; size column reads ∅","PRODUCT.md:659, row 6"],["Files in editor plus app-shell","43, of which 41 byte-identical to sgnk-md","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Graph view, run through the admission test","items":[["Feature requests mentioning it","0 of 89","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.2 row 6"],["Internal graph size","3,848 nodes","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.2 row 6"],["Transclusions it produced","45, all of them documentation of the feature","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.2 row 6"],["Verdict of that test","Fails gate 1; demote to Invoked, never marketed; delete if mentions stay at 0 for two quarters","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.2 row 6"],["In the plan's feature build-cost table","Absent","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55-67"],["Built","Yes, 685 lines, force-graph, tag-coloured, hover-focus","src/modules/graph/; FEATURE-GAP-REPORT.md:41"],["Global hotkeys in the whole app","3: mod+k, mod+p, mod+g","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/KnowledgeUI.tsx:56-58"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where attention is supposed to go on the editor","cols":["Element","Position in the predicted scan path"],"rows":[["Top bar and left side panel: tabs, tree, unreviewed bars","First"],["Document heading and opening lines","Second"],["Right side panel","Last"],["AI panel","Off the path, collapsed"],["Graph view","Not in the plan's scan model at all"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete both surfaces and their stores","gains":["The shipped product matches the plan exactly"],"costs":["Deletes 685 plus 33 lines of working code","Graph view is the cheapest signal for an Obsidian migrant"],"system":"Two modules removed; divergence from sgnk-md grows.","screens":"A status-bar control and a hotkey go.","money":"None.","what":"Delete bookmarks and graph view and their stores. The product matches the plan exactly; 685 plus 33 lines of working code go, and every deletion is a merge conflict with sgnk-md."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep both, remove the refusal line, and rename the storage keys","gains":["No deletion risk, and the old sgnk-md name leaves storage"],"costs":["The refusal list gets shorter and less credible","Restraint stops being visibly true at rest"],"system":"Storage key rename only.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both, remove the refusal line, rename the storage keys. No deletion risk, a shorter and less credible refusal list, and restraint stops being visibly true at rest."},{"k":"c","label":"Demote both to the command palette and release the hotkey","gains":["Keeps the code, frees screen space and a hotkey"],"costs":["A third state the refusal list has no word for","They stay as maintenance nobody has budgeted"],"system":"Routing change; `mod+g` freed.","screens":"Two palette entries replace a control.","money":"None.","what":"Move bookmarks and graph view behind the command palette and free the hotkey. The code stays, the idle screen clears, and both are one keystroke of typing away."},{"k":"d","label":"Leave both until the fork decision is made","gains":["One decision settles many instances at once"],"costs":["The published plan keeps describing a different product","The hotkey stays spent"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Leave both until the fork decision is made. One decision settles many instances, and the published plan keeps describing a product the code is not."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Demotion is reversible: a routing change that frees a hotkey and clears the idle screen.","The plan's feature test already reached this verdict for graph view: 0 of 89 requests."],"flip":"If the fork decision on sgnk-md goes to merge rather than fork-and-own, deletion becomes free and option a wins.","linked":["F21"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:81","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:222","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT.md:659","src/modules/editor/presentation/bookmarks.ts:31","src/modules/editor/presentation/EditorPane.tsx:53","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:413","docs/FEATURE-GAP-REPORT.md:41","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q6-feature-discipline.md","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/KnowledgeUI.tsx:58","merged: F29"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Bookmarks and the graph sit in the right rail the MVP is building.","recWhy":"Both surfaces ship, neither was chosen, and graph view holds one of three global hotkeys in a product whose first screen has a nine-control budget. Demotion to the command palette is the reversible move: the code stays, the hotkey is freed for the highlight, the idle screen clears, and an Obsidian migrant who wants the graph can still find it by typing. Deleting 718 lines of working code to satisfy one word on a refusal list is a merge conflict with the parent repository forever, for no capability gained. The plan's own feature test reached the same verdict on graph view: zero of 89 requests. Q5 carries this as bookmarks and graph to the palette. If FF3 goes to merge rather than freeze, deletion becomes free and a wins."},{"id":"F25","cat":"Features","sub":"Search","weight":"medium","q":"Where does the search index live, given our no-document-bytes rule forbids the server index the plan asks for?","lede":"Two sections of the plan prescribe opposite fixes for the same measured problem.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Repo bytes","items":["Fetched in the browser with the user's token"],"tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Index","items":["Per open file, lazily","Client worker over the whole repo","Server index, closed off by the byte path"],"side":"the choice","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Query","items":["Title, tag and folder scopes over MiniSearch today"],"tone":"sunk"}],"note":"77 MB is parsed per cold start today, on the inherited implementation.","caption":"Which layer holds the index, and what that costs the promise"},"stakes":"Search over unopened files is most of a stranger's vault on day one.","state":["The technical plan prescribes server full-text search on web and a local index on desktop.","The week-0 decision to keep document bytes off our server rules out a server index.","Search is one word inside the editor row of the feature table."],"tension":["The 77 MB figure was measured against an implementation that loads the whole snapshot.","A lazily built per-file index is slow or incomplete on a cold vault.","A server index reopens hosting and the legal section with it."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Search, as measured and as prescribed","items":[["Parsed per cold start today","77 MB","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233"],["Section 9's fix","Server full-text search in the web app; local index on desktop","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233"],["Week-0 byte path's fix","Index lazily per open file in the client; server index off the table","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:44, :50"],["Current implementation","MiniSearch over the vault snapshot with title, tag and folder scopes","docs/FEATURE-GAP-REPORT.md:45"],["Feature row for search","None; one word inside the editor","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55"],["Published performance measures promised","Time to first keystroke cold; typing latency on a 10,000-word document","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Client-only, lazily indexed per open file","gains":["Cheapest to build and to reason about"],"costs":["Unopened files are unsearchable, most of a new vault","A visible regression against every competitor"],"system":"Index on open; no snapshot load.","screens":"Results carry a scope caveat.","money":"None.","what":"Index only files as they are opened, on the client. Cheapest to build, and most of a new vault is unsearchable, which every competitor does better."},{"k":"b","label":"Client index built in a worker over the whole repo, cached in IndexedDB","gains":["Full search and the promise both hold"],"costs":["First connect on a large repo is slow","Browser memory bounds it, and web is the trial"],"system":"A worker, persistence, an invalidation rule.","screens":"A first-connect progress state.","money":"None.","what":"Build the whole-repo index in a web worker on the client and cache it in IndexedDB. Full search and the no-bytes promise both hold; first connect on a big repo is slow."},{"k":"c","label":"Server index as the technical plan says, with the promise narrowed","gains":["Best search on any repo size"],"costs":["Puts document text on our server, reopening hosting","The legal section changes with it"],"system":"An index service and retention rules.","screens":"No visible difference.","money":"Infrastructure cost the plan assumes away.","what":"Index on our server, as the technical plan says, and narrow the promise. Best search at any size, and document text lands on our server, which reopens hosting and the legal section."},{"k":"d","label":"Full search on desktop only; the web app searches open files","gains":["No worker, no server"],"costs":["Makes the trial visibly worse than the download","The web app is where a stranger meets us"],"system":"Two implementations.","screens":"A stated limitation in the web app.","money":"None.","what":"Full search on desktop only; the web app searches open files. No worker and no server, and the trial a stranger meets is visibly worse than the download."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A worker-built client index keeps the promise and still gives whole-vault search.","The technical plan's server-search line must be corrected either way; a later decision overrides it."],"flip":"If first-connect indexing measures badly on a repo this size, option a is the fallback.","linked":["AC1","F19"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:44","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:50","docs/FEATURE-GAP-REPORT.md:45"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The launcher has search, so where the index lives is a first build question.","recWhy":"Two sections of the plan prescribe opposite fixes for the same measured problem: the technical plan says a server index, the week-zero decision says no document bytes on our server, and the later decision wins. A worker-built client index over the whole repository, cached in IndexedDB, keeps the promise and still searches every file. First connect on a large repository will be slow and browser memory bounds it, and the web is where a stranger meets us, so that cost is real and has to be measured rather than assumed. Q9 in the final set carries this as the client-side index. The 77 MB figure that scared people was measured against an implementation that loads the whole snapshot at once, which a worker does not. If first-connect indexing measures badly on a repository the size of this one, a is the fallback and it should say so on the page.","dupes":{"dropped":["AC15"],"areas":["Access, offline & install"],"why":"Access, offline & install also recommended a client-side index, but built lazily as files are opened, which is option a here, not the whole-repo worker index. Where the browser gets the bytes from is decided on that area's web byte-path card.","agree":false}},{"id":"F27","cat":"Features","sub":"Onboarding","weight":"medium","q":"Are the launcher's four templates files or generator prompts, and does Handover survive having no standard?","lede":"The launcher cards are the first thing a new user sees, and nobody has said what a click does.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"The launcher","panes":[{"label":"The four cards","w":2,"rows":[{"label":"Blank document","meta":"file"},{"label":"Spec","meta":"file"},{"label":"Decision record","meta":"MADR"},{"label":"Handover","meta":"no published standard","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Changelog","meta":"Keep a Changelog 1.1.0"}]},{"label":"If they are prompts","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Needs a key","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Needs a network","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Dead with AI hidden","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"First-run promise: open a folder, edit in thirty seconds, no account.","caption":"Two different products behind the same four cards"},"stakes":"A stranger with no key opens the product and finds four dead cards.","state":["The plan's launcher mockup shows blank, four templates, and existing edits sorted unreviewed-first.","The prototype draws Blank, Spec, Decision record, Handover and Changelog.","Three of the four cards cite a published standard; Handover cites none."],"tension":["A template file needs no model, key or network; a generator needs all three.","The generator is the feature the founders say they tested.","Two card types in one row is more than a first session carries."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two possible meanings of a template card","cols":["","Template file","Generator prompt (idea mode)"],"rows":[["Needs a model","No","Yes"],["Needs the user's key","No","Yes"],["Works with hide-all-AI on","Yes","No"],["Works offline in the desktop app","Yes","No"],["Build cost","Near zero, four .md files","Already built, needs structured output (2d) and BYO key (3d)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the record says about the document types","items":[["Recurring types named","spec, decision record, handover, changelog","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:95"],["What the prototype draws","Blank document, Spec, Decision record (MADR), Handover, Changelog (Keep a Changelog 1.1.0)","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html, S0 launcher"],["Standards named in the rendering rules","MADR and Nygard decision records; RFC 7322; Keep a Changelog 1.1.0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:525"],["No standard body","Runbooks and PRDs; a structure is offered and labelled as such","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:526"],["Handover standard","None named anywhere in the plan","measured: PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:525-526"],["Idea mode's five kinds already coded","prd, frd, brd, product-note, spec","src/modules/ai/application/doc-prompts.ts:10"],["Onboarding evidence","Templates in the vault, not a gallery","r20-q6-feature-discipline.md, 103.3"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Four template files, with Handover labelled a house structure","what":"Clicking a card writes a markdown file into the folder and opens it.","gains":["Works offline, with no key, and with AI hidden"],"costs":["First run never shows the AI capability the founders tested","A file is a thin thing to call a template"],"system":"Four `.md` files and a copy action.","screens":"The launcher as drawn, one label changed.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Four generator prompts running idea mode","what":"Clicking a card generates a document from the repo and the user's answers.","gains":["Every card produces something specific to this repo"],"costs":["A stranger with no key sees four dead cards","Bring-your-own key has to land before the pilot"],"system":"Launcher wired to the generate route.","screens":"Launcher cards gain a key state.","money":"Model cost lands on the first click."},{"k":"c","label":"Two of each, with the generator cards visibly requiring a key","gains":["Nothing is dead for an unkeyed user"],"costs":["Four cards become two concepts in a first session","The most explaining for the least commitment"],"system":"Two behaviours behind one card component.","screens":"The launcher gains a second card style.","money":"None.","what":"Two template files and two generator cards that visibly need a key. Nothing is dead for an unkeyed user, and four cards become two concepts in a first session."},{"k":"d","label":"Swap Handover for AGENTS.md","what":"Every card cites something external, reusing the docs scan's missing-doc template.","gains":["Reuses work the scan already needs"],"costs":["Drops the one recurring type no other feature covers","Handover is the type this studio writes most"],"system":"Shares a template with the docs scan.","screens":"One card renamed.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Only template files keep the first-run promise for a user with no key.","Handover must be labelled an offered structure, as the rendering rules require."],"flip":"If bring-your-own key lands early and first-run users reach for generation, option c becomes affordable.","linked":["F10","F5","F9"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:239","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:525","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:526","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:95","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html","src/modules/ai/application/doc-prompts.ts:10","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q6-feature-discipline.md"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The launcher shows four templates. What a template is decides the first screen.","recWhy":"The launcher cards are the first thing a stranger sees and nobody had said what clicking one does. A template file needs no model, no key and no network: click, and a markdown file lands in the folder and opens. A generator needs all three, and a stranger with no key would see four dead cards. Q4 and Q10 together settle it: the first thirty seconds work with nothing configured, and the user's own key is a hard gate before any AI route, so on first run the cards have to be files. Three of the four cite a published standard; Handover cites none, because it is this studio's own structure, and the rendering rules require it to be labelled as an offered structure rather than a standard. If the key lands early and first-run users reach for generation, two of each becomes affordable."},{"id":"F26","cat":"Features","sub":"Agent writes","weight":"high","q":"Do we ship a control over which files an agent may write, or refuse the job in writing?","lede":"The product exists to adjudicate agent writes and cannot say which files an agent may touch.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Agent writes anywhere in the repo","tone":"warn"},{"label":"No scope rule exists","note":"0 hits across 163 inventory rows","tone":"stop"},{"label":"frontmatter sees it afterwards","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Panel groups unreviewed by path","tone":"accent"},{"label":"An MCP write path could refuse","note":"binds only agents that use it","tone":"sunk","dashed":true}],"note":"Review after the fact is not the same as scope before the fact.","caption":"Where a scope rule could live, and what exists instead"},"stakes":"A review product that cannot scope a write invites the obvious question in the first demo.","state":["Nothing in the feature table mentions write scope; the 163-row inventory returns zero.","The security rules cover plugins, code execution and ambient access, not scope.","The only place our rule could live is on writes through our MCP server."],"tension":["Prevention requires a write path we control, and we may never have one.","Noticing requires a group-by in a panel already being built.","The omission is larger than several rows listed with no evidence at all."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Scope control, searched for","items":[["Feature rows in the inventory","163","PRODUCT.md:655-862"],["Rows mentioning allowlist, denylist, scope control, which files, permission to write or gitignore","0","r26-c4-features.md, finding R1"],["Rows in the plan's feature build-cost table mentioning it","0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55-67"],["Rows in the inventory whose evidence column reads NONE","36 of 163 (22.1%)","r26-c4-features.md, finding F1"],["Where a scope rule would naturally live","The MCP server, 6 days, in the build after the pilot, untested","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the product controls today, and what it does not","cols":["Control","Present"],"rows":[["See what changed since a person last read it (review state)","Planned, in the pilot"],["Revert one span (review state)","Planned, in the pilot"],["Refuse an ambiguous splice (engine)","Partly: non-locality is listed as an improvement still to make (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510)"],["Say which paths an agent may write","Nowhere in the plan or the inventory"],["Ceilings per file (4 MB, 200,000 lines)","Stated in the operating rules (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:551)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship a repo-level write-scope file enforced at the MCP write path","gains":["The scope story becomes real where writes pass through us"],"costs":["Binds only agents using our server, itself untested","A control a user can walk around reads worse than none"],"system":"A scope file, a parser, a refusal path.","screens":"A scope editor, or a hand-written file.","money":"None.","what":"A write-scope file in the repo, enforced where writes pass through our MCP server. Real where it applies; it binds only agents that use our server, and that path is untested."},{"k":"b","label":"Refuse it in writing: we review writes, we do not police them","gains":["Honest, free, consistent with the no-ambient-access posture"],"costs":["A prospect may ask why a review product cannot scope","It concedes the question rather than answering it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Refuse it in writing: we review writes, we do not police them. Honest, free, and a prospect may ask why a review product cannot say which files an agent may touch."},{"k":"c","label":"Surface it read-only: group unreviewed spans by path","gains":["Close to zero cost inside a panel already funded"],"costs":["Notices after the fact rather than preventing","Weaker than a permissions system sounds"],"system":"A group-by in the review panel.","screens":"The review panel gains a path grouping.","money":"None.","what":"Group unreviewed spans by path, read-only, in the panel already being built. Close to zero cost; it notices after the fact rather than preventing."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer until the agent-write-authority decision is settled","gains":["No design committed to the wrong model"],"costs":["The gap stays in the plan while outsiders read it","The kind of omission a reviewer finds first"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Defer until the agent-write-authority decision is settled. No design for the wrong model, and the gap stays visible in the plan to anyone reading it."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Grouping unreviewed spans by path answers the real question at close to zero cost.","It leaves option a open if the week-one MCP package shows agents will write through us."],"flip":"If twenty team conversations name write scope as the reason they would pay, option a becomes the offer.","linked":["F1","FF12"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:551","docs/PRODUCT.md:655","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c4-features.md"],"when":"beta","whenWhy":"Controlling what an agent may write belongs with the kit, once the kit is real.","recWhy":"The product exists to adjudicate what agents write and cannot say which files an agent may touch, and that omission is larger than several rows that carry no evidence at all. Prevention needs a write path we control, which is the MCP server, which is after the pilot and untested; a control a user can walk around reads worse than none. Noticing needs only a group-by in a panel that is already funded, and on instruction files it is exactly the Phase 1 view: here are the files agents obey, here is which one an agent changed, grouped by where it lives. That answers the question a team actually asks, which is what happened, at close to zero cost, and it leaves the enforcing version open for the day agents write through us. If the twenty name write scope as the reason they would pay, a becomes the offer."},{"id":"F13","cat":"Features","sub":"Teams","weight":"medium","q":"Do comments live only in the review file, or do we also export an interchange format?","lede":"A comment in the review file kept beside the document is invisible to anyone not running frontmatter, which is most reviewers.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"spec.md and its separate review file","lines":[{"t":"The API returns 200 on failure.","mark":"span"},{"t":"","note":"three ways to attach a comment"},{"t":".frontmatter/review.jsonl","mark":"sidecar","note":"renders nowhere, reads everywhere"},{"t":"{>>needs a source<<}","mark":"critic","note":"toolkit last committed 2021-02-27"},{"t":"> [!comment] needs a source","mark":"in-file","note":"writes into the user's file"}],"note":"The oldest constraint: nothing is added to markdown that breaks it elsewhere.","caption":"Three carriers for one comment, and what each costs the file"},"stakes":"Put comments in the file and the product breaks its own rule in its own feature.","state":["The plan puts comments in the review file, never in the document.","The rendering rules repeat it: review state and comments go in the review file.","An earlier plan imported and exported CriticMarkup and pandoc track-changes."],"tension":["Review-file-only comments reproduce the isolation we criticise SpecKit Companion for.","criticmarkup.com is parked and the toolkit has been still since 2021.","Visible on GitHub means writing into the user's file, refused everywhere else."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The interchange option, measured","items":[["criticmarkup.com","Parked domain","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3306, fetched"],["CriticMarkup/CriticMarkup-toolkit","848 stars, last commit 2021-02-27","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3306, fetched"],["Google Docs public API","Cannot create suggestions at all","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:178, fetched"],["The record's own earlier verdict","Materialise to JSON always; CriticMarkup optionally and only as a byte format we own","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3306"],["An earlier plan's position","CriticMarkup plus pandoc --track-changes, both directions","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1043"],["Current plan's position","Sidecar only, never in the file","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:64"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each carrier costs the user","cols":["Carrier","Renders on GitHub","Readable without frontmatter","Survives a byte-exact edit"],"rows":[["Sidecar .frontmatter/","Not shown","Yes, plain JSONL","Yes"],["CriticMarkup in the file","Renders as literal braces","Yes, ugly","The comment is bytes, so edits move it"],["HTML comment in the file","Hidden","Yes","Same"],["Callout block in the file","Renders as a callout","Yes","Same"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Separate file only, as specified","what":"Comments live in `.frontmatter/`, plain text, anchored by byte range and hash.","gains":["Every touched file still renders everywhere"],"costs":["Invisible to a reviewer who opens the file on GitHub","Reproduces the lock-in objection we raise about others"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Comments in the review panel and hover card.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Separate file as the source of truth, with an export to CriticMarkup on demand","gains":["Answers the lock-in objection without touching the file"],"costs":["Exports into a format still since 2021","One more output to keep correct"],"system":"A serialiser over the review file.","screens":"An entry in the export menu.","money":"None.","what":"Comments live in the review file beside the document and export to CriticMarkup on demand. The file is never touched; the export answers the lock-in objection."},{"k":"c","label":"Separate file plus an optional in-file callout for open comments","gains":["Comments become visible where reviewers already are"],"costs":["Writes into the user's file, refused everywhere else","The exception must enter the rendering rules"],"system":"A writer path that adds bytes on purpose.","screens":"A materialise control per comment.","money":"None.","what":"Comments in the review file, plus an optional callout written into the document for open ones. Visible where reviewers already read, and it writes into a file we promised never to write."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer comments to the third build and ship shared read-state alone","gains":["Removes the decision and halves the team build"],"costs":["Teams asking for comments get nothing","Read state alone is what GitHub bundles at $4"],"system":"No comment schema.","screens":"The review panel without a comment thread.","money":"Thins the team offer further.","what":"Defer comments to a later build and ship shared read-state alone. Halves the team build, and teams asking for comments get what GitHub already bundles at $4."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Review-file-only is the right default. The export is cheap insurance that never touches the file.","Treat CriticMarkup as a byte format we own, as the project record concluded."],"flip":"If twenty team conversations say reviewers will not leave GitHub, option c becomes worth pricing.","linked":[],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:64","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:528","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:178","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3306","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1043"],"when":"beta","whenWhy":"Comments are a beta slot in the plan, and the format question comes with them.","recWhy":"A comment kept in a file beside the document is invisible to anyone who opens the document on GitHub, which is most reviewers, and that reproduces the isolation this product criticises in SpecKit Companion. Writing the comment into the document is refused everywhere else in the plan, because bytes we did not touch stay untouched, and an in-file callout renders as a callout in every other tool. The review file as the source of truth with an export to CriticMarkup on demand keeps the promise and answers the objection. CriticMarkup itself has been still since 2021 and its site is parked, so treat it as a byte format we own and maintain, as the record concluded. Q11 puts share-and-comment in Phase 2; this card decides where those comments live when it arrives."},{"id":"F1","cat":"Features","sub":"Review state","weight":"critical","q":"Do we prove the review anchor survives another tool's write before spending eighteen days on the store?","lede":"Review state, the mark on every span you have not read, is the pilot's largest item. Its anchor is untested on the write it exists for.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Agent rewrites the file","note":"Claude Code, Cursor, git checkout","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Bytes move, hashes change","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"The journal never saw it","note":"git, other editors and agents bypass it","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Splice locator relocates spans","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Highlight lands on the right span","tone":"good"},{"label":"Highlight is wrong in its own demo","tone":"stop","dashed":true}],"note":"Never run. This is the only scenario the product exists for.","caption":"The out-of-band write, and the two ways the anchor can end"},"stakes":"If relocation fails on an agent's rewrite, the highlight is wrong exactly when the demo needs it right.","state":["Store, highlight, revert and panel: 18 days, all in the pilot.","The anchor is a byte range plus a content hash, not the editor's write log.","No store exists in `src/` today. `.frontmatter/` appears only in the certificate command's contract."],"tension":["Eighteen days at the measured 1.21 engineering days per week is about fifteen calendar weeks.","Whether the anchor survives another tool's write is still open after the 2026-09-08 review.","Paragraph anchors relocate better but lose span precision; git blob anchors lose the working tree."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Review state as specified, against the measured rate","items":[["Store, rendering, revert, panel","18 engineering days","Exhibit 1, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week","1.21","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227, 10 of 58 calendar days touched src/, src-tauri/ or test/"],["Review state alone at that rate","about 15 calendar weeks","derived: 18 ÷ 1.21"],["Evidence class in the plan's own table","Untested","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188"],["Review-state store in src/ today","None: no .frontmatter/review.jsonl writer, no per-span hash store, no review module","measured: src/modules/ holds ai, ai-tools, app-shell, auth, drafts, editor, export, graph, mdmax, preview, repository, share, vault"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Purpose-built markdown-review extensions, VS Code Marketplace, 2026-09-08","note":"PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24. mddiff, published 2026-06-03, is this product's own sentence already shipped.","data":[["All 13 extensions, summed",1217],["Largest single extension",400],["mddiff",74]],"unit":"installs"},{"type":"bars","title":"Obsidian forum, one population, one metric, 2026-09-08","note":"PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410. The rendering bug the plan treats as a channel, against the closest existing request for review state.","data":[["Nested code block rendering bug",501],["Suggestion Mode like Google Docs/Word",3],["Tracking changes within notes",1],["List of changes since last view",0]],"unit":"likes"},{"type":"stat","title":"What is built and what is assumed","items":[["Review-state store in src/ today","None: .frontmatter/ appears only in src/modules/mdmax/domain/cert-contract.ts, not as a store","measured: grep for review.jsonl / sidecar / .frontmatter/ over src/"],["Anchor design","byte range + content hash, relocated by the splice locator","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56"],["Tested against an agent write","No","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146, listed as still open after round 2"],["Nearest shipped pattern","SpecKit Companion .spec-context.json, 9,367 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Writers that bypass the journal, per the plan's own note about the review file","cols":["Writer","Reaches the file","Reaches the journal"],"rows":[["frontmatter's own splice","Yes","Yes"],["The user's agent (Claude Code, Cursor)","Yes","No"],["git checkout, merge, rebase","Yes","No"],["Any other editor","Yes","No"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Prove relocation on a real agent rewrite first, then commit the eighteen days","gains":["Answers the load-bearing assumption for a sixth of the cost"],"costs":["Delays the first visible highlight by two to three days","The prototype becomes the product; the store goes unbudgeted"],"system":"Relocation, tested on files we did not write.","screens":"None. A script and a diff.","money":"None.","what":"Spend two or three days proving a review mark survives a real agent rewrite before the eighteen-day store is built. If it does not survive, the store was never worth building."},{"k":"b","label":"Build store and rendering now (11d), treat relocation failures as a later fix","gains":["Highlight on screen seven engineering days earlier"],"costs":["A structural failure surfaces at week 9, eleven days sunk","Revert stays a git operation; the demo's one-key revert is missing"],"system":"Store and rendering on an unproven locator.","screens":"Editor screen: highlight and unreviewed bars.","money":"None.","what":"Build the store and the rendering now, eleven days, and treat a mark that loses its place as a later fix. Highlight on screen sooner; a structural failure surfaces at week nine."},{"k":"c","label":"Narrow the anchor to whole paragraphs","gains":["Fewer false relocations on ordinary agent rewrites","Smaller store, simpler rendering"],"costs":["Gives up span precision, the half Google charges for","Weakens attribution"],"system":"The span table becomes a paragraph table.","screens":"Hover card loses byte ranges.","money":"Drops the granularity a comparable is paid for.","what":"Narrow the anchor to whole paragraphs. Fewer false relocations on ordinary rewrites, and the span-level precision that attribution and the paid tier rest on goes."},{"k":"d","label":"Demote review state to a feature and lead with spec-kit self-certification","what":"The highlight still ships; it stops being the reason the product exists.","gains":["Frees eighteen days for the engine and the scan","Honest to the 2026-09-08 review result"],"costs":["The plan then has no headline","Most other rows were justified by this loop"],"system":"Store still needed, smaller and unrushed.","screens":"The review panel stops being the front door.","money":"Pro loses the capability its price anchored to."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["What survived the 2026-09-08 review is review across tools on a working tree, and only a tool-agnostic anchor delivers that.","It costs two to three days against eighteen, on a milestone already set for weeks 3 to 4."],"flip":"If relocation is unreliable on ordinary agent rewrites, option c is the honest retreat and the span-level pitch goes.","linked":["E22","F26","F3","F33","P13","PR1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:363","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:124","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","merged: F2"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The anchor test protects review state, which is deferred.","recWhy":"Eighteen days at the measured rate is about fifteen calendar weeks, and every one of them rests on an assumption nobody has tested. That a mark anchored to a byte range and a content hash finds its span again after another tool rewrote the file. If it does not, the store renders history rather than state. Two or three days on a real agent rewrite settles it for a sixth of the cost, and the milestone is already set for the start of Phase 1. On instruction files the question is sharper still, because those files are rewritten by agents by design, and a review mark that cannot survive that is a review mark on the wrong product. Option c is the honest retreat if relocation fails: paragraphs relocate better and the span-level claim goes with them.","dupes":{"dropped":["R2"],"areas":["Research & evidence"],"why":"Research & evidence asked only when to run the test, and recommended running it in week 0 and making it the demo. That matches option a here: prove the anchor survives a real agent rewrite before committing the eighteen days.","agree":true}},{"id":"F3","cat":"Features","sub":"Attribution","weight":"critical","q":"Do we reopen attribution as a paid claim, given no platform has shipped span-level authorship?","lede":"Attribution, who or what wrote each span, is the only one of our four claims nobody occupies. Its demotion used the wrong comparison.","visual":{"kind":"matrix","x":["file level","span level"],"y":["free","paid"],"points":[{"label":"Google Docs blue dot","x":0.1,"y":0.05},{"label":"git-ai commit trailers","x":0.22,"y":0.05},{"label":"Workspace part-level authorship","x":0.62,"y":0.82},{"label":"iA Writer 8 authorship","x":0.72,"y":0.34},{"label":"claude-edits-scm, 88 installs","x":0.88,"y":0.05},{"label":"frontmatter attribution","x":0.95,"y":0.55,"us":true}],"note":"The only span-level implementation found anywhere has 88 installs.","caption":"Who marks authorship, at what granularity, and who charges"},"stakes":"Give this away and we hand over the one half a live comparable is paid for.","state":["It reads commit trailers and git-ai notes: 3 days, in the pilot, free.","The plan's vocabulary audit records it as demoted: shown when available, never the headline.","Most pilot hover cards would read \"prompt not recorded\" until the MCP server exists."],"tension":["An unoccupied claim is either an unmet need or a proven non-need.","Gating requires billing, and the pilot has none.","The gated half stays thin until writes carry a prompt, which needs MCP."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Authorship marking: what sold and what did not","cols":["Product","Form","Price","Result"],"rows":[["iA Writer 7 (2023)","Consumer authorship marking","Paid app","Obsidian port: 208 downloads; best VS Code extension: 366 installs"],["Google Docs","File-level blue dot","Free","Every tier"],["Google Workspace","See who changed a part of a document","Business Standard and above","Sold today, verified 2026-09-08"],["frontmatter, attribution as planned","Span-level, from trailers and notes","Free","Untested"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What attribution costs and where it sits","items":[["Build","3 engineering days (readers only)","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57"],["Stage","readers in the pilot, prompts via MCP in the build after it","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57"],["Price today","Free: Individual gets everything from the editor through file-scoped AI","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287"],["Pro tier price it would join","$4 to $5 per user per month","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288"],["What the pilot card usually says","prompt not recorded: no write path carries a prompt until the MCP server exists","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep attribution free and in the pilot as a hover card","gains":["No billing to build for the pilot"],"costs":["Gives away the half a live comparable charges for","Leaves the only unoccupied claim unsold"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Editor hover card, as drawn.","money":"None.","what":"Attribution stays free, as a hover card in the pilot. Nothing to bill for, and the half a live comparable charges for is given away."},{"k":"b","label":"Split it Google's way: file-level author free, span-level author paid","gains":["Copies a live priced split at our price band"],"costs":["Adds a gating surface while the pilot has no billing","The paid half is thin until MCP carries prompts"],"system":"Two readers plus an entitlement check.","screens":"Hover card gains a locked state.","money":"Puts a paid-elsewhere capability behind the seat price.","what":"Split it the way Google does: who touched the file is free, who wrote each span is paid. Copies a priced split at our band; the paid half stays thin until writes carry a prompt."},{"k":"c","label":"Lead with attribution and rebuild the pitch around it","gains":["The only claim four platforms did not absorb"],"costs":["Reverses a demotion made on measured evidence","Attribution is absent on most writes until MCP"],"system":"Attribution becomes the surface everything hangs off.","screens":"The editor leads with authorship.","money":"Reprices both tiers on an untested claim.","what":"Lead the pitch with attribution. The only claim four platforms did not absorb, and it reverses a demotion made on measured evidence while most writes still carry no author."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut attribution from the pilot and ship it with the MCP write path","gains":["Saves three days now"],"costs":["The pilot never sees attribution at all","Concedes the unoccupied claim for a quarter"],"system":"Attribution folds into the MCP work.","screens":"Hover card keeps time and byte range.","money":"None.","what":"Cut it from the pilot and ship it with the MCP write path later. Saves three days now, and the pilot never shows the one claim nobody occupies."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Google is the only company on record selling this, at span level, in our price band.","The demotion compared a consumer app in a personal document. Strike it from the vocabulary audit."],"flip":"If twenty team conversations say git blame solves authorship, keep attribution free and out of Pro.","linked":["D6","F1","F33","P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:139","https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=dfarkash.claude-edits-scm"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Attribution is after the pilot in the plan.","recWhy":"Attribution, which text a machine wrote and which a person did, is the one of the four claims that no platform has shipped at span level, and its demotion rested on iA Writer, a consumer app in a personal document. That was the wrong comparison. Google is the only company on record selling span-level authorship, from Business Standard, with file-level free, and that split sits in our price band. On instruction files it is the whole Team tier: what did an agent change in the file other agents obey. Copy the split. Free for the file, paid for the span, and accept that the paid half reads thin until the MCP path carries prompts, because a row that appears only when it has content never advertises an absence. If the twenty say git blame already answers authorship, keep it free and out of Pro.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK15","P4"],"areas":["Market & competition","Product & definition"],"why":"Market & competition and Product & definition both recommended option b here: coarse authorship free, span-level authorship paid. Market & competition drew the free line at commit granularity rather than file level. Making attribution the headline is also an option on the Product & definition card about the headline.","agree":true}},{"id":"F7","cat":"Features","sub":"Repo docs scan","weight":"high","q":"Does the docs scan ship its commodity half first, or the staleness heuristic that is the only novel check?","lede":"Ten days, and two of its three checks are already free in the editor the buyer has open.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"The repo docs scan","panes":[{"label":"Proposals","w":2,"rows":[{"label":"Stale section: doc older than the code it cites","meta":"4d, unspecified","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Broken link","meta":"free in VS Code, off by default","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Missing AGENTS.md or CHANGELOG","meta":"2d","tone":"neutral"}]},{"label":"What was read","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"41 markdown files","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"package.json scripts","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"git dates","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"zero source files","tone":"sunk"}]}],"note":"No model call in the scan. Generation reuses idea mode and the user's key.","caption":"The screen, and which of its three checks nobody else ships"},"stakes":"Six of ten days rebuild what the buyer's editor already ships and turns off by default.","state":["Staleness 4 days, missing-doc templates 2 days, the scan screen 4 days, all in the pilot.","Link checking already exists as `link-doctor`.","The scan is also the diagnostic that scopes a Zephyrus services engagement."],"tension":["The staleness rule is unwritten beyond doc-older-than-the-code-it-cites.","The evidence panel is most credible about a check nobody else runs.","False positives destroy a linter's trust, so VS Code ships its own off."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The three checks and who already does them free","cols":["Check","Incumbent","Reach","What frontmatter adds"],"rows":[["Broken links","VS Code built-in markdown link validation; marksman language server","Bundled with the editor, OFF by default","Proposals with review state"],["Prose and markdown lint","Vale; markdownlint","6,087 and 6,326 stars","Not attempted"],["Missing standard docs","readme-md-generator","11,131 stars, abandoned 2022","AGENTS.md and CHANGELOG templates"],["Stale section (doc older than the code it cites)","None found in the corpus","none","The actual differentiator, 4 days, unspecified"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Repo docs scan cost and class","items":[["Staleness heuristic","4 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Missing-doc templates","2 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Evidence side panel UI","4 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Model calls in the scan","Zero: git dates, package.json scripts, link resolution","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Evidence class","Founder direction","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Second role","The diagnostic that briefs a Zephyrus engagement","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:478"],["What the evidence side panel lists","41 markdown files, package.json scripts, git dates, zero source files","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:253"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship all ten days as specified","gains":["The scan screen is the prototype's most complete","It doubles as the services funnel"],"costs":["Six of ten days rebuild two commodity checks","No written rule for the novel half before coding"],"system":"Git dates, script parsing, link resolution.","screens":"The scan screen, complete.","money":"Feeds the fixed-scope engagement.","what":"Ship all ten days: staleness, missing-doc templates and the scan screen. The prototype's most complete screen, and six of the ten days rebuild checks the editor already has."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship links and missing docs first (6d), hold staleness for a written rule","gains":["Fastest visible screen, and most is already written"],"costs":["Demos as markdownlint with a nicer panel","Competes with a bundled check the incumbent disables"],"system":"Reuses link-doctor; no new heuristic.","screens":"Scan screen, no staleness panel.","money":"None.","what":"Links and missing docs first, six days, staleness held until the rule is written. Fastest screen, and it demos as markdownlint with a nicer panel."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship staleness first (4d plus UI) and cite VS Code for link checking","gains":["Puts the novel check in front of the first stranger"],"costs":["Loses the completeness that makes the panel persuasive","Pointing at a competitor inside our screen is awkward"],"system":"Git dates and code references only.","screens":"Scan screen with one proposal type.","money":"None.","what":"Staleness first, four days plus the panel, and point at VS Code for link checking. The novel check in front of the first stranger; awkward to cite a competitor in our screen."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut it from the pilot and keep it as the consultancy's diagnostic script","gains":["Ten days back for the review loop"],"costs":["Retention loses its one lever independent of AI use","A founder must run the funnel by hand"],"system":"No product code.","screens":"The scan screen goes.","money":"Keeps the engagement, loses the self-serve path.","what":"Cut it from the pilot and keep it as the consultancy's hand-run diagnostic. Ten days back, and retention loses its one lever that needs no key."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The evidence panel makes the screen credible, and it is most credible about a novel check.","Write the staleness rule as a rule before it is coded, then add links."],"flip":"If week-1 conversations name broken links as the pain and never staleness, invert to option b.","linked":["F31","F5","G18","P11"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:253","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:426","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:478","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:13331","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:13371","src/modules/ai/application/link-doctor.ts"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The docs scan is after the pilot.","recWhy":"Two of the scan's three checks, broken links and missing standard documents, are free in the editor the buyer already has open, and link checking exists here already as link-doctor. Staleness, a document older than the code it describes, is the one check nobody ships, and it is also the one with no written rule behind it yet. The evidence panel is what makes the screen credible, and it is most credible about a check nobody else runs. So write the staleness rule as a rule before a line of code, ship that with its panel, and cite the host for links rather than rebuilding them. The scan is also the diagnostic that scopes a services engagement, so the hand-run version earns money while the product version is built. If the first conversations name broken links as the pain and never staleness, invert to b.","dupes":{"dropped":["P19"],"areas":["Product & definition"],"why":"Product & definition recommended the opposite order, option b here: ship broken links and missing standard docs, and defer staleness. Its question also asked whether the scan is a product feature or the consultancy's diagnostic; selling services off the scan is a Go-to-market & channels card.","agree":false}},{"id":"F11","cat":"Features","sub":"Refactor","weight":"high","q":"Does vault-wide refactor move into the pilot as the one capability with a counted public vote?","lede":"The only feature in the plan whose demand was measured as votes on a specific request.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"a spec in the vault","lines":[{"t":"---","note":"frontmatter block"},{"t":"tags: [review-state]","mark":"rename"},{"t":"---"},{"t":"See [[review-state]] for the anchor.","mark":"rename"},{"t":"```md"},{"t":"tags: [review-state]","mark":"refuse","note":"inside a fence"},{"t":"```"},{"t":"...and review-state in prose.","mark":"refuse","note":"ambiguous, not a target"}],"note":"Two renames, two refusals, every hunk shown before it is written.","caption":"What a vault rename splices, and what it declines to guess"},"stakes":"The best-evidenced capability waits behind six features with weaker evidence.","state":["Rename a tag, heading or key across the vault: 8 days, after the pilot.","Its evidence is 86 likes on a request about links breaking on rename.","The engine exists; handling non-locality, where distant text changes a match's meaning, is still to do."],"tension":["Eight days are mostly multi-file apply and per-hunk review, reusing the review surface.","It depends on the fix that wires the engine behind every write.","If review state is the headline, the pilot should say one thing."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Vault-wide refactor's evidence against the pilot rows","items":[["Vault-wide refactor: broken links on rename","86 likes","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:63; DECIDE.md:105"],["Review state","Untested as a product","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188"],["Idea mode","Founder user test, no notes attached","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23"],["Decision flow","Founder user test, no notes attached","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23"],["Repo docs scan","Founder direction","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:61"],["Vault-wide refactor build","8 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:63"],["Vault-wide refactor stage today","the build after the pilot","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:63"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What vault-wide refactor needs that already exists","cols":["Requirement","Status"],"rows":[["Byte-range splice across a file","Built: mdmax, 3,614 lines across 13 files, byte-exactness measured on 8,513 real files (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:492, :498)"],["Refusal on fenced or ambiguous matches","Designed; non-locality is listed as an improvement still to make, not a shipped refusal (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510)"],["Multi-file apply with per-hunk review","Not built: this is the 8 days"],["Engine wired behind product writes","Not built: fix #4, 8 days (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:217)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave it in the build after the pilot as planned","gains":["The pilot says one thing to ten strangers"],"costs":["The best-evidenced capability waits behind six weaker ones","The engine's real strength is not in the pilot"],"system":"No change.","screens":"The refactor preview stays after the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"Leave vault-wide refactor in the build after the pilot, as planned. The pilot says one thing to its people; the best-evidenced capability waits behind six weaker ones."},{"k":"b","label":"Move it into the pilot and drop the decision flow to the build after the pilot","gains":["Trades a weaker evidence class for a stronger one","Reuses the review panel for hunks"],"costs":["The pilot grows by four days net","It still needs the engine-wiring fix first"],"system":"Multi-file apply that changes only the edited bytes.","screens":"The refactor preview enters the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"Move it into the pilot and push the decision flow out. Trades a weaker evidence class for a counted vote and reuses the review panel for hunks, at four net days more."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship the single-file half in the pilot, the vault-wide half in the build after it","gains":["Reuses the review surface with no new apply path"],"costs":["Not what the 86 likes asked for","It demos as find-and-replace with manners"],"system":"The same apply, on one file only.","screens":"A hunk list in the editor.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the single-file half in the pilot, the vault-wide half after. Reuses the review surface with no new apply path, and it is not what the 86 likes asked for."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship it first, before review state, as the pilot demo","gains":["Leads with the strongest evidence and the engine's strength"],"costs":["Abandons review state as the headline, a prior decision","The review surface is built either way"],"system":"Engine-first sequencing.","screens":"The refactor preview becomes the front door.","money":"None.","what":"Ship it first, before review state, as the pilot's demo. Leads with the engine's real strength, and abandons a headline that was already decided."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Phase 0 is the front door: paste, clean up, export, local only. Eight days on a refactor engine is not in it.","The counted vote still stands, so the row keeps its estimate and its stage in the build after the pilot."],"flip":"If the twenty pilot people ask for a vault-wide refactor unprompted, it moves forward and the paste path waits.","linked":["F1","F5"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:63","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:217","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:492","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510","docs/DECIDE.md:105","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Vault wide refactor is not in the first build.","recWhy":"Rename a tag or a heading across a vault is the only feature in the plan whose demand was counted as votes on a specific request, 86 of them, and the engine that does it exists. It still does not belong in Phase 0. Phase 0 is the front door, paste and clean up and export with nothing sent anywhere, judged by whether twenty strangers export something, and eight days on a multi-file apply path with per-hunk review is a different product from that. The recheck flipped this card's recommendation for exactly that reason, and the counted vote keeps its estimate and its stage in the build after. If the twenty ask for it unprompted, it moves forward and the paste path waits, because a counted request from a real user outranks a plan."},{"id":"F15","cat":"Features","sub":"Sync","weight":"high","q":"Does sync stay on the roadmap at fifteen-plus days, or become conflict surfacing over the user's own git?","lede":"Sync is the one price individuals are proven to pay, and the largest single build in the plan.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"The user's files","items":["A folder, or a git repo"],"tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Git","items":["Already syncs a repo, free, for the buyer we target"],"tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Conflict surfacing","items":["Splice journal, compare-and-swap, never a CRDT"],"side":"settled","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Sync service","items":["15+ days, two builds after the pilot","Obsidian sells this at about $4"],"tone":"warn"}],"note":"Zed staked the opposite architecture on CRDTs in public on 2026-09-01.","caption":"Which layer owns multi-device, and which is already free"},"stakes":"Fifteen days for an audience the product does not currently target.","state":["Sync is git merge, conflicts shown never guessed: 15-plus days, in the third stage, Max.","Obsidian Sync at about $4 is the one proven individual price.","The architecture is settled: git merge, a write log, and writes that only land if nobody changed the file first."],"tension":["For a repo user, git already syncs, free.","The measured demand comes from folder users the VS Code route abandons.","Zed Delta bet publicly on real-time merge of everyone's edits on 2026-09-01; our rebuttal is unwritten."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Sync, as the record measures it","items":[["Rank among loved attributes","#1","DECIDE.md:107"],["Rank among hated attributes","#3","DECIDE.md:107"],["Rank as a switching trigger","#1","DECIDE.md:107"],["Issues touching it","1,251 of 43,656","DECIDE.md:107"],["The proven individual price","About $4, Obsidian Sync","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:159"],["Sync build","15 or more engineering days, two builds after the pilot","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:66"],["Settled architecture, may not be reopened","git merge plus splice journal plus compare-and-swap, never a CRDT","DECIDE.md:171"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Who needs sync, and whether they already have it","cols":["User","Documents live in","Already synced"],"rows":[["Developer with a repo","git","Yes, free"],["Obsidian user with a vault","A folder","No, this is who pays $4"],["Web-app user on Chrome, local folder","A folder","No"],["Team, in the build after the pilot","A repo","Yes, plus the review file"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep it in the third build at fifteen-plus days as planned","gains":["Preserves the one individual price proven in the category"],"costs":["Fifteen days for an audience we do not target","The largest row and the last scheduled"],"system":"A sync service with its own storage.","screens":"Multi-device and conflict views.","money":"The only proven individual price.","what":"Keep sync at fifteen-plus days in the third build, as planned. Preserves the one individual price the category has proven, for an audience the plan does not target."},{"k":"b","label":"Cut it and state plainly that git is the sync","gains":["Removes the largest build in the plan"],"costs":["Gives up the only individual price in the category","Folder users get nothing across devices"],"system":"A git client and a watcher, already planned.","screens":"A commit and push affordance.","money":"No individual revenue line.","what":"Cut it and say plainly that git is the sync. Removes the largest build in the plan, gives up the only proven individual price, and folder users get nothing across devices."},{"k":"c","label":"Reframe it as conflict surfacing over the user's existing git","gains":["What the settled architecture already says, far smaller"],"costs":["It does not carry the $4 the category charges","The name sync leaves the roadmap"],"system":"A write that fails if the file changed, plus a review-file merge driver.","screens":"A conflict view already planned after the pilot.","money":"No new price; protects the team tier.","what":"Reframe it as conflict surfacing over the user's own git. What the settled architecture already describes, at a fraction of the build, without the word sync on the roadmap."},{"k":"d","label":"Hold until the first-buyer decision is made","gains":["No design committed to the wrong audience"],"costs":["A fifteen-day row with no owner stays on the roadmap","Our rebuttal to real-time merge stays unwritten while Zed publishes"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Hold until the first buyer is chosen. Nothing designed for the wrong audience, and a fifteen-day row with no owner stays on the roadmap while Zed publishes."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Conflict surfacing is what the settled architecture already says, at a fraction of the build.","It extends to folder users later without redesign."],"flip":"If the first buyer is the non-developer whose documents are not in a repo, schedule the fifteen days.","linked":["E35"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:66","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:159","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:366","docs/DECIDE.md:107","docs/DECIDE.md:171","https://zed.dev/blog/agentic-xanadu"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Sync is the paid tier, phase four.","recWhy":"The architecture is settled and it already says what sync is here: git merge, a write log, and writes that land only if nobody changed the file first, conflicts shown and never guessed. For a repository user git already syncs, free. What the fifteen-day row was buying was a separate sync store for folder users, who are the audience the VS Code route abandoned, and it carried the one price individuals are proven to pay, Obsidian Sync at about $4. Reframing it as conflict surfacing over the user's existing git is what the architecture says and a fraction of the build. Q13's Pro tier lists storage and sync, and this is what that sync means: hosted storage for the files, git for the merge, never a second store. The rebuttal to Zed Delta's real-time-merge bet stays unwritten while this waits, and E35 owns that.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK26"],"areas":["Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition recommended moving the merge, but not the transport, into the build after the pilot. That is the same choice as option c here: the user's git carries the files and we handle the conflicts. It adds a timing, the build after the pilot, which this option leaves open.","agree":true}},{"id":"F23","cat":"Features","sub":"Rendering","weight":"high","q":"Does nested-construct rendering become a feature row in our own editor, now that the plugin is cut?","lede":"The most-voted request in the only population with a vote has no owner, estimate or stage.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"Our own Live mode","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Additive Obsidian extension"},{"title":"Drop the claim"}],"rows":[{"label":"Serves the rendering bug with 501 likes","cells":["Yes","No, it serves review state","No"]},{"label":"Cost","cells":["Unknown; live preview is core rendering","Decoration over ranges in a separate file","Zero"]},{"label":"Fixes the three contradicting sections","cells":["Yes","Partly","Yes"]},{"label":"Re-entry into a vault opened daily","cells":["No","Yes","No"]},{"label":"Writes into the user's files","cells":["No","No","No"]}],"note":"Obsidian's Live Preview tree is depth-1 by construction, verified twice.","caption":"Three ways to answer the strongest demand signal in the corpus"},"stakes":"The plan states a rendering rule it has no work behind, in three places that disagree.","state":["The rendering rules say nested constructs render correctly, shipped first as the free plugin.","The go-to-market section cut that plugin as unbuildable; the tier table still lists it.","The feature table has no row for it."],"tension":["Live preview is core rendering, so the estimate is unknown until scoped.","501 likes against 0 for our headline, in the same forum.","A certificate saying your file renders wrong in our editor is indefensible."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Obsidian forum, one population, one metric, 2026-09-08","note":"PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410. The rendering bug against the closest existing requests for review state.","data":[["Nested code block rendering bug",501],["Suggestion Mode like Google Docs/Word",3],["Tracking changes within notes",1],["List of changes since last view",0]],"unit":"likes"},{"type":"table","title":"What three sections of the plan say about the same thing","cols":["Section","Statement"],"rows":[["Section 21 rendering rules (line 524)","Nested constructs render correctly, shipped first as the free plugin"],["Section 12 channels (line 304)","The free Obsidian plugin is CUT; the parse is unreachable"],["Exhibit 6 channels row (line 204)","Free Obsidian live-preview plugin, week 2; kill under 200 installs in 14 days"],["Exhibit 1 (lines 55-67)","No feature row for nested-construct rendering"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Make it a feature row in our own editor, with an estimate and a stage","gains":["The strongest counted demand in the corpus gets an owner"],"costs":["Core rendering, and the estimate is unknown","It competes with the review loop for the same weeks"],"system":"Our own CodeMirror parse, containment included.","screens":"Live mode changes; no new screen.","money":"None.","what":"Make nested-construct rendering a feature row in our own Live mode, with an estimate and a stage. The strongest counted demand in the corpus gets an owner; the estimate is unknown."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the additive Obsidian surface instead","what":"Review state as a CodeMirror extension over the review file's byte ranges, inside Obsidian.","gains":["Re-entry from a surface they open daily"],"costs":["It serves review state, so the 501 likes stay unserved","A second surface with no rendering claim"],"system":"A decoration layer over the review file.","screens":"An Obsidian pane, not one of ours.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Drop the rendering claim and fix the three contradicting sections","gains":["The document stops contradicting itself in three places"],"costs":["Gives up the only demand signal measured at this size","Live mode loses its most concrete differentiator"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Drop the rendering claim and fix the three sections that contradict each other. The document stops disagreeing with itself, and the one signal measured at this size goes unserved."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep it as an engine-only claim through the certificate","what":"The engine's certificate says how a construct renders elsewhere; our renderer makes no promise.","gains":["No renderer work"],"costs":["A rendering certificate from an editor that renders it wrong","The three sections still contradict each other"],"system":"Detector work only.","screens":"A certificate output.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["If the 501-against-0 finding counts at all, it has to appear as work.","Our CodeMirror Live mode is the one surface where we control the parse."],"flip":"If the fix needs a parser rewrite rather than containment, option c is honest and the claim goes.","linked":[],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","docs/DECIDE.md:106","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Nested rendering is editor polish for later.","recWhy":"Five hundred and one likes against zero, in the only forum where anyone votes, and the plan gives the request no owner, no estimate and no stage, while three sections of the plan contradict each other about whether we ship it at all. If that count means anything it has to appear as work. The plugin route is cut and cannot serve it anyway, because the parse it needs was verified unreachable twice. Our own CodeMirror Live mode is the one surface where we control the parse, and Live stays in the pilot under D8 and Q5, so the row belongs there. What the estimate turns out to be is unknown until someone scopes it; core rendering could be days or a parser rewrite. If it is the rewrite, c is honest and the claim goes, because a certificate that says your file renders wrong in our own editor is indefensible."},{"id":"F33","cat":"Features","sub":"Review state","weight":"high","q":"Is a span highlighted because it changed, or because a machine wrote it?","lede":"The general form of this feature is Almanac's. Almanac raised $45M and switched it off in January 2025.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2025-01-31","label":"Almanac shuts down","note":"read receipts, approvals, doc history","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2026-07-03","label":"VS Code merges review/unreview","note":"agents window, experimental, undocumented","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-24","label":"Antigravity ships Agent Edits","note":"Uncommitted and Branch panes","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-28","label":"Notion ships per-change approval","note":"agent edits approved one at a time","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-09-03","label":"Claude Code v2.1.260 diff panel","note":"opens itself, three scopes, no reviewed mark","tone":"warn"}],"note":"Everything dated 2026 ships the agent-scoped form free.","caption":"Nine weeks of absorption, and the corpse underneath the general form"},"stakes":"Highlight everything and we rebuild a feature with a $45M failure behind it.","state":["The feature highlights every span changed since a person last read it.","Attribution is a separate row, read only when the write carried it.","Nothing in the plan restricts the highlight to machine-authored spans."],"tension":["Highlighting everything is honest and cheap, and has a dead precedent behind it.","Scoping to agent writes needs attribution, which is unbuilt.","This repo alone holds 4,446,724 markdown words, a debt nobody clears."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Highlight every changed span, as specified","gains":["Simplest rule to explain and implement, with no dependency"],"costs":["The shape Almanac sold, at scale, before shutting down","On a large repo, a surface nobody works through"],"system":"Hash per span; no author field.","screens":"Editor and review panel as drawn.","money":"None.","what":"Highlight every span that changed since a person last read it, as specified. The simplest rule with no dependency, and the shape Almanac sold at scale before shutting down."},{"k":"b","label":"Highlight every span; sort and default the panel to agent-authored spans","what":"The store stays general, the surface leads with what a machine wrote.","gains":["Keeps the cheap store and takes the claim that survived review"],"costs":["Two behaviours to explain in one feature","The default is only as good as the attribution"],"system":"An author field, read when present.","screens":"The review panel gains a default filter.","money":"Ties the surface to the paid split."},{"k":"c","label":"Highlight only spans a machine wrote","gains":["Exactly the claim nothing observed occupies"],"costs":["Useless where the write carried no authorship, which is most","Makes review state depend on an unbuilt row"],"system":"Author required, so unknown means no highlight.","screens":"The editor shows fewer highlights.","money":"None.","what":"Highlight only spans a machine wrote. Exactly the claim nothing occupies, useless wherever a write carried no author, which is most writes until the MCP path exists."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship a and decide scope after the five-second screenshot test","gains":["The test is scheduled for weeks 1 to 2 anyway"],"costs":["The store schema is set before the question is asked","Retrofitting an author field later costs more"],"system":"General store; author added later.","screens":"No change now.","money":"None.","what":"Ship every-changed-span now and decide the scope after the five-second test. The store schema is set before the question is asked; adding an author field later costs more."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Keeping the store general costs nothing, so make the choice in the panel.","Leading with agent-authored spans is the only version no observed product offers."],"flip":"If authorship is absent on most real agent writes, option b collapses into a.","linked":["F1","F3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:124","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward","https://www.notion.com/releases","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anthropics/claude-code/main/CHANGELOG.md","https://antigravity.google/blog/vcs-and-terminal"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Both answers describe the highlight, which is deferred.","recWhy":"Highlighting everything that changed is honest, cheap and has a dead precedent behind it: it is the general form Almanac sold on $45M and switched off in January 2025. Highlighting only what a machine wrote is the claim nothing occupies, and it depends on attribution, which most writes will not carry until the MCP path exists. Keeping the store general costs nothing, so the choice belongs in the panel, not the schema: record every change, sort and default the view to agent-authored spans, and let a person widen it. On instruction files that default is the feature: what did an agent change in the file other agents obey, and did anyone look. It is the only version no observed product offers. If authorship turns out absent on most real agent writes, b collapses into a and the schema is still right."},{"id":"F37","cat":"Features","sub":"TEMPLATES","weight":"high","when":"after","whenWhy":"Templates are the paid surface later. The MVP ships one kit shape, so this waits for the pilot.","q":"Do templates vary by project type, and who writes them: us, the user, or a model each time?","lede":"One kit shape fits a web app badly and a research repo worse, and a template is cheap to make and costly to keep.","stakes":"Every template is a thing to maintain, and a stale template writes a stale kit into somebody's repository.","state":["The plan describes one document kit, not a set of them.","The launcher iterations show four templates, which is a first-screen decision already in `F27`.","The 33 file structure in the founder's own docs folder is one shape, aimed at a product build."],"tension":["A template is high perceived value for very little work.","A set of them is a maintenance surface that grows with every project type we name."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"A small fixed set we write and maintain: web app, library, research, internal tool","gains":["Predictable output we can actually check","Each one can be red-proofed against a real repo"],"costs":["Four things to keep current","Anybody outside those four gets a bad fit"],"system":"A template store with versioning.","screens":"A picker in the kit flow.","money":"Maintenance time, not money.","what":"Four templates we write and keep: web app, library, research, internal tool. Predictable output that can be red-proofed against a real repository; anyone outside the four gets a poor fit."},{"k":"b","label":"One shape, and the model adapts it from the answers","gains":["Nothing to maintain","Fits any project type on the first try"],"costs":["Output varies run to run, which makes the consistency check harder","Nothing to show on a pricing page"],"system":"No store.","screens":"None.","money":"Higher tokens per kit.","what":"One shape the model adapts from the answers. Nothing to maintain and it fits any project on the first try; output varies run to run and there is nothing to list on a pricing page."},{"k":"c","label":"Users write and share their own, and we host them","gains":["The set grows without us","A reason to come back"],"costs":["Moderation, and hosting other people's content reopens `L1`","Nothing to show on day one"],"system":"A template registry with identity.","screens":"Browse and publish screens.","money":"Storage and moderation time.","what":"Users write templates and we host them. The set grows without us, and hosting other people's content reopens every question in L1, plus moderation."},{"k":"d","label":"No templates. One kit, and the user edits what they get","gains":["Least work, and the editor is right there"],"costs":["Loses the clearest paid surface after storage","Every user does the same adaptation by hand"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No templates; one kit, and the user edits what they get. The least work, and every user does the same adaptation by hand while the clearest paid surface after storage goes unused."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["A fixed small set is the only option where we can red-proof the output against a real repository before anybody relies on it.","It is also the only one that gives the paid tier something visible to list."],"flip":"If the twenty hand made kits come out nearly identical across project types, (b) is cheaper and (a) is invented work.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"Who writes the template, and who keeps it current","cols":["A fixed set we keep","One adaptive shape","User written"],"rows":[{"label":"Maintenance","cells":["ours, grows","none","theirs"]},{"label":"Output predictable","cells":["yes","varies per run","no"]},{"label":"Can be red-proofed","cells":["yes","hard","no"]},{"label":"Visible on a pricing page","cells":["yes","no","yes"]},{"label":"Reopens hosting duties","cells":["no","no","yes"]}],"note":"Hosting user-written templates puts other people's content on our servers, which is `L1` again.","caption":"Three ways to have templates, and what each costs later"},"sources":["docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1"],"linked":["F27","F34","PR24"],"recWhy":"A template is high perceived value for very little work, and a set of them is a maintenance surface that grows with every project type anyone names, so the answer is a small fixed set, not an open one. Four we write and keep, one per project shape, is the only option where the output can be red-proofed against a real repository before anyone relies on it, which the consistency check in F34 needs. It is also the only option with something visible to list on the Pro tier. One adaptive shape makes the consistency check harder because the output moves run to run. User-written templates are somebody else's content on our servers, which is L1 again. If the twenty hand-made kits come out nearly identical across project types, the fixed set is invented work and b is cheaper."},{"id":"F4","cat":"Features","sub":"Answer in place","weight":"medium","q":"Does answer-in-place stay in the pilot now that its Q-and-A half is cut and it serves about 1,400 files?","lede":"It was briefly the headline and is now a two-day feature for one toolchain's marker syntax.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Public spec.md files carrying the marker","n":3368,"unit":"files","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Live after reading 50 files hit by hit","n":1415,"unit":"files","tone":"accent"}],"note":"42% of a read-every-hit sample, spec-kit syntax only.","caption":"The addressable population after both corrections"},"stakes":"Two days on a demo moment that survives only because it used to be the headline.","state":["It finds spec-kit's NEEDS CLARIFICATION markers and replaces just the marker's bytes: 2 days, in the pilot.","The Q-and-A half is cut: spec-kit writes that line only after the user answers.","Smallest row in the plan's feature table, and the only demand counted hit by hit."],"tension":["It is below the noise floor of any schedule this plan can defend.","One toolchain, which may clear its markers in chat before a file opens.","Generalising adds a settings surface, which the plan's five-gate feature test fails automatically."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The marker population, after two corrections","items":[["Public spec.md files carrying the marker","3,368","measured 2026-09-08, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58"],["Sample read hit-by-hit","50 files","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58"],["Genuinely live in that sample","42%","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58"],["Estimated live files","about 1,400","derived: 3,368 x 0.42 = 1,415"],["Earlier claim, withdrawn","62,976 markers, of which 49,408 were spec-kit's own checklist line","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:178"],["Build","2 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Which half survives","cols":["Half","Status","Reason"],"rows":[["NEEDS CLARIFICATION marker","Kept","3,368 files carry it; 42% live"],["Q: to A: bullet","CUT","spec-kit appends the bullet only after acceptance, so it is a closed record by construction"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep it in the pilot at two days as specified","gains":["Cheapest row in the feature table, and it demos in one keystroke"],"costs":["Serves one toolchain and about 1,400 public files","Adds a row nobody asked for by name"],"system":"Marker detector over the byte-exact writer.","screens":"Answer-in-place screen, as drawn.","money":"None.","what":"Keep it in the pilot at two days. The cheapest row in the table and a fifteen-second demo, for one toolchain's marker and about 1,400 public files."},{"k":"b","label":"Generalise the detector to any marker the user configures","gains":["Serves far more files than one toolchain's syntax"],"costs":["A settings surface, which the five-gate feature test fails","Every false positive lands inside our own review claim"],"system":"Pattern registry plus settings persistence.","screens":"A row in settings.","money":"None.","what":"Generalise the detector to any marker a user configures. Serves far more files, and adds a settings surface the plan's own feature test fails outright."},{"k":"c","label":"Move it to the build after the pilot and spend the two days on the anchor prototype","gains":["Buys two days for the question that gates everything"],"costs":["Loses a demo beat that reads in fifteen seconds","Nothing else fills the empty-folder moment"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Its screen leaves the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"Move it to the build after the pilot and spend the two days on the anchor prototype instead. Loses a small demo beat; buys time on the question that gates everything."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut it and fold marker answering into the file-scoped AI panel","gains":["One fewer row to specify and maintain"],"costs":["The distinctive part is the exact byte range, and it goes","The panel needs a key, so the marker case dies unkeyed"],"system":"Deletes the detector; the panel carries it.","screens":"Absorbed into the AI panel.","money":"None.","what":"Cut it and fold marker answering into the AI panel. One fewer row, and the distinctive part, replacing exactly the marker's bytes, goes with it."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The card is staged after the pilot; a recommendation to keep it in the pilot contradicts its own stage.","Two days is small, and Phase 0 is paste and export. The marker feature lands in the build after, denominator stated."],"flip":"If a pilot person arrives with spec-kit markers in hand, two days is cheap enough to pull forward.","linked":["F5","F8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:178","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:126"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Answer in place serves a narrow file population and is not in the first build.","recWhy":"This was once a headline and is now two days for one toolchain's marker syntax, which is the right size for it. The card is staged after the pilot and its old recommendation said keep it in the pilot, which contradicted its own stage; Phase 0 is paste and export and the marker feature is neither. Two days is small enough that a pilot person arriving with spec-kit markers in hand pulls it forward, and the demand was counted by hand, about 1,400 live files, so the row keeps its denominator. Generalising adds a settings surface, and every false positive would land inside our own review claim. Folding it into the AI panel loses the one distinctive thing about it, the exact byte range, and needs a key the marker case does not.","dupes":{"dropped":["P5"],"areas":["Product & definition"],"why":"Product & definition asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option a here: keep answer-in-place in the pilot as specified, markers only, two days.","agree":true}},{"id":"F31","cat":"Features","sub":"Import","weight":"medium","q":"Does import get a feature row, given migration from Notion is already sold as a service?","lede":"The product sells a migration service and ships a plain markdown file picker.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Notion export .zip","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"UUID filenames, broken links","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Our importer: .md picker only","note":"101 lines, no zip, no rewriting","tone":"stop"},{"label":"A founder does it by hand","note":"inside a fixed-scope engagement","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Or an internal tool the studio runs","tone":"accent"}],"note":"The counted importer demand is Obsidian's population, not this buyer.","caption":"What a migration actually costs when no importer exists"},"stakes":"Every migration costs founder hours in a business argued to sell without them.","state":["The plan's feature table has no import row.","Migration from Notion sits inside the Zephyrus services engagement.","A ChatGPT export importer ships in Phase 0 through chatgpt-to-claude, so import is not zero."],"tension":["Selling migration by hand contradicts the argument that the product does the selling.","The counted importer demand is Obsidian's users, not this buyer.","Notion exports are messy, so a public importer is a long maintenance commitment."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Importer demand, as counted","items":[["Open issues in the Obsidian importer repo that are Import from X requests for formats with no importer","47 of 58 (81%)","r1-migration-fidelity.md:35"],["notion-labelled issues in that repo","58 total, 55 closed (94.8%), the most-churned importer there","r1-migration-fidelity.md:36"],["notion-to-md npm downloads","1.36M per month","r1-migration-fidelity.md:187"],["Import rows in the plan's feature build-cost table","0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55-67"],["What ships today","ImportModal, 101 lines: .md/.markdown/.txt file picker, no zip, no link rewriting","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/ImportModal.tsx"],["Where migration appears in the plan","A services deliverable","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:478"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship a Notion-zip importer as a feature row in the build after the pilot","what":"Zip handling, UUID filename cleanup and link rewriting, in the product.","gains":["The one import path with counted demand"],"costs":["One more format to keep correct as Notion changes","The demand was counted on a different population"],"system":"A zip reader, normaliser and link rewriter.","screens":"The import modal grows a format path.","money":"Removes founder hours per migration."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep migration as a service and build an internal tool","gains":["Serves the paying case at a fraction of the cost"],"costs":["Every migration still costs founder hours","The tool has no users, so it stays fragile"],"system":"A script, not a module.","screens":"None.","money":"Migration stays billable and manual.","what":"Migration stays a service, and we build an internal tool to do it. The paying case is served at a fraction of a public importer's cost; each migration still costs founder hours."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the file picker and document the Notion export path","gains":["Near zero cost"],"costs":["Does not fix UUID filenames or broken links","It reads as advice where a tool was expected"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A documentation page.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the file picker and write up the Notion export path. Near zero cost, and it reads as advice where a tool was expected, with the UUID filenames and broken links unfixed."},{"k":"d","label":"No import at all; frontmatter opens folders and repos","gains":["Smallest surface"],"costs":["Contradicts a service the plan already sells","Deletes working code for no gain"],"system":"Removes a modal.","screens":"The import control disappears.","money":"None.","what":"No import at all; the product opens folders and repositories. The smallest surface, and it contradicts a service the plan already sells."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The migration case is priced, and it belongs to the engagement, not a free editor.","An internal tool serves it at a fraction of a supported public importer."],"flip":"If two or more pilot users arrive from Notion and stall on the export, option a follows.","linked":["F7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:478","docs/FEATURE-GAP-REPORT.md:99","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r1-migration-fidelity.md:35","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/ImportModal.tsx"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Import is not in the first build.","recWhy":"The product sells a Notion migration as a service and ships a plain file picker, which looks like a contradiction until you ask who the importer would serve. The counted demand for a Notion importer comes from Obsidian's users, not from a developer with a repository, and Notion exports are messy enough that a public importer is a maintenance commitment that never ends. An internal tool serves the paid engagement at a fraction of that cost. What changed this weekend is that import is no longer zero. chatgpt-to-claude turns a ChatGPT export into a clean working directory, stdlib Python, reads only, and that is exactly the Phase 0 front door, paste what a model gave you and get a document. So the ChatGPT path ships free in Phase 0 and Notion stays a service. If two or more pilot users arrive from Notion and stall, a follows."},{"id":"F38","cat":"Features","sub":"SLIDES","weight":"medium","when":"after","whenWhy":"Slides are a retention feature, not a reason to arrive, so they wait until somebody is already here.","q":"Do slides ship as one command with no options, as a slide editor, or not at all?","lede":"Markdown to slides is solved by Marp, Slidev and reveal.js, all free, all established, all with mindshare.","stakes":"Built as a feature it is weeks that win nobody. Built as one command it is days that make a visit worth more.","state":["The direction given on 13 September asks for presentations generated from the document.","Marp and Slidev both exist, are reachable and are free. Checked 2026-09-13.","The slide separator convention in markdown is roughly a decade old and widely known."],"tension":["Nobody changes editor to get slides, so this cannot be an acquisition feature.","Somebody who has just cleaned up a chat output is glad a deck falls out for nothing."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One command, one fixed template, no options","gains":["Days rather than weeks","The whole value is not having opened Keynote"],"costs":["No control, so anybody who wants control leaves","One template will suit some documents badly"],"system":"A renderer over the existing HTML path.","screens":"One menu item and a preview.","money":"None.","what":"One command, one fixed template, no options. Days of work, and the whole value is that nobody had to open Keynote; one template will suit some documents badly."},{"k":"b","label":"A slide editor with layouts, themes and per-slide control","gains":["Competes properly with the established tools"],"costs":["Weeks of polish against free incumbents with mindshare","Pulls the product away from being an editor"],"system":"A second document model.","screens":"A slide workspace.","money":"None.","what":"A slide editor with layouts, themes and per-slide control. Weeks of polish against free incumbents with mindshare, and it pulls the product away from being an editor."},{"k":"c","label":"Export to Marp or Slidev format and let their tools render it","gains":["Almost free to build","Their ecosystem does the hard part"],"costs":["The output leaves our product, so we see none of it","A conversion, not a feature"],"system":"A format writer.","screens":"A menu item.","money":"None.","what":"Export to Marp or Slidev format and let their tools render. Almost free, and the output leaves our product so we never see it; a conversion rather than a feature."},{"k":"d","label":"No slides","gains":["Nothing to build or keep"],"costs":["Turns down a cheap reason for a visit to be worth more"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No slides. Nothing to build or keep, and a cheap way to make a visit worth more is turned down."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Days of work for a feature that makes an existing visit worth more is a good trade, and weeks of work for it is not.","The value here is that the user did not have to open a presentation tool, and that survives having no options."],"flip":"If people ask for control rather than for slides, (c) hands them to a tool that already has it, for almost nothing.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"Three ways to do slides, and what each is worth","cols":["One command","A slide editor","Export to Marp"],"rows":[{"label":"Build","cells":["days","weeks","hours"]},{"label":"Reason to switch to us","cells":["no","no","no"]},{"label":"Makes a visit worth more","cells":["yes","yes","no, output leaves"]},{"label":"Competes with Marp and Slidev","cells":["no","yes, badly","no"]}],"note":"Marp and Slidev were both reachable and free when checked on 2026-09-13.","caption":"Slides, rated on what they are actually worth to us"},"sources":["docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1","docs/GENERATION-SURFACE-2026-09-13-2113.md:1"],"linked":["F34"],"recWhy":"Markdown to slides is a solved problem with free, good, established tools, and the separator convention is a decade old, so nobody changes editor to get slides and this can never be an acquisition feature. It can be a retention one. Somebody who has just pasted a chat output and cleaned it up is glad a deck falls out for nothing, and the entire value of that moment is that they did not have to open a presentation tool. That value survives having no options, and dies under weeks of polish against Marp and Slidev. One command, one template, days of work, never on the landing page as a reason to switch. If people ask for control rather than for slides, c hands them to a tool that already has it, for almost nothing."},{"id":"FL12","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"First run","weight":"critical","q":"What does a stranger meet in the first thirty seconds, and does the 83% refusal gate the pilot?","lede":"Five of six flows open with a scan that refuses 83% of real vaults, and no screen is a first run.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"Launch","note":"no folder, no separate file, nothing scanned","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Templates first, as drawn","note":"Open folder is a toolbar icon","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Open a folder","note":"the ordering specified twice in the record","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Scan walks the tree","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"6,613 of 6,614 files refused","note":"zero-indent block sequences","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Apology or guarantee line","note":"same scan, two readings","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Sandbox of real .md files","note":"the fallback when the repo refuses","tone":"accent"}],"note":"Review state needs no frontmatter write, so a write-path refusal need not block a read.","caption":"The first thirty seconds, which no screen has drawn."},"stakes":"The first thirty seconds carry the worst number, 17% of vaults opening cleanly against a 99.9% target, and that screen is undrawn.","state":["The launcher leads with templates; Open folder is a small toolbar button.","PRD and flow research both put Open an existing folder first.","Two engine bugs cost 7 days: a list at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file, and an old-style line ending adds a second frontmatter block."],"tension":["Review state needs no frontmatter write, so a write refusal should not block reading.","The flow research says ship no flow until both engine bugs land."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Documented first-run orderings","cols":["Product","Order","Source"],"rows":[["Obsidian","Two choices only: \"Create new vault\" or \"Open folder as vault\", recorded as \"co-equal, not buried\"; import is step 5 of 6","c4-onboarding-activation.md:50-51"],["Obsidian sandbox","A real disposable vault, opened deliberately from Help: \"Not shown automatically at first run\"","c4:82"],["Linear","Demo workspace first, create workspace second","c4:55"],["Pattern across Obsidian, Linear, Raycast, Vercel","First run teaches one mechanic on content the product supplies, and defers the user's own corpus","c4:62"],["PRD v2 step 1","Open an existing folder: primary, sandbox second","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1763"],["a research report, flow 1 step 1","Open an existing folder (primary), Try a sandbox (secondary)","r21-s7:37"],["frontmatter prototype","Blank + 4 templates first; existing edits below; Open folder in the toolbar","prototype:291, :302, :286"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"First-run constraints already written down","items":[["First-run screens in the prototype","0 of 9","prototype:282-687"],["K1, keystrokes to first value","≤ 3","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1846"],["C, concepts to learn","At most 3: file, folder, view","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1848"],["V0, visible controls at rest","≤ 9","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1845"],["Walkthrough, tour, What's New modal","banned","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1764, NN/g"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The scan, measured","items":[["Foreign files refused","6,613 of 6,614","zero-indent block sequences, FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1814"],["Aggregate vault refusal","83%","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214"],["Z target","≥ 99.9%","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1851; today 17%"],["Flows that open with the scan","5 of 6","r21-s7:169"],["Ten folders tried → refusals met","8.3","r21-s7:9, derived from 83%"],["Corpus composition","7 Obsidian vaults, one wiki = 77% of 8,513 files","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"],["the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug","7 engineering days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214-215"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two status lines available for the same scan","cols":["Version","Text","Line"],"rows":[["Apology (today's behaviour, undrawn)","\"163 of 1,040 files could not be read.\"","r21-s7:39"],["Guarantee","\"1,040 files open. 163 use a list style the editor will not edit yet. None have been modified.\"","r21-s7:47"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Folder primary, sandbox second, and split the read path from the write path","what":"A write-path refusal never blocks reading; the sandbox is disposable real files.","gains":["Most of the 83% then affects only writing","Confines most of the 83 percent refusal to writing, which review state does not need at all."],"costs":["A sandbox to maintain, and an unestimated read/write seam","No engineering estimate exists yet for the read/write seam this split would create."],"system":"A seam between reader and writer.","screens":"Draws the first-run screen.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Gate: no stranger until the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug are fixed","gains":["The first impression is the product working","Matches the flow research's own recommendation: ship no flow until both bugs land."],"costs":["Seven days of fix-first before every human test","Seven days of fix-first happens before a single human sees the product."],"system":"The scheduled fixes land first.","screens":"Adds the missing first-run screen.","money":"Delays every week-1 signal.","what":"Gate the pilot: no stranger sees it until the flush-left list bug and the old line-ending bug are fixed. The first impression is the product working, after seven days of fix-first."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship as drawn: templates first, with a guarantee-shaped status line","gains":["Demonstrates refuse-rather-than-guess at once, at no cost","Costs nothing extra; the status line already exists to be reworded from apology to guarantee."],"costs":["A stranger whose repo is 83% unreadable leaves","83 percent of foreign vaults hit this exact refusal, measured on the corpus in hand."],"system":"No change.","screens":"One status line.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the launcher as drawn, templates first, with a guarantee-shaped status line. Demonstrates refuse-rather-than-guess at once and free; a stranger whose repo is 83 percent unreadable leaves."},{"k":"d","label":"Curate the pilot to spec-kit and superpowers repos the engine handles","gains":["Removes the failure from the pilot","Two named repo types, spec-kit and superpowers, are already how the week 3 to 4 test is scoped."],"costs":["The exit test then proves nothing about vaults","A pilot that never meets a real vault cannot speak to the population it is refusing today."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Curate the pilot to spec-kit and superpowers repositories the engine already handles. Removes the failure from the pilot, and the exit test then proves nothing about real vaults."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The pilot's loop is read, highlight, revert, and a revert writes bytes the file already had.","Splitting the paths makes the worst number minor for the tier that ships first."],"flip":"If the read and write split costs more than the seven days both fixes cost, gate instead.","linked":["E1","FL14","FL18"],"sources":["docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:291","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:286","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:302","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1763","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1814","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1845","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1851","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/c4-onboarding-activation.md:51","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/c4-onboarding-activation.md:62","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/c4-onboarding-activation.md:82","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r21-s7-flow-design.md:37","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r21-s7-flow-design.md:47","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r21-s7-flow-design.md:169","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:215","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","merged: FL13"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"First thirty seconds is the launcher and first run, which the MVP builds.","recWhy":"Five of six flows open with a scan that refuses 83 percent of real vaults, and most of that number is one Obsidian habit, a list starting at the left edge, which is a write-path refusal. Reading never needed the write path. The pilot's loop is read, highlight, revert, and a revert writes back bytes the file already had. Splitting the read path from the write path means a refusal to write never blocks opening the file, which turns the worst number in the product into a minor one for the tier that ships first. Folder first because that is where a stranger's real documents are; a disposable sandbox second for someone with nothing to open. Q4 carries this as the first thirty seconds. If the split costs more than the seven days both engine fixes cost, gate instead and take the fixes first."},{"id":"FL21","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"EMPTY STATE","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"An empty editor is the first thing a stranger sees. What sits in that space decides whether they try anything.","q":"What is on screen when nothing is open, and what can a stranger press to see the product work?","lede":"A bare editor is the goal, and a bare editor with nothing to press is a blank page a stranger leaves.","stakes":"This is the whole first impression. Too much furniture and it is not bare. Nothing at all and there is no way in.","state":["The plan calls for a bare bones editor with the md.sgnk.ai look.","The launcher iterations in the gallery show four templates and a recent list.","Nothing in the product today demonstrates what it does without the user supplying a document first."],"tension":["Every affordance added to the empty state is a concept the reader has to hold.","A stranger with no file has nothing to paste and no reason to stay."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One paste target and nothing else, with a single line of instruction","gains":["Truest to the bare bones claim","Nothing to learn"],"costs":["A stranger with nothing to paste still leaves","No demonstration of generation at all"],"system":"No sample content ships.","screens":"One empty state.","money":"None.","what":"One paste target and a single line of instruction. Truest to the bare bones claim and nothing to learn; a stranger with nothing to paste leaves, and generation is never shown."},{"k":"b","label":"Paste target, plus one sample document that shows a kit being made","gains":["Shows the thing that is actually different, not the editor","Costs one document to write, not a feature"],"costs":["A sample is a maintenance item and goes stale","One more concept in the empty state"],"system":"One bundled sample.","screens":"Empty state with a try line.","money":"Model tokens if the sample regenerates.","what":"A paste target plus one sample document that turns into a kit. Shows the part that is actually different; the sample is a document to keep current, and one more concept."},{"k":"c","label":"The launcher as drawn: four templates and a recent list","gains":["A clear way in for a returning user","Templates are the paid surface later"],"costs":["Four templates is four concepts before a single word is written","A first time user has no recents, so half of it is empty"],"system":"Template store.","screens":"The launcher.","money":"None.","what":"The launcher as drawn: four templates and a recent list. Clear for a returning user; four concepts before a word is written, and a first-timer's recents are empty."},{"k":"d","label":"Paste target with a live example that types itself out","gains":["Shows the product with no action from the reader","Needs no separate document to write or maintain, since the example lives in the interaction itself."],"costs":["Motion in an editor reads as a toy","Accessibility work for reduced motion and screen readers"],"system":"An animation loop.","screens":"Animated empty state.","money":"None.","what":"A paste target with a live example that types itself out. Shows the product with no action from the reader; motion in an editor reads as a toy, and it needs reduced-motion work."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The editor is not the thing worth demonstrating, because every editor looks the same when empty.","One sample that turns into a kit shows the part nobody else does, and it costs a document rather than a feature."],"flip":"If the twenty hand made kits show people arrive already holding a document, (a) is enough and cheaper.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"What sits in the empty space","cols":["Paste only","Paste plus one sample","The full launcher"],"rows":[{"label":"Concepts on screen","cells":["1","2","4 or more"]},{"label":"Works with nothing to paste","cells":["no","yes","yes"]},{"label":"Shows generation","cells":["no","yes","no"]},{"label":"Build cost","cells":["hours","a day","a week"]},{"label":"Stays bare","cells":["yes","yes","no"]}],"note":"Generation is the part worth showing. An empty editor demonstrates nothing that a text area does not.","caption":"Three empty states, against the bare bones budget"},"sources":["docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1","decisions/mockups-data.js:1","docs/GENERATION-SURFACE-2026-09-13-2113.md:1"],"linked":["AC20","F27","FL12"],"recWhy":"A bare editor is the goal and a bare editor with nothing in it is a blank page somebody leaves, so the question is what the fewest things on that page can be. Every editor looks the same when empty, which means the editor is not the thing worth demonstrating. What nobody else does is turn a paste into a document kit and show the instruction files an agent will obey. One sample document that does that, before the stranger has typed a word, demonstrates the difference at the cost of a document rather than a feature. That leaves the empty state at two concepts, a paste target and a sample, which is a budget P15's four nouns can carry. The launcher as drawn is four templates and an empty recents list for everyone new. If the twenty hand-made kits show people arrive already holding a document, a is enough."},{"id":"FL3","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Interaction floor","weight":"high","q":"Do the table-stakes interaction days go into the pilot, and does Cmd-K become insert-link?","lede":"The sequence spends 69 of about 70 engineering days before reaching the line reading table stakes and polish.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":132,"cols":[{"title":"Whole floor"},{"title":"Cheap half","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Ship as is"}],"rows":[{"label":"Engineering days added","cells":["13-21, midpoint 17.5","2.5-3.5","0"]},{"label":"Pilot total","cells":["about 87","about 73","about 70"]},{"label":"Cmd-K","cells":["insert link","insert link","file switcher"]},{"label":"Palette, outline key, switcher","cells":["done","done","as inherited"]},{"label":"Table editing, HTML paste","cells":["done","not done","not done"]},{"label":"Extra calendar weeks at 1.21 d/wk","cells":["about 14","about 3","0"]}],"note":"Day counts are from a round 24 report, stated as days for two founders against a one-author commit record.","caption":"What each amount of floor buys, and what it costs in weeks."},"stakes":"The exit test is six of ten strangers keeping it, so one who cannot bold a heading is lost.","state":["The measured floor is eight items, 13-21 days, none scheduled.","Insert-link is mouse-only, and Cmd-K opens the note switcher."],"tension":["Typora, Bear, iA Writer, Obsidian and VS Code all bind Cmd-K to insert-link.","At 1.21 engineering days a calendar week, 17.5 more days is fourteen weeks."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The sequence, added up (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227)","items":[["Fix-first","24 days","NF-1, NF-3, CI, engine wiring, byte budget, BYO key, structured output"],["Review state","18 days","store 6 + rendering 5 + revert 3 + panel 4"],["Attribution and answer in place","6 days",""],["Idea mode and decision flow","8 days",""],["Repo docs scan","10 days",""],["File-scoped AI","3 days",""],["Named blocks, total","69 days","against a stated ~70 for MVP-0"],["Left for table stakes and polish","~1 day","derived: 70 − 69"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The measured floor, and whether it is scheduled (CRITIQUE.md:602-610)","cols":["Item","Cost (two founders, per r24-v1:31)","In any MVP stage"],"rows":[["⌘1 to 6, ⌘K link, list/quote/task toggles, checkbox, move-line","0.5 d","No"],["Outline hotkey + fuzzy heading jump","0.5 d","No"],["Palette that indexes everything and shows hotkeys","1 to 2 d","No"],["Quick-switcher deltas: recents-on-empty, create-on-miss, ⌘⏎ new tab","0.5 d","No"],["HTML paste → markdown","2 to 3 d","No"],["Table editing: Tab nav, add/remove row and column, alignment","5 to 8 d","No"],["Splice-backed table writes","+2 d","No"],["Customisable keymap","3 to 4 d","No"],["Total","13 to 21 d, midpoint 17.5","0 of 8"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Re-measured against the current plan","items":[["Occurrences of \"hotkey\" in PRODUCT-BRIEF.md","0","grep"],["Occurrences of \"keymap\" or \"table editing\"","0","grep"],["Occurrences of \"palette\"","1","line 55, as an existing F1 capability, not as scheduled work"],["Command palette today","14 static commands, no hotkey column, no recents, no pinning","r24-v1:37"],["17.5 days in calendar terms","70 calendar days as person-days; 104 to 140 as pair-days","CRITIQUE.md:616"]]},{"type":"table","title":"⌘K across the three artefacts and the field","cols":["Where","⌘K does","⌘P does","Insert link"],"rows":[["frontmatter code","Quick note switcher (KnowledgeUI.tsx:56)","Command palette (:57)","implemented, toolbar only (Toolbar.tsx:102)"],["frontmatter prototype","Commands (prototype:315)","Files (:315)","not shown"],["Typora, Bear, iA, Obsidian, VS Code","Insert link (CRITIQUE.md:614)","Switcher or palette","⌘K"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The interaction floor this sits inside","items":[["Authoring keys bound today","3","CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:224-226 (no ⌘1 to 6, no list, no quote, no task)"],["`toggleTaskAtLine`","written, unbound","toolbar-transforms.ts:112"],["`insertLink`","written, unbound","toolbar-transforms.ts:234"],["Cost of the whole hotkey group","0.5 day for two people","CRITIQUE.md:602, item 1 of 8; r24-v1's table header states its costs are for two founders"],["Occurrences of \"hotkey\" or \"keymap\" in the current plan","0","grep of PRODUCT-BRIEF.md"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"The whole floor into the pilot, bindings moved to markdown convention","gains":["The pilot judges the review idea, not the editor","Matches five incumbents, Typora, Bear, iA Writer, Obsidian and VS Code, which all bind the same key this way."],"costs":["The pilot goes from 70 to 87 days, fourteen more weeks","87 days at the measured 1.21 engineering days a week is roughly seventeen more calendar weeks."],"system":"Table editing needs the editor's write engine, which changes only the bytes you edited and never rewrites the whole file.","screens":"Hotkeys screen, table toolbar, palette.","money":"The delay is the cost.","what":"The whole table-stakes floor into the pilot, eight items, bindings moved to markdown convention. The pilot judges the review idea rather than the editor, at fourteen more weeks."},{"k":"b","label":"The cheap half only: hotkeys, outline key, palette, switcher deltas","what":"Cmd-K becomes insert-link, the switcher moves to Cmd-O.","gains":["The first ninety seconds for about 4% of budget","Covers 6 of the 8 measured floor items for about 3 of the 17.5-day midpoint."],"costs":["Leaves out table editing and HTML paste, both disqualifying","Table editing and HTML paste are named in the same table as disqualifying gaps if missing."],"system":"A keymap table over existing toolbar actions.","screens":"The palette gains a hotkey column.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship the pilot on the current floor and the current bindings","gains":["Fastest to the exit test, on real feedback","Zero new engineering days before the exit test the pilot is built to run."],"costs":["Cmd-K on a pasted URL opens the file switcher","Command-K already opens a note switcher in the code, so this is a real, live collision."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the pilot on the floor and bindings as they are. Fastest to the exit test, and Cmd-K on a pasted URL opens the file switcher instead of making a link."},{"k":"d","label":"Resolve the form factor first: an extension inherits floor and keymap","gains":["Deletes the floor, the keymap and bring-your-own-key at once","An extension inherits the host's own keymap, deleting all 13 to 21 days of this decision at once."],"costs":["Forfeits Live mode; an 82,265-install incumbent holds the name","The best of 13 markdown-review extensions in that niche still only reaches 400 installs."],"system":"A different shell around the same engine.","screens":"Kills launcher, tree, mode switcher.","money":"Removes desktop certificate costs.","what":"Resolve the form factor first; an extension inherits the host's floor and keymap. Deletes the floor, the keymap and the key work at once, and forfeits Live mode."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Three days buys the floor a reviewer meets in the first two minutes.","Cmd-K to insert-link is the cheapest fix: the function exists, bound to nothing."],"flip":"If the form-factor decision goes to a VS Code extension, the host owns floor and keymap, and (d) wins.","linked":["FF1","FL12","FL2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/CRITIQUE.md:602","docs/CRITIQUE.md:614","docs/CRITIQUE.md:616","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v1-table-stakes.md:31","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v1-table-stakes.md:37","src/modules/editor/presentation/toolbar-transforms.ts:234","src/modules/editor/presentation/Toolbar.tsx:102","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/KnowledgeUI.tsx:56","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:315","merged: FL17"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Table stakes editing is what the first build is. Cmd K is part of it.","recWhy":"The sequence spends 69 of about 70 engineering days before it reaches the line that says table stakes and polish, and the measured floor is eight items at 13 to 21 days, none of them scheduled. The whole floor is fourteen more weeks at the measured rate, which Phase 0 cannot carry. But the floor is not one thing. The cheap half, hotkeys, an outline key, the palette and the switcher deltas, is about three days and it is what a reviewer meets in the first two minutes. Cmd-K to insert-link is the clearest case: Typora, Bear, iA Writer, Obsidian and VS Code all bind it that way, the function already exists here, bound to nothing, and today Cmd-K on a pasted URL opens the file switcher. Table editing and HTML paste are the expensive half and F21 already decided the table editor goes. Q5 carries this as part of the chrome block.","dupes":{"dropped":["F24"],"areas":["Features"],"why":"Features asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option b here: budget the cheap half of the editing floor. Its cheap half names rebinding Cmd-K to insert-link, which this option does not list.","agree":true}},{"id":"FL4","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Review fatigue","weight":"critical","q":"Do we run the 40-hunk head-to-head against `git add -p` in weeks 1-2?","lede":"The critique holds a costed argument that review interaction is theatre on any change big enough to need it.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":126,"cols":[{"title":"frontmatter review surface","tone":"accent"},{"title":"`git add -p`, free"}],"rows":[{"label":"Keystrokes on 40 hunks","cells":["80 minimum","80 minimum"]},{"label":"Words to read to judge","cells":["about 4,400","about 4,400"]},{"label":"Total review","cells":["17-25 min","17-25 min"]},{"label":"Against agent generation","cells":["about 60 s, so 17-25x","same ratio"]},{"label":"Hunk grain","cells":["word","line"]},{"label":"Works on an unsaved buffer","cells":["yes","no"]}],"note":"What survives the comparison is word grain, dirty buffers and the hash line.","caption":"The same 40 hunks, in the paid surface and the free one."},"stakes":"Eighteen days of review-state build rest on an interaction that may collapse to accept-all.","state":["Weeks 1-2 lists six items and the 40-hunk comparison is not among them.","Review state (what changed since you last read it) is 18 days: store 6, rendering 5, revert 3, panel 4."],"tension":["Review takes 17-25x as long as generation on a 40-hunk change.","Cisco found no useful review above 250 lines. That is source code, not prose."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The 40-hunk review, costed (CRITIQUE.md:626-635)","items":[["Keystrokes, minimum","80","40 × (1 nav + 1 decision)"],["Words to read to judge","~4,400","40 hunks × ~110 words"],["Careful review @200 wpm","22 min","4,400 / 200"],["Total review","17 to 25 min","reading + 3 s/hunk decision"],["Agent generation","~60 s","same change"],["Review : generation","17 to 25×","CRITIQUE.md:637: \"the product's structural problem\""]]},{"type":"table","title":"What already ships free, and what does not","cols":["Capability","Where","Cost to user"],"rows":[["Per-hunk discard of uncommitted agent changes","`git restore -p`, `git checkout -p`, `git add -p`, git 2.50.1 (CRITIQUE.md:639)","free, offline, for years"],["Accept or reject each hunk in an editor","Zed agent panel, re-verified live 2026-08-31 (CRITIQUE.md:622)","$0"],["Word-grain hunks, hunks in a dirty buffer, the hash line","frontmatter only (CRITIQUE.md:641)","the whole delta"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Run the head-to-head at the start of Phase 1, before the 18 days of build","what":"Ten strangers review one prepared 40-hunk change in both surfaces.","gains":["The only cheap test that can refute 18 days","Costs nothing but a prepared file and a script, against 18 days of build the result could refute."],"costs":["Needs a prepared 40-hunk change and a script","The comparison itself needs a genuine 40-hunk change prepared, not a trivial one."],"system":"A mock and a terminal.","screens":"One mock walking 40 hunks.","money":"Ten conversations beyond the 35 planned."},{"k":"b","label":"Build the surface, then compare at the ten-stranger pilot","gains":["Tests the product, not a mock","Tests the real, shipped surface rather than a mock that may not match it."],"costs":["A failure means 18 days and the exit test","A negative result only arrives after the 18 days and the ten-stranger exit test are both spent."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"18 days at risk.","what":"Build the review surface, then compare it against git add -p in the pilot. Tests the real product rather than a mock; a failure costs eighteen days and the exit test."},{"k":"c","label":"Change the unit so the comparison does not apply: file or section, not hunk","gains":["Sidesteps the ratio; matches the free incumbent","Matches what a free incumbent, git add -p, already ships at the file or section grain."],"costs":["Gives up the per-span claim, the differentiator","Per-span review is the differentiator named elsewhere in the corpus; this trades it away."],"system":"The unit becomes file or heading.","screens":"The panel becomes a file list.","money":"None.","what":"Change the unit so the comparison does not apply: review by file or section, not by hunk. Sidesteps the ratio and matches the free incumbent, and gives up the per-span claim."},{"k":"d","label":"Accept the ratio and design for skim: file level by default, hunks on demand","gains":["Honest about how a large change is read","Matches the measured reading rate directly: 22 minutes to read what an agent writes in 60 seconds."],"costs":["Two units everywhere, and it pre-empts the counter","Building two units, file-level and hunk-level, is more surface to design and maintain than one."],"system":"Stored per span, shown per file.","screens":"Adds a level switch to the panel.","money":"None.","what":"Accept the ratio and design for skimming: file level by default, hunks on demand. Honest about how a large change is read; two units everywhere, and it pre-empts the counter."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The cheapest way to refute 18 days of build, and the thresholds are written.","It also decides the review unit, which four decisions here inherit."],"flip":"If under 4 of 10 ask for review state after the mock, the plan stops at week 3.","linked":["FL19","FL5","FL6"],"sources":["docs/CRITIQUE.md:626","docs/CRITIQUE.md:637","docs/CRITIQUE.md:639","docs/CRITIQUE.md:641","docs/CRITIQUE.md:643","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","https://static1.smartbear.co/support/media/resources/cc/book/code-review-cisco-case-study.pdf","https://sback.it/publications/icse2018seip.pdf"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A head to head on review hunks needs review state.","recWhy":"The critique carries a costed argument that per-hunk review is theatre on any change big enough to need it. Review takes 17 to 25 times as long as generation on a forty-hunk change, and Cisco found no useful review above 250 lines, though that was source code. Eighteen days of store, rendering, revert and panel rest on the per-span unit being one a person will actually work through. The cheapest test that can refute those eighteen days is ten strangers reviewing one prepared forty-hunk change in both surfaces, with the thresholds already written. It runs at the start of Phase 1, before the instruction-file review build, which is why the label was corrected from weeks 1 to 2. The same test decides the review unit, and four other decisions here inherit that answer. If under four of ten ask for review state after the mock, the plan stops at week 3."},{"id":"FL14","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Cold start","weight":"critical","q":"On first open of an unseen repo, what is the baseline, and which surface carries the pilot?","lede":"Ten strangers bring their own repos. The plan says neither what their first document looks like nor where writes appear.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":138,"cols":[{"title":"Fresh agent output, the demo"},{"title":"Stranger's existing repo, the pilot","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Everything unreviewed","cells":["Every paragraph highlighted, as intended","Every paragraph highlighted, the stated failure"]},{"label":"Baseline at first open","cells":["Nothing highlighted until the agent writes","Nothing highlighted, counter reads zero"]},{"label":"Baseline at the last commit","cells":["The fresh spec is highlighted","Only uncommitted work is highlighted"]},{"label":"Notices a write while open","cells":["Desktop only, no watcher in the web app","Desktop only"]},{"label":"Works with no git","cells":["No, it degrades to first-open baseline","No"]},{"label":"Anchor survives another tool's write","cells":["Untested","Untested"]}],"note":"The anchor question is still open after two adversarial rounds, and both other rows depend on it.","caption":"Three cold-start rules against the two scenarios that matter."},"stakes":"Either a stranger's first document is entirely highlighted, the stated failure, or the counter reads zero.","state":["The demo is every paragraph highlighted on a spec the agent just wrote.","The review file is created on first use; no rule says what counts as already read."],"tension":["The only natural baseline is the working tree, which needs git, which is desktop-only.","Whether the review anchor, byte range plus hash, survives another tool's write is still open."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two cold-start rules and what each first screen looks like","cols":["Rule","Fresh agent output (the demo)","Stranger's existing 3,000-word repo (the pilot)"],"rows":[["Everything unreviewed","Every paragraph highlighted: the intended demo","Every paragraph highlighted, reads as \"highlighted\", the stated test failure"],["Baseline at first open","Nothing highlighted until the agent writes again","Nothing highlighted; counter reads 0; nothing to show"],["Baseline at last commit, uncommitted work unreviewed","The agent's fresh spec is highlighted","Only what changed since the last commit is highlighted: usually a little, sometimes nothing"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What is fixed, and what is open","items":[["Sidecar unit","one JSON line per span: path, byte start, byte end, content hash, reviewed-by, reviewed-at","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Cold-start rule stated anywhere","no","grep of PRODUCT-BRIEF, GAPS, WEEK0"],["Pilot users bringing their own repos","10 of 10","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231"],["Five-second test failure words","\"highlighted\" or \"error\"","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411"],["GitHub Viewed state","free at every tier, per file, auto-unmarked on change, private to each reviewer","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56"],["Local git","desktop only","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:100"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each surface can do for the core loop (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:92-100)","cols":["Capability","Web, GitHub repo","Web, local folder (Chrome/Edge)","Desktop"],"rows":[["Review state","Yes; the review file is committed","Yes","Yes"],["Background watcher","No","No","Yes"],["Local git","No","No","Yes"],["Notices an agent write while the file is open","No (derived from the watcher row)","No (derived)","Yes"],["MCP server: writes that carry the prompt","No","No","Yes"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The untested mechanism","items":[["Out-of-band anchor survival","still open after 2 adversarial rounds","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"],["Anchor design","byte range + content hash, relocated by the splice locator","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Journal as anchor","explicitly rejected: \"git, other editors and agents bypass it\"","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135"],["Week 3 to 4 test in the plan","the highlight works on files frontmatter did not write, on one spec-kit and one superpowers repo","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354"],["Review-state code in src/ today","none","grep for review.jsonl / unreviewed / reviewState → 0 files"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Baseline at the last commit; pilot on desktop","what":"Anything uncommitted at first open is unreviewed.","gains":["Uses the working tree, which the product claims as its differentiator","Matches the product's own stated differentiator: the working tree, which only git can see."],"costs":["Puts Tauri, notarisation and identity on the critical path","Local git is desktop-only, so this option cannot exist on the web surface at all."],"system":"Reads git; degrades without it.","screens":"The highlight's meaning changes on first open.","money":"Certificates before week 10."},{"k":"b","label":"Everything unreviewed at first open; pilot on either surface","gains":["Honest, and no git dependency, so surfaces match","No git dependency means the same rule works identically on every surface at once."],"costs":["A stranger's first document is fully highlighted, the stated failure","This is the exact failure the five-second test names: a fully highlighted first document."],"system":"Simplest rule; no git read.","screens":"First open looks highlighted.","money":"None.","what":"Everything counts as unreviewed at first open, on either surface. Honest and needs no git, so the surfaces match; a stranger's first document is fully highlighted, the stated failure."},{"k":"c","label":"Baseline at first open; pilot on the web app with a focus refresh","gains":["Clean first screen, and no install for the trial","No install needed for the trial, matching the web-first funnel the pilot is built around."],"costs":["The first session may demonstrate nothing at all","A session that shows nothing highlighted also demonstrates nothing about the product."],"system":"A re-read on window focus.","screens":"No new screen.","money":"None.","what":"Baseline at first open, pilot on the web with a refresh on focus. A clean first screen and no install for the trial; the first session may demonstrate nothing at all."},{"k":"d","label":"Anchor test first, then last-commit baseline; the surface is settled by Q3","gains":["Answers the one mechanism never run, at almost no cost","The untested anchor mechanism, open after two adversarial rounds, finally gets run before anything else."],"costs":["The surface stays unsettled for two more weeks","Two more weeks pass before the web-versus-desktop question this depends on is even settled."],"system":"No change until the test returns.","screens":"No change until the test returns.","money":"None.","what":"Run the anchor test first, then set the baseline at the last commit. The one mechanism never run gets run for almost nothing; the surface itself is settled by Q3."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Baseline and surface are both downstream of a mechanism that has never been run.","Weeks 3-4 already open spec-kit and superpowers repos, so the test is one line."],"flip":"If the anchor does not survive another tool's write, the review file design changes and this question is replaced.","linked":["FF8","FL12","FL18"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:45","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:100","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:106","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","merged: FL15"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A baseline on first open is review state.","recWhy":"Ten strangers bring their own repositories, and the plan says neither what their first document looks like nor where a write appears. The natural baseline is the working tree against the last commit, so that anything uncommitted at first open is unreviewed and a fresh clone is clean. And that baseline needs git, which the web has only through the GitHub read path. Both halves sit downstream of a mechanism nobody has run: whether the review anchor survives another tool's write. Run that test first, at the start of Phase 1 on the spec-kit and superpowers repositories already being opened there, then set the last-commit baseline on whatever the anchor turns out to be. The surface is no longer this card's to decide; Q3 put the pilot on the web, and the label was corrected to say so."},{"id":"FL18","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Lifecycle","weight":"critical","q":"Does a span have a seen state between changed and reviewed, or only the two?","lede":"The review file records reviewed-by and reviewed-at and nothing else, so scrolling past a span and judging it are one event.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Untouched","meta":"hash matches","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Changed","meta":"hash differs","via":"a write","tone":"warn","current":true},{"label":"Seen","meta":"scrolled into view","via":"scroll","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Reviewed","meta":"accept, revert or skip","via":"a key","tone":"good"}],"note":"Only Untouched and Reviewed exist in the separate file today. Seen is the state the store cannot express.","caption":"The review lifecycle, and the missing middle."},"stakes":"Without a seen state the bulk-clear gate, the exit measure and any dismissal rate read one boolean.","state":["Each review-file line holds path, byte range, hash, reviewed-by, reviewed-at: one state, no action kind.","The drawer offers accept, revert and skip; the spec names none.","The demo clears on reading; the exit measure counts acts."],"tension":["A gated bulk clear needs viewport tracking, which is a seen state renamed.","Clinical alerting is one boolean at scale: 12,659 of 14,612 alerts overridden, 88.3% of typed reasons meaningless."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Three states, and the separate file records which act cleared a span","what":"Changed, seen and reviewed, with an action kind: scrolled, accepted, reverted, skipped, bulk.","gains":["The exit measure can tell a reader from a clicker","The only shape where a reader and a person who merely scrolled past are ever distinguishable."],"costs":["One field, one transition, and viewport tracking the code lacks","Viewport tracking, the mechanism a gated bulk clear also needs, does not exist in the code."],"system":"An action kind and a seen transition.","screens":"Two highlight treatments rather than one.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Two states, and only an explicit act clears a span","gains":["The counter means judged, and no viewport tracking","Matches the clinical-alerting lesson directly: a boolean at scale invites near-total override."],"costs":["Contradicts the demo, and every span costs a keystroke","Contradicts the demo's own premise, that opening a file is what clears its highlights."],"system":"No change.","screens":"The demo has to be re-cut.","money":"None.","what":"Two states, and only an explicit act clears a span. The counter means judged and there is no viewport tracking; it contradicts the demo, and every span costs a keystroke."},{"k":"c","label":"Two states, and scrolling past a span clears it","gains":["Matches the demo, zero friction on a long document","Zero friction on a long document, matching exactly what the demo shows happening today."],"costs":["Scrolling fast clears a file; the measure counts scrolling","Fast scrolling would clear a whole file, and the exit measure would be counting scroll speed."],"system":"Viewport tracking for the wrong purpose.","screens":"No panel act is required.","money":"None.","what":"Two states, and scrolling past a span clears it. Matches the demo with zero friction on a long document; scrolling fast clears a file, and the measure counts scrolling."},{"k":"d","label":"Two states now, with an action-kind field reserved in the separate file","gains":["No behaviour to build; readers do not break later","No new behaviour ships now, so no pilot reader is exposed to code that does not exist yet."],"costs":["Every pilot measurement is taken on the boolean","Every pilot number collected in the meantime is still only ever a boolean, not an action kind."],"system":"A field nothing writes yet.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Two states now, with an action-kind field reserved in the review file. Nothing to build and nothing breaks later; every pilot measurement is taken on a boolean."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Three states settle the demo and exit-measure disagreement: fade at seen, clear at reviewed.","The review file needs an action kind anyway, so only the seen transition is new."],"flip":"If pilot counts show seen and reviewed within a few per cent, the extra state buys nothing.","linked":["E37","FL11","FL19","FL20","FL5","FL6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:45","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:459","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Span states are review state.","recWhy":"The review file records reviewed-by and reviewed-at and nothing else, so scrolling past a span and judging it are one event, and the demo and the exit measure disagree about which one they mean. Clinical alerting is the cautionary case: one boolean at scale produced 12,659 overrides of 14,612 alerts and 88.3 percent of the typed reasons were meaningless. Three states, changed, seen and reviewed, settle the disagreement: fade at seen, clear at reviewed, and record which act cleared it, scrolled, accepted, reverted, skipped or bulk. The review file needs an action kind anyway under FL5, so only the seen transition is new, and viewport tracking is the one piece of code that does not exist. If pilot counts show seen and reviewed within a few percent of each other, the extra state bought nothing and it collapses."},{"id":"FL19","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"The review surface","weight":"critical","q":"Is the primary surface every changed span, or only the spans a rule selects?","lede":"Every review product converged on an exhaustible list, and every measurement of one says it stops being read.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Prescribing sessions","n":500274,"unit":"sessions","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Sessions where any of 31 rules fired","n":935,"unit":"sessions","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Of those alerts, accepted","n":57.5,"unit":"per cent","tone":"good"}],"note":"The comparison: enumerated alerting is overridden 55-98% across 34 studies.","caption":"The alerting system that earned acceptance fired in 0.19% of sessions."},"stakes":"The plan's panel is an exhaustible list, and the measured systems that keep acceptance fire rarely.","state":["Review state ships a panel with a counter; the tree carries unreviewed bars.","The panel is a persistent list rather than a per-session diff."],"tension":["The one clinical system that earned acceptance fired in 0.19% of 500,274 sessions.","Suppression means ranking, and the product's core rule says every view derives from the file."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Suppression by default, with the full list one click away","gains":["Matches every measured system that kept acceptance","The one clinical system that earned real acceptance fired in 0.19 percent of 500,274 sessions, not on every span."],"costs":["A wrong rule hides the thing that mattered","A wrong suppression rule hides exactly the span a reader most needed to see."],"system":"The filter must derive from the bytes.","screens":"A filtered default and a full list.","money":"None.","what":"Suppression by default, with the full list one click away. Matches every measured system that kept acceptance; a wrong rule hides the thing that mattered."},{"k":"b","label":"Enumeration as today: every changed span, in document order","gains":["No rule to explain, and nothing hidden","Matches the projection law's own core claim: every view derives from the file, nothing hidden."],"costs":["The list stops being read before it ends","A list long enough stops being read before a person reaches its end."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Enumeration as today: every changed span in document order. No rule to explain and nothing hidden, and the list stops being read before it ends."},{"k":"c","label":"A budget rather than a filter: a fixed number of spans a sitting","gains":["Respects the measured wear-out without ranking anything","Sidesteps building any ranking logic while still respecting the measured wear-out effect."],"costs":["An arbitrary number, and deferred spans still accumulate","The budget number is picked, not measured, and deferred spans keep piling up regardless."],"system":"A counter and a cut-off, no ranking.","screens":"The panel gains an end-of-batch state.","money":"None.","what":"A budget rather than a filter: a fixed number of spans per sitting. Respects the measured wear-out without ranking; an arbitrary number, and deferred spans accumulate."},{"k":"d","label":"Enumeration, plus a dismissal rate that retires a span class","gains":["Suppression is learned rather than guessed","Turns the ranking rule into something the pilot's own usage teaches rather than something guessed upfront."],"costs":["Needs live counts, so the first weeks enumerate","Needs live counts to work at all, so the first weeks of the pilot enumerate everything anyway."],"system":"Per-class dismissal counts, locally.","screens":"Classes quietly stop appearing.","money":"None.","what":"Enumerate, plus a dismissal rate that retires a span class once ignored past a threshold. Suppression is learned rather than guessed; the first weeks enumerate while counts build."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Systems that keep acceptance fire rarely; systems that enumerate are overridden 55-98%.","A deterministic filter keeps that core rule, and the full list hides nothing."],"flip":"If the 40-hunk test shows strangers finishing an exhaustive list without accept-all, (b) stands.","linked":["D4","FL11","FL18","FL4","FL6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:383","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:458","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34450669/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35673040/","https://static1.smartbear.co/support/media/resources/cc/book/code-review-cisco-case-study.pdf","https://sback.it/publications/icse2018seip.pdf","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5aa2b9ede5274a3e391e37f3/MHRA_GxP_data_integrity_guide_March_edited_Final.pdf"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Which spans show is review state.","recWhy":"Every review product that measured itself converged on the same finding: an exhaustible list stops being read before it ends, and enumerating systems are overridden between 55 and 98 percent of the time across 34 studies. The one clinical system that earned acceptance fired in 0.19 percent of half a million sessions. So the surface has to be selective by default and honest about it: a deterministic filter chooses what to show first, and the full list is one click away so nothing is hidden. On instruction files the filter has an obvious first rule, agent-authored changes to a file other agents obey come before anything else. The risk is a wrong rule hiding the thing that mattered, which is why the full list has to stay reachable and why D4's dismissal rate matters. If the forty-hunk test shows strangers finishing an exhaustive list without reaching for accept-all, b stands.","dupes":{"dropped":["D24"],"areas":["Design, UI & attention"],"why":"Design, UI & attention asked the same question with the same four choices and recommended the same answer, option a here: surface only what a stated rule selects, and keep the rest one step away.","agree":true}},{"id":"FL2","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Keyboard","weight":"high","q":"Which keyboard model ships for review: single letters, modifiers, or letters scoped to the drawer?","lede":"Three written key maps disagree, the code has none, and single letters raise a WCAG level-A issue.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":118,"cols":[{"title":"Round 21 map: walk-and-act"},{"title":"Round 24 map: Tab and y/n"},{"title":"Prototype drawer","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Walk","cells":["j / k","Tab / Shift-Tab","j / k, per the hint chip"]},{"label":"Accept","cells":["a","y, Enter accepts all","a"]},{"label":"Reject or revert","cells":["r means reject","n","r means revert"]},{"label":"Third verb","cells":["e, edit then accept","Esc discards","s, skip; absent from the hint"]},{"label":"WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.4","cells":["fires under a screen reader","clear, modifiers only","fires under a screen reader"]}],"note":"r means reject in one map and revert in another. Those are opposite acts on one span.","caption":"Three maps for one decision, none of them in the code."},"stakes":"The key `r` means reject in one map and revert in another, which are opposite acts on one span.","state":["The code's hotkey type allows only `mod+k`, `mod+p` and `mod+g`, and no review key exists.","The plan says one key reverts any span, and never names it."],"tension":["Single letters are fastest, and WCAG SC 2.1.4 flags them at level A.","A remap screen costs 3-4 days, and no screen in the plan draws one."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Four keyboard models in the corpus for one decision","cols":["Source","Walk","Accept","Reject / revert","Other"],"rows":[["r21-s6:87","j / k","a","r = reject","e = edit-then-accept"],["r24-v4:146","Tab / Shift-Tab","y, Enter = accept all","n","Esc = discard"],["prototype:459 (S4 drawer buttons)","n/a","a · accept","r · revert","s · skip"],["prototype:458 (same drawer, hint chip)","j k","a","r","`s` missing from the hint"],["prototype:387 (S2 hover card)","n/a","\"Mark reviewed\"","\"Revert · ⌘⇧Z\"","n/a"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What exists in the code today","items":[["Global hotkeys bound","3","use-hotkey.ts:5 (mod+k, mod+p, mod+g)"],["Authoring keys bound","3","CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:224-226 (Mod-b/i/e)"],["Review keys bound","0","no review-state code in src/ (grep: review.jsonl, reviewState, unreviewed → 0 files)"],["Customisable keymap","none","r24-v1:36 costs it at 3 to 4 days and names this exact line: \"`use-hotkey.ts:5` literally types the world as…\""],["WCAG remedy on record","remap, modifier, or focus-scope","k4-accessibility.md:106, SC 2.1.4, level A"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Single letters everywhere, with a remap screen in the pilot","gains":["Fastest map, and matches the drawer as drawn","Single letters are the fastest binding measured in the corpus's own comparison table."],"costs":["Adds a 3-4 day remap screen the plan never draws","No review key exists in the code at all today: zero hits for review.jsonl or unreviewed."],"system":"A stored keymap and a rebinding screen.","screens":"Adds a Settings hotkeys screen.","money":"None.","what":"Single letters everywhere, with a remap screen shipped in the pilot. The fastest map and it matches the drawer as drawn; the remap screen is three or four days nobody planned."},{"k":"b","label":"Modifier keys only: j and k walk, Cmd-Enter accepts, Cmd-Shift-Z reverts","gains":["Clears SC 2.1.4 with no remap UI","Modifier keys are what the WCAG remedy itself names as one of three compliant options."],"costs":["Slower per hunk; the drawer's buttons are redrawn","Every prototype screen shown so far draws single letters, not modifier chords."],"system":"One keymap table.","screens":"Redraws the drawer's hint chip.","money":"None.","what":"Modifier keys only: j and k to walk, Cmd-Enter to accept, Cmd-Shift-Z to revert. Clears the accessibility criterion with no remap screen; slower per hunk, and the drawer is redrawn."},{"k":"c","label":"Single letters, active only while the review drawer has focus","gains":["Single-letter speed, no remap screen, one meaning for `r`","Costs no remap screen, the 3 to 4 day item the customisable-keymap line already prices."],"costs":["New focus rule; keys do nothing outside the drawer","Focus-scoping is a new interaction rule nothing in the code implements yet."],"system":"One focus rule around the drawer.","screens":"The drawer becomes a focus container.","money":"None.","what":"Single letters that work only while the review drawer has focus. Single-letter speed, no remap screen, one meaning for r; a new focus rule, and the keys do nothing outside the drawer."},{"k":"d","label":"No review keyboard in the pilot, buttons only","gains":["Removes a decision, costs nothing, no accessibility exposure","Zero accessibility exposure, since a mouse-only control needs no keyboard remedy at all."],"costs":["A 40-hunk review becomes mouse-driven, the slowest version sold","Every keyboard model in the comparison table assumes some key does something."],"system":"No change.","screens":"The hint chip comes off.","money":"None.","what":"No review keyboard in the pilot; buttons only. Removes the decision and the accessibility exposure, and a forty-hunk review becomes the slowest thing anyone has sold."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["One focus rule gives single-letter speed and modifier-level compliance.","The accessibility research reads the criterion as allowing focus-only shortcuts, so no remap screen is needed."],"flip":"If review acts run from the document body, focus scoping cannot apply and only modifiers comply.","linked":["D15","FL18","FL19","FL3"],"sources":["src/modules/app-shell/presentation/use-hotkey.ts:5","src/modules/app-shell/presentation/KnowledgeUI.tsx:56","src/modules/editor/presentation/CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:224","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:458","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:459","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r21-s6-renders-that-earn-it.md:87","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v4-ai-native-editing.md:146","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r24-v1-table-stakes.md:36","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k4-accessibility.md:106","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A keyboard model for review needs review.","recWhy":"Three written key maps disagree with each other, the code binds no review key at all, and single-letter shortcuts raise a WCAG level-A issue because a typed letter in the wrong place does something to your document. The way out is scope. Single letters that act only while the review drawer has focus are as fast as the drawer as drawn, and the accessibility research reads the criterion as allowing focus-only shortcuts, so no remap screen has to be built and drawn. Modifier-only keys comply but make every hunk slower and force a redraw. Buttons only make a forty-hunk review a mouse job. The one thing that would break c is if review acts are triggered from the document body rather than the drawer, because then focus scoping cannot apply and only modifiers comply."},{"id":"FL5","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Bulk actions","weight":"high","q":"Does a one-click Mark all read ship in the pilot, and what may it clear?","lede":"The prototype draws two bulk-clear buttons, and the critique names a bulk clear as what makes review theatre.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Launcher, as drawn","lines":[{"t":"3 projects, 128 documents","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"7 unreviewed","mark":"warn"},{"t":"sorted unreviewed first","mark":"neutral"}]},"after":{"title":"One click later","lines":[{"t":"3 projects, 128 documents","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"0 unreviewed","mark":"good"},{"t":"nothing was read","mark":"stop"},{"t":"the separate file cannot tell","mark":"stop"}]},"via":"Mark all read","note":"The counter is retention lever one and the pilot's exit measure. Both go to zero together.","caption":"The counter emptied without a document being read."},"stakes":"A button that zeroes the counter without reading removes the reason to return and the evidence.","state":["The prototype has Mark all read and accept all in file.","The review state spec lists the highlight, one-key revert and a counter, and no bulk action."],"tension":["Opening frontmatter is how the count reaches zero, and a bulk clear removes that trigger.","The pilot's week-2 gate is one review action, which a bulk clear satisfies."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What each artefact says about bulk clearing","cols":["Artefact","Position"],"rows":[["Prototype's answer-in-place screen (`prototype:523`)","\"Mark all read\": one click, whole file, no gate"],["Prototype's refactor-preview screen (`prototype:648`)","\"accept all in file\" alongside per-hunk accept/skip"],["r21-s7:61, flow 2 step 5","\"`Accept all` is **disabled** while any uncited paragraph remains\""],["CRITIQUE.md:643","\"If ≥5 hit 'accept all' in both, the interaction is theatre and MVP-0 has no demo\""],["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56 (review state spec)","No bulk action specified at all"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the counter is load-bearing for","items":[["Retention lever #1","the counter","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425: \"opening frontmatter is how the count goes to zero\""],["Exit measure input","spans reviewed (accept, revert, skip)","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19"],["Pilot gate","≥1 review action in week 2","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22"],["States the drawer already offers","3: accept, revert, skip","prototype:459; the sidecar spec at :135 names none of them"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No bulk clear in the pilot","gains":["The counter means one thing; the pilot measures truly","Matches the retention lever as written: the count reaches zero only by opening the file."],"costs":["A 200-span backlog's only escape is abandoning the file","A 200-span backlog has no way out except abandoning the file entirely."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Removes two prototype buttons.","money":"None.","what":"No bulk clear in the pilot. The counter means one thing and the pilot measures truly; a two-hundred-span backlog's only escape is abandoning the file."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship Mark all read, recorded in the separate file as a bulk clear","gains":["An escape exists and bulk clears stay separable","The prototype already draws exactly this control with no gate, so it costs no new design."],"costs":["A clicker still clears the counter","CRITIQUE.md names the exact failure: if five or more people hit accept-all, the interaction is theatre."],"system":"The review file grows an action kind.","screens":"Keeps the button, adds copy.","money":"None.","what":"Ship Mark all read, recorded in the review file as a bulk clear distinct from a reviewed span. An escape exists; a clicker still clears the counter, and the file knows it was a click."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship it gated: enabled only when every span has been scrolled into view","gains":["Closest to what a review is meant to mean","The only option that requires a span to have actually been seen before it counts as reviewed."],"costs":["Needs viewport tracking; a disabled button reads as broken","Viewport tracking is code that does not exist anywhere in the corpus today."],"system":"Depends on the seen-state decision.","screens":"The button gains a disabled state.","money":"None.","what":"Ship it gated: enabled only once every span has scrolled into view. Closest to what a review is meant to mean; needs viewport tracking, and a disabled button reads as broken."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship Mark all read ungated, as drawn","gains":["Matches the prototype, and is the least surprising","Matches the prototype exactly, so nothing about the drawn screens needs to change."],"costs":["Counter, lever and exit measure clear in one click","The same CRITIQUE line calls this exact shape the interaction that makes MVP-0 have no demo."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Ship Mark all read ungated, exactly as the prototype draws it. Least surprising, and the counter, the retention lever and the exit measure all clear in one click."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A backlog with no escape loses a returning user, so one must exist.","The honest version costs one field, and the review file needs an action kind anyway."],"flip":"If pilot counts show bulk clears dominating individual clears, the interaction has failed and the unit changes.","linked":["FL18","FL20","FL4"],"sources":["docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:523","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:648","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r21-s7-flow-design.md:61","docs/CRITIQUE.md:643","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Mark all read is review state.","recWhy":"The prototype draws two bulk-clear buttons and the critique names a bulk clear as precisely what turns review into theatre, so both instincts are right and the question is whether the two can be told apart afterwards. A returning user with a two-hundred-span backlog and no escape abandons the file, which loses them; that is the case for shipping it. A clicker who clears the counter in one click satisfies the week-two gate falsely; that is the case against. Recording the bulk clear as its own action kind in the review file resolves it for the cost of one field. The escape exists, and the exit measure can tell a reader from a clicker because the file says which one happened. The review file needs an action kind anyway under FL18. If pilot counts show bulk clears dominating, the interaction has failed and the unit changes."},{"id":"FL6","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"The counter","weight":"high","q":"What does the review counter count, and may it be a dot?","lede":"The plan and the PRD take opposite positions on the badge, and the prototype shows four numbers for one document.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Answer-in-place screen, one document","panes":[{"label":"Body and breadcrumb","w":36,"rows":[{"label":"written by spec-kit, 3 questions for you","meta":"prototype:495","tone":"warn"},{"label":"6 highlighted spans in the body","meta":"prototype:503-510","tone":"neutral"}]},{"label":"Outline","w":30,"rows":[{"label":"Functional requirements 5 of 5","meta":"prototype:520","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Non-goals 1 of 1","meta":"prototype:520","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"total 6","meta":"agrees with the body","tone":"accent"}]},{"label":"Status bar","w":34,"rows":[{"label":"7 unreviewed spans, 92%","meta":"prototype:514","tone":"stop"},{"label":"2 questions open","meta":"prototype:514","tone":"warn"}]}],"note":"7 divided by 0.92 is 7.61 spans, so the printed percentage has no possible span base.","caption":"Four surfaces, four numbers, one document."},"stakes":"The counter is the launcher sort key, the tree bar, the panel headline and the exit measure.","state":["The plan makes it retention lever one, and PRD v2 bans red-dot counts.","Five surfaces carry the count, including a dot on the editor tab."],"tension":["A bar reading as progress implies bytes: a one-word span is not a section.","Anthropic's VS Code extension already ships an unread dot and a tab badge."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The percentage cannot be a span ratio on any screen (computed from the prototype's own printed numbers)","items":[["Editor, auth.md","3 unreviewed · 64% → 4.69 total spans","prototype:394"],["Review panel","3 unreviewed · 88% → 3.41 total spans","prototype:455, :458"],["Answer in place (clarify), spec.md","7 unreviewed · 92% → 7.61 total spans","prototype:514"],["Split view","2 unreviewed · 31% → 6.45 total spans","prototype:591"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Three counts of one document on one prototype screen","cols":["Surface","Reads","Line"],"rows":[["Status bar","7 unreviewed spans · 92%","prototype:514"],["Outline","5 + 1 = 6 unreviewed","prototype:520"],["Document body","6 highlighted spans (`class=\"m1\"`)","prototype:503-510"],["Breadcrumb","3 questions for you","prototype:495"],["Status bar and right side panel, questions","2 questions open / 2","prototype:514, :517"],["Tree bar, spec.md","92% (width, no unit); plan.md and tasks.md both 100%","prototype:484-486"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Everywhere the number appears","items":[["Launcher card bars","8 cards: 64%, 88%, 31%, 12%, 45%, 22% unreviewed; two read \"all reviewed\"","prototype:304-311"],["Launcher status","7 unreviewed across 128 documents","prototype:315"],["File tree","per-file bar","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409"],["Editor status bar","3 unreviewed spans · 64%","prototype:394"],["Review drawer headline","3 unreviewed in this file","prototype:458"],["Exit measure","spans reviewed","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two positions, both current","cols":["Document","Says","Line"],"rows":[["Plan v15, §17 retention","The counter is lever #1; the return trigger is created by the user's own agent, daily","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425"],["PRD v2, §17 retention","\"Banned outright: streaks · badges · XP or levels · … · red-dot unread counts\"","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:1783"],["PRD v2, §18.5 affordances","\"no badges, dots or status-bar chips **as a discovery strategy**\": scoped to discovery, not to the number itself","FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2:2029"],["Plan v15, threats","Anthropic's extension already ships \"a missing unread dot\" and \"the editor tab badge showing unread\" (CHANGELOG 2.1.261)","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560"],["Prototype","Count shown in 5 places simultaneously","prototype:315, :304, :329, :394, :458"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the plan claims for the counter, and its evidence class","items":[["Retention levers, listed in the plan","7","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425-431"],["Levers with evidence that they retain","0","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:24: \"Reasoning. No evidence that any lever retains\""],["Editors publishing retention data","0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:417"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Spans everywhere, plain number, never a dot","gains":["Matches the store, so the number cannot drift","Matches the exit measure directly: spans reviewed is already the number the pilot counts."],"costs":["A one-word span counts as a 400-word section","Every launcher card bar in the prototype is drawn as a percentage, not spans, so those all change."],"system":"One counting function, four surfaces.","screens":"Recomputes four bars; deletes the dot.","money":"None.","what":"Count spans everywhere, as a plain number, never a dot. Matches the store so the number cannot drift; a one-word span counts the same as a four-hundred-word section."},{"k":"b","label":"Bytes for the bar, spans for the count","gains":["The bar means what a progress bar implies","A byte-based bar cannot drift from the file the way a hand-maintained percentage can."],"costs":["Two units, and the headline stops being holdable","None of the eight prototype screens showing a percentage currently distinguish the two units."],"system":"Two derived figures instead of one.","screens":"Bar and count both need labels.","money":"None.","what":"Bytes for the bar, spans for the count. The bar then means what a progress bar implies; two units, and the headline number stops being something a person can hold."},{"k":"c","label":"Files at the launcher and the tree, spans inside the document","gains":["One small integer at the top level","Keeps the launcher's single integer, the number the plan calls the daily return trigger."],"costs":["Loses the per-span claim where a buyer compares","A buyer comparing tools sees only a file count, not the per-span claim the product is built on."],"system":"A per-file rollup over the review file.","screens":"Launcher and tree change, not the panel.","money":"None.","what":"Files at the launcher and in the tree, spans inside a document. One small integer at the top level; the per-span claim is lost where a buyer compares products."},{"k":"d","label":"No persistent count: the panel lists what is unread when opened","gains":["Honours the ban outright, cleanest against the controls budget","Zero retention levers in the plan currently have any evidence they retain anyone at all."],"costs":["Removes the plan's only daily return trigger","Removes the one trigger the plan names as created by the user's own agent, daily."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Deletes the bars and the tab dot.","money":"The retention argument is rewritten.","what":"No persistent count anywhere; the panel lists what is unread when opened. Honours the badge ban outright, and removes the plan's only daily return trigger."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Spans are the only unit that costs nothing and cannot drift from the store.","The PRD's badge ban is scoped to discovery, so a plain number is inside it."],"flip":"If the 40-hunk test moves the review unit to files or sections, the counter follows and (c) wins.","linked":["FL19","FL4","FL5"],"sources":["docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:514","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:520","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:495","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:329","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:1783","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:2029","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19","merged: FL7"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The counter counts review state.","recWhy":"The plan makes the unreviewed count its first retention lever and the PRD bans red-dot counts, and the prototype resolves that by showing four different numbers for one document, which is worse than either. Spans are the only unit that costs nothing to compute and cannot drift from the review file, because they are what the file records. A plain number is inside the PRD's ban, which is scoped to discovery badges, not to a count a person asked to see. The honest weakness is that a one-word span and a section count the same, and a bar drawn from spans implies bytes it does not measure. That is why D4 cuts the surfaces to three and D23 removes every percentage. If the forty-hunk test moves the unit to files or sections, the counter follows and c wins."},{"id":"FL11","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Kill lines","weight":"high","q":"Which review numbers stop the build: the five-second highlight test, and a dismissal rate?","lede":"Every other Phase 1 test has a number that stops the build. The highlight test has none.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Five people, editor screenshot, 5 s","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"They name the highlight","note":"changed, provenance, highlighted, error","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"No threshold stated","note":"the plan names the words, not a count","tone":"stop","edge":"today"},{"label":"Build proceeds either way","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Ten people, 6 of 10 line","note":"matches every other arm and the exit test","tone":"accent","edge":"proposed"},{"label":"Redesign the treatment","note":"a treatment change, not a scope change","tone":"good"}],"note":"Tricorder puts an analyser on probation at a 10% not-useful rate; nothing here is instrumented at all.","caption":"The one test with no line, and the line proposed for it."},"stakes":"A test with no threshold gets argued with, which is how three refuted headlines survived.","state":["Weeks 1-2 shows five people the editor screenshot for five seconds.","The plan names the failing answers, with no count and no consequence."],"tension":["Ten people matches every other arm and the exit test; five does not.","Google's Tricorder puts an analyser on probation at 10% not-useful, having observed about 5%."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Kill lines in the plan, and the one without","cols":["Test","Threshold","Line"],"rows":[["Ten developers, review mock","under 4 of 10 unprompted → stop","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321, :337"],["Engine refusal class","over 10 of 7,969 by day 24 → falsified","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:327"],["Ten strangers, the pilot's exit","6 of 10 keep it","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206"],["Obsidian plugin channel","under 200 installs in 14 days leads to a kill decision, but the channel is cut at line 304, so the line is stale","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204"],["Five-second highlight test","none stated","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352, :411"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the highlight is competing against, in the one population with a vote","items":[["Nested-code-block rendering bug","501 likes, 117 posts, 18,331 views","Obsidian forum, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410"],["\"List of changes made to shared vault since last view\"","0 likes, 251 views, 1 post in nearly 3 years","same forum, same date"],["\"Tracking changes within notes\"","1 like","same"],["\"Suggestion Mode like Google Docs/Word\"","3 likes","same"],["People already scheduled for weeks 1 to 2 conversations","35","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352 (10 developers, 5 who bill, 20 team conversations)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Both get a line: 6 of 10 on the highlight test, and a live dismissal rate","what":"A span class ignored past a stated rate stops being surfaced.","gains":["The signal is checked before build and after launch","Matches every other arm's kill-line pattern: a number and a stated consequence, not just a name."],"costs":["Five more conversations, and a local dismissal counter","Adds a local dismissal counter that nothing in the code today implements."],"system":"Per-class dismissal counts in the local file.","screens":"A rule about what stops appearing.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"The highlight test only, widened to ten people at 6 of 10","gains":["Comparable with the other arms, and cheap to act on","Ten matches the size already used for the ten-developer and ten-stranger tests elsewhere."],"costs":["Nothing catches the signal decaying after launch","Answers only whether people notice at launch, not whether the feeling survives daily use."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"The highlight test only, widened to ten people at six of ten. Comparable with the other arms and cheap to act on; nothing catches the signal decaying after launch."},{"k":"c","label":"The dismissal rate only, instrumented from the first pilot week","gains":["Measures action rather than naming; no week-1 load","Measures what people actually do, closer to Tricorder's 10 percent not-useful probation line."],"costs":["The treatment is built before it is checked","The surface it measures is built and shipped before any number exists to check it against."],"system":"Adds the dismissal count.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"The dismissal rate only, instrumented from the first pilot week. Measures what people do rather than what they say; the treatment is built before it is checked."},{"k":"d","label":"Both advisory: signals that feed the design, not gates","gains":["Schedule certainty, and no number to defend","Cheapest to run: 35 people are already scheduled across the other weeks 1 to 2 conversations."],"costs":["A test with no line leaves the surface unfalsifiable","Every other arm in the plan has a number that can say stop; this one alone would not."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Both as advisory signals that feed the design rather than gates. Schedule certainty and no number to defend, and a surface that can never be proven wrong."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Ten and 6-of-10 match the plan's sampling, so the result sits beside the other arms.","The dismissal rate is the only thing that catches the signal decaying after launch."],"flip":"If the mock test returns under 4 of 10, the product stops at week 3 first.","linked":["D18","FL19"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19","https://static.googleusercontent.com/media/research.google.com/en//pubs/archive/43322.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35673040/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"These numbers stop a build that is not running.","recWhy":"Every other Phase 1 test carries a number that stops the build, and the highlight test, five people looking at a screenshot for five seconds, names its failing answers with no count and no consequence. Ten people at six of ten matches the sampling every other arm uses, so the result sits beside them instead of being an anecdote. The dismissal rate is the other half, and it is the one that matters after launch. Google's Tricorder puts an analyser on probation at ten percent not-useful, having observed about five, and a span class ignored past a stated rate should stop being surfaced. One number before build, one number after, and the surface can be falsified at both points. If the mock test returns under four of ten, the product stops at week 3 before any of this runs."},{"id":"FL20","cat":"Flow & interaction","sub":"Lifecycle","weight":"medium","q":"Does clearing a span require a typed reason, and on which acts?","lede":"Two evidence lines point opposite ways: alert fatigue says one keystroke, the compliance buyer wants a why.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":132,"cols":[{"title":"No reason"},{"title":"Reason on every act"},{"title":"Reason on rejects only","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Keystrokes per span","cells":["one","one plus a sentence","one, or a sentence on a revert"]},{"label":"What gets recorded","cells":["who and when","who, when and why","who, when, and why on reverts"]},{"label":"A compliance reader gets","cells":["a list of hashes","a justification per span","the exceptions carry a reason"]},{"label":"What the evidence says","cells":["fatigue evidence favours it","88.3% of typed reasons were meaningless","exception reporting is adjudicated"]},{"label":"Cost","cells":["none","a text field on the common path","a text field on the rare path"]}],"note":"No regulation opened requires a reason, or per-span review at all.","caption":"Where a typed reason is worth its keystroke."},"stakes":"A reason field on the common act is what made 88.3% of clinical justifications meaningless.","state":["The review file records reviewed-by and reviewed-at, and no reason.","The drawer offers accept, revert and skip with no text field."],"tension":["Of 14,612 clinical alerts, 12,659 were overridden and 88.3% of typed justifications were meaningless.","No regulation opened requires a reason, or per-span review at all.","The UK regulator MHRA permits an audit trail reviewed by exception rather than as a full list."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No reason anywhere: one keystroke clears a span","gains":["The fastest act, which the fatigue evidence favours","Directly matches what the fatigue evidence favours: the fewer required steps, the more real the act."],"costs":["A revert records no lesson; the buyer gets hashes","A revert with no typed reason leaves a buyer holding only hashes, nothing to learn from."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"No reason anywhere; one keystroke clears a span. The fastest act, which the fatigue evidence favours; a revert records no lesson, and a buyer gets a file of hashes."},{"k":"b","label":"A reason on rejections and overrides, optional on approvals","gains":["The common path stays fast, the rare path informative","Keeps the common accept path exactly as fast as option a while adding record where it matters most."],"costs":["Two acts behave differently, and revert slows","Two acts now behave differently, and the rarer one, revert, becomes the slower one."],"system":"One optional string on the action line.","screens":"An inline field on revert.","money":"None.","what":"A typed reason on rejections and overrides, optional on approvals. The common path stays one keystroke and the rare path is informative; two acts behave differently."},{"k":"c","label":"A reason on every act, stored in the separate file","gains":["The strongest record for an answerable buyer","The strongest paper trail available for a buyer who has to answer for what was reviewed."],"costs":["This design produced 88.3% meaningless reasons","This exact shape, a reason on every act, is what produced 88.3 percent meaningless reasons in the cited study."],"system":"A required string on every line.","screens":"A field on every act.","money":"None.","what":"A reason on every act, stored in the review file. The strongest record for whoever is answerable, and exactly the design that produced 88.3 percent meaningless reasons."},{"k":"d","label":"No reason in the pilot; add the field when a compliance buyer asks","gains":["No design work now; a real requirement shapes it","No design work happens on a requirement nobody has actually asked for yet."],"costs":["The pilot's reverts carry no reason to learn from","Every revert made during the pilot itself is recorded with nothing to learn from later."],"system":"No change now, a format change later.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"No reason in the pilot; add the field when a compliance buyer asks for it. No design work now, and the pilot's reverts carry nothing to learn from."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The cheap act stays one keystroke, so the fatigue evidence holds on the common path.","A revert is the only place the product learns what the agent got wrong."],"flip":"If the five who bill for documents say an approval without a basis is worthless, (c) is the ask.","linked":["FL18","FL5"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:459","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5aa2b9ede5274a3e391e37f3/MHRA_GxP_data_integrity_guide_March_edited_Final.pdf","https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-01-01/title-21.xml?part=11"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Clearing a span is review state.","recWhy":"Two lines of evidence point opposite ways. Alert fatigue says a clearing act has to be one keystroke or people stop doing it; the compliance buyer wants a why beside every decision. The clinical data settles which acts get the field: of 14,612 alerts, 12,659 were overridden and 88.3 percent of the typed justifications were meaningless, because a reason demanded on every act becomes a reason typed on none. So the common path, approval, stays one keystroke, and the rare path, a rejection or an override, asks why. A revert is also the only moment the product learns what the agent got wrong, and on an instruction file that reason is the next learned rule. No regulation opened requires a reason at all, and MHRA permits an audit trail reviewed by exception, so c is not required by anyone."},{"id":"D8","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Editor scope","weight":"critical","q":"Does the pilot ship four editor modes, or three?","lede":"Four modes is the largest surface, built only because every editor has them.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"Ship four","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Three, cut Split","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Two, Live and Read","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Modes showing the highlight","cells":["3 of 4, Edit does not","2 of 3, Edit does not","2 of 2"]},{"label":"Raw source available","cells":["yes","yes","no"]},{"label":"Dedicated screens to keep","cells":["the editor and the split screen","the editor","the editor"]},{"label":"Stated purpose that is not review","cells":["2 of 4: Edit and Split","1 of 3: Edit","0"]},{"label":"What the developer buyer misses","cells":["nothing","a side-by-side render","the source view; reads as a toy"]}],"note":"Purposes quoted from the wireframes doc; the editor's estimate costs the modes as inherited, not as new build.","caption":"What each cut keeps, and what it costs the buyer."},"stakes":"Table-stakes surface is paid for before the novel feature exists, at 1.21 days weekly.","state":["The editor ships Live, Edit, Split and Read: 7 days of engine fixes, 8 of wiring.","Edit mode does not show the highlight at all, by the wireframes' rule."],"tension":["Audits called the table-stakes cost larger than the gap in the new feature.","Four platforms now ship an agent-diff pane free, so nobody chooses on mode count."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The schedule the mode count is spent against","items":[["Engineering days for the pilot","about 70","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week, 2026-07-13 to 2026-09-08","1.21","10 of 58 calendar days touched src/, src-tauri/ or test/ (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227)"],["Sibling repo's best-ever rate","1.91/week","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Last 30 days on this repo","0.23/week","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Calendar weeks implied at 1.21","58","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["What the editor's own estimate covers","\"Exists. NF-1/NF-3 fixes (7d), engine wiring (8d)\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55: the modes are costed as inherited, not as new build"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each mode is for, and whether the highlight shows there","cols":["Mode","Stated purpose (docs/_wireframes.md:17)","Tint renders?"],"rows":[["Live (default)","\"the default and the one that matters\"","yes"],["Edit","\"when the markdown itself is the thing you are working on\"","no"],["Split","\"for learning the syntax or checking a render\"","yes, in the rendered pane"],["Read","\"for review and printing\"","yes"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship all four","gains":["Nothing a developer expects is missing","Matches what four separate platforms already ship free: an agent-diff pane, so parity here costs nothing extra to explain."],"costs":["Four modes, two themes, a 58-week schedule","58 calendar weeks at the measured build rate is over a year before a stranger sees the pilot."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship all four modes. Nothing a developer expects is missing; four modes, two themes and a 58-week schedule."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship two for the pilot (Live and Read) and add Edit and Split in the build after it","gains":["Both remaining modes show the review highlight","Both modes kept show the review highlight, the one thing this whole area exists to make visible."],"costs":["No raw-source mode reads as incomplete","No raw-source view at all reads as an incomplete editor to the developer buyer this targets."],"system":"Two modes wired.","screens":"The control loses two buttons.","money":"None.","what":"Ship two for the pilot, Live and Read, and add Edit and Split after. Both remaining modes show the highlight; no raw-source mode reads as incomplete to a developer."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship three (Live, Edit, Read) and cut Split","gains":["Keeps what the developer buyer looks for first","Cuts the weakest-job mode and its screen"],"costs":["The saving is small if the modes are inherited"],"system":"One mode and one screen removed.","screens":"The split screen goes.","money":"None.","what":"Ship three, Live, Edit and Read, and cut Split. Keeps what a developer looks for first and cuts the mode with the weakest job; a small saving if the modes are inherited."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship all four and budget zero days, as the editor's estimate already implies","gains":["Makes the editor estimate's assumption explicit","States plainly what the estimate already assumes, rather than leaving a zero-day mode count implicit."],"costs":["25 files diverged from sgnk-md; the fork is not free","25 of 226 files have already diverged from the sibling, so 'inherited for free' is not quite true today."],"system":"No change; the estimate is restated.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship all four and budget zero days, which is what the editor estimate already assumes. Makes the assumption explicit; 25 files have diverged from sgnk-md and the fork is not free."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Edit is what a developer looks for first; Split has the weakest job.","Cutting one mode is the cheapest first answer to the 58-week schedule."],"flip":"If auditing the sgnk-md fork shows all four modes survive the highlight work untouched, d is honest.","linked":["D11","D20","D9","FF2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:55","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:229","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","docs/_wireframes.md:17","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/c6-screens.md:21","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:361","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:530"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Four modes or three is the editor the first build ships.","recWhy":"Four modes is the largest surface in the editor and it exists because every editor has four, not because anyone chose them. Edit is what a developer looks for first, a raw-source view, and its absence reads as incomplete. Live is the mode with the counted vote behind it and the one an extension could never host. Read is where review happens. Split has the weakest job of the four and its own screen to maintain, and cutting it is the cheapest first answer to a 58-week schedule. Q5 carries this as three modes. The honest caveat is that if the modes are inherited untouched from sgnk-md the saving is small, and FF3's freeze means they mostly are. If auditing the fork shows all four survive the highlight work untouched, d is honest and the count stops mattering."},{"id":"D7","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"AI placement","weight":"high","q":"Does the AI prompt open at the cursor, or stay a docked strip?","lede":"Our screen report says every shipped product prompts at the cursor. We dock a strip.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Editor: where the AI prompt can be asked for","panes":[{"label":"Under the document","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"AI · this file · collapsed · ⌘J opens","meta":"","mark":"prompt","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Carries the your-key reassurance copy","meta":"nothing runs until you ask","mark":"","tone":"neutral"}]},{"label":"Right rail, bottom","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"AI edit · ⌘J","meta":"","mark":"prompt","tone":"warn"},{"label":"No review tab for pending AI hunks","meta":"what the report asked for","mark":"missing","tone":"stop"}]},{"label":"At the cursor","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Nothing","meta":"where every named incumbent puts it","mark":"","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"Both existing affordances are bound to the same shortcut.","caption":"Two entry points to one feature, and neither is at the cursor."},"stakes":"Two AI entry points on a screen whose stated goal is that AI does not compete.","state":["The editor screen ships a docked strip and a separate rail button.","The wireframes defend it: a sidebar makes AI a place you go.","The screens research report asked the opposite: prompt at the selection."],"tension":["This is the one placement question where a report gave a clear answer.","As a VS Code extension, the host would decide this for us."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three positions on where the AI prompt goes, all in the corpus","cols":["Source","Position","Reason given"],"rows":[["c6-screens.md:22, 2026-08-28","prompt at the cursor; side panel keeps a review tab","every shipped convention; a docked prompt \"reads as bolted-on\""],["_wireframes.md:11","docked strip under the document","\"a thing you do to what you are looking at\""],["prototype S2 (:391, :405)","both: docked strip and a side panel prompt button, both ⌘J","not stated"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the incumbent evidence actually establishes","items":[["Incumbents opening an AI panel expanded on load","0 of 5 named","Google Docs, Notion, Cursor, Zed, Obsidian Copilot (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:381)"],["Google Docs' stated behaviour","\"By default, the Gemini bar automatically minimizes to clear your screen.\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:381"],["AI affordances on our editor today","2","the strip (:391) and the rail button (:405), both bound to ⌘J"],["Reaction counts behind the hide-all-AI switch","412 (Zed), 30 (VS Code), 181 (Telegram)","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298, :382"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"At the cursor only; the rail button becomes the review surface for pending hunks","gains":["Matches every product the buyer already uses","One entry point, and the rail gains review"],"costs":["A first-run user must discover ⌘J","Zero of the five named incumbents, Google Docs among them, open an AI panel expanded by default."],"system":"One deletion, one relocation.","screens":"The editor loses the strip.","money":"None.","what":"The prompt opens where the cursor is and nowhere else, and the rail button becomes the place pending hunks are reviewed. One entry point; a first-run user has to find the shortcut."},{"k":"b","label":"Docked strip only; delete the rail button","gains":["Keeps the discoverability it was built for","Matches the wireframes' own stated reasoning for why AI should be a place you go, not a popup."],"costs":["Ships against the only convention evidence we have","The one placement question with a clear report answer, and this ships the opposite of it."],"system":"One deletion.","screens":"The rail loses a button.","money":"None.","what":"Docked strip only, and delete the rail button. Keeps the discoverability the strip was built for, and ships against the only convention evidence the research found."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep both","gains":["No work","Requires editing nothing, since both surfaces already exist in the current build."],"costs":["Two entry points to one feature on the quietest screen","The quietest screen in the product ends up with two different ways to reach one feature."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both the strip and the rail button. No work, and two entry points to one feature on the quietest screen in the product."},{"k":"d","label":"At the cursor, with the strip shown on first run and dismissed after first use","gains":["Discoverability without a permanent surface","Splits the difference: a first-time user sees the strip, a returning one keeps a single clean surface."],"costs":["A dismissed-forever element is hard to find again","Once dismissed, the strip has no second appearance, so a user who missed it never sees it again."],"system":"A first-run flag.","screens":"The editor gains a first-run variant.","money":"None.","what":"At the cursor, with the strip shown on first run and dismissed after first use. Discoverability without a permanent surface; an element dismissed forever is hard to find again."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One deletion and one relocation; the prototype went the other way unexplained.","It gives the rail the job the report wanted: review, not a prompt."],"flip":"If the ten-developer walkthrough shows nobody finds ⌘J, discoverability is the problem.","linked":["D20","D3"],"sources":["docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/c6-screens.md:22","docs/_wireframes.md:11","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:391","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:405","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:381","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The AI writing box sits on new documents in the MVP, so its shape is decided now.","recWhy":"The screens research is the one place a report gave a clear answer on placement: every shipped product prompts at the cursor, and we dock a strip. The wireframes defended the strip on the grounds that a sidebar makes AI a place you go, which is true and is also why every competitor moved the prompt to the selection. Opening at the cursor is one deletion and one relocation, and it hands the rail the job the report wanted for it: review of pending hunks, not a prompt box. Q5 carries this as the AI prompt at the cursor. The one real cost is that a first-run user has to discover the shortcut, and the ten-developer walkthrough is where that gets measured. If nobody finds it, the problem is discoverability and d's first-run strip is the fix, not a permanent dock."},{"id":"D11","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Design system","weight":"high","q":"Do we bring the stylesheet in line with our design system before the pilot, or after?","lede":"The app's stylesheet is a copy of sgnk-md's, and its header says so.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Canonical system","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Prototype the pilot sees","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Shipped app","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Accent","cells":["#1a5cff","#1a5cff","#18181b"]},{"label":"Corners","cells":["square","square, border-radius:0","6 / 8 / 12px"]},{"label":"Icons","cells":["inline SVG","inline SVG, 110 uses","Material Symbols web font"]},{"label":"Self-description","cells":["frontmatter","frontmatter","\"sgnk-md design system\""]},{"label":"On the record as a defect","cells":["no","no","three times since 2026-08-29, fixed zero"]}],"note":"The highlight's meaning is carried by the accent, so the two palettes disagree about the product's one signal.","caption":"Three visual systems; the pilot sees one and installs another."},"stakes":"Every visual reaction the pilot gives is about a palette we do not ship.","state":["The stylesheet's header reads `sgnk-md design system`; it ships `--accent: #18181b` and rounded corners.","The prototype uses `--accent:#1a5cff` and square corners.","Both PRDs recorded the divergence; the product document calls it free to fix."],"tension":["The five-second test would run on a palette the product does not ship.","The two stylesheets disagree on the colour that carries the highlight's meaning."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three descriptions of the same product's visual system","cols":["Where","Accent","Corners","Status"],"rows":[["Canonical system (quoted at docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:819)","#1a5cff","square, three rounding exceptions","the intended system"],["Clickable prototype (docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:14, :116)","#1a5cff","square (`border-radius:0` on the highlight)","what the pilot is shown"],["Shipped app (src/app/globals.css:12, :34, :56)","#18181b","6 / 8 / 12px","what the pilot would install"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How long the divergence has been on the record","items":[["First recorded","PRD v1's design-system contradiction subsection, 2026-08-29","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:815, :817"],["Recorded again","PRD v2's architecture section and PRODUCT.md","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:430, docs/PRODUCT.md:997"],["PRODUCT.md's own verdict","\"Free to fix, embarrassing to charge money in front of\"","docs/PRODUCT.md:997"],["Mentions in the current plan","0","no occurrence of globals.css, design system, icons or corners in docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md"],["Fork state the plan does report","181 of 226 src/ files identical, 25 already diverged; 41 of 43 editor files identical","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223: note the plan's own two figures leave 20 files unaccounted for"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Reconcile the tokens before the pilot: accent, corners, contrast, gated in CI","gains":["A one-file edit the product document calls free","Shares one pass with the contrast-token fix"],"costs":["Components assuming the near-black accent need a look","Any component built assuming the near-black accent needs a visual check before the token changes under it."],"system":"Token edits in one file.","screens":"Colour and radius everywhere.","money":"None.","what":"Reconcile the tokens before the pilot: the blue accent, square corners, the failing contrast, gated in CI. A one-file edit the product document calls free, sharing a pass with D12."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the sibling's stylesheet and reconcile after the pilot","gains":["Zero work before the pilot"],"costs":["Any visual feedback from the pilot is discarded","The stylesheet's own header still reads sgnk-md design system, which the pilot would then ship unchanged."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the sibling's stylesheet and reconcile after the pilot. Zero work now, and any visual feedback from the pilot is feedback on a palette we will not ship."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the sibling's stylesheet as canonical and retire the other one","gains":["Ends the divergence with the least code to change","The least code to change of any option, since it deletes a file rather than editing tokens inside it."],"costs":["Gives up the blue that carries the highlight's meaning","Gives up #1a5cff, the exact blue the review highlight and every primary control depend on for meaning."],"system":"The design document is rewritten.","screens":"The prototype is redrawn.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the sibling's stylesheet as canonical and retire ours. Ends the divergence with the least code to change; gives up the blue that carries the highlight's meaning."},{"k":"d","label":"Reconcile only the tokens the highlight depends on","gains":["Smallest change that makes tested and shipped screens agree","Touches only the tokens the five-second test's own screenshot actually depends on."],"costs":["The app still looks like sgnk-md elsewhere","Every other screen in the app keeps looking like the sibling product's stylesheet, not this one's."],"system":"Two token edits.","screens":"The highlight and accent controls.","money":"None.","what":"Reconcile only the tokens the highlight depends on. The smallest change that makes tested and shipped screens agree; everywhere else the app still looks like sgnk-md."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One file's edit, on the record since 2026-08-29 and absent from the current plan.","Doing it first means the five-second test and the walkthrough run on the real palette."],"flip":"If we ship as a VS Code extension instead, only the highlight tokens matter.","linked":["D1","D13","D21"],"sources":["src/app/globals.css:12","src/app/globals.css:34","src/app/globals.css:56","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:815","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:819","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:430","docs/PRODUCT.md:997","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:14","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48","src/app/globals.css:2","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:116"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The MVP copies the sibling look. Aligning the stylesheet is that work.","recWhy":"The app's stylesheet is a copy of sgnk-md's and its header says so, with a near-black accent and rounded corners, while every prototype and the five-second test use the electric blue and square corners of the design system. Run the pilot on the sibling's palette and every visual result is about a product we will not ship. Reconciling the tokens is one file's edit, on the record since 29 August and absent from the plan, and it shares a pass with the contrast-token fix that D12 folded into this card, so gating it in CI comes almost free. Q5 carries it. Adopting the sibling's stylesheet instead gives up the blue that carries the highlight's meaning. If the surface ever becomes an extension, only the highlight tokens matter and d is enough.","dupes":{"dropped":["D12"],"areas":[],"why":"D11's recommended option already says 'accent, corners, failing contrast', which is the whole of D12. D12's own flip condition points back at D11 and says to do both edits once.","agree":true}},{"id":"D13","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Accessibility","weight":"high","q":"Do icons and export fonts stop depending on Google's CDN, or ship as they are?","lede":"When the icon font does not arrive, 28 controls display the words `check_circle` and `close`.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"The same toolbar, font present and font absent","panes":[{"label":"Font present","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Icon, icon, icon","meta":"ligatures resolve","mark":"","tone":"good"},{"label":"28 call sites across 20 files","meta":"GoogleIcon.tsx","mark":"","tone":"neutral"}]},{"label":"Font absent","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"check_circle","meta":"ligature text","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"close","meta":"ligature text","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"download","meta":"ligature text","mark":"","tone":"stop"}]},{"label":"Where that happens","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Desktop app offline","meta":"advertised","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Exported HTML on a reader's machine","meta":"export","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Strict CSP or a blocked font host","meta":"not considered","mark":"","tone":"warn"}]}],"note":"No font-display and no preload anywhere in the stylesheet or the layout.","caption":"One failure mode, and the three places the plan already promises."},"stakes":"The offline case we advertise is where the interface prints its icon names.","state":["The stylesheet imports the icon font, and `GoogleIcon` renders its ligature text.","Both export paths emit preconnects and a Google Fonts link."],"tension":["The desktop app advertises everything offline except the model; both defects fire there.","An export leaves the organisation, so every reader's request reaches a third party."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Icon delivery, as built","items":[["Delivery method in the shipped app","Material Symbols web font, ligature text","src/app/globals.css:2, src/shared/presentation/GoogleIcon.tsx:60 and :63"],["JSX call sites","28 across 20 files","measured across src/"],["Inline <svg> elements in src/","4","measured"],["font-display declarations","0","measured in src/app/globals.css and src/app/layout.tsx"],["preload hints for the icon font","0","measured in the same two files"],["Delivery method in the prototype the pilot sees","inline SVG symbols, 110 <use href=\"#i-…\"> references","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html"]]},{"type":"table","title":"When the font is absent, and what shows","cols":["Scenario","In the plan?","What the user sees"],"rows":[["Desktop app offline","yes: \"Everything except the model\" works offline (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:99)","the icon name as text"],["Exported HTML opened on a reader's machine","yes: export (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67)","the icon name as text"],["Web app, slow first load","yes: the web app is the trial","the icon name, then icons"],["Strict CSP or a blocked font host","not considered in the plan","the icon name as text"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the export template does against what the plan promises","cols":["Plan statement","Where","What the export does"],"rows":[["Desktop works offline for \"Everything except the model\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:99, exhibit 2","exported HTML needs a network for its fonts"],["\"No document byte ever passes through a Zephyrus server\" (proposed)","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","still true, but a third party sees each reader's request"],["export already built and never run","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67","the template has not been reviewed against either claim"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The export path, measured","items":[["External font requests per exported document","1 stylesheet plus the font files it pulls","src/modules/export/presentation/print-css.ts:29"],["Preconnect hints to third parties","2","fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com, print-css.ts:27-:28"],["Export paths that include them","2 of 2","export-doc.ts:97 and pdf-doc.ts:131"],["Export code, written and never executed","945 lines across 8 files","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67"],["Verification budget in the plan","0d + 1h to verify it runs","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Inline SVG for the 28 call sites, and embed the export fonts as base64","gains":["The failure disappears and the app matches its screenshots","No third party sees a reader of an export"],"costs":["28 edits, a sprite, and weight per export","28 call sites across 20 files is real, mechanical work, not a single config change."],"system":"Icons inlined; export embeds faces.","screens":"None, if the sprite matches.","money":"None.","what":"Inline SVG at the 28 icon call sites and embed the export fonts as base64. The failure disappears and no third party sees who reads an export; 28 edits and weight per file."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the web font with font-display and a preload; use a system stack in exports","gains":["Cheaper, and the export gains no licence question","Costs less than a full rewrite while still addressing the worst-case blocked-network failure in the live app."],"costs":["The offline case still fails in the app","The desktop app's own 'everything except the model works offline' claim stays false regardless."],"system":"Two stylesheet edits.","screens":"Exports change typeface.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the web font with font-display and a preload, and use a system stack in exports. Cheaper, and no licence question on the export; the offline case still fails in the app."},{"k":"c","label":"Change nothing","gains":["No work","The only option that spends zero engineering hours before the pilot ships."],"costs":["Ships a defect that fires where we advertise","The defect fires precisely on the two surfaces, offline desktop and exported files, the plan advertises as safe."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Change nothing. No work, and a defect ships that fires exactly where the product advertises offline."},{"k":"d","label":"Do only the surfaces other people see: the demo icons and the export template","gains":["Protects the two surfaces a stranger sees","Fixes the two surfaces an outside reader actually sees, before touching the parts only the team sees."],"costs":["Two delivery mechanisms in one codebase, which caused this","Leaves two different icon-delivery mechanisms live in one codebase, the exact condition that caused this."],"system":"Partial migration.","screens":"Demo path and exports.","money":"None.","what":"Fix only the surfaces other people see: the demo icons and the export template. Protects what a stranger meets; two delivery mechanisms in one codebase, which is what caused this."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["28 mechanical edits fix a defect the first PRD recorded, and it fires where we advertise.","The prototype has already drawn the sprite, so the assets exist."],"flip":"If the licence forbids embedding in a redistributed file, the export needs a system stack.","linked":["D11"],"sources":["docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:817","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:99","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","merged: D14","src/app/globals.css:2","src/modules/export/presentation/export-doc.ts:97","src/modules/export/presentation/pdf-doc.ts:131","src/modules/export/presentation/print-css.ts:27","src/modules/export/presentation/print-css.ts:29","src/shared/presentation/GoogleIcon.tsx:60","src/shared/presentation/GoogleIcon.tsx:63"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Fonts and icons from an outside network break the offline claim in the first build.","recWhy":"When the icon font does not arrive, 28 controls display the words check_circle and close, and both export paths ship a Google Fonts link so every reader of an exported document makes a request to a third party. The desktop app advertises everything offline except the model, which is where both defects fire. This weekend made it worse than the card knew: the build itself failed inside a network sandbox because next/font fetches Google Sans at build time, so the dependency is on the CDN, not just the icons. Inline SVG at the 28 call sites and embedded fonts in exports remove the failure and the third-party request together, the prototype has already drawn the sprite, and Q9 carries it under nothing leaves the machine. If the font licence forbids embedding in a redistributed file, the export takes a system stack."},{"id":"D15","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Accessibility","weight":"high","q":"Does Tab indent in the editor, or move focus?","lede":"The PRD records this binding as absent and says keep it so. It is bound four times.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"Keyboard user tabs into the editor","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"indentWithTab inserts an indent","note":"bound in 2 files, 4 lines","tone":"warn","edge":"Tab"},{"label":"Focus never leaves","note":"SC 2.1.2, Level A","tone":"stop","edge":"Tab again"},{"label":"No hint, nothing announced","note":"EditorView.announce: 0 call sites","tone":"stop"}],"note":"The PRD's own row forbids fixing a trap by documenting the escape key in a help modal.","caption":"The Level A failure the corpus records as already avoided."},"stakes":"A keyboard user who reaches the editor cannot leave it, and the plan says otherwise.","state":["`indentWithTab` is bound in the main editor and the inline block editor.","The PRD says zero occurrences in `src/`, and marks that as measured.","WCAG 2.1.1 and 2.1.2 are Level A, the minimum conformance level."],"tension":["2.1.2 needs discoverability at the trap, not a help modal.","`EditorView.announce` has zero call sites, so no escape route can be announced."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The claim against the code","cols":["Source","Statement"],"rows":[["docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3358, §35.2","\"`indentWithTab` appears 0 times in `src/`\" [measured]; keep it that way"],["src/modules/editor/presentation/CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:18, :233","imported and bound in the main editor keymap"],["src/modules/editor/presentation/live/InlineBlockEditor.tsx:13, :64","imported and bound in the inline block editor"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the criterion requires","items":[["SC 2.1.1 Keyboard, SC 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap","Level A","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3358"],["Occurrences claimed in the PRD","0","measured wrongly"],["Occurrences in the code today","4 lines across 2 files","measured by me"],["PRD's stated anti-recommendation","do not \"fix\" a trap by documenting the escape key in a help modal","same row: 2.1.2 requires discoverability at the point of trap"],["EditorView.announce call sites in src/","0","so no escape route can be announced to a screen reader today"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Remove indentWithTab from both keymaps; Tab moves focus","gains":["Clears a Level A failure outright","The only option that clears a Level A accessibility failure outright, with no conditional escape hatch."],"costs":["Writers who Tab to indent lose a habit","Every developer who currently reaches for Tab to indent loses a habit with no replacement offered."],"system":"Two keymap edits.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Remove indentWithTab from both keymaps; Tab moves focus. Clears a level-A accessibility failure outright; writers who Tab to indent lose a habit every editor gives them."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep Tab-to-indent and add Escape-then-Tab with a visible hint on focus","gains":["Keeps the editing behaviour a developer expects","Satisfies 2.1.2 if the hint sits at the trap"],"costs":["Needs screen-reader announcements; the code makes none","EditorView.announce has zero call sites in the code, so no screen reader can hear the escape route exists."],"system":"A keymap edit plus announce calls.","screens":"A hint on editor focus.","money":"None.","what":"Keep Tab-to-indent and add Escape-then-Tab to leave, with a visible hint on focus. Keeps the expected habit and satisfies the criterion if the hint is at the trap and announced."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep it in the main editor only; remove it from the inline block editor","gains":["Keeps the habit where a developer chose to be","Preserves the indent habit specifically where a developer chose to be, the main editor, not the inline one."],"costs":["The main editor is still a trap","The main editor, the surface used most, remains exactly the trap the criterion exists to prevent."],"system":"One keymap edit.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep it in the main editor only, remove it from the inline block editor. Keeps the habit where a developer chose to be; the main editor is still a trap."},{"k":"d","label":"Make it a setting under Keymap","gains":["Settings already has a Keymap group","Reuses a Keymap settings group that already exists, rather than adding new UI."],"costs":["The default is still this decision, and it traps","Whatever the shipped default is, it is still this same decision, just deferred to a settings screen."],"system":"One preference.","screens":"Settings gains a row.","money":"None.","what":"Make it a setting under Keymap. Settings already has a Keymap group; the default is still this decision, and the default traps."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Escape-then-Tab is the conventional CodeMirror answer and keeps the expected habit.","It clears 2.1.2 only if announced, so budget the first announce calls."],"flip":"If the announce work does not land with it, b does not clear 2.1.2.","linked":["D9","FL2"],"sources":["src/modules/editor/presentation/CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:18","src/modules/editor/presentation/CodeMirrorEditor.tsx:233","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/InlineBlockEditor.tsx:64","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3358","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3395","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/InlineBlockEditor.tsx:13","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:606"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Tab behaviour is basic editing. Strangers hit it in the first minute.","recWhy":"The PRD records Tab-to-indent as absent and says keep it that way, and it is bound in four places, in the main editor and the inline block editor. A Tab that indents is a keyboard trap under WCAG 2.1.2 at level A, the minimum, and it is also what every developer expects an editor to do. Escape-then-Tab to leave, with a visible hint the moment the editor takes focus, is the conventional CodeMirror answer and keeps the habit, and it clears the criterion only if the escape route is announced to a screen reader, which the code does not do yet. EditorView.announce has zero call sites. Q5 carries this, and the announce calls are the budget line that comes with it. If the announce work does not land alongside, b does not actually clear 2.1.2 and a is the honest fallback."},{"id":"D21","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Dark mode","weight":"medium","q":"Does dark mode ship in the pilot, given the highlight is fainter and unreviewed there?","lede":"Two themes doubles every visual decision here, and the highlight is 1.185:1 in the untested one.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"Light","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Dark","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Review highlight on the page","cells":["1.111:1","1.185:1"]},{"label":"Review vs proposal highlight","cells":["ΔE76 4.10","ΔE76 8.85"]},{"label":"Prototype --faint secondary text","cells":["2.544:1 FAIL","3.991:1 FAIL"]},{"label":"Shipped app --muted","cells":["2.64:1 FAIL","3.41:1 FAIL"]},{"label":"Screenshots reviewed by anyone","cells":["9","0"]}],"note":"Ratios derived from the prototype's and the app's declared hexes; the plan's attention section names no theme.","caption":"The same decisions, measured in the theme nobody has looked at."},"stakes":"Two themes with no stated test theme makes every visual reaction unattributable.","state":["The stylesheet carries a full dark palette and a `prefers-color-scheme` fallback.","The first control in Settings is Theme: System, Light, Dark."],"tension":["Every question on this list has a second answer in dark, unchecked.","The dark palette has its own AA failures, so the contrast fix covers it anyway."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The same decisions, measured in both themes","cols":["Measure","Light","Dark"],"rows":[["Review highlight against the page","1.111:1","1.185:1"],["Review highlight vs proposal highlight","ΔE76 4.10","ΔE76 8.85"],["Prototype --faint secondary text","2.544:1 FAIL","3.991:1 FAIL"],["Prototype hairline divider","1.240:1","1.312:1"],["Shipped app --muted","2.64:1 FAIL","3.41:1 FAIL"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What has been reviewed in dark","items":[["Screenshots specified for the five-second test","1","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411: no theme named"],["Themes named anywhere in the attention section","0","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:400-:413"],["Exhibits or files in docs/ that name a dark render","0","the nine prototype PNGs in docs/screens/ carry no theme in their names or captions"],["Contrast failures in the dark palettes","at least 2","prototype --faint 3.991:1 and shipped --muted 3.41:1; derived by me"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship both and fix the dark contrast failures alongside the light ones","gains":["Meets the expectation and clears both palettes","The one option where a result on either theme is directly comparable to the other."],"costs":["Doubles the test matrix for an unreviewed design","Doubles the surfaces needing the five-second test and the contrast fixes both."],"system":"Token edits in both palettes.","screens":"All, in dark.","money":"None.","what":"Ship both themes and fix the dark contrast failures alongside the light ones. Meets the expectation and clears both palettes; doubles the test matrix for a design nobody has reviewed in dark."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship light only for the pilot; keep the dark tokens unreachable","gains":["Every result is attributable to one design","Every test result is then attributable to exactly one design, with no theme variable to control for."],"costs":["Removing a working control reads as a regression","Removing a control users already see in Settings today reads as taking something away."],"system":"The theme control is hidden.","screens":"Settings loses a control.","money":"None.","what":"Ship light only for the pilot and make the dark tokens unreachable. Every result is attributable to one design; removing a working control reads as a regression."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship both, and run the five-second test in dark because it is the harder case","gains":["Measures the case most likely to fail","Tests the theme most likely to fail first, rather than the one already used in every screenshot so far."],"costs":["Answers one theme while screenshots use the other","A single test still only answers for one theme while the shipped screenshots keep using the other."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship both and run the five-second test in dark, the harder case. Measures what is most likely to fail; answers one theme while every screenshot uses the other."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship both and add dark renders of the nine screens to the prototype","gains":["Close to free: the prototype already toggles theme","Turns an unreviewed surface into a reviewed one"],"costs":["A review is not a measurement"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Nine dark renders.","money":"None.","what":"Ship both and add dark renders of the nine screens to the prototype. Close to free since the prototype already toggles theme; a review is not a measurement."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Cutting dark mode from a developer tool costs more goodwill than it saves.","The tokens are already written; what is missing is that nobody looked."],"flip":"If the extended five-second test finds the dark highlight unnoticed, b is honest.","linked":["D1","D18","D2"],"sources":["src/app/globals.css:89","src/app/globals.css:129","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:22","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:28","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-2026-08-29.md:819","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:605","src/app/globals.css:31"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Dark mode is a first build question because the sibling look has one.","recWhy":"Two themes double every visual decision on this list, and the highlight in the dark palette is 1.185:1, untested by anyone. Cutting dark mode from a developer tool costs more goodwill than it saves, and the first control in Settings is the theme picker, so removing it reads as a regression. The tokens are already written, with a full dark palette and a prefers-color-scheme fallback; what is missing is that nobody has looked. Adding dark renders of the nine screens to the prototype is close to free because the prototype already toggles theme, and it turns an unreviewed surface into a reviewed one before anyone is shown it. The dark palette has its own contrast failures and the D11 fix covers both palettes in one pass. Q5 carries this. If the extended five-second test finds the dark highlight goes unnoticed, b is honest."},{"id":"D1","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"The highlight","weight":"critical","q":"Does the review highlight stay colour-only at 1.11:1, or gain a non-colour channel?","lede":"The only mark this product draws is quieter than the dividers beside it.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"auth.md, Live mode, left gutter","lines":[{"t":"  ## Token lifetime","mark":"","note":"reviewed"},{"t":"| Refresh tokens expire after 30 days.","mark":"unreviewed","note":"wash 1.111:1"},{"t":"| Rotation is silent and unlogged.","mark":"unreviewed","note":"same wash"},{"t":"  Sessions end on sign-out.","mark":"","note":"reviewed"}],"note":"The gutter bar is the proposed second channel; the wash inside the text stays as designed.","caption":"Where a non-colour channel would sit, outside the reading line."},"stakes":"If nobody registers the mark, every review number measures an unseen screen.","state":["As drawn, changed spans carry a light highlight and nothing else.","The highlight is a wash at 1.111:1 on white, under the 1.24:1 hairline.","The plan promises never colour alone, yet the inset shadow reuses the fill's hex."],"tension":["With colour alone, easy to ignore also means easy to miss.","Whether a text wash falls under WCAG 1.4.11 is contested; a gutter ends that."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"How loud each mark is against its own background","note":"Contrast ratios derived by me from the prototype's declared hexes (docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:13, :14, :16, :22) using the WCAG 2.x relative-luminance formula. The 3:1 line is the floor the plan's own PRD applies to borders that identify a control (docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3357). Whether a text-background wash falls under that rule is contested (the same PRD row says \"do not raise decorative dividers\"), so read the line as the standard the design is being argued against, not a settled verdict.","data":[["Review highlight #eef3ff on white",1.111],["AI proposal highlight #f4efff on white",1.127],["Selected span #dfe8ff on white",1.226],["Hairline divider #e4e7ec on white",1.24],["Tab identity colour #cfe0ff on white",1.333],["Review highlight #15213d on dark #0f1115",1.185]],"unit":":1 contrast","threshold":3,"thresholdLabel":"WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 floor the PRD applies to an identifying boundary"},{"type":"table","title":"What the plan says about the highlight, in three places","cols":["Where","What it says"],"rows":[["§10, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247","\"a light tint and nothing else\""],["§16, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:408","\"ignorable at rest (no border, no icon)\""],["§22, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:540","\"the tint is never colour alone\""]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship the colour-only wash and let the five-second test decide","gains":["Cheapest: nothing to build or re-test","Every measured mark, including the hairline divider at 1.24, sits close to the same faint band."],"costs":["The never-colour-alone promise stays false","The inset shadow already reuses the fill's own hex, so the promise is broken twice, not once."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the colour-only wash and let the five-second test decide. Nothing to build or re-test, and the promise never to rely on colour alone stays false."},{"k":"b","label":"Add one gutter mark at 3:1 and keep the text wash quiet","gains":["Keeps the never-colour-alone promise, ends the 1.4.11 argument","Sits at the left, where people scan first"],"costs":["Line numbers want the same gutter width","A second element competing for the left gutter is a real layout conflict, not just a style choice."],"system":"One gutter decoration.","screens":"Editor, review panel, Split, Read.","money":"None.","what":"Add one gutter mark at 3:1 and keep the text wash quiet. Keeps the promise and ends the WCAG argument; line numbers want the same gutter width."},{"k":"c","label":"Raise the wash itself to 3:1","gains":["One token, impossible to miss","Removes any ambiguity about whether the mark is visible at all, on either theme."],"costs":["The document then looks marked up","At 3:1 the wash reads closer to an error state than a neutral 'this changed' signal."],"system":"One token.","screens":"Every highlighted screen.","money":"None.","what":"Raise the wash itself to 3:1 contrast. One token to change and impossible to miss; the document then looks marked up everywhere."},{"k":"d","label":"Make the channel a setting: wash, wash plus gutter, or gutter only","gains":["Nobody has to be right first time","Lets the five-second test compare all three channels in the same recruiting round."],"costs":["The default is still this decision","Three shipped variants is three times the surface to keep in sync across light and dark."],"system":"A preference plus the gutter work.","screens":"One control in Settings.","money":"None.","what":"Make the channel a setting: wash, wash plus gutter, or gutter only. Nobody has to be right first time, and the default is still this decision."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The plan already promises never colour alone; a gutter mark is the cheapest way to keep it.","The Tang eye-tracking study's cost was attention inside the text; a margin sits outside."],"flip":"If the five-second test shows people spot the wash unprompted, the gutter is decoration.","linked":["D18","D2","D21","D4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:402","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:408","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:540","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:13","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:14","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:116","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3357","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The review highlight is deferred.","recWhy":"The only mark this product draws is quieter than the hairline dividers beside it, a wash at 1.11:1 under a 1.24:1 line, and the plan promises never to rely on colour alone while doing exactly that. A gutter mark at 3:1 keeps the promise and ends the argument about whether a text wash falls under the contrast rule, because a margin mark plainly does. It also sits where people scan first, at the left, and it is outside the text, which is where the Tang eye-tracking study found the attention cost lived. Raising the wash makes every document look marked up. On instruction files the gutter mark is the whole signal, one line in the margin that says an agent changed this rule and nobody has read it. If the five-second test shows people spotting the wash unprompted, the gutter is decoration and can go.","dupes":{"dropped":["FL10"],"areas":["Flow & interaction"],"why":"Flow & interaction asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option b here: keep the colour and add one non-colour mark. It specified the mark as a one-pixel left rule on the span, with no icon.","agree":true}},{"id":"D2","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"The highlight","weight":"critical","q":"Two washes for proposed and unreviewed, or does the shipped border mark proposals?","lede":"One wash means bytes are on disk, the other means nothing was written.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Review highlight #eef3ff","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Proposal highlight #f4efff","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Shipped .cm-ai-suggestion","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Contrast on white","cells":["1.111:1","1.127:1","1.280:1"]},{"label":"Border","cells":["none","none","1px inset, about 1.95:1 on the page"]},{"label":"Corners","cells":["square","square","3px radius"]},{"label":"Means","cells":["written, unreviewed","proposed, not written","proposed, not written"]},{"label":"Bytes on disk","cells":["yes","no","no"]},{"label":"Built today","cells":["no","no","yes, merged"]}],"note":"The two planned washes are 4.10 apart on delta-E76, against a just-noticeable difference near 2.3.","caption":"Three span treatments: two are drawn, one is already shipping."},"stakes":"Confusing them means reverting unchanged bytes, or trusting text never written.","state":["In the wireframes, purple is proposed; blue is written but unreviewed.","The two wash colours differ by 4.10 in delta-E76.","The shipped `.cm-ai-suggestion` class draws a blue fill, inset border and pulse."],"tension":["The distinction with a data consequence rides on the weakest channel.","Keeping the border breaks the plan's no-border, no-icon rule."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The two highlights, measured against each other","items":[["Light mode, review #eef3ff vs proposal #f4efff","ΔE76 = 4.10","derived by me from docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:14; JND is about 2.3"],["Dark mode, #15213d vs #22193a","ΔE76 = 8.85","derived from docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:22"],["Contrast against the page, review vs proposal","1.111 vs 1.127","the two are indistinguishable by luminance; only hue separates them"],["Scheduled tests that show both states","0","the five-second test names one screenshot and one tint (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352, :411)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each state means for the file","cols":["Tint","State","Bytes on disk","What accepting does"],"rows":[["Blue #eef3ff","written, unreviewed","yes","flips a flag, changes zero bytes (docs/_wireframes.md:29)"],["Purple #f4efff","proposed","no","writes new bytes"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The shipped highlight against the planned highlight","cols":["Property","Shipped .cm-ai-suggestion","Planned review highlight"],"rows":[["Fill","#0055ff at 16% → #d6e4ff on white (1.280:1); #d2e0fb on the app's #fafafa (1.274:1)","#eef3ff → 1.111:1 on white; 1.065:1 on #fafafa"],["Border","inset 1px at 30% over that fill → ≈#93b6fc, 1.53:1 against the fill and 1.95:1 against the page","none: \"no border, no icon\""],["Corners","3px radius","square (border-radius:0 in the prototype, :116)"],["Hue","blue, for a proposal","blue means written-and-unreviewed; purple means proposal"],["Motion","sgnk-ai-pulse keyframes, unguarded","none specified"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Motion, measured","items":[["@keyframes blocks in globals.css","4","measured"],["animation: declarations","3","measured"],["prefers-reduced-motion guards","0","measured; also flagged at docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3366"],["Files in src/ carrying review-highlight state","0","the planned tint is unbuilt; the competing treatment is merged"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep both washes as drawn and delete the shipped highlight","gains":["One vocabulary; the attention rules stay enforceable","Deletes a shipped highlight rather than keeping two systems that already disagree on delta-E."],"costs":["The distinction rests on a 4.1 delta-E","A 4.10 delta-E is below the roughly 2.3 just-noticeable-difference threshold for most viewers."],"system":"Delete the class, build the decoration.","screens":"Answer-in-place screen and review panel unchanged.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both washes as the wireframes draw them and delete the shipped highlight class. One vocabulary; the distinction between proposed and written rests on a 4.1 delta-E."},{"k":"b","label":"Adopt the shipped bordered highlight as the proposal mark, and correct the plan","gains":["Survives greyscale, dark mode and colour-blindness","Reuses working code at near-zero cost"],"costs":["The no-border rule then covers one mark"],"system":"Keep the class, guard the pulse.","screens":"Answer-in-place screen and review panel redraw the span.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the shipped bordered highlight as the proposal mark and correct the wireframe note. Survives greyscale, dark mode and colour-blindness; the no-border rule then has one exception."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep both washes and add a dashed left rule to the proposal","gains":["Keeps the palette, makes the distinction non-hue","Keeps the existing palette exactly as designed, so no wash needs re-tuning."],"costs":["Builds a second treatment when one exists","Builds a second visual treatment when the shipped border already does the same job for free."],"system":"One decoration, plus a deletion.","screens":"Answer-in-place screen and review panel.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both washes and add a dashed left rule to proposals. Keeps the palette and makes the distinction non-hue; builds a second treatment when a working one exists."},{"k":"d","label":"Take the proposal out of the document; it lives in the review panel","gains":["One wash to test, one meaning to teach","The one option with zero motion, since the shipped highlight also carries an unguarded pulse animation."],"costs":["Forfeits proposal-in-place, which idea mode and file-scoped AI assumed","Idea mode and file-scoped AI were both designed assuming a proposal shows inline, not in a panel."],"system":"Proposals render in the panel.","screens":"Answer-in-place screen changes shape.","money":"None.","what":"Take proposals out of the document and show them only in the review panel. One wash to test and one meaning to teach; forfeits proposal-in-place, which idea mode assumed."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A border is the channel the distinction needs, already merged and working.","Correcting a wireframe note costs less than rebuilding it in another colour."],"flip":"If the review panel replaces inline accept and reject, delete the class.","linked":["D1","D18","D21","D9"],"sources":["docs/_wireframes.md:29","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:14","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:22","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:116","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:408","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","src/app/globals.css:833","src/app/globals.css:835","src/app/globals.css:836","src/app/globals.css:844","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3366","merged: D17","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Both washes mark review state.","recWhy":"One wash means bytes are on disk and the other means nothing was written yet, and that is the distinction with a data consequence, riding on two blues that differ by 4.1 in delta-E, the weakest channel there is. The shipped .cm-ai-suggestion class already draws a fill with an inset border, it survives greyscale and dark mode and colour-blindness, and it is merged and working. Adopting it as the proposal mark means correcting one note in a wireframe rather than rebuilding a treatment in another colour, and the plan's no-border rule then covers exactly one mark, which is a fine exception for the one distinction that matters. Option d, proposals only in the panel, gives up proposal-in-place, which idea mode and the file-scoped AI both assumed. If the review panel ever replaces inline accept and reject, delete the class."},{"id":"D3","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"The highlight","weight":"high","q":"Should the review highlight be switchable at all, and is it on by default?","lede":"Settings ships a toggle that turns off the one thing this product adds.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Settings, as built","panes":[{"label":"Five toggles on one page","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Hide all AI features","meta":"evidence-backed","mark":"keep","tone":"good"},{"label":"Mark machine-written text until a person reviews it","meta":"default on","mark":"removes the product","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Show the unreviewed bar in the file tree","meta":"default on","mark":"chrome","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Theme, keymap and the rest","meta":"appearance","mark":"","tone":"neutral"}]}],"note":"The settings copy already says the highlight is file state, not AI.","caption":"Which switches remove chrome, and which remove the product."},"stakes":"A participant who switches the highlight off is still counted as its user.","state":["Settings ships a highlight toggle and a file-tree-bar toggle, both on.","The hide-all-AI switch keeps the highlight, because it is file state, not AI.","The pilot's exit measure counts days opened, spans reviewed and proposals accepted."],"tension":["The wireframes say every toggle we add is a decision we failed to make.","With the highlight off, 41 of 43 editor files are byte-identical to sgnk-md."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Settings toggles on the settings screen, counted","items":[["Toggles on the settings page","5","counted in docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html at :608, :612, :615, :616, :619"],["Of those, toggles that disable the product's core signal","2",":615 \"Mark machine-written text…\" and :616 \"Show the unreviewed bar in the file tree\""],["The stated constraint","\"Every toggle we add is a decision we failed to make\"","docs/_wireframes.md:47"],["Editor files byte-identical to the sibling app","41 of 43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223: what is left when the highlight is off"],["What the pilot's exit test counts","\"days-opened, spans reviewed, proposals accepted\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:431: all three read zero for a user who switched the highlight off"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep both toggles, default on","gains":["Nobody can call the mark forced","No user can say the mark was forced on them without a visible way to turn it off."],"costs":["The exit measure reads zero for anyone who opts out","Two of the settings screen's five toggles already disable a core signal the pilot exists to measure."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep both toggles, default on. Nobody can call the mark forced, and the exit measure reads zero for anyone who switches it off."},{"k":"b","label":"Remove the highlight toggle; keep the tree-bar toggle","gains":["Two fewer states to design and test","The pilot measures one product"],"costs":["A vocal user can object to a removed control","The wireframes' own stated rule is that every added toggle is a decision the design failed to make."],"system":"Delete one preference.","screens":"Settings loses a row.","money":"None.","what":"Remove the highlight toggle and keep the tree-bar toggle. Two fewer states to design and test, the pilot measures one product; a vocal user may object to a removed control."},{"k":"c","label":"Make the highlight per-document, stored in frontmatter","gains":["Settings live in the file, the only source of truth","The file remains the single source of truth for its own state, matching the projection law elsewhere."],"costs":["A new key to read, write and refuse on","Adds a frontmatter key nobody asked for, the same tension raised on the review-anchor cards."],"system":"One key, plus a refusal path.","screens":"Properties panel gains a row.","money":"None.","what":"Make the highlight per-document, stored as a frontmatter key. Settings live in the file, the only source of truth; a new key to read, write and refuse on."},{"k":"d","label":"Replace both with one quiet mode","what":"One switch dims every review signal at once.","gains":["One decision instead of two","Reduces five settings-screen toggles to effectively one for a reader trying to understand the panel."],"costs":["Still ships an off switch for the product","An off switch, however named, still exists and still zeroes out the pilot's three exit measures."],"system":"One preference across surfaces.","screens":"Every surface gains a dimmed state.","money":"None."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The hide-all-AI switch already draws the line: AI is optional, file state is not.","A second switch contradicting it lets a participant use the pilot without the highlight."],"flip":"If a participant asks for it unprompted, the need is real and c fits.","linked":["D1","D4"],"sources":["docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:608","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:615","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:616","docs/_wireframes.md:47","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:385","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:431","src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts:53"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A switch for a highlight that does not ship yet.","recWhy":"Settings ships a toggle that turns off the one thing this product adds, and with it off, 41 of 43 editor files are byte-identical to sgnk-md, so a participant who flips it is testing the sibling. The hide-all-AI switch already draws the line that matters: AI is optional, file state is not, and the highlight is file state. A second switch that contradicts that line lets someone run the whole pilot without the feature the pilot exists to measure. Remove it, keep the tree-bar toggle because that one is a preference about density rather than about the product, and accept that a vocal user may object. The wireframes' own rule applies: every toggle we add is a decision we failed to make. If a participant asks for it unprompted, the need is real and per-document in frontmatter fits, because then the file itself says so."},{"id":"D4","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Attention","weight":"high","q":"How many surfaces carry the unreviewed count, and where does the total sit?","lede":"The editor states one fact in five places, and both totals sit where reading stops.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Editor: every surface stating the same fact","panes":[{"label":"Top bar and left rail, read first","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Dot on the active tab","meta":"6px, title=unreviewed","mark":"cut","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Per-file bars in the tree","meta":"return trigger","mark":"keep","tone":"good"},{"label":"No total anywhere in this zone","meta":"","mark":"gap","tone":"stop"}]},{"label":"Document, read second","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"The highlight","meta":"1.111:1 on white","mark":"keep","tone":"accent"}]},{"label":"Right rail and status bar, read last","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Drawer counter, 3 unreviewed","meta":"","mark":"move","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Status bar, 3 unreviewed spans · 64%","meta":"","mark":"move","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Per-heading counts in the outline","meta":"","mark":"cut","tone":"warn"}]}],"note":"Amber does three jobs: a lot unreviewed, an open question, a STALE verdict.","caption":"Five surfaces for one fact, with the total in the last-read column."},"stakes":"Repeating a count everywhere teaches the reader to stop seeing it.","state":["The editor states the count five times; the plan's attention rules never mention a status bar.","Amber has three meanings and no threshold; one file reads amber and plain."],"tension":["The attention rules accept the highlight only if it is ignorable at rest.","Clinical alerting overrides 55 to 98% across 34 studies; 86.6% of 14,612 alerts."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The attention section's predicted reading order against where the counter actually is","cols":["Region","The attention section's predicted order","Carries the counter?"],"rows":[["Top bar (tabs)","first","no: only a 6px dot on the active tab, `title=\"unreviewed\"` (:329)"],["Left side panel (tree)","first","no: per-file percentage bars, no total (:346-:353)"],["Document body","second","the highlight itself"],["Right side panel","last","yes: the review panel's drawer counter (:458)"],["Bottom status bar","not mentioned in the attention section","yes: \"3 unreviewed spans · 64%\" (:394)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How well sourced the placement rule is","items":[["Evidence rows in the attention section's evidence table","3 plus one scheduled test","Tang et al. (:408), NN/g F-pattern (:409), Obsidian forum (:410); the five-second test (:411) is untested"],["Of those, rows that bear on where things go","2","Tang and NN/g; the Obsidian row is about demand, not placement"],["Of those two, carrying a URL or a fetch date","1","the Tang arXiv id at :408; the NN/g F-pattern row at :409 carries neither, while other NN/g citations in the corpus do (e.g. docs/REFERENCES.md:274, with URL and [fetched])"],["Eye-tracking runs on our own screens","0","§16 opening line, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:402"],["Gap register's grade for the attention section","Medium","\"Two sources, one of them a lab study on code, not prose. No measurement on our screens\" (docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:26)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Surfaces carrying unreviewed state, counted on the prototype","items":[["On the editor","5","tab dot :329, tree bars :346-:353, outline counts :398, status-bar total :394, the tint"],["On the review panel","5","tab dot :415, tree bars :426-:431, status-bar total :455, drawer counter :458, the highlight (the outline is replaced by the drawer)"],["On the launcher screen","3","per-card bar :304-:311, per-card \"NN% unreviewed\" label, footer \"7 unreviewed\" :315"],["Amber threshold, stated in the plan","none","and it is applied inconsistently: 88% is amber on S2 (:351) and not amber on S0 (:305), for the same file"],["Distinct meanings carried by `--warn` amber","3","a lot unreviewed (:351), open questions (:495, :514), STALE verdict (:716)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The colours doing semantic work across the prototype","cols":["Colour","Meaning","Where"],"rows":[["Accent blue #1a5cff","unreviewed share, and every primary control, and every link","tree bars (:87), buttons, links (:37)"],["Wash blue #eef3ff","this span is unreviewed","the tint (:116)"],["Tab blue #cfe0ff","nothing: decorative tab identity, one of four","`--t-blue` (:16), tab underline"],["Amber #b7791f","a lot unreviewed, and open questions, and STALE","tree bar over threshold (:88, :351); S3b questions (:495, :514); S13 verdict (:716)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave all five surfaces and both totals where they are","gains":["No work, and no screenshot redrawn","Costs nothing today and no screenshot needs redrawing before the five-second test runs."],"costs":["Retention rests on a number where people look last","The bottom status bar, the one surface reading a real total, sits outside the attention section's own reading-order prediction."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave all five surfaces and both totals as they are. No work and no screenshot redrawn; retention rests on a number placed where people look last."},{"k":"b","label":"Cut to three: the highlight, the tree bars, and one total above the tree","gains":["The total sits above the bars it summarises","One bar colour, so amber is freed"],"costs":["A reviewer loses the per-heading count","A reviewer working heading by heading loses the per-heading count the outline currently shows."],"system":"A rail header, two deletions.","screens":"Editor and review panel lose two surfaces.","money":"None.","what":"Cut to three: the highlight in the text, the bars in the tree, and one total above the tree. The total sits above what it summarises and amber is freed; the per-heading count goes."},{"k":"c","label":"Cut to two: the highlight and one total, tree bars included in the cut","gains":["The quietest surface, and the smallest to test","The smallest surface to test, and the only one matching the attention rule that a mark must be ignorable at rest."],"costs":["Removes the retention trigger, the bar seen before opening","Removes the tree bar, which the retention plan calls the count a person sees before ever opening a file."],"system":"Deletions only.","screens":"Launcher and tree lose bars.","money":"None.","what":"Cut to two: the highlight and one total, tree bars included in the cut. The quietest surface and the smallest to test; the bar seen before opening a file, the retention trigger, goes."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep five, but give amber one meaning and a stated threshold","gains":["Fixes the inconsistency without cutting anything","Only two of five citations behind the current placement carry a URL or a fetch date at all."],"costs":["Five surfaces for one fact remain","Leaves the same fact stated on five separate surfaces for a reader to reconcile."],"system":"A token rule and one constant.","screens":"Every amber screen re-checked.","money":"None.","what":"Keep all five, give amber one meaning and a stated threshold. Fixes the inconsistency without removing anything; five surfaces for one fact remain."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The tree bar is the retention plan's reason to open the app, not the tab dot.","A total above the bars is a summary, not a fifth surface."],"flip":"If the five-second test shows the status bar is read first, the total stays.","linked":["D1","D18","D23","D3","FL19"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:26","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:408","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:409","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:413","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:305","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:329","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:346","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:351","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:394","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:398","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:455","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:458","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:495","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:716","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:88","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34450669/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35673040/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anthropics/claude-code/main/CHANGELOG.md","merged: D5"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The unreviewed count is review state.","recWhy":"The editor states one fact, how much is unreviewed, in five places, and both totals sit where reading stops, in a tab dot and a status bar. The attention rules accept the highlight only if it is ignorable at rest, and five surfaces for one number is not that; clinical alerting overrides between 55 and 98 percent of the time, and the mechanism is repetition. Three surfaces do the work: the highlight where the change is, the bars in the tree that give a reason to open a file, and one total above the bars it summarises. The tree bar is the retention plan's reason to open the app; the tab dot never was. On instruction files those bars are over the handful of files agents obey, which is the scope P2 and D23 set, not the whole repository. If the five-second test shows the status bar is read first, keep the total there."},{"id":"D23","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Attention","weight":"high","q":"Does any surface ever state a share of a repository as unreviewed?","lede":"The percentage badge has the least evidence for it and the most against.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Alerts shown to clinicians","n":14612,"unit":"alerts","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Overridden","n":12659,"unit":"alerts","tone":"stop"}],"note":"86.6% overridden; 88.3% of the typed justifications carried no meaning.","caption":"The mature version of an always-on badge, measured."},"stakes":"A number nobody can clear in one sitting trains dismissal, and the highlight follows.","state":["The launcher states a share three times; the editor and review panel once each.","The retention plan makes that count the daily return trigger.","No source produces a repository share. The circulating 18% figure is unverified."],"tension":["Almanac shipped read receipts on $45M and switched off on 2025-01-31.","This repo holds 4,446,724 markdown words. At 240 wpm any share is weeks."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep every share and count as built","gains":["Demonstrates well: the launcher reads as a queue","Reads as a queue a person can work through, matching how the launcher already presents cards."],"costs":["A repository share is unactionable, and unactionable warnings get overridden","Clinical alerting research puts unactionable-warning override between 55 and 98 percent across 34 studies."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep every share and count as built. Demonstrates well because the launcher reads as a queue; a repository share is unactionable, and unactionable warnings get overridden."},{"k":"b","label":"Counts only; no percentage anywhere","gains":["A count of spans can reach zero","A raw count is the one number in this whole set that can honestly reach zero."],"costs":["No denominator, so files cannot be compared","Two files with different sizes but the same count of unreviewed spans read as equally urgent when they are not."],"system":"Stop computing the share.","screens":"Launcher cards and both status bars.","money":"None.","what":"Counts only, no percentage anywhere. A count of spans can reach zero, which a share cannot; no denominator, so two files cannot be compared."},{"k":"c","label":"A share inside an open file only; the tree keeps a bar with no number","gains":["The file is the unit a person can finish","Nothing claims what the corpus cannot support"],"costs":["The launcher loses its at-a-glance ranking"],"system":"The share is per open file.","screens":"Launcher cards lose their labels.","money":"None.","what":"A share inside an open file only; the tree keeps a bar with no number on it. A file can be finished in a sitting; the launcher loses its at-a-glance ranking."},{"k":"d","label":"Nothing outside an open review session, tree bars included","gains":["The strongest answer to the override evidence","Matches the override evidence most literally: nothing claims a number outside an actual review session."],"costs":["Deletes the only mechanical reason to return","The tree bar is, on the retention plan's own account, the only mechanical reason to come back at all."],"system":"Unreviewed marks render only in a session.","screens":"Launcher and tree lose marks.","money":"None.","what":"No count outside an open review session, tree bars included. The strongest answer to the override evidence, and it deletes the only mechanical reason to come back."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["A file is finishable in a sitting; a repository is not.","The tree bar survives as a fill without a number, keeping the retention trigger."],"flip":"If a participant reads a folder share and opens that folder, a stays.","linked":["D3","D4"],"sources":["docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:304","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:305","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:315","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:394","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:455","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:616","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:425","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward","https://sback.it/publications/icse2018seip.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34516216/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A repository wide percentage is the element the attention research argues hardest against.","recWhy":"A percentage of a repository unreviewed has the least evidence for it and the most against. No source in the corpus produces a repository share, the circulating 18 percent figure is unverified, and this repository alone holds 4.4 million markdown words, which at reading speed makes any share a matter of weeks and therefore a number nobody acts on. Almanac shipped read receipts on $45M and switched them off. A file is different: a person can finish a file in a sitting, so a share inside an open file means something. The tree keeps a bar with no number, which preserves the reason to open the app without claiming what the corpus cannot support. This is the repo-wide claim P2 dropped, made concrete in the interface. If a participant reads a folder share and opens that folder because of it, a stays."},{"id":"D6","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Hover card","weight":"high","q":"Does the attribution hover card ship in the pilot, when no pilot source fills it?","lede":"Attribution is the one claim nobody has shipped, and both readers need a commit.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Hover card on an uncommitted working tree","panes":[{"label":"Four rows, as drawn","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Changed: since you last reviewed, 2h ago","meta":"hash in a separate file","mark":"fills","tone":"good"},{"label":"Bytes: the span's range","meta":"splice engine","mark":"fills","tone":"good"},{"label":"Author: claude-sonnet-5, trailer a3f9c2","meta":"needs a commit","mark":"empty","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Prompt: not recorded","meta":"needs MCP, the build after the pilot","mark":"empty","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"The prototype mocks the card on a committed span, so the empty case never appears.","caption":"Which rows can be filled in the window the highlight marks."},"stakes":"The only unoccupied claim debuts as two rows reading `not recorded`.","state":["Attribution ships trailer and note readers in the pilot, MCP in the build after.","The product targets a working tree with no commit, so neither source exists."],"tension":["Recent research puts attribution as the only one of four claims nobody shipped.","Zed Delta entered private beta on 2026-08-12, committing publicly to span attribution."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What each hover-card row can be filled from, and when","cols":["Row","Source","Available on an uncommitted working tree?","Ships"],"rows":[["Changed","sidecar hash comparison","yes","the pilot"],["Bytes","splice engine","yes","the pilot"],["Author (carries the model name too)","commit trailer or git-ai note; MCP","no: needs a commit","the pilot for readers, the build after the pilot for MCP"],["Prompt","MCP write only","no","the build after the pilot, and marked untested (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The gap between the card and the container","items":[["The product's stated container","\"a working tree that has no pull request\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20"],["The pilot's attribution readers","commit trailer, git-ai note","both post-commit only, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57"],["Estimated cost of those readers","3 engineering days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57"],["Measured engineering days available per calendar week","1.21","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Sidecar filename in the plan vs the prototype","review.jsonl vs provenance.jsonl","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56 against docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:617: two names for one file, unreconciled"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship the card as designed, blank rows and all","gains":["Honest, and it says why a row is empty","Nothing is hidden from the reviewer, including the fact that a row genuinely has no source yet."],"costs":["Advertises an absence on the claim we need","Advertises, on the one claim of the four nobody has shipped, that this product has not shipped it either."],"system":"Trailer and note readers.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the attribution card as designed, blank rows and all. Honest, and it says why a row is empty; it advertises an absence on the one claim nobody has shipped."},{"k":"b","label":"Two rows now; add author and prompt only when a source exists","gains":["The card is always full","Saves most of three days for engine work"],"costs":["Delays the Google paid-authorship precedent until after the pilot","Zed Delta committed publicly to span attribution on 12 August, while this option waits until after the pilot."],"system":"Card renders from the review file.","screens":"The card loses two rows.","money":"None.","what":"Two rows now, the ones that always fill; add author and prompt only when a source exists. The card is always complete; the Google paid-authorship precedent waits."},{"k":"c","label":"Bind the author row to the file's last commit and label it as file-level","gains":["Fills the row on any repo with history","The only option needing no new engineering: the file's last commit already exists wherever git does."],"costs":["Invites reading a file-level fact as span-level"],"system":"Git log per file, not span.","screens":"The row changes label.","money":"None.","what":"Bind the author row to the file's last commit and label it file-level. Fills the row on any repository with history; invites reading a file-level fact as a span-level one."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut the post-commit readers and pull the MCP write path into the pilot","gains":["The only path that fills author and prompt at write"],"costs":["6 days, desktop only, agent routing untested","1.21 measured engineering days a week makes a 6-day, desktop-only, untested feature a real schedule risk."],"system":"MCP into the pilot; readers drop.","screens":"The card is unchanged, but full.","money":"None.","what":"Cut the post-commit readers and pull the MCP write path into the pilot. The only path that fills author and prompt at write time; six days, desktop only, agent routing untested."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The two rows that always work are the two that prove the engine.","Rows added only when they have content never advertise an absence."],"flip":"If the meeting confirms attribution as the surviving claim, the source is the question.","linked":["D1","F3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:247","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:385","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:617","https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=dfarkash.claude-edits-scm","https://zed.dev/blog/agentic-xanadu"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Attribution is deferred, so its hover card is too.","recWhy":"Attribution is the one of the four claims nobody has shipped, and in the pilot the two readers that would fill its card need a commit, which a working tree does not have, so most cards would read prompt not recorded. A row that is empty advertises an absence on the claim we most need to look real. Two rows that always have content, the byte range and the hash, prove the engine works. Author and prompt appear only when a source exists to fill them, and a card that only shows what it knows never looks broken. On instruction files the author row is the whole point, which agent wrote this rule, and it fills as soon as the MCP path carries a prompt. If the meeting confirms attribution as the surviving claim, the source becomes the question and d is the honest cost of answering it early."},{"id":"D18","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Testing","weight":"high","q":"What does the five-second test measure, at what sample size, and what stops the build?","lede":"The one test that could settle every highlight question is five people, no pass line.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"As specified","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"As proposed","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Participants","cells":["5","15"]},{"label":"First question","cells":["what does the highlight mean?","did you notice anything marked?"]},{"label":"Highlights shown","cells":["1","2: written and proposed"]},{"label":"Pass line","cells":["none; two failing words named","10 of 15 notice unprompted, 2 or fewer read it as an error"]},{"label":"Build days gated on the result","cells":["0","review-state rendering waits"]},{"label":"Decisions it can answer","cells":["none, since it cannot fail","non-colour channel, proposal mark, count surfaces, repo share"]}],"note":"Asking what the highlight means presupposes it was seen, so a participant who missed it still produces an answer.","caption":"The test as written cannot fail; the test as proposed can."},"stakes":"The gate deciding whether the headline feature is visible is a phrase, not a measure.","state":["The plan specifies five people, one editor screenshot, five seconds, one question.","Only two failing answers are named, and there is no pass line or protocol.","No build day depends on it; rendering is weeks 5 to 7 either way."],"tension":["Comprehension is the wrong measure. The literature is about dismissal: 55 to 98% override.","Those figures come from clinical alerting and code review, so the transfer is analogy."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the test asks, against what the highlight decisions need to know","cols":["Question the design needs answered","Asked by the test as specified?"],"rows":[["Did you notice anything marked on this page?","no: the question presupposes the highlight was seen (the colour-only-vs-channel decision)"],["What do you think the mark means?","yes"],["Can you tell these two marks apart?","no: only one highlight is shown (the washes-vs-border decision)"],["Where did you look first?","no: this is what would test the attention section's F-pattern prediction (the decision on where the unreviewed count sits)"],["What is the pass line?","not stated: only two failing answers are named"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The test as scheduled","items":[["Participants","5","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Stated pass criterion","none","only \"highlighted\" and \"error\" named as failures, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411"],["Observable outcome the row promises","\"Kill signals at day 14\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Build days gated on the result","0","review-state store and rendering are in weeks 5 to 7 regardless, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:355"],["Gap register severity for the attention section","Medium","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:26"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Run it as written: five people, one screenshot, one question","gains":["Cheapest, and it may surface the word error","The cheapest version to run, and it might still surface the literal word 'error' from a real reader."],"costs":["Produces an anecdote that cannot fail","A five-person, one-question test cannot produce a result that fails; there is no line it can cross."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"About an hour.","what":"Run it as written: five people, one screenshot, one question. The cheapest version, and it produces an anecdote that cannot fail."},{"k":"b","label":"Rewrite it: n=15, noticing first, both highlights, a stated pass line","gains":["Produces numbers that can kill the design","Feeds four highlight decisions from one recruitment"],"costs":["Recruiting 15 in the same fortnight is the cost","Recruiting fifteen people inside the same fortnight already booked for other weeks-1-to-2 tests is a real cost."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Two screenshots.","money":"Recruitment for 15.","what":"Rewrite it: fifteen people, a noticing task first, both highlight treatments, a stated pass line. Numbers that can kill the design; recruiting fifteen in a fortnight is the cost."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep n=5 but make it a noticing test with a 4-of-5 line","gains":["Falsifiable for the same hour","Falsifiable within the same single hour of testing the plan already budgets for five people."],"costs":["n=5 still cannot separate marginal from bad","Five people still cannot statistically separate a marginal result from a genuinely bad one."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"About an hour.","what":"Keep five people but make it a noticing test with a four-of-five line. Falsifiable for the same hour; five still cannot separate marginal from bad."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop it and decide the highlight on the contrast floors alone","gains":["Grounds the decision in numbers we hold","Uses contrast numbers already measured and sitting in the corpus, so it needs no new recruiting at all."],"costs":["Loses the chance to learn people read blue as error","Loses any chance of learning that people read the colour blue itself as an error state."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Drop it and decide the highlight on the contrast floors alone. Grounds the decision in numbers we hold; loses the chance to learn that people read blue as an error."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["One exercise answers four decisions here, for ten more participants.","A test with no pass line cannot produce the day-14 kill signal promised."],"flip":"If the ten-developer walkthrough can carry these questions, recruit once, not twice.","linked":["D1","D2","D23","D4","FL11"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:355","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:413","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:26","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35673040/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38658357/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A five second test measures a screen the pilot will have.","recWhy":"The one test that could settle every highlight question on this list is specified as five people, one screenshot, five seconds and one question, with two failing answers named and no pass line. It cannot fail, so it cannot produce the day-14 kill signal the plan promises. Fifteen people, a noticing task first, both highlight treatments, and a pass line written before anyone is shown anything turns it into an instrument, and one recruitment then feeds four decisions here, D1, D2, D4 and D21. The dismissal literature that motivates all of this is clinical and code-review data, so the transfer to prose is by analogy, and a real noticing test is how the analogy gets checked. If the ten-developer walkthrough can carry these questions, recruit once rather than twice."},{"id":"D9","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Editor scope","weight":"medium","q":"Clicking a highlighted span in Live mode breaks the mark and the actions. Fix which half?","lede":"The editable unit is a whole top-level block, and the block editor drops the highlight.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"spec.md: clicking a highlighted word inside a list","lines":[{"t":"## Acceptance criteria","mark":"","note":"reviewed"},{"t":"- The token refreshes silently.","mark":"unreviewed","note":"highlighted"},{"t":"  - Rotation is not logged.","mark":"unreviewed","note":"highlighted"},{"t":"- Sessions end on sign-out.","mark":"","note":"reviewed"}],"note":"splitIntoSegments iterates tree.children only, so the whole list is one editable unit; the inline editor it opens carries no decoration.","caption":"Where the mark and the byte range stop agreeing."},"stakes":"The revert-one-span demo works on a paragraph and degrades on a list item.","state":["`splitIntoSegments` reads only `tree.children`, so every segment is top-level.","Clicking swaps in a raw CodeMirror instance with no review decoration."],"tension":["The plan says nested constructs render correctly; editing stays per top-level block.","The editor's write engine changes only the bytes you edited and never rewrites the whole file, so review actions never needed the splitter."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The split, as implemented","items":[["Nodes iterated","tree.children only","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/block-split.ts:48"],["Editable unit","one top-level markdown block","heading, paragraph, list, fenced code, blockquote, table, frontmatter"],["Lines in the splitter","93","the property it guarantees is loss-less reassembly, not fine granularity"],["Obsidian's equivalent limitation","\"The Live Preview tree is depth-1 by construction\"","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127, in a row that grades the plugin channel \"Not viable\""],["Measurement of how often review spans sit inside nested constructs","unmeasured","no report in the corpus counts this"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where the highlight renders, against where byte-level truth is most visible","cols":["Surface","Tint renders?","Shows raw bytes?"],"rows":[["Live (rendered)","yes","no"],["Live, block open for editing","no","yes"],["Edit (raw source)","no","yes"],["Split","yes, in the rendered pane","yes, in the source pane"],["Read","yes","no"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What is built today","items":[["Files in src/ carrying review-highlight state","0","measured: no file matches unreviewed / reviewTint / reviewState"],["Estimated cost of review-state rendering","5 engineering days","F2 row, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56"],["CodeMirror version installed","@codemirror/view ^6.43.0","package.json:47: decorations are native"],["EditorView.announce call sites in src/","0","measured; also recorded at docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3395"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep block granularity and the rendered-views-only rule, and document both","gains":["Zero build, and loss-less reassembly stays proven","The 93-line splitter's one proven property, loss-less reassembly, stays untouched and unre-tested."],"costs":["The mark vanishes under the cursor, in the default mode","The mark disappearing is exactly what happens in Live, the mode the plan calls the default that matters."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep block granularity and the rendered-views-only rule, and document both. Zero build and the loss-less reassembly stays proven; the mark vanishes under the cursor in the default mode."},{"k":"b","label":"Actions on byte ranges, and carry the highlight into the inline block editor","gains":["Uses the write engine's unit, leaving the splitter's proof alone","Fixes the case users hit by accident"],"costs":["A forty-line block still opens as raw source","A forty-line block still has to open as raw, unhighlighted source when someone clicks into it."],"system":"Actions bind to byte ranges.","screens":"Live keeps the highlight while editing.","money":"None.","what":"Run review actions on byte ranges and carry the highlight into the inline block editor. Uses the write engine's own unit and fixes the case people hit by accident; a long block still opens raw."},{"k":"c","label":"Extend the splitter one level: list items and blockquote children","gains":["Covers the two constructs spec files are made of","Targets the two constructs, list items and blockquotes, that spec files are actually made of."],"costs":["Loss-less reassembly must be re-proved","Loss-less reassembly, the splitter's one measured guarantee, has to be proved again from scratch."],"system":"Splitter change plus a corpus re-proof.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Extend the splitter one level, into list items and blockquote children. Covers the constructs spec files are made of; loss-less reassembly has to be re-proved."},{"k":"d","label":"Render the highlight everywhere, Edit mode included, as one decoration set","gains":["One implementation, used three times","Ships one implementation once instead of three separate treatments across three view modes."],"costs":["A highlight behind monospace is a different legibility problem","A highlight rendered behind monospace source is a different legibility problem nobody has measured yet."],"system":"One decoration set across modes.","screens":"Edit gains the highlight.","money":"None.","what":"Render the highlight everywhere as one decoration set, Edit mode included. One implementation used three times; a highlight behind monospace is a different legibility problem."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A mark that vanishes under the cursor in the default mode is a defect.","Byte-range actions cost nothing new; the write engine works that way."],"flip":"If the content-hash prototype shows most spans are whole paragraphs, a is enough.","linked":["D1","D8"],"sources":["docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3395","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:56","docs/_wireframes.md:17","merged: D10","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/InlineBlockEditor.tsx:64","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/block-split.ts:16","src/modules/editor/presentation/live/block-split.ts:45"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The broken mark is in review highlighting.","recWhy":"Click a highlighted span in Live mode and the block swaps to a raw CodeMirror instance with no review decoration, so the mark vanishes under the cursor in the default mode, which is a defect rather than a limitation. The write engine already changes only the bytes you edited and never rewrites the file, so review actions never needed the block splitter at all. They can run on byte ranges directly, which is the engine's own unit and costs nothing new. Carrying the highlight into the inline editor fixes the case people hit by accident. Extending the splitter into nested constructs means re-proving loss-less reassembly, which was hard-won. If the content-hash prototype shows most spans are whole paragraphs, the block unit was enough and a suffices."},{"id":"D20","cat":"Design, UI & attention","sub":"Prototype scope","weight":"medium","q":"What does the prototype owe before the pilot: the three undrawn screens, or a benchmark?","lede":"Nine screens exist. Three that carry claims do not, and none met a competitor.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Prototype coverage before the pilot","panes":[{"label":"Drawn: 9","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Launcher, editor, review drawer, two variants","meta":"pilot","mark":"","tone":"good"},{"label":"Settings, repo docs scan","meta":"pilot","mark":"","tone":"good"},{"label":"Refactor preview, generate","meta":"after the pilot","mark":"","tone":"sunk"}]},{"label":"Undrawn: 3","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Open a folder, first run","meta":"retention lever","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Conflict, the only blocking pop-up","meta":"the sync rule","mark":"","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Team view, behind the paywall","meta":"the whole paid tier","mark":"","tone":"stop"}]},{"label":"Never compared","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Ten screen queries, written 2026-08-28","meta":"blocked twice by a security check","mark":"","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Screens seen outside this session","meta":"0","mark":"","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"Every placement decision so far was made from written conventions rather than from interfaces.","caption":"What the pilot sees, and what nothing was compared with."},"stakes":"The screens deciding retention, data safety and the paid tier are the undrawn three.","state":["Nine screens exist; refactor preview comes in the build after the pilot, generate a build later.","The wireframes name three more: first run, conflict and team view.","A prompt-injection safety block refused the benchmark twice; its queries are written."],"tension":["The retention plan rests on a first-session screen nobody has drawn.","Four platforms shipped an agent-review pane in six weeks, so comparables exist."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The three undrawn screens and what each carries","cols":["Screen","Stated role","What depends on it"],"rows":[["Open a folder screen","\"first run only\" (docs/_wireframes.md:51)","The retention section's first-session retention lever: edit in thirty seconds, no account (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429)"],["Conflict screen","\"the only blocking modal\" (docs/_wireframes.md:51)","the sync doctrine: git merge, never a CRDT, divergence surfaced not guessed"],["Team view screen","\"the only screen behind the paywall\" (docs/_wireframes.md:37)","the entire paid tier, graded High in docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:25"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Prototype coverage","items":[["Screens in the clickable prototype","9","section markers at docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:281-:686"],["Screens named in the wireframes","13, launcher through export","docs/_wireframes.md; the map at :51 covers S0 to S10, and S13 appears only in the plan and the prototype"],["Of the nine, screens after the pilot","2","S8 is marked MVP-1 (:624), S12 is marked MVP-2 (:660)"],["People outside the authoring session who have seen any of them","0","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:27"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The ten queries already written, and which decisions they would settle","cols":["Maps to","Decision it would inform"],"rows":[["Launcher screen, 2 queries","the empty state and first-run shape (the prototype-scope decision)"],["Editor screen, AI placement, 3 queries","the AI-placement decision: prompt at the cursor or docked"],["Editor's right side panel and the evidence side panel, 1 query","The decision on where the unreviewed count sits and how many surfaces carry it"],["Review panel, 1 query","the accept/reject grammar"],["Settings, 1 query","the highlight-switch decision: which toggles are conventional"],["Pricing and upsell, 2 queries","outside this area"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Cost and standing","items":[["Cost to run","one fresh session","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:39"],["Rounds on which it was attempted and refused","at least 2","2026-08-28 (c6-screens.md:12, h1-design-patterns.md:2) and 2026-09-08 (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:375)"],["Screens drawn without it","9","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:27: \"seen by nobody outside this session\""],["Position in the ordered list of what makes the plan solid","item 11 of 12","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:109"],["Founder involvement required","none","one of seven founder-independent items, docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:112"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Draw the first-run screen only, before the pilot","gains":["Covers the retention claim with least evidence"],"costs":["The conflict modal ships undesigned into the worst moment"],"system":"None.","screens":"First-run screen added.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Run the benchmark in a fresh session, then draw the first-run screen","gains":["Every placement question gets a shipped reference","One session, no founder time, queries written"],"costs":["Comparables are conventions, not evidence about our users"],"system":"None.","screens":"First-run screen added; the nine revised.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Draw all three undrawn screens and skip the benchmark","gains":["Nothing the plan promises is missing"],"costs":["Three screens to keep consistent at 1.21 days a week"],"system":"None.","screens":"First run, conflict, team view added.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Neither; test on the nine that exist","gains":["No delay at all"],"costs":["Retention, sync and paid-tier claims have no visual"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The benchmark costs one session, no founder time, and answers placement from evidence.","Every participant reaches the undrawn first-run screen, and it carries retention."],"flip":"If the paid tier comes up in the twenty team conversations in weeks 1 to 2, draw the team view first.","linked":["D18","D21","D4","D7"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:109","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:25","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:27","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:39","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:375","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:387","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429","docs/_wireframes.md:37","docs/_wireframes.md:51","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:281","docs/prototype/frontmatter-prototype.html:624","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/c6-screens.md:12","merged: D19"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"Drawing three screens or buying a benchmark is work to schedule, not a choice."},{"id":"E1","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Write path","weight":"critical","q":"Do we patch the refusing branch now, let the residual decide any structural fix, and is that fix the `yaml` CST or our own lexer?","lede":"Five of six flows open with a write that refuses 83% of real vaults.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"note.md","lines":[{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"tags:","mark":"good","note":"top-level key: the locator finds it"},{"t":"- obsidian","mark":"stop","note":"column zero: the writer refuses here"},{"t":"- vault","mark":"stop"},{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"# Note body","mark":"sunk"}],"note":"A dash at column zero continues the key above it. The branch reads it as a bare line and returns the input unchanged.","caption":"The shape 6,613 of 7,969 corpus frontmatter blocks emit"},"stakes":"Publish, share and every property edit fail silently on the vaults our corpus is made of.","state":["One branch refuses any line neither indented nor a comment, so lists starting at the left edge fail.","6,615 of 7,969 frontmatter files refuse a single property edit, which is 83.01%.","`yaml@2.9.0` is already a dependency, and nothing imports its CST layer."],"tension":["Patch is a day. Structural fix is 2.0 founder-weeks, an inference, not a measurement.","`CST.stringify` round-trips the refused shape byte-identically, but the engine audit advises against YAML-library edits.","No residual is agreed as the trigger, so it gets argued under deadline."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The refusal, reproduced at HEAD today","items":[["Files in pinned corpus","8,513","test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json"],["Files with frontmatter","7,969","docs/ENGINE.md:487"],["Refused on a single `set`","6,615 = 83.01%","docs/ENGINE.md:487"],["Of those, zero-indent block sequence","6,611 by first-offending-line attribution, and 6,613 by the key-addressability rule's count","docs/ENGINE.md:487, docs/ENGINE.md:1101: the doc explains the gap as an attribution-method difference, not corpus drift, and names `node scripts/corpus-foreign.mjs verify` as the authority"],["Throws / corruptions among the 1,354 that proceeded","0 / 0","docs/ENGINE.md:146"],["Reproduced in this session","yes: `set` on `---\\ntags:\\n- a\\n- b\\n---` returns the input unchanged","live run against src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts at HEAD"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Three documents, three prices, and the disagreement is not the one that was reported","cols":["Document","Scope","Price"],"rows":[["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214-215","the left-edge list bug (4 d) plus the old line-ending bug (3 d)","7 engineering days"],["docs/ENGINE.md:622","this same decision, covering all four YAML defects","2.0 founder-weeks; CRITIQUE reads that as 10 working days"],["docs/DECIDE.md:82-83","the old line-ending bug (4 pts) plus the left-edge list bug (6 pts)","10 pts, which at DECIDE's own stated rate of 38 pts ≈ 41 days (docs/DECIDE.md:73) is ≈ 10.8 days"],["docs/CRITIQUE.md:795","calls it \"a ~2× disagreement… unnoticed. SERIOUS\"","It derives those same 10 points as \"≈ 2.7 focused days\". That does not follow from the pilot's day-rate section in DECIDE, and re-deriving it puts DECIDE and ENGINE within a day of each other. The real outlier is the brief's 7 days, on narrower scope"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Three ways to locate a key's bytes","cols":["Approach","Handles zero-indent","Refusal policy","New dependency"],"rows":[["Hand-rolled line scan (today)","no: 6,613 files refused","ours, 20 sites","none"],["`yaml` CST tokens for the range, our refusal on top","yes, measured byte-identical round trip","ours, unchanged","none: already a direct dependency"],["Two-phase lexer of our own","yes, by design","ours","none, and a second YAML implementation to keep in agreement with the installed one"],["`yaml` Document layer / full parse-and-emit","yes","the library's","none, but it regenerates bytes"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What is already paid for and unused","items":[["`yaml` version declared","^2.9.0","package.json:81"],["Imports of `Parser` or `CST` across src/ and scripts/","0","docs/ENGINE.md:320"],["CST round trip on the refused shape","68 B → 68 B, byte-identical, every token carrying an absolute offset","docs/ENGINE.md:288"],["Publish refusals the shape causes","6,613 files","docs/ENGINE.md:1101"],["What the engine's roadmap section on these YAML defects actually anti-recommends","handing over mutation to a value tree, not reading token offsets","docs/ENGINE.md:650"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Patch, and pre-commit to our own two-phase lexer if the residual exceeds 10","gains":["Full control of the refusal set"],"costs":["A second YAML implementation, and unmeasured where CST is measured"],"system":"New lexer beside the writer.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Patch the branch now and pre-commit to writing our own two-phase YAML lexer if more than ten files still refuse. Full control of the refusal set; a second YAML implementation to maintain."},{"k":"b","label":"Patch the branch, ship, accept whatever residual it leaves","gains":["Most vaults become editable this week"],"costs":["The spec's exit condition means nothing, and it reopens mid-build"],"system":"One branch in the block scanner.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Patch the branch, ship it, and accept whatever refusals remain. Most vaults become editable this week; the spec's exit condition then means nothing and it reopens mid-build."},{"k":"c","label":"Patch, red-proof it first, pre-commit to the CST route above a residual of 10","gains":["The residual decides, not an opinion","No new dependency; `yaml` is already paid for"],"costs":["About a day, plus the duty to refuse what CST accepts"],"system":"CST supplies the byte range; refusal stays ours.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Patch the branch, prove the fix with a test that fails first, and pre-commit to the yaml library's CST route if more than ten of 7,969 files still refuse. About a day."},{"k":"d","label":"Skip the patch and build the CST-backed locator first","gains":["One rewrite instead of two"],"costs":["Two founder-weeks before most vaults become editable","On an estimate the audit marks as inference"],"system":"Replaces the block scanner outright.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Skip the patch and build the CST-backed locator first. One rewrite instead of two, at two founder-weeks before most vaults become editable, on an estimate the audit calls inference."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The patch is cheap and restores availability; the unmeasured structural fix should wait for a number.","CST is the only structural route already measured and already paid for as a dependency."],"flip":"A patched writer still refusing more than 10 of 7,969 makes the structural fix mandatory.","linked":["E12","E18","E3","FL12"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/ENGINE.md:622","docs/ENGINE.md:646","docs/ENGINE.md:487","docs/ENGINE.md:650","docs/ENGINE.md:288","docs/ENGINE.md:320","docs/DECIDE.md:73","docs/DECIDE.md:82","docs/CRITIQUE.md:795","specs/engine/nf-001-zero-indent-sequence.md:47","src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts:197","package.json:81","merged: E4"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The editor refuses most real vaults over a flush left list. Strangers cannot open their own files.","recWhy":"One branch refuses any line that is neither indented nor a comment, so a list starting at the left edge, which is how most Obsidian users write, makes the editor refuse the file: 6,615 of 7,969 frontmatter files, 83 percent. The patch is a day. The structural fix is two founder-weeks on an estimate the audit itself marks as inference, not measurement. So patch first, with the test shown failing against today's code before it passes, and let the residual decide whether the structural work is needed at all. Above ten files, commit to the CST route, because yaml is already a dependency, its CST layer round-trips the refused shape byte-identically, and nothing else structural has been measured. Q14 carries this as the first item on the floor. Writing our own lexer is control bought with a second implementation nobody has measured."},{"id":"E5","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Byte claim","weight":"critical","q":"Do we fix the trailing-comment deletion before \"we change only the bytes you touched\" goes on a public page?","lede":"The product's single sentence is refuted by the one engine file carrying real traffic, and it reproduces in one line.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"On disk","lines":[{"t":"share: old  # keep me","mark":"good"},{"t":"b: 2","mark":"neutral"}]},"after":{"title":"After set share=new","lines":[{"t":"share: new","mark":"stop"},{"t":"b: 2","mark":"neutral"}]},"via":"spliceFrontmatterValue","note":"The value emitter replaces from the colon to end of line, so the comment goes with it.","caption":"Reproduced at HEAD: the comment is deleted, silently"},"stakes":"The first person who tests the byte claim on a commented key finds the counter-example in five seconds.","state":["Setting a value on `share: old  # keep me` drops the comment.","The sibling `spliceFrontmatterKey` promises in its own doc comment that the comment survives.","The critique grades it severe; it is not in the pilot's 38 points."],"tension":["Positioning, the demo and the decision summary all rest on this sentence.","The fix is confined to the value emitter; its failing-first test is two lines."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The claim and the measured behaviour","cols":["Where the claim is made","Wording","Status"],"rows":[["docs/DECIDE.md:60","\"edits them byte-exactly, changing only the bytes you touched\"","refuted by the trailing-comment-deletion defect"],["src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts header","the module's stated contract is to leave every other byte untouched","refuted by the trailing-comment-deletion defect, in the same file"],["docs/ENGINE.md:1000, demo beat 5","\"Everything else in the file is the same byte it was.\"","refuted for any `key: value # comment`"],["`spliceFrontmatterKey` doc comment, same file","\"any trailing comment … preserved\"","the sibling function keeps it; the value setter does not"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Severity and reach","items":[["CRITIQUE severity","SEVERE","docs/CRITIQUE.md:757"],["Reaches a user today","yes, on any `key: value # comment`","docs/CRITIQUE.md:757"],["In the pilot's 38 points","no","docs/CRITIQUE.md:757; docs/DECIDE.md:73"],["Product code paths through this function","2: share-writer.ts and PropertiesPanel.tsx","grep over src/"],["Reproduced at HEAD in this session","yes, with a clean control","live run"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Narrow the public claim to \"only the key's own line\"","gains":["True as written, no engineering"],"costs":["Gives up the property that separates the engine from a serialiser"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Positioning copy.","money":"None.","what":"Narrow the public claim to only the key's own line. True as written and no engineering; gives up the property that separates the engine from a serialiser."},{"k":"b","label":"Fix it, and state the exception in the copy until it lands","gains":["Honest in the interim"],"costs":["A caveat on the one sentence the pitch rests on"],"system":"None.","screens":"Caveat on the landing copy.","money":"None.","what":"Fix it, and state the exception in the copy until the fix lands. Honest in the interim, and a caveat on the one sentence the whole pitch rests on."},{"k":"c","label":"Fix it before any public copy makes the byte claim","what":"The emitter replaces the value token, leaving whitespace and any trailing comment intact.","gains":["The sentence becomes true and survives a sceptic re-running the demo"],"costs":["Half a day inside the file with the most traffic"],"system":"Value emitter replaces a token, not a line.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship the copy now and fix later","gains":["No delay"],"costs":["The demo invites the exact test that refutes it, unrecoverably"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the copy now and fix later. No delay, and the demo invites the exact test that refutes it, in front of whoever runs it."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Everything in the pitch is downstream of one sentence, and this makes it false with no setup.","The test must be shown failing against today's emitter before its pass means anything."],"flip":"Nothing short of dropping the byte claim from the positioning.","linked":["E18","E2","E23","P3"],"sources":["src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts:118","docs/ENGINE.md:59","docs/ENGINE.md:1000","docs/CRITIQUE.md:757","docs/DECIDE.md:60"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Phase 0 carries the byte-exactness proof line, so the claim goes public. One engine file refutes it in one line.","recWhy":"Set a value on a line that reads share: old followed by a comment, and the comment is deleted. That refutes we change only the bytes you touched in one line, in the one engine file carrying real traffic, and the sibling function promises in its own doc comment that the comment survives. Everything in the pitch is downstream of that sentence, and Phase 0 puts it on the landing page as the proof line under the headline, which is why this card moved into the MVP floor this weekend. The fix is confined to the value emitter and its failing-first test is two lines. Half a day inside the file with the most traffic, done before any public copy makes the claim, because a demo that invites the sceptic to re-run it has to survive the re-run. Nothing short of dropping the byte claim from the positioning flips this."},{"id":"E12","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Enforcement","weight":"critical","q":"Does CI land in week one, ahead of the fix for files the editor refuses over a flush-left list, and which benchmarks run per push?","lede":"With no continuous verification, every engine fix on the roadmap is riskier than it needs to be.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":180,"cols":[{"title":"Every push","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Nightly","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Weekly","tone":"sunk"}],"rows":[{"label":"Unit tests and typecheck","cells":["yes","",""]},{"label":"Corpus byte pin","cells":["yes, and not in `verify` today","",""]},{"label":"Splice oracle over 7,969 files","cells":["yes, as a band not an equality","",""]},{"label":"Quadratic canary","cells":["yes, red on purpose until the wikilink regex fix","",""]},{"label":"Certificate over the corpus","cells":["no","yes",""]},{"label":"Seven-engine bench with kramdown","cells":["no","yes","full sweep"]}],"note":"A slow required check is a check people learn to skip; the audit names that as the way to train it.","caption":"The split the eight benches are proposed under"},"stakes":"Nothing stops a regression landing, in a repo where four gates have reported green while blind.","state":["There is no `.github` directory, and `npm run verify` skips the corpus check.","The engine audit ranks CI first of nine at half a founder-week; the critique agrees."],"tension":["The eight benches cost more than the CI file, which multiplies everything else.","An equality-pinned refusal count goes red when a corpus file is added.","A gate never observed failing is not evidence, so the red run matters more."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What exists and what enforces it","cols":["Artifact","State","Enforced by"],"rows":[["The vitest suite under test/","green locally","nothing"],["`npm run arch`: 208 files, 0 violations, MIN_SCANNED_FILES = 50","green and honest","nothing on push"],["`npm run corpus`: the 8,513-file byte pin","exists as a script","not in the `verify` chain"],["`npm run budget`","`echo 'No bundle budget configured yet — skipping'`","a gate that always passes"],[".github/workflows","does not exist","none"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How the two reviews rank it","items":[["ENGINE roadmap rank","1 of 9","docs/ENGINE.md:732"],["Cost","0.5 founder-weeks","docs/ENGINE.md:623"],["Plan's fix-list position","#3, 1 day","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216"],["CRITIQUE's one-sentence verdict","\"the first thing to ship is not NF-1, it is a `.github/workflows` file\"","docs/CRITIQUE.md:862"],["Gates that have reported green while blind","4","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216; docs/DECIDE.md:84"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The eight benches and what they cost (docs/ENGINE.md:414-423)","cols":["Bench","Corpus","Proposed cadence","Cost / assertion"],"rows":[["gate-throughput","all 8,513","every push","63.5 ms total for the whole corpus"],["gate-ceilings","7 synthetic fixtures","every push","milliseconds"],["quadratic-canary","`[[` × 10 to 80 KB","every push","asserts slope < 1.3 and 80 KB < 250 ms: today 80 KB measures 2,575.7 ms, so this bench is red against current code until the size-guard decision lands"],["offset-integrity","the 475 non-BMP files","every push","fast"],["splice-oracle","all frontmatter files","every push","309 ms measured for set-then-delete across all 8,513"],["fold-idempotence","rendered HTML for all 8,513","nightly","~100 ms of fold over 2.3 MB, measured on 946 files"],["cert-matrix","400-file stratified sample","nightly","minutes"],["cert-full","all 8,513","weekly","unsettled: ENGINE gives 5.6 min, ≈45 min and 92.4 min in three sections, see the engine-dependency decision"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"CI first, per-push limited to unit tests plus the corpus byte pin","gains":["Lands this week, with no bench authoring on the critical path"],"costs":["The write-path oracle and quadratic canary move off the merge path"],"system":"Workflow plus two checks.","screens":"None.","money":"One day.","what":"CI first, with per-push checks limited to unit tests and the corpus byte pin. Lands this week with no bench authoring on the critical path; the write-path oracle moves off the merge path."},{"k":"b","label":"No CI; rely on `npm run verify` locally","gains":["Nothing to build"],"costs":["A green local suite cannot stop a bad commit"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No CI; rely on npm run verify locally. Nothing to build, and a green local suite cannot stop a bad commit from landing."},{"k":"c","label":"CI first with a deliberate red run, and the five-per-push split as written","what":"Workflows file, then the audit's split: five benches per push, two nightly, one weekly.","gains":["Every subsequent fix lands with evidence","The red run answers this repo's history of blind gates"],"costs":["All eight benches must be written first; the canary starts red"],"system":"`.github/workflows`, eight benches, `corpus` in `verify`.","screens":"None.","money":"Half a week to a day."},{"k":"d","label":"Everything per push","gains":["Nothing can regress unnoticed"],"costs":["A multi-minute certificate on every merge trains people to skip it"],"system":"One workflow, no split.","screens":"None.","money":"CI minutes.","what":"Everything runs on every push. Nothing can regress unnoticed, and a multi-minute certificate on every merge trains people to skip it."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Two independent reviews rank this first, for the same reason at roughly the same price.","Four gates here have reported green while scanning nothing."],"flip":"If bench authoring pushes CI past week one, ship the reduced per-push set first.","linked":["E1","E17","E23","E25","PR23"],"sources":["package.json:19","package.json:20","docs/ENGINE.md:656","docs/ENGINE.md:732","docs/ENGINE.md:623","docs/ENGINE.md:414","docs/ENGINE.md:418","docs/ENGINE.md:425","docs/ENGINE.md:427","docs/ENGINE.md:660","docs/ENGINE.md:2168","docs/CRITIQUE.md:862","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:216","docs/DECIDE.md:84","merged: E13"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"No automated checks run at all. One day of work guards everything after it.","recWhy":"There is no .github directory, npm run verify skips the corpus check, and this repository's history includes four gates that reported green while scanning nothing, which is why the deliberate red run matters more than the green. Two independent reviews rank CI first of nine engine items, for the same reason at roughly the same price: half a founder-week that multiplies every fix after it. The audit's split, five benches per push, two nightly, one weekly, keeps the merge path fast and the slow certificate off it. The cost is that all eight benches have to be written first, and the canary starts red, which is the point. Q14 carries CI in week one with a deliberate red run. If bench authoring pushes CI past week one, ship the reduced per-push set first and add the rest behind it."},{"id":"E17","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Correctness","weight":"critical","q":"Do we wire the size guard in where files are read, and replace the quadratic wikilink regex, or accept a 41-second parse?","lede":"The guard the performance story rests on has never been called, and the regex it bounds runs on every vault open.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"Vault open","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"walk every file","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"WIKILINK_RE, two live copies","note":"quadratic in the number of `[[`","tone":"warn"},{"label":"320 KB of `[[` occupies the app 41 s","tone":"stop"},{"label":"shapeGate: 4 MB, 200,000 lines","note":"written, tested, zero call sites","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"explainFailure: nothing renders it","note":"the refusal copy already exists","tone":"sunk"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Three of the seven ShapeFailure variants have no producer, because the gate they belong to is never called.","caption":"The path every user takes, and the guard that is not on it"},"stakes":"A single hostile file blocks the app on the path every user takes, and the fix is written and measured.","state":["The size guard `shapeGate` and its byte, line and time limits have zero product call sites.","The quadratic `WIKILINK_RE` runs in the vault parser and is copied in the preview.","The guard measures 16 ms on a 4 MB string."],"tension":["JavaScript has no atomic groups, so the linear scanner is simpler and faster.","Wiring the gate alone leaves the quadratic for every file under the ceiling."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Wikilink regex cost against input size (docs/ENGINE.md:362)","note":"markdown-parser.ts, live and ungated today. Fitted log-log slope k = 1.98: quadratic. BUDGET_MS.keystroke is 250 ms.","data":[["10 KB",42.9],["40 KB",649.2],["160 KB",10384.3],["320 KB",41527.3]],"unit":"ms","threshold":250,"thresholdLabel":"keystroke budget, 250 ms"},{"type":"stat","title":"What is wired and what is not","items":[["Symbols of 13 engine files reachable from product code","1: `decodeStrict`","verified by grep over src/"],["`shapeGate` call sites","0","verified"],["ShapeFailure variants no code can produce","3 of 7","docs/ENGINE.md:371"],["Corpus files that reach any gate limit","0: the corpus proves the happy path only","docs/ENGINE.md:392"],["Gate throughput when it does run","8,513 files in 63.5 ms = 286 MB/s, 0 refusals, 0 throws","docs/ENGINE.md:344"],["Largest corpus file","939,763 bytes: 22.4% of MAX_BYTES","docs/ENGINE.md:392"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Neither; ship and watch","gains":["Nothing to build"],"costs":["A 320 KB file of `[[` occupies the app for 41 seconds"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Neither; ship and watch. Nothing to build, and a 320 KB file of open double brackets occupies the app for 41 seconds."},{"k":"b","label":"Replace the regex only","gains":["Kills the availability problem, with no new refusal states"],"costs":["The 4 MB and 200,000-line ceilings stay unenforced"],"system":"One scanner.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Replace the quadratic wikilink regex with a linear scanner only. Kills the availability problem with no new refusal states; the 4 MB and 200,000-line ceilings stay unenforced."},{"k":"c","label":"Wire the gate only","gains":["Refuses the hostile file before the parse"],"costs":["The quadratic stays for every file in the corpus"],"system":"Two call sites.","screens":"Refusal state.","money":"None.","what":"Wire the size gate only. Refuses the hostile file before the parse; the quadratic regex stays for every file under the ceiling."},{"k":"d","label":"Wire `shapeGate` at both ingress sites, replace the regex with a scanner","gains":["Bounds the hostile case at O(limit)","Three refusal types nothing could trigger get a real trigger"],"costs":["Two ingress sites change, and refusal copy needs rendering"],"system":"Gate at snapshot and search-index; one scanner.","screens":"File tree shows a refused file with a reason.","money":"None.","what":"Wire shapeGate at both places files are read, and replace the regex with a linear scanner. Bounds the hostile case at the limit; two ingress sites change and refusal copy needs rendering."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The only engine defect that is a live availability problem on a path every user takes.","The gate is written and measured, so the cost is wiring rather than design."],"flip":"A pilot that ships without vault ingest, which shrinks the exposure enough for the gate alone.","linked":["E12","E15","E23"],"sources":["src/modules/vault/infrastructure/markdown-parser.ts:12","src/modules/preview/presentation/markdown/wikilinks.tsx:10","src/modules/vault/application/get-snapshot.ts:172","src/modules/vault/infrastructure/search-index.ts:175","docs/ENGINE.md:49","docs/ENGINE.md:344","docs/ENGINE.md:362","docs/ENGINE.md:371","docs/ENGINE.md:467","docs/ENGINE.md:469"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Phase 0 opens a stranger's folder. The guard is written and never called, and the regex runs on every open.","recWhy":"The size guard the whole performance story rests on, shapeGate with its byte, line and time limits, has zero product call sites, and the quadratic wikilink regex it was meant to bound runs on every vault open, copied into the preview as well. A 320 KB file of open double brackets occupies the app for 41 seconds. This is the only engine defect that is a live availability problem on a path every user takes, and it moved into the MVP floor this weekend because Phase 0 opens a stranger's folder and that is the first path they take. The gate is written and measured at 16 milliseconds on a 4 MB string, so the cost is wiring rather than design, and JavaScript has no atomic groups, so the linear scanner is both simpler and faster than the regex. If the pilot ships without vault ingest at all, the gate alone shrinks the exposure enough."},{"id":"E21","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Scope","weight":"high","q":"Do we commit to writing inside the document body in the MVP, or keep rendered views read-only until one needs it?","lede":"The write contract covers only frontmatter, so every rendered view that lets you edit has nowhere to write.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"spec.md","lines":[{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"status: draft","mark":"good","note":"addressable today"},{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"- [ ] ship the gate","mark":"accent","note":"one code unit, atomic, leaf span"},{"t":"> [!note] carrier","mark":"accent","note":"no closer to lose"},{"t":"```ts","mark":"warn","note":"an unclosed fence swallows the document"},{"t":"Free prose that an agent rewrote.","mark":"stop","note":"boundary genuinely ambiguous"}],"note":"Leaf, delimited spans are safe to address first; the ambiguous prose case is the expensive half.","caption":"What the writer can address, and what it cannot"},"stakes":"With no body locator, every rendered view is a viewer rather than an editor.","state":["The writer addresses only top-level frontmatter keys. There is no body locator.","The engine audit prices body writing at 3.0 founder-weeks, rated high risk.","Five product write-backs have no path at all today."],"tension":["Checkbox toggle, kanban status, calendar drag, accepted hunk and section restore all need it.","An unclosed fence swallows the document, and every save here is a publishable commit.","A body span boundary can be legitimately ambiguous."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Write-backs with no path today (docs/ENGINE.md:1270-1276)","cols":["Write-back","Target span","Today"],"rows":[["Kanban drag to a new status","a field inside a list item, or a frontmatter key","frontmatter only; a card whose status is in the body has no write path"],["Checkbox toggle","the three units `[ ]` in a TaskMarker","none"],["Calendar drag to a new date","a date token in a list item or heading","frontmatter only"],["Accepted AI hunk","an arbitrary block range","none"],["Section-level restore","heading through the next heading of depth ≤ d","none"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What makes the leaf spans the safe first target","items":[["TaskMarker span, all marker shapes tested","exactly 3 code units, state char at from+1","docs/ENGINE.md:1348"],["A checkbox toggle is therefore","a one-unit splice, not a line rewrite","docs/ENGINE.md:1348"],["Why a fence is the opposite case","CommonMark 0.31.2 section 4.5: an unclosed fence swallows every line to end of document, and every save is a commit","docs/ENGINE.md:1350"],["List items followed by a lazy continuation","19,135 of 60,000 = 31.9%","docs/ENGINE.md:1378"],["Setext headings in the corpus","15 across 11 of 8,513 files = 0.129% of headings","docs/ENGINE.md:1388"],["Roadmap position","rank 5 of 9, behind CI, fold run, YAML defects, detectors","docs/ENGINE.md:736"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leaf, delimited spans only, after the pilot: task marker, fenced block, callout body","gains":["Delivers checkbox toggles and editable `> [!kind]` callouts","Defers the genuinely ambiguous case"],"costs":["Rendered views stay partly read-only; two contracts until the general case"],"system":"A span enum with three kinds, a locator per kind.","screens":"Checkbox toggles and callout edits write back.","money":"None.","what":"Task markers, fenced blocks and callout bodies only, and only after the pilot. Delivers checkbox toggles and editable callouts, defers the ambiguous prose case; rendered views stay partly read-only."},{"k":"b","label":"Build the full span enum now","gains":["Opens every render profile at once"],"costs":["Three founder-weeks of the highest-risk work, with no CI underneath"],"system":"Full body locator.","screens":"Every rendered view becomes editable.","money":"None.","what":"Build the full body-span enum now. Opens every render profile at once; three founder-weeks of the highest-risk work in the plan, with no CI underneath it yet."},{"k":"c","label":"Task marker only","gains":["Smallest commitment, proving the model where duplicates are worst"],"costs":["Leaves `> [!kind]` callouts, which the render design depends on, unwritable"],"system":"One locator.","screens":"Checkbox toggles only.","money":"None.","what":"Task markers only. The smallest commitment, proving the address model where duplicates are worst; callouts, which the render design depends on, stay unwritable."},{"k":"d","label":"Render lane stays read-only, no body writes at all","gains":["No ambiguous boundary to defend"],"costs":["Every rendered view becomes a viewer, which the projection law forbids"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Rendered views lose edit affordances.","money":"None.","what":"The render lane stays read-only with no body writes at all. No ambiguous boundary to defend, and every rendered view becomes a viewer, which the projection law forbids."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Smallest spans first: a task marker cannot disturb indentation, tags or a trailing comment.","Leaf spans prove the address model on real traffic before the prose case is attempted."],"flip":"A corpus replay showing path-plus-ordinal resolving below 99.5%, collapsing the model back to content plus refusal.","linked":["E16","E22","E26"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:624","docs/ENGINE.md:677","docs/ENGINE.md:736","docs/ENGINE.md:1270","docs/ENGINE.md:1333","docs/ENGINE.md:1348","docs/ENGINE.md:1350","docs/ENGINE.md:1378"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Writing inside the document body is what an editor does. The question names the MVP itself.","recWhy":"The write contract covers only frontmatter keys, so every rendered view that lets you edit has nowhere to write: checkbox toggles, kanban status, calendar drag, an accepted hunk and section restore all have no path today. Writing into the body is priced at three founder-weeks and rated high risk, because an unclosed fence swallows the document, every save here is a publishable commit, and a body span's boundary can be legitimately ambiguous. Leaf, delimited spans first, after the pilot: a task marker cannot disturb indentation, tags or a trailing comment, and a callout body is bounded on both sides. That proves the address model on real traffic before the prose case is attempted. Q6 carries this as body writes waiting until after the pilot. A corpus replay showing path-plus-ordinal resolving below 99.5 percent collapses the model back to content plus refusal."},{"id":"E33","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Scope","weight":"high","q":"Is non-English correctness and text scaling in scope for the pilot, or declared out?","lede":"The plan calls the byte-to-UTF-16 offset map the engine's highest-risk part, and our Chinese vault cannot be written.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Solid","tone":"good"},{"title":"Broken","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Astral characters","cells":["round-trip correctly",""]},{"label":"Surrogate splits","cells":["refused, not rounded",""]},{"label":"A CJK key such as `分类`","cells":["","SAFE_KEY cannot address it"]},{"label":"Documents whose length is a multiple of 512","cells":["","wrong offset with `ok: true`"]},{"label":"Files with a non-BMP character in a key","cells":["20, 0.25%",""]}],"note":"The arithmetic is in good shape; the two things that make non-English writing work are the key predicate and the offset fix.","caption":"Which half of the non-English story is actually broken"},"stakes":"Writing the scope down now is cheap. Left undecided, it comes back as a defect report from Chinese, Japanese and Korean users.","state":["The plan says any byte-to-UTF-16 crossing that skips the offset map corrupts CJK text.","The gap register records that nobody Chinese, Japanese or Korean has been asked.","20 corpus files carry a non-BMP character inside a key."],"tension":["The two fixes that make non-English writing work are already on the list.","The search tokeniser is separate and can be deferred without deferring correctness."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Non-English exposure in the pinned corpus","items":[["Files containing surrogate pairs","475 of 8,513 = 5.6%","docs/ENGINE.md:398"],["Files where bytes equal UTF-16 units","6,568 of 8,513, so 22.8% diverge","docs/ENGINE.md:398"],["Files with a non-BMP character inside a frontmatter key","20 = 0.25%","docs/ENGINE.md:1100"],["`分类` as a key","232 files","docs/ENGINE.md:1105"],["`器械` / `主要训练肌肉`","54 / 53 files","docs/ENGINE.md:1105"],["Chinese vault size","oldwinter__knowledge-garden, 961 files, of which 905 carry an unaddressable key","test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json; docs/ENGINE.md:1105"],["`SAFE_KEY.test('分类')`","false","verified live this session"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Which parts are solid and which are not","cols":["Part","State"],"rows":[["Branded U16Offset / ByteOffset, one conversion point","built and tested against Buffer.byteLength at every offset"],["`u16` refuses rather than rounds inside a surrogate pair","built; a splice of `title: a😀b` round-tripped correctly"],["`OffsetMap.toU16` at 512-multiples","returns end-of-document with ok:true for the last 512 bytes, reproduced today by the wrong-answer defects decision"],["Addressing a CJK frontmatter key","refused by SAFE_KEY: the whole Chinese vault, per the key-writability decision"],["A CJK-aware search tokeniser","not built"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Defer the decision until a user asks","gains":["Nothing to decide now"],"costs":["The plan names this the highest-risk seam and leaves it undecided"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer the decision until a user asks. Nothing to decide now, and the plan names this the engine's highest-risk seam while leaving it undecided."},{"k":"b","label":"In scope: the key predicate and the offset fix, both already scheduled","what":"Non-English documents are writable in the pilot; the CJK search tokeniser is explicitly deferred.","gains":["Opens the Chinese vault using two fixes already on the list","Keeps the highest-risk seam owned"],"costs":["Ties the pilot to two fixes, and CJK search stays poor"],"system":"The key-rule and offset-map fixes, no new work.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"In scope for reading, out for writing","gains":["Today's behaviour, said out loud, at zero engineering cost"],"costs":["A read-only editor for a whole language group"],"system":"A named refusal.","screens":"Refusal copy on those keys.","money":"None.","what":"In scope for reading, out for writing. Today's behaviour said out loud at zero engineering cost, and a read-only editor for a whole language group."},{"k":"d","label":"Out of the pilot, stated in writing","gains":["Honest and narrow"],"costs":["Writes off the population that produced the hardest cases"],"system":"None.","screens":"A stated limitation.","money":"None.","what":"Out of the pilot, stated in writing. Honest and narrow, and it writes off the population that produced the hardest cases the engine has."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Both fixes are already scheduled, so scope here costs coordination rather than engineering.","Defer the search tokeniser explicitly, so deferral is a decision rather than an omission."],"flip":"Ten English-language pilot repos, which makes a read-only boundary defensible if written down.","linked":["E15","E3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233","docs/ENGINE.md:398","docs/ENGINE.md:1100","docs/ENGINE.md:1105","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:42","src/modules/mdmax/domain/offsets.ts:127","src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts:53"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"If other scripts are out of scope, say so before a stranger opens a file we cannot handle.","recWhy":"The plan calls the byte-to-UTF-16 offset map the engine's highest-risk part and says any crossing that skips it corrupts CJK text, and our own Chinese vault cannot be written today because of the key rule. Twenty corpus files carry a non-BMP character inside a key. The two fixes that make non-English writing work, the key predicate in E3 and the offset fix in E15, are already on the list, so putting this in scope costs coordination rather than engineering. Text scaling came in when D22 merged into this card and belongs with it. The search tokeniser is separate and can be deferred, and it should be deferred explicitly, so the deferral is a decision rather than an omission. Q14 carries non-English and text scaling in scope. Ten English-language pilot repositories would make a read-only boundary defensible if written down, and no more than that.","dupes":{"dropped":["D22"],"areas":["Design, UI & attention"],"why":"Design and Engine both asked whether non-Latin scripts are in scope for the pilot. D22's 'support CJK and RTL' and E33's 'in scope' are the same answer. Text scaling came in with D22 and is named in the kept question so it does not get lost.","agree":true}},{"id":"E2","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Write path","weight":"critical","q":"Does the frontmatter writer get a discriminated-union return before any other engine work, or does the bare string ship again?","lede":"A refusal and a successful no-op are the same value, so the product hands out a share link that cannot resolve.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"set public_slug","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"writer refuses","note":"one of 20 `return src` sites","tone":"warn","edge":"same value as success"},{"label":"share-writer commits `next`","note":"no check on the return","tone":"stop"},{"label":"empty commit, slug never written","tone":"stop"},{"label":"makeSetShare returns publicUrl","tone":"stop"},{"label":"the link 404s","tone":"stop"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Every step reports success. Nothing in the chain can tell a refusal from a no-op.","caption":"How a refusal becomes a broken public link"},"stakes":"A stranger gets a URL that cannot work, from a product that claims it refuses rather than guesses.","state":["The frontmatter writer returns its input unchanged on refusal, at 20 sites.","The share writer computes `next` and commits it with no check.","The properties panel sees `next === content` and silently drops the refusal."],"tension":["Cheapest engine fix, and not in the pilot's 38 points.","Fixing the bug where a list starting at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file cuts refusals; dead links stay live.","You cannot tell whether it worked while success and refusal share a value."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What each caller does with a refusal today","cols":["Call site","Behaviour on refusal","User sees"],"rows":[["share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:25","computes `next`, commits it unconditionally","a public URL that 404s; commit log says it worked"],["preview/presentation/PropertiesPanel.tsx:95, 106, 111, 130","every write goes through `emit`, which returns early at `next === content`","nothing at all: the edit vanishes, and the file's own comment concedes it"],["Total `return src` sites in the writer","20","re-counted at HEAD this session; docs/CRITIQUE.md:556"],["Product code paths through the writer","2 files: share-writer.ts and PropertiesPanel.tsx","grep over src/"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How often the silent path fires","items":[["Foreign-vault publish refusal rate","83.01%","docs/ENGINE.md:487"],["Refusals distinguishable from success today","0","the return type has no room for one"],["Named failure codes mdmax already emits elsewhere","19 typed codes among 32 uppercase symbols","docs/ENGINE.md:978"],["Where the refusal copy already exists and is unrendered","`explainFailure` in shape-gate.ts, with zero callers","docs/ENGINE.md:467"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave it; treat refusal as out of scope for the pilot","gains":["Nothing to build"],"costs":["A pilot user can hand a stranger a broken link"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it; treat refusal as out of scope for the pilot. Nothing to build, and a pilot user can hand a stranger a share link that resolves to nothing."},{"k":"b","label":"Union return now, before fixing the left-edge list bug","what":"`{ ok: true, text } | { ok: false, reason, at, hint }`, both callers branching on `ok`.","gains":["Kills the dead-link path today and makes later refusals renderable"],"costs":["Two call sites change, and a refusal needs copy nothing renders"],"system":"Writer signature; `share-writer` stops committing on refusal.","screens":"Refusal state in the properties panel.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Fix the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug first, then the union","gains":["Fewer refusals to render when the union lands"],"costs":["The dead-link path stays live, and it is live today"],"system":"No change until the fixes land.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fix the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug first, then the union return. Fewer refusals to render when the union lands; the dead-link path stays live, and it is live today."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the string; add a traced sibling for tests only","gains":["Unblocks the locality and refusal-safety properties"],"costs":["Leaves the product's silent failure exactly where it is"],"system":"New test-facing export.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the bare string and add a traced sibling for tests only. Unblocks the locality and refusal-safety properties, and leaves the product's silent failure exactly where it is."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["It makes every other engine fix observable, and it costs least.","It is the only defect that hands a user a wrong artefact rather than doing nothing."],"flip":"Cutting the share path out of the pilot entirely, which makes option c sufficient.","linked":["E1","E16","E3"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:502","docs/ENGINE.md:961","docs/ENGINE.md:1012","docs/ENGINE.md:576","docs/CRITIQUE.md:556","docs/CRITIQUE.md:660","src/modules/share/infrastructure/share-writer.ts:25","src/modules/preview/presentation/PropertiesPanel.tsx:95"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The writer return type serves engine callers that do not exist yet.","recWhy":"The frontmatter writer returns its input unchanged when it refuses, at twenty call sites, and a successful no-op returns the same value, so nothing downstream can tell the two apart. The share writer computes the next content and commits it without a check, and the properties panel sees next equals content and silently drops the refusal. That is the one engine defect that hands a user a wrong artefact rather than doing nothing: a share link that cannot resolve. A discriminated union, ok with the text or not-ok with a reason and a position, costs two call sites and makes every later engine fix observable, because a refusal finally has somewhere to be shown. Do it before the left-edge fix, not after, because until then nobody can see whether that fix worked either. If the share path leaves the pilot entirely, c becomes sufficient.","dupes":{"dropped":["FL8","D16"],"areas":["Flow & interaction","Design, UI & attention"],"why":"Flow & interaction and Design, UI & attention both recommended the typed refusal return, option b here. Two narrower questions stay open under it: where a refusal is placed, which Flow & interaction answered with its placement ladder, and how it is shown, which Design, UI & attention answered with a line under the field.","agree":true}},{"id":"E6","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Certificate","weight":"critical","q":"Do we run the five-regex bake-off and sharpen the detectors before the certificate is priced into a tier?","lede":"The engine's own audit says the honest product may be a 200-line linter, and names the one-day test. Nobody ran it.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Certificate as built","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Five-regex linter","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Explains of all findings","cells":["100%","99.8% on the sample"]},{"label":"Top three constructs cover","cells":["93.8%","93.8%"]},{"label":"DESTROY verdicts in 10,176 blocks","cells":["0","0"]},{"label":"Ruby dependency","cells":["yes, for GitHub Pages","none"]},{"label":"Build cost","cells":["two days, spent","one day, unspent"]},{"label":"Largest attribution bucket","cells":["`__unattributed__`, 392 of 845","n/a"]}],"note":"If agreement exceeds about 95%, the certificate's product claim is whatever is left over.","caption":"What the paid tier is being priced on, against the cheap alternative"},"stakes":"Two tiers are priced on an apparatus that may reduce to five regexes.","state":["Pro and Max both sell the certificate, `mdmax cert`, at $4 to $5 per user monthly.","The kill condition for \"it is a rule set\" fires at 99.8%; three constructs cover 93.8% of attributions.","392 of 845 attributions are blocks no detector claimed, so the condition is unreadable."],"tension":["Sharpening the detectors is a precondition for reading the kill condition; two founder-weeks.","Dropping unclaimed blocks makes the condition fire by construction, so it is advised against."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"What the certificate's findings reduce to (800-file corpus sample)","note":"Kill condition 4 fires when the top five constructs exceed 80% of BROKEN attributions: the apparatus reduces to a static rule set. docs/ENGINE.md:816-828, 899.","data":[["Top-5 constructs' share of attributions",99.8],["Top-3 constructs (html-comment, yaml-frontmatter, pipe-in-prose)",93.8],["Kill-condition-4 threshold",80]],"unit":"%","threshold":80,"thresholdLabel":"kill condition 4 fires above this"},{"type":"stat","title":"The numbers that make the counter-case","items":[["Blocks / cells in the 800-file run","10,176 / 61,056","docs/ENGINE.md:816"],["DESTROY verdicts","0","docs/ENGINE.md:828, docs/ENGINE.md:899"],["Files carrying a BROKEN block","94.25%","docs/ENGINE.md:820"],["Cost of the bake-off","1 day","docs/ENGINE.md:899"],["Tiers currently priced on the certificate","2: Pro gates and the Max B2B line","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:475"],["Blocker underneath the test","the CLI does not start, see the CLI-wiring decision","reproduced this session"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Attribution quality in the completed runs","items":[["`__unattributed__` share, 405-file run","392 of 845 attributions","docs/ENGINE.md:540"],["`__unattributed__` share, four-vault run","393 of 852","docs/ENGINE.md:138"],["Constructs defined","19","counted live in constructs.ts"],["Carrying an `invented` provenance","6 of 19","counted live in constructs.ts"],["Known false-positive shapes that reproduce","6","docs/ENGINE.md:217"],["Detector cost","0.09 ms mean, 11.0 ms worst over 400 files","docs/ENGINE.md:349"],["Audit's own cost for construct completeness","2.0 founder-weeks","docs/ENGINE.md:628"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The six false positives that reproduce today (docs/ENGINE.md:217)","cols":["Shape","Found","Should be"],"rows":[["`html-comment` inside indented code","1","0"],["`angle-bracket-text` inside indented code","1","0"],["`a || b`","1","2"],["`[[A]][[B]]`","1","2"],["one-column table","6","0"],["`coffee` fence","1","0"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Sharpen the detectors, run the bake-off, price only on the residual","gains":["The kill condition becomes readable","The paid claim becomes a measured residual"],"costs":["A day for the bake-off, plus rewriting two tiers on high agreement"],"system":"Detector fixes plus a throwaway linter.","screens":"None.","money":"Decides whether Pro and Max can be priced.","what":"Sharpen the detectors, run the five-regex bake-off, and price the certificate only on whatever the regexes cannot reproduce. A day for the test; two tiers rewritten if agreement is high."},{"k":"b","label":"Run it and publish the result whichever way it falls","gains":["Strongest public position available"],"costs":["Publishes the five regexes, before the residual is understood"],"system":"Same as a.","screens":"A published comparison page.","money":"None directly.","what":"Run the bake-off and publish the result whichever way it falls. The strongest public position, and it publishes the five regexes before the residual is understood."},{"k":"c","label":"Drop the certificate from the paid tiers and ship the five-rule linter","gains":["Cheap, honest and fast"],"costs":["Forfeits the one artefact the plan calls categorically different"],"system":"Deletes the bench and the certificate path.","screens":"Removes the certificate surface.","money":"Removes the Max line as written.","what":"Drop the certificate from the paid tiers and ship the five-rule linter. Cheap, honest and fast; forfeits the one artefact the plan calls categorically different."},{"k":"d","label":"Price now, run the bake-off later","gains":["No delay to the commercial plan"],"costs":["A tier built on the apparatus is hard to unwind"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Prices a tier on an untested claim.","what":"Price now and run the bake-off later. No delay to the commercial plan, and a tier built on the apparatus is hard to unwind if the apparatus turns out to be five rules."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["A day's test with both instruments on disk decides whether a team buys an apparatus or rules.","Pricing first is the expensive order; a tier on a pricing page is hard to unwind."],"flip":"A large, specific residual such as the lone-tilde case, which keeps the certificate and the tier as written.","linked":["E10","E7","E9","PR21"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:899","docs/ENGINE.md:816","docs/ENGINE.md:828","docs/ENGINE.md:820","docs/ENGINE.md:540","docs/ENGINE.md:578","docs/ENGINE.md:217","docs/ENGINE.md:118","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:475","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:512","src/modules/mdmax/domain/constructs.ts","merged: E27"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The certificate is after the pilot.","recWhy":"The engine's own audit says the honest product may be a 200-line linter, names the one-day test that would settle it, and nobody has run it. Three constructs explain 93.8 percent of what the certificate finds, and the kill condition for it is a rule set fires at 99.8 percent. Except that 392 of 845 attributions are blocks no detector claimed, so the condition cannot be read until the detectors are sharpened. Both instruments are on disk. A day's test decides whether a team is buying an apparatus or a handful of rules, and pricing first is the expensive order because a tier on a pricing page is hard to take back. Q13 no longer prices a tier on the certificate, so the pressure is off, but the question is the same: run the bake-off before the certificate is sold as anything. A large, specific residual keeps it; a small one makes c honest.","dupes":{"dropped":["F30","MK22","P21"],"areas":["Features","Market & competition","Product & definition"],"why":"Features and Product & definition recommended the same as option a here: run the five-regex comparison before any tier is priced on the certificate. Market & competition recommended a different first step: fix the CLI, run the certificate end to end once, then decide. Which tiers exist at all is decided in Pricing & tiers.","agree":false}},{"id":"E7","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Certificate","weight":"critical","q":"Should the certificate judge each construct against the spec that defines it, keep majority vote, or stop reporting MUTATE?","lede":"The certificate reports PASS when the engines disagree most, and marks spec-correct engines corrupt when they are outvoted.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"The engines","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"The certificate says","tone":"stop"},{"title":"What is true","tone":"good"}],"rows":[{"label":"`~~gone~~`","cells":["split 3 / 2 / 2","PASS on all seven cells","three visibly different renderings"]},{"label":"`- [x] done`","cells":["split 4 / 1 / 1 / 1","the three correct ones are CORRUPT","the four have no GFM task lists"]},{"label":"Task lists in the vault run","cells":["172 of 484 attributions","","not a corner case"]}],"note":"A reference set by majority inverts precisely where the majority is uninformed.","caption":"Two measured cells, wrong in opposite directions"},"stakes":"Every published number and every priced gate inherits a verdict inverted at both ends.","state":["Today a reference is set only when a strict majority of engines agree, with no tie.","With no reference `classify` never reaches MUTATE, so every cell reports PASS."],"tension":["The per-construct map is hand-written and needs the missing CommonMark ambiguity catalogue.","Suppressing MUTATE keeps the false PASS, which is the half that empties a PASS."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Two measured cells, both wrong (docs/ENGINE.md:149-150)","cols":["Input","Engine split","Certificate says","Truth"],"rows":[["`~~gone~~`","3 / 2 / 2 (`<del>`, `<s>`, literal `~~`)","all 7 cells PASS","maximum disagreement, reported as agreement"],["`- [x] done`","4 / 1 / 1 / 1","remark-app, github-pages and marked = CORRUPT/MUTATE","those three are the ones implementing GFM correctly"],["Share of vault-run attributions that are `task-list`","172 of 484","none","the second-largest attributed construct"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How much the answer moves with the question","items":[["`AGENTS.md`, 7 targets","PASS 326 / CORRUPT 38","docs/ENGINE.md:808"],["Same file, 4 targets","PASS 202 / CORRUPT 6","docs/ENGINE.md:808: both MUTATE rows gone"],["Reason","`--targets` is not a filter; consensus is computed over the selected set","docs/ENGINE.md:808"],["Dead option in the code","`void oracle`: a caller-settable `oracleEngineId` that provably changes nothing","src/modules/mdmax/application/certify.ts:259; graded MINOR at docs/CRITIQUE.md:766"],["What the plan already commits to","\"Replace the modal reference with a declared per-construct expectation named in `targets.ts`\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:506"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Fall back to the CommonMark oracle","what":"Judge every construct against the spec, tables included.","gains":["One reference, no per-construct map"],"costs":["356 MUTATEs on a five-file sample, nearly all false"],"system":"Reverts an earlier design.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep modal consensus, suppress MUTATE in every product surface","gains":["Cheap; nothing structural changes"],"costs":["Keeps the false PASS, and loses the verdict catching silent loss"],"system":"Verdict filter at the surface.","screens":"Fewer rows shown.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the majority vote and suppress the MUTATE verdict in every product surface. Cheap and nothing structural changes; the false PASS stays, and the verdict that catches silent loss is gone."},{"k":"c","label":"Report divergence with no verdict","gains":["Always true; no reference to defend"],"costs":["Never actionable; gives up the verdict a third party can replay"],"system":"Removes `classify`.","screens":"Matrix without verdicts.","money":"Removes the artefact's claim.","what":"Report divergence between engines with no verdict at all. Always true and nothing to defend; never actionable, and a third party can no longer replay a verdict."},{"k":"d","label":"Declared per-construct expectation, named in `targets.ts`","what":"The reference is the set of engines implementing the spec that defines the construct.","gains":["Fixes both ends of the inversion","Keeps MUTATE, which catches structural loss"],"costs":["A construct-to-spec map to write and maintain","Needs the missing CommonMark ambiguity catalogue"],"system":"Reference selection moves into the target table.","screens":"Certificate shows why a cell failed.","money":"Precondition for pricing the certificate."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["PASS fires where the engines disagree most, which inverts the verdict rather than weakening it.","Delete `void oracle` in the same commit, since a settable option that changes nothing misleads."],"flip":"The regex bake-off showing the apparatus reduces to a rule set, when the reference stops mattering.","linked":["E6","E9"],"sources":["src/modules/mdmax/application/certify.ts:256","src/modules/mdmax/application/certify.ts:259","docs/ENGINE.md:149","docs/ENGINE.md:165","docs/ENGINE.md:141","docs/ENGINE.md:808","docs/CRITIQUE.md:766","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:506"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The certificate is after the pilot.","recWhy":"The certificate reports PASS exactly where the engines disagree most, because a reference is set only when a strict majority agree, and with no reference the classifier never reaches MUTATE, so every contested cell reads as a pass. It also marks a spec-correct engine as corrupt when the wrong majority outvotes it. That is an inversion, not a weakening, and suppressing MUTATE keeps the half that empties the PASS. A declared per-construct expectation, the reference being the set of engines that implement the spec defining that construct, fixes both ends and keeps the verdict that catches structural loss. It needs a construct-to-spec map and the CommonMark ambiguity catalogue that is still missing, which is real work. Delete the settable oracle option in the same commit, because an option that changes nothing misleads."},{"id":"E18","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Measurement","weight":"critical","q":"What must be true before any engine number is published: a second population, the two remaining gates, or nothing?","lede":"Every engine number is measured on seven Obsidian vaults, one wiki is 77% of the files, and the buyer is absent.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Seven Obsidian vaults","tone":"warn"},{"title":"READMEs, prior audit","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Blocks BROKEN on at least one target","cells":["18.74%","5.78%"]},{"label":"Ratio between the two populations","cells":["3.24× on the same code",""]},{"label":"YAML frontmatter present","cells":["7,969 of 8,513 files","none"]},{"label":"Task lists in the attributions","cells":["172 of 484","none"]},{"label":"Contains the plan's buyer","cells":["no","closer"]}],"note":"The population moved the headline number by 3.24× with the code untouched.","caption":"What the pinned corpus is answering, against what the plan is asking"},"stakes":"Three headline numbers have already been withdrawn here, and the next is measured on an audience the plan has dropped.","state":["The corpus pins 7 vaults, 8,513 files; one wiki contributes 6,586.","The engine audit forbids publishing a fidelity or coverage percentage until four gates land.","Gates 1 and 2 are done, though an audit table still lists them as missing."],"tension":["The corpus has zero BOM, CRLF, bare-CR and unterminated-frontmatter files.","A fidelity percentage without the refusal rate is how the three withdrawals happened."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The pinned corpus, by vault (test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json, recomputed)","cols":["Vault","Files","Share"],"rows":[["community-archive_obsidian-hub","6,586","77.36%"],["oldwinter__knowledge-garden","961","11.29%"],["quanru_obsidian-example-lifeos","540","6.34%"],["s-blu_obsidian_dataview_example_vault","264","3.10%"],["kepano_kepano-obsidian","103","1.21%"],["erazlogo_obsidian-history-vault","40","0.47%"],["CyanVoxel_Obsidian-Vault-Template","19","0.22%"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the corpus cannot see (docs/ENGINE.md:1746-1756)","items":[["Files with a leading BOM","0","the BOM defect was unreachable from the corpus"],["Files with CRLF anywhere","0","the CRLF branch is corpus-untested"],["Files with a bare CR","0","NF-3 is corpus-unreachable and still broken"],["Files with unterminated frontmatter","0","that refusal is corpus-unreachable"],["Files with a zero-indent block sequence","6,613","the refusal driver: an Obsidian default"],["Same code, README population","5.78% blocks BROKEN","docs/ENGINE.md:134"],["Same code, Obsidian vault population","18.74%","docs/ENGINE.md:139"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The four gates that lift the ban (docs/ENGINE.md:211-214), re-checked at HEAD","cols":["Gate","State today","Evidence"],"rows":[["the left-edge list bug's zero-indent red proof","exists: 99 lines","test/corpus/foreign/nf-001-red-proof.test.ts; the engine's pre-publish test checklist still calls it a ghost"],["the old line-ending bug's bare-CR red proof","exists: 82 lines","test/corpus/foreign/nf-003-red-proof.test.ts; same stale row"],["the spaced-key bug's unaddressable-key residual","does not exist","docs/ENGINE.md:213"],["Whole-corpus behavioural sweep","scratch script only","docs/ENGINE.md:214"],["`npm run spec`","4 specs, 0 errors, 0 warnings","run in this session"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why the ban exists","items":[["Headline numbers withdrawn in this programme","3","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13"],["Same code, README population","5.78% blocks BROKEN","docs/ENGINE.md:134"],["Same code, Obsidian vault population","18.74%","docs/ENGINE.md:139"],["Ratio","3.24x, from the population alone","docs/ENGINE.md:141"],["Files the writer can currently address","17%","docs/ENGINE.md:220"],["What the audit says not to gate on","tests 5 to 8: they protect the certificate, which is not yet customer-facing","docs/ENGINE.md:220"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the standing ban; describe the mechanism only","gains":["No withdrawal risk"],"costs":["No falsifiable claim, which is what separates the engine from an assertion"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the standing ban on publishing any engine percentage; describe the mechanism only. No withdrawal risk, and no falsifiable claim, which is what separates an engine from an assertion."},{"k":"b","label":"Publish now, always with the denominator and the population named","gains":["Gets a checkable number out, free"],"costs":["Risks a fourth withdrawal, on an audience we are not targeting"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish now, always with the denominator and the population named. A checkable number gets out free; a fourth withdrawal is a real risk, on an audience we do not target."},{"k":"c","label":"Second population and the two remaining gates, then publish as one fraction","what":"Pin agent-written repos and READMEs, then land the residual test for keys with spaces and the corpus sweep.","gains":["The number becomes about the buyer","Keeps the existing 8,513-file byte gate"],"costs":["Delays the first public number by days; an audit table needs correcting"],"system":"A second pinned corpus and two tests.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Replace the corpus with an agent-written one","gains":["Aligns the measurement with the route"],"costs":["Discards the pinned byte gate, and the hard cases with it"],"system":"New corpus, new hashes.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Replace the Obsidian corpus with an agent-written one. Aligns the measurement with the buyer; discards the pinned byte gate and the hard cases it holds."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The corpus answers a question about Obsidian users while the plan asks about developers running agents.","The same code moved 3.24× when the population changed, so the population decides the number."],"flip":"The pilot buyer turning out to be the Obsidian user, in which case the corpus is already right.","linked":["E1","E23","E5","R7"],"sources":["test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/ENGINE.md:134","docs/ENGINE.md:139","docs/ENGINE.md:141","docs/ENGINE.md:207","docs/ENGINE.md:211","docs/ENGINE.md:213","docs/ENGINE.md:214","docs/ENGINE.md:220","docs/ENGINE.md:1746","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13","merged: E19"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Nothing publishes an engine number in the first build.","recWhy":"Every engine number is measured on seven Obsidian vaults, and one wiki contributes 6,586 of the 8,513 files, so the corpus answers a question about Obsidian note-takers while the plan asks about developers running agents. The same code moved 3.24 times when the population changed once already, so the population decides the number, and three headline claims have already been withdrawn here for publishing a fidelity figure without its refusal rate. Pin a second population, agent-written repositories and READMEs, land the two remaining gates, the key-predicate residual and the corpus sweep, and publish one fraction with its denominator. Keep the vault corpus as the byte gate, because it holds the hard cases. On instruction files the second population is exactly the AGENTS.md sample already taken. If the pilot buyer turns out to be the Obsidian user, the corpus was right all along."},{"id":"E3","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Write path","weight":"high","q":"Do we make every key the writer can locate writable, or keep an ASCII allowlist that cannot address 942 files?","lede":"Frontmatter keys with spaces, like `date created`, cannot be edited yet; two of our largest vaults use them most.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Keep SAFE_KEY","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Widen the class","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Addressability predicate","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"`date created`","cells":["refused","writable","writable"]},{"label":"`分类`","cells":["refused","writable","writable"]},{"label":"Files made writable of 942","cells":["0","most","936"]},{"label":"Sequence item mis-spliced","cells":["never","5 corpus files","never"]},{"label":"Definitions of the rule","cells":["two: engine and panel","two","one"]}],"note":"Widening the character class buys the same files and introduces a structural corruption path.","caption":"Three ways to decide whether a key can be addressed"},"stakes":"Two vaults in our own corpus stay read-only, and CJK keys cannot be written at all.","state":["The key rule is `SAFE_KEY = /^[A-Za-z0-9_.$-]+$/`, copied at five properties-panel sites.","942 files, 11.8% of those with frontmatter, carry a key that rule cannot address.","The engine audit measured the replacement: 936 files become writable, 6 stay refused."],"tension":["The honest boundary is what the locator can find, not which characters are safe.","Codepoint equality with NFC on the request is a commitment, not a detail.","0 corpus keys are quoted on disk, so the quoting cases are theoretical."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What SAFE_KEY cannot address, measured on the pinned corpus","items":[["Top-level key lines","29,293","docs/ENGINE.md:1089"],["Occurrences failing SAFE_KEY","2,452 = 8.4%","docs/ENGINE.md:1092 (§68.5 quotes 2,447/8.35%: the 5-line gap is the aliases sequence items counted below)"],["Files with at least one unaddressable key","942 = 11.8% of frontmatter files","docs/ENGINE.md:1093"],["`date created` / `date modified`","812 / 811 files","docs/ENGINE.md:1105"],["`分类`","232 files","docs/ENGINE.md:1105"],["Files unlocked by the new key-addressability predicate","936 of 942 = 99.36%","docs/ENGINE.md:1115"],["of which the predicate alone is enough","750","docs/ENGINE.md:1113-1120"],["of which the left-edge list bug fix must also land before publish succeeds","186","docs/ENGINE.md:1113-1120"],["Quoted keys anywhere in 8,513 files","0","docs/ENGINE.md:1094"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Why widening the character class is not the same fix","cols":["Approach","Unlocks","Fails on"],"rows":[["Widen the class to allow spaces and non-ASCII","most of the 942","5 corpus lines that are sequence items under `aliases:` would be read as top-level keys and spliced into"],["Predicate: NFC, one line, no control char, no edge space, no `: `, no ` #`, no leading indicator","936 of 942","6 files: 5 sequence items and one template line, all correct refusals"],["Keep SAFE_KEY","0","942 files, and the panel keeps a second copy of the rule"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Widen the character class to permit spaces and non-ASCII","gains":["Cheapest possible change"],"costs":["Writes into a list item on 5 corpus files: corruption, not refusal"],"system":"One regex.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Widen the character class to permit spaces and non-ASCII. The cheapest change, and on five corpus files it writes into a list item, which is corruption rather than refusal."},{"k":"b","label":"Predicate, but ASCII-only for the pilot","gains":["Reaches 1,623 `date created` and `date modified` occurrences"],"costs":["Leaves the Chinese vault refused, where most CJK evidence came from"],"system":"Predicate with a character gate.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt a real key predicate but ASCII-only for the pilot. Reaches 1,623 occurrences of date created and date modified; leaves the Chinese vault refused, where most CJK evidence came from."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the engine doc's key predicate, one definition, both modules","gains":["936 of 942 files become writable, measured","One definition of the rule, not two"],"costs":["Commits us to codepoint equality with NFC on the request"],"system":"New `addressableKey`; the panel's five sites move to it.","screens":"Panel renders a refusal kind, not a red border.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the engine document's key predicate, one definition shared by both modules. 936 of 942 refused files become writable, measured; commits to codepoint equality with NFC on the request."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the ASCII allowlist and refuse those keys with a named message","gains":["Honest, and cheap once refusals are reported"],"costs":["11.8% of frontmatter files stay unwritable; two vaults read-only"],"system":"Refusal reason only.","screens":"Refusal copy in the panel.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the ASCII allowlist and refuse those keys with a named message. Honest and cheap once refusals render; 11.8 percent of frontmatter files stay unwritable and two vaults read-only."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Designed, measured against the corpus, and it carries a test that must fail on today's code first.","Costs about what option b costs and adds no corruption path."],"flip":"A real vault with case-duplicate or non-NFC keys, which forces the equality rule to be decided first.","linked":["E1","E2","E33","E36"],"sources":["src/modules/share/domain/splice-frontmatter.ts:53","src/modules/preview/presentation/PropertiesPanel.tsx:105","docs/ENGINE.md:1043","docs/ENGINE.md:1092","docs/ENGINE.md:1093","docs/ENGINE.md:1094","docs/ENGINE.md:1115","docs/ENGINE.md:1250","docs/ENGINE.md:213"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Key addressability serves the review file.","recWhy":"Keys with spaces, like date created, cannot be edited, and 942 files carry one, 11.8 percent of everything with frontmatter, concentrated in two of our largest vaults. The rule that refuses them is a character allowlist copied at five sites. The honest boundary is not which characters are safe but what the locator can find, and the engine document's predicate was designed that way. Measured against the corpus, 936 files become writable, six stay refused, and it carries a test that must fail on today's code first. It costs about what the ASCII-only version costs and adds no corruption path, which widening the character class does on five files. E33 in the MVP set names this as already scheduled. The commitment it makes, codepoint equality with NFC applied on the request, is real and it is the right one."},{"id":"E8","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Certificate","weight":"high","q":"Which engines run when, and which dependencies get declared and pinned, so a third party can recompute a certificate?","lede":"One of seven engines costs forty times the other six combined, and the function defining PASS is not in our dependency list.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Ambient on the machine","items":["ruby at /opt/homebrew","kramdown 2.5.2 gems"],"side":"no Gemfile.lock","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Hoisted, undeclared","items":["entities@8.0.0","marked"],"side":"resolve out of node_modules","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Declared in package.json","items":["yaml","github-slugger"],"side":"pinned","tone":"good"},{"label":"Stamped in the certificate","items":["mdmax/fold@1","benchId"],"side":"does not move when the above do","tone":"accent"}],"note":"`escapeText` from `entities` is the definition of PASS. A silent major bump changes every verdict without moving the stamp.","caption":"What a verdict actually depends on, against what the stamp records"},"stakes":"A verdict whose meaning can change without the stamp moving is not auditable, and it is what we would sell.","state":["103 files: 338 ms on six engines, 14,020 ms on seven.","`entities` and `marked` are in neither `dependencies` nor `devDependencies`.","No Gemfile, no Gemfile.lock, no .ruby-version: gem versions come from the machine."],"tension":["Interactive surfaces need single-digit milliseconds; the published artefact needs the GitHub Pages row.","Under `npm ci --omit=dev` the production domain layer loses its import entirely.","The engine audit gives three whole-corpus cost figures, so the nightly cannot be sized."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Wall time for the same certification run (docs/ENGINE.md:542)","note":"kepano vault, 103 files. The seven-engine number is 41.5x the six-engine one; the difference is one spawnSync per block.","data":[["6 JS engines (ms)",338],["7 engines with kramdown (ms)",14020]],"unit":"ms"},{"type":"stat","title":"Cost, reproducibility, and one unresolved number","items":[["kramdown share of wall time","96.9%","docs/ENGINE.md:830"],["Per-block cost","51.2 ms","docs/ENGINE.md:830"],["Whole corpus, JS only","34.9 s","docs/ENGINE.md:870"],["Whole corpus, with kramdown","the document gives three answers: ≈45 min (docs/ENGINE.md:542), 92.4 min (docs/ENGINE.md:871), and 5.6 min (docs/ENGINE.md:354, quoted again at :427)","all three are marked [derived] from different bases; this must be re-derived once before it is used to size a nightly job"],["Gemfile.lock / .ruby-version in repo","absent","docs/ENGINE.md:231; verified: no such files"],["Cost if one ruby process is kept alive","~2 ms/block","docs/ENGINE.md:356"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The engine's real dependency graph vs the declared one","cols":["Package","Used by","Declared in package.json","Failure it enables"],"rows":[["`entities@8.0.0`","fold.ts, verdict.ts","no: hoisted from parse5 and markdown-it, marked dev","`escapeText` changes; every verdict changes; `mdmax/fold@1` does not move"],["`marked`","one of seven bench engines","no: one lockfile dependent, `mermaid`","a mermaid upgrade silently changes `benchId`"],["`github-slugger@2.0.0`","slug.ts","yes, package.json:55","none"],["ruby + kramdown 2.5.2 + kramdown-parser-gfm 1.1.0","bench.ts","no Gemfile, no Gemfile.lock, no .ruby-version: verified","the seventh engine's version is whatever the machine has"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Cost to close","items":[["Declare `entities` and `marked`","2 lines of package.json","docs/ENGINE.md:229-230"],["Add the entities version to the fold stamp","1 field","docs/ENGINE.md:218"],["Add a Gemfile.lock","1 file","docs/ENGINE.md:231"],["Reproducibility gaps closed by those three","3 of the 5 in the table","docs/ENGINE.md:228-232: the other two are the CLI (E10) and the certificate schema, which needs a version bump"],["What the plan already budgets","0.5 days, for the dependency half only","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:225"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build the persistent-ruby protocol first","what":"Build a persistent Ruby protocol first, one process kept alive behind a line-delimited pipe. Takes the certificate from 60 ms a block to about 2; new infrastructure, dependencies still undeclared.","gains":["Takes the cert from 60.7 ms/block to roughly 2 ms"],"costs":["New infrastructure first, and it leaves the dependencies undeclared"],"system":"New subprocess protocol in infrastructure.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Six JS engines interactively, the seventh nightly, every dependency pinned","gains":["Interactive paths stay in single-digit milliseconds","Three of five reproducibility gaps close for half a day"],"costs":["Two run modes, and one whole-corpus figure must be re-derived"],"system":"Bench split by mode; dependency and stamp changes.","screens":"None.","money":"Precondition for a replayable certificate.","what":"Six JavaScript engines on interactive paths, the seventh Ruby engine nightly, every dependency pinned. Single-digit milliseconds where a person waits; two run modes to keep."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep all seven everywhere and declare nothing","gains":["Nothing to build"],"costs":["A 16-second run rules out every interactive surface"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep all seven engines everywhere and declare nothing. Nothing to build, and a sixteen-second run rules out every interactive surface."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop kramdown; mark github-pages declared rather than local","gains":["Removes the ruby dependency and the reproducibility hole"],"costs":["Gives up measurement on the surface most users publish to","Uncertifiable share goes from 8 of 15 to 9"],"system":"Bench loses an engine; `targets.ts` changes fidelity.","screens":"One fewer row.","money":"Weakens the certificate's claim.","what":"Drop kramdown and mark GitHub Pages as declared rather than measured. Removes the Ruby dependency and the reproducibility hole; gives up measurement on the surface most people publish to."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Interactive surfaces need the six, the published artefact needs the seven, nothing needs both at once.","Pick one whole-corpus basis and re-derive it once."],"flip":"The certificate never becoming a published artefact, which makes GitHub Pages a declaration like the other eight.","linked":["E10","E6","E9"],"sources":["src/modules/mdmax/infrastructure/bench.ts:52","src/modules/mdmax/infrastructure/bench.ts:74","docs/ENGINE.md:542","docs/ENGINE.md:354","docs/ENGINE.md:356","docs/ENGINE.md:229","docs/ENGINE.md:230","docs/ENGINE.md:231","docs/ENGINE.md:218","docs/ENGINE.md:2125","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:225","package.json:55","merged: E11"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Reproducibility matters when someone outside recomputes a verdict.","recWhy":"One of seven engines costs forty times the other six combined, 14 seconds against 338 milliseconds on 103 files, and two of the packages the certificate's verdict depends on are in neither dependency list. The Ruby engine's gem versions come from whatever machine runs it. A third party cannot recompute a certificate under those conditions, which defeats the point of one. Interactive surfaces need the six fast engines; the published artefact needs the seventh, because GitHub Pages is where most people publish; nothing needs both at once. Six interactively, the seventh nightly, every dependency pinned, closes three of five reproducibility gaps for half a day. The three whole-corpus cost figures in the audit disagree with each other, so pick one basis and re-derive it once. If the certificate never becomes a published artefact, GitHub Pages becomes a declaration like the other eight."},{"id":"E9","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Certificate","weight":"high","q":"Which certificate surface reaches an outsider first: MCP tool, public web checker, GitHub Action, or none until the bake-off?","lede":"The certificate only draws people in if a stranger can run it, and every candidate surface imports a broken CLI.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"MCP tool","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Web checker","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"GitHub Action","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Effort, engineer-days","cells":["2","4","3"]},{"label":"Consumer","cells":["the agent, before it writes","a stranger, once","someone else's CI"]},{"label":"Rendering work","cells":["none","a full UI","a log format"]},{"label":"First impression","cells":["a summary and non-PASS cells","94.25% of real files show BROKEN","a red build on adoption day"]},{"label":"Blocked by","cells":["the CLI fix","the CLI fix and the dataset","the CLI fix and a baseline"]}],"note":"Returning a whole certificate from an MCP call is a 24.4× blowup on the source, so the tool must summarise.","caption":"The three live surfaces, priced in the engine doc"},"stakes":"Build the wrong surface first and we spend a week reaching a claim the bake-off may retire.","state":["No certificate surface exists; the engine audit ranks and prices five.","94.25% of real files carry a BROKEN block, so an Action starts red.","The audit advises against a badge: a verdict depends on target set and bench id."],"tension":["The best top-of-funnel artefact also shows our renderer as the loudest offender.","Publishing would force the eight declared, unmeasured rows to carry current dates, as nothing else does.","Every surface here is downstream of one day of CLI work."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What publishing costs and exposes","items":[["Compute, JS only","34.9 s","docs/ENGINE.md:870"],["Compute, with kramdown","unsettled, see the engine-dependency decision; ENGINE gives 92.4 min, ≈45 min and 5.6 min in three places","docs/ENGINE.md:871, :542, :354"],["Artifact size uncompressed","0.35 to 0.43 GB","docs/ENGINE.md:872"],["Surfaces that are declarations, not measurements","8 of 15 = 53.3%","docs/ENGINE.md:69; src/modules/mdmax/domain/targets.ts:125"],["Declaration staleness today","39 days","DECLARED_LAST_VERIFIED = 2026-08-01, computed against 2026-09-09"],["Files carrying a BROKEN block","94.25%","docs/ENGINE.md:820"],["`frontmatter-app` CORRUPT cells on AGENTS.md","25 of 38, all from our own soft-line-break choice","docs/ENGINE.md:830"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The five surfaces, ranked (docs/ENGINE.md:836-843)","cols":["Surface","Days","For","Measured failure mode"],"rows":[["Make the CLI executable","1","everything else imports it","fails today with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND; do not ship as npx until the frontmatter-app MUTATE row is fixed or excluded"],["MCP tool `certify_markdown`","2","an agent that can act on VOID and LEAK before writing","whole certificate is 24.4x source size: return summary plus non-PASS cells"],["Public web checker","4","top of funnel; 4.1 ms/file makes a request free","kramdown must not sit behind it synchronously at 51.2 ms/block"],["GitHub Action","3","repeat exposure inside someone else's CI","default-fail is a red build on adoption day for 94.25% of files"],["In-editor panel","8","retention; the fix is one keystroke away","a panel showing 25 MUTATEs trains its own dismissal"],["Badge","1","nothing defensible","anti-recommended outright: target subsetting alone moved AGENTS.md from 38 CORRUPT to 6"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Public web checker, with the corpus dataset published beside it","gains":["Best top of funnel and a citable number"],"costs":["Four days of UI","A stranger's first impression is 94% broken on their own files"],"system":"Web surface plus a 0.35 to 0.43 GB artefact.","screens":"A checker page and a dataset page.","money":"Hosting cost for the artefact.","what":"A public web checker with the corpus dataset published beside it. The best top of funnel and a citable number; four days of interface, and a stranger's first sight is 94 percent broken."},{"k":"b","label":"GitHub Action first, with a baseline file, failing only on new CORRUPT and worse","gains":["Repeat exposure inside a repo we do not own"],"costs":["Needs the baseline mechanism, or the default build is red"],"system":"Action wrapper plus baseline storage.","screens":"CI output only.","money":"The surface the Max line is priced on.","what":"A GitHub Action first, with a baseline file, failing only on new corruption. Repeat exposure inside repositories we do not own; without the baseline every default build starts red."},{"k":"c","label":"None until the bake-off settles what the certificate claims","gains":["No channel built for a claim that may not survive"],"costs":["Delays every distribution surface, with nothing for a stranger to run"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No certificate surface until the bake-off settles what the certificate claims. No channel built for a claim that may not survive, and nothing for a stranger to run meanwhile."},{"k":"d","label":"MCP tool first, returning a summary plus non-PASS cells","gains":["Cheapest at two days, no rendering work","Puts the verdict in front of the agent before it writes"],"costs":["Reaches only people already running agents, with no artefact to cite"],"system":"MCP tool over the CLI path.","screens":"None.","money":"None directly; it is the integration story.","what":"An MCP tool first, returning a summary plus the non-PASS cells. Two days, no rendering, and the agent using it can act before it writes; only reaches people already running agents."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Cheapest, no rendering, and the agent using it can act on a verdict before writing.","Put whichever surface goes first behind the bake-off, so no channel is built for an untested claim."],"flip":"A pilot buyer who does not run agents, which moves the web checker ahead of the MCP tool.","linked":["E10","E6","E8","G20"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:836","docs/ENGINE.md:839","docs/ENGINE.md:840","docs/ENGINE.md:841","docs/ENGINE.md:862","docs/ENGINE.md:905","docs/ENGINE.md:866","docs/ENGINE.md:870","docs/ENGINE.md:872","docs/ENGINE.md:820","docs/ENGINE.md:830","docs/ENGINE.md:911","docs/ENGINE.md:69","src/modules/mdmax/domain/targets.ts:24","src/modules/mdmax/domain/targets.ts:125","merged: E30"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The certificate is after the pilot.","recWhy":"A certificate only draws anyone in if a stranger can run it, and every candidate surface imports a CLI that does not currently start. Of the five surfaces the audit prices, the MCP tool is cheapest at two days, needs no rendering, and puts the verdict in front of an agent before it writes, which is the moment a verdict is worth having. It reaches only people already running agents, which after this weekend is the buyer. The web checker is the better funnel and the worse first impression, because 94 percent of real files carry a broken block and a stranger's first sight of us would be their own files failing. Whichever surface goes first sits behind E6's bake-off, so no channel is built for a claim that may not survive it. If the pilot buyer turns out not to run agents, the web checker moves ahead."},{"id":"E10","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Wiring","weight":"high","q":"Is making `mdmax cert` runnable and running it over the whole corpus the first engine commit?","lede":"The certificate is the only thing that can execute twelve of thirteen engine files, and it has never executed them.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"node scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND","note":"domain/offsets, missing extension","tone":"stop"},{"label":"ts-resolve.mjs: no importer","tone":"warn"},{"label":"12 engine files unreachable","tone":"stop"},{"label":"bake-off, fold run, MCP tool, dataset","note":"all blocked behind one day","tone":"sunk"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Every corpus number in circulation was produced by hand-writing the resolve hook the repo already contains.","caption":"One missing register, five blocked decisions"},"stakes":"Every engine number feeding the other decisions comes from a hook nobody has committed.","state":["`node scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs` fails with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`; `cert` is not among the 26 scripts.","The CLI pads each verdict before comparing it to `PASS`, so every verdict prints red.","The certificate's comparison step, `mdmax/fold@1`, has run four times, never over all 8,513 files."],"tension":["The plan lists this tenth, at one day, behind nine other items.","The full JS-only run takes 34.9 seconds, so the real cost is wiring.","Four samples give 24.35%, 25.26%, 33.45% and 18.74%, with no principled way to pick."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"State of the CLI at HEAD","items":[["`node scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs <file>`","ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on `.../mdmax/domain/offsets`","reproduced this session"],["`cert` in package.json scripts","absent: 26 scripts, none is cert","verified; package.json:8"],["Importers of scripts/ts-resolve.mjs anywhere in the repo","0: it is named only in prose","verified by grep"],["Works with `node --import ./scripts/ts-resolve.mjs`","yes: all seven engines load, including kramdown 2.5.2","docs/ENGINE.md:526"],["Second defect found while demoing","`paint(s.padEnd(18))` compares against the padded string, so every verdict renders red including PASS","scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs:195; docs/ENGINE.md:1004"],["Days since the commit claiming it landed","37 at the time the plan was written","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Four fold runs, four answers","cols":["Sample","Files","Blocks BROKEN","Source"],"rows":[["Every-nth stride","300","24.35%","docs/ENGINE.md:819"],["Every-nth stride","800","25.26%","docs/ENGINE.md:819"],["Three vaults (kepano, erazlogo, s-blu)","405","33.45%","docs/ENGINE.md:534"],["Four vaults, all seven targets","419","33.48%","docs/ENGINE.md:138"],["Four vaults, excluding frontmatter-app","419","18.74%","docs/ENGINE.md:139"],["Whole corpus","8,513","never run","docs/ENGINE.md:718"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What it costs to settle","items":[["Full corpus, JS engines only","34.9 s","docs/ENGINE.md:870"],["Full corpus, with kramdown","unsettled, see the engine-dependency decision","docs/ENGINE.md:871, :542, :354"],["Prerequisite","a runnable CLI, the subject of this decision","reproduced failing this session"],["Roadmap rank of running it","2 of 9, ahead of the YAML defects","docs/ENGINE.md:733"],["Audit's framing","\"an unknown, not a task — the corpus exists, the fold exists, nobody has pressed the button\"","docs/ENGINE.md:718"],["What the result also sizes","the 1.5 founder-weeks costed for fixing the fold, which may turn out to be zero","docs/ENGINE.md:718"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Register the resolve hook, add a `cert` script, fix padEnd, run the full fold","gains":["Unblocks the regex bake-off, the dataset and the MCP tool","Replaces four disagreeing numbers with one carrying a corpus id"],"costs":["One day ahead of user-visible fixes"],"system":"Node register hook, one script entry, one fix.","screens":"The demo stops printing every verdict red.","money":"None.","what":"Register the resolve hook, add a cert script, fix the padEnd comparison, and run the full fold over all 8,513 files. One day; every verdict currently prints red, passes included."},{"k":"b","label":"After fixing the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug","what":"Fix the left-edge list and line-ending bugs first; wire the CLI in week two.","gains":["User-visible refusals fall first"],"costs":["The corpus numbers stay hand-produced and five decisions stay blocked"],"system":"No change until then.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Delete the CLI; certify only under vitest","gains":["Removes a broken entry point"],"costs":["Gives up third-party reproducibility, and every public surface with it"],"system":"Deletes the script.","screens":"None.","money":"Removes the certificate as a sellable artefact.","what":"Delete the CLI and certify only under vitest. Removes a broken entry point; gives up third-party reproducibility and every public surface that depended on it."},{"k":"d","label":"Accept the 800-file stride sample as the standing fold figure","gains":["A number today with defensible sampling"],"costs":["Differs from the vault runs by nine points, visibly contradicting them"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Accept the 800-file stride sample as the standing fold figure. A number today with defensible sampling, differing from the vault runs by nine points and visibly contradicting them."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One day makes twelve unreachable files runnable, and today every verdict prints red including passes.","Two sections of the engine audit rank it first; everything else imports this path."],"flip":"Cutting the certificate from the product entirely, which makes option c the honest cleanup.","linked":["E6","E8","E9"],"sources":["scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs:195","docs/ENGINE.md:526","docs/ENGINE.md:838","docs/ENGINE.md:1004","docs/ENGINE.md:718","docs/ENGINE.md:733","docs/ENGINE.md:819","docs/ENGINE.md:870","docs/ENGINE.md:138","docs/ENGINE.md:139","docs/ENGINE.md:412","docs/ENGINE.md:228","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","package.json:8","mdmax-cert.mjs:195","merged: E34"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Making the certificate runnable is engine work, deferred with it.","recWhy":"The certificate is the only thing that can execute twelve of the thirteen engine files, and it has never executed them. The script fails on module resolution, cert is not among the 26 npm scripts, and the comparison pads each verdict before checking it against PASS, so every verdict prints red including the passes. Its fold step has run four times, never over the whole corpus, and the four samples give 24, 25, 33 and 19 percent with no principled way to choose. One day of wiring makes twelve unreachable files runnable and replaces four disagreeing numbers with one that carries a corpus id, and every other engine decision here imports this path, so two sections of the audit rank it first. Q14 carries CI first, and cert runnable is part of that. If the certificate is cut from the product, c is the honest cleanup."},{"id":"E15","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Correctness","weight":"high","q":"Do we fix the two defects that return a wrong answer and report success, or defer until a caller reads offsets back?","lede":"One returns end-of-document with `ok: true`; the other prints a wrong column in the only engine error users see.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"The engine reports","tone":"stop"},{"title":"What is true","tone":"good"}],"rows":[{"label":"Probe past the last checkpoint, length 512","cells":["end of document, `ok: true`","a real offset"]},{"label":"Same at length 1024 and 1536","cells":["end of document, `ok: true`","a real offset"]},{"label":"`é` + 0xFF","cells":["column 2","column 2"]},{"label":"`éé` + 0xFF","cells":["column 1","column 3"]},{"label":"`ééé` + 0xFF","cells":["column 4","column 4"]}],"note":"Both failures are silent: one carries `ok: true`, the other is a plausible number in an error message.","caption":"Two defects reproduced at HEAD, both reporting success"},"stakes":"Per-hunk accept reads byte anchors back, so a wrong conversion becomes a wrong byte range in the main review feature.","state":["The offset map's checkpoint table never assigns its last slot.","At lengths that are exact multiples of 512 every probe returns end-of-document.","The UTF-8 checker `decodeStrict` binary-searches on a condition that is not monotone on multi-byte input."],"tension":["`toByte` is unaffected, so nothing reaches a user from the offset defect today.","A test probing only low offsets at 512, 1024 and 1536 passes on the unfixed code.","The column impact is minor; a binary search over an unsorted property shipped green."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Reproduced at HEAD in this session: the shape matters","items":[["Length 512","512 of 513 probes wrong; first wrong at byte 0","live run"],["Length 1024","512 of 1025 probes wrong; first wrong at byte 512","live run"],["Length 1536","512 of 1537 probes wrong; first wrong at byte 1024","live run"],["Length 2048","512 of 2049 probes wrong; first wrong at byte 1536","live run"],["Every wrong answer","`{ok: true, value: <document length>}`: indistinguishable from a valid result","live run"],["Length 513 (control)","0 of 514 wrong","live run"],["Fix","size the table `Math.ceil(text.length / BLOCK) || 1`","docs/ENGINE.md:99"],["Existing offsets test cases","16, none on a 512-multiple document","docs/ENGINE.md:99; counted live (the §68.1 and §68.3 tables say 22, which does not match the file)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Which test shape finds it (docs/ENGINE.md:1863)","cols":["Generator","Offsets checked","Failures against the unfixed code"],"rows":[["Uniform random (emoji every 7th, lengths 400 to 2000)","4,966","0"],["Boundary-straddling (surrogate low unit on a 512 boundary)","9,363","600, first at pad=511, delta exactly +3"],["Same generator against the fixed code","9,363","0"],["A test at 512/1024/1536 that probes only low offsets","none","0 at 1024 and 1536: it must probe at or past the last checkpoint"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Measured columns, reproduced live this session","cols":["Input","Reported column","Correct column"],"rows":[["`é` + 0xFF","2","2"],["`éé` + 0xFF","1","3"],["`ééé` + 0xFF","4","4"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Reach and cost","items":[["Reaches a user today","yes: the only mdmax defect that does","docs/ENGINE.md:47; docs/CRITIQUE.md:762"],["CRITIQUE grading","MINOR in impact, SERIOUS as signal","docs/CRITIQUE.md:762"],["Where it surfaces","search-index.ts and get-snapshot.ts, via explainFailure","docs/ENGINE.md:47; verified by grep"],["Failure-locate performance today","10.6 ms for an invalid byte at offset 4,194,303","docs/ENGINE.md:346"],["Budget for that operation","coldOpen, 50 ms","docs/ENGINE.md:346"],["Headroom","4.7x on the current bisect; a bounded forward decode fits the same tier","docs/ENGINE.md:346"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Fix both now, with tests shown failing against the unfixed code first","what":"One character-class change in the checkpoint fill, and a bounded forward decode.","gains":["Closes two defects that report success, one file each"],"costs":["The offset test must walk every offset, not sample three lengths"],"system":"Two domain files.","screens":"The refusal shown when a file is read in can carry a correct location.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Fix both, and adopt the boundary-straddling generator with them","gains":["Catches the next instance of the same class"],"costs":["Pulls the property-testing decision in as a dependency"],"system":"Adds a generator file.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fix both and adopt the boundary-straddling generator with them. Catches the next instance of the same class; pulls the property-testing decision in as a dependency."},{"k":"c","label":"Fix the offset map now; drop line and column from the UTF-8 message","gains":["Always correct and cheaper, with no bisection to maintain"],"costs":["A byte offset is less useful to someone opening the file"],"system":"One domain file plus a message change.","screens":"Refusal copy loses the location.","money":"None.","what":"Fix the offset map now and drop line and column from the UTF-8 error message. Always correct and cheaper with no bisection to maintain; a byte offset is less useful to a person."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer both until a caller reads offsets back","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Ships the review feature on a conversion that can return end-of-document"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer both until something reads offsets back. No work now, and the review feature ships on a conversion that can return end-of-document for a real position."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["A wrong answer reporting `ok: true` is what this engine's type system exists to prevent.","Fix before the anchor work, where a wrong offset first becomes a wrong byte range."],"flip":"The property-testing decision landing the same week, which gets the whole class for barely more effort.","linked":["E17","E22","E23"],"sources":["src/modules/mdmax/domain/offsets.ts:127","src/modules/mdmax/domain/shape-gate.ts","docs/ENGINE.md:41","docs/ENGINE.md:47","docs/ENGINE.md:99","docs/ENGINE.md:346","docs/ENGINE.md:1863","docs/ENGINE.md:467","docs/CRITIQUE.md:761","docs/CRITIQUE.md:762","merged: E29"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Both defects sit in engine paths the first build does not call.","recWhy":"Two defects return a wrong answer and report success. The offset map's checkpoint table never assigns its last slot, so at any length that is an exact multiple of 512 every probe returns end-of-document with ok true. The UTF-8 checker binary-searches on a condition that is not monotone on multi-byte input, so the only engine error a person ever sees prints the wrong column. A wrong answer that reports ok is what this engine's type system exists to prevent, and both fixes are one file each: a character-class change in the checkpoint fill, and a bounded forward decode. Fix them before the review anchor work, because that is where a wrong offset first becomes a wrong byte range. The test has to walk every offset, not sample three lengths, because a test probing 512, 1024 and 1536 passes on the unfixed code."},{"id":"E16","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Correctness","weight":"high","q":"Do we rewrite `safeInsert`, delete it, or ship the review flow without a placement gate?","lede":"The gate meant to stop a write changing what a document means refuses the safe case and permits the unsafe one.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"safeInsert says","tone":"stop"},{"title":"What is true","tone":"good"}],"rows":[{"label":"After a heading, before a thematic break","cells":["ADJACENT_TO_SETEXT_UNDERLINE","safe: no insertion converts it"]},{"label":"After a paragraph, before a thematic break","cells":["ADJACENT_TO_SETEXT_UNDERLINE","safe"]},{"label":"Inside a fenced code block","cells":["ok: true","the marker lands as visible text"]},{"label":"Inside an inline code span","cells":["ok: true","the marker lands as visible text"]},{"label":"Callers in `src/`","cells":["zero","the flagship needs one"]}],"note":"The pre-check ORs over a two-line lookback and treats a bare `---` as a setext underline.","caption":"The placement gate, measured both ways"},"stakes":"Per-hunk accept and reject is priced at 16 points, and its write gate refuses its primary case.","state":["`placement.ts` has zero callers anywhere in `src/`.","Both wrong refusals and both wrong permissions reproduce today.","The critique grades it severe once `land()`, which applies accepted changes, ships."],"tension":["Both defects are small, and both have failing tests available today.","The right shape, per-span-kind permitted deltas, belongs to the body-span decision: three founder-weeks."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The gate's measured behaviour (docs/ENGINE.md:45)","cols":["Input","What it should do","What it does"],"rows":[["`# Title\\n\\n---\\n\\nBody\\n`: insert at the break","allow: the `---` is a thematic break","refuses, ADJACENT_TO_SETEXT_UNDERLINE"],["`Body text\\n\\n---\\n\\nMore\\n`","allow","refuses, same reason"],["Insert inside a ```js fence","refuse","{ok:true}: the marker becomes visible code"],["Insert inside an inline code span","refuse","{ok:true}"],["Paragraph, callout or heading at a true block boundary","allow","SKELETON_CHANGED"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Standing","items":[["Product call sites","0","verified by grep over src/"],["Test assertions","16, opening with a genuine red proof","docs/ENGINE.md:121, docs/ENGINE.md:198"],["What the tests never assert","what it accepts beyond markers","docs/ENGINE.md:198"],["CRITIQUE severity","SEVERE the moment `land()` ships","docs/CRITIQUE.md:760"],["Is `land()` built","no: no `land()`, no splice journal, no review file anywhere in src/","verified by grep"],["Points the pilot assigns the feature it gates","16","docs/DECIDE.md:87"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Rewrite both defects now, before `land()` is designed","what":"Fix the setext lookback and add the skip-region check for fences and code spans.","gains":["Makes the gate usable for per-hunk accept","Keeps it decoupled from the body-span decision"],"costs":["Work on a file nothing calls yet"],"system":"One domain file, then wired by `land()`.","screens":"None yet.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Fold the rewrite into the body-span work","gains":["The architecturally right shape, one design instead of two"],"costs":["Makes per-hunk accept wait on a three-founder-week high-risk item"],"system":"Placement becomes part of the span contract.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fold the rewrite into the body-span work. The architecturally right shape, one design instead of two; per-hunk accept then waits on a three-founder-week high-risk item."},{"k":"c","label":"Leave it unwired until a caller exists","gains":["Costs nothing now"],"costs":["Puts the review flow's write gate on a late rewrite's critical path"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it unwired until a caller exists. Costs nothing now, and puts the review flow's write gate on a late rewrite's critical path."},{"k":"d","label":"Delete it; gate writes on block-skeleton equality alone","gains":["Removes 154 lines nothing calls"],"costs":["Gives up the check that a boundary does not change the parse"],"system":"Deletes a domain file.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Delete it and gate writes on block-skeleton equality alone. Removes 154 lines nothing calls; gives up the check that a boundary does not change the parse."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Both defects are small with failing tests, and the gate stands between a hunk and a changed meaning.","Fixing now keeps it decoupled from the body-span decision, which is bigger and less certain."],"flip":"Per-hunk accept and reject leaving the pilot, which makes leaving it unwired defensible if written down.","linked":["E21","E22","E26"],"sources":["src/modules/mdmax/domain/placement.ts","docs/ENGINE.md:45","docs/ENGINE.md:121","docs/ENGINE.md:198","docs/ENGINE.md:1464","docs/CRITIQUE.md:760","docs/DECIDE.md:87"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Placement gating serves the review flow.","recWhy":"The gate meant to stop a write from changing what a document means refuses the safe case and permits the unsafe one. It flags a paragraph before a thematic break as a setext underline risk when it is not, and it lets a marker land inside a fenced block as visible text. Nothing in src calls it today, which is why it has not hurt anyone yet, and the critique grades it severe the moment land, which applies accepted changes, ships. Both defects are small, both have failing tests available now, and fixing them keeps the gate decoupled from the body-span decision, which is three founder-weeks of the highest-risk work in the plan. On instruction files the gate is what stops an accepted hunk from changing which rule a nested list belongs to. If per-hunk accept leaves the pilot, leaving it unwired is defensible if written down."},{"id":"E20","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Parsing","weight":"high","q":"Does the engine adopt @lezer/markdown for incremental parsing, or stay on remark with a typed one-way boundary?","lede":"Two committed documents give opposite answers, and the premise the plan's answer rests on was measured and does not hold.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Bytes on disk","items":["the file","the only source of truth"],"tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Engine","items":["remark / micromark","OffsetMap","splice writer"],"side":"cold, once, per document","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Editor","items":["CodeMirror","@lezer/markdown decorations"],"side":"viewport-budgeted, per keystroke","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Boundary","items":["typed, one-way, lint-enforced"],"side":"an editor offset cannot reach the splice","tone":"good"}],"note":"Lezer offsets are UTF-16 code units, the same unit mdast reports, so adopting it buys nothing against the byte contract.","caption":"Which layer owns parsing, and what the boundary refuses"},"stakes":"A stale editor offset reaching the writer puts correct-looking bytes in the wrong place, on every save.","state":["The plan says lezer is vendored so review spans survive every keystroke.","The engine audit says keep remark and make the boundary one-way and typed.","Lezer reads a frontmatter block as a rule plus a setext heading."],"tension":["The engine has no keystroke loop: its work is cold, once, per document.","The two numbers that drive the product live below the markdown grammar entirely.","One way costs 4,849 ms per corpus pass; the other writes bad offsets."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Measured, on the same inputs (docs/ENGINE.md:1962-2017)","cols":["Question","@lezer/markdown","remark / micromark"],"rows":[["Position unit","UTF-16 code units","UTF-16 code units"],["`# 🌊 tide` heading span","[0,9)","offset 0..9: identical; byte length is 11"],["Frontmatter block","HorizontalRule + SetextHeading2","addressable, via remark-frontmatter"],["Bare-CR document, 21 units","one ATXHeading1 covering everything","heading[0,3) paragraph[4,8) list[9,20)"],["Emits HTML for the certificate","no","yes"],["Full-corpus parse, 8,513 files","566 ms","4,849 ms"],["Incremental reparse, 50 largest docs","0.46 ms with fragment reuse (1.83 ms cold)","51.37 ms full"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Cost of each answer","items":[["Adopt lezer in the engine","rewrite 3 domain files plus the bench","docs/ENGINE.md:1940"],["Typed one-way boundary","~60 lines plus one lint rule","docs/ENGINE.md:1940"],["Do nothing (today)","0 lines, and a stale-tree splice stays representable","docs/ENGINE.md:1938"],["Engine parse cost either way","the engine is cold, once, per document, in a worker: there is no keystroke loop in it","docs/ENGINE.md:1996"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave both trees with no declared boundary","gains":["Costs nothing"],"costs":["Keeps available the one failure the contract exists to prevent"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave both parse trees in place with no declared boundary. Costs nothing, and keeps available the one failure the write contract exists to prevent: a stale editor offset reaching the writer."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep remark; add the typed tree boundary and the import lint rule","gains":["A stale editor offset cannot reach the writer","Both documents stop contradicting each other"],"costs":["Keeps the slower full-corpus parse and one more lint rule"],"system":"A branded editor-offset type and one import rule.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep remark and add a typed one-way boundary plus an import lint rule. A stale editor offset cannot reach the writer; the slower full-corpus parse stays, and one more lint rule."},{"k":"c","label":"Vendor lezer into the engine for incremental parsing","gains":["566 ms per corpus pass instead of 4,849 ms"],"costs":["Buys throughput for no consumer, and makes the misparse structural"],"system":"Rewrites three domain files and the bench.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Vendor lezer into the engine for incremental parsing. 566 milliseconds per corpus pass instead of 4,849; throughput bought for a consumer that does not exist, and the misparse becomes structural."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer until a feature needs sub-100 ms structural reparse","gains":["Honest deferral"],"costs":["Leaves the plan and the engine doc contradicting each other in writing"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer until a feature needs sub-100 millisecond structural reparse. Honest deferral, and the plan and the engine document keep contradicting each other in writing."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Incrementality is an editor property being bought for an engine consumer that does not exist.","Whichever way this goes, one of the two documents has to change in the same session."],"flip":"A shipped feature that genuinely needs sub-frame structural feedback on a 900 KB document.","linked":["E15","E21","E22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233","docs/ENGINE.md:1927","docs/ENGINE.md:1938","docs/ENGINE.md:1940","docs/ENGINE.md:1962","docs/ENGINE.md:1970","docs/ENGINE.md:1996","docs/ENGINE.md:2007","docs/ENGINE.md:2017","docs/ENGINE.md:2102"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Incremental parsing is a performance choice for later.","recWhy":"Two committed documents give opposite answers, and the premise the plan's answer rests on was measured and does not hold. The plan says lezer is vendored so review spans survive every keystroke, but the engine has no keystroke loop, its work is cold and once per document, and the two numbers that drive the product live below the markdown grammar entirely. Lezer also reads a frontmatter block as a thematic break plus a setext heading, which is a structural misparse of the thing we edit most. Keep remark, add a typed one-way boundary so an editor offset can never reach the writer, and add the lint rule that enforces it. Whichever way this goes, one of the two documents has to change in the same session so they stop contradicting each other. If a shipped feature genuinely needs sub-frame structural feedback on a 900 KB document, revisit."},{"id":"E22","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Scope","weight":"high","q":"Do review anchors carry their position in the document's structure, or a content hash alone?","lede":"Review state, which spans a person has read, is the product, and its re-anchoring figure describes code nobody has written.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Content hash only","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Hash plus multiplicity","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"False positives, 43,056 attempts","cells":["141","16"]},{"label":"Of those, in the exact-hash lane","cells":["115, undetectable","0"]},{"label":"Resolve rate","cells":["about half a point higher","98.65%"]},{"label":"Task lines unique within their file","cells":["67.4%","67.4%"]},{"label":"Cross-document resolve without a scope check","cells":["35.1%","0 with the check"]}],"note":"One integer field converts an 8.8× worse false-positive rate into zero errors in the lane where nothing can catch them.","caption":"The multiplicity ablation, everything else identical"},"stakes":"A false positive replaces bytes the user never pointed at, in the feature the product is named for.","state":["The engine audit classifies the published 99.627% re-anchoring claim as aspirational: no anchor store exists.","A task line's text is unique within its file for 67.4% of items.","Anchors resolve into the wrong file 35.1% of the time without scope checking."],"tension":["Eight fields to persist and version makes every threshold change a migration.","A hash-only anchor refuses one task item in four, the span written to most."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Uniqueness by anchor kind, measured over the corpus","items":[["Task-line trimmed text unique within its file","2,278 of 3,378 = 67.436%","docs/ENGINE.md:1291"],["Same, plus one line of context","2,554 of 3,378 = 75.607%","docs/ENGINE.md:1291"],["ATX heading full ancestor path unique within its file","37,413 of 37,489 = 99.797%","docs/ENGINE.md:1291"],["Corpus blocks sitting in a same-hash group of 2 or more","6.73%","docs/ENGINE.md:1596"],["Anchors from one corpus file resolving into a different file","1,401 of 3,992 = 35.095%","docs/ENGINE.md:1628"],["Same, with the quote gate raised to 64 characters","31.06%: length gates do not separate them","docs/ENGINE.md:1628"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The multiplicity ablation: 448 files, 43,056 attempts (docs/ENGINE.md:1593-1596)","cols":["Configuration","Resolve rate","False positives","FP in the exact-hash lane"],"rows":[["With `multiplicity`","98.6505%","16 (0.0372%)","0"],["Without: unique hash means accept","99.1410%","141 (0.3275%)","115"],["Cost of the field","0.49 points of resolve rate","none","none"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Structural path and ordinal only","gains":["99.797% unique for headings, small record"],"costs":["Silent on reorder: a moved section relocates the anchor confidently"],"system":"Path index.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Structural path and ordinal only. 99.797 percent unique for headings and a small record; silent on reorder, so a moved section relocates the anchor confidently to the wrong place."},{"k":"b","label":"Content hash plus refusal whenever two or more blocks match","gains":["Much simpler to persist and version, and honest"],"costs":["Refuses one task item in four, on the span written to most"],"system":"Hash store plus a duplicate check.","screens":"Frequent unresolvable spans.","money":"None.","what":"Content hash, with a refusal whenever two or more blocks match. Much simpler to persist and honest; refuses one task item in four, on the span written to most."},{"k":"c","label":"In-session offsets only, re-derived on every load","gains":["Correct within a session and cheap"],"costs":["An agent's proposal cannot survive a save, which is the scenario"],"system":"No store.","screens":"Review state resets on reload.","money":"None.","what":"In-session offsets only, re-derived on every load. Correct within a session and cheap; an agent's proposal cannot survive a save, which is the scenario the product exists for."},{"k":"d","label":"Full record: hash, multiplicity, ordinal, docId, quote, context, heading path","what":"The anchor the engine audit specifies, with scope checked before any content is examined.","gains":["Zero false positives on exact-hash matches, at 98.65% resolved","Survives rename and reorder"],"costs":["Eight fields to persist; every threshold change becomes a migration"],"system":"Anchor record plus resolver, versioned.","screens":"Review panel explains an unfound span.","money":"None."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["One integer field turns an 8.8× worse false-positive rate into zero errors on exact-hash matches.","Publish both harnesses under one manifest with the mutation mix stated, rather than arguing them."],"flip":"A harness matching the mutation mix with no exact-hash false positives and no multiplicity field.","linked":["E21","E36","E37","F1"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:124","docs/ENGINE.md:1291","docs/ENGINE.md:1516","docs/ENGINE.md:1529","docs/ENGINE.md:1593","docs/ENGINE.md:1596","docs/ENGINE.md:1628","docs/ENGINE.md:1714"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Anchors are review state.","recWhy":"The published 99.627 percent re-anchoring figure describes code nobody has written; there is no anchor store. What has been measured is that a task line's text is unique within its file only 67.4 percent of the time, and that anchors resolve into the wrong file 35.1 percent of the time without a scope check. A hash-only anchor refuses one task item in four. The full record, hash, multiplicity, ordinal, document id, quote, context and heading path, with scope checked before content is examined, gives zero false positives on exact-hash matches at 98.65 percent resolved and survives rename and reorder. One integer field, multiplicity, turns an 8.8 times worse false-positive rate into zero. Eight fields to version is the honest cost. Publish both harnesses under one manifest with the mutation mix stated rather than arguing them."},{"id":"E26","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Refusals","weight":"high","q":"Do we refuse edits whose effect reaches beyond the changed bytes, or keep writing near reference-link definitions?","lede":"There are edits where every byte in the changed range is correct and prose elsewhere changes meaning anyway.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"guide.md","lines":[{"t":"See the [spec][s] before you start.","mark":"accent","note":"renders as a link only because of line 9"},{"t":"","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"…","mark":"sunk"},{"t":"","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"[s]: https://example.com/spec","mark":"stop","note":"delete this and line 1 becomes literal text"}],"note":"Measured across four CommonMark engines, unanimous in both directions. No byte in the edited range changes.","caption":"An edit at the bottom that changes the meaning at the top"},"stakes":"The engine keeps a silent failure of exactly the kind it exists to eliminate.","state":["The plan names this class of edit and says nothing implements it.","The class also covers HTML block condition 7 and setext underlines.","The corpus has 15 setext headings across 11 of 8,513 files."],"tension":["Nothing measures how often the shape occurs, so the availability cost is unknown.","An unmeasured refusal rate on top of today's 83% could make the editor unusable.","The PRD advises against auto-normalising the ambiguous case outright."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The three non-local classes named","cols":["Class","Mechanism","Status"],"rows":[["Reference links","`[foo][bar]` means link or literal text depending on a definition elsewhere, perhaps later","measured unanimous across four engines; undeclared"],["HTML block condition 7","block boundaries depend on surrounding context","named, unmeasured"],["Setext underlines","the spec records four possible interpretations","placement.ts already refuses near them, with a parse-verified fixture; 15 in the corpus, 0.129% of headings"],["Corpus incidence of the first two","unmeasured","no figure exists in the record"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why it belongs to the engine and not the UI","items":[["Engine boundary rule","\"the engine may do anything that is decidable from the bytes and refusable when it is not\"","docs/ENGINE.md:767"],["Source of the diagnosis","CommonMark's own author, on why he built Djot","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:488"],["Standing anti-recommendation","do not build an auto-fixer that silently normalises ambiguous indentation, reference-link placement, or a bare-CR line ending","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:5829"],["Refusal codes mdmax already emits","19 typed failure codes","docs/ENGINE.md:978"],["Refusal rate the writer already carries","83.01%","docs/ENGINE.md:487"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Declare all three; refuse only the destructive case and warn on the rest","what":"Refuse deleting or moving a reference-link definition; warn where the other two are touched.","gains":["Closes the one case where meaning is lost silently","Adds no refusals to ordinary edits"],"costs":["A warning can be ignored, and two behaviours need explaining"],"system":"One refusal reason, two warnings, one pre-scan.","screens":"A warning state beside the refusal state.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Leave it undeclared","gains":["No refusals added"],"costs":["A silent failure of the class the engine exists to eliminate"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave the class undeclared. No refusals added, and a silent failure of exactly the class the engine exists to eliminate stays live."},{"k":"c","label":"Measure corpus incidence first, then set the refusal set","gains":["Makes the availability cost a number, and the corpus is on disk"],"costs":["Delays the declaration, leaving the silent case live another week"],"system":"A counting script.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Measure how often the shape occurs in the corpus first, then set the refusal set. Makes the availability cost a number and the corpus is on disk; the silent case stays live another week."},{"k":"d","label":"Declare all three classes and refuse edits that touch them","gains":["The strongest correctness position, one rule, no case analysis"],"costs":["Adds unmeasured refusals on top of 83%, to prove a rare point"],"system":"Three refusal reasons and a whole-file pre-scan.","screens":"More refusals in the panel.","money":"None.","what":"Declare all three classes and refuse any edit that touches them. The strongest correctness position, one rule; unmeasured refusals on top of today's 83 percent, to prove a rare point."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Deleting a definition at the bottom changes a paragraph at the top, and the user cannot see it.","Declare all three in writing either way, so the next reader does not rediscover them."],"flip":"The corpus measurement showing the three classes together touch under a percent of files.","linked":["E16","E2","E21"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:488","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:128","docs/ENGINE.md:767","docs/ENGINE.md:1388","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:5829"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Edit blast radius is engine correctness for later.","recWhy":"There are edits where every byte in the changed range is correct and prose elsewhere changes meaning anyway. Delete a reference-link definition at the bottom of a file and a paragraph at the top loses its link, and the user cannot see it. The same class covers HTML block condition 7 and setext underlines, and the corpus holds 15 setext headings across 11 files, so it is rare and it is real. Nothing measures how often the shape occurs, and an unmeasured refusal rate on top of today's 83 percent could make the editor unusable, so refuse only the destructive case, deleting or moving a definition, and warn on the other two. Declare all three in writing either way, so the next reader does not rediscover them. If the corpus measurement shows the three together touch under a percent of files, the warnings can become refusals."},{"id":"E32","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Strategy","weight":"high","q":"Do we adopt a dated kill condition for the engine, and which one?","lede":"The engine doc wrote its own falsifiers, none carries a date, and the number that decides them is not instrumented.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Under 1 in 20 hit a refusal","tone":"warn"},{"title":"1 in 5 or more hit one","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"What it proves","cells":["nobody meets the guarantee","the guarantee is met constantly"]},{"label":"Which defence dies","cells":["the six engine moments are theatre","refusal UX becomes a churn risk"]},{"label":"Where the budget belongs","cells":["projections, not the engine","refusal copy and recovery"]},{"label":"Readable today","cells":["no","no"]}],"note":"There is no value of this number at which both defences survive, and nobody can currently read it.","caption":"The refusal-rate pincer, per activated user per 30 days"},"stakes":"Without a dated test the engine cannot be shown wrong, and this programme has already withdrawn three claims.","state":["The engine audit's own finding: the product works today with the engine almost entirely disconnected.","Five falsifiers are listed with thresholds and none has a date.","The foreign-vault open rate, the Z metric, needs 99.9% and sits at 17% today."],"tension":["Instrumenting depends on the measurement decision; counts live in a local readable file.","The critique's 40-person survey by 2026-12-31 is the only dated falsifier on record."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The falsifiers already written (docs/ENGINE.md:1025-1031)","cols":["Claim","Measurement","Threshold","State"],"rows":[["Deterministic guarantees retain","Obsidian projection plugins vs AI capability installs","7,289,307 against 1,005,651 = 7.25x in favour of projections","already refuted, from outside the thesis"],["Nobody hits the refusal, so nobody values it","`cert_refusal` per activated user, 30 days","fewer than 1 in 20 means the six moments are theatre","not instrumented"],["The refusal is the ceiling, not the floor","foreign-vault open rate, the Z metric","target 99.9%; today 17%","measured, far from target"],["Users want the guarantee but not visibly","day-7 return split by whether the byte diff was shown, as a release cohort","activated users must retain 2x","no cohort exists"],["Buyers pay for consolidation, not fidelity","the first 25 sales conversations","none","zero conversations held"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The alternative kill line in the record","items":[["CRITIQUE's proposal","40 strangers on their own repos by 2026-12-31, Ellis wording","docs/CRITIQUE.md:330"],["Its threshold","under 40% \"very disappointed\" and fewer than 5 of 40 naming whole-file rewrites","docs/CRITIQUE.md:330"],["Its consequence","stop the editor, keep the engine, ship it as a free plugin","docs/CRITIQUE.md:330"],["The plan's only engine kill line","patched corpus still refusing over 10 of 7,969 by day 24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:327"],["The pincer, stated in the record","\"There is no value of that metric at which both the refusal-UX defence and the engine-as-differentiator defence survive\"","docs/CRITIQUE.md:662"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Adopt the 40-person survey by 2026-12-31","gains":["A date, a stated consequence, and no instrumentation needed"],"costs":["Tests interest rather than the engine, and recruiting is real"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"Recruiting time.","what":"Adopt the 40-person survey by 31 December 2026 as the kill condition. A date and a consequence with no instrumentation; it tests interest rather than the engine, and recruiting is real."},{"k":"b","label":"Adopt the falsifier table with dates, and instrument `cert_refusal` in the pilot","gains":["Makes the engine falsifiable on its own terms","The refusal count becomes a reading rather than an argument"],"costs":["Depends on the measurement decision, and adds a local counter"],"system":"A refusal counter in the local usage file.","screens":"None; the count lives in a readable file.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the falsifier table the engine document already wrote, with dates on each, and instrument cert_refusal in the pilot. The refusal count becomes a reading rather than an argument."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the foreign-vault open rate alone","gains":["One number, already measured at 17%, tied to the fixes"],"costs":["Says nothing about value, and cannot read the refusal count"],"system":"A corpus assertion.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the foreign-vault open rate alone. One number, already measured at 17 percent, tied to the fixes; says nothing about value and cannot read the refusal count."},{"k":"d","label":"No engine kill condition","gains":["Nothing to run"],"costs":["The engine cannot be wrong","Three headline claims have already been withdrawn here"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No engine kill condition. Nothing to run, and the engine can never be wrong, in a repository where three headline claims have already been withdrawn."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The falsifiers are written and measured against; what is missing is dates and one instrument.","Under 1 in 20 users hitting a refusal means theatre; over 1 in 5 means churn risk."],"flip":"A measurement decision landing on self-reporting, which makes `cert_refusal` uninstrumentable.","linked":["E18"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:1023","docs/ENGINE.md:1025","docs/ENGINE.md:1027","docs/ENGINE.md:1028","docs/ENGINE.md:1030","docs/CRITIQUE.md:330","docs/CRITIQUE.md:662","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:327"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A kill line for engine work that is deferred.","recWhy":"The engine document wrote its own falsifiers, five of them with thresholds, and none carries a date, and the number that decides them, how often a real user hits a refusal, is not instrumented. The audit's own finding is that the product works today with the engine almost entirely disconnected, which is why a kill condition matters: without one, the engine cannot be wrong, and three headline claims have already been withdrawn in this repository. Put dates on the table that exists and instrument one counter in the pilot, so the refusal rate is read rather than argued. Under one in twenty users hitting a refusal means the refusals are theatre; over one in five means churn. The counter lives in a local readable file, which depends on the measurement decision in AC17. If that decision lands on self-reporting, the counter is uninstrumentable and c is what remains."},{"id":"E35","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"The review file","weight":"high","q":"Does Zed Delta's bet on CRDTs, real-time merge of everyone's edits, reopen \"never a CRDT\", and do we answer them by name?","lede":"A team with ten years of CRDT work reached the opposite conclusion in public. Our position is one table row.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Delta, on CRDTs","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"frontmatter, on git","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Where the state lives","cells":["a database beside the editor","a plain-text file in the repo"]},{"label":"What convergence guarantees","cells":["all replicas agree","nothing; git surfaces a conflict"]},{"label":"A write from outside the tool","cells":["not in the model","the case the product exists for"]},{"label":"A concurrent prose edit","cells":["merged automatically","refused and shown"]},{"label":"What a reader needs installed","cells":["their client","nothing"]}],"note":"Our objection is about document bytes, where interleaving produces byte-identical garbage. It was never argued for the review file.","caption":"Same thesis, opposite architecture"},"stakes":"The competitive set now holds a purpose-built rival, and our answer to their architecture is unwritten.","state":["The engine audit's list of things we will not build gives CRDTs one sentence of reasoning.","The plan states it only in its sync feature row.","Delta's 2026-09-01 post stakes span-level attribution on CRDTs."],"tension":["The argument is being had in public and we are not in it.","The review file is append-only JSONL, where the ban's reasoning does not obviously reach."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Reopen it for the review file only","gains":["Removes review-file merge conflicts; convergence on append-only is cheap"],"costs":["A binary review file breaks the plain-text promise"],"system":"A new review-file format and a library.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Reopen the CRDT ban for the review file only. Removes review-file merge conflicts and convergence on an append-only log is cheap; a binary review file breaks the plain-text promise."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the ban for prose, rebut Zed by name, decide the review file separately","gains":["Answers the objection before anyone raises it, at writing cost"],"costs":["Puts us on record against a team that may ship first"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the ban for prose, rebut Zed Delta by name in writing, and decide the review file separately. Answers the objection before anyone raises it; puts us on record against a team that may ship first."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the ban everywhere, say nothing publicly","gains":["No public commitment"],"costs":["The comparison gets made without us"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the ban everywhere and say nothing publicly. No public commitment, and the comparison gets made in rooms we are not in."},{"k":"d","label":"Reopen the whole position","gains":["Takes the competitor's evidence seriously"],"costs":["Contradicts a settled decision with no new measurement"],"system":"Rewrites the write path.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Reopen the whole position. Takes the competitor's evidence seriously, and contradicts a settled decision with no new measurement behind the reversal."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The ban is about prose edits interleaving and holds there; nobody argued it for an append-only record.","Naming Delta in writing costs less than being asked about them unprepared."],"flip":"A demonstration that Delta's span anchors survive an out-of-band write our locator cannot follow.","linked":["E22","E37","F15"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:759","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:66","docs/CRITIQUE.md:523","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1730","research briefing 2026-09-09"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The sync position matters when sync ships, which is the paid tier.","recWhy":"A team with ten years of CRDT work reached the opposite conclusion in public on 1 September, staking span-level attribution on real-time merge of everyone's edits, and our position is one table row with one sentence of reasoning. The ban is about prose edits interleaving: CRDTs converge, and for prose they converge on byte-identical garbage, which is the measured finding the settled decision rests on, and it has not changed. Nobody argued that reasoning for an append-only review log, where convergence is cheap and harmless, so that question is separate and E37 owns it. Keep the ban where it applies, write the rebuttal naming Delta so it exists before the question is asked, and decide the review file on its own merits. Q11's no-live-editing position rests on this. If Delta shows span anchors surviving an out-of-band write our locator cannot follow, that is new evidence and this reopens.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK28"],"areas":["Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition recommended the same as option b here: keep the position and write the rebuttal naming Zed. It also adds Delta to the market table. It did not separate the review file from prose.","agree":true}},{"id":"E36","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"The review file","weight":"high","q":"Where does document identity live: in a key written into the file's own frontmatter, or in the review file, keyed by path?","lede":"A rename done in a terminal never fires our hook, and plain text cannot hold a stable item id.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"spec.md","lines":[{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"title: Payments spec","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"fm_id: 8f2c4a1e","mark":"accent","note":"option c: one key we write into the user's file"},{"t":"tags:","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"- spec","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"---","mark":"neutral"}],"note":"Renders as an ordinary property everywhere. It is still a byte we added to a document nobody asked us to change.","caption":"Option c in bytes, against a promise to add nothing to the file"},"stakes":"A rename performed outside the app orphans every review record for that document.","state":["The engine audit settles that anchors carry a `docId`, and that `docId` is not the path.","`mv`, a git checkout and an Obsidian move never fire a rename hook.","The plan keys the review file, a separate file kept next to the documents, by file path."],"tension":["The plan's own rule: add nothing to markdown that breaks it elsewhere.","A YAML key breaks nothing and is still a byte we wrote unasked."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No stored id; content-address the first-seen bytes","gains":["Adds nothing to the file, no migration"],"costs":["Dies the moment the document is edited outside frontmatter"],"system":"A hash index.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No stored id; content-address the first-seen bytes. Adds nothing to the file and needs no migration; dies the moment the document is edited anywhere outside its frontmatter."},{"k":"b","label":"Path first, content-address as a fallback, refuse on mismatch","gains":["Nothing written into the file, and the refusal is visible"],"costs":["Refuses on rename-plus-edit, the common agent case"],"system":"Two indexes and a refusal reason.","screens":"An unresolvable-document state.","money":"None.","what":"Path first, content-address as a fallback, refuse on mismatch. Nothing written into the file and the refusal is visible; refuses on rename-plus-edit, which is the common agent case."},{"k":"c","label":"Write one `fm_id` key into the document's frontmatter, once, on first review","gains":["Survives `mv`, a checkout and an Obsidian move","The review file stops depending on the path"],"costs":["We write a byte nobody asked for","Waits on the list-refusal and key-rule fixes to land"],"system":"A first-review write through the frontmatter writer.","screens":"The properties panel shows a key we own.","money":"None.","what":"Write one fm_id key into the document's frontmatter, once, on first review. Survives mv, a checkout and an Obsidian move; a byte written that nobody asked for."},{"k":"d","label":"Review file keyed by path, with a rename hook","gains":["Nothing written into the user's file; simplest to implement"],"costs":["The hook never fires for the renames that matter"],"system":"A path index plus a watcher.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Key the review file by path and add a rename hook. Nothing written into the user's file and simplest to build; the hook never fires for the renames that matter."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["It is the only option that survives all three outside operations the engine audit names.","Write it on first review, never on open, so a read-only pass modifies nothing."],"flip":"A corpus replay showing path plus content-address resolving above 99.5% across rename-and-edit pairs.","linked":["E22","E3","E37"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:1516","docs/ENGINE.md:1529","docs/ENGINE.md:1626","docs/ENGINE.md:1628","docs/ENGINE.md:1632","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:527","research briefing 2026-09-09"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Document identity is a review file question.","recWhy":"A rename done in a terminal, a git checkout, or an Obsidian move never fires our hook, and plain text cannot hold a stable item id, so a review file keyed by path loses every mark the moment a file moves. The engine audit settles that anchors carry a document id and that the id is not the path. Writing one fm_id key into the document's frontmatter is the only option that survives all three outside operations, and it costs a byte nobody asked for, which is the honest tension against the rule that we change only what you touched. Write it on first review, never on open, so a read-only pass modifies nothing, and it waits on the list-refusal and key-rule fixes so it can be written into every file. A corpus replay showing path plus content-address resolving above 99.5 percent across rename-and-edit pairs would make b enough."},{"id":"E37","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"The review file","weight":"high","q":"What wins when `review.jsonl` and the document disagree, and how does it merge across branches?","lede":"Two people review on two branches, and on a fresh clone git has no driver for our review file.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"Branch A appends a record","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Branch B appends a record","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"git merge","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"No .gitattributes","note":"conflict markers inside JSONL","tone":"stop","edge":"default"},{"label":"merge=union","note":"both lines kept, built in, no local config","tone":"good","edge":"shipped"},{"label":"Reader dedupes by span and hash","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Orphan record marked, never pruned","tone":"accent"}],"perRow":3,"note":"A custom merge driver needs config a clone does not carry, so it silently falls back to a conflict.","caption":"The two paths a merged review file can take"},"stakes":"The review file holds review state; a conflicted or pruned one loses the record of what was read.","state":["The review file is one JSON line per span, plain text, in the repo.","No `.gitattributes` is shipped and no merge behaviour is written down anywhere.","The projection law makes the document authoritative, so the review file can only be stale."],"tension":["Union merge keeps every line and makes duplicates the reader's problem.","Pruning records that match no span destroys the evidence something was reviewed and moved."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship `.gitattributes` with `merge=union`, dedupe on read, mark orphans","gains":["Works on a fresh clone with no local configuration","Keeps the evidence a span was reviewed then moved"],"costs":["The reader must dedupe, and the file needs compaction"],"system":"A shipped `.gitattributes`, dedupe on read, an orphan flag.","screens":"The review panel shows records whose span moved.","money":"None.","what":"Ship a .gitattributes with merge=union for the review file, dedupe on read, and mark orphaned records. Works on a fresh clone with nothing installed; the reader dedupes and the file needs compaction."},{"k":"b","label":"A custom merge driver registered on first run","gains":["Semantic merges, resolving duplicates at merge time"],"costs":["Needs local config a fresh clone does not carry"],"system":"A driver binary plus install steps.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A custom merge driver registered on first run. Semantic merges that resolve duplicates at merge time; needs local configuration a fresh clone does not carry."},{"k":"c","label":"One file per person, so two people never touch one file","what":"`.frontmatter/review.<person>.jsonl`.","gains":["Merges cannot conflict; trivial to reason about"],"costs":["The team view becomes a join across files"],"system":"Per-person file naming.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Leave it; resolve conflicts by hand","gains":["Nothing to build"],"costs":["A conflicted JSONL is the first thing a two-person pilot meets"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it and resolve conflicts by hand. Nothing to build, and a conflicted JSONL is the first thing a two-person pilot meets."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Union is the only merge that works on a fresh clone with nothing installed.","Retaining and marking orphans is forced: a moved span is the event we show."],"flip":"A pilot repo where two people review the same span at the same hash with different verdicts.","linked":["E22","E35","E36","FL18","L13"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/ENGINE.md:124","research briefing 2026-09-09"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The review file is deferred, so its merge behaviour is too.","recWhy":"Two people review on two branches and merge, and git has no driver for our review file, so the first thing a two-person pilot meets is a conflict in a JSONL nobody can read. Union merge keeps every line and works on a fresh clone with no local configuration, which is the only merge that does. A custom driver needs setup a clone does not carry, and one file per person turns the team view into a join. The projection law makes the document authoritative, so the review file can only ever be stale. A record that matches no span is not garbage to prune; it is the evidence that a span was reviewed and then moved, which is exactly the event the product shows. Dedupe on read, mark orphans, compact occasionally. If two people review the same span at the same hash with different verdicts, that conflict is real and the reader has to surface it.","dupes":{"dropped":["FL16"],"areas":["Flow & interaction"],"why":"Flow & interaction recommended one review file per person, option c here. This card recommends the opposite shape: one shared file, merged as a union and deduplicated on read. Flow & interaction's question also covered what a teammate sees.","agree":false}},{"id":"E23","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Testing","weight":"medium","q":"Do we adopt fast-check and all twelve specified property tests, only four of them, or widen the corpus instead?","lede":"Three of the four defects this engine has shipped live in corpus rows with a count of zero.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Corpus, a byte pin","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Properties","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Files with a BOM","cells":["0","generated"]},{"label":"Files with CRLF","cells":["0","generated"]},{"label":"Files with a bare CR","cells":["0","generated"]},{"label":"Catches the BOM class","cells":["no","only the locality property"]},{"label":"Catches the offset-map defect","cells":["no","only a boundary-biased generator"]},{"label":"In the `verify` chain today","cells":["no","n/a"]}],"note":"The corpus is a sample of what people wrote; a property is a statement about what the code must do.","caption":"Why the standing gate cannot see three of four shipped defects"},"stakes":"Two open byte-destructive defects have no instrument that can see them.","state":["No property tests exist; `npm run corpus` hashes files and is not in `verify`.","The engine audit specifies twelve properties across four files on fast-check 4.9.0.","The corpus has zero BOM, CRLF, bare-CR and unterminated-frontmatter files."],"tension":["Twelve properties plus four generator files is real time at 1.21 engineering days a week.","Four catch every shipped defect; eight guard surfaces that do not exist yet."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Which property catches which shipped defect (docs/ENGINE.md:1854-1859)","cols":["Defect","Round-trip","Locality","Idempotence","Offset map"],"rows":[["BOM (fixed, f47555f)","passes on the bug","catches it","passes on the bug","n/a"],["OffsetMap off-by-three (fixed, cc1d451)","n/a","n/a","n/a","catches it: 600 wrong answers"],["Zero-indent sequence (open)","passes","passes","passes","n/a"],["Bare CR (open, live today)","n/a","catches it","n/a","n/a"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Generator shape decides whether it fires at all","items":[["Uniform generator vs the unfixed OffsetMap","0 failures in 4,966 offsets","docs/ENGINE.md:1863"],["Boundary-straddling generator, same code","600 failures in 9,363","docs/ENGINE.md:1863"],["Corpus-plus-properties, run together","surfaced 21 round-trip failures neither instrument reports alone","docs/ENGINE.md:1760"],["Proposed CI budget for all twelve","7.0 s, fail the build above 12 s","docs/ENGINE.md:1913, docs/ENGINE.md:1915"],["Zero-indent sequence caught by any property","no, and correctly so; properties encode the contract, and the contract is what is wrong","docs/ENGINE.md:1865"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Neither","what":"Today's state.","gains":["No authoring cost"],"costs":["Two open byte-destructive defects stay invisible to the standing gate"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Widen the corpus instead: add BOM, CRLF and bare-CR files","gains":["Cheaper and familiar; keeps one instrument"],"costs":["Only finds what someone thought to add, and states no contract"],"system":"Corpus additions and new hashes.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Widen the corpus instead: add BOM, CRLF and bare-CR files. Cheaper and familiar, one instrument; it only finds what someone thought to add and states no contract."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt four: round-trip, locality, refusal safety, offset map","gains":["Covers every shipped defect class at a third of the authoring","Corpus goes into `verify` in the same commit"],"costs":["Construct and fold properties wait; two suites to grow"],"system":"New dev dependency and two test files.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt four properties: round-trip, locality, refusal safety, offset map. Covers every shipped defect class at a third of the authoring; the corpus check joins verify in the same commit."},{"k":"d","label":"Adopt fast-check and all twelve properties as specified","gains":["Covers surfaces not yet built","One authoring pass instead of two"],"costs":["Four generator files is the real cost, not the 7 seconds","Eight of them guard code that does not exist"],"system":"New dev dependency and four test files.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt fast-check and all twelve properties as specified. Covers surfaces not yet built in one pass; four generator files is the real cost, and eight guard code that does not exist."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The four that would have caught every shipped defect are worth authoring; the other eight guard nothing.","Store regressions as literal counterexamples: a seed stops testing anything once the generator changes."],"flip":"Body writing and review anchors landing, which makes the construct and fold properties concrete.","linked":["E12","E15","E18"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:1730","docs/ENGINE.md:1746","docs/ENGINE.md:1760","docs/ENGINE.md:1854","docs/ENGINE.md:1863","docs/ENGINE.md:1865","docs/ENGINE.md:1889","docs/ENGINE.md:1913","docs/ENGINE.md:1915","package.json:19"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Property tests protect engine behaviour that is deferred.","recWhy":"Three of the four defects this engine has shipped live in corpus rows with a count of zero. The corpus has no BOM, no CRLF, no bare-CR and no unterminated-frontmatter files, so the standing gate could never have caught them. The audit specifies twelve properties across four generator files. Four of them, round-trip, locality, refusal safety and the offset map, would have caught every defect that actually shipped; the other eight guard body writing and review anchors, which do not exist yet. Author the four, put the corpus check into verify in the same commit, and store every regression as a literal counterexample rather than a seed, because a seed stops testing anything the moment the generator changes. When body writing and anchors land, the construct and fold properties become concrete and the other eight are worth writing then."},{"id":"E24","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Distribution","weight":"medium","q":"If we publish the engine, what shape and what licence, and does the name survive?","lede":"The package name is free, the product name is taken in two registries, and the repo has no licence file.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"mdmax-cli","items":["bin","bench","ruby subprocess","MCP server"],"side":"node only","tone":"warn"},{"label":"mdmax/cert subpath","items":["constructs","fold","verdict"],"side":"pulls entities","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"mdmax core","items":["splice","anchors","admission"],"side":"browser-safe, no node built-ins","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Licence","items":["Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE"],"side":"withholds trademark rights","tone":"good"}],"note":"A splice-only consumer must never pull `entities` or `node:child_process` into its graph.","caption":"Two packages from one repo, and what each consumer inherits"},"stakes":"Publishing the wrong shape freezes an API and a name we would then have to break.","state":["`mdmax` returns 404 on npm and `frontmatter` returns 200.","There is no LICENSE file in the repo.","The VS Code incumbent has 82,265 installs and holds the `@frontmatter` scope."],"tension":["A single package pulls `entities` and a subprocess into every writer-only consumer's graph.","Three packages fragment the version vector for no gain.","Apache-2.0 section 6 withholds trademark rights; MIT leaves that to trademark law."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Registry and licence state","items":[["`mdmax` on npm","HTTP 404: unregistered","docs/ENGINE.md:2128, fetched 2026-08-30"],["`@sgnk/mdmax`","HTTP 404","docs/ENGINE.md:2129"],["`frontmatter` on npm","HTTP 200: taken","docs/ENGINE.md:2130"],["`@frontmatter` npm scope","held by the VS Code incumbent","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md"],["VS Code incumbent","82,265 installs, alive at v10.12.0 (2026-08-21)","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md"],["LICENSE file in repo","absent","verified: no LICENSE* exists"],["Engine export declarations","88","docs/ENGINE.md:2121"],["External consumers","1 symbol, at 2 sites","verified by grep"],["Runtime deps of the core","github-slugger (ISC), entities (BSD-2-Clause, undeclared, see the dependency-declaration decision)","docs/ENGINE.md:2125"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Two packages, Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE, ship under `mdmax`","what":"A browser-safe core with a `mdmax/cert` subpath, and `mdmax-cli` for bin, bench and ruby.","gains":["A writer-only consumer pulls neither `entities` nor a subprocess","`mdmax` is free in the registry we would ship to"],"costs":["Two release trains, and a public API surface committed"],"system":"Barrel, subpath exports, two package manifests.","screens":"None.","money":"Zero registry cost; the name stays unsolved."},{"k":"b","label":"Do not publish; keep the engine inside the product","gains":["No public commitment to any API"],"costs":["Removes the one route where our reach is someone else's distribution"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do not publish; keep the engine inside the product. No public commitment to any API, and the one route where our reach becomes someone else's distribution is closed."},{"k":"c","label":"One package, MIT","gains":["Simplest to publish and to adopt"],"costs":["Pulls the ruby subprocess into every consumer"],"system":"One manifest.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"One package under MIT. Simplest to publish and adopt; pulls the Ruby subprocess and the entities package into every consumer's dependency graph."},{"k":"d","label":"Publish the CLI only, not the library","gains":["No API commitment, smaller surface to support"],"costs":["Needs the CLI to run first, and it does not"],"system":"One bin package.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish the CLI only, not the library. No API commitment and a smaller support surface; needs the CLI to run first, and it does not."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The certificate is a published claim about seven third-party engines, and a fork must not issue it.","Add the LICENSE file whatever else is decided; there is none today."],"flip":"Deciding never to publish, which leaves the licence file worth adding and everything else moot.","linked":["E14","E25","FF12","L20"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:2128","docs/ENGINE.md:2130","docs/ENGINE.md:2131","docs/ENGINE.md:2231","docs/ENGINE.md:2237","docs/ENGINE.md:2243","docs/ENGINE.md:2251","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Publishing the engine is after the pilot.","recWhy":"mdmax is free on npm and frontmatter is taken there and in the VS Code store, where the incumbent holds the @frontmatter scope with 82,265 installs. There is no LICENSE file in the repository at all, which means the default is all rights reserved and nobody may lawfully run the engine, and that has to be fixed whatever else is decided. Two packages, a browser-safe core with a cert subpath and a separate CLI for bin, bench and Ruby, means a writer-only consumer pulls neither entities nor a subprocess. Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE rather than MIT because the certificate is a published claim about seven third-party engines and Apache's section 6 withholds trademark rights, so a fork cannot issue certificates under the mark. This waits on Q16 for the product name and on FF12's timing, after the pilot. If publishing is never wanted, the licence file is still worth adding today."},{"id":"E25","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Boundary","weight":"medium","q":"Do we cut the engine to a 31-symbol barrel, move the frontmatter writer inside it, and enforce the boundary mechanically?","lede":"There is no API, there is a folder other folders reach into, and the engine's central function lives outside it.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Today","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Barrel plus an engine element type","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Public symbols","cells":["88 exports, no index.ts","31 in six capability groups"]},{"label":"Where the splice writer lives","cells":["`share/domain`","`mdmax/domain`"]},{"label":"Copies of the key rule","cells":["two: engine and panel","one"]},{"label":"Would a gate catch mdmax importing vault?","cells":["no, all three permit it","yes"]},{"label":"Transitive `node:` leak into the browser","cells":["undetected","caught by the purity harness"]}],"note":"`export *` would freeze `unsafeU16` as public API forever, so the barrel has to be enumerated.","caption":"What the boundary costs to state, and what stating it buys"},"stakes":"A purity violation discovered after publication is a breaking change rather than a refactor.","state":["88 export declarations across 13 files, no index.ts, one outside module deep-importing it.","The frontmatter writer lives in `share/domain`, and the properties panel imports it across a boundary.","All three architecture gates allow the engine to import the vault module."],"tension":["`infrastructure/bench.ts` imports `node:child_process`, so one barrel export reaches the client bundle.","A per-file lint rule cannot see a transitive node built-in three files down."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The surface, measured","items":[["Engine files / lines","13 / 3,614","docs/ENGINE.md:2120"],["`export` declarations","88","docs/ENGINE.md:2121"],["Symbols consumed from outside","1: `decodeStrict`","verified by grep"],["Imports mdmax makes from other modules","0: the rule holds and nothing enforces it (this decision's own gap)","docs/ENGINE.md:546"],["Real `throw` sites","2","docs/ENGINE.md:2124"],["Proposed barrel","31 symbols in six capability groups","docs/ENGINE.md:2137"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Three structural moves that come with the barrel","cols":["Move","Reason","Risk if skipped"],"rows":[["Move splice-frontmatter.ts into mdmax/domain/","it is the engine's central contract living outside the module","nothing enforces its purity; the UI grew a second SAFE_KEY. Move it, do not copy it: two writers is how the shipped write paths diverged in the first place"],["bench.ts behind a node-only subpath","it imports node:child_process, node:fs, node:path","one barrel export pulls node built-ins into the browser bundle"],["Collapse slugStamp/foldStamp/normalizeStamp into one stamp()","a consumer recording three of four stamps has an unreproducible artifact","certificates that cannot be replayed"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Would each gate catch mdmax importing from vault? (docs/ENGINE.md:548-552)","cols":["Gate","What it checks","Catches it"],"rows":[["eslint-plugin-boundaries","maps every module domain to the single type `domain`; allows domain to domain","no"],["`npm run arch`","layer direction only; layerOf() discards the module name","no"],["import-boundary-report.mjs","substring ban on @/server, @/lib, @/components","no"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Cost, and the traps to avoid","items":[["Fix","one boundaries element type, declared before the generic per-module patterns so it wins","docs/ENGINE.md:554"],["Anti-recommendation","do not move mdmax to src/shared: shared-domain is importable by everything and would still permit the reverse import","docs/ENGINE.md:554"],["What a per-file lint rule cannot see","a transitive `node:fs` three files down through a type-only-looking import: exactly how bench.ts would leak into a browser bundle","docs/ENGINE.md:2170"],["Why the new harness needs a denominator guard","clean-architecture-report.mjs once reported 0 violations and exit 0 whether it had scanned 208 files or zero; today it carries MIN_SCANNED_FILES = 50, so `arch` itself can no longer go blind","docs/ENGINE.md:2168, docs/ENGINE.md:554"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave it; deep imports are fine for one consumer","gains":["No work"],"costs":["True until the second consumer, and packaging assumes several"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it; deep imports are fine for one consumer. No work, and true only until the second consumer, which packaging assumes."},{"k":"b","label":"Barrel and the eslint rule only","gains":["One line of config; catches the obvious case"],"costs":["Misses the transitive case, which is how bench.ts reaches a browser bundle"],"system":"index.ts plus a lint rule.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"An enumerated barrel and the eslint rule only. One line of config that catches the obvious case; misses the transitive one, which is how bench.ts reaches a browser bundle."},{"k":"c","label":"`export *` from a barrel","gains":["Immediate"],"costs":["Freezes all 88 symbols including `unsafeU16`, permanently"],"system":"One line.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"export star from a barrel. Immediate, and it freezes all 88 symbols including the unsafe ones as public API, permanently."},{"k":"d","label":"Barrel, the three moves, and the transitive purity harness in `verify`","what":"An enumerated index.ts, the writer moved inside, an `engine` element type, a harness with a denominator guard.","gains":["One public surface instead of a folder","Catches the transitive leak a lint rule cannot see"],"costs":["One more harness, which must not pass by scanning nothing"],"system":"index.ts, one file move, one element type, one harness.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["An enumerated barrel exposes 31 chosen symbols instead of all 88.","The denominator guard matters: a gate here reported a clean pass scanning zero files."],"flip":"Deciding the engine is never lifted out of this repo, which makes the barrel and lint rule enough.","linked":["E14","E2","E24"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:546","docs/ENGINE.md:548","docs/ENGINE.md:554","docs/ENGINE.md:575","docs/ENGINE.md:2120","docs/ENGINE.md:2121","docs/ENGINE.md:2133","docs/ENGINE.md:2137","docs/ENGINE.md:2149","docs/ENGINE.md:2159","docs/ENGINE.md:2168","docs/ENGINE.md:2170","merged: E28"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The barrel is engine housekeeping.","recWhy":"There is no engine API, there is a folder other folders reach into. 88 export declarations across 13 files, no index, the frontmatter writer living in share/domain and imported across a boundary by the properties panel, and all three architecture gates letting the engine import the vault module. Worse, bench.ts imports node:child_process, so one export can reach the client bundle three files down, where a per-file lint rule cannot see it. An enumerated barrel of 31 chosen symbols, the three moves that put the writer inside the engine, and a transitive purity harness in verify fix all of it. The harness needs a denominator guard, because a gate in this repository once reported a clean pass having scanned zero files. If the engine is never lifted out of this repository, the barrel and the lint rule are enough on their own."},{"id":"E14","cat":"Engine & technical","sub":"Enforcement","weight":"medium","q":"Do we replace the size-budget script, which today only runs `echo`, with a real bundler budget, or delete it?","lede":"A product that wants to sell document gates in CI ships a size gate that is a print statement.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"package.json today","lines":[{"t":"\"budget\": \"echo 'No bundle budget…'\"","mark":"stop"},{"t":"not called by `verify`","mark":"stop"}]},"after":{"title":"After a real bundler run","lines":[{"t":"mdmax core: measured gzip size","mark":"good"},{"t":"ceiling set after two weeks of readings","mark":"accent"}]},"via":"one bundler","note":"The proposed ceilings rest on a 15 to 18 KB estimate the engine doc says must be replaced by a real number first.","caption":"Two placeholders: a script that prints, a ceiling that was guessed"},"stakes":"The packaging plan says the engine core drops into another host, and nothing has measured its size.","state":["The `budget` npm script is an `echo`, and `verify` does not call it.","The engine audit proposes 20 KB gzipped for the core, 45 KB with the certificate.","Those ceilings rest on a count of 57,318 stripped source bytes, not a real bundle."],"tension":["A gate failing on day one for unactionable reasons trains people to ignore it.","A gate that always passes is equally useless.","The packaging decision needs the size number either way."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What is actually known about size","items":[["Engine source, all files","3,614 lines / 153,178 bytes","docs/ENGINE.md:2120"],["Comment and whitespace share","51.9%","docs/ENGINE.md:2126"],["Stripped code, browser-eligible files only","57,318 bytes","docs/ENGINE.md:2127"],["Estimated gzip after minification","15 to 18 KB","docs/ENGINE.md:2243: marked [derived, estimate]"],["Proposed ceiling for the core","20 KB gzip, 1 runtime dep","docs/ENGINE.md:2243"],["Proposed ceiling for `mdmax/cert`","45 KB gzip, 2 runtime deps","docs/ENGINE.md:2244"],["Actual bundler measurement","none: no bundler is configured for the engine","package.json:20"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Real bundler, report the number, set the ceiling after two weeks of readings","gains":["Same work, no false red","Gives the packaging decision a real number"],"costs":["No enforcement for two weeks"],"system":"Bundler reports into CI output.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Real bundler, with the proposed ceilings as the gate from day one","gains":["A working gate this week"],"costs":["The ceilings rest on stripped source bytes; the first run may be red"],"system":"Bundler in the verify chain.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Leave it","gains":["Nothing to do"],"costs":["Keeps a script whose only effect is making `verify` look longer"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Delete the script","gains":["One fewer thing pretending to be a check"],"costs":["Gives up the number the distribution story depends on"],"system":"Removes a script entry.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["We need the size number either way, and a ceiling set before real readings would be a guess.","Land it with the CI file, not as separate work."],"flip":"Deciding never to publish the engine as a package, which makes deleting the script the honest answer.","linked":["E12","E24","E25"],"sources":["package.json:19","package.json:20","docs/ENGINE.md:2243","docs/ENGINE.md:2244","docs/ENGINE.md:2127","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:218"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"Replacing a check that only prints a line is a chore with a known fix."},{"id":"AC1","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Web byte path","weight":"critical","q":"On the web, does the browser read the repository, or does our server hand the bytes on?","lede":"The plan says no document byte touches our servers. As of now, our server parses the whole repository.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"Browser, per file","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Browser, tarball","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Server tarball (today)","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Whole-vault read inside 5,000 req/hr","cells":["No: 5,833 calls","Yes, one call","Yes, one call"]},{"label":"Who touches the bytes","cells":["Browser only","Browser only","Our server, then the browser"]},{"label":"Claim survives","cells":["Until a feature reads everything","Yes","No"]},{"label":"Untested assumption","cells":["None","Zipball redirect under CORS","None: it already ships"]},{"label":"Ceiling","cells":["Per-file latency","88.1 MB unzipped in a tab","16.4x the Vercel body cap"]}],"note":"The repo docs scan reads every markdown file by definition, so the first column breaks on a pilot feature.","caption":"Three read shapes against GitHub's rate limit and the byte claim."},"stakes":"Without it, the web app is one more hosted editor competing with free ones.","state":["The 8 September decisions draft makes the same promise.","The code reads with our own GitHub token, downloads the zipball and parses it on our server."],"tension":["At 5,000 requests an hour, a 5,832-file vault cannot be read file by file.","No test shows a browser can follow GitHub's zipball redirect."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The numbers that constrain the byte path","items":[["GitHub installation rate limit, floor","5,000 req/hr","r20-q4:38, fetched 2026-08-31; hard cap 12,500"],["Whole-vault per-file read of the founder's md vault","5,833 calls","5,832 .md + 1 tree call = 1.17× the floor; r20-q4:40"],["Markdown bytes in that vault","88.1 MB","88,137,142 bytes, measured by find/cat 2026-08-31, r20-q4:40"],["The same vault measured two days earlier","77.5 MB across 4,548 .md","r11 c2:19, measured 2026-08-29: same vault, different date, not a contradiction"],["Installation token lifetime","1 hour","r20-q4:33; expired token returns 401, so mint per request"],["Whether a browser can follow api.github.com's zipball redirect to codeload","never tested in this corpus","the WEEK0 CORS note covers the REST API generally, not the zipball redirect"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Four read shapes, and what each costs","cols":["Read shape","Fits the rate limit?","Who touches the bytes","Preserves the claim?"],"rows":[["Browser, per file, lazy","For opening files as the user clicks them, yes. For any whole-vault pass (search index, the repo docs scan) no: 5,833 calls is 1.17× the floor","Browser only","Yes, until a feature needs the whole vault"],["Browser, tarball fetched and unzipped in the page","Yes, 1 call","Browser only","Yes; ceiling is browser memory at 88.1 MB, and the redirect is untested under CORS"],["Browser, raw.githubusercontent.com","Yes: unauthenticated, off the REST bucket, sends access-control-allow-origin: *","Browser only","Yes, but public repositories only (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["Server, tarball plus a server-rendered snapshot (today's code)","Yes, 1 call","Our server, then the browser","No, this is the shipped path"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Browser reads each file as you open it, with a published vault ceiling","gains":["No byte reaches us while you open files"],"costs":["5,833 calls, over the 5,000 an hour limit"],"system":"A per-file reader in the browser.","screens":"A vault-size limit on the repo picker.","money":"None.","what":"The browser fetches each file from GitHub as you open it, with a supported vault size printed on the page. Nothing reaches us, and a 5,832-file vault needs 5,833 calls against a 5,000 an hour cap."},{"k":"b","label":"Browser fetches the tarball and unzips it in the page","gains":["One call, inside the rate limit","Bytes never leave the browser"],"costs":["88.1 MB of markdown in a tab","The zipball redirect is untested from a browser"],"system":"Unzip in a worker; drop the server snapshot.","screens":"A loading state on first connect.","money":"Function time drops.","what":"One tarball request, unzipped inside the page, so a whole vault is one call and no byte reaches us. The cost is 88.1 MB of markdown held in a tab and a redirect nobody has tested from a browser."},{"k":"c","label":"Our server reads the tarball, as today, and we drop the claim for the web","gains":["Already built, no days"],"costs":["Spends the differentiator a rival cannot copy"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Function time scales with vault size.","what":"Our server keeps downloading and parsing the tarball as it does today, and the web page stops claiming zero bytes. No build time, and the one claim a rival cannot copy is spent for it."},{"k":"d","label":"No repo lane on the web at all","gains":["True by construction"],"costs":["The web app stops being a finishable trial"],"system":"Delete the GitHub client from the web.","screens":"Drag-and-drop only.","money":"None.","what":"The web app opens pasted and dragged-in files only; a repository needs the desktop build. True by construction, and it removes the finishable trial from the surface strangers arrive on."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["One tarball call is the only whole-vault read within the limit that spares our server.","The pilot's repo docs scan reads every file, so per-file reading fails. Test the redirect first."],"flip":"A test shows a browser cannot follow the zipball redirect, or 88 MB crashes a tab.","linked":["AC14","AC19","AC3","F25"],"sources":["docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:39","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:54","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:221","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:368","src/shared/infrastructure/github/client.ts:30","src/shared/infrastructure/github/client.ts:78","src/app/api/vault/snapshot/route.ts","src/modules/vault/infrastructure/snapshot-cache.ts","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:9","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:38","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:40","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/c2-architecture-decisions.md:19"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Where the bytes travel is the storage design the plan reverses. It decides privacy and the free tier.","dependsOn":["L1"],"recWhy":"The plan promises that no document byte touches our servers, and today the server reads the whole repository with our own token: it downloads the zipball, unzips it and parses every file on our side. Reading file by file from the browser cannot replace that, because a 5,832-file vault is 5,833 calls against GitHub's 5,000 an hour limit, and the repo scan the pilot depends on reads every file. One tarball request is the only whole-vault read that sits inside the limit and spares our server, so the browser fetches the tarball and unzips it in the page. Two things are unmeasured and both are cheap to test before a line is written: whether a browser can follow GitHub's zipball redirect, and whether 88.1 MB of markdown in a tab is survivable. Q9 carries this as the first item of the privacy block. Either test failing sends this to per-file reading with a published ceiling.","dupes":{"dropped":["L2"],"areas":["Legal, privacy & data"],"why":"Legal, privacy & data asked whether to rewrite the web app to a browser-only path before the first stranger or withdraw the zero-bytes claim, and recommended the rewrite. Option b here is that rewrite (option a would be too); option c is withdrawing the claim.","agree":true}},{"id":"AC3","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"GitHub access","weight":"critical","q":"Does the GitHub App launch with three permissions, or four with `pull_requests: write`?","lede":"As of now, every repo read uses our own token. Whatever replaces it cannot quietly widen its permissions later.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"A repo admin clicks Install","note":"no org owner, no ticket","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Consent screen, three lines","note":"contents, metadata, emails","tone":"accent","edge":"then"},{"label":"Repo picker, one repo","note":"about forty seconds","tone":"neutral","edge":"then"},{"label":"Token minted per request","note":"one hour, never persisted","tone":"neutral","edge":"then"},{"label":"Later: add pull_requests","note":"every installation owner re-approves","tone":"warn","edge":"if"},{"label":"Unapproved installs keep the old set","note":"so the launch list is frozen in practice","tone":"stop","edge":"and"}],"note":"Any organisation permission replaces the first node with an org owner and a ticket.","caption":"Why the launch permission list cannot be widened quietly."},"stakes":"One permission too many needs an organisation owner; too few means everyone consents again.","state":["Every repository call carries one operator token, a shared token that belongs to us.","Sign-in is limited to a single GitHub login, `sagnikmitra` by default.","No installation code exists yet, so a pilot with strangers cannot run."],"tension":["Changing permissions re-prompts every owner, and unapproved installs keep the old set.","With no organisation permissions, a repo admin can install it alone."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The permission ladder, from GitHub's own docs (fetched 2026-08-31, a research report)","cols":["Permission","Buys","Cost of asking"],"rows":[["contents: write","Read bytes, splice writes, blobs/trees/commits/refs, create a branch","Required for every save"],["metadata: read","Repo listing; force-granted with any other permission","None"],["emails: read","Billing contact","One line on the consent screen"],["pull_requests: write","We open the PR ourselves","A fourth line, and a re-consent campaign if added later"],["Any organization permission","Org-wide install, seat provisioning","Every install routes through an org owner and a ticket, instead of a repo admin acting alone in forty seconds"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What is in the code today","items":[["Repository tokens in the product","1, operator-held","GITHUB_REPO_TOKEN, env.ts:85 and client.ts:30"],["Logins permitted to sign in","1","ALLOWED_GH_LOGIN default \"sagnikmitra\", env.ts:47, enforced at auth-options.ts:82"],["Installation-flow code in src/","0 occurrences","grep for \"installation\" returns nothing"],["Installation token TTL, if built","1 hour","mint per request, never persist (r20-q4:33)"],["Token format change already rolling out","stateless ghs_APPID_JWT since 2026-04-27","anything assuming a fixed ghs_ length breaks (r20-q4:36)"],["Permission changes after launch","every installation owner re-approves; unapproved installs keep the old set","r20-q4:37"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Three permissions, and no organisation permission, ever","gains":["A three-line consent screen, repo picker","Review before merge costs no extra permission"],"costs":["No PR with a title and body","Adding it later means everyone consents again"],"system":"Push a branch, not a PR.","screens":"A compare link after a save.","money":"None.","what":"Contents read and write, metadata, and pull requests read, with no organisation permission anywhere. A three-line consent screen with a repo picker, and a repo admin can install it alone."},{"k":"b","label":"The same three, plus pull_requests:write taken at launch","gains":["The flow a team reviewer wants"],"costs":["A fourth consent line when trust is lowest"],"system":"A PR builder from saved edits.","screens":"A review-and-open-PR step.","money":"None.","what":"The same three plus pull_requests: write from day one, so the app can open a pull request with a title and body. A fourth consent line at the moment trust is lowest."},{"k":"c","label":"OAuth with the repo scope","gains":["Fewest steps to a first file"],"costs":["All or nothing across every repository reachable"],"system":"One long-lived token, no installation.","screens":"A sign-in screen, no install.","money":"None.","what":"Plain OAuth with the repo scope. The fewest steps to a first file, and it is all or nothing across every repository the account can reach, which is the consent a stranger refuses."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the operator token through the pilot and build the App afterwards","gains":["Zero build cost; the pilot starts earlier"],"costs":["The pilot measures nothing about real repositories"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep our operator token through the pilot and build the App later. Zero build cost and an earlier start, and a pilot that never touches a stranger's repository measures nothing about real ones."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["No organisation permission matters for distribution as much as for security.","A pushed branch with a compare link still gets review before merge. Delete `GITHUB_REPO_TOKEN` either way."],"flip":"The buyer is the team, not the solo developer, so take `pull_requests: write` at launch.","linked":["AC1","AC19"],"sources":["src/config/env.ts:47","src/config/env.ts:85","src/modules/auth/infrastructure/auth-options.ts:82","src/shared/infrastructure/github/client.ts:30","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:22","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:27","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:37","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:151","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q7-one-page.md:96","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q7-one-page.md:46"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Sign in and per user storage need the app permissions settled first.","dependsOn":["AC1"],"recWhy":"Every repository call today carries one operator token of ours, and sign-in accepts a single GitHub login, so a pilot with strangers cannot run until an App exists. The permission set cannot widen quietly later: adding one re-prompts every owner, and installations that do not approve keep the old set. Three permissions and no organisation permission, because that absence is what lets a repository admin install alone, which is distribution as much as security. A pushed branch with a compare link still gets review before merge, so the fourth permission buys a title and a body, not the review. Q2 settles the buyer as the team, which is what this card's flip names, and AC22 rules repository writes out for its own reason. The value of repository access sits on the reading side and the duty on the writing side. The flip has fired and three holds anyway. Delete GITHUB_REPO_TOKEN either way."},{"id":"AC14","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Web byte path","weight":"critical","q":"Do we delete the server-side PDF route or amend the claim, and does folder-to-one-PDF get built?","lede":"Two of the five exports send the whole file to a server, not one, and the browser print path is written but wired to nothing.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Export menu, as shipped","panes":[{"label":"Export","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"Download .md","meta":"Blob in the browser","mark":"local","tone":"good"},{"label":"Export HTML","meta":"Blob in the browser","mark":"local","tone":"good"},{"label":"Export Word (.doc)","meta":"HTML with a .doc name, in the browser","mark":"local","tone":"good"},{"label":"Open PDF (print)","meta":"Headless Chromium on our server","mark":"server","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Download PDF","meta":"Headless Chromium on our server","mark":"server","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"Two of five items send the whole file to us. The browser print path is written at `export-doc.ts:130` and nothing calls it.","caption":"Where each export runs, read from the shipped menu on 2026-09-13."},"stakes":"A security reviewer finds a route contradicting the published architecture, and that reads worse than the feature.","state":["Both PDF items call the server route. `ExportMenu.tsx:108` opens it in a tab, `:114` downloads it.","`openPrintView` exists at `export-doc.ts:130`, is exported, and no file imports it.","Export Word is the HTML renderer with a `.doc` name, not a real docx (`ExportMenu.tsx:95`)."],"tension":["Every statement of the claim gains an exception, and competitors will quote it back.","Folder-to-one-PDF is the only export with demonstrated demand."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The five export menu items, and where each runs","cols":["Menu item","Where it runs","Document bytes reach our server?"],"rows":[["Download .md","Browser, Blob + object URL","No"],["Export HTML","Browser, Blob","No"],["Export Word (.doc), Word opens HTML","Browser, Blob","No"],["Open PDF (print)","Browser, Blob + window.print()","No"],["Download PDF","Server route, headless Chromium on Linux","Yes, the whole file"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the route actually does","items":[["File fetched","server-side","container.getFile(vaultPath), route.ts:60"],["Renderer","@sparticuz/chromium + puppeteer-core","dynamically imported inside the handler"],["External calls at render time","mermaid via CDN, KaTeX via CDN","route.ts header, lines 4 to 13"],["Platform gate","process.platform === \"linux\"","route.ts:108: cannot run in the desktop app"],["Browser PDF path already shipped","\"Open PDF (print)\" is a live menu item","ExportMenu.tsx:159 → export-doc.ts:130"],["Plan's claim","\"zero document bytes\" in the control plane","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:123 and :133"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete the server route and wire the browser print path that already exists","gains":["The claim becomes true with no exception","No CDN fetches while rendering a document"],"costs":["Browser print output varies by engine, so page breaks are not ours to control","Folder-to-one-PDF still needs a server renderer if it ships"],"system":"Delete the server PDF route.","screens":"The export menu goes five to four.","money":"Removes a Chromium cold start.","what":"`openPrintView` is written and unused. Wiring it is small, and it makes the zero-bytes claim true with no exception to explain."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep it and amend the claim to name the exception","what":"The claim becomes zero document bytes except a PDF you export.","gains":["Keeps the better PDF output"],"costs":["Any competitor can quote the exception"],"system":"No code change, one architecture edit.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep it for the web and build a native PDF path on desktop","gains":["Best output on both surfaces"],"costs":["Two renderers to keep byte-identical"],"system":"A second renderer inside Tauri.","screens":"None visible.","money":"The web route still bills.","what":"The server route stays for the web and a native PDF path is built on desktop. The best output on both surfaces, and two renderers that have to stay byte-identical forever."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep it and say nothing","gains":["No work; the better output stays"],"costs":["The first security review finds it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the route and say nothing. No work and the better output stays; the first security review finds the exception and the claim reads as a lie."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One export format is not worth an exception on the whole claim.","The replacement already ships above it, and the route can never run on desktop."],"flip":"Folder-to-one-PDF ships, so a server renderer is unavoidable and the exception is added first.","linked":["AC1"],"sources":["src/app/api/export/pdf/[...path]/route.ts:60","src/app/api/export/pdf/[...path]/route.ts:108","src/modules/export/presentation/export-doc.ts:115","src/modules/export/presentation/export-doc.ts:130","src/modules/export/presentation/ExportMenu.tsx:159","src/modules/export/presentation/ExportMenu.tsx:160","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:123","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","src/modules/export/presentation/ExportMenu.tsx:108","src/modules/export/presentation/ExportMenu.tsx:114","src/modules/export/presentation/ExportMenu.tsx:95"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan now allows holding bytes, so the export route can stay. Confirm it.","dependsOn":["L1"],"recWhy":"Two of the five exports send the whole document to a server today: both PDF items call the server route, one opens it in a tab at ExportMenu.tsx:108 and one downloads it at :114. The browser print path, openPrintView at export-doc.ts:130, is written, exported and imported by nothing. Export Word is the HTML renderer with a .doc name. One export format is not worth an exception on the whole claim, because every statement of it would gain a clause that competitors quote back. Delete the route and wire the print path that already exists; browser print output varies by engine, so page breaks stop being ours to control, and that is the honest trade. The route can never run on desktop anyway. Q9 carries this in the privacy block. Folder-to-one-PDF is the only export with demonstrated demand, and if it ships it needs a server renderer, so the exception is written before that feature, not after.","dupes":{"dropped":["F16"],"areas":["Features"],"why":"Both decide the server PDF route. F16's 'cut the server PDF route, write a print path' is AC14's option a. The folder-to-one-PDF clause came in with F16 and is named in the kept question.","agree":true}},{"id":"AC20","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"THE FRONT DOOR","weight":"critical","when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A stranger hits a login wall before they see an editor. It is the first screen and it blocks the funnel.","q":"Does the editor open and work without an account, or does the login wall at the front door stay?","lede":"`/` is the login screen when nobody is signed in, so nobody can try the product before joining it.","stakes":"The kit funnel needs strangers to arrive, paste something and see it work. Today they cannot open a file.","state":["`(vault)/layout.tsx` returns `<LoginScreen />` when there is no actor, so the home page is the wall.","Measured on 2026-09-13: dillinger.io serves a Monaco editor on its landing page with zero sign-in words in the HTML.","StackEdit and Excalidraw also show no sign-in words on the landing page. HackMD shows sixteen and Typst eight."],"tension":["Every tool a developer reaches for to do one quick markdown job asks nothing of them first.","An account is needed the moment a document is stored or shared, and not before."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Open editor at `/`, local only, account asked for at the first save or share","gains":["A stranger can paste and export in the first ten seconds","The funnel gets a top, which it does not have today"],"costs":["Two states to build and test, signed in and not","Local drafts need a migration into the account on first sign-in"],"system":"The vault layout stops short-circuiting to the login screen; drafts go to IndexedDB.","screens":"A new signed-out editor state, and a prompt at the first save.","money":"None. Anonymous use costs no storage.","what":"The editor opens at / with local drafts and asks for an account at the first save or share. A stranger can paste and export in ten seconds; two states to build, and local drafts migrate on sign-in."},{"k":"b","label":"A separate `/try` page with the editor, and the wall stays at `/`","gains":["No change to the signed-in app","One page to measure the funnel against"],"costs":["Two editors to keep in step, which is the fork problem again","The landing page still turns strangers away"],"system":"A second route with its own state.","screens":"One new page.","money":"None.","what":"A separate /try page carries the editor and the wall stays at /. Nothing changes for signed-in users; two editors to keep in step, and the landing page still turns strangers away."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the wall and rely on the unlisted kit link as the way in","gains":["Nothing new to build","Every user is known from the first byte"],"costs":["Nobody can try the editor, which is the product","The kit link shows output, never the thing that made it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the wall and let the unlisted kit link be the way in. Nothing to build and every user is known; nobody can try the editor, which is the product."},{"k":"d","label":"Open editor at `/` and never require an account for local work at all","gains":["The strongest version of the privacy claim","No sign-in to lose a user at"],"costs":["Sharing and the kit both need identity, so the account returns anyway","No way to reach a user who is about to churn"],"system":"Account becomes optional throughout.","screens":"Sign-in never blocks.","money":"No email list.","what":"The editor opens at / and local work never needs an account at all. The strongest privacy claim; sharing and the kit need identity, so the account returns anyway."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The measured comparison is one sided. The tools that win this job ask nothing first.","Local only costs no storage and no legal surface, because nothing reaches us until the user chooses."],"flip":"If the pilot shows strangers arrive through the kit link and never through the editor, (c) is honest and cheaper.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"What a stranger can do before being asked who they are","cols":["dillinger.io","StackEdit","frontmatter today"],"rows":[{"label":"Sign-in words in the landing HTML","cells":["0","0","the page is the login screen"]},{"label":"Editor visible on arrival","cells":["yes, Monaco","yes","no"]},{"label":"Can paste a document","cells":["yes","yes","no"]},{"label":"Can export it","cells":["yes","yes","no"]},{"label":"Account required to start","cells":["never","never","always"]}],"note":"Counted on 2026-09-13 by fetching each landing page and stripping script blocks first.","caption":"Entry friction, measured against the tools doing this job"},"sources":["src/app/(vault)/layout.tsx:11","src/proxy.ts:44","docs/GENERATION-SURFACE-2026-09-13-2113.md:1","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1"],"linked":["AC3","F19","PL12"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Entry friction, measured on 2026-09-13","note":"Counted by fetching each landing page and counting sign-in, sign-up, log-in and create-account in the HTML with script blocks stripped.","items":[["dillinger.io","0 sign-in words, Monaco editor in the landing HTML","measured 2026-09-13"],["stackedit.io","0 sign-in words","measured 2026-09-13"],["excalidraw.com","0 sign-in words","measured 2026-09-13"],["hackmd.io","16 sign-in words","measured 2026-09-13"],["typst.app","8 sign-in words","measured 2026-09-13"],["frontmatter today","the page IS the login screen","src/app/(vault)/layout.tsx:11"]]}],"recWhy":"(vault)/layout.tsx returns the login screen when there is no actor, so the home page is the wall and nobody can try the product before joining it. Measured on 13 September: dillinger.io serves a Monaco editor on its landing page with zero sign-in words in the HTML, StackEdit and Excalidraw also show zero, HackMD shows sixteen and Typst eight. The tools that win the quick markdown job ask nothing first. The gate is three lines and the editor never checks it, so opening it is a small change; the real work is the second state and the draft migration on first sign-in. Local only costs no storage and no legal surface because nothing reaches us until the user chooses. Q3 carries this as the first decision of what ships. If the pilot shows strangers arrive through the kit link and never through the editor, (c) is honest and cheaper, and that is the only thing that changes this."},{"id":"AC13","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Keys and AI","weight":"high","q":"Where does the user's AI key live on the web, and does a local model ship?","lede":"The plan's web answer is an OS keychain, which browsers lack, and its local-model deferral cites only drafts.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Web","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Desktop","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Our server","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Where the key rests","cells":["Nowhere: session memory, retyped","OS keychain via Tauri","Envelope-encrypted, held by us"]},{"label":"Worst case","cells":["One tab lifetime","Lost on a machine change","We run up your provider bill"]},{"label":"Friction","cells":["Retyped on every reload","Once per machine","None"]},{"label":"Works with no network","cells":["No","No: the model needs one","No"]},{"label":"Verdict in the research","cells":["Allow, with the friction visible","Recommended","Refuse at v1"]}],"note":"A local model is the only thing that would change the third row, and it is deferred.","caption":"Key custody by surface, with the failure mode each carries."},"stakes":"Store a key in the browser and one renderer flaw lets someone spend on the user's provider account.","state":["The plan's feature table gives bring-your-own-key and keychain three days, marked as working on the web.","It never says where the web keeps the key, and defers the local model."],"tension":["The provider-keys research refuses browser storage, because we render untrusted markdown.","The deferral's 10-of-13 figure for small-model fabrication is in no report or source."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Key custody, with the named failure mode for each (a research report)","cols":["Where the key lives","Failure mode","Verdict in the research"],"rows":[["Browser localStorage","One XSS in a markdown renderer reads it; survives logout; readable by any script on the origin","Refuse"],["Browser session memory, retyped each session","Retyped on every reload; blast radius is one tab-lifetime","Allow on web, with the friction visible"],["OS keychain via Tauri","Lost on machine change; no team sharing","Recommended, desktop only"],["Our server, envelope-encrypted","A breach is not \"documents leaked\", it is \"we ran up your provider bill\"","Refuse at v1"],["Never stored","Nothing to leak, nothing to run","The default, and the free tier"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Browser-origin calls, measured with curl 2026-08-30 (a research report)","items":[["Anthropic, without the header","HTTP 400, no access-control-allow-origin","preflight to POST /v1/messages fails"],["Anthropic, with anthropic-dangerous-direct-browser-access","HTTP 200, ACAO *, credentials true, max-age 600","a real POST from evil.example.com returned 401 and the browser could read it"],["OpenAI","reflects any origin verbatim","tested 4 origins including literal null: all reflected, no opt-in, no warning"],["Google","identical behaviour","same 4 origins reflected"],["Conclusion recorded","the browser is not the blocker; the constraint is custodial","r20-q3 §100.1"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the plan cites against what the corpus holds","items":[["Cited in the plan","\"constrained small models fabricate in 10 of 13\"","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:173"],["Where else that figure appears","only in earlier versions of this same plan","PILOT-PLAN v10/v10.1/v11/v12 and PLAN v13/v14 print HTML, circular, not corroborating"],["Occurrences in any research report, PRD section or engine document","0","searched docs/research/ (235 reports) and docs/engine/"],["What is traceable: constrained-decoding coverage","Guidance 41%, llama.cpp 39%, XGrammar 28%, Outlines 3%","docs/engine/PLAN.md:1210; a coverage measure on \"GitHub Hard\" schemas, not a fabrication rate"],["Also traceable and adjacent","Grammar-Aligned Decoding (NeurIPS 2024): naive masking \"distorts the output distribution\"","engine/PLAN.md:1211: valid and bad, which is the same shape of failure"],["The argument that needs no number","a local model in a refuse-rather-than-guess product is self-refuting","r19-p2 §91.4, stated as [inference]"],["Also true and unrelated to model size","no AI feature works offline in any architecture at our budget","r19-p2 §91.4"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Session memory on the web, OS keychain on desktop, no local model","gains":["Exposure ends with the tab","Nothing is stored in the browser"],"costs":["Retyping the key on every reload","Two designs instead of one"],"system":"A keychain on desktop, memory on web.","screens":"A key field marked not saved.","money":"None. Spend stays with the user.","what":"The key lives in memory for the tab on the web and in the OS keychain on desktop, with no local model. Exposure ends when the tab closes; the key is retyped after every reload."},{"k":"b","label":"localStorage on the web, with the blast radius documented","gains":["No friction after the first entry"],"costs":["A bearer credential inside an untrusted-markdown renderer"],"system":"A browser store the security model forbids.","screens":"A key field and a warning.","money":"None.","what":"localStorage on the web with the blast radius written down. No friction after the first entry, and a bearer credential sits inside a page that renders untrusted markdown."},{"k":"c","label":"No AI on the web at all: a desktop feature, keyed from the OS keychain","gains":["The cleanest custody story"],"costs":["Weakens the web app, which is the trial"],"system":"Remove the AI path from web.","screens":"No AI panel on the web.","money":"None.","what":"No AI on the web at all: a desktop feature keyed from the OS keychain. The cleanest custody story, and it weakens the surface the trial runs on."},{"k":"d","label":"Bundle a small local model so desktop AI needs no key and no network","gains":["Closes the offline-AI gap every design leaves"],"costs":["Small models fabricate, which breaks the refuse-not-guess promise"],"system":"A bundled runtime and weights.","screens":"A model picker with a local option.","money":"Installer size against zero spend.","what":"Bundle a small local model so desktop AI needs no key and no network. Closes the offline gap every design leaves; small models fabricate, which breaks refuse rather than guess."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Session memory is the only web option whose exposure ends with the tab.","Never our server: a breach runs up your provider bill. Cite the engine plan's decoding figures instead."],"flip":"Under 40% of people who click \"connect a model\" finish key setup.","linked":["AC1","AC12","F10"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:62","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:97","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:45","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q3-provider-keys.md:30","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q3-provider-keys.md:36","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q3-provider-keys.md:136","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q3-provider-keys.md:144","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:173","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:93","docs/engine/PLAN.md:1210","docs/engine/PLAN.md:1211","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md","merged: AC16"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Strangers bring their own key on the web. Where it is kept is a first build answer.","recWhy":"The plan's web answer is an OS keychain, which browsers do not have, and its local-model deferral cites a 10-of-13 fabrication figure that appears in no report or source. The provider-keys research refuses browser storage because this product renders untrusted markdown, and a bearer credential next to untrusted content is the standard exfiltration setup. Session memory is the only web option whose exposure ends with the tab; the price is retyping the key on every reload, and that friction is visible. Never our server, because a breach there runs up your provider bill. Desktop uses the OS keychain. Q10 carries this as the user's own key, a hard gate before any stranger reaches an AI route. Cite the engine plan's decoding figures for the local-model deferral instead of the number nobody can find. Under 40 percent finishing key setup would send this to (b) with the risk documented."},{"id":"AC4","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Browser lanes","weight":"high","q":"Do we ship the File System Access API on the web, when two earlier rounds cut it?","lede":"The plan reversed a research decision without noting it, and the deciding number is free to measure.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Someone opens the web app","note":"no install, no account yet","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Chrome or Edge on desktop","note":"folder picker, direct writes","tone":"good","edge":"yes"},{"label":"Firefox, Safari, mobile","note":"no folder picker exists","tone":"stop","edge":"no"},{"label":"Connect a GitHub repo","note":"the one lane that works everywhere","tone":"accent","edge":"either"},{"label":"Download the desktop build","note":"a conversion step at peak intent","tone":"warn","edge":"otherwise"}],"note":"Chrome Android is unreconciled between two sources and should not be built on.","caption":"The fork a visitor with a plain folder hits, by browser."},"stakes":"Ship it and some share of desktop visitors meet an unsupported-browser refusal on arrival.","state":["The plan ships it, on desktop Chrome and Edge only.","No folder-picker code (`showDirectoryPicker`) exists yet."],"tension":["Nobody has measured the refusal rate; the only figure is an assumption.","Chrome on Android is disputed: MDN data says 132, caniuse says no."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Support, as three sources in this corpus report it","cols":["Source","Chrome/Edge desktop","Firefox","Safari / iOS","Chrome Android"],"rows":[["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:102 (caniuse, 2026-09-08)","Yes","No","No","No"],["r20-q4 §101.1 line 11 (MDN BCD, 2026-08-31)","Chrome 86+, Edge mirrors","version_added: false","version_added: false","132+"],["s4-mobile-cross-device.md:202 (2026-08-31)","n/a","n/a","n/a","BCD 132 vs caniuse and_chr 151 \"n\". Recorded as unreconciled: do not build on either number without re-testing"],["18-simplicity.md:283 (2026-08-31)","n/a","n/a","BCD says safari 15.2 for the handle interfaces; caniuse says \"n\" for the feature at every iOS version through 26.6. Both correct at different granularity, so cite the picker, not the interface","same unreconciled note"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The refusal rate is assumed, not measured","items":[["Chromium share of desktop browsing used in the estimate","~70%","r19-p2 §91.3, explicitly [derived] from an assumed input"],["Share assumed for our audience","80%","also assumed: the source's words are \"call it 80%\""],["Resulting refusal rate","1 in 5 desktop visitors","at 10,000 users, 2,000 first experiences that are a refusal"],["Times the real rate has been measured","0","no analytics on browser mix exists in the corpus"],["Cost of measuring it properly","a script tag on the landing page","the browser mix of people who arrive does not require the adapter to exist"],["API status flag in MDN BCD","experimental: true, secure-context only","fetched 2026-08-31"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship it on Chrome and Edge, labelled as limited before the click","gains":["Zero-install access to a plain folder"],"costs":["Two weeks the schedule has no room for"],"system":"A local-folder adapter for the editor.","screens":"A folder picker and support line.","money":"None.","what":"showDirectoryPicker on Chrome and Edge, with the limitation named before the click. Zero-install access to a plain folder on those browsers, and about two weeks the schedule does not have."},{"k":"b","label":"Cut it: the desktop build owns plain folders, the web build owns GitHub","gains":["What both earlier rounds recommended","No adapter, browser matrix or Android question"],"costs":["A download at the moment of peak intent","Lost sign-ups there, with no distribution to absorb it"],"system":"One fewer adapter to maintain.","screens":"A download prompt instead.","money":"None.","what":"No folder access in the browser at all: the desktop build owns plain folders, the web build owns GitHub. What both earlier rounds recommended, and a download at the moment of peak intent."},{"k":"c","label":"Instrument the landing page for browser mix now, decide when the number arrives","gains":["Turns an assumed refusal rate into data"],"costs":["Free only if the pilot is web-on-a-repo anyway"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Log the browser mix on the landing page now and decide when four weeks of data exist. Turns an assumed refusal rate into a number, free only if the pilot runs on a repository anyway."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship it, with drag-and-drop read-only as the Firefox and Safari fallback","gains":["No visible refusal on any browser"],"costs":["Review state, what you have read, silently fails for them"],"system":"A read-only session adapter as well.","screens":"A drag target that cannot save.","money":"None.","what":"The File System Access API where the browser has it, and drag-and-drop read-only everywhere else. No visible refusal on any browser; review state silently fails for the fallback users."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["FL12 and PL12 in the MVP set put folder first and drag-in with no account on the web, and the MVP decision governs.","Ship the File System Access API where the browser has it, with drag-and-drop read-only as the fallback everywhere else."],"flip":"If the browser mix on the landing page shows under half can use the API, drag-in becomes primary and the API the extra.","linked":["AC12","AC17","AC6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:102","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:41","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:11","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:80","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q7-one-page.md:78","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:202","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/18-simplicity.md:283"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Direct folder access was cut twice and is not in the first build.","recWhy":"Two earlier research rounds cut the File System Access API and the plan shipped it without noting the reversal, and no showDirectoryPicker code exists. The deciding number, how many visitors arrive on a browser that has the API, has never been measured; the only figure in the plan is an assumption, and Chrome on Android is disputed between MDN and caniuse. It sits after the pilot because FL12 and PL12 in the MVP set already put folder first and drag-in with no account on the web, and the MVP decision governs. When built, ship the API where the browser has it and drag-and-drop read-only as the fallback, so nobody sees a refusal. The honest cost is that review state cannot persist for the fallback users and the page has to say so. Q3 and Q4 carry the surface. If the landing page shows under half of visitors can use the API, drag-in becomes primary and the API the extra."},{"id":"AC12","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Offline","weight":"high","q":"Do we build real offline reading for the web app, or say online-only and fix the plan's table?","lede":"The plan publishes an offline capability the code does not have, in the table a buyer reads.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":220,"cols":[{"title":"The plan's table claims","tone":"warn"},{"title":"The code does","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Web, GitHub repo","cells":["Cached open files only","Nothing: content arrives via /api/, which the worker passes through"]},{"label":"Web, local folder","cells":["Cached","No File System Access code exists in src/ at all"]},{"label":"Web, Firefox, Safari, mobile","cells":["No","Correct"]},{"label":"Desktop","cells":["Everything except the model","No bundled assets, so no window content offline"]}],"note":"The service worker is 98 lines and caches two static assets.","caption":"The table a stranger reads to choose a surface, against the file."},"stakes":"A buyer picks a surface on a row wrong in two of four columns.","state":["The plan's 'What works where' table, its second exhibit, claims cached open files in both web columns.","The service worker treats page loads as network-only and never intercepts `/api/`."],"tension":["An earlier round refused a half-working offline mode in writing.","IndexedDB evicts least-recently-used data, so the store is never the record."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The service worker as it exists","items":[["Lines","98","public/sw.js"],["Assets cached","2","/favicon.png and /theme-init.js, line 14"],["HTML navigations","network-only","line 63; req.mode === navigate is never served from cache"],["/api/ and /login","never intercepted","lines 52 to 58: which is where note content arrives"],["Cache key","sgnk-md-v2","line 13, still the sibling product's name"],["Offline fallback that already exists","an inline HTML page saying \"md.sgnk.ai is unreachable\"","lines 63 to 76: so the shell-offline courtesy is shipped, with the wrong product's domain in it"],["Offline reading today","impossible","s4:18, [derived] from the two /api/ rows above"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the plan claims against what the code does","cols":["Surface","The \"what works where\" platform table's claims","Code does"],"rows":[["Web, GitHub repo","Cached open files only","Nothing: content comes from /api/, which the SW passes through"],["Web, local folder","Cached","No File System Access code exists in src/ at all"],["Web, Firefox/Safari/mobile","No","Correct"],["Desktop","Everything except the model","No filesystem plugin, no bundled assets, so no window content without a network"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build real offline reading: N most-recent files plus pins, in IndexedDB","gains":["Makes the published claim true"],"costs":["Days the schedule does not have"],"system":"An IndexedDB cache with pins and eviction.","screens":"A per-file offline toggle.","money":"None.","what":"Cache the N most recent files plus pins in IndexedDB and serve them when the network is gone. Makes the published claim true; days the schedule does not have, and the store is evictable."},{"k":"b","label":"State that the web build is online-only, and correct the plan's platform table","gains":["Costs one edit","Makes offline a real reason to download"],"costs":["Removes a capability from the web column","Concedes the trial surface is weaker"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Say the web build is online-only and fix the plan's platform table. One edit, and offline becomes a real reason to download the desktop app; the web column loses a capability."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the shell-offline page and fix its copy","gains":["Nearly free; the page already exists"],"costs":["The user still cannot read anything"],"system":"Edit the service worker.","screens":"The offline page.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the shell-offline page and correct its copy, which still names md.sgnk.ai. Nearly free because the page exists; the user still cannot read a file."},{"k":"d","label":"Leave the table as written and revisit after the pilot","gains":["No work"],"costs":["The plan keeps publishing a capability that is absent"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave the table as written and look again after the pilot. No work, and the plan keeps publishing a capability the code does not have to the person choosing a surface."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Correct the table, because it is what a stranger reads to choose a surface.","Do (c) alongside it: the offline page still names the sibling product's md.sgnk.ai."],"flip":"The pilot finds offline reading is why people stop using the web app.","linked":["AC4","FF8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:99","public/sw.js:13","public/sw.js:52","public/sw.js:63","public/sw.js:70","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q7-one-page.md:48","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:18","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:60","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:153","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:222"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Offline reading is not in the first build.","recWhy":"The plan's platform table, its second exhibit, claims cached open files in both web columns, and the service worker treats page loads as network-only and never intercepts the API, so the claim is false in the table a buyer reads to choose a surface. An earlier round refused a half-working offline mode in writing, and IndexedDB evicts least-recently-used data, so a browser store can never be the record. Correct the table, and fix the offline page's copy alongside it, because that page still names the sibling product. Offline then belongs to the desktop build, which is a reason to download it rather than a gap. Q3 has the web editor first and the Mac app later from the same tree. If the pilot shows offline reading is why people stop using the web app, (a) is worth its days."},{"id":"AC19","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Web byte path","weight":"high","q":"On the web against a GitHub repo, is review state written per action, batched, or to a branch?","lede":"Review state, what you have read, is a file in your repo, so every review action is a write.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"You clear a span","note":"one line appended to review.jsonl","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Commit per action","note":"the noisiest history a repo can have","tone":"stop","edge":"or"},{"label":"Batch on idle and on close","note":"lost if the tab dies first","tone":"accent","edge":"or"},{"label":"A separate branch","note":"invisible to git log on main","tone":"neutral","edge":"or"},{"label":"Browser only, never written","note":"not durable across machines","tone":"warn","edge":"or"}],"note":"On desktop this is one file write. On the web it is a git operation each time.","caption":"Four ways one cleared span reaches the repository."},"stakes":"Review state not written back does not carry across machines, the one claim still standing.","state":["The plan says the web app commits the review file alongside the documents.","Nothing specifies when that file is written, or by what git operation."],"tension":["Review marks come far faster than edits, so one commit per mark floods the history.","Writes count against the same 5,000 an hour GitHub limit as reads."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One commit per review action","gains":["Durable the moment it is made"],"costs":["Their history fills with commits they did not make"],"system":"A commit per interaction through the client.","screens":"None, until they read git log.","money":"None.","what":"One commit per review action. Durable the moment it is made; the user's history fills with commits they did not make, and each write counts against the 5,000 an hour limit."},{"k":"b","label":"Batch: write on idle, on file close and on tab close","gains":["One commit a session, not dozens","Still lands in the repository"],"costs":["A killed tab loses that session's marks","Two machines can disagree for a whole session"],"system":"A write timer and a tab-close handler.","screens":"A saved indicator in the panel.","money":"None.","what":"Write on idle, on file close and on tab close. One commit a session that still lands in the repository; a killed tab loses that session's marks."},{"k":"c","label":"Write to a separate branch, never to the working branch","gains":["The user's branch history is untouched"],"costs":["A fresh clone without the branch shows nothing"],"system":"A review-file branch and a merge policy.","screens":"None, unless a conflict surfaces.","money":"None.","what":"Write review state to a separate branch, never the working branch. The user's branch history is untouched; a fresh clone without that branch shows nothing."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep web review state in the browser, and write the file only on desktop","gains":["No repository writes on the trial surface"],"costs":["Not durable across machines, the surviving claim"],"system":"A browser store with no git path.","screens":"Marks are local to this browser.","money":"None.","what":"Keep web review state in the browser and write the file only on desktop. No repository writes on the trial surface; not durable across machines, which is the surviving claim."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["One commit a session keeps history readable and still lands in the repository.","(d) forfeits the one claim nothing else reaches: durable across machines."],"flip":"The pilot shows people review in short bursts and lose marks to closed tabs.","linked":["AC1","AC17","AC3","FF8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:95","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r20-q4-repo-and-folders.md:38"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Writing review state to a repository is deferred with review state.","recWhy":"Review state, what you have read, is a file in the repository, so on the web every review action is a git write, and nothing in the plan says when that write happens. Marks come far faster than edits: one commit per mark floods the history with commits the user did not make and spends the same 5,000 an hour budget the reads need. Batching on idle, file close and tab close gives one commit a session that still lands in the repository; a killed tab loses that session's marks and two machines can disagree for a session. (d) forfeits the one claim nothing else in the market reaches, durable across machines. E37 decides how two people's review files merge and E36 how a record survives a rename; this card is the third leg. It sits after the pilot because Q6 makes the instruction-file view read-only first. Short-burst reviewing that loses marks to closed tabs sends this to (a)."},{"id":"AC21","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"OFFLINE AND KEYS","weight":"high","when":"after","whenWhy":"Offline is a second runtime to build and support, so it belongs after the pilot rather than in it.","q":"Does offline mode run the user's own key, a local model, or an account-billed route, and which ships first?","lede":"Offline with the user's own key is the one feature here that nothing in the comparison set offers.","stakes":"It is a real differentiator and a second runtime. Shipping it early buys distinctiveness and doubles the support surface.","state":["The direction given on 13 September proposes local generation via the user's key or an aggregator route.","`AC13` already asks where a key lives on the web, and this is the offline half of the same question.","Nothing in the tools checked on 2026-09-13 offers generation with a key the user holds."],"tension":["Holding no key and no bytes is the strongest version of the privacy claim.","Every model provider behaves differently, so supporting the user's choice is support cost that never ends."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"The user's own key only, one provider at first","gains":["No model bill and no metering to build","The privacy claim stays true with no exception"],"costs":["A key is a real barrier for anyone who does not have one","One provider will not suit everyone"],"system":"A key store on the device.","screens":"A settings pane.","money":"No model cost to us.","what":"Generation runs on a key the user holds, one provider at first. No model bill, no metering and no ceiling to build; a key is a real barrier for anyone without one."},{"k":"b","label":"The user's key, or an aggregator route billed to us on the top tier","gains":["Works for people without a key","A clear thing the top tier buys"],"costs":["Metering, a ceiling and an abuse surface, which is `PR10`","We hold a key that pays for other people's work"],"system":"A metered proxy.","screens":"Usage and a ceiling.","money":"Model spend, capped.","what":"The user's key, or an aggregator route billed to us on the top tier. Works for people without a key and gives the top tier a clear purchase; metering, a ceiling and an abuse surface, which is PR10."},{"k":"c","label":"A local model, no key and no network at all","gains":["The strongest possible privacy claim","No running cost"],"costs":["Output quality is far below hosted models for this task","Large download, and it rules out weak machines"],"system":"A bundled runtime.","screens":"A download step.","money":"None.","what":"A local model with no key and no network. The strongest privacy claim and no running cost; output far below hosted models for this task, a large download, and weak machines are out."},{"k":"d","label":"No offline mode. The web app is the product","gains":["One runtime to build, test and support"],"costs":["Gives up the clearest differentiator found in the research","The desktop shell then has no reason to exist"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No offline mode; the web app is the product. One runtime to build and support; the clearest differentiator in the research goes, and the desktop shell has no reason to exist."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["A key the user holds costs us no model spend, no metering and no ceiling, which is three things not built.","It keeps the privacy claim exact, and exactness is the part competitors cannot copy cheaply."],"flip":"If most pilot users turn out not to hold a key, (b) is the only version that works and `PR10` has to be answered first.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"Who pays for the model, and what each choice costs us","cols":["Their key","Their key or ours","Local model"],"rows":[{"label":"Our model spend","cells":["none","capped","none"]},{"label":"Metering to build","cells":["no","yes","no"]},{"label":"Works without a key","cells":["no","yes","yes"]},{"label":"Output quality","cells":["hosted","hosted","well below"]},{"label":"Privacy claim","cells":["exact","has an exception","exact"]}],"note":"Nothing in the tools checked on 2026-09-13 offered generation on a key the user holds.","caption":"Three ways to run generation offline, and what each costs"},"sources":["docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1"],"linked":["AC13","PR10","PR7"],"recWhy":"Nothing in the tools checked on 13 September offers generation on a key the user holds, so offline with your own key is the one feature here that nothing in the comparison set has. A key the user holds costs us no model spend, no metering and no ceiling, three things that are not built, and it keeps the privacy claim exact, which is the part a competitor cannot copy cheaply. AC13 is the web half of the same question and lands the same way. One provider at first, because every provider behaves differently and supporting the user's choice is support cost that never ends. This sits after the pilot because Q3 has the desktop surface arriving later and Q10 already settles the key. If most pilot users turn out not to hold a key, (b) is the only version that works and PR10, the metered route with a daily breaker, has to be answered first."},{"id":"AC22","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"REPOSITORY ACCESS","weight":"high","when":"after","whenWhy":"Repository access is the riskiest item on the list and it reopens `L1`, so it is last rather than planned.","q":"If we ever read a user's repository, is it read only, and is writing ruled out in writing?","lede":"Reading a repository to write better documents about it is a different proposition, legally and commercially, from writing to it.","stakes":"Writing to a customer's repository makes us an intermediary holding their code, with every duty `L1` costs at 74 founder hours.","state":["The direction given on 13 September proposes connecting GitHub and the local machine on the top plan.","`L1` prices the intermediary duties that holding customer content brings.","Claude Code, Cursor and Codex already run on the local machine and do this well."],"tension":["A kit written with the repository in view is far better than one written from answers alone.","Every byte of customer code we touch is a duty, and the value is nearly all on the reading side."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Read only, forever, and say so in the operating rules","gains":["Most of the value at a fraction of the duty","A claim competitors with write access cannot make"],"costs":["Users will ask for write and we will keep refusing","Still means holding code in memory while reading"],"system":"A read-scoped token and no write path.","screens":"A connect screen naming the scope.","money":"Duties for transit, not storage.","what":"Read only, forever, written into the operating rules. Most of the value at a fraction of the duty and a claim write-access tools cannot make; users will ask for write and we keep refusing."},{"k":"b","label":"Read and write, on the top tier, as the direction proposes","gains":["The agent loop closes inside our product"],"costs":["Reopens every duty in `L1` at the moment of the first write","Direct competition with tools that are already good at it"],"system":"Write scopes, commits, conflicts.","screens":"A review and commit flow.","money":"Counsel, and the 74 hour pack.","what":"Read and write on the top tier, as the 13 September direction proposes. The agent loop closes inside our product; every duty in L1 reopens at the first write, against tools already good at it."},{"k":"c","label":"Never touch a repository. The user pastes what they want considered","gains":["Zero new duty and zero new surface","Keeps the zero-bytes claim exact"],"costs":["Kits stay weaker than they could be","A manual step at the moment the user is least patient"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Never touch a repository; the user pastes what they want considered. Zero new duty and the zero-bytes claim stays exact; kits stay weaker, and a manual step when patience is lowest."},{"k":"d","label":"Read only, and only on the desktop build where bytes never leave the machine","gains":["The strongest privacy story available","No transit duty at all"],"costs":["Needs the desktop app, which `FF8` says is not in the pilot","Splits the product in two"],"system":"Local file access in the shell.","screens":"A desktop-only connect screen.","money":"None.","what":"Read only, and only on the desktop build where bytes never leave the machine. The strongest privacy story; needs the desktop app FF8 keeps out of the pilot, and splits the product."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Nearly all the value of repository access is on the reading side, and nearly all the duty is on the writing side.","Ruling writing out in the operating rules turns a refusal into a claim, which is how this product argues everywhere else."],"flip":"If the pilot shows users will not paste and will not connect a read-only token either, (c) is the honest fallback.","visual":{"kind":"compare","title":"Reading a repository against writing to one","cols":["Read only","Read and write","Never connect"],"rows":[{"label":"Reopens L1","cells":["partly, transit","fully","no"]},{"label":"Kit quality","cells":["much better","much better","weakest"]},{"label":"Competes with Cursor and Claude Code","cells":["no","yes","no"]},{"label":"Can be stated as a refusal","cells":["yes","no","yes"]},{"label":"Founder hours of legal pack","cells":["part","74","none"]}],"note":"The 74 hour figure is the publishing and intermediary pack costed at PRD v2 line 4194.","caption":"Where the value sits, and where the duty sits"},"sources":["docs/FEATURE-RESEARCH-2026-09-13-2130.md:1","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194","docs/MVP-PLAN-2026-09-13.md:1"],"linked":["FF8","L1","L6"],"recWhy":"Reading a repository to write better documents about it is a different proposition, legally and commercially, from writing to it. Nearly all the value of repository access is on the reading side, a kit written with the code in view is far better than one written from answers, and nearly all the duty is on the writing side. Every byte we write into customer code is L1's intermediary duty arriving at once, against Claude Code, Cursor and Codex, which already do this well on the local machine. Ruling writing out in the operating rules turns a refusal into a claim, which is how this product argues everywhere. It is also why AC3 keeps three permissions though Q2 settled the buyer as the team. It sits after the pilot because the top plan that connects GitHub is Q13's team tier and comes later. If users will not paste and will not connect a read-only token either, (c) is the honest fallback."},{"id":"AC17","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Pilot surface","weight":"medium","q":"Where does the pilot's usage file live, when three of four access paths cannot hold it?","lede":"The measurement decision and the access model were written together and never checked against each other.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"In the user's repo","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Per surface","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Day-14 question","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Web, GitHub repo","cells":["A commit a day into a stranger's repo","Browser storage, no commit","Works"]},{"label":"Web, local folder","cells":["Yes, a silent disk write","Yes","Works"]},{"label":"Web, drag-in","cells":["Impossible: nothing persists","Browser storage","Works"]},{"label":"Desktop","cells":["Yes, once the fs plugin exists","Yes","Works"]},{"label":"What the gate then reads","cells":["Counted","Counted","Self-reported"]}],"note":"The gate is 6 of 10, and the draft's own fallback already concedes the self-report.","caption":"The exit measure against the four access paths."},"stakes":"The pass mark reads this file, which cannot exist on three of four paths.","state":["The 8 September draft puts it in `.frontmatter/usage.jsonl`, a line a day.","The pass mark is use on 6 of the last 10 working days.","pwa's /pilot app with its private Blob store is now the instrument; tester feedback goes there with consent, not documents."],"tension":["Via GitHub on the web, it is a daily commit to a stranger's repo.","With drag-and-drop or Firefox nothing persists, so it cannot exist."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The usage file on each access path","cols":["Access path","Can hold .frontmatter/usage.jsonl?","Cost"],"rows":[["Web, GitHub repo","Only as a commit","One commit per day into the user's repo, from a product that promises not to touch it"],["Web, local folder (Chrome/Edge)","Yes","Silent local write, needs the File System Access adapter that the browser-access decision recommends cutting"],["Web, drag-in","No","Session only, not persisted"],["Desktop","Yes","Needs the filesystem plugin that is absent from Cargo.toml"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the gate needs to observe","items":[["Exit measure","opened on ≥6 of the last 10 working days, plus ≥1 review action in week two","WEEK0:22"],["Strangers","10","6 of 10 is the gate"],["How it reaches us","not at all by default, pasted once, on day 14","WEEK0:21"],["Stated fallback if local files are rejected","one message on day 14; \"accept that the gate is then self-reported\"","WEEK0:27"],["Vendor usage database","none, by architecture","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:313"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep it in the repo, batched to one commit a week","gains":["The user owns the file"],"costs":["Still impossible with drag-and-drop or Firefox"],"system":"A weekly commit through the GitHub client.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"The daily line stays in the repo at .frontmatter/usage.jsonl, batched into one commit a week. The user owns the file; drag-and-drop and Firefox still have nowhere to write it."},{"k":"b","label":"Store it per surface, and the stranger pastes whichever they have","gains":["Works on every path, Firefox included","No commit into anyone's repository"],"costs":["Browser storage is not the user's repo","Clearing site data makes a zero ambiguous"],"system":"Browser storage on web, a file on desktop.","screens":"A settings row to copy it.","money":"None.","what":"Each surface keeps its own count where it can, and on day 14 the tester pastes whichever they have. Works on every path; a cleared browser makes a zero ambiguous."},{"k":"c","label":"Drop the file and use the day-14 question the draft already proposes","gains":["Zero build, works everywhere"],"costs":["Self-reported, which the measure existed to avoid"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No file; ask the day-14 question the draft already proposes. Zero build and works everywhere; self-reported, which is what the measure existed to avoid."},{"k":"d","label":"Opt-in upload of the daily line to the control plane, off by default","gains":["The only reliable count"],"costs":["Makes counts personal data keyed to an account"],"system":"An endpoint and a consent record.","screens":"A consent toggle in settings.","money":"It brings in data-protection obligations.","what":"Opt-in upload of the daily line to the control plane, off by default. The only reliable count, and it makes usage personal data keyed to an account."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The measure must work on any pilot surface, and only per-surface storage does.","Keep the draft's settled parts: counts only, pasted once on day 14."],"flip":"The pilot moves to a signed desktop build, where the repo file works.","linked":["AC19","AC4","B10"],"sources":["docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:27","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:313","src-tauri/Cargo.toml"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The usage file measures a pilot that has not run.","recWhy":"The measurement decision and the access model were never checked against each other. The 8 September draft puts a usage line a day in .frontmatter/usage.jsonl with a pass mark of use on 6 of the last 10 working days. Through GitHub on the web that is a daily commit into a stranger's repository; with drag-and-drop or on Firefox nothing persists, so the file cannot exist at all. Three of four access paths cannot hold it. Per-surface storage is the only form that works on every path, and the settled parts of the draft survive: counts only, pasted once on day 14, no upload. The pwa /pilot app with its private store takes tester feedback. E32's refusal counter lives in the same local file, so this decision also decides whether the engine's kill condition can be instrumented. Q15 defines what passed means. A signed desktop build, where the repo file works, flips this back to (a)."},{"id":"AC8","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Desktop shell","weight":"critical","q":"Do we rebuild the Tauri shell as a bundled local app before signing, or keep it internal?","lede":"The desktop app cannot open a local file, and signing it would vouch for a swappable website.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"The shell today","lines":[{"t":"frontendDist points at https://md.sgnk.ai","mark":"remote"},{"t":"security.csp","mark":"null"},{"t":"tauri-plugin-fs","mark":"absent"},{"t":"Filesystem permissions in capabilities","mark":"none"},{"t":"Bundled assets, so a window offline","mark":"none"}]},"after":{"title":"After the rebuild","lines":[{"t":"Frontend bundled inside the app","mark":"local"},{"t":"Scoped filesystem capability","mark":"opens files"},{"t":"A real content security policy","mark":"set"},{"t":"Update channels","mark":"one, signed"},{"t":"Works with no network","mark":"yes"}]},"via":"4 to 8 weeks","note":"The research calls signing the current configuration security theatre.","caption":"What signing would attest to today, against what it should attest to."},"stakes":"Signing the shell as it stands certifies code any web deploy can replace.","state":["The desktop config loads the app from https://md.sgnk.ai, with no content security policy.","The app has shell, os, process and dialog plugins; no filesystem, no updater.","Its permissions grant no file access, so it opens nothing."],"tension":["The remote site is a second, unsigned update channel.","The rebuild is a 4-to-8-week item the schedule has no days for."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The shell as configured, read from the repo","items":[["frontendDist / devUrl / window url","https://md.sgnk.ai","tauri.conf.json: the shell loads remote content, not bundled assets"],["security.csp","null","the one mitigation Tauri offers for a remote-origin window is off"],["bundle.macOS.signingIdentity","null","nothing is signed; Tauri's hardened runtime default applies only when an identity exists"],["tauri-plugin-fs in Cargo.toml","absent","the desktop app cannot read a local file at all"],["tauri-plugin-updater in Cargo.toml","absent","no update channel of any kind"],["Filesystem permissions in capabilities/default.json","none","core, event, window, webview, shell, os, process, clipboard, dialog only"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each path actually delivers","cols":["Path","Opens a local file?","One verified update channel?","Offline?"],"rows":[["Sign and ship the shell as it is","No","No, two channels, one unsigned","No, no bundled assets means no window content without a network"],["Bundle the frontend, add scoped fs, set a CSP, then sign","Yes","Yes","Yes"],["Keep it unsigned as an internal beta","No","n/a","No"],["Drop the desktop build from the pilot","n/a","n/a","n/a"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Bundle the frontend, declare a scoped fs capability, set a real CSP, then sign","gains":["The only version worth shipping"],"costs":["The schedule shows only a one-day rename"],"system":"Bundled assets, `tauri-plugin-fs`, a real CSP.","screens":"A folder picker and real offline.","money":"None beyond the days.","what":"Bundle the frontend into the binary, add a scoped filesystem capability and a real content security policy, then sign. The only version worth shipping; four to eight weeks against a one-day rename."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the remote shell as an unsigned internal artefact only","what":"A wrapper for smoke-testing window chrome and menus.","gains":["Costs nothing and keeps the option open","Honest about what it is"],"costs":["Not a shipping posture","Must never appear on a download page"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None public.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Sign and notarise the shell now, so there is a download to point at","gains":["A download link exists"],"costs":["Vouches for code any web deploy can change"],"system":"No shell change, only the pipeline.","screens":"A download page for something broken.","money":"99 USD/year for a wrapper.","what":"Sign and notarise the shell as it is so a download link exists. A link exists, and the signature vouches for whatever the last web deploy put at md.sgnk.ai."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop the desktop build from the pilot entirely","gains":["Consistent with a web-first pilot"],"costs":["Removes offline and local folders this year"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No desktop build in the pilot at all. Consistent with a web-first pilot; offline and local folders leave the year."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Rule out (c) for good. Signing a URL wrapper certifies only the last web deploy.","(b) costs nothing and keeps the option open while the schedule is decided."],"flip":"The schedule puts the pilot on a desktop build, so (a) starts now.","linked":["AC10","AC18","AC6","FF8"],"sources":["src-tauri/tauri.conf.json","src-tauri/Cargo.toml","src-tauri/capabilities/default.json","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:113","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:115","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:121","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/35-platform-surfaces.md:311","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p2-offline-or-online.md"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"The desktop app is outside the first build.","recWhy":"The desktop config loads the app from https://md.sgnk.ai with no content security policy, carries shell, os, process and dialog plugins but no filesystem plugin, and grants no file access, so the app cannot open a local file. Signing that would certify a swappable website: every web deploy becomes an unsigned update channel, which rules (c) out for good. The rebuild that makes it worth shipping, a bundled frontend with a scoped filesystem capability and a real policy, is four to eight weeks, and the schedule shows a one-day rename. Keep the shell as an unsigned internal artefact for smoke-testing window chrome and menus, never on a download page. That costs nothing and keeps the option open. Q3 puts the Mac app after the web editor, from the same tree, which is why this sits at not now. If the pilot moves to a desktop build, (a) starts immediately."},{"id":"AC6","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Desktop platforms","weight":"high","q":"Does v1 of the desktop app ship macOS only, and what does signing Windows actually cost?","lede":"The plan promises three installers and states a Windows price that matches nothing measured.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"macOS only","tone":"accent"},{"title":"+ Windows, Trusted Signing","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"All three, OV certificate","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Year-one certificates","cells":["99 USD","99 + 119.88 USD","99 + 219 USD"]},{"label":"CI, two releases a week, private repo","cells":["193.44 USD","216.53 USD","216.53 USD"]},{"label":"Hardware token","cells":["None","None, managed HSM","Yes, and no build machine to hold it"]},{"label":"Debuggable on hardware we own","cells":["Yes","No, Windows is not","No"]},{"label":"Unverified before it enters a budget","cells":["Nothing","Individual eligibility, signature quota","Reseller quotes, not CA lists"]}],"note":"The plan's 150 USD Windows floor is below every quote in this corpus.","caption":"What each platform mix costs in year one, at measured prices."},"stakes":"One person cannot debug Windows SmartScreen reputation on a machine they do not own.","state":["The plan promises a notarised .dmg, a signed .exe at 150-400 USD a year, and an AppImage.","The build targets are dmg and app only, and package.json has no Windows or Linux target."],"tension":["The cheapest measured certificate is 219 USD a year, so 150 is wrong.","Azure Trusted Signing is 119.88 USD a year, no hardware token, unverified."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Year-one direct-distribution cost, three platforms, private repo","items":[["Apple Developer Program","99 USD/year","fetched 2026-08-29"],["Windows OV certificate, lowest measured quote","219 USD/year","Comodo/Sectigo via reseller; the plan's \"$150\" low end is below every measured quote"],["Microsoft Partner Center account","0 USD","no registration fee for either account type; the plan does not mention this"],["CI, two releases a week","216.53 USD/year","104 × 2.082 USD per release [derived]"],["Year-one floor, all three","534.53 USD","99 + 219 + 216.53 [derived]"],["macOS share of per-release CI cost","89.3%","1.860 of 2.082 USD, the only CI line worth optimising"],["macOS-only CI, private repo","193.44 USD/year","104 × 1.860 [derived]; on a public repo all CI is 0 USD"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Per-release CI cost by platform, at GitHub's published rates","note":"Estimated build minutes (Linux 12, Windows 15, macOS universal 30) × published per-minute rates, fetched 2026-08-29. Minutes are inference: no release build was run; rates are fetched. Public repos are free.","unit":"USD per release","data":[["macOS universal",1.86],["Windows",0.15],["Linux",0.072]]},{"type":"table","title":"Windows signing routes, all fetched 2026-08-29","cols":["Route","Price","Hardware token","SmartScreen"],"rows":[["Azure Trusted Signing, Basic","9.99 USD/mo = 119.88/yr, plus 0.005 USD per signature above an unstated quota","None, managed HSM at FIPS 140-3 level 3","Not stated in what was fetched; eligibility for individuals unverified"],["OV certificate (Comodo/Sectigo)","219 USD/yr","Yes","Warns until reputation accrues, and reputation is per file hash, so every release restarts the climb"],["OV (GoGetSSL / DigiCert)","289 / 400 USD/yr","Yes","Same"],["EV (Comodo/Sectigo → DigiCert)","287 → 685 USD/yr","Yes","Immediate reputation, no warning; business vetting from India is the real cost"],["Microsoft Partner Center account","0 USD one-time","n/a","n/a"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the plan says against what was measured","items":[["Plan's stated Windows range","150 to 400 USD/year","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104 and :231"],["Lowest measured quote of any kind","119.88 USD/year","Azure Trusted Signing Basic, 12 × 9.99"],["Lowest measured certificate quote","219 USD/year","so the plan's \"$150\" floor matches nothing in the corpus"],["Plan's stated hardware requirement","\"hardware token required\"","false for the Trusted Signing route"],["Saving, Trusted Signing vs OV, year one","99.12 USD","435.41 against 534.53 [derived]"],["Recorded caveats","reseller quotes not CA list; SSL.com's own pages returned 404; token surcharge is a search summary, not fetched","k9:37, k9 cost table"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"macOS only in v1, said plainly on the download page","gains":["macOS is 89.3% of CI spend","Notarisation is debuggable on hardware we own"],"costs":["Every Windows visitor bounces, uncounted","Contradicts what the plan promises"],"system":"Keep the build targets unchanged.","screens":"A download page saying macOS only.","money":"99 USD/year plus 193 USD CI.","what":"One notarised .dmg and a download page that says macOS only. Notarisation is debuggable on hardware we own and macOS is 89.3 percent of CI spend; every Windows visitor bounces uncounted."},{"k":"b","label":"macOS and Windows, with Windows signed through Azure Trusted Signing","gains":["Cheapest measured Windows route"],"costs":["The signature quota is unknown, so 119.88 is a floor"],"system":"A Windows target, a second CI path.","screens":"A second download button.","money":"119.88 USD/year on top of Apple.","what":"macOS plus Windows signed through Azure Trusted Signing at 119.88 dollars a year. The cheapest measured Windows route; the signature quota is unknown, so that price is a floor."},{"k":"c","label":"macOS and Windows, with Windows signed by an OV certificate on a token","gains":["No eligibility question to resolve first"],"costs":["219 USD a year, and a USB token CI cannot use"],"system":"Token-bound signing, which CI cannot automate.","screens":"A second button, SmartScreen warning.","money":"219 USD/year.","what":"macOS plus Windows signed with an OV certificate on a hardware token. No eligibility question to resolve; 219 dollars a year and a USB token that CI cannot hold."},{"k":"d","label":"Measure the OS mix on the site for four weeks, then choose","gains":["Turns the deciding number into data cheaply"],"costs":["Delays the desktop build by a month"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Log the operating system mix on the site for four weeks and choose from the number. Cheap and it settles the order; the desktop build waits a month."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One person can debug notarisation on the Mac we have, but not SmartScreen reputation.","macOS is 89.3% of per-release CI cost. Correct the plan's Windows price too."],"flip":"The OS mix comes back Windows-first, which the research says reverses the order.","linked":["AC10","AC18","AC4","AC8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json","package.json:24","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:43","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:53","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:125","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:137","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/35-platform-surfaces.md:324","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:17","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:37","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:79","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:131","merged: AC7"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"The desktop app is outside the first build.","recWhy":"The plan promises three installers and a Windows signing price of 150 to 400 dollars a year that matches nothing measured. The cheapest certificate found costs 219 dollars a year and needs a hardware token, and Azure Trusted Signing is 119.88 with an unknown signature quota. The build targets are dmg and app only, and package.json has no Windows or Linux target, so the promise is on paper. One person can debug notarisation on the Mac we own; nobody can debug SmartScreen reputation, which is earned by install volume and not by a certificate. macOS is 89.3 percent of per-release CI cost already. So v1 is macOS only, said plainly on the download page, and the plan's Windows price gets corrected in the same edit. This sits at not now because Q3 puts the desktop surface after the web editor. If the OS mix on the site comes back Windows-first, the research says the order reverses."},{"id":"AC10","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Updates","weight":"high","q":"Does the desktop app ship an auto-updater, and is the endpoint a Cloudflare Worker or a static file?","lede":"The signing key is unrecoverable, one person holds it, and the endpoint decides whether a release can be undone.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"No updater","meta":"where we are today","tone":"neutral","current":true},{"label":"Key generated","meta":"first public build, three copies held","via":"ship","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Updates flowing","meta":"one signed manifest per release","via":"release","tone":"good"},{"label":"Key lost","meta":"installed users unreachable, permanently","via":"one slip","tone":"stop"}],"note":"There is no revocation, no rotation and no second signer in the manifest format.","caption":"The one transition in this lifecycle with no way back."},"stakes":"Lose the key and every installed user can never be updated again.","state":["The plan requires a signed updater before the first public build.","Nothing is built: `tauri-plugin-updater` is absent and no endpoint is named."],"tension":["Signature checks cannot be switched off, so a lost key strands every user.","A static manifest cannot roll back: the app refuses any lower version."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The key liability, from Tauri's own docs via k9","items":[["Signature verification","cannot be disabled","k9:93: the key is mandatory once the updater ships"],["Lost private key","installed users can never be updated again","k9:101: no revocation, no rotation, no second signer in the manifest format"],["Where the key may live","env vars only, .env files do not work","k9:102, TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY / _PASSWORD"],["Recommended custody","3 independent copies","k9:103: password manager plus two offline media in two locations, plus the CI secret"],["Manifest validation order","whole file validated before the version check","k9:96: one malformed platform entry breaks updates for every platform"],["The asymmetry, stated","signingIdentity null is recoverable; a lost updater key is not","k9:105"],["Installed base at pilot","10","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Static manifest against a dynamic endpoint (k9:99, k9:143)","cols":["","Static JSON on R2","Worker in front of R2"],"rows":[["Can serve a lower version (rollback)","No, the client refuses it","Yes, via version_comparator"],["Retrofit cost","Ship a new build to the stranded population","n/a"],["Egress","Free","Free"],["Operational surface","A file","One more deployable"],["Failure mode","Forward-only fixes; a bad release is fixed only by a newer release","A Worker outage stops update checks"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Hosting cost either way","items":[["R2 storage","0.015 USD/GB-month; 10 GB-month free","fetched 2026-08-29"],["R2 egress","free, all storage classes","which is what makes download volume irrelevant"],["Artifact volume, 3 platforms × ~15 MB × 104 releases","4.68 GB/year cumulative","k9:55 [derived]; artifact size is an assumption, not a measurement"],["Manifest reads (Class B)","0.36 USD/million; 10M free","fetched"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No auto-updater in v1: an in-app version check that opens the download page","gains":["No key, no manifest, no permanent liability","Replaceable later without stranding anyone"],"costs":["Every update is a manual download","A support cost at ten thousand users"],"system":"One version endpoint and a link.","screens":"A one-line update notice.","money":"None.","what":"No updater. The app checks a version file and opens the download page when a newer build exists. No signing key to lose and nothing to strand anyone; every update is a manual download."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the updater against a Worker-backed endpoint from the first public build","gains":["Rollback exists the first time it is needed"],"costs":["Creates a credential whose loss strands every user"],"system":"Updater plugin, signing key, a Worker.","screens":"An update prompt and restart.","money":"Free at this volume.","what":"tauri-plugin-updater against a Cloudflare Worker endpoint from the first public build. Rollback exists the first time it is needed; a key whose loss strands every user comes with it."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship the updater against a static JSON manifest on R2","gains":["Nothing to operate, and free"],"costs":["No rollback: the client refuses a lower version"],"system":"Updater plugin, signing key, one file.","screens":"An update prompt and restart.","money":"Free.","what":"The updater against a static JSON manifest on R2. Nothing to operate and free; the client refuses any lower version, so a bad release cannot be undone."},{"k":"d","label":"Static now, a Worker later","gains":["Reads as the prudent middle"],"costs":["The no-downgrade rule is compiled in, so the later switch reaches nobody"],"system":"Same as (c), plus a missing migration.","screens":"Same as (c).","money":"Free.","what":"Static manifest now, a Worker later. Reads as the prudent middle; the no-downgrade rule is compiled into the client, so the later switch never reaches the users who need it."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The real risk is losing the key, not a bad release.","A version check that opens the download page needs no signing key."],"flip":"The user base outgrows manual re-downloads, or a second person holds the key.","linked":["AC6","AC8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","src-tauri/Cargo.toml","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:93","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:96","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:101","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:105","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:55","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:99","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:125","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:143","merged: AC11"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"The desktop app is outside the first build.","recWhy":"Tauri's updater checks signatures and that check cannot be switched off, so the signing key is a permanent liability: it is unrecoverable, one person holds it, and losing it strands every installed copy. Nothing is built, the updater plugin is absent and no endpoint is named. A static manifest cannot roll back either, because the client refuses any version lower than its own, and a Worker that can roll back still needs the key. The real risk in year one is losing the key, not shipping a bad release. An in-app version check that opens the download page needs no key, no manifest and no operations, and can be replaced with a real updater later without stranding anyone, which is the property none of the other three have. Q3 has the desktop surface arriving after the web editor. When the user base outgrows manual re-downloads, or a second person holds the key, (b) is the route."},{"id":"AC18","cat":"Access, offline & install","sub":"Desktop platforms","weight":"medium","q":"Do we commit to direct download only and rule out the Mac App Store, or design for it?","lede":"The Mac App Store's sandbox blocks the folder access the product needs, and nothing rules the store out.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"Direct download only","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Direct, bookmarks built in","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Mac App Store in v1","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"App Sandbox","cells":["None","None yet","Mandatory"]},{"label":"Home folder","cells":["Full access","Full access","Container directory only"]},{"label":"Holding a folder across launches","cells":["A path","Security-scoped bookmarks","Security-scoped bookmarks"]},{"label":"Commission","cells":["None","None","15% or standard"]},{"label":"What the choice costs","cells":["Store discovery, never quantified here","A persistence layer nobody asked for","The seam the product is built around"]}],"note":"The bookmark mechanics are marked not opened in the source, so treat the specifics as unverified.","caption":"What the store's sandbox costs the file-access design."},"stakes":"Deciding late is expensive: bookmarks change the file-access design, not a build setting.","state":["The plan names no stores, only a .dmg, .exe and AppImage.","The store requires App Sandbox, which gives the app a container, not the home folder."],"tension":["Holding a folder open across launches routes every access through saved bookmarks.","No evidence shows store discovery matters for this category."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the Mac App Store requires","items":[["App Sandbox","mandatory for MAS","fetched, Apple App Sandbox doc, via k9:135"],["Home folder access under the sandbox","not granted","container directory only"],["Holding a folder across launches","security-scoped bookmarks via open panels","the source marks the bookmark mechanics as not opened: treat specifics as unverified"],["Commission","15% under the Small Business Program, else standard","fetched 2026-08-29"],["Extra MAS build requirements","embedded.provisionprofile, bundle.category, ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption, a separate tauri.appstore.conf.json, a universal build","fetched, Tauri App Store guide"],["Guideline 4.2, verbatim","an app \"should include features, content, and UI that elevate it beyond a repackaged website\"","fetched, s4:114, and our shell loads https://md.sgnk.ai [derived from tauri.conf.json]"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Direct download only, stated on the site: no Mac App Store, no Microsoft Store","gains":["No sandbox, commission or review queue","File access stays as simple as the engine needs"],"costs":["Forfeits store discovery, never quantified","Closes a channel a managed fleet might require"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A download page naming the channel.","money":"No commission, no review cycle.","what":"Direct download only, stated on the site, with no Mac App Store or Microsoft Store listing. No sandbox, commission or review queue; store discovery is forfeited, and it was never quantified."},{"k":"b","label":"Direct download now, with security-scoped bookmarks designed in from the start","gains":["Bookmarks are cheaper designed in than retrofitted"],"costs":["More code in the part that must not be wrong"],"system":"A bookmark store in the file layer.","screens":"None visible.","money":"None.","what":"Direct download now, with security-scoped bookmarks designed in from the first version. Cheaper designed in than retrofitted; more code in the part that must not be wrong."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship to the Mac App Store in v1","gains":["Store discovery and Apple-handled payments"],"costs":["The sandbox restricts our core: file access"],"system":"A store config, universal build, bookmarks.","screens":"A store listing and folder grant.","money":"15% under the Small Business Program.","what":"Ship to the Mac App Store in v1. Store discovery and Apple-handled payments, and App Sandbox gives the app a container, not the home folder."},{"k":"d","label":"Leave it undecided","gains":["No channel ruled out"],"costs":["Reopening later means rebuilding file access"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave the channel undecided. No channel ruled out, and reopening it later means rebuilding file access around bookmarks."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The sandbox restricts file access, the one thing the product does.","No evidence shows store discovery matters here, so the constraint buys nothing measurable."],"flip":"A buyer segment that can only install from a store, such as a managed fleet.","linked":["AC6","AC8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:35","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r12/k9-desktop-distribution.md:135","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/35-platform-surfaces.md:384","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:114"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"The desktop app is outside the first build.","recWhy":"The Mac App Store requires App Sandbox, which gives the app a container rather than the home folder, and holding a folder open across launches then routes every access through security-scoped bookmarks. File access is the one thing the product does, and the engine's splice-only writer needs it plain. No evidence found shows store discovery matters for this category, so the constraint buys nothing measurable. Direct download, said on the site, and the plan already names only a .dmg, .exe and AppImage. This sits at not now because Q3 puts the desktop surface after the web editor and Q13 leaves enterprise unpriced until somebody asks. A managed fleet that can only install from a store is the one buyer that reopens it, and then (b) is the preparation, not (c)."},{"id":"MK4","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Buyer","weight":"critical","q":"Which buyer does the first version serve, and do idea mode and decision flow stay in the pilot while that is settled?","lede":"Two pilot features were validated on a buyer who runs no agent, and a third segment sits unread.","visual":{"kind":"matrix","x":["reachable in 90 days","not reachable this year"],"y":["no budget line","budget and liability"],"points":[{"label":"AI-native developer","x":0.12,"y":0.18,"us":true},{"label":"Obsidian user blocked at work","x":0.35,"y":0.3},{"label":"Startup docs owner","x":0.3,"y":0.5},{"label":"Liable services operator","x":0.75,"y":0.82},{"label":"Regulated QMS team","x":0.9,"y":0.9},{"label":"Compliance-adjacent writer","x":0.88,"y":0.75}],"note":"Persona ranking from the critique document, lines 233-242; the Obsidian segment from the segment-strategy research.","caption":"Everyone with pain and a budget is out of reach inside 90 days"},"stakes":"Choosing wrong costs the first version, not a sprint. The pilot's scope is committed in week 3.","state":["The plan's decision list puts this choice in week 1.","Idea mode and decision flow ship on founder tests with no notes attached.","A segment-strategy research note ranks Obsidian power users first, and nothing cites it."],"tension":["The segments in real pain sit outside the product: SOC 2, or `.docx`.","The developer has `git diff` and appeared 0 times in 355 high-intent comments.","The Obsidian segment gave 0 likes to review state, tracking which edits you have read, and 501 to a rendering bug."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Six personas, ranked by how badly they hurt (CRITIQUE.md:233-242)","cols":["Persona","Pain today","Verdict"],"rows":[["Compliance-adjacent writer","A signed artifact that does not match its source is an audit finding","Fire exists but is unreachable: no SOC 2, no attorney review, 9-18 month cycle"],["Liable services operator (not one of the six)","\"Two rounds of revisions\" silently becoming unbounded free labour","Fire exists but the substrate is wrong: their document is .docx, where dealfluence/adeu already ships this engine"],["Startup docs owner","Docs, changelog, roadmap drift","Warm; budgeted at $20-40/seat; needs multiplayer presence first"],["Agency lead","Notion export is 900 files of Untitled 3.md","Warm; blocked by the 83% publish refusal"],["AI-heavy knowledge worker","Unreviewable agent diffs","The record's own v1 target; its only measured instance is the founder's machine"],["Solo dev","He has git diff","Cold: 0 of 355 in the highest-intent thread"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Which archetype this is","items":[["Sequoia Arc classification","Hard Fact, not Hair on Fire","\"customers have resigned themselves to just living with the problem\" (CRITIQUE.md:244)"],["What Hard Fact requires","market education","CRITIQUE.md:244"],["Education budget today","₹0 and two blog posts","one defective since 2026-08-10 (CRITIQUE.md:244)"],["Falsifier already written","20 outreach conversations","if 5 or more name whole-file rewrites unprompted, P4 is a segment (CRITIQUE.md:246)"],["Evidence attached to the idea-mode and decision-flow founder tests","none","\"Founders' statement in chat. No notes, no participants, no protocol\" (GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The signal the segment study ranks first","items":[["Obsidian forum, \"Obsidian for web\"","246,568 views, 244 posts, 928 likes","six years, still active in 2026 (research/frontmatter-segment-strategy.md §1.1)"],["Docker pulls of linuxserver/obsidian","414,917","streaming a whole desktop over VNC into a browser tab, as a workaround"],["obsidian-git stars","11,540","README warns mobile git is \"very unstable\""],["Stated willingness-to-pay band for that segment","$2-6 per month","anchored to Obsidian Sync $4"],["The study's own caveat on that segment","\"skews free-first — treat as acquisition and evangelism more than revenue\"","§1.1 honest verdict"],["Obsidian forum likes for review state","0","251 views, 1 post in nearly three years (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"AI-native developer first; idea mode and decision flow to the build after the pilot","gains":["Consistent with the channels and the review file","Matches the buyer Q2 on the final set has already settled on, so nothing here has to be re-argued."],"costs":["Discards the only two user-tested features","Leaves the pilot on untested review state"],"system":"Idea mode and decision flow slip a tier.","screens":"Template cards and decision flow leave the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"The developer running an agent is the buyer; idea mode and decision flow move to the build after the pilot. Consistent with the channels and the review file; the pilot rests on untested review state."},{"k":"b","label":"The liable person first; keep idea mode and decision flow in the pilot","gains":["A segment with a budget line","A segment with an actual budget line, unlike the developer buyer who appears zero times in 355 comments."],"costs":["Their document is `.docx`; an MIT rival ships there","SOC 2 and a nine to eighteen month cycle"],"system":"A `.docx` path we have refused.","screens":"The repo tree becomes wrong.","money":"SOC 2 and legal review, unbudgeted.","what":"The person liable for the document is the buyer; idea mode and decision flow stay. A segment with a budget line, whose document is .docx, with a nine to eighteen month cycle and an MIT rival."},{"k":"c","label":"Put the Obsidian segment on the table as a third candidate","gains":["The largest verified pain artefact, 246,568 views","246,568 views is the single largest verified pain artefact found anywhere in this research."],"costs":["That population voted 0 for review state","Free-first, so acquisition not revenue"],"system":"A vault-shaped web path.","screens":"The launcher opens vault-first.","money":"A $2-6 band, below every planned price.","what":"Obsidian power users join as a third candidate. The largest verified pain artefact at 246,568 views, from a population that gave review state zero likes and is free-first: acquisition, not revenue."},{"k":"d","label":"Pre-register the rule: idea mode and decision flow stay; the 20 conversations decide","gains":["The only waiting that cannot be rationalised later","The pre-registered pass mark, five of twenty naming the problem unprompted, cannot be argued away after the fact."],"costs":["Week-3 scope stays conditional","Binds us to a number we may dislike"],"system":"No change until week 3.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Write the rule before the calls: idea mode and decision flow stay, and the twenty conversations decide the buyer against a pass mark set in advance. Week-3 scope stays conditional on a number."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Weeks 1-2 are reserved for 20 conversations, and the pass mark is already set: five unprompted mentions.","Deciding now means choosing between personas whose only measured instance is the founder's own machine."],"flip":"If fewer than five of twenty name whole-file rewrites unprompted, option b is forced.","linked":["MK16","MK31","PR1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59-60","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:366","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:52","docs/CRITIQUE.md:233","docs/CRITIQUE.md:242","docs/CRITIQUE.md:244","docs/CRITIQUE.md:246","docs/research/frontmatter-segment-strategy.md","merged: MK24"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan names people who build with coding agents. Confirm the buyer before anything is written for them.","recWhy":"Two pilot features were validated on a buyer who runs no agent, the developer appeared zero times in 355 high-intent comments, and the Obsidian population gave review state zero likes and a rendering bug 501. Every persona here has one measured instance, the founder's own machine, so choosing now is guessing. Q2 gives the working position: the team running coding agents, and the organisation above it, with the individual as the same artefact at one file. That is the hypothesis the twenty conversations test, and the pass mark is already written, five unprompted mentions of whole-file rewrites. Pre-registering is the only form of waiting that cannot be rationalised afterwards, so this card stays (d): the rule is fixed first, and Q2 is what it tests. Fewer than five of twenty forces (b), the liable person, and that is written down before the first call."},{"id":"MK16","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Size","weight":"critical","q":"Do we accept that this is a few-hundred-customer business, or change the denominator before building?","lede":"Two independent routes land on the same buyer population, one eighth of the revenue goal the corpus carries.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Qualified humans at a 5x private multiplier","n":81000,"unit":"people","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Payers, optimistic (20% switch x 8% pay)","n":1296,"unit":"payers","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Payers, central (10% x 5%)","n":405,"unit":"payers","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Payers, pessimistic (2% x 2.5%)","n":41,"unit":"payers","tone":"stop"}],"note":"The ₹20 lakh a month figure carried elsewhere needs 3,339 payers. The central case is 12.1% of it.","caption":"Bottom-up from live GitHub counts, with the switch and pay rates swept"},"stakes":"This decides whether frontmatter is a product funded by services or a company.","state":["Monthly running costs are about ₹1.09 lakh, covered by 54-78 consulting hours.","The plan states no target customer count anywhere.","The central case of 405 payers covers those costs with ₹46,400 spare."],"tension":["Spontaneous concern is 0.032%, so a 2% switch rate is 62 times the signal.","At 4% monthly churn the paying base levels off at 804 and stays there.","Six firms publish note-taking market sizes that disagree 8.2x for one year, so quote none."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Buyer population, four scenarios, against what the revenue goal needs","note":"Bottom-up from api.github.com 2026-08-31, sensitivity on the private-repo multiplier and the switch and pay rates. CRITIQUE.md:281-286","data":[["Pessimistic (2% switch x 2.5% pay)",41],["Central (10% x 5%)",405],["Optimistic (20% x 8%)",1296],["Generous (20x private multiplier)",5184]],"unit":"paying customers","threshold":3339,"thresholdLabel":"payers needed for the ₹20L/month goal"},{"type":"stat","title":"The two independent routes, and the constraints on them","items":[["Bottom-up qualified population at a 5x private multiplier","81,000 humans","10,800 repos x 5 x 1.5 humans (CRITIQUE.md:277)"],["Top-down cross-check, generous end","5,160 payers","against bottom-up 5,184; the two routes agree within 0.5% (CRITIQUE.md:288)"],["Measured spontaneous-concern rate","0.032%","even a 2% switch rate is 62x the observed signal (CRITIQUE.md:279)"],["Central case against the ₹20L/month goal","12.1%","CRITIQUE.md:284, :293"],["Churn ceiling at c=4%/month","the stock asymptotes at 804 payers forever","inside the TAM band (CRITIQUE.md:295)"],["Central case against the ₹1.09L/month nut","clears it with ₹46,400 spare","\"a real, if small, business\" (CRITIQUE.md:292)"],["What the 1.26M visitor requirement actually is","a 24-to-36-month cumulative figure, not year one","and the plan is \"missing at least one channel of a size nobody has yet named\" (BUSINESS.md:941, :1086)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Accept the small shape: a few hundred customers, funded by services","gains":["Covers the monthly running cost with room","Covers the ₹1.09 lakh monthly cost with ₹46,400 to spare, on the plan's own central case."],"costs":["The ₹20 lakh a month goal and the 1.26M-visitor funnel go","Forecloses any outside funding conversation"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Caps the business near its running cost.","what":"Accept the small shape: a few hundred customers funded by services. Covers the ₹1.09 lakh monthly cost with room; forecloses the ₹20 lakh goal, the 1.26 million visitor funnel and any funding talk."},{"k":"b","label":"Change the denominator: sell to teams at a seat price","gains":["27,778 seats at $30 reaches the goal","27,778 seats at $30 a seat is the one arithmetic path in the corpus that reaches the ₹20 lakh goal."],"costs":["The three-inbound-teams trigger has fired zero times","Nothing in Pro has been paid for"],"system":"Seat counting, team boundaries, invoicing.","screens":"A team admin surface that does not exist.","money":"Higher ceiling, no evidence.","what":"Change the denominator: sell to the team running agents at a seat price. 27,778 seats at $30 reaches the goal; the three-inbound-teams trigger has fired zero times and Pro has never been paid for."},{"k":"c","label":"Measure the private-repo multiplier before accepting either number","gains":["It is the difference between 41 and 5,184","Answers the one multiplier the corpus itself calls the difference between 41 and 5,184 possible customers."],"costs":["The corpus marks it unmeasurable from outside","The measurement may not exist"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Measure the private-repo multiplier before accepting either number. It is the difference between 41 and 5,184, and the corpus marks it unmeasurable from outside."},{"k":"d","label":"Reject the gating assumption: the population is anyone with markdown in a repo","gains":["Every number rises if it is true","Every revenue number in the plan rises at once if this wider population turns out to be the real one."],"costs":["The wider set has `git diff`","Five docs-drift launches scored 1, 2, 2, 2 and 4"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Reject the gating assumption: anyone with markdown in a repo is the population. Every number rises if true; that wider set has git diff, and five docs-drift launches scored 1, 2, 2, 2 and 4."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Q2 moves the buyer to the team running coding agents and the organisation above it, which is a different population.","60,000 projects carry AGENTS.md, one org holds 88 files, and the vendor documents an org-wide policy file with no tooling."],"flip":"If three of the twenty teams say the which-file-wins view told them nothing, the denominator collapses back to (a).","linked":["MK29","MK4","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/CRITIQUE.md:266","docs/CRITIQUE.md:277","docs/CRITIQUE.md:279","docs/CRITIQUE.md:281","docs/CRITIQUE.md:284","docs/CRITIQUE.md:288","docs/CRITIQUE.md:292","docs/CRITIQUE.md:295","docs/BUSINESS.md:941","docs/BUSINESS.md:1086"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The size of the business is judged after the pilot gives a number.","recWhy":"Two independent routes land on the same buyer population, one eighth of the revenue goal the corpus carries. A central case of 405 payers, levelling off at 804 at 4 percent monthly churn, covers ₹1.09 lakh of monthly costs with ₹46,400 spare. Spontaneous concern is 0.032 percent, so any assumed switch rate dwarfs the signal. Q2 changes the denominator rather than the arithmetic. The buyer is the team running coding agents and the organisation above it, 60,000 projects carrying AGENTS.md, one organisation holding 88 files, a vendor documenting an org-wide policy file with no tooling around it. The cost: the three-inbound-teams trigger has fired zero times and nothing in Pro has ever been paid for. Six firms publish note-taking market sizes that disagree 8.2 times, so none is quoted. Three of twenty teams saying the which-file-wins view told them nothing collapses this back to (a)."},{"id":"MK27","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Precedent","weight":"critical","q":"Do we name Almanac in the pitch, or wait for someone in the room to name it for us?","lede":"Almanac shipped our whole feature sentence, raised $45M, and switched off on 31 January 2025.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Almanac, at its height","lines":[{"t":"Read Receipts","mark":"ok"},{"t":"Version Control Mode, Layers, Approvals","mark":"ok"},{"t":"Doc History diffing, deprecation reviews","mark":"ok"},{"t":"$9M seed, $34M Series A, Tiger Global","mark":"ok"},{"t":"7-figure ARR, 50+ staff","mark":"ok"}]},"after":{"title":"get.almanac.io today","lines":[{"t":"Shut down 2025-01-31","mark":"x"},{"t":"Cause stated as capacity, not demand","mark":"!"},{"t":"Their AI content product took the team","mark":"!"},{"t":"The feature list is still on the live site","mark":"!"}]},"via":"four years","note":"Their 2020 Show HN, \"GitHub for Docs\", scored 6 points and drew no comments.","caption":"Our feature sentence, funded to $45M, switched off"},"stakes":"Almanac already failed at the read-receipt half with $45M, and anyone who knows the space will raise it first.","state":["The headline is review state across people and tools, knowing who has read which change. That is Almanac's Read Receipts.","Almanac appears zero times in the product plan, market table included.","Their farewell page states the cause as capacity, not demand."],"tension":["Capacity is the honest reason or the polite one. Nothing opened settles it.","The general form is what Almanac sold. The agent-scoped, in-repo form is not.","$45M and fifty people did not make the general form work."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Almanac, from opened sources","items":[["Features still listed on the live site","Read Receipts, Version Control Mode, Layers, Approvals, Doc History diffing","get.almanac.io"],["Funding","$9M seed plus a $34M Series A led by Tiger Global, September 2021","thesaasnews.com"],["Scale before shutdown","7-figure ARR, 50+ staff","saasclub.io podcast summary, a secondary source, not the founder's writing"],["Shutdown date","2025-01-31","get.almanac.io/go-forward"],["Cause as stated","capacity, not demand","their AI content product \"grew to tens of thousands of users in a matter of months\""],["Their 2020 Show HN, \"GitHub for Docs\"","6 points, no comments","hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/23339147"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Name Almanac first, in the plan and in the pitch, with the narrowing it forces","gains":["Turns the strongest objection into evidence we read","Forces the exact narrowing the headline decision needs, before a sceptic forces it in a worse room."],"costs":["Puts a $45M failure before every reader","Invites why two succeed where fifty did not"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Name Almanac first, in the plan and in the pitch, with the narrowing it forces on the headline. Turns the strongest objection into evidence we read; puts a $45 million failure before every reader."},{"k":"b","label":"Add it to the market table only, not to the pitch","gains":["An honest table for the cost of one row","Costs one row in a table nobody outside the company reads before a pitch meeting."],"costs":["A sceptic still catches us flat in a meeting","The narrowing it forces goes unmade"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Add Almanac to the market table only, not the pitch. An honest table for one row; a sceptic still catches us flat in a meeting and the narrowing goes unmade."},{"k":"c","label":"Say nothing and answer if asked","gains":["Nothing to explain unprompted","Costs nothing to prepare, and most rooms may simply never raise a five-year-old shutdown unprompted."],"costs":["Silence reads as not having looked","We lose the framing of the answer"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Say nothing and answer if asked. Nothing to explain unprompted; silence reads as not having looked, and we lose the framing of the answer."},{"k":"d","label":"Treat the shutdown as a demand verdict and drop the review-state direction","gains":["Takes the strongest negative precedent seriously","Takes the single strongest negative precedent in the whole research corpus at full weight, not partial weight."],"costs":["The stated cause is capacity, uncontradicted","A fifth pivot on a single data point"],"system":"Review state leaves the plan.","screens":"The whole review surface.","money":"None.","what":"Treat the shutdown as a demand verdict and abandon review state. Takes the strongest precedent seriously; the stated cause is capacity, uncontradicted, and it is a fifth pivot on one point."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Two adversarial rounds missed a $45M precedent, and that blind spot is the bigger problem.","Naming it first is also the cheapest way to force the narrowing the headline decision needs."],"flip":"If a former Almanac customer or employee says demand was the real cause, option d is on the table.","linked":["MK16","MK29","P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:145-160","https://get.almanac.io/","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward","https://www.thesaasnews.com/news/almanac-raises-34-million-in-series-a/","https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/23339147"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Naming a dead competitor is pitch copy.","recWhy":"Almanac shipped our whole feature sentence, read receipts included, raised $45 million, and switched off on 31 January 2025, and two adversarial rounds missed it; it appears zero times in the product plan. The blind spot is the bigger problem, and naming the precedent first is the cheapest way to force the narrowing the headline needs. The general form, review state on any document for any team, is what Almanac sold and fifty people could not make work. The in-repo form on the files agents obey is not, and Q1 and Q6 state that narrower thing. Their farewell page states capacity, not demand, and nothing opened settles whether that is the honest reason or the polite one, and the card says so. It also answers why two people succeed where fifty did not, before the question is asked. A former Almanac customer or employee saying demand was the real cause puts (d) back on the table.","dupes":{"dropped":["P23"],"areas":["Product & definition"],"why":"Product & definition asked about Almanac and Zed Delta together and recommended naming both first, unprompted, with dates and stated causes. For Almanac that is option a here. How Delta is answered is on the Engine & technical card about the CRDT rebuttal.","agree":true}},{"id":"MK7","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Channels","weight":"high","q":"Two channels were cut on measured evidence, and six places in the plan still ship them. What replaces each?","lede":"The Obsidian plugin and the Chrome extension are marked CUT in the channel list and still relied on in five other places.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"The current plan as published","lines":[{"t":"Channel list: Obsidian plugin CUT","mark":"x"},{"t":"Tier table: plugin is the week-2 channel","mark":"!"},{"t":"Ninety days: \"plugin shipped\" in weeks 1-2","mark":"!"},{"t":"Retention table: plugin is a retention lever","mark":"!"},{"t":"Product family: plugin and Chrome extension ship","mark":"!"},{"t":"Rendering: nested fix ships first as the plugin","mark":"!"}]},"after":{"title":"One coherent plan","lines":[{"t":"Additive review-state CodeMirror extension","mark":"+"},{"t":"?src= parameter with a written next action","mark":"+"},{"t":"Chrome extension deleted from the product family","mark":"x"},{"t":"Retention lever replaced or struck","mark":"x"}]},"via":"one edit","note":"All six locations verified in the current plan.","caption":"Both cuts are correct; the document has not caught up with either"},"stakes":"A plan that cuts a channel on one page and schedules it on four is not trusted.","state":["The plan's channel list cuts both the Obsidian plugin and the Chrome extension.","The plugin still ships in five other sections: tiers, the ninety days, retention, the product family and rendering.","The product-family table still lists the Chrome extension in the build after the pilot."],"tension":["Obsidian's Live Preview syntax tree is one level deep by design, verified twice.","The two plugins that do fix nesting write an invisible `U+200C` character into user files.","A `?src=` link that opens a public file in the web app is a demo, not a channel, until its next step is written."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The same document, five places, two answers","cols":["Location","What it says"],"rows":[["Exhibit 17, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","Free Obsidian plugin: cut. The parse is unreachable"],["Exhibit 6, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","Channels: free Obsidian live-preview plugin, week 2; kill under 200 installs in 14 days"],["Exhibit 19, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","Weeks 1-2 work: \"plugin shipped\""],["Exhibit 24, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","Retention lever: the Obsidian live-preview fix stays installed in a vault they open daily"],["Exhibit 26, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","Family member: Chrome extension, Obsidian plugin, free; channels; the build after the pilot, week 2"],["§21, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","Nested constructs render correctly (the 501-like bug, shipped first as the free plugin)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why round 2 cut it","items":[["Obsidian Live Preview parse tree depth","1, by construction","a CodeMirror StreamLanguage tree; \"this fence is inside a list item\" does not exist in it (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)"],["What a plugin can change","appearance only","on undocumented DOM classes Obsidian has already deleted once (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)"],["What the two plugins that do change nested behaviour write into user files","U+200C","which a byte-exact product cannot ship (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)"],["Closest measured analogue, first 14 days","115 installs","against our own 200 kill line (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)"],["Obsidian live-preview changelog lines in 2025","9 across 5 of 12 months","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304. Flagged: CRITIQUE.md:362 measures the same surface as \"51 live preview hits in the 142 releases of 2025-26\" and calls Obsidian \"still actively fixing\" it, two readings of one question, unreconciled"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why the extension was cut","items":[["Claimed browser reading population","1.5M","summed a 500,000-user reader with a 1,000,000-user clipper (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["Requests to read a .md URL in the clipper's tracker","0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"],["Real reading population","~647,000","its leader has not shipped a store build in over two years (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["raw.githubusercontent.com CORS header","access-control-allow-origin: *","so the web app needs no extension (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["Cost of the replacement","hours","no store listing, no Manifest V3 (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)"],["What GitHub already ships at that exact surface","rendered markdown, rich diff, per-file Viewed state, free","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Accept both cuts and delete both from all six places","gains":["One coherent document","Leaves one coherent document instead of six places quietly disagreeing with the plan's own channel table."],"costs":["Retention loses a lever, the family its channel","The pilot has no channel before superpowers"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Both cuts stand and both surfaces come out of all six places: tiers, the ninety days, retention, the product family, rendering and the later build. One coherent document; retention loses a lever."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the additive Obsidian extension and a `?src=` link with a stated job","gains":["Needs no nesting information, and reuses the review file","Costs no nesting fix, since the additive extension never needs Obsidian's one-level-deep parse tree."],"costs":["A different product in that vault, never sized","That audience voted 0 for review state"],"system":"A CodeMirror decoration drawn from the review file.","screens":"Adds a read view and drops the plugin screens.","money":"None.","what":"An additive Obsidian extension needing no nesting information, plus a ?src= link with a stated job. Reuses the review file; a second product in a vault whose audience voted zero for review state."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship `?src=` only and drop every Obsidian surface","gains":["Hours of work, no store listing","The cheapest option in hours, since it needs no store listing and no plugin review at all."],"costs":["Abandons the largest measured demand signal","Still no week-2 channel we own"],"system":"Hours.","screens":"A read view on the web app.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the ?src= link only and no Obsidian surface at all. Hours of work and no store listing; it abandons the largest measured demand signal and still leaves week 2 without a channel we own."},{"k":"d","label":"Reject the cuts and spike the plugin again","gains":["Would restore the plan as written","Would restore the plan exactly as written, if the spike's result were ever going to change."],"costs":["The spike returned negative twice","A day cannot change the one-level-deep finding"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"A day of the 1.21 engineering days a week.","what":"Reject both cuts and spike the plugin a third time. Restores the plan as written; the spike returned negative twice and a day cannot change Obsidian's one-level-deep syntax tree."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["An additive Obsidian extension is a second surface against Q3, one surface first. Both cuts stand.","Delete both from all six places; a ?src= link can return later with a stated job and no Obsidian behind it."],"flip":"If a pilot person cannot use the product without Obsidian, the additive route is re-costed against Phase 2.","linked":["MK4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:524","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:62","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127","merged: MK8"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Two cut channels are still promised in six places. Which surface replaces each has four real answers, so it waits for the pilot.","recWhy":"The channel list cuts the Obsidian plugin and the Chrome extension, and five other sections still ship them, so the plan contradicts itself in six places. The technical finding behind the cut is settled. Obsidian's Live Preview syntax tree is one level deep by design, verified twice, and the two plugins that do fix nesting write an invisible U+200C character into user files, which is exactly the thing this product exists to refuse. An additive extension would be a second surface, and Q3 puts one surface first with everything else waiting on the same tree. So both cuts stand and both names come out of all six places. A ?src= link that opens a public file in the web app is a demo until its next step is written; it can return later with a stated job and no Obsidian behind it. If a pilot person cannot use the product without Obsidian, the additive route is re-costed against Phase 2."},{"id":"MK12","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Competitors","weight":"high","q":"Do we let the measured ceiling in this niche stand, or open the products behind it first?","lede":"Thirteen extensions and seven plugins sit in this niche and nobody in the corpus has opened one.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"All 13 markdown-review extensions","n":1217,"unit":"installs","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Ceiling for any single one","n":400,"unit":"installs","tone":"warn"},{"label":"mddiff: our exact sentence, June 2026","n":74,"unit":"installs","tone":"stop"}],"note":"In Obsidian, Drift does the same job at 893 downloads against a 143 million download registry.","caption":"The VS Code niche enumerated, ending at our own sentence"},"stakes":"The difference between a market ceiling and thirteen bad listings decides how much weight this bears.","state":["The plan's key figures lead with 1,217 installs across 13 extensions, with a ceiling of 400.","The plan's closing section reads the same number as a niche that is real and available.","Drift does this job at 893 downloads and is missing from the count."],"tension":["One number drives two opposite conclusions inside one document.","A ceiling and a distribution failure look identical from outside. Half a day separates them.","human-review reached 1,241 stars in six weeks, so distribution is not always the constraint."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The niche, enumerated","items":[["Purpose-built markdown-review extensions in the VS Code Marketplace","13","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["Their combined installs","1,217","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9"],["Ceiling for any single one","400","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9"],["mddiff, our sentence shipped 2026-06-03","74 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24"],["Obsidian plugins in the same niche","7, none above 1,540 downloads","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559"],["SpecKit Companion, the nearest shipped review-file pattern","9,367 installs","review state in .spec-context.json, spec-kit only (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135)"],["git-ai, attribution after the fact","61,517 installs","commit granularity, nothing per span (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:151)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The Obsidian registry, by what the plugin does (CRITIQUE.md:350-359)","cols":["Plugin","What it does","Downloads","Stars"],"rows":[["Obsidian Git","the JTBD incumbent, already installed","3,075,875","none"],["Claudian","embeds Claude Code and other agents in the vault","1,958,668","15,064"],["Copilot (Brevilabs)","runs agents inside the vault, paid tiers","1,783,652","7,651"],["Tag Wrangler","tag rename and merge","1,067,863","none"],["Global Search and Replace","vault-wide replace","65,569","none"],["Drift","detects external file changes, side-by-side diffs per chunk","893","8"],["Schema Refactor","renames properties across frontmatter","66","none"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The same number used two ways in one document","cols":["Location","Reading"],"rows":[["Key figures, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9","1,217 total, ceiling 400: the headline did not survive"],["§1 risk callout, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","mddiff is this sentence, shipped three months ago, at 74 installs"],["§23 Opportunities, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559","\"Real, narrow, and small\": thirteen extensions and seven plugins already sit in it, none above 1,540"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Treat 400 as the ceiling and publish it as the threshold to beat","gains":["Makes the direction testable rather than arguable","Costs nothing to publish: the 400-install figure already exists in the plan's own key figures."],"costs":["Prices success below the monthly nut","Passing it would still not be a business"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"A published threshold below the nut.","what":"Publish 400 installs as the ceiling and the threshold to beat. Makes the direction testable; it prices success below the monthly running cost, so passing it would still not be a business."},{"k":"b","label":"Open the top three extensions and Drift before letting the number count","gains":["Separates a ceiling from twenty bad listings","Half a day settles a question the plan currently answers two different ways in two sections."],"costs":["It may return that they are good and unwanted","Half a day, at 1.21 engineering days a week"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Install and open the top three extensions and Drift before the number is allowed to count. Half a day at 1.21 engineering days a week, and it separates a real ceiling from twenty bad listings."},{"k":"c","label":"Discount the number because none of them does the cross-person, in-repo form","gains":["Consistent with the narrowed headline","Matches the narrowed headline exactly: none of the thirteen do the cross-person, in-repo form at all."],"costs":["Every one of the thirteen could have said it","Unfalsifiable without users"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Discount the 1,217 because none of the twenty does the cross-person, in-repo form. Consistent with the narrowed headline, and unfalsifiable: every one of the thirteen could have said the same."},{"k":"d","label":"Pick one reading and stop publishing both","gains":["Removes a contradiction on page one","The cheapest fix available: one editorial choice, no research and no half-day spent opening products."],"costs":["Does not decide whether the ceiling is real"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Pick one reading of the number and stop publishing both. Removes a contradiction from page one, and does not decide whether the ceiling is real."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Nobody in this corpus has opened any of the twenty, and one half-day covers both stores.","Twenty bad listings would leave the direction open, while a real ceiling would close it."],"flip":"If the leading extensions are well-made, well-listed and still under 400, the ceiling is real.","linked":[],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:24","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:151","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:559","docs/CRITIQUE.md:350","docs/CRITIQUE.md:353","docs/CRITIQUE.md:355","docs/CRITIQUE.md:356","docs/CRITIQUE.md:367","docs/CRITIQUE.md:436","https://api.github.com/repos/petergyang/human-review","merged: MK19"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The ceiling matters when we are selling into it.","recWhy":"One number, 1,217 installs across thirteen extensions with a ceiling of 400, drives two opposite conclusions inside one document: the key figures call it a ceiling and the closing section a niche that is real and available. Drift does the same job at 893 downloads and is missing from the count. Nobody in the corpus has opened any of the twenty products, and a ceiling and a distribution failure look identical from outside. Half a day covers both stores. Twenty bad listings would leave the direction open; well-made, well-listed products still under 400 would close it. This sits after the pilot because Q2 has moved the buyer to the team running agents, where the comparable set is Spec Kit and Kiro rather than these extensions, so the reading changes the pitch, not the build. human-review reached 1,241 stars in six weeks, so distribution is not always the constraint."},{"id":"MK13","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Incumbents","weight":"high","q":"What is the pre-committed response when a platform ships the half we are building?","lede":"Four platforms shipped agent-change review in nine weeks. The plan states what they have not built.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2026-07-03","label":"VS Code merges review/unreview","note":"PR #324218, milestone 1.128.0, undocumented","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-07-19","label":"VS Code markdown feedback editor","note":"issues #326539 and #326540, milestone 1.130.0","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-24","label":"Antigravity ships Agent Edits pane","note":"agent edits, uncommitted and branch scopes","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-28","label":"Notion ships per-change approval","note":"agent edits approved one change at a time","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-09-03","label":"Claude Code v2.1.260 diff panel","note":"opens itself, three scopes, no reviewed marker","tone":"stop"}],"note":"The VS Code work is gated to an experimental Agents window and the live docs page documents neither feature.","caption":"Nine weeks of absorption; the plan watches one vendor"},"stakes":"The business plan's war-game gives this the shortest warning, three to nine months, and the plan has no written response.","state":["The risk table says re-check at week 12, and the plan's closing section says monthly.","The response in both is one sentence: persistence across sessions, people and tools.","The war-game's pre-committed response sits in the business plan, outside the product plan."],"tension":["The named trigger has partly fired: a first-party surface writing to a user path.","Re-checking one changelog is wrong when four shipped, one of them Microsoft.","The war-game names its own failure mode: refusing to delete features when the trigger fires."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What Anthropic already ships","items":[["Claude Code VS Code extension installs","24.89M","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:150"],["Plans as a markdown document with inline comments","since 2.1.70, 2026-03-06","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:150"],["Persisted unread state","shipping","2.1.261 fixes a missing unread dot and the tab badge showing unread (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560)"],["Diff panel","2.1.260","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560"],["Open request for a diff-review UI","262 reactions","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:150"],["What is unbuilt by them","persistence across people and tools","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560"],["The war-game's named trigger to watch","a first-party surface that writes to a user-owned path","BUSINESS.md:1152, :1236"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two re-check cadences in the same document","cols":["Location","Cadence","Response stated"],"rows":[["Exhibit 18 risks, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:322","week 12","\"persistence across sessions, people and tools is what they do not build\""],["§23 Threats, PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560","monthly, not at week 12","same sentence"],["War-game scenario D, BUSINESS.md:1148-1157","3-9 months, shortest fuse on the list","stop building what a vendor could ship; reposition as the verification layer under their output; ship the certificate as an MCP tool they can call"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Monthly check across all four platforms, with the written response","gains":["The response is chosen while it is cheap","Reconciles the plan's own two different re-check cadences, week 12 and monthly, into one."],"costs":["Commits us in advance to deleting features","Recurring work at 1.21 engineering days a week"],"system":"Constrains the roadmap.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A monthly check across all four platforms with the written response beside it: which features get deleted when the trigger fires. Chosen while cheap; recurring work at 1.21 engineering days a week."},{"k":"b","label":"Weekly changelog check, no pre-committed response","gains":["Cheapest possible early signal","Cheapest possible early-warning signal, needing only a weekly look at four public changelogs."],"costs":["The warning arrives and nothing happens","The failure mode the war-game names"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A weekly changelog check and no pre-committed response. The cheapest early signal, and the failure mode the war-game names: the warning arrives and nothing happens."},{"k":"c","label":"Week 12 only, as the risk table says","gains":["Least effort","Matches the risk table exactly as written today, with no new recurring work added."],"costs":["Four platforms shipped inside nine weeks","A changelog line can land in any month"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Re-check at week 12 only, as the risk table says. The least effort; four platforms shipped inside nine weeks and a changelog line can land in any month."},{"k":"d","label":"Concede the working-tree half and build only the cross-person, in-repo half","gains":["Removes the dependency on four roadmaps","Removes the dependency on four separate companies' roadmaps deciding this product's own timeline."],"costs":["Shrinks the pilot to the least-evidenced part","Nothing to demo a free panel lacks"],"system":"Review state's diff view becomes a read of the host editor's diff.","screens":"The panel keeps the counter, loses the diff.","money":"None.","what":"Concede the working-tree half and build only the cross-person, in-repo half. Removes the dependency on four roadmaps; shrinks the pilot to the least-evidenced part."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Monthly matches a fuse measured at three to nine months and reconciles two cadences.","If the deletion is decided in advance, a small team can make it faster than a platform can ship."],"flip":"If the headline decision settles on the cross-person, in-repo form, options d and a converge.","linked":["MK17"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:150","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:322","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560","docs/BUSINESS.md:1148","docs/BUSINESS.md:1152","docs/BUSINESS.md:1154","docs/BUSINESS.md:1156","docs/BUSINESS.md:1236","https://www.notion.com/releases","https://antigravity.google/blog/vcs-and-terminal","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anthropics/claude-code/main/CHANGELOG.md"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A platform response plan is for when there is something to defend.","recWhy":"Four platforms shipped agent-change review in nine weeks, one of them Microsoft, and the named trigger has partly fired: a first-party surface writing to a user path. The plan says re-check at week 12 in one place and monthly in another, and the pre-committed response lives in the business plan, outside the product plan, as one sentence. Monthly matches a fuse measured at three to nine months and reconciles the two cadences, and the point of writing the response now is that a small team can delete features faster than a platform can ship if the deletion was decided in advance. Q6 has already moved the differentiator to the instruction-file view and read-only review across a repository, which is the cross-person, in-repo half, so (d) and (a) have largely converged, as this card's flip predicted. The monthly check now watches whether a platform ships that half too."},{"id":"MK17","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Evidence","weight":"high","q":"What standing competitive discipline do we keep, and who owns it?","lede":"Three published numbers have been withdrawn, and nothing says when any market-table row is read again.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Fetched","meta":"with a date","via":"","tone":"good"},{"label":"Quoted","meta":"date dropped","via":"in a doc","tone":"neutral","current":true},{"label":"Stale","meta":"nobody notices","via":"weeks pass","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Withdrawn","meta":"three so far","via":"a control","tone":"stop"}],"note":"62% of the record's load-bearing claims needed correction on first primary-source check.","caption":"How every number in the market table has actually decayed"},"stakes":"Without a discipline the table decays silently and the next correction happens in a meeting.","state":["The market table carries one date for the whole table, and no row has its own read date.","The risk table is ten one-line risks, and the business plan's war-game is not carried into it.","The war-game's table of eight early-warning signals is nowhere in the plan."],"tension":["The war-game rates \"the category never forms\" as most likely, and the plan omits it.","Seven of eight signals watch someone else. The eighth does not exist.","A cadence nobody keeps is worse than none, because readers assume the rows are fresh."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The war-game's six scenarios, and their lead times (BUSINESS.md:1111-1185)","cols":["Scenario","Lead time","Still applies to the review-state thesis?"],"rows":[["A · Obsidian ships conflict review","6-12 months from trigger; the setting shipped 2025-08-18","Partly: it is about sync, not review state"],["B · Notion or Coda makes files on disk the store of record","18-36 months; base rate is never","Yes, unchanged"],["C · GitHub ships a real editor over repo markdown","12-24 months","Yes, and stronger: GitHub already ships Viewed state free"],["D · A model vendor ships a filesystem-backed document surface","3-9 months, the shortest fuse","Yes, and it has partly fired: persisted unread state is shipping"],["E · A funded rival ships the same thesis with eight people","6-9 months to parity, 0 to the claim","Yes, unchanged"],["F · Nobody moves and the category never forms","no trigger; highest probability","Yes, and it is the one the current risk table omits"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the instrumentation table found","items":[["Signals in the table","8","BUSINESS.md:1229-1238"],["Signals that watch someone else","7","recoverable by publishing a measurement or deleting a roadmap lane (BUSINESS.md:1240)"],["Signals that watch us","1","zero paid checkouts after 60 days on our own funnel"],["Whether that one exists","no","\"our own funnel — does not exist yet\" (BUSINESS.md:1238)"],["Scenarios recoverable in under a fortnight by one person","A through E","BUSINESS.md:1240"],["Scenarios not recoverable at all","F","and it is the most likely"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The three withdrawn numbers, and what replaced each (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:176-178)","cols":["Withdrawn","Why","Replacement"],"rows":[["62,976 unanswered markers","49,408 were spec-kit's own checklist line","about 1,500 live markers; later measured at 3,368 files, 42% live in a 50-file sample (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58)"],["496,000 stars as channel size","stars measure attention to a CLI","active-repo counts: spec-kit 11,072 repos / ~1,660 active; superpowers 75-85k / 22-30k"],["\"Uncopyable\"","trailers and git-ai attribute at commit level","struck"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Monthly re-read of the market table, fetch date on every row, plus the eight signals","gains":["Turns a snapshot into a series","Turns a single dated snapshot into a running series a reader can trust to still be current."],"costs":["Recurring work at 1.21 engineering days a week","A missed cadence reads as freshness"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A monthly re-read of the market table, a fetch date on every row, and the war-game's eight early-warning signals. Turns a snapshot into a series; recurring work, and a missed month reads as freshness."},{"k":"b","label":"Fetch date on every figure, no cadence, plus the two signals that have triggers","gains":["Zero standing cost at this team size","Costs nothing ongoing at this team's size, unlike a monthly commitment nobody has capacity to keep."],"costs":["Nothing is noticed until someone looks","Five scenarios stay unassessed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A fetch date on every figure, no cadence, and the two signals that already have triggers. Zero standing cost at this team size; nothing is noticed until someone looks."},{"k":"c","label":"Re-run the six scenarios against review state and replace the risk table","gains":["Tests the scenarios against the current thesis","Tests all six war-game scenarios against what the product actually claims today, not what it claimed in June."],"costs":["A day or two of writing","More risk rows than the plan wants"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Re-run the six war-game scenarios against the current thesis and replace the risk table. Tests the scenarios against what we now sell; a day or two of writing and more risk rows than the plan wants."},{"k":"d","label":"Retire the war-game and treat the risk table as the whole register","gains":["No ambiguity about which document governs","Removes any ambiguity about which of two documents, the market table or the war-game, actually governs."],"costs":["Ten one-line rows carry no triggers","That is what made the war-game useful"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Retire the war-game and let the ten-line risk table be the whole register. No ambiguity about which document governs; ten one-line rows carry no triggers, which is what made the war-game useful."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A one-person team cannot keep up a monthly sweep, and a missed one makes stale rows look fresh.","A fetch date on every figure gives the same protection for one edit."],"flip":"If a second person joins, option a is affordable; if the shell is an extension, option c.","linked":["MK12","MK13"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:143","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:176-178","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:319-330","docs/BUSINESS.md:1092","docs/BUSINESS.md:1111","docs/BUSINESS.md:1229","docs/BUSINESS.md:1238","docs/BUSINESS.md:1240","docs/DECIDE.md:212","merged: MK23"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Competitive watching is a standing habit, not a first build gate.","recWhy":"Three published numbers have been withdrawn from this repository, the market table carries one date for the whole table, and nothing says when any row is read again. A cadence nobody keeps is worse than none, because readers assume the rows are fresh, and a one-person team at 1.21 engineering days a week cannot keep a monthly sweep. A fetch date on every figure gives the same protection for one edit: a reader can see the age of a number and decide for themselves. The war-game's table of eight signals is nowhere in the plan, and seven of the eight watch someone else; the two that have triggers come in. The war-game rates the category never forming as the most likely outcome and the plan omits it, which MK29 carries. If a second person joins, the monthly sweep in (a) becomes affordable; if the shell ships as an extension, the scenarios in (c) are worth re-running."},{"id":"MK29","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Precedent","weight":"high","q":"Five products with this feature set are dead. Do we publish why this one differs, or leave them unnamed?","lede":"The category has a repeated failure mode, and in three of the five cases the survivors named it.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2014-05-30","label":"Editorially closes","note":"paying users would not have been enough","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2016-06-01","label":"Poetica dead","note":"acquired 1 March; goals stated as at odds","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2020-03-03","label":"Atrium shuts down","note":"after $75.5M raised","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2022-02-01","label":"Upsource, end of sales","note":"industry moving off standalone review tools","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2025-01-31","label":"Almanac switches off","note":"$45M; cause stated as capacity","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Codebrag, SpaceCode and Swimm sit alongside Upsource; Draft and Penflip are dead with the cause unverified.","caption":"Every product that sold this shape, and the date it stopped"},"stakes":"A plan that does not name the dead products in its own category is refuted by anyone with five years in it.","state":["The plan has no section on dead products, and its risk table leaves out the category never forming.","The business plan's war-game rates scenario F, where the category never forms, as most likely, with no trigger.","The 2026-09-09 sweep opened five shutdowns with dates and three stated causes."],"tension":["Money, acquisition, category drift and capacity are four different failures.","Upsource's stated cause bites: standalone review tools lose to review inside the tool.","That reading argues for the VS Code extension and against the standalone editor, which is the form-factor decision."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The graveyard, from opened sources","items":[["Editorially","closed 2014-05-30","stated that even if all users paid, it would not have been enough (stet.editorially.com)"],["Poetica","acquired 2016-03-01, dead 2016-06-01","goals described as at odds (blog.poetica.com via web.archive.org)"],["Atrium","shut down 2020-03-03","after $75.5M (techcrunch.com)"],["JetBrains Upsource","end of sales 2022-02-01","cited a gradual industry trend away from standalone code review tools"],["Almanac","shut down 2025-01-31","cause stated as capacity (get.almanac.io/go-forward)"],["Draft and Penflip","dead, cause unverified","death confirmed; do not state a cause"],["Codebrag, SpaceCode, Swimm","dead","standalone code review, same shelf as Upsource"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Publish the graveyard with dates and stated causes, and answer Upsource directly","gains":["Credible to anyone who has been in the category","Turns the war-game's own highest-rated risk, the category never forming, into named, checkable evidence."],"costs":["Every reader now knows the base rate","The honest answer points away from standalone"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish the five dead products with dates and causes, and answer Upsource directly. Credible to anyone from the category; readers learn the base rate, and the answer points away from standalone."},{"k":"b","label":"Publish only the ones whose cause we verified, and say the rest are unverified","gains":["Nothing overstated","Overstates nothing: every cause listed has actually been confirmed by re-reading the primary source."],"costs":["A shorter list reads as a curated one","The pattern is weaker missing two of five"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish only the shutdowns whose cause was verified and mark the rest unverified. Nothing overstated; a shorter list reads as a curated one and the pattern is weaker missing two of five."},{"k":"c","label":"Carry scenario F into the risk table and leave the graveyard out","gains":["Fixes the risk table's largest omission","Fixes the single largest gap in the current risk table for the cost of one new row."],"costs":["A row is not an argument","The five names are the evidence for it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Carry scenario F, the category never forms, into the risk table and leave the names out. Fixes the risk table's largest omission; a row is not an argument, and the five names are the evidence for it."},{"k":"d","label":"Say nothing about dead products","gains":["Nothing to explain","Costs nothing to write, since it commits to naming no failed competitor at all."],"costs":["Anyone naming Upsource or Almanac gets no answer","Silence reads as not having looked"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Say nothing about dead products. Nothing to explain; anyone naming Upsource or Almanac gets no answer, and silence reads as not having looked."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The five names are the strongest evidence for scenario F, the war-game's most likely outcome.","Quote each cause exactly, and mark Draft and Penflip as cause unverified."],"flip":"If the twenty conversations show teams buying a standalone review surface, this becomes a footnote.","linked":["MK17","MK27"],"sources":["docs/BUSINESS.md:1092","docs/BUSINESS.md:1111","http://stet.editorially.com/articles/goodbye/","https://techcrunch.com/2020/03/03/atrium-shuts-down/","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Publishing why we differ is launch copy.","recWhy":"Five products with this feature set are dead, the 9 September sweep opened all five with dates and three stated causes, and the war-game rates the category never forming as the most likely scenario while the risk table leaves it out. The five names are the strongest evidence for that scenario, so they go in with each cause quoted exactly and Draft and Penflip marked cause unverified. Upsource's stated cause bites hardest: standalone review tools lose to review inside the tool people already use. That points at the surface decision, and Q3 answers it with one web surface first and the Mac app later from the same tree, so the pitch has to say why a standalone editor survives where Upsource did not. The files agents obey have no tool today, so there is nothing to lose to. If the twenty conversations show teams buying a standalone review surface, this becomes a footnote."},{"id":"MK21","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Positioning","weight":"medium","q":"Do we publish the Djot arithmetic as a public position, or say nothing about markdown dialects?","lede":"Djot is the improved-markdown design by CommonMark's own author, and the first thing a reviewer raises.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"spec.md","lines":[{"t":"# Payment spec","mark":""},{"t":"","mark":""},{"t":"The runtime reads [foo][bar] before boot.","mark":"?","note":"a link, or literal text"},{"t":"","mark":""},{"t":"...400 lines...","mark":""},{"t":"","mark":""},{"t":"[bar]: https://example.com/bar","mark":"-","note":"delete this and line 3 changes meaning"}],"note":"Measured across four CommonMark engines, unanimous in both directions.","caption":"Non-locality: bytes elsewhere decide what these bytes mean"},"stakes":"Publishing teaches every reader that a better markdown exists and that we declined it.","state":["The plan's markdown-format section names Djot and settles it on adoption: no new format.","The smart-punctuation objection was tested on 2026-09-08 and fails.","Nothing in the go-to-market says whether any of it is public."],"tension":["Djot's diagnosis lands on us: seventeen emphasis rules, five of them pure tie-breakers.","Djot pulls 4,984 npm downloads against `marked`'s 275,363,833.","The sharper claim is the refusal to guess, and the engine declares no refusal today."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Djot against the incumbent, measured to 2026-09-06","items":[["@djot/djot npm downloads, one month","4,984","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486"],["marked npm downloads, same window","275,363,833","ratio 55,250 : 1 (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486)"],["Djot GitHub stars","2,038","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486"],["Best Djot VS Code extension","247 installs","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486"],["Djot support among Obsidian community plugins","0 of 7,422","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486"],["Feature requests at Hugo, Astro or Jekyll","zero","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486"],["The smart-punctuation objection against byte-exactness","tested 2026-09-08 and fails","source-to-source round-trips are byte-identical (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:490)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What Djot is right about, and what it costs us to say so","cols":["Djot's diagnosis","Our position"],"rows":[["Seventeen numbered emphasis rules, five of them pure tie-breakers","We inherit all of them (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:488)"],["`[foo][bar]` means different things depending on a definition elsewhere in the file, which makes accurate highlighting nearly impossible","Named in the markdown-format section's item 5 (non-locality as a declared refusal) as the engine's real refusal condition, and none of the refusals is currently declared (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510)"],["The fix is a new syntax","Refused on adoption: 55,250 : 1"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Publish the arithmetic as the standing answer to \"why not a better markdown\"","gains":["Pre-empts the strongest technical objection with numbers","Answers the strongest technical objection with real numbers before a reviewer has to ask for them."],"costs":["Puts a competitor's design before every reader","Concedes the format we build on is flawed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A page or a section of one.","money":"None.","what":"Publish the Djot arithmetic as the answer to why not a better markdown: seventeen emphasis rules, five tie-breakers, 4,984 downloads against 275 million. A rival's design before every reader."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep it internal and answer only when asked","gains":["Nothing in the marketing teaches anyone Djot"],"costs":["If the question is common, nothing to point at","Leaves the marketing with nothing prepared the moment the question is actually asked."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the arithmetic internal and answer only when asked. Nothing in the marketing teaches anyone Djot; if the question is common there is nothing public to point at."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish the non-locality refusal instead, framed as what the engine does","what":"A product claim about refusing to guess, not a format argument.","gains":["Turns a defensive answer into a differentiator","Turns a defensive answer about someone else's format into a positive claim about this one's own behaviour."],"costs":["No refusal is declared in the engine today","Publishing first is a promise, not a fact"],"system":"The refusals must be declared and tested.","screens":"A refusal state in the editor.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Say nothing about dialects at all","gains":["Keeps the message narrow","Keeps the public message narrow, with nothing about markdown dialects to explain or defend."],"costs":["The first technical reviewer has no answer","That is what the gap register flagged"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Say nothing about dialects at all. Keeps the message narrow, and the first technical reviewer gets no answer, which is what the gap register flagged."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The section exists so the answer is ready when someone raises Djot in a meeting.","Option c is the better public claim but waits until the refusals are declared."],"flip":"If a reviewer raises Djot unprompted in more than one of the twenty conversations, publish.","linked":["MK25"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:486","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:488","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:490","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:68"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A public position on dialects is not first build.","recWhy":"Djot is the improved markdown by CommonMark's own author and the first thing a technical reviewer raises. The plan settles it on adoption, no new format, and the smart-punctuation objection was tested on 8 September and fails. Djot's diagnosis still lands on us, seventeen emphasis rules with five pure tie-breakers, and publishing that arithmetic puts a competitor's design in front of every reader while conceding the format we build on is flawed. The section exists so the answer is ready in a meeting, not so it leads the marketing. The better public claim is (c), the refusal to guess on non-local edits, and E26 shows the engine declares no such refusal today, so publishing it first would be a promise rather than a fact. When the refusals are declared, (c) replaces (b). A reviewer raising Djot unprompted in more than one of the twenty conversations publishes the arithmetic."},{"id":"MK25","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Positioning","weight":"medium","q":"Which shelf does the buyer file this under, and do we accept the price on that shelf?","lede":"Buyers file a product under a category they already know, and on the shelf this plan sits on, the price is zero.","visual":{"kind":"matrix","x":["the job is somebody else's","the job is ours"],"y":["price is $0","price is thousands a year"],"points":[{"label":"Markdown editor","x":0.12,"y":0.06},{"label":"AI code editor","x":0.35,"y":0.28},{"label":"AI review of machine changes","x":0.88,"y":0.72},{"label":"Redlining for law firms","x":0.6,"y":0.86},{"label":"Docs platform","x":0.28,"y":0.95},{"label":"frontmatter as positioned today","x":0.15,"y":0.06,"us":true}],"note":"Shelves and owners from the critique document, lines 252-260; the redlining prices from draftable.com/pricing.","caption":"Where the buyer files it now, against where the job actually sits"},"stakes":"Filing under markdown editor means $0, and the team tier carries the whole business.","state":["The positioning leads with refusals: no plugins, no hosting, no lock-in.","The market table is organised by what tools do, not by what a buyer searches for.","The market table's own price note says the editor is worth ₹0."],"tension":["Leading with refusals reads as a markdown editor with fewer features.","The review-of-machine-changes incumbents review code with a model; we use a hash.","Redlining has budgets and a trained buyer, and nobody there sells below word level."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The shelves, and who owns each (CRITIQUE.md:252-260)","cols":["Shelf","Who owns it","The price on it"],"rows":[["Markdown editor / notes app","Obsidian: 16.1% of 30,065 SO devs, free without limits, 7,092 plugins","$0 for the editor; $4 for sync"],["AI code editor / agent surface","Cursor ($20 reference), Zed","$0-20"],["AI review of machine changes: the only shelf where the job is ours","CodeRabbit $24-48/seat, Greptile $30/seat","4-9x our top tier, sold to teams with budgets"],["Docs platform","Mintlify $450/mo, GitBook $65-249/site, ReadMe $100-150","not a shelf a solo founder enters"],["Source-of-truth workspace","nobody, and that is the problem","no search volume, no grid, no budget line"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the category owner gives away","items":[["Obsidian editor","free, no limits","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:156"],["Obsidian Sync","$4","the one proven individual price (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161)"],["Obsidian commercial licence","$50/user/year","confers no functional benefit, optional since Feb 2025, bought by 25+ seat organisations (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161)"],["Zed","free","reviews per hunk (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:157)"],["Free OSS floor in the category","AppFlowy 76,040 stars, AFFiNE 71,976, SiYuan 46,023, Logseq 44,669","CRITIQUE.md:254"],["The corpus's own conclusion about the default filing","\"another markdown editor, and the price on that shelf is $0\"","CRITIQUE.md:260"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"File under agent enablement, priced against Kiro's $20 per user row","gains":["The one shelf where the job is genuinely ours","The one shelf, per Q1's own sentence, where the job described is genuinely nobody else's."],"costs":["They review code with a model; we use a hash","Invites a capability comparison we cannot answer"],"system":"Review gates in CI must work.","screens":"The landing page leads with the review panel.","money":"Anchors at $24-30 a seat.","what":"File it under agent enablement and price the team rung against Kiro's $20 per user row. The one shelf where the job is ours; the incumbents there review code with a model and we use a hash."},{"k":"b","label":"File under markdown editor and accept $0, with a paid team tier on top","gains":["Matches what the free tier genuinely is","Matches what the free tier actually is today, rather than asking a buyer to guess its category."],"costs":["The price on that shelf is zero","The team tier carries the entire business"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Editor revenue is zero by definition.","what":"File it under markdown editor, accept that the editor is worth $0, and sell a team tier above it. Matches what the free tier is; the team tier then carries the entire business."},{"k":"c","label":"File under prose redlining and sell to teams that already pay for diff","gains":["Draftable sells word-level diff at $129-261 yearly","Draftable's $129 to $261 a year is the best pricing evidence for this kind of work found anywhere in the corpus."],"costs":["Their substrate is Word and PDF","Nobody there sells below word level"],"system":"A `.docx` path we have refused.","screens":"A redline view that does not exist.","money":"The highest observed prices in the corpus.","what":"File under prose redlining and sell to teams already paying for diff. Draftable sells word-level diff at $129 to $261 a year; their substrate is Word and PDF, and nobody sells below word level."},{"k":"d","label":"Refuse to pick and lead with the refusals as today","gains":["Distinctive and honest","The most honest position available, since it makes no claim about a shelf the evidence does not support."],"costs":["On the evidence he files it at $0"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Refuse to pick a shelf and lead with the refusals, as today. Distinctive and honest, and on the evidence the buyer files it at $0 anyway."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The positioning leaves the filing to the buyer, and he files it where the price is zero. Agent enablement is a shelf with a price.","Kiro's Pro row is $20 per user and Tessl uses the term itself, so the shelf exists and the twenty can be asked against it."],"flip":"If the shell lands standalone for individuals, he files it under markdown editor anyway.","linked":["MK30","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:145-160","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:156","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/CRITIQUE.md:252","docs/CRITIQUE.md:254","docs/CRITIQUE.md:256","docs/CRITIQUE.md:258","docs/CRITIQUE.md:260","https://draftable.com/pricing"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Shelf and price come with the pricing work.","recWhy":"Buyers file a product under a category they already know, and the positioning as written leaves the filing to them. No plugins, no hosting, no lock-in reads as a markdown editor with fewer features, and on that shelf the market table's own note says the price is zero. Agent enablement is a shelf with a price. Kiro's Pro row is $20 per user, Tessl uses the term itself, and Q1's sentence, where you write, review and keep current the files your agents obey, is a job on that shelf and on no other. Q13 prices the team rung against it at $15 to $20 a seat. The honest cost is the capability comparison it invites. The incumbents there review code with a model and we review changes with a hash, and the answer has to be that we do the half they do not, keeping the files current across people. If the shell lands standalone for individuals only, the buyer files it under editor regardless."},{"id":"MK30","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Adjacent markets","weight":"medium","q":"Do we go after prose redlining and the legal buyer this year, or state it as adjacent and decline?","lede":"Draftable sells word-level prose diff alone at $129 to $261 a user a year, thirty times our plan.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Draftable","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Diffchecker","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"frontmatter","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Price","cells":["$129/user/yr Business; $261 Legal","$15, $20 or $40 per user a month","Planned $4-5 per user a month"]},{"label":"Substrate","cells":["Word and PDF","Any text","Markdown in git"]},{"label":"Granularity sold","cells":["Word level","Word level","Byte level"]},{"label":"Buyer","cells":["Law firms with a budget line","Anyone with a card","Undecided"]},{"label":"Mac client","cells":["None","Web only","Web and desktop"]}],"note":"Nobody in this thirty-year category sells below word level, so byte precision sits under the category floor.","caption":"Prose diff alone sells for thirty times our planned price, to lawyers"},"stakes":"The legal vertical is where diff has a budget line, on a substrate we refused.","state":["The market table has no redlining row at all.","The segment with the most measured pain writes `.docx`, where an MIT rival ships.","The positioning and the engine are both markdown-only."],"tension":["Byte-exact editing is finer than anything this category sells, and no trained buyer asks for it.","The prices are the highest in the corpus and the buyer already searches.","Atrium raised $75.5M selling into law firms and shut down on 2020-03-03."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The redlining shelf, from opened sources","items":[["Draftable","$129/user/yr Business, $261/user/yr Legal","draftable.com/pricing; no Mac client"],["Diffchecker","$15, $20 and $40 per user per month","diffchecker.com/pricing"],["Litera","$16M to about $250M revenue, 15,000+ customers, 99% of the Am Law 100","litera.com/our-story, a self-claim, undated at both ends"],["Atrium","shut down 2020-03-03 after $75.5M","techcrunch.com"],["Granularity sold anywhere in the category","never below word level","so byte precision is under the floor"],["Draftable's customer count","1,300, 1,500, 2,500 and 4,000 all appear on its own pages","use the prices, never a count"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Write the paragraph: redlining is adjacent, not competitive, and we decline it","gains":["A stated boundary beats a missed one","A stated boundary, unlike an unstated one, cannot be caught out later as an accidental gap."],"costs":["Concedes the segment with the most measured pain","Gives up the highest observed prices"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Write the paragraph: redlining is adjacent, not competitive, and we decline it. A stated boundary beats a missed one; it concedes the segment with the most measured pain and the highest prices."},{"k":"b","label":"Add a redlining row to the market table and price against it","gains":["Prose diff alone at $129-261 reframes our price","Costs one row in a table, and Draftable's prices are the best pricing evidence anywhere in this research."],"costs":["Comparing a markdown tool to a Word tool","Invites a substrate question we answer with a refusal"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Raises the plausible ceiling on the paid tier.","what":"Add a redlining row to the market table, price against it, and state the decline in the same row. The best pricing evidence in the corpus for no build; a markdown tool compared with a Word tool."},{"k":"c","label":"Build a Word path and go after the legal buyer","gains":["The one segment with measured, priceable pain","The one segment in the whole market table with measured, named, priceable pain today."],"costs":["Contradicts the substrate the engine is built on","Atrium burned $75.5M and shut down"],"system":"A second substrate the engine cannot handle.","screens":"A redline view that does not exist.","money":"A long enterprise cycle, unbudgeted.","what":"Build a Word path and sell to the legal buyer. The one segment with measured, priceable pain; it contradicts the substrate the engine is built on, and Atrium burned $75.5 million there."},{"k":"d","label":"Ignore the category entirely","gains":["No edit","No engineering, no positioning change, and no new claim to defend in front of a legal buyer."],"costs":["A reviewer asks why diff sells for $261 and we charge $54","No prepared answer"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ignore the category. No edit, and a reviewer asks why diff alone sells for $261 a year while we charge $54, with no prepared answer."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The row costs nothing and the prices are the best pricing evidence in the corpus.","State the decline in the same row. We are not building a Word path, and Atrium is why."],"flip":"If the twenty conversations surface a team keeping contracts as markdown in git, option c becomes a segment.","linked":["MK25","MK31"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:145-160","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:298","docs/CRITIQUE.md:307","https://draftable.com/pricing","https://www.diffchecker.com/pricing/","https://www.litera.com/our-story","https://techcrunch.com/2020/03/03/atrium-shuts-down/","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:145"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A second buyer comes after the first.","recWhy":"Draftable sells word-level prose diff alone, no editor, at $129 to $261 a user a year, and the market table has no redlining row, so the best pricing evidence in the corpus is missing from the document that prices us. The row costs nothing and reframes the number: byte-exact editing is finer than anything that category sells. The decline goes in the same row, because the segment with the most measured pain writes .docx, where an MIT rival already ships, and the positioning and the engine are markdown-only. Atrium raised $75.5 million selling into law firms and shut down on 3 March 2020. Q13's team band of $15 to $20 a seat is priced against Kiro, not against Draftable, and the row explains why we sit below a tool that does less. A team keeping contracts as markdown in git, surfaced by the twenty conversations, is the one thing that makes (c) a segment."},{"id":"MK31","cat":"Market & competition","sub":"Segments","weight":"medium","q":"Do we target regulated software teams keeping quality documents as markdown in git?","lede":"The only adjacent buyer already on our substrate, and no regulation opened requires what we sell.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Draft","meta":"in the repo","via":"","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Reviewed","meta":"per span","via":"separate file","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Approved","meta":"whole document","via":"a signature","tone":"good","current":true},{"label":"Superseded","meta":"record retained","via":"21 CFR 11","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"21 CFR 11.10(e): record changes shall not obscure previously recorded information.","caption":"Regulation mandates the third step; our claim sits in the second"},"stakes":"Claiming a regulatory requirement that does not exist loses this buyer fastest.","state":["The buyer decision offers only the developer or the liable person.","The compliance writer needs SOC 2 and nine to eighteen months.","The plan's operating rules name GST, the IT Rules and DPDP, and no quality regime."],"tension":["MHRA permits audit trails reviewed by exception rather than in full.","The EMA's 2024 reflection paper is non-binding and names no granularity.","Pharma mandates whole-document approval; aviation mandates a per-page revision date."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the regulators actually say, from opened texts","items":[["21 CFR 11.10(e)","\"Record changes shall not obscure previously recorded information.\"","ecfr.gov, retention, not per-span review"],["MHRA GxP data integrity guide","audit trails may be reviewed as a list \"or by an 'exception reporting' process\"","assets.publishing.service.gov.uk"],["EMA reflection paper, 9 Sept 2024","model-generated text needs a quality review mechanism","non-binding; names no granularity"],["Any regulation mandating per-span review","none found","pharma mandates whole-document approval; aviation a per-page revision date"],["What the compliance persona needs first","SOC 2, attorney review, a 9-18 month cycle","CRITIQUE.md:233"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Target regulated software teams whose quality documents live in git","gains":["The only adjacent buyer on our substrate","The only adjacent buyer whose documents already live on this product's exact substrate, markdown in git."],"costs":["SOC 2, security review, nine to eighteen months","None of it fits inside ninety days"],"system":"Retention, audit export and an approval object.","screens":"An approval and export surface.","money":"SOC 2 and legal review, unbudgeted.","what":"Target regulated software teams whose quality documents live in git. The only adjacent buyer already on our substrate; SOC 2, a security review and nine to eighteen months, none inside ninety days."},{"k":"b","label":"Design for them, chase the developer, and say so in writing","gains":["Costs little today and keeps the door open","Costs almost nothing today: a design note and a sentence, not a sales motion or an engineering sprint."],"costs":["Designing for a buyer we never talk to is what produced idea mode and decision flow","No revenue from it this year"],"system":"The review file must never obscure a previous record.","screens":"None yet.","money":"None.","what":"Design so their records are never destroyed, chase the developer, and say both in writing. Cheap today and keeps the door open; designing for a buyer we never met produced idea mode and decision flow."},{"k":"c","label":"Ignore the segment until a customer asks","gains":["Smallest surface","The smallest possible surface: no design change, no positioning change, nothing to maintain."],"costs":["Retrofitting non-destructive records is expensive","Loses the one adjacent buyer on our substrate"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ignore the segment until a customer asks. The smallest surface; retrofitting non-destructive records is expensive and it loses the one adjacent buyer on our substrate."},{"k":"d","label":"Quote the regulators in the marketing and let readers infer a requirement","gains":["Reads as authority to a non-specialist","Reads as authority to a reader who has not opened the underlying regulation themselves."],"costs":["No regulation opened mandates per-span review","A compliance buyer punishes the missing caveat"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Quote the regulators in the marketing and let readers infer a requirement. Reads as authority; no regulation opened mandates per-span review, and a compliance buyer punishes the missing caveat."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Their documents are already markdown in git, true of no other buyer with a budget.","The blocker is the sales cost. SOC 2 and a nine to eighteen month cycle miss ninety days."],"flip":"If two or more of the twenty conversations are with regulated teams already in git, option a wins.","linked":["MK30","MK4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:366","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:535-551","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:41","docs/CRITIQUE.md:233","https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-01-01/title-21.xml?part=11","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/reflection-paper-use-artificial-intelligence-ai-medicinal-product-lifecycle_en.pdf","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:535"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A second buyer comes after the first.","recWhy":"Regulated software teams keeping quality documents as markdown in git are the only adjacent buyer already on our substrate, which is true of no other buyer with a budget. Nothing opened requires what we sell: MHRA permits audit trails reviewed by exception, the EMA's 2024 reflection paper is non-binding and names no granularity, pharma mandates whole-document approval and aviation a per-page revision date. The blocker is sales cost, SOC 2 and a nine to eighteen month cycle, and Q13 leaves enterprise unpriced until somebody asks. So design for them, which mostly means never destroying a review record, and chase the buyer Q2 names. The caveat in (b) is written on the card because designing for a buyer nobody talked to is exactly what produced two features validated on the wrong person. Two or more of the twenty conversations landing on regulated teams already in git makes (a) the segment."},{"id":"PR1","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Tier contents","weight":"critical","q":"What does Pro gate on, and how long must the journal be kept?","lede":"Neither thing behind the Pro gate is in the feature list. One exits with a module error.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Retained journal","tone":"warn"},{"title":"CI gate, mdmax cert","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Attribution","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Feature row with days","cells":["None. The plan says so itself","None; 1 day to wire","3 days for the readers"]},{"label":"Runs today","cells":["No","No. ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND","No. Estimated, unbuilt"]},{"label":"In the free tier now","cells":["Current state only","n/a","Yes, one of the eight free features"]},{"label":"Someone charges for it","cells":["Obsidian 1 month to 12 months","Docs-as-code CI seats","Google Workspace Business Standard"]}],"note":"Google gates span-level authorship and gives the file-level signal away free.","caption":"Three candidate gates, against whether they exist and anyone sells them."},"stakes":"Nobody can build against a price for things no planned feature builds.","state":["Pro is $4 to $5 per user: 90-day edit journal, CI gates.","Neither exists: no feature-list row builds retention; the CI check `mdmax cert` is unwired.","Attribution is free today, a free-tier feature."],"tension":["Attribution is the only one of four claims nobody shipped. It is free.","90 days collides with the CERT-In 180-day log floor.","Two rows still route CI gates to Max."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What Pro charges for, and whether it exists","cols":["Pro object","F-row","Days estimated","Runs today"],"rows":[["Retained splice journal, 90 days","none - PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289 says so itself","none","no"],["Review gates in CI (mdmax cert)","none","1 day to wire in (section 9, item 10, line 224)","no - ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND, re-run in this audit"],["Shared review state and comments: included in Pro, but stated not to be the reason it gates","Shared review state and comments","10d","not built"],["The MCP server: included in Pro, but stated not to be the reason it gates","The MCP server","6d","not built"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the cert gate's absence looks like on disk, re-checked in this audit","items":[["`node scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs --bench-info`","ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND","cannot resolve `src/modules/mdmax/domain/offsets`, imported from certify.ts"],["`cert` in package.json scripts","absent","26 scripts present; none is `cert`"],["`.github/` directory","does not exist","so no GitHub Action can be required on any path"],["Plan's own estimate to wire it","1 day","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The same document routes CI gates to two different tiers","items":[["The tiers and prices table, section 11","Pro","\"pulled forward from Max, because enforcement is what GitHub sells at $4\" (line 288)"],["The feature-by-tier build matrix, section 8","Max","AI row, MVP-2 column reads \"Document gates in CI\" (line 202)"],["The tier-trigger upsell table, section 18","Max","upsell row for CI gates routes to Max (line 443)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The anchor ladder against the offered ladder, with what is actually verified","cols":["Product","Lower rung","Upper rung","Status of the price step"],"rows":[["Obsidian Sync","1 month history","12 months history","retention step fetched (s4:63); price mapping [SS], recorded as a discrepancy (x5:236)"],["HackMD","free tier version cap","unlimited, Prime $5 annual / $8 monthly","prices fetched (r7/g1:115); the \"Recent 10 versions\" free cap appears in no research file"],["frontmatter as written","current state only, free","90 days, $4-5","one rung; nothing between 90 days and Max's \"beyond a year\""]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Where 90 days came from, and what the law now requires","items":[["Section 22, operating rules","\"Delete the record's 'no row-level retention beyond 90 days' rule\"","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545 - the notified text refutes it"],["Live log floor today","180 days of ICT logs, rolling, inside Indian jurisdiction","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:544 (CERT-In)"],["From 2027-05-13","one year of access logs, and processing logs surviving account deletion","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545 (DPDP Rules 6(1)(e), 8(3))"],["Retention as a feature row","does not exist","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep retention as the gate, write it as a feature row first","gains":["The plan's own Max row instructs it","Comparables do charge for retention"],"costs":["Unestimated work on a 58-week schedule","90 days moves again under CERT-In"],"system":"Append-only journal, retention policy, deletion job.","screens":"A history view. No screen shows one.","money":"No revenue until that row lands.","what":"Keep the 90-day journal as the gate and write it as a feature row first. The plan's Max row instructs it and comparables charge for retention; unestimated work, and 90 days moves again under CERT-In."},{"k":"b","what":"Free keeps commit-trailer attribution; Pro gates per-span, prompt-bearing provenance.","label":"Gate on attribution, the one claim nobody else shipped","gains":["Google sells exactly this at exactly this tier","Attribution readers and MCP server already carry estimates"],"costs":["Takes a feature out of the free tier","Needs MCP: six days, untested"],"system":"The split runs through the attribution readers.","screens":"Hover card gains a locked state.","money":"No build beyond the MCP server."},{"k":"c","what":"Pro is the second seat on a repo. Nothing withheld.","label":"Gate on people, not features","gains":["Nothing to build, one sentence to explain"],"costs":["Honour system, the same problem as the journal"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A seat count in settings.","money":"Fastest to charge for. Rests on goodwill."},{"k":"d","label":"Name no gate until the conversations return","gains":["Publishes no price with nothing behind it"],"costs":["No revenue line for the whole build"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No pricing page.","money":"None.","what":"Name no gate until the twenty conversations return. Publishes no price with nothing behind it, and leaves no revenue line for the whole build."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Google sells span-level authorship at exactly this tier. Nobody occupied it in nine weeks.","No new build: attribution readers are estimated, MCP is already in Pro."],"flip":"If the conversations name shared review state (who read what), not provenance, as the paid thing, take (a) or (c).","linked":["F1","MK4","PR2","PR21","PR23","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:57","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:202","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:443","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:131","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:139","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r8to10/s4-mobile-cross-device.md:63","merged: PR6","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:65"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"What the paid tier gates is one of the five decisions the meeting settles.","dependsOn":["PR24"],"recWhy":"Neither thing behind the Pro gate exists: no feature row builds a 90-day journal, mdmax cert exits with a module error, and 90 days collides with the CERT-In 180-day log floor anyway. Attribution, which agent wrote which span, is the one headline claim nobody shipped in nine weeks, and Google sells exactly it at exactly this tier, so it is the paid thing. Q13 then places it. Pro at $6 to $8 buys state across your own devices, sync, a share link, your key and the kit, while the team rung at $15 to $20 a seat buys state across people, who changed what and review state on the files agents obey. So Pro gates on sync and sharing, and attribution is the rung above: (b) with the tier named. The cost stands: attribution leaves the free tier and needs six untested MCP days. If the conversations name shared review state rather than provenance as the paid thing, (a) or (c) returns.","dupes":{"dropped":["F12","PL15"],"areas":["Features","Plan, scope & sequencing"],"why":"Features recommended keeping retention and specifying it as an append-only file inside the review store, which is option a here. Plan, scope & sequencing recommended option d: publish no Pro price until the team conversations say what teams are gated on. Neither recommended gating Pro on attribution.","agree":false}},{"id":"PR24","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Model","weight":"critical","q":"Do we charge for the editor, or give it away and charge for state?","lede":"The argument that we price on the wrong axis was made on 2026-08-31 and never answered.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Charge for the editor"},{"title":"Editor free, charge for state","tone":"ok"}],"rows":[{"label":"What Obsidian does","cells":["n/a","Free editor, $4 sync"]},{"label":"What the plan does","cells":["Pro tier at $4 to $5 a seat","n/a"]},{"label":"His stated numbers","cells":["free / ₹299 / ₹699 a month","n/a"]},{"label":"Comparable pays for","cells":["Nothing in this category","Retention, sync, enforcement"]}],"note":"Two different shapes, not two numbers.","caption":"Where the money is asked for"},"stakes":"We would price what this category never paid for, and give away what it does.","state":["The plan prices a team tier at $4 to $5 per user per month.","The founder's own stated numbers are free / ₹299 / ₹699 per user per month.","No message records him accepting the per-seat dollar figure."],"tension":["₹699 is about $7.90, above every India AI anchor.","$4 to $5 is a team price benchmarked on GitHub; ₹299 is consumer.","The plan names its own retention contradiction: Max in one table, Pro in another."],"evidence":[{"type":"quote","title":"The two positions, both verbatim","items":[["His numbers, 2026-08-28","'free plan, 299 per month plan, 699 per month plan'","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2026-09-09.md:571"],["The unanswered argument, 2026-08-31","'Pricing is wrong on the axis, not the number. Obsidian gives the editor away and charges $4 for sync'","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:91"],["What the plan carries","team tier at $4 to $5 per user per month","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the plan's per-seat team price","gains":["Anchored to a comparable teams already pay","Enforcement is what GitHub sells at this price"],"costs":["No record of the founder accepting it","Prices a team product before any team conversation"],"system":"Needs the retained journal, which no planned feature builds.","screens":"Adds billing and seat management.","money":"Requires seats to exist to earn anything.","what":"The plan's per-seat team price, $4 to $5 a user a month. Anchored to a comparable teams already pay and enforcement is what GitHub sells at that price; no record of the founder accepting it."},{"k":"b","label":"His numbers: free / ₹299 / ₹699 per month","what":"Individual tiers in rupees, India-first, with a world tier priced separately.","gains":["It is what he specified, twice","Individual pricing needs no team first"],"costs":["₹699 sits above ChatGPT Go ₹399 and Gemini ₹199 to ₹399","Dodo's fee is 12.7% of a ₹299 charge"],"system":"No retention build for the free tier.","screens":"A simple upgrade surface.","money":"Lead with annual or fees eat the monthly tier."},{"k":"c","label":"Editor free, charge for state across people and devices","gains":["Prices on the axis the category pays for","A free editor is the distribution the plan lacks"],"costs":["Earns nothing from the solo user who exists today","Shared state is the most expensive thing to build"],"system":"Requires review-file sync. Not built.","screens":"Adds account and sharing surfaces.","money":"Slowest to first revenue, largest ceiling.","what":"The editor is free and the charge is for state across people and devices: sync, sharing, who changed what, review state. Prices on the axis the category pays for; earns nothing from the solo user."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Every comparable gives the editor away and charges for state: Obsidian $4, HackMD $5.","It is compatible with his rupee numbers: (c) is the axis, (b) the points on it."],"flip":"If the twenty conversations return that teams pay for the editor itself, (a) is right.","linked":["B1","P1","PR1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:474","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2026-09-09.md:571","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:91","docs/HANDOVER-ANSWERS-2-2026-09-09.md:181"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Charge for the editor or for state. The plan proposes state, and it sets the whole tier table.","recWhy":"The argument that we price on the wrong axis was made on 31 August and never answered. Every comparable gives the editor away and charges for state, Obsidian at $4 for Sync and HackMD at $5. A free editor is also the distribution the plan otherwise lacks, and Q3 opens it at / with no account. So the editor is free and the charge is for state. Across your own devices in Pro, sync and a share link, and across people in the team rung, who changed what and review state on the files agents obey. The founder's rupee numbers are points on that axis rather than a different one. ₹699 sits above every India AI anchor, though, and Dodo's fee is 12.7 percent of a ₹299 charge, which is why Q13's bands are $6 to $8 and $15 to $20 in dollars. The cost: the solo user who exists today pays nothing. If the twenty conversations return that teams pay for the editor itself, (a) is right."},{"id":"PR7","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Free tier","weight":"high","q":"Does free stay uncapped, and which founder-hour figure do we budget against?","lede":"The free tier's cost is founder time. The plan and two reports give three different numbers.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"The plan's cost rules","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Unit-economics report","tone":"accent"},{"title":"AI-economics report","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Founder-hours at 10,000 users","cells":["About 90 a month","76.8 a month","300, cumulative across signups"]},{"label":"Priced in rupees","cells":["No","₹1,30,560 a month at ₹1,700/h","No"]},{"label":"Against the whole cloud bill","cells":["Not stated","₹25,930 a month","n/a"]},{"label":"How it is tagged","cells":["Stated","Modelled, derivation shown","Marked as inference"]}],"note":"The same population, three answers, and no line converts any of them into rupees in the plan.","caption":"What the free tier costs, according to three documents in one repo."},"stakes":"A free tier whose cost is unmeasured can consume the only person building the product.","state":["Tier table, row one: Individual, free, no limits, eight pilot features, own key.","The plan budgets 90 founder-hours a month at 10,000 users.","The plan's upsell table names a free AI quota, which \"no limits\" rules out."],"tension":["Founder time costs five times the whole infrastructure bill at 10,000 users.","The support coefficient, tickets per paying user monthly, is stated as 0.10 and 0.30.","The tier cannot be both no-limits and subject to a quota."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Three founder-hour figures for the same free tier","items":[["The plan, section 7 cost rules","about 90 founder-hours/month at 10,000 users","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137 - no rupee figure, no free/paid split"],["A research report, modelled","76.8 founder-hours/month at 10,000 users (400 paid) = Rs130,560","r26-c6:62, at Rs1,700/h; founder time is 5.0x total cloud+AI (r26-c6:66)"],["A research report, BYO-key support only","300 founder-hours across 10,000 signups","r19-p1:63 - cumulative onboarding, tagged [inference], not a monthly rate"],["Support coefficient underlying all of it","stated as 0.10 and 0.30 tickets/paid-user/month in the same repo","r26-c6:50 - a 3x spread that moves Rs299 from FATAL to SEVERE"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What a free user costs, according to a research report","items":[["Median free user, total","Rs7.87/month","r26-c6:115; 86% of it is founder time"],["Founder time at 10,000 free users","Rs68,000/month","r26-c6:117, at Rs1,700/h"],["Total cloud + AI at 10,000 users","Rs25,930/month","r26-c6:62"],["Hosted inference on free","Rs0.00","BYO-key only, zero by construction (r26-c6:114)"],["Plan's own infra figure","about $0 at 100 users, about $390/month at 10,000","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The contradiction between the tiers and prices table and the tier-trigger upsell table","cols":["Line","Text","Implication"],"rows":[["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287","\"Free, no limits\"","no quota exists"],["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:444","\"asks for more than their free quota allows\"","a quota exists"],["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:537","\"the free tier has no service commitment\"","support is disclaimed but the hours are still spent"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep free uncapped; adopt one number and price it on the page it appears","gains":["Keeps the distribution story intact","Forces three estimates into one arguable number"],"costs":["Ticket-deflecting docs are unbuilt, unestimated","Commits to a cost uncertain threefold"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Delete the free-quota line from the upsell table.","money":"₹1,30,560 a month of founder time at 10,000 users.","what":"Free stays uncapped, and the founder-hour cost is one number, ₹1,30,560 a month at 10,000 users, printed where it is used. Keeps the distribution story; commits to a cost uncertain threefold."},{"k":"b","label":"Free with a soft cap on the support driver, never on features","gains":["Bounds the worst case, keeps every feature free"],"costs":["Any cap makes no-limits untrue","The cap needs a number nobody measured"],"system":"One counter and one threshold.","screens":"A cap notice that reads generous.","money":"Caps the tail. Costs nothing directly.","what":"Free with a soft cap on the support driver and never on features. Bounds the worst case; any cap makes no limits untrue, and it needs a number nobody has measured."},{"k":"c","label":"Free for individuals, licence asked for above a seat count","gains":["The only B2B motion two people can run"],"costs":["Voluntary, so revenue is unpredictable","Obsidian gave up the mandatory version"],"system":"No enforcement code by definition.","screens":"A licence page and a supporters wall.","money":"Unpredictable. The only measured B2B mechanism here.","what":"Free for individuals, with a licence asked for above a seat count. The only B2B motion two people can run; voluntary, and Obsidian gave up the mandatory version."},{"k":"d","label":"Measure the support coefficient before deciding","gains":["The one input the report says would change its verdict"],"costs":["Ten users cannot resolve a threefold spread","Defers the page copy past the meeting"],"system":"A local, opt-in ticket count.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Measure the support coefficient before deciding. The one input the report says would change its verdict; ten users cannot resolve a threefold spread and the page copy waits past the meeting."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Support, not compute, is the cost structure, and the two figures differ fourfold.","Use the ₹1,30,560 figure, the only one with working shown, and delete the quota line."],"flip":"If the ten pilot users exceed 0.02 tickets each a month, add a soft cap before launch.","linked":["B6","PR10","PR17","PR8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:444","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:537","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:50","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:62","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p1-ai-economics.md:26","http://stet.editorially.com/articles/goodbye/"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Each kit costs model tokens, so whether the free tier is capped is a first build answer.","dependsOn":["PR10"],"recWhy":"The free tier costs founder time, not compute. At 10,000 users support is five times the whole infrastructure bill, and the plan and two reports give three different figures for it because the support coefficient is stated as 0.10 and 0.30 in the same repository. The tier also cannot be both no limits and subject to the AI quota the upsell table names, so the quota line is deleted. Q13 keeps free uncapped, the editor, paste and export, local only, because a free editor is the distribution the plan otherwise lacks, and Obsidian shows the shape works. Adopt the ₹1,30,560 figure, the only one with its working shown, and price it on the page it appears on. Ticket-deflecting documentation is the unbuilt, unestimated cost of this answer. If the ten pilot users exceed 0.02 tickets each a month, a soft cap on the support driver goes in before launch, and features stay free either way."},{"id":"PR10","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Hosted AI","weight":"high","q":"What unit does hosted AI meter in, and where is the dollar ceiling?","lede":"Hosted AI is priced at cost plus margin over model costs three reports disagree about having read.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"User's own key","note":"Free, uncapped, the pilot default","tone":"good"},{"label":"Hosted, cheap lane","note":"Unmetered up to the daily ceiling","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Daily dollar breaker","note":"Server-side, checked before the vendor call","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Frontier lane","note":"Metered in actions, shown as a count","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Ceiling reached","note":"Refuse and say so; never bill through","tone":"stop"}],"note":"One call a minute for 24 hours is $1,254 a month on the cheap model alone.","caption":"Where the ceiling has to sit for the abuse case to be bounded at all."},"stakes":"With no unit and no cap there is no price, and abuse is unbounded until a spending breaker exists.","state":["The tier table reads: hosted AI, cost plus margin, off by default.","\"Capped\" appears three times in the plan; no cap number appears anywhere.","One UI call a minute for 24 hours costs $1,254 to $2,507 a month."],"tension":["We cannot set a margin over model prices our reports disagree about.","The abuse case is a cheap call on a timer.","Dollars metering publishes our unit cost and invites comparison with the raw API."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The abuse ceiling against a seat price","items":[["1 call / 60s, 24h, Haiku 4.5","$1,254/month","r26-c6:124, derived from p90 restructure cost"],["Same pattern on Sonnet 5","$2,507/month","same"],["Against a Rs599 subscription grossing $6.28","199x gross revenue","r26-c6:129, the corpus's own comparison"],["Against a $5 seat","about 251x gross","derived from the same figure; the corpus does not state this one"],["Control that stops it","server-side daily dollar breaker, checked before dispatch","r19-p4:76 and :82; rate limits and concurrency-1 do not"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Whether the record has read a model price","cols":["Claim","Date","What it says"],"rows":[["r19-p1:13-28, eight vendor pages","2026-08-31","anthropic.com/pricing and docs.claude.com pricing both HTTP 200 JS-shell; OpenAI 403; \"zero [fetched] prices\"; every model price tagged [SS]"],["r19-p4:19","2026-08-31","\"Haiku 4.5 rates - $1/MTok in, $5/MTok out [fetched, anthropic.com/pricing, 2026-08-31]\""],["BUSINESS.md:719","2026-08-30","\"Claude Sonnet 5 is $2/MTok input and $10/MTok output ... [fetched, docs.claude.com pricing, read 2026-08-30]\""],["Net effect","-","every downstream cost number rests on which of these is true, and nobody has reconciled them"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Actions, with a server-side daily dollar breaker","gains":["A report already did the design work","Survives a tokeniser change without repricing"],"costs":["Proxy, ledger and breaker: none are estimated","Actions must be defined and then defended"],"system":"Metering proxy, per-account ledger, breaker before every call.","screens":"A usage line and a refusal state.","money":"Bounded downside per account per day.","what":"Actions as the unit, cheap calls unmetered, and a server-side daily dollar breaker. A report already designed it and it survives a tokeniser change; proxy, ledger and breaker are all unestimated."},{"k":"b","label":"Dollars spent, shown as a running float","gains":["Impossible to mis-price"],"costs":["Publishes our unit cost","Invites comparison with the raw API"],"system":"A ledger and a breaker. No taxonomy.","screens":"A live spend readout.","money":"Thin margin, fully visible.","what":"Dollars spent, shown as a running total. Impossible to mis-price; it publishes our unit cost and invites comparison with the raw API."},{"k":"c","label":"No hosted AI; bring your own key forever","gains":["Zero inference cost and zero abuse surface"],"costs":["Loses anyone who has never held an API key"],"system":"Nothing to build.","screens":"The key field, already planned.","money":"No AI revenue, no AI cost.","what":"No hosted AI at all; bring your own key forever. Zero inference cost and zero abuse surface; it loses anyone who has never held an API key."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the wording, specify in the build after the pilot","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Leaves an unbounded liability described as capped"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Unbounded in the worst case.","what":"Set it in the build after the pilot."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Actions, with cheap calls left unmetered, is the only shape both easy to understand and bounded.","The wording is the risk: say unmetered up to a daily ceiling, never free."],"flip":"If nobody without a key ever reaches the AI panel in the pilot, (c) is cheaper.","linked":["PR21","PR3","PR7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:202","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:290","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:444","docs/BUSINESS.md:719","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p4-credits-and-limits.md:82","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r19-p1-ai-economics.md:26","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:124"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Metering and a ceiling on AI spend protect the free tier from the first week.","recWhy":"Hosted AI is priced at cost plus margin over model costs that three reports disagree about, capped appears three times in the plan and no cap number appears anywhere, and the abuse case is trivial. One UI call a minute for 24 hours costs $1,254 to $2,507 a month. Actions are the only unit that is both easy to understand and bounded, cheap calls stay unmetered, and a server-side daily dollar breaker turns an unbounded liability into a number. Dollars would publish our unit cost. Q10 makes the user's own key the default and Q13 says hosted AI, if ever, is metered in actions with a daily breaker, so this card decides the shape of something that may never ship. That is why the wording matters more than the build: say unmetered up to a daily ceiling, never free. If nobody without a key reaches the AI panel in the pilot, (c) is cheaper and the breaker is never built."},{"id":"PR21","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Tier ladder","weight":"high","q":"Which tier ladder ships, and does Max survive as a third rung?","lede":"Three committed artefacts name three different ladders. The most specific is a rules file with a billing webhook.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":175,"cols":[{"title":"The current plan","tone":"accent"},{"title":"firestore.rules, July","tone":"warn"},{"title":"BUSINESS.md","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Rungs","cells":["Individual / Pro / Max / Hosted AI","free | pro | team","Free / Pro / Power / Work"]},{"label":"Currency","cells":["USD","None","INR"]},{"label":"Encodes billing","cells":["No","Yes: priceId, provider, customerId","No"]},{"label":"Meters usage","cells":["No","Yes: aiTokens, noteCount, bytesUsed","No"]},{"label":"Holds documents","cells":["No","Yes: content up to 900,000 bytes","No"]}],"note":"A build team reading the repo finds the middle column first, because it is the most specific.","caption":"Three committed files, three ladders: which describes a shipped design?"},"stakes":"Whichever ladder is chosen, two of three artefacts are wrong, and one is a security rules file.","state":["The plan runs Individual, Pro, Max, Hosted AI.","The Firestore rules file defines a server-owned enum: free, pro, team.","The business plan runs Free, Pro, Power and Work."],"tension":["Max holds retention, admin and SSO; SSO is deferred until asked.","The rules file caps note content at 900,000 bytes, an undecided design.","Two plan tables still send CI gates to Max."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three ladders, three artefacts","cols":["Artefact","Ladder","Dated","Billing implied"],"rows":[["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:285-290","Individual / Pro, team / Max / Hosted AI","2026-09-08","none until the build after the pilot"],["firestore.rules:22","free | pro | team","file dated 2026-07-25","billing webhook, Admin SDK, usage meter"],["docs/BUSINESS.md:448-451","Free / Pro Rs299 / Power Rs599 / Work Rs3,999 flat","2026-08-30","monthly and annual, INR"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the rules file already designs","items":[["plan enum","free | pro | team","client may only ever write 'free'; upgrades via billing webhook (firestore.rules:22-24)"],["billing/{uid}","status, priceId, currentPeriodEnd, provider, customerId","owner-read only, no client writes (firestore.rules:29-32)"],["usage/{uid}/months","aiTokens, noteCount, bytesUsed","\"metered server-side\" (firestore.rules:34-36)"],["Note bodies","content: string, <= 900000 bytes","firestore.rules:61 - the document-holding design decision 7 puts to the meeting"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the four exhibits say Max contains","cols":["Exhibit","Contents claimed","Line"],"rows":[["16 (section 11, pricing)","retention beyond a year, admin, SSO when asked","289"],["6 (section 8, tiers; two builds after the pilot)","sync, multi-device, version-history retention, export, document gates in CI","199-203"],["25 (section 18, upsell)","CI gates; and \"Pro or Max\" for the second device","442-443"],["22 (operating rules)","SSO is not a feature until a customer refuses to pay without it, at about 20 seats","535"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Whether Max's contents can be built or priced","items":[["Retention beyond a year","no feature row exists","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289 says so explicitly"],["Sync","15 days or more, two builds after the pilot","\"sync is the one proven individual price\" (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:66)"],["Export","already built, never run, 0d + 1h to verify","945 lines wired at EditorPane.tsx:907 (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:67)"],["SSO / admin","deferred by rule until a customer refuses to pay without it","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:535"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Two tiers: free and Pro. Delete Max until someone asks","gains":["Removes three contradictory descriptions and a rung","The reappearance trigger is already written"],"costs":["Gives up the anchoring a top tier provides","Anything parked in Max needs a home"],"system":"One paid entitlement instead of two.","screens":"A two-column pricing page.","money":"Loses whatever a decoy tier is worth.","what":"Two tiers, free and Pro, with Max deleted until someone asks. Removes three contradictory ladders and a rung; anything parked in Max needs a home, and a top tier's anchoring goes."},{"k":"b","label":"free / pro / team, matching the code","gains":["Least churn, and the names a build team finds"],"costs":["Adopts the vocabulary of a file that may go"],"system":"The plan changes; the rules file does not.","screens":"Renamed columns.","money":"None.","what":"free, pro and team, matching the enum in the Firestore rules file. The least churn and the names a build team finds; it adopts the vocabulary of a file that may go."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep three rungs and fix the stale rows","gains":["Cheapest edit, keeps the ladder intact"],"costs":["Max still holds one unbuilt feature and two refused"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Three columns, the third hollow.","money":"None.","what":"Keep three rungs and fix the stale rows. The cheapest edit; Max still holds one unbuilt feature and two features refused until asked."},{"k":"d","label":"Settle whether Firebase holds documents first, then the plan is the only source","gains":["Removes the contradiction at its root","Unblocks the operating rules, which research found blocked"],"costs":["A deletion, so it needs the meeting","Does not itself answer how many rungs"],"system":"Deletes a committed security rules file.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Decide first whether the Firebase document store ships."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Max holds one unbuilt feature and two deferred until asked, so it has nothing to sell.","The operating rules already set Max's return: the first team refusing to pay without SSO."],"flip":"If we adopt a commercial licence, the third rung returns as a per-organisation licence.","linked":["E6","PR1","PR2","PR23","PR8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:202","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:285","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:290","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:442","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:443","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:535","docs/BUSINESS.md:448","firestore.rules:22","firestore.rules:29","firestore.rules:34","firestore.rules:61","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:142","merged: PR22"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Which rungs exist, and whether the top one survives, is the same decision.","dependsOn":["PR24"],"recWhy":"Three committed artefacts name three different ladders: the plan runs Individual, Pro, Max and Hosted AI, the Firestore rules file defines free, pro and team, and the business plan runs Free, Pro, Power and Work. Max holds retention, admin and SSO; retention is unbuilt, SSO deferred until a team refuses without it, and two tables still route CI gates there, so Max has nothing to sell. Two tiers on the page at launch, free and Pro, and the operating rules already write Max's return: the first team refusing without SSO. Q13 names a team rung at $15 to $20 a seat for the instruction-file view, which arrives with Phase 1 rather than at launch. The ladder grows to three when the third rung holds something, and free, pro, team is the enum it grows into. Q9 deletes firestore.rules, which answers (d). A commercial licence under PR8 returns the third rung as a per-organisation licence."},{"id":"PR2","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Enforcement","weight":"critical","q":"Where does the retained journal live, and what happens when a team stops paying?","lede":"The paid object is a plain-text file the free client writes. Honour system, or the zero-bytes promise is false.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"Payment lapses","note":"The journal is already on their disk","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Client stops appending","note":"Past history intact and readable","tone":"good","edge":"a"},{"label":"Client truncates the file","note":"Deletes a file the user owns","tone":"stop","edge":"b"},{"label":"Server-held, then dropped","note":"Zero document bytes stops being true","tone":"warn","edge":"c"},{"label":"Rule: features, never files","note":"The plan's upsell rules","tone":"accent"}],"note":"Only one branch leaves every stated promise standing.","caption":"The three things that can happen to a journal in the customer's own repo."},"stakes":"Either the pricing page describes an unenforceable gate, or the architecture line the pitch leads with is false.","state":["The review file, `.frontmatter/review.jsonl`, is plain text in your repo.","Pro is the same file plus history and prompts, kept 90 days.","Our control plane holds identity and billing, and no document bytes."],"tension":["In the repo, the free client writes it. Nothing is withheld.","On our server it carries prompts and hashes, breaking the zero-bytes promise.","Obsidian made its licence optional because a local app cannot enforce one."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Where the journal can live, and what each costs","cols":["Location","Enforceable","Zero-bytes claim survives","Plain-text promise survives"],"rows":[["User's repo, plain text","no - honour system","yes","yes"],["Our server","yes","no - carries prompts and per-span hashes","n/a"],["User's repo, encrypted to a control-plane key","yes","yes","no - stops being readable without frontmatter"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The precedent the corpus already measured","items":[["Obsidian commercial licence","mandatory until Feb 2025","made optional because it was unenforceable on a local app (r26-c8:21)"],["What the founder says the money comes from","Sync and Publish, not the licence","\"Obsidian makes money from its add-on services\" - Steph Ango (r26-c8:20)"],["Named orgs on the licence wall","84 or 146","two agents read the same server-rendered page a day apart and disagreed (r26-c8:39)"],["Amazon's share of the named floor","61.8%","r26-c8:37, own parse 2026-08-31"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The three possible lapse behaviours, and what each breaks","cols":["Behaviour","Journal after lapse","Rule broken"],"rows":[["Stop writing history; file stays","complete up to the lapse date, then frozen","none - but Pro becomes a subscription to future history only"],["Truncate to current state","user loses history they already had","\"loses features, never files\" (line 449) and BUSINESS.md:632"],["Journal held server-side, read-only 30 days then dropped","deleted from our systems","\"No document byte ever passes through a Zephyrus server\" (WEEK0-DECISIONS:35)"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What BUSINESS.md's lapse table still describes that the current plan no longer has","items":[["Hosted sync across devices","stops at lapse","F12 is MVP-2, 15d+, not built"],["Published pages on *.frontmatter.page","live, frozen, indefinitely","hosting is a refusal in v15 (§12 positioning)"],["Hosted version history","read-only 30 days, then dropped","no retention F-row exists (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289)"],["Hosted AI credits","zero at lapse","hosted AI is an unspecified add-on (PR10)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Repo, honour-system by design, and lapse only stops new entries","gains":["Breaks no promise made in bold elsewhere","Nothing to build for the lapse path"],"costs":["Pro becomes a licence, not a lock"],"system":"One entitlement check before appending. No server storage.","screens":"Pricing line: your history stays, we stop adding.","money":"Revenue voluntary in effect. No storage cost.","what":"The journal stays in the repo, honour system by design, and a lapse only stops new entries. Breaks no promise made in bold elsewhere; Pro becomes a licence rather than a lock."},{"k":"b","label":"Server-held journal, and rewrite the zero-bytes promise","gains":["A real gate with real enforcement"],"costs":["Loses the positioning line the plan leads with","Drags prompts into DPDP and CERT-In's 180-day floor"],"system":"Hosted store, retention job, processor agreements.","screens":"A lapse banner and read-only history.","money":"Storage and compliance cost from the first team.","what":"A server-held journal and a rewritten zero-bytes promise. A real gate with real enforcement; it loses the line the plan leads with and drags prompts into DPDP and CERT-In's 180-day floor."},{"k":"c","label":"Repo, encrypted to a control-plane key","gains":["Enforceable, and bytes never leave the machine"],"costs":["Breaks readable-without-frontmatter, which makes the review file safe to adopt"],"system":"Key management on top of the review-file writer.","screens":"An encrypted-file state nobody has designed.","money":"Key infrastructure, small but permanent.","what":"The journal in the repo, encrypted to a control-plane key. Enforceable and bytes never leave the machine; it breaks readable-without-frontmatter, which is what makes the review file safe to adopt."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop retention as the gate entirely","gains":["Removes the contradiction at its root"],"costs":["Pro is left with a CI gate that does not run"],"system":"No journal store at all.","screens":"No history view.","money":"None until another gate is named.","what":"Retention stops being a gate at all. Removes the contradiction at its root, and Pro is left with a CI gate that does not run."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["PR1 in the MVP set gates Pro on attribution, not on a retained journal, so where the journal lives is moot.","Retention stays a feature of the review file in the user's repo, honour system by design, and gates nothing."],"flip":"If the twenty conversations name retention rather than attribution as the paid thing, (a) returns as written.","linked":["L17","PR1","PR21","PR23","PR8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:289","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:449","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/BUSINESS.md:618","docs/BUSINESS.md:632","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:21","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:39","merged: PR14"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A retained journal belongs to the deferred review work.","recWhy":"The paid object as written is a plain-text file the free client writes into the user's repository, so either Pro is an honour system or the zero-bytes promise is false. There is no third shape, and Obsidian made its licence optional for the same reason, a local app cannot enforce one. PR1 in the MVP set gates Pro on attribution rather than a retained journal, and Q13 gives Pro sync and sharing, so where the journal lives no longer decides anything. Retention stays what it is, a feature of the review file in the user's repo, honour system by design, gating nothing. It sits after the pilot because the contradiction is resolved upstream and the remaining work is one edit to the tier table. If the twenty conversations name retention rather than attribution as the paid thing, (a) returns as written, and (b) and (c) stay ruled out because each breaks a promise the product makes in bold."},{"id":"PR3","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Price level","weight":"critical","q":"What is the price, and which category is it anchored to?","lede":"Two bands from the research: Pro $6 to $8 against Obsidian Sync, Team $15 to $20 a seat against Kiro's Pro row.","visual":{"kind":"matrix","x":["$30 per user per year","$300 per user per year"],"y":["bought by a person","bought by an organisation"],"points":[{"label":"Obsidian Sync","x":0.07,"y":0.15},{"label":"frontmatter Pro","x":0.09,"y":0.45,"us":true},{"label":"Draftable Business","x":0.37,"y":0.85},{"label":"GitBook, per user","x":0.42,"y":0.8},{"label":"Diffchecker","x":0.56,"y":0.5},{"label":"Draftable Legal","x":0.86,"y":0.95}],"note":"Draftable and Diffchecker sell comparison alone, with no editor and no review state.","caption":"Annual price per user against buyer. Every figure from the vendor's page."},"stakes":"Price against the wrong category and the number is either uncollectable or not credible.","state":["Pro is $4 to $5 per user per month, period unstated: $48 to $60 a year.","Two anchors are annual-billed rates; GitHub Team $4 has no fetched source.","$5 needs 1,018 subscribers to cover monthly costs, counting founder time."],"tension":["Draftable charges $129 to $261 per user a year for comparison with no editor.","Diffchecker charges $15 to $40 per user per month for the same narrow job.","The conversations carry only the low number, so they can return one answer."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Anchors, split by billing period, with provenance","cols":["Comparable","Annual rate","Monthly rate","Provenance in the record"],"rows":[["Obsidian Sync","$4/user/mo","$5/user/mo","r7/g1:114, fetched 2026-08-29"],["Obsidian Publish","$8/site/mo","$10/site/mo","r7/g1:114, fetched 2026-08-29"],["HackMD Prime","$5/seat/mo","$8/seat/mo (stated 37.5% annual discount)","r7/g1:115, fetched 2026-08-29"],["Confluence","-","$6.70 and $13.20 per user/mo","r25-w3:42, read 2026-08-31"],["GitHub Team","-","$4 asserted","no fetched source in 237 research files; GAPS:25 credits \"the refuter\""],["frontmatter Pro, as written","not stated","$4-5","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288 - no period declared"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the price implies against founder cost and category discourse","items":[["Break-even India monthly price at Rs1,700/h founder time","Rs333.25","r26-c6:45, from support cost 0.1445 h per paying user per month"],["$4 at USD/INR 95.39","Rs381.56","derived; ECB rate date 2026-08-28 via frankfurter"],["$5 at USD/INR 95.39","Rs476.95","derived"],["Modal price in the HN pricing corpus","$5/mo, 75 of 259 mentions (29.0%)","r25-w3:77, measured"],["Sentiment at $5/mo in that corpus","11 positive vs 10 negative","r25-w3:84 - \"$5 is already the contested zone, not the safe one\""]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Paying seats needed to cover the Rs1,08,918 two-founder monthly nut","note":"Gross calculated at USD/INR 95.39 (ECB rate date 2026-08-28). Contribution uses r26-c6's measured World Pro $5 contribution of Rs352.66. The eff rows come from r26-c6:178-180's own table, which nets out founder support time at Rs1,700/h; the $30 row is its 8.6 teams times 5 seats. The nut comes from r26-c6:171, matching PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292.","data":[["$4/seat, on gross revenue",286],["$5/seat, on gross revenue",229],["$5/seat, on contribution (Rs352.66)",309],["$5/seat, after founder support time (eff Rs107.01)",1018],["$30/editor in 5-seat teams, same eff basis",43]],"unit":"seats","threshold":114,"thresholdLabel":"the 114 seats the plan already withdrew as too many"},{"type":"stat","title":"Support load per rupee, from r26-c6:184","items":[["One 5-seat team at $30/seat","contributes Rs12,622/month","consumes 0.5 founder-hours/month"],["58 India Pro subscribers, same revenue","Rs12,622/month","consume 8.4 founder-hours/month - 17x worse"],["Teams to fund one founder at $30/editor","5.1","r26-c6:180, at a Rs63,918 one-founder nut"],["Individual seats to fund one founder at $5","598","same table, same eff basis"],["Live comparables at the $30 level","CodeRabbit $24 and $48/seat; Greptile $30/seat/mo","r25-w3:47-48 - AI code review, not markdown editors"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"$5 monthly, $4 annual, per seat - the editor anchor","gains":["Full parity with the anchor already chosen","Makes the annual discount an explicit lever"],"costs":["Needs 1,018 subscribers once founder time is costed","Anchors to editors, not to diff tools"],"system":"Two SKUs and a proration path.","screens":"Pricing page with a period toggle.","money":"$48 to $60 per seat per year.","what":"$5 monthly and $4 annual per seat, the editor anchor. Full parity with the anchor already chosen; it needs 1,018 subscribers once founder time is costed, and anchors to editors, not diff tools."},{"k":"b","label":"$10-20 per editor per month - the diff-tool anchor","gains":["Draftable sells less than this for $129 to $261"],"costs":["No markdown editor charges this","The Pro gate is unbuilt"],"system":"Same billing, different number.","screens":"The page must justify the level.","money":"$120 to $240 per editor per year.","what":"$10 to $20 per editor a month, the diff-tool anchor. Draftable sells less than this for $129 to $261 a year; no markdown editor charges it and the Pro gate is unbuilt."},{"k":"c","label":"Flat, per repository or per organisation","gains":["No seat accounting and no proration"],"costs":["Kills seat expansion, the main growth engine"],"system":"No seat model. One entitlement per repo.","screens":"No invite-and-count flow.","money":"Predictable per logo, capped per logo.","what":"A flat price per repository or per organisation. No seat accounting and no proration; it kills seat expansion, the main growth engine."},{"k":"d","label":"Carry both numbers into the twenty conversations","gains":["The conversations cost nothing to run twice","Separates rejecting $5 from rejecting paying"],"costs":["A two-price script can read as unserious","Noisier answers with only twenty"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No pricing page until the answer lands.","money":"Defers revenue two weeks.","what":"Both bands go into the conversations: Pro $6 to $8 against Obsidian Sync, Team $15 to $20 a seat against Kiro. Separates rejecting a number from rejecting paying; two prices may read as unserious."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The plan calls the price a hypothesis, then asks a question with only one possible answer.","Both numbers cost one extra sentence. Also state the period and strike the unfetched GitHub anchor."],"flip":"If fewer than five of twenty engage with the higher number at all, (a) is settled honestly.","linked":["MK16","MK25","PR1","PR11","PR12","PR17"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:25","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:85","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r7/g1-business-model-unit-economics.md:114","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r7/g1-business-model-unit-economics.md:115","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w3-willingness-to-pay.md:77","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w3-willingness-to-pay.md:84","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:61","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:178","https://draftable.com/pricing","https://www.diffchecker.com/pricing/","https://obsidian.md/pricing","merged: PR9"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The plan sets no new price. Tests start from the numbers already on record.","recWhy":"The plan calls the price a hypothesis and then asks a question with only one possible answer, because the conversations carry only the low number; a team rejecting $5 and a team rejecting paying at all look identical. Two anchors in the plan are annual-billed rates quoted as monthly, and the GitHub Team $4 figure has no fetched source, so it is struck. The research gives two bands with sources. Pro at $6 to $8 against Obsidian Sync, which is the paid half of a free editor, and Team at $15 to $20 a seat against Kiro's Pro row, and Q13 adopts both as the working prices. Carrying both into the conversations costs one extra sentence, and the period is stated each time. $5 needed 1,018 subscribers to cover monthly costs once founder time was counted, which is why the low band alone was never a business. Fewer than five of twenty engaging with the higher band settles (a) honestly.","dupes":{"dropped":["B4","MK10"],"areas":["Business & operations","Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition recommended publishing no price until the twenty team conversations return, close to option d here. Business & operations recommended taking a single $30 per editor per month into them and negotiating down, above any number here. Whether cross-person review state is the paid gate is the Pro gate card in this area.","agree":false}},{"id":"PR4","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Time to revenue","weight":"critical","q":"Do we ship a checkout before the build after the pilot, or wait eighteen months to sell?","lede":"The pilot has no sign-up or billing. At the plan's measured pace, first revenue is 81 weeks out.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"Now","label":"No sign-up, no billing in the pilot","note":"The plan's tier table","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"Weeks 1-2","label":"Twenty team conversations","note":"Opinions, no money, no threshold","tone":"warn"},{"when":"About 58 weeks","label":"The pilot ships","note":"70 engineering days at 1.21 a calendar week","tone":"warn"},{"when":"About 81 weeks","label":"First point a team can pay","note":"Vault-wide refactor, shared review state and comments, the MCP server and folder-to-PDF add 28 days","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Every date derives from the density the plan measured on this repo.","caption":"The gap between the plan's first revenue event and today."},"stakes":"We could spend eighteen months building against a price nobody was ever asked to pay.","state":["The pilot ships no sign-up, billing, sync, hosting or hosted AI.","The pilot is 70 engineering days at a measured 1.21 a calendar week.","105 reports, 374,866 words, zero willingness-to-pay work."],"tension":["The services engagement is available now: four a year hit the ₹1L monthly milestone.","Failing publicly at week 2 costs less than failing privately at week 81.","Editorially closed in 2014 saying full conversion still would not have been enough."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Calendar weeks to a sellable product at measured velocity","note":"70 engineering days for the pilot plus 28 estimated days for the build after the pilot (vault-wide refactor 8 days plus shared review state and comments 10 days plus the MCP server 6 days plus folder-to-one-PDF 4 days), divided by the density in each label. Densities measured on this repo 2026-07-13 to 2026-09-08 (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227).","data":[["At 1.21 eng-days/week (measured here)",81],["At 1.91 eng-days/week (sibling repo best ever)",51],["At 0.23 eng-days/week (last 30 days here)",426]],"unit":"calendar weeks","threshold":52,"thresholdLabel":"one year"},{"type":"stat","title":"What is not sellable during that window, and what is","items":[["The pilot's billing and sign-up","both absent","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205"],["First paid signal in the plan","a team paying after 20 conversations","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206"],["Instances of waitlist, pre-order or willingness-to-pay work in the record","0 each, across 105 reports and 374,866 words","r26-c7:22 (finding D6), measured"],["The corpus's own pre-order proposal","a refusal-contract page with a paid $49 pre-order button","r26-c7:122"],["Its kill number","zero pre-orders from 100 qualified conversations kills the price, not the product","r26-c7:125"],["Revenue available now instead","Rs3,00,000 per 10-day engagement, 4/yr = Rs12,00,000","r23-u4:62, r23-u4:84"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep billing out until the build after the pilot","gains":["Cleanest pilot, no billing code to maintain"],"costs":["No money and no price evidence for 81 weeks"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No checkout, no account.","money":"None until then.","what":"Billing waits until the build after the pilot. The cleanest pilot with no billing code to maintain, and no money and no price evidence for 81 weeks."},{"k":"b","what":"A refusals page: named price, real checkout, full refund, no feature gate.","label":"Ship the pre-order page the corpus already specified","gains":["One falsifiable demand test before the build","Kill number already written: zero pre-orders from 100 conversations"],"costs":["Needs a payment rail and refund policy early","Takes money for something that does not exist"],"system":"A checkout and a refunds path.","screens":"One page: refusals, price, button.","money":"$49 refundable per pre-order."},{"k":"c","label":"Optional licence and a supporters wall from day one","gains":["Revenue from day one with no engineering"],"costs":["Voluntary money on a product with no users","Sets the anchor low and hard to move"],"system":"A payment link. No entitlement checks.","screens":"A supporters page.","money":"Small, unpredictable, immediate.","what":"An optional licence and a supporters wall from day one. Revenue from day one with no engineering; voluntary money on a product with no users sets the anchor low and hard to move."},{"k":"d","label":"Sell the services engagement instead","gains":["Revenue this month from clients who exist"],"costs":["The product gets no price evidence at all","Founder hours split; client work capped at 78 hours monthly"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹2,40,000 to ₹3,00,000 per engagement.","what":"Sell the services engagement instead and let it fund the build. Revenue this month from clients who exist; the product gets no price evidence, and founder hours split."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Our research made this criticism and nothing changed: the demand test sits after the build.","It is one page, and it turns a pricing opinion into a fact in two weeks."],"flip":"If over five teams sign an intent to buy by the build after the pilot, the pre-order adds nothing.","linked":["PR13","PR16","PR17","PR20"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:229","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:22","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:122","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:125","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:62","http://stet.editorially.com/articles/goodbye/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Checkout comes with the paid tier in phase four.","recWhy":"The pilot ships no sign-up and no billing, it is 70 engineering days at a measured 1.21 a calendar week, and 105 reports over 374,866 words contain zero willingness-to-pay work, so at the plan's own pace first revenue is 81 weeks out. Our own research made this criticism and the demand test still sits after the build. The pre-order page the corpus already specified is one page, a named price, a real checkout and a full refund, and it turns a pricing opinion into a fact in two weeks. Its kill number is already written, zero pre-orders from a hundred conversations. Failing publicly at week 2 costs less than failing privately at week 81, and Editorially closed in 2014 saying full conversion would not have been enough, the outcome a pre-order finds early. PR16 settles the rail before the first sale. Over five teams signing an intent to buy before that build makes the page redundant."},{"id":"PR8","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Commercial licence","weight":"high","q":"Do we ship an optional commercial licence alongside seats?","lede":"Obsidian made the only measured B2B licence in this category optional, and kept growing.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Obsidian licence, 2020 to Feb 2025","lines":[{"t":"Mandatory for commercial use","mark":"ok"},{"t":"$50 per user per year","mark":"ok"},{"t":"No functional benefit attached","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Unenforceable: local app, no sign-up","mark":"x"}]},"after":{"title":"From 2025-02-20","lines":[{"t":"Optional. A supporter payment","mark":"ok"},{"t":"Over 10,000 organisations, on their own page","mark":"ok"},{"t":"Money comes from Sync and Publish","mark":"ok"},{"t":"Eight staff, no investors","mark":"ok"}]},"via":"made optional, unenforceable","note":"The vendor with the largest measured base in the category stopped requiring it and did not shrink.","caption":"What happened to the only B2B licence anyone in this category has run."},"stakes":"The plan dismisses the only measured B2B mechanism in one line.","state":["One plan line dismisses it: no functional benefit, optional since February 2025.","No licence appears in the tier table.","The unit-economics report prescribes $50 per user per year, Obsidian's unit."],"tension":["A licence confers no features, so it costs nothing to ship.","The precedent had five mandatory years. We start at the voluntary end.","Obsidian's founder says the money comes from Sync and Publish, not the licence."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two readings, and the number neither could reproduce","cols":["Report","Recommendation","Key figure","Read on"],"rows":[["A research report on unit economics","Ship $50/user/year at Obsidian's exact unit","Flat licence is 59.6x under at 50 people, 119.3x at 100","2026-08-31"],["A research report on B2B licensing","Do not copy it; the precedent does not transfer","Mandatory until Feb 2025; made optional because unenforceable","2026-08-31"],["A research report on licence counts","Ship the licence motion first","146 named orgs, 17,725 licences, $886,250/yr floor","2026-08-30"],["The same B2B research report, recounted","-","84 named orgs, 16,175 licences, $808,750/yr floor; Amazon 61.8%","2026-08-31"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the licence would need to be worth","items":[["Monthly nut","about Rs1.09L","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Licences at $50/user/yr to cover it at USD/INR 95.39","274 user-years","derived: 1,08,918 x 12 / (50 x 95.39) = 274.1"],["Named orgs holding 500+ licences on the largest wall","5 of 84 (6.0%)","r26-c8:29-31, measured 2026-08-31"],["Median named logo on that wall","$1,250/yr; 11 such logos is our survival year","r26-c8:165, derived"],["Deal size a $50 unit can reach","$25,000 ACV needs 500 licences at one company","derived from the same tier table"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship an optional commercial licence alongside seats","gains":["No SSO, SCIM, audit log or SOC 2 behind it"],"costs":["May earn nothing for years","The precedent had five mandatory years we lack"],"system":"A payment link and a name on a page.","screens":"A licence page and a supporters wall.","money":"Unpredictable upside at no build cost.","what":"An optional commercial licence, $50 per user a year, alongside seats. No SSO, SCIM, audit log or SOC 2 behind it; it may earn nothing for years, and Obsidian's precedent had five mandatory years."},{"k":"b","label":"No licence; teams buy seats only","gains":["One SKU and one story"],"costs":["Forgoes the only measured B2B mechanism in the category"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Seat revenue only.","what":"No licence; teams buy seats only. One SKU and one story; it forgoes the only measured B2B mechanism in this category."},{"k":"c","label":"Licence instead of seats","gains":["One number to quote a procurement team"],"costs":["Gives up seat expansion, the main growth engine"],"system":"No seat model at all.","screens":"No invite-and-count flow.","money":"Predictable per logo, capped per logo.","what":"A licence instead of seats. One number to quote a procurement team; it gives up seat expansion, the main growth engine."},{"k":"d","label":"Re-count the wall first, decide after the conversations","gains":["One hour settles which of two reports is right"],"costs":["Delays past the meeting","The logo wall was never the measure: 10,000 organisations"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Re-count Obsidian's logo wall first and decide after the conversations. One hour settles which report is right; the wall was never the measure, Obsidian's own page names over 10,000 organisations."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The wall-count objection is moot: Obsidian's own page names over 10,000 organisations.","An honour-system Pro is a licence anyway. Naming one costs a page and no code."],"flip":"If a team asks how to pay for commercial use, then asks what it enforces, take (b).","linked":["PR2","PR21","PR3","PR7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:161","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:285","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:220","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:21","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:39","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:61","https://obsidian.md/blog/free-for-work/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Licence shapes follow the pricing decision.","recWhy":"Obsidian made the only measured B2B licence in this category optional in February 2025 and kept growing, and its own page names over 10,000 organisations, which makes the logo-wall objection moot. A licence confers no features, so it costs a page and no code, and an honour-system Pro is a licence anyway, so naming one only says out loud what PR2 already accepted. It goes alongside seats, not instead of them, because seats are the growth engine and Q13's team rung is seat-priced. The honest caveats stand: Obsidian's founder says the money comes from Sync and Publish, not the licence, and the precedent had five mandatory years we start without, so this may earn nothing for a long time. This sits after the pilot because Q13 leaves enterprise unpriced until someone asks. If a team asks how to pay for commercial use and then asks what it enforces, the answer is nothing, and (b) is cleaner."},{"id":"PR11","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Billing period","weight":"high","q":"Do we default to monthly or annual, and which business-plan page survives?","lede":"The business plan defaults to annual on one page and monthly on another: a 466-basis-point gap.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Monthly, ₹299","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Annual, ₹2,499","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"All-in payment cost","cells":["9.29% of gross","4.63% of gross"]},{"label":"Renewal events a year","cells":["Twelve","One"]},{"label":"Retries on a failed debit","cells":["One attempt, no ladder","One attempt, no ladder"]},{"label":"Surprise-charge risk","cells":["Low: the amount is small","High: bank SMS, one attempt, ₹2,499"]},{"label":"UK withdrawal windows","cells":["One, at purchase","A fresh fourteen days each renewal"]}],"note":"The annual plan is a 466-basis-point fee reduction, not a retention device.","caption":"What the billing period costs on the rail we actually run on."},"stakes":"A build team reading the two halves will implement whichever it opens first.","state":["The plan states a per-user-per-month price and no billing period.","One section defaults Indians to annual; its pricing page, to monthly.","All-in cost is 9.29% of gross monthly against 4.63% annual."],"tension":["The fee arithmetic favours annual by 466 basis points on every customer.","The Indian rail sends a pre-debit notice and gets one attempt, no retries.","A surprising ₹2,499 charge on that rail is a declined card."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the Indian payment network and the fee schedule do to each period","cols":["Constraint","Monthly","Annual"],"rows":[["Renewal events per year","12","1"],["Debit attempts per event","1, no retry","1, no retry"],["Bank pre-debit notification","12 chances to cancel","1"],["All-in payment cost (Rs299 vs Rs2,499, Dodo)","9.29% of gross","4.63% of gross - a 466 bps saving"],["Failure concentration","spread","one lumpy anniversary at the least persuadable moment"],["UK withdrawal windows","1 at purchase","1 at purchase, plus a fresh 14 days at each renewal"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Numbers the period decision must clear","items":[["AFA-free per-transaction ceiling","Rs15,000","RBI/2022-23/73, CO.DPSS.POLC.No.S-518/02.14.003/2022-23, 16 June 2022 [fetched, BUSINESS.md:593]; §82.3's own recitation of the same circular is tagged [SS] at line 209"],["A $5 annual seat at USD/INR 95.39","Rs5,723","derived - clears the ceiling with 2.6x headroom"],["Grace after a failed debit","14 days, one reminder, not a sequence","BUSINESS.md:231 and :614 - matched to the statutory withdrawal window; there is no dunning ladder to build"],["Annual plans vs monthly, churn per unit time","about 40-60% lower","BUSINESS.md:184, tagged [SS] - Profitwell/Paddle, not opened"],["Involuntary churn floor for our ARPC band","about 1.30%","Recurly $10-25 ARPC band (r25-w4:33); our ARPC sits below Recurly's lowest band, so this is a floor, not an estimate"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Monthly default, annual offered and priced","gains":["Lowest checkout-failure rate on our rail","Churn signal arrives twelve times faster"],"costs":["Concedes 466 basis points on non-annual customers","Twelve chances a year for involuntary lapse"],"system":"Two SKUs and a proration path.","screens":"Period toggle above the columns, monthly first.","money":"9.29% of gross on the monthly mix.","what":"Monthly by default, annual offered and priced. The lowest checkout-failure rate on the Indian rail and a churn signal twelve times faster; concedes 466 basis points on non-annual customers."},{"k":"b","label":"Annual default, monthly available","gains":["Captures the fee saving by default","Raises cash earlier, cuts involuntary churn"],"costs":["A surprised customer on a one-attempt rail declines","One high-variance churn event a year"],"system":"Same two SKUs, different default.","screens":"Toggle defaults to annual.","money":"4.63% of gross. Better cash position.","what":"Annual by default, monthly available. Captures the fee saving and raises cash earlier; a surprised customer on a one-attempt rail declines, and churn becomes one high-variance event a year."},{"k":"c","label":"Annual only","gains":["Simplest ledger, best fee arithmetic"],"costs":["Excludes anyone who will not commit a year"],"system":"One SKU.","screens":"No toggle.","money":"Best rail economics, smallest addressable set.","what":"Annual only. The simplest ledger and the best fee arithmetic; it excludes anyone who will not commit a year."},{"k":"d","label":"Monthly only until twenty teams pay","gains":["Nothing to build, fastest churn signal"],"costs":["Forgoes the fee saving for the whole first year"],"system":"One SKU.","screens":"No toggle.","money":"9.29% of gross throughout.","what":"Monthly only until twenty teams pay. Nothing to build and the fastest churn signal; it forgoes the fee saving for the whole first year."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Offering annual captures most of the 466 points. Defaulting to it risks a one-attempt decline.","Publish the period in the tier table and make the business plan pick one."],"flip":"If the annual mix is low enough that the fee saving goes unclaimed, switch the default.","linked":["PR12","PR13","PR16","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/BUSINESS.md:203","docs/BUSINESS.md:229","docs/BUSINESS.md:455","docs/BUSINESS.md:543","docs/BUSINESS.md:719","docs/BUSINESS.md:723"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Billing period is a checkout detail.","recWhy":"The business plan defaults to annual on one page and monthly on its pricing page, and the plan states a per-user-per-month price with no period, so the first thing to fix is that the tier table names the period at all. The fee arithmetic favours annual by 466 basis points, 9.29 percent all-in monthly against 4.63 percent annual. The Indian rail decides the default anyway: it sends a pre-debit notice and gets one attempt with no retries, and ₹15,000 per transaction is an architectural constant, so a surprising annual charge on that rail is a declined card. Monthly by default keeps the checkout failure rate lowest and delivers the churn signal twelve times faster, which matters more in the first year than the fee. Offering annual, priced, captures most of the 466 points from the people who choose it. If the annual mix stays low enough that the saving goes unclaimed, the default switches."},{"id":"PR12","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Currency","weight":"high","q":"One USD price worldwide, or a published India price and country-adjusted (PPP) bands?","lede":"The plan quotes dollars for the price and rupees for costs, with no geo policy.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Obsidian","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Craft","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"frontmatter today","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Served to an Indian IP","cells":["USD only, no rupee string","₹526.70 a month","Not decided"]},{"label":"Geo-prices to India","cells":["No","Yes, upward","No policy written"]},{"label":"The withdrawn ₹299 against it","cells":["Below it","Well below it","Withdrawn in the current plan"]},{"label":"EU signal","cells":["n/a","n/a","USD and INR only is part of the GDPR Art 3(2)(a) argument"]}],"note":"The two live comparables measured from an Indian IP do opposite things, and both charge more than our band.","caption":"What Indian buyers are actually charged by the nearest comparables."},"stakes":"A rupee price on a global page reads as domestic software, and two prices screenshot badly.","state":["Pro is quoted in USD, the monthly costs in rupees.","Nothing says whether an Indian or European buyer sees a different number.","The operating rules: price in USD and INR only, do not localise."],"tension":["Obsidian serves no rupee string; Indians pay the full dollar price.","Craft serves an Indian IP ₹526.70 a month, above our band, not below.","A GDPR Article 27 EU representative costs €420 to €852 a year, 3.6 to 7.2% of annual burn."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What comparables serve to an Indian IP, read 2026-08-31","cols":["Product","Shown to an Indian IP","Geo-priced"],"rows":[["Obsidian Sync","$4 / $5, zero rupee strings on the page","no"],["Craft Plus","Rs526.70/mo annual, Rs658.30 monthly","yes, automatically"],["Superhuman (at coda.io)","Rs1,250/mo Basic Pro","yes"],["Postman (founded Bengaluru)","USD - $9 / $19 / $49","no"],["BrowserStack, Chargebee","USD","no - Indian companies selling globally quote USD"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Where a $4-5 seat lands against those","items":[["$5 at USD/INR 95.39","Rs476.95","derived, ECB rate date 2026-08-28"],["Craft Plus, India","Rs526.70","frontmatter Pro is 0.91x of it"],["Superhuman, India","Rs1,250","frontmatter Pro is 0.38x of it"],["EU representative, if we sell into the EU","EUR420-852/yr = Rs46,645-94,622","fetched prighter.com/pricing 2026-08-31 (r26-c8:70)"],["Corpus rule on wealth-signal pricing","never by device, IP-to-income or browser; geographic PPP tiers only if disclosed and uniform within a country","BUSINESS.md:409"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One USD price worldwide, no geo-pricing","gains":["One number to publish, defend and change","No screenshot argument on Hacker News"],"costs":["Indian buyers pay the full dollar price"],"system":"One SKU per period.","screens":"One pricing page.","money":"Highest revenue per Indian customer, fewest of them.","what":"One USD price worldwide and no geo-pricing. One number to publish, defend and change, and no screenshot argument on Hacker News; Indian buyers pay the full dollar price."},{"k":"b","label":"USD list plus a published India price","gains":["Meets the home market where the network is"],"costs":["Reads as domestic-market software","₹299 undercut Indian willingness to pay"],"system":"Two price books, one checkout decision.","screens":"A country selector.","money":"Lower per Indian customer, possibly more of them.","what":"A USD list price plus a published India price. Meets the home market where the network is; reads as domestic software, and ₹299 undercuts measured Indian willingness to pay."},{"k":"c","label":"USD list plus published PPP bands","gains":["Defensible when disclosed and uniform per country"],"costs":["A policy, a table and an abuse surface","Every band is a number somebody argues with"],"system":"A country-to-band table and VPN handling.","screens":"A banded pricing page.","money":"More conversions, lower average, more support.","what":"A USD list price plus published purchasing-power bands. Defensible when disclosed and uniform per country; a policy, a table and an abuse surface, and every band is a number somebody argues with."},{"k":"d","label":"USD only until the conversations return","gains":["No wrong answer published"],"costs":["Leaves the page unfinished if checkout ships early"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No pricing page.","money":"None.","what":"USD only until the conversations return. No wrong answer published; the page stays unfinished if checkout ships early."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The category leader does not geo-price, and Indians pay its dollar rate anyway.","Both products that do geo-price to India charge more than our proposed price."],"flip":"If most of the twenty are Indian and name the dollar figure as the objection, publish an India price.","linked":["PR11","PR16","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:550","docs/BUSINESS.md:409","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:24","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:70","https://obsidian.md/pricing"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Regional pricing is a checkout detail.","recWhy":"The plan quotes dollars for the price and rupees for the costs with no geo policy, and the operating rules already say price in USD and INR only and do not localise. The category leader settles it: Obsidian serves no rupee string and Indians pay its dollar price anyway, and both products that do geo-price to India charge more than our proposed band, Craft at ₹526.70 a month. One number is one thing to publish, defend and change, and it removes the screenshot argument. A GDPR Article 27 representative at €420 to €852 a year is a cost of selling into the EU at all, not of the price policy. Q13's bands are in dollars. This sits after the pilot because PR4's pre-order page is the first place a price is shown. If most of the twenty are Indian and name the dollar figure itself as the objection, a published India price goes on the page."},{"id":"PR16","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Merchant of record","weight":"high","q":"Do we sell through a merchant of record, or bill direct and register for tax?","lede":"The plan defers it as an enterprise feature, but it decides whose name is on the invoice.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Customer","items":["Sees an invoice","Disputes to a card network"],"side":"never sees the difference","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Merchant of record","items":["Sales-tax registration and remittance","Invoice issuance","Card-network relationship","Dispute paperwork"],"side":"4% + 15¢, +0.5% subscription","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Studio Zephyrus","items":["Data-fiduciary duty, never transferred","Statutory rights above the refund floor","The product itself"],"side":"stays ours either way","tone":"sunk"}],"note":"Only the middle layer is optional, and it has to be chosen before the first sale.","caption":"What a merchant of record absorbs, and what stays ours regardless."},"stakes":"The first EU or US sale brings a tax obligation before any buyer could ask for this.","state":["It is deferred with SSO and SOC 2 until a buyer refuses without it.","Dodo charges 4% plus 15¢ on Indian cards, plus 1.5% international.","The fixed 15¢ alone is 4.79% of a ₹299 charge."],"tension":["SSO is a feature a buyer asks for. This is a legal identity.","It must be settled before the first sale, so waiting for a refusal fails.","The cut is worst precisely at the price we intend to charge."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Fee exposure on each route, all fetched 2026-08-30","items":[["Dodo Payments, headline rate","4% + 15c India domestic; 4% + 40c US domestic; +1.5% international; +0.5% subscriptions","BUSINESS.md:719"],["Dodo, fixed-cent drag on a small monthly charge","15c is 4.79% of Rs299 at USD/INR 95.39, up from 4.15% at 82.69","BUSINESS.md:723, derived"],["Dodo, dispute","$30 = Rs2,861.70","6.0x a $5 monthly seat, 7.5x a $4 seat"],["Dodo, refund","$1 = Rs95.39","0.2x a $5 seat"],["Stripe India, dispute lost","Rs2,000 plus principal","Rs1,000 if won"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the merchant of record absorbs, and what does not move","cols":["Absorbed by the MoR","Stays ours"],"rows":[["Sales-tax registration and remittance across jurisdictions","the reason the dispute happened"],["Invoice issuance and the card-network relationship","statutory rights above the MoR's own floor"],["Dispute paperwork and evidence submission","transitioning the workspace state, which only we can do"],["Payment-data controllership","data-fiduciary duty for content under DPDP and GDPR"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Merchant of record from the first sale","gains":["One counterparty handles tax, invoices and disputes","No jurisdiction arrives as a surprise"],"costs":["4 to 5.5% plus a cent charge worth 4.79%","Worst at the price we intend to charge"],"system":"One integration, one webhook, one reconciliation surface.","screens":"Their checkout, our page.","money":"9.29% of gross monthly, 4.63% annual.","what":"A merchant of record from the first sale, one counterparty for tax, invoices and disputes. No jurisdiction arrives as a surprise; 4 to 5.5 percent plus a 15 cent charge that is 4.79 percent of ₹299."},{"k":"b","label":"Bill direct; register for tax as it arises","gains":["Keeps the margin and the direct relationship"],"costs":["Cross-border registration and disputes on one person","Every new country is a project"],"system":"A billing integration plus tax logic we own.","screens":"Our own checkout.","money":"Lower cut, unbounded administrative cost.","what":"Bill direct and register for tax as it arises. Keeps the margin and the direct relationship; every new country is a project and cross-border disputes land on one person."},{"k":"c","label":"Merchant of record for non-India sales, direct for India","gains":["Keeps domestic margin, outsources hard jurisdictions"],"costs":["Two ledgers and two reconciliation surfaces for two people"],"system":"Two billing paths and a routing rule.","screens":"Two checkouts.","money":"Best blended rate, worst operational cost.","what":"Merchant of record for non-India sales, direct billing for India. Keeps domestic margin and outsources the hard jurisdictions; two ledgers and two reconciliation surfaces for two people."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer until a foreign buyer asks","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["The first EU sale arrives with VAT and no mechanism"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Nothing now, an unbounded obligation later.","what":"Defer until a foreign buyer asks. No work now; the first EU sale arrives with VAT owed and no mechanism to pay it."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["At this volume one dispute or one foreign registration costs more than the cut.","Take it off the deferred list. Nobody can refuse it before the first sale."],"flip":"If billing direct plus outsourced tax filing beats the merchant-of-record cut below 200 seats, (b) is cheaper.","linked":["L19","PR11","PR12","PR13","PR4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:535","docs/BUSINESS.md:576","docs/BUSINESS.md:580","docs/BUSINESS.md:719","docs/BUSINESS.md:723"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"How money is collected comes with checkout.","recWhy":"The plan defers this with SSO and SOC 2 until a buyer refuses without it, and it is not that kind of thing. SSO is a feature a buyer asks for, this is whose name is on the invoice, and it must be settled before the first sale, since nobody can refuse it beforehand. At this volume one dispute or one foreign tax registration costs more than the cut, and a two-person company cannot run a VAT registration per country. The cut is honest and it is worst exactly at our price, Dodo's fixed 15 cents alone is 4.79 percent of a ₹299 charge, one more reason Q13's bands sit at $6 to $8, not ₹299. Indian cards get one payment attempt, which the merchant of record handles on our behalf. It comes off the deferred list and ships with PR4's page. If direct billing plus outsourced tax filing beats the cut below 200 seats, (b) is cheaper and the switch is a rail change, not a rewrite."},{"id":"PR17","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Evidence and kill number","weight":"high","q":"How do we get pricing evidence before the build after the pilot, and what kills the paid tier?","lede":"Every other test in the plan carries a kill number. The one that decides the business does not.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Randomised price test","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Twenty conversations","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Paid pre-order","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Time to a result","cells":["57 months at plausible traffic","Weeks 1 and 2","Weeks 1 and 2"]},{"label":"What it returns","cells":["An elasticity","Seat counts and opinions","Money, or nothing"]},{"label":"Kill number today","cells":["None; struck from the plan","None written","Zero from 100 qualified"]},{"label":"What it costs","cells":["Traffic we do not have","Hours already budgeted","A checkout and a refund policy"]},{"label":"Prices it can test","cells":["Three","Whatever is in the script","One"]}],"note":"The plan has scheduled the middle column and written the threshold for the right-hand one.","caption":"Three instruments, and the only one carrying a written kill number."},"stakes":"Without a threshold written in advance the test cannot fail, and one chosen afterwards proves nothing.","state":["The paid tier stays a hypothesis until twenty conversations return seat counts.","No threshold is written, while under 4 of 10 kills review state (who has read what).","The three-price randomised test would take 57 months and was struck."],"tension":["Desk research on eleven gaps measured installs and attention, never payment.","As scheduled the conversations test only $4 to $5, so two rejections look identical.","Twenty conversations return opinions. A pre-order returns money."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Power analysis at the plan's own funnel, from BUSINESS.md, section 83.2 to 83.3","cols":["Test","n per arm","Months at 5,000 visitors/mo","Viable"],"rows":[["Paid conversion, 20% lift, 3 arms","95,455","57","no"],["Paid conversion, 50% lift, 3 arms","17,341","10.4","no - 50% is not a real hypothesis"],["Revenue per visitor, 10% difference","347,000","208","no"],["Free-signup rate, 15% drop, 2 arms","6,570","2.6","marginal, and answers a different question"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The inputs those numbers rest on","items":[["Visitor to free signup","9.0%","plan funnel assumption (BUSINESS.md:345)"],["Free to paid","5.0%","developer median, settled in the record"],["Baseline paid conversion per visitor","0.45%","derived: 0.09 x 0.05"],["Realistic month-6 traffic, solo founder, no paid acquisition","3,000-8,000 visitors/month","[inference] in BUSINESS.md:345, not measured"],["Traffic that makes the 20% test worth revisiting","about 40,000 visitors/month sustained","BUSINESS.md:386 - drops the three-arm test to 7.2 months"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Kill numbers the plan does write, and the one it does not","cols":["Test","Kill number","Where"],"rows":[["Review state as the product","under 4 of 10 unprompted","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321"],["The pilot's exit","under 6 of 10 strangers still using it after two weeks","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206"],["Obsidian plugin channel","under 200 installs in 14 days","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204"],["Pre-order page, if the checkout-timing decision goes that way","zero pre-orders from 100 qualified conversations","r26-c7:125"],["Paid tier / 20 team conversations","none written","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the conversations have to establish","items":[["Seat counts per team","unmeasured","the input to every revenue number in §11"],["What teams are gated on","unmeasured","the input to PR1's tier contents"],["Whether anyone pays for review state in any shell","still open after two adversarial rounds","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"],["Willingness-to-pay work anywhere in the programme","0 instances across 105 reports and 374,866 words","r26-c7:22, measured"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Kill under 5 of 20 naming a gate at a named price; both prices in the script","gains":["Falsifiable, and it tests the axis as well","One script answers price and kill together"],"costs":["Twenty is small and the signal is soft","Interviews over-report acceptable prices"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None spent. The answer arrives in week 2.","what":"Kill the paid tier if fewer than 5 of 20 name a gate at a named price, with both bands in the script. Falsifiable and it tests the axis too; twenty is small and interviews over-report prices."},{"k":"b","label":"Kill under 3 of 20 giving a seat count and a budget line","gains":["A harder bar that filters talk from procurement"],"costs":["Three of twenty is a thin thread","A 15% hit rate is noise with twenty people"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Kill under 3 of 20 giving a seat count and a budget line. A harder bar that filters talk from procurement; three of twenty is a thin thread and 15 percent is noise at this size."},{"k":"c","label":"Replace the conversations with a paid pre-order","gains":["The only instrument returning a fact, not an opinion","The kill number is already written and dated"],"costs":["Needs a checkout before the product exists","Tests one price, not the axis"],"system":"A checkout and a refunds path.","screens":"One page.","money":"$49 a pre-order, refundable.","what":"Replace the conversations with a paid pre-order. The only instrument that returns a fact rather than an opinion, and its kill number is already dated; it tests one price, not the axis."},{"k":"d","label":"Free-signup-rate test only, labelled price anchoring","gains":["The one randomised measurement that fits the traffic"],"costs":["Answers signup suppression, not what anyone pays","2.6 months is most of the window"],"system":"Two landing variants and a counter.","screens":"Two pricing pages.","money":"None, and no revenue evidence.","what":"A free-signup-rate test only, labelled price anchoring. The one randomised measurement the traffic can support; it answers signup suppression, not what anyone pays, in 2.6 months."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Five of twenty is high enough that a polite yes fails, low enough a niche survives.","Put both candidate prices in the script, and fold willingness-to-pay into the same conversations."],"flip":"If we ship the pre-order page, (c) replaces this gate.","linked":["G15","PL7","PR20","PR3","PR4","PR7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/BUSINESS.md:384","docs/BUSINESS.md:399","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:22","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c7-d2c.md:125","merged: PR18"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Pricing evidence comes from the pilot.","recWhy":"Every other test in the plan carries a kill number and the one that decides the business does not. The paid tier stays a hypothesis until twenty conversations return seat counts, with no threshold written, while under 4 of 10 already kills review state. Desk research on eleven gaps measured installs and attention and never payment, and as scheduled the conversations test only $4 to $5, so two different rejections look identical. Five of twenty naming a gate at a named price is high enough that a polite yes fails and low enough that a niche survives, and putting both of Q13's bands in the script tests the axis in the same call. Q15 sets the pilot's own pass marks beside this one. The three-price randomised test would take 57 months and stays struck. If PR4's pre-order page ships, the pre-order replaces this gate, because money is a fact and a conversation is an opinion.","dupes":{"dropped":["R16"],"areas":["Research & evidence"],"why":"Research & evidence asked whether the two-week tests ask about money, and recommended the same as option a here: a price question with both price rungs in the script. It would ask it in all forty conversations, not only the twenty with teams. It sets no kill threshold.","agree":true}},{"id":"PR20","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Services line","weight":"high","q":"Do we sell the services engagement as the first revenue line, at ten days or eight?","lede":"The plan funds the build with consulting hours and lists the same engagement as its last upsell.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Ten days, ₹3,00,000","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Eight days, ₹2,40,000","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Sellable this month","cells":["Not without the kappa","Yes"]},{"label":"What days 7-8 sell","cells":["Eval discipline: kappa 0.1 against a 0.7 bar, n=9","Withheld until the kappa is honest"]},{"label":"Four a year","cells":["₹12,00,000, exactly the monthly nut","4 x ₹2,40,000 = ₹9,60,000"]},{"label":"Line item on the rate card","cells":["Absent","Absent"]},{"label":"Against the product funnel","cells":["One client equals 63,060 visitors","Same"]}],"note":"The engagement and the funding line are the same money, counted once.","caption":"The first revenue line, priced two ways, against what is defensible today."},"stakes":"The one revenue line available this month is priced on a discipline whose own measurement failed.","state":["The engagement sits in the upsell table at ₹3,00,000 fixed scope.","The ₹1.09L monthly costs are covered by 54 to 78 consulting hours.","Its eval kappa was measured once: 0.1 against a 0.7 bar, on nine samples."],"tension":["The upsell runs through the docs scan, a pilot feature about a year away.","Days 7 to 8 sell an eval discipline whose own measurement failed.","Engagement hours are hours not spent on the 81-week build."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"One engagement against the product funnel","items":[["Four engagements a year","Rs12,00,000 = Rs1,00,000/month","r23-u4:62"],["Product path to the same Rs1L/month","284 paying users, 5,675 free signups, 63,060 cumulative visitors","r23-u4:62, from the record's own conversion rates"],["Founder time for four engagements","360 hours = 16.6% of 2,172 annual engineering hours","r23-u4:62, derived"],["Price at eight days, days 7-8 withheld","Rs2,40,000","r23-u4:99"],["The kappa that gates days 7-8","0.1 against a 0.7 bar, n=9, dark 27 days","r23-u4:10, measured"],["Line item on the studio rate card today","none","r23-u4:105"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two funding lines the plan carries separately","cols":["Line","Amount","Where"],"rows":[["Monthly nut","about Rs1.09L","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Consulting hours to cover it","54-78/month","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Productised engagement","Rs3,00,000 fixed scope, listed as an upsell","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:445, :465"],["Four of them a year","Rs12,00,000 = the Rs1L/month milestone","r23-u4:62"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Sell the ten-day engagement at ₹3,00,000 now","gains":["Revenue this month from clients who exist","Four a year cover all monthly costs"],"costs":["Days 7 to 8 sell a kappa of 0.1","Not quotable from the rate card today"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None; the docs scan is the diagnostic later.","money":"₹12,00,000 a year at four engagements.","what":"Sell the ten-day engagement at ₹3,00,000 now. Revenue this month from clients who exist and four a year cover all monthly costs; days 7 and 8 sell an eval discipline whose kappa measured 0.1."},{"k":"b","label":"Sell an eight-day version at ₹2,40,000, evals as phase two","gains":["Sellable this month with nothing outstanding","Eighty per cent of revenue, none of the objection"],"costs":["Leaves the highest-value days until the kappa is honest","Still forty hours a month off the build"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹9,60,000 a year at four engagements.","what":"Sell an eight-day version at ₹2,40,000, evals as a later phase. Sellable this month, eighty percent of the revenue and none of the objection; forty hours a month off the build."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep it as a downstream upsell from the docs scan","gains":["Keeps the founder's hours on the build"],"costs":["The lead-generating scan is a year away","No conversion data for any productised diagnostic"],"system":"No change.","screens":"The scan's proposal list becomes the brief.","money":"None for about a year.","what":"Keep it as a downstream upsell from the docs scan. Keeps the founder's hours on the build; the scan that generates the lead is a year away and no diagnostic has conversion data."},{"k":"d","label":"Do not sell it; protect the build","gains":["Maximum product velocity"],"costs":["Contradicts the funding line assuming 54 to 78 client hours"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"No services revenue, no funding.","what":"Do not sell it and protect the build. Maximum product velocity; it contradicts the funding line that assumes 54 to 78 client hours a month."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Eight days at ₹2,40,000 is revenue this month at a scope our research defends.","It removes the one honest objection, and the services report proposes that shape itself."],"flip":"If a client will pay for the eval days specifically, re-run the kappa first, then sell (a).","linked":["PR17","PR4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:445","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:465","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:10","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:62","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:105"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Services revenue is a separate line from the product.","recWhy":"The plan funds the build with 54 to 78 consulting hours a month and then lists the same engagement as its last upsell, behind a docs scan about a year away. The engagement exists now, and the only honest objection to it is days 7 and 8. They sell an eval discipline whose own measurement failed, a kappa of 0.1 against a 0.7 bar on nine samples, and selling a judge before its alignment passes is the guess this product refuses elsewhere. Eight days at ₹2,40,000 removes that objection, is sellable this month at a scope the services report itself proposes, and covers the ₹1.09 lakh monthly cost with just over four sales a year. The cost is real: forty hours a month off an 81-week build. B2, how the build is funded, is the fact behind this card and the founders answer it on the site. If a client will pay for the eval days specifically, the kappa is re-run first and then (a) is sold."},{"id":"PR23","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Unit of sale","weight":"high","q":"Is the CI gate priced per repository or per seat?","lede":"Every comparable that sells a documentation check prices per site or per project. The plan prices it per person.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Per seat","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Per repository","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Who buys it","cells":["Every reader on the team","The one person who wants the gate"]},{"label":"What the comparables do","cells":["GitBook: $12 per user per month","Mintlify Pro $450/mo; ReadMe Pro $250/mo"]},{"label":"GitBook charges both","cells":["$12 per user per month","plus $65 per site per month"]},{"label":"Enforceable at all","cells":["Honour system, per the journal decision","The Action runs or it does not"]},{"label":"Bills readers who never review","cells":["Yes","No"]}],"note":"The plan calls the gate the B2B line but never says what unit it sells in.","caption":"The unit a CI gate is bought in, against what the comparables charge."},"stakes":"Enforcement is the one paid thing with a natural payment checkpoint, and per-seat wastes it.","state":["The upsell table routes the gate to Max, comparing CI seats.","The family table sells the CI check, `mdmax cert`, per team.","The plan pulled it into Pro per user. No document names the unit."],"tension":["A CI check is bought by the repo owner, not by everyone reading.","Mintlify, GitBook and ReadMe all price per site or per project.","Only per repo can the GitHub Action check payment."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Per seat, folded into Pro as written","gains":["One SKU and one price to explain"],"costs":["Bills readers who never see a CI run","Wastes the one enforceable checkpoint"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Revenue scales with readers, not repos.","what":"Per seat, folded into Pro as written. One SKU and one price to explain; it bills readers who never see a CI run and wastes the one enforceable checkpoint."},{"k":"b","label":"Per repository, sold separately from seats","gains":["Matches every docs-platform comparable","The buyer is the repo owner who wants it"],"costs":["Two paid objects on one page","Needs repo-level identity, which nothing builds today"],"system":"A repo-scoped entitlement the GitHub Action checks.","screens":"A repos list with a per-repo toggle.","money":"Priced against $250 to $450 monthly comparables.","what":"Per repository, sold separately from seats. Matches every docs-platform comparable; the buyer is the repo owner who wants it. Two paid objects on one page, and repo identity nothing builds today."},{"k":"c","label":"Per organisation, flat, any number of repos","gains":["One number for a procurement conversation"],"costs":["One number must fit two repos and two hundred"],"system":"An org-scoped entitlement.","screens":"One switch per organisation.","money":"Predictable per logo, capped per logo.","what":"Per organisation, flat, any number of repos. One number for a procurement conversation; the same number must fit two repos and two hundred."},{"k":"d","label":"Do not price it until the command runs","gains":["Refuses to price a command that exits with an error"],"costs":["Leaves Pro without its enforceable half"],"system":"Wire the command; no billing work.","screens":"None.","money":"None until it runs.","what":"No price until mdmax cert runs. Refuses to price a command that exits with an error; it leaves Pro without its enforceable half."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The buyer of a build gate owns the build, and every comparable charges per project.","Per repo keeps the seat price low while the gate carries a number docs platforms justify."],"flip":"If the same person buys seats and the gate and wants one invoice, fold back into (a).","linked":["E12","PR1","PR2","PR21","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:288","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:443","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:475","https://mintlify.com/pricing"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The gate being priced is deferred.","recWhy":"A CI check is bought by the owner of the repository, not by everyone who reads it, and Mintlify, GitBook and ReadMe all price theirs per site or per project. The plan is the only document in the set that prices a build gate per person. Per repository also matches the one place payment can be enforced, because a GitHub Action can check a repo's entitlement and cannot check a reader's. Keeping the gate off the seat price keeps Q13's Pro band low while the gate carries a number the docs platforms have already justified. The costs are two paid objects on one page and repo-level identity, which nothing builds today, and mdmax cert itself exits with a module error, so this is priced after the pilot and after the command runs. If the same person buys seats and the gate and wants one invoice, it folds back into (a)."},{"id":"PR13","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Refunds and withdrawal","weight":"medium","q":"What refund and withdrawal terms ship at checkout?","lede":"One chargeback costs thirty dollars plus the principal. A refund costs one. Neither policy is written.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"Refund on request","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Non-refundable line","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Cost at a 2% refund rate","cells":["About 2.08% of gross","Nil"]},{"label":"Per dispute at Dodo","cells":["$1 per refund, dispute avoided","$30 per dispute, plus the principal"]},{"label":"EU fourteen-day right","cells":["Subsumed; no waiver flow needed","Waiver needed, and getting it wrong makes the supply free"]},{"label":"Fits the rest of the plan","cells":["Yes; the files are theirs either way","No; it contradicts every other trust position"]},{"label":"India e-commerce rules 2020","cells":["Policy must be displayed","Policy must be displayed"]}],"note":"At this price the refund is cheap and the dispute is not.","caption":"Refund posture, and what it does to the EU withdrawal question."},"stakes":"A slightly wrong waiver makes the supply free by law, and no policy turns remorse into a chargeback.","state":["The pricing section says nothing about refunds or withdrawal.","The business plan drafts the full policy: 30-day first payment, 90-day prorated renewal.","It specifies the three EU waiver artefacts, then advises against them."],"tension":["Benchmarks are bimodal: Obsidian writes non-refundable, 37signals refunds on request.","One dispute costs six to seven and a half months of a seat.","The 90-day prorated-renewal clause as drafted has no cap."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What a refund and a dispute each cost at a $4-5 seat","items":[["Dodo refund fee","$1 = Rs95.39","fetched dodopayments.com/pricing, 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:580)"],["Dodo dispute fee","$30 = Rs2,861.70","same; at USD/INR 95.39"],["Dispute against a $4 seat","7.50x the monthly charge","derived"],["Dispute against a $5 seat","6.00x the monthly charge","derived"],["Stripe India, dispute lost","Rs2,000 plus principal","BUSINESS.md:580, fetched 2026-08-30; Rs1,000 if won"],["Cost of the generous policy","2.08% of gross at a 2% refund rate","BUSINESS.md:518 - 2.74-3.51% of contribution (BUSINESS.md:524)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two shapes in the benchmark set","cols":["Product","Policy","Read"],"rows":[["Obsidian","\"All fees are non-cancellable and non-refundable and are based on Services purchased and not actual usage\"","obsidian.md/terms, 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:530)"],["Paddle","non-refundable unless law requires; higher local consumer rights always apply","paddle.com/legal/refund-policy, 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:531)"],["37signals","refund on request for a forgotten renewal; prorated \"for the remaining whole months\"; 60 days of data kept to migrate","basecamp.com/about/policies/refund, 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:532)"],["frontmatter, drafted","30 days first charge, worldwide; 90-day prorated renewal; same-day refund instead of a chargeback","BUSINESS.md:644-664"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What each path costs","items":[["Waiver taken correctly","0","needs three artefacts, all three, every time"],["Waiver taken incorrectly","the whole supply is free","Art 14(4)(b), fetched EUR-Lex 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:542)"],["Right granted unconditionally","at most 14 days of one subscription per withdrawal","at $5/month that is about $2.30"],["EU representative, due from the first EU seat","EUR420-852/yr = Rs46,645-94,622","fetched prighter.com/pricing, 2026-08-31 (r26-c8:70)"],["UK annual renewals","a fresh 14-day window at every renewal","Paddle, fetched paddle.com/legal/refund-policy 2026-08-30 (BUSINESS.md:543)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The three artefacts the exemption needs","cols":["Artefact","Requirement"],"rows":[["Prior express consent","separate unticked checkbox, not bundled into terms; store boolean, exact string version, timestamp"],["Performance actually begun","the provisioning event, timestamped"],["Durable-medium confirmation","receipt email repeating the acknowledgement text back (Art 7(2) / 8(7))"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship the drafted policy; grant the withdrawal right unconditionally","gains":["Removes most dispute cost for 2.08% of gross","Deletes a compliance surface and a checkout step"],"costs":["Forgoes the revenue a waiver would retain"],"system":"A refunds path. No consent capture.","screens":"A refunds page and one checkout line.","money":"About 2.08% of gross at a 2% refund rate.","what":"Ship the drafted policy: a thirty-day refund on the first payment, a capped prorated refund on renewal, the EU withdrawal right unconditional. Removes most dispute cost for 2.08 percent of gross."},{"k":"b","label":"Non-refundable, with the waiver taken properly","gains":["The category norm for file-first tools"],"costs":["A slightly wrong waiver makes the supply free","Each dispute costs months of that seat"],"system":"Consent capture, performance-start event, durable-medium receipt.","screens":"An unticked box with statutory wording.","money":"Highest retained revenue, highest tail risk.","what":"Non-refundable, with the EU waiver taken properly. The category norm for file-first tools; a slightly wrong waiver makes the supply free, and each dispute costs months of that seat."},{"k":"c","label":"Thirty-day first-charge refund only","gains":["Covers remorse at a bounded cost"],"costs":["Leaves the forgotten annual renewal, which drives chargebacks"],"system":"A refunds path with a date check.","screens":"One line at checkout.","money":"Bounded, smaller than (a).","what":"A thirty-day first-charge refund only. Covers remorse at a bounded cost; it leaves the forgotten annual renewal, which is what drives chargebacks."},{"k":"d","label":"No published policy until there is a customer","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["India's e-commerce rules require the policy displayed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"An obligation the day checkout ships.","what":"No published policy until there is a customer. No work now; India's e-commerce rules require the policy displayed before the first sale."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The asymmetry decides it: a $5 refund is trivial, a dispute is $30 plus principal.","The policy is written and checked against four fetched benchmarks. Cap the prorated clause and publish."],"flip":"If the measured refund rate goes above 2%, turn the thirty-day window, not the policy.","linked":["PR11","PR16","PR4"],"sources":["docs/BUSINESS.md:518","docs/BUSINESS.md:531","docs/BUSINESS.md:542","docs/BUSINESS.md:543","docs/BUSINESS.md:552","docs/BUSINESS.md:580","docs/BUSINESS.md:644","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:550","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c8-b2b.md:70","merged: PR15"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Refund terms come with checkout.","recWhy":"One chargeback costs thirty dollars plus the principal and a refund costs one, so a single dispute is six to seven and a half months of a seat, and the asymmetry decides the policy on its own. The business plan already drafts it, thirty days on the first payment and a prorated refund on renewal, checked against four fetched benchmarks that are bimodal, Obsidian non-refundable and 37signals refunding on request. Granting the EU withdrawal right unconditionally deletes a compliance surface and a checkout step for 2.08 percent of gross, and the drafted clause gets a cap before it ships because as written it has none. India's e-commerce rules require the policy displayed, so (d) is not available once checkout exists, and PR4 puts checkout early. If the measured refund rate goes above 2 percent, the thirty-day window is the dial to turn, not the policy."},{"id":"PR19","cat":"Pricing & tiers","sub":"Grandfathering","weight":"medium","q":"Do early subscribers keep their price forever, for three years, or until the next change?","lede":"Break-even sits between what $4 and $5 buy. A perpetual promise may mean running near cost forever.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Founding cohort","meta":"price held, stated at checkout","tone":"accent","current":true},{"label":"Continuous","meta":"price held","via":"keeps paying","tone":"good"},{"label":"Lapsed 60 days","meta":"price forfeit","via":"lapse","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Tier changed","meta":"current price applies","via":"upgrade","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"The drafted policy already names every transition; only the population it covers is open.","caption":"When a held price is forfeited under the policy BUSINESS.md drafted."},"stakes":"A perpetual promise made at a near-cost price cannot be withdrawn without breaking it publicly.","state":["The plan has one line: price changes, email before the next charge.","The business plan drafts a full policy and then argues against it.","Break-even at the founder's own rate is ₹333.25 a month."],"tension":["A three-year sunset costs goodwill with the earliest users, who talk.","The break-even input is unsettled: support tickets are 0.10 and 0.30 in one repo.","Two policies side by side invite the question from customer 101."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Why the promise is expensive here specifically","items":[["Break-even monthly price at Rs1,700/h founder time","Rs333.25","r26-c6:45, from 0.1445 founder-hours per paying user per month"],["$4 a seat at USD/INR 95.39","Rs381.56","derived; 1.14x break-even"],["$5 a seat at USD/INR 95.39","Rs476.95","derived; 1.43x break-even"],["Support cost per paying user per month","Rs245.65","r26-c6:22, the largest single line"],["Confidence in that line","low - the coefficient is stated as 0.10 and 0.30 in the same repo","r26-c6:50, a 3x spread"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The drafted policy's forfeit events","cols":["Event","Legacy price"],"rows":[["Subscription stays continuous","kept, for as long as it is continuous"],["Lapse over 60 days, then resubscribe","forfeit - stops churn-and-return arbitrage"],["Tier upgrade","forfeit; new tier at current list"],["Annual to monthly","forfeit; the annual discount was consideration for the commitment"],["Currency or PPP reassignment","never applied retroactively"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Perpetual while the subscription is continuous","gains":["Strongest trust signal, easiest to state"],"costs":["Caps blended revenue permanently near break-even","The break-even input is uncertain threefold"],"system":"A price snapshot per account.","screens":"One line at checkout.","money":"Locks early revenue close to cost.","what":"The early price is perpetual while the subscription stays continuous. The strongest trust signal and the easiest to state; it caps blended revenue near a break-even that is uncertain threefold."},{"k":"b","label":"Three-year sunset with ninety days' notice","gains":["Standard, and lets the price move later"],"costs":["Costs goodwill with the cohort supplying word of mouth"],"system":"A price snapshot plus an expiry date.","screens":"A notice in the in-product list.","money":"Recovers revenue at year three.","what":"A three-year sunset with ninety days' notice. Standard, and the price can move later; it costs goodwill with the cohort that supplies word of mouth."},{"k":"c","label":"No grandfathering at all","gains":["Simplest ledger, no legacy prices"],"costs":["Removes a real reason to buy early"],"system":"No snapshot needed.","screens":"The price-change email rule, already written.","money":"Full flexibility on price.","what":"No grandfathering at all. The simplest ledger with no legacy prices; it removes a real reason to buy early."},{"k":"d","label":"Perpetual for the first hundred, then no grandfathering","gains":["Buys the founding cohort at a countable cost","Leaves the price free to move afterwards"],"costs":["Two policies to publish and one number to defend"],"system":"A snapshot on the first hundred accounts.","screens":"Both policies visible at checkout.","money":"Caps the concession at a hundred accounts.","what":"Perpetual for the first hundred subscribers, then none. Buys the founding cohort at a countable cost and leaves the price free afterwards; two policies to publish and one number to defend."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The first hundred matter more as advocates than as revenue, so their price can be a promise.","Everyone after sits under a policy that can move, because break-even is close."],"flip":"If the pilot measures support tickets at the low end, (a) becomes affordable and reads better.","linked":["PR11","PR17","PR3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:536","docs/BUSINESS.md:419","docs/BUSINESS.md:426","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:45","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c6-unit-economics.md:50"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Price grandfathering comes with checkout.","recWhy":"Break-even at the founder's own rate is ₹333.25 a month per seat, which sits between what $4 and $5 buy, and the support input behind it is unsettled, 0.10 and 0.30 tickets per user in one repository. A perpetual promise to everyone could mean running near cost forever; no promise at all removes the one reason to buy before the product is finished. The first hundred matter more as advocates than as revenue, so their price can be a promise at a countable cost, and everyone after sits under a policy that can move, because break-even is close and Q13's bands are still working numbers. The cost is two policies side by side and the question from customer 101, which is answered with the number. This sits after the pilot because PR4's page is where the first hundred are counted. If the pilot measures support at the low end, (a) becomes affordable and reads better."},{"id":"G15","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"First hundred","weight":"critical","q":"Where do the ten pilot strangers and the twenty teams come from, and who approaches them?","lede":"Everything after week 3 depends on ten people nobody has identified, from a query never run.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Visitors a loop would have to deliver","n":22222,"unit":"visitors","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Free signups at 9%","n":2000,"unit":"signups","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Paying customers at 5%","n":100,"unit":"customers","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"No measured loop produces the top figure at n=0.","caption":"What a loop would owe, against zero measured loops."},"stakes":"The pilot's exit test, the price hypothesis and the two-week test all rest on people we have not found.","state":["The plan's ten pilot users bring their own repos and agents; no source is named.","The pricing section keeps the paid tier a hypothesis until twenty team conversations.","The code-search query that would find them is written down and has never been run."],"tension":["No customer interviews exist across 105 reports and 374,866 words.","Cold booking twenty conversations in a fortnight is optimistic at 1.21 days a week.","A warm no is indistinguishable from politeness, so the ten must be strangers."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The instrument, and its status","items":[["The query","Public repos containing both CLAUDE.md and a hand-written handover/context file","docs/THESIS.md:747"],["What it yields","A qualified lead with a visible email in git log","docs/THESIS.md:747"],["Times run","0: \"has never been run, by the record or by me\"","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1191"],["Why not","Unauthenticated /search/code returns 401; gh fails TLS through the research proxy. The record said so and the auditor confirmed it","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1102"],["Customer-discovery vocabulary across 105 reports and 374,866 words","0 instances each of customer discovery, design partner, interviews, waitlist, beta tester, pre-order, user test, willingness to pay","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1102"],["What the critique says is missing","\"Ten named humans, with emails, in a file, who have said they want this\"","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1102"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why a loop cannot supply the ten, or the hundred","items":[["Visitors needed for the first 100 paying customers","22,222 (100 divided by 5% conversion = 2,000 signups, divided by 9% visitor-to-signup)","r25-w4:90"],["Measured loops that produce them at n=0","None","r25-w4:92"],["Corpus conclusion","\"The first hundred cannot come from a loop; it must be hand-recruited, one at a time, from people the founder can name\"","r25-w4:94"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What rests on the twenty conversations","items":[["Round-2 verdict on payment","\"Whether anyone pays for review state in any shell\": eleven gaps measured installs and attention, never payment","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"],["What Pro sells at the build after the pilot","Shared review state, comments and MCP: the first is what GitHub Team bundles at $4, the other two untested","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:54"],["Retention band the tier sits in","Top-quartile annual gross retention 60 to 70% under $50/month; 23% for AI-native products under $50","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Withdrawn earlier claim","\"114 seats at $8 covers the nut\": withdrawn because the feature was GitHub's at $4 and the band would require replacing 34-46 of 114 seats a year","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Monthly nut the tier would have to reach","about ₹1.09L, currently covered by 54 to 78 consulting hours","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Founder capacity in the same fortnight","1.21 engineering days per calendar week measured; 0.23 in the last 30 days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Run the code search now; recruit both cohorts cold from that list","gains":["Unbiased on both sides","Produces the programme's first named humans"],"costs":["Twenty cold conversations are not bookable in a fortnight","Contacting the list is a separate decision"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"An afternoon, plus a token already held.","what":"Run the written code search now and recruit both cohorts cold from the list. Unbiased on both sides and produces the first named humans; twenty cold conversations are not bookable in a fortnight."},{"k":"b","label":"Recruit both cohorts from the studio's network","gains":["Fastest to book","Everyone answers"],"costs":["Ten friendly reads falsify nothing","A warm no reads as politeness"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Recruit both cohorts from the studio's network. Fastest to book and everyone answers; ten friendly reads falsify nothing, and a warm no reads as politeness."},{"k":"c","label":"Cold list for the ten; ten warm and ten cold for the twenty","gains":["The exit test stays a stranger test","Twenty become bookable inside a fortnight"],"costs":["Halves the sample each side of the price question","Depends on the search running this week"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"An afternoon for the search.","what":"The cold list for the ten strangers; ten warm and ten cold for the twenty teams. The exit test stays a stranger test and twenty become bookable; it halves the sample each side of the price question."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer the twenty conversations until after the pilot","gains":["Removes a fortnight of load from a one-person team","The ten still run on schedule"],"costs":["The build proceeds with the revenue hypothesis untested","The pricing section already calls that the open question"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"The paid tier stays unevidenced.","what":"Defer the twenty conversations until after the pilot. Removes a fortnight of load from one person; the build proceeds with the revenue hypothesis untested."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The exit test proves nothing without strangers, so the ten come from the cold list.","A warm-versus-cold split on the twenty is itself the finding, and it is bookable."],"flip":"If the search returns fewer than fifty qualified repos with visible emails, the network is all there is.","linked":["G16","G22","G27","PR17"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:313","docs/THESIS.md:747","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1102","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1191","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:90","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:94","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:54","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","merged: G17"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Phase zero needs twenty people outside the studio. Where they come from blocks the first step.","dependsOn":["R1"],"recWhy":"Everything after week 3 depends on ten people nobody has identified, from a code-search query that is written down and has never been run, and across 105 reports and 374,866 words there are no customer interviews at all. The exit test in Q15 proves nothing without strangers, because a warm no is indistinguishable from politeness, so the ten come from the cold list. Twenty cold conversations in a fortnight at 1.21 days a week is optimistic, and the warm-versus-cold split on the twenty is itself a finding: warm teams saying yes while cold teams do not is an answer. The cost is a halved sample each side of the price question, which PR17's kill number allows for. Q2 says who the twenty are, teams running coding agents, found by the files they commit. Under fifty qualified repositories with visible emails means the network is all there is.","dupes":{"dropped":["R3"],"areas":["Research & evidence"],"why":"Research & evidence recommended recruiting from the founders' client list, plus sgnk-md's users if that list exists, which is the warm-network route of option b here. Contacting sgnk-md's users as one source is a separate card in this area.","agree":false}},{"id":"G16","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"First hundred","weight":"high","q":"Do we contact sgnk-md's existing users?","lede":"They are the only real users this codebase has had, and nobody has asked them anything.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Today","lines":[{"t":"0 interviews across the whole programme","mark":"stop"},{"t":"Every demand claim inferred from public posts","mark":"warn"},{"t":"202 of 228 files shared with sgnk-md"}]},"after":{"title":"If a user list exists","lines":[{"t":"Ten people who have used this codebase","mark":"good"},{"t":"Note-takers, not agent operators","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Outbound to people who signed up for another product","mark":"warn"}]},"via":"one founder answer","caption":"The only real users this codebase may have, and their limit."},"stakes":"Demand claims are inferred from public complaints, while a live user list may sit nearby.","state":["The gap register lists sgnk-md's users as untapped and waiting on a founder decision.","202 of sgnk-md's 228 files are shared with frontmatter.","The whole programme has conducted zero interviews so far."],"tension":["They are note-takers, so they cannot settle the agent-review question.","They signed up for a different product, with its own consent history.","Talking to them first risks contaminating the stranger test the plan gates on."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"How closely the two products are the same thing","items":[["Shared source files","202 of 228 identical","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:479"],["Editor files byte-identical to sgnk-md","41 of 43","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141"],["Files unique to frontmatter in the editor shell","0","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141"],["Still visible in the shipped source, checked 2026-09-09","src/app/sitemap.ts opens \"sgnk-md sitemap\"; sgnk-md string literals in the drafts store, bookmarks, editor store, instrumentation and CodeMirrorEditor; src-tauri productName is \"sgnk-md\""],["Interviews conducted across the whole programme","0","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35"],["What it would cost to tap","\"Founders: is there a user list, and may we contact ten?\"","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:36"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Establish whether a list exists, and ask ten of them","gains":["The cheapest interviews available","The only people with a real usage history"],"costs":["Wrong population for the agent-review question","Outbound to people who signed up elsewhere"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Establish whether a list exists, and ask ten of sgnk-md's users. The cheapest interviews available and the only people with a real usage history; the wrong population for the agent question."},{"k":"b","label":"Look at usage data only; no outreach","gains":["No consent question and no imposition","Available without waiting on a founder decision"],"costs":["Answers how, never why","Nothing about willingness to pay"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Look at usage data only, with no outreach. No consent question and no waiting on a founder; it answers how, never why, and nothing about willingness to pay."},{"k":"c","label":"No; sgnk-md's users are the wrong population for this buyer","gains":["Keeps the pilot clean","No outbound to a list collected elsewhere"],"costs":["Throws away the only existing users the codebase has","Leaves zero interviews standing"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No; they are the wrong population for this buyer. Keeps the pilot clean; it throws away the only users this codebase has had and leaves zero interviews standing."},{"k":"d","label":"Ask them, but only after the ten strangers","gains":["Preserves the exit test's independence"],"costs":["Two months of latency on the cheapest interviews"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ask them, but only after the ten strangers. Preserves the exit test's independence; two months of latency on the cheapest interviews there are."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Zero interviews is the largest hole in the programme, and this is the cheapest way to non-zero.","The founders can say whether the list exists in a sentence. That is where it is stuck."],"flip":"If the list is empty or was never collected, the ten strangers are the only interviews available.","linked":["G15"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:36","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:112","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:479"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The sibling has users and the MVP wears its look. Whether we approach them is a now question.","recWhy":"sgnk-md's users are the only real users this codebase has had, 202 of its 228 files are shared with frontmatter, and nobody has asked them anything; the whole programme stands at zero interviews. They are note-takers, so they cannot settle the agent-review question Q2 asks, and they signed up for a different product with its own consent history, both of which the ask has to respect. Even so, ten conversations with people who have used the editor are the cheapest way from zero to non-zero, and the editor is what Q3 ships first. The stranger test stays separate: these are not the ten and are not counted as them. The question is stuck on a fact only the founders have, whether a list exists at all. If it is empty or was never collected, the ten strangers are the only interviews available, and G15 carries the whole weight."},{"id":"G12","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Published pages","weight":"medium","q":"Do exported and shared pages carry a frontmatter mark?","lede":"The plan defers this until measured, and a mark already ships on one of the two surfaces.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"Our hosted note","tone":"warn"},{"title":"A page they deployed","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Whose domain","cells":["frontmatter.in","Theirs"]},{"label":"Mark today","cells":["\"Hosted on frontmatter.in\" footer","None, in 945 lines of export code"]},{"label":"Which research objection bites","cells":["Only file-is-truth: markup with no byte in the file","All three, including the link-scheme pattern"]},{"label":"Who decided it","cells":["Nobody. It arrived with the feature","The plan defers it as unmeasurable"]}],"note":"The research objects to a backlink on a user's own page. Only one column is that.","caption":"One mark ships unreviewed; the other is deferred as immeasurable."},"stakes":"A footer nobody decided on already ships on our hosted pages, while the export mark is deferred.","state":["The plan defers marked exports to a later build, as not a channel until measured.","The hosted note already carries a \"Hosted on frontmatter.in\" footer.","Checked 2026-09-09: the 945-line export module carries no mark."],"tension":["The research objects to links on a user's domain, which does not exist yet.","Its third objection applies here: the footer is markup that is not in the file.","The export cut used Obsidian download ratios, which count note-takers."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The two surfaces, and what the research actually objects to","items":[["Mark on the hosted public note","\"Hosted on frontmatter.in\", href=\"/\", on the same domain as the note itself","src/modules/share/presentation/PublicNoteView.tsx:29"],["Mark on exported output","None. No mark anywhere in the 945-line export module (checked 2026-09-09)"],["Research position","\"No automatic backlink to our marketing site from user pages\"","r17-h7:111"],["Reasons given","The user's page becomes an advertisement they did not agree to run; tens of thousands of identical footer links is the classic link-scheme pattern; it contradicts file-is-truth because the footer is markup we inject with no representative in the user's file","r17-h7:113"],["Whether those reasons bite today","Only the third does. The first two describe a link on a user's own domain, which does not exist yet: every shared note is served from ours"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The population the export cut was decided on","items":[["What the cut used","Obsidian plugin-download ratios: a note-taker population","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:60"],["What the refuter counted instead","443,392 root CNAME files; 113,536 astro configs","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:60"],["Gap register's verdict","\"The cut may be right for the wrong reason\"","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:60"],["Export code that already exists","Single-document export, 945 lines, wired, never run","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:133"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No mark anywhere; remove the shipped footer too","gains":["Cleanest against the file-is-truth position"],"costs":["Forfeits the growth loop entirely","The exported-pages row in the channel table should then go"],"system":"Remove the footer from PublicNoteView.","screens":"The public note loses its footer.","money":"None.","what":"No mark anywhere, and the shipped hosted footer comes out too. Cleanest against the file-is-truth position; the growth loop goes entirely, and the exported-pages channel row with it."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the footer, add a linked mark on exported pages, add an opt-out","gains":["Keeps the growth loop, with an opt-out","Both surfaces finally say the same thing"],"costs":["Exports carry the devaluation risk the research warns of","Those links sit on someone else's domain"],"system":"A mark in the export module and a settings toggle.","screens":"A settings row; a footer in exported HTML.","money":"Unmeasured. There is no deploy count.","what":"Keep the footer, add a linked mark on exported pages, with an opt-out. Keeps the growth loop and both surfaces agree; exports carry the devaluation risk, and the links sit on someone else's domain."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the hosted footer; a small unlinked wordmark on exports, off by default","gains":["Leaves the shipped surface alone","No link scheme, because nothing is linked"],"costs":["Weakest growth effect of the three","Still injects markup absent from the file"],"system":"An optional wordmark in the export module.","screens":"A settings row, off by default.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the hosted footer; a small unlinked wordmark on exports, off by default. Leaves the shipped surface alone and forms no link scheme; the weakest growth effect, and markup absent from the file."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep both marks and add a deploy counter so the channel is measurable","gains":["Makes the exported-pages channel row falsifiable"],"costs":["Counts an event on a user's page, which telemetry forbids","A service to run for an unknown channel"],"system":"A counter endpoint we would have to run.","screens":"A dashboard nobody asked for.","money":"Server cost for a channel of unknown size.","what":"Keep both marks and add a deploy counter so the channel can be measured. Makes the channel row falsifiable; counts an event on a user's page, which the telemetry rule forbids."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The research's objection is about somebody else's domain, and only the export half is that.","An unlinked wordmark cannot form the link scheme it warns about."],"flip":"If a custom-domain deploy path ships, an exported mark lands on somebody else's domain and the objections apply.","linked":["L6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:311","src/modules/share/presentation/PublicNoteView.tsx:29","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:111","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:113","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:60","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:133","src/modules/share/presentation/PublicNoteView.tsx:30"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Every free kit carries a mark back to us. That badge is part of the funnel design.","recWhy":"The plan defers marked exports until measured, and a mark already ships on one of the two surfaces: the hosted note carries a Hosted on frontmatter.in footer, while the 945-line export module carries none. The research objection is to links on a user's own domain, and only the export half is that. An unlinked wordmark cannot form the link scheme it warns about, and off by default means nobody gets one they did not choose. The third objection stands and is stated: the mark is markup that is not in the file, which is why it stays small, unlinked and optional. Q11 carries this exactly. The export cut in the plan used Obsidian download ratios, which count note-takers, so the growth estimate behind (b) was never for this buyer. If a custom-domain deploy path ships, an exported mark lands on somebody else's domain and every objection applies, and the mark is revisited then."},{"id":"G22","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Allocation","weight":"critical","q":"Which single channel gets the founder's launch hours in the first quarter?","lede":"Eight channels, two cut and three of them requests to strangers, share a measured one day a week.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Channel rows in the plan's table","n":8,"unit":"rows","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Still carrying a live channel","n":7,"unit":"rows","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Blocked by nobody and nothing unbuilt","n":1,"unit":"row","tone":"accent"}],"note":"The one unblocked row is published research. The plan itself calls every channel borrowed.","caption":"Eight channels, one that nothing blocks."},"stakes":"At 1.21 days a week the plan cannot run seven channels, and none produces the first cohort.","state":["The channel table has eight rows; the plan's summary calls every channel borrowed.","No section allocates founder time to any channel.","Measured capacity is 1.21 days a week; 0.23 over the last thirty days."],"tension":["Hand recruitment is the only unborrowed channel and the only one producing research.","It does not compound, so quarter two starts from nothing.","Two maintainers can end the toolchain channel with one reply each."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The channel table's own status, row by row","cols":["Channel","Status in the current plan","Depends on"],"rows":[["Obsidian plugin","CUT (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304)","none"],["superpowers","Week 2 (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305)","A maintainer's permission and an unbuilt MCP server"],["spec-kit","Second (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306)","A maintainer's permission and a 20-file sample"],["Plan mode","Demoted (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307)","An untested file location: one hour to check"],["Published research","Now (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:308)","Nothing: the only unblocked row"],["Show HN","Pilot exit (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:309)","6 of 10, then 1.4% (all Show HN) to 5.7% (this category) odds of clearing 100 points"],["Chrome extension / Product Hunt","CUT / the build after the pilot (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310)","A ?src= parameter that is unbuilt; an exclusion the record already made"],["Exported pages with a mark","Two builds after the pilot (docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:311)","A deploy count that does not exist"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The arithmetic the allocation has to survive","items":[["Visitors needed for the first 100 paying customers","22,222","r25-w4:90"],["Measured loops that produce them at n=0","None","r25-w4:92"],["Everything in the channel table, summed, optimistic, year one","350k to 500k visitors; the 1.26M requirement is a 24-to-36-month cumulative figure","r17-h7:153"],["Corpus conclusion","\"The first hundred cannot come from a loop; it must be hand-recruited\"","r25-w4:94"],["Measured founder capacity","1.21 engineering days per calendar week; 0.23 in the last 30 days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Hand recruitment: the code-search list, the ten users, the twenty teams","gains":["The only unborrowed channel","The only one answering whether anyone pays"],"costs":["Does not scale and does not compound","Consumes the same hours as the build"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"No spend. Founder hours only.","what":"Hand recruitment: the code-search list, the ten users, the twenty teams. The only unborrowed channel and the only one that answers whether anyone pays; it does not scale or compound."},{"k":"b","label":"Toolchain documentation: superpowers, then spec-kit","gains":["Highest relevance per reader","Reaches 22 to 30k active repos if accepted"],"costs":["Entirely outside our control","Produces no interviews"],"system":"An integration page, eventually an MCP server.","screens":"One integration page.","money":"None.","what":"Toolchain documentation: superpowers first, then spec-kit. The highest relevance per reader and 22,000 to 30,000 active repos if accepted; entirely outside our control and produces no interviews."},{"k":"c","label":"Content and the validator","what":"The only compounding channels, both slow.","gains":["Compounds, and the engine produces the material","Neither needs a stranger's permission"],"costs":["Nothing to show for three quarters","The validator waits on the first round of engine fixes"],"system":"The validator needs a client-side engine build.","screens":"A research index and a validator page.","money":"Seven engineering days before the validator is honest."},{"k":"d","label":"Launch events: Show HN and Product Hunt","gains":["Visible, free, confers credibility on a solo founder","One day of work per quarter"],"costs":["1.4 to 5.7% odds of clearing 100 points","Decays to zero in 96 hours"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A launch page.","money":"None.","what":"Launch events: Show HN and Product Hunt. Visible, free, and credibility for a solo founder at one day a quarter; 1.4 to 5.7 percent odds of clearing 100 points, and it decays in 96 hours."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The first 100 paying customers need 22,222 visitors, and no measured loop delivers that.","Only conversations answer whether anyone pays, which the eleven listed gaps never touched."],"flip":"If the superpowers maintainer says yes in week 2, a channel reaching 22 to 30k repos outranks recruitment.","linked":["FF12","G15","G26","G6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:300","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:558","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:90","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:92","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:94","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:153"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Launch hours come when there is something to launch.","recWhy":"Eight channels, two of them cut and three of them requests to strangers, share a measured 1.21 engineering days a week, 0.23 over the last thirty, and no section allocates founder time to any of them. The first hundred paying customers need about 22,222 visitors and no measured loop delivers that, so the question is not which channel scales but which one answers something. Only conversations answer whether anyone pays, which eleven desk-research gaps never touched, and hand recruitment is the only channel not borrowed from someone else's audience. It does not compound, and quarter two starts from nothing. G6 asks the superpowers maintainer in the same fortnight because one reply decides whether a second channel exists. A yes from the maintainer in week 2 means a channel reaching 22,000 to 30,000 repositories outranks recruitment for the launch hours."},{"id":"G1","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Headline","weight":"high","q":"Which tests judge Phase 0 and Phase 1, and at what n?","lede":"The old headline tests measured claims four free tools now ship. The phases need judges written before they start.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Arm 1 · what the AI wrote","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Arm 2 · what the agent changed","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Arm 3 · what nobody reviewed","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Already shipped by","cells":["iA Writer 7, 2023; nobody at span level","Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex, VS Code","Almanac, 2021 to 2025"]},{"label":"Free where it ships","cells":["No","Yes, in four editors","It was paid, and it closed"]},{"label":"Best pull measured","cells":["208 downloads for the Obsidian port","266 reactions, for a per-session panel","0 likes, 1 post in three years"]},{"label":"Reads per arm at ten people","cells":["3","3","4"]}],"note":"Arm 1 was demoted two rounds ago and is still in the test.","caption":"Two judges, one per phase, written before either starts"},"stakes":"With ten people across three arms, a day-14 kill cannot be traced to one headline.","state":["The go-to-market section names three headlines; the week-by-week schedule says two framings.","The gap register also says three arms.","Arm 1, what the AI wrote, is carried as demoted, not deleted."],"tension":["Arm 2 now ships free in Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex and VS Code.","Arm 3 is Almanac's feature sentence. Almanac raised $45M and shut down 2025-01-31.","Ten people split three ways cannot separate a bad arm from a bad product."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Arm 1 has already been sold, twice, by other people","items":[["iA Writer 7 shipped authorship marking","2023","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175"],["Obsidian port of it, downloads","208","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175"],["Best VS Code extension in that niche, installs","366","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175"],["Reddit demand (the plan says both things)","The capture loop (section 12) says the top twenty \"AI wrote\" posts contain no request for authorship marking; the exhibit on Reddit asks says \"one Reddit ask\". Different samples, same document","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296 and :175"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"What the target buyer complains about: 300 unbiased posts, r/ClaudeAI + r/cursor + r/ChatGPTCoding","note":"Full table at docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w1-reddit-recovered.md:78-87. All seven rows shown; percentage of 300 posts.","unit":"% of 300 posts","data":[["Price",15.7],["Reviewing agent output",14],["Context window / memory",11],["Usage limits / quota",9.7],["Agent broke something",4],["Markdown / docs files",3.3],["Byte-exact editing vocabulary",0.3]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Phase 0: ten of twenty export. Phase 1: three on a team say the view was news","gains":["Each phase has one number agreed before it starts","Both are observable without instrumentation beyond the pilot app"],"costs":["Twenty named people have to exist first","Three on a team needs at least one team in the twenty"],"system":"The pilot app records exports and the day-14 answer.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Phase 0 passes when ten of twenty export a document; Phase 1 when three people on one team say the which-file-wins view was news. One number per phase, agreed first; twenty named people must exist."},{"k":"b","label":"One test for both phases: the highlight test at n=15 with a stated pass line","gains":["Cheap and already designed on D18"],"costs":["Measures noticing, not use or payment"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"One test for both phases: the highlight test at n=15 with a stated pass line. Cheap and already designed on D18; it measures noticing, not use or payment."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the three arms as written","gains":["Nothing to redesign"],"costs":["Arm 2 shipped free in four editors; the test cannot show demand for us"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the three arms as written. Nothing to redesign; arm 2 now ships free in four editors, so the test cannot show demand for us."},{"k":"d","label":"No pre-registered test; judge by what the twenty say","gains":["Nothing to write"],"costs":["A bar set after the result is not a bar"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"No pre-registered test; judge by what the twenty say. Nothing to write, and a bar set after the result is not a bar."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["A judge written before the phase starts is the only kind that can say no, and both numbers are observable with what exists.","The old arms tested a headline four free tools shipped in nine weeks; testing it cannot show demand for us."],"flip":"If the twenty cannot include a single team, Phase 1's judge has to be rewritten before Phase 1 starts.","linked":["P2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:175","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:110","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w1-reddit-recovered.md:79"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The old two week tests are replaced by the twenty hand made kits.","recWhy":"The old headline tests measured claims four free tools now ship. Arm 2, what the agent changed in the working tree, is in Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex and VS Code, and arm 3 is Almanac's feature sentence, which raised $45 million and shut down. Ten people split three ways cannot separate a bad arm from a bad product. Each phase needs one judge written before it starts, because that is the only kind that can say no. Phase 0, the open editor at /, passes when ten of twenty strangers export a document, which the pilot app can observe. Phase 1, the instruction-file view, passes when three people on a team say the view told them something they did not know, the unprompted-demand test Q15 keeps. Both are observable with what exists. The cost is that twenty named people must exist first, which is G15. If the twenty cannot include a team, Phase 1's judge is rewritten before Phase 1 starts."},{"id":"G6","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Toolchains","weight":"high","q":"What do we ship to the superpowers and spec-kit maintainers in week 2, and how do we ask?","lede":"Week 2's channel is a request nobody has made, and its named artefact does not exist in the repository.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Agent writes the spec","note":"architectural path only","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Commits it","note":"the file is already in the repo","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"\"Please review it\"","note":"the terminal prints a path","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Terminal ends","note":"no review surface of its own","tone":"stop"},{"label":"A README line points here","note":"needs a maintainer's yes","tone":"accent","dashed":true},{"label":"An MCP entry","note":"no MCP server exists in src/","tone":"stop","dashed":true}],"note":"Spikes stay in chat and the bounded path writes no plan document.","caption":"Where superpowers stops, and which of our two artefacts exists."},"stakes":"The plan's first channel depends on a stranger's yes and on a server nobody has written.","state":["The plan's week-2 action is a README line, an MCP entry and a pull request.","Checked 2026-09-09: no MCP dependency, no script, zero MCP references in src/.","Nobody has approached either maintainer; still open after research round two."],"tension":["superpowers halts, names a repo path, and asks a human to review a file.","Our pitch to spec-kit criticises spec-kit's own default artefact, which makes first contact awkward.","A rejected pull request is public and permanent; an issue is neither."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two targets, sized 2026-09-06 and 2026-09-08","cols":["Toolchain","Reach","What it writes","Where it stops"],"rows":[["obra/superpowers","75 to 85k public repos; 22 to 30k active in 30 days; marketplace counter 1,009,371 (cached)","Brainstorms, designs, writes the spec, commits it, then asks the human to review it","Terminal. No editor, no review state"],["github/spec-kit","11,072 public repos; about 1,660 active","specify/clarify/plan/tasks/implement; writes markdown then leaves","No review surface; its default checklist is ticked by the agent"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What is still unknown about the channel","items":[["Whether either maintainer would accept a README line","Unmeasured, listed as still open after round 2","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"],["Share of superpowers sessions that take the architectural path (the only path that writes a document)","Unmeasured, listed as still open","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146"],["Outreach made to date","None","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:58"],["A caveat the capture loop section (12) already carries","Only the architectural path writes a document; spikes stay in chat, bounded says \"no plan document\", and the plan document gets no review gate, only the spec does","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What exists today for the week-2 artefact","items":[["MCP server in src/","None: no modelcontextprotocol dependency, no server module, zero MCP references in src/ (checked 2026-09-09)"],["MCP in package.json scripts","Absent from all 26: arch, budget, build, corpus, decide, dev, doc, format, format:check, lint, pdf, record, refs, spec, start, tauri (×6), test, test:watch, tree, typecheck, verify"],["MCP server scheduled","The build after the pilot","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203"],["The MCP server, agents writing through it","Listed in the plan's own Untested row","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188"],["mdmax cert, the other CI-facing artefact","Never wired to a runnable command; exits 1 with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND; cert is not one of the 26 scripts","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224"],["Measured build rate","1.21 engineering days per calendar week; 0.23 in the last 30 days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What option (d) would have to overcome","items":[["src-tauri identity, checked 2026-09-09","productName \"sgnk-md\", identifier \"ai.sgnk.md\": the desktop build still ships as the sibling app","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3, :5"],["Fix-first item 9","\"src-tauri carries the sibling app's identity\", 1 day, not yet done","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:222"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Documentation PR to both, with the MCP server built first","gains":["Reaches both populations at once"],"costs":["Adds build days to a build-nothing fortnight","A rejection is public in two repos"],"system":"A local MCP server, due in the build after the pilot.","screens":"A config entry in the user's agent.","money":"Six engineering days at 1.21 days a week.","what":"A documentation pull request to superpowers and spec-kit, with the MCP server built first. Reaches both populations; build days in a build-nothing fortnight, and a rejection is public in two repos."},{"k":"b","label":"superpowers only: ask in an issue, then a README line and one integration page","gains":["Highest relevance, larger population, reversible","Ships in hours, not days"],"costs":["Forgoes spec-kit's 11,072 repos for now"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One integration page on our own site.","money":"None.","what":"superpowers only: ask in an issue first, then a README line and one integration page. Highest relevance and reversible, ships in hours; spec-kit's 11,072 repos wait."},{"k":"c","label":"No outbound; publish our own integration page and wait to be found","gains":["No permission needed and no rejection risk"],"costs":["Depends on search, which contributes nothing for three quarters","Neither community learns it exists"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One page.","money":"None.","what":"No outbound; publish our own integration page and wait to be found. No permission and no rejection risk; search contributes nothing for three quarters and neither community learns it exists."},{"k":"d","label":"Move the toolchain channel to the build after the pilot, where the MCP server lives","gains":["Consistent with what is actually built"],"costs":["Week 2 has no channel once Obsidian is cut","Maintainers no closer to a yes"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Move the toolchain channel to the build after the pilot, where the MCP server lives. Consistent with what is built; week 2 has no channel once Obsidian is cut."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The MCP entry cannot ship in week 2, because no MCP server exists in src/.","An issue costs nothing and gets a yes or a no before a public PR exists."],"flip":"If the superpowers maintainer says no, the channel is our own page and week 2 has nothing outbound.","linked":["G22","G24","G8"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:148","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:58","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:188","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:222","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","merged: G7"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Approaching maintainers comes with a product to show.","recWhy":"Week 2's channel is a request nobody has made, and its named artefact does not exist. Checked on 9 September, there is no MCP dependency, no script and zero MCP references in src, so the MCP entry cannot ship in week 2 whatever else is decided. superpowers halts, names a repository path and asks a human to review a file, the exact moment our product exists for, so it is the higher-relevance population and the larger one. Our pitch to spec-kit criticises spec-kit's own default artefact, which makes first contact awkward, and G8 says the sentence behind it is not yet publishable. An issue costs nothing and returns a yes or a no before a public pull request exists; a rejected PR is public and permanent, an issue is neither. A no from the maintainer leaves our own page as the channel, which G22 absorbs.","dupes":{"dropped":["B17","MK5"],"areas":["Business & operations","Market & competition"],"why":"Business & operations and Market & competition both recommended asking both maintainers in week 0, in writing, before the channel depends on them; Market & competition would publish whatever they answer. No option here asks both first: option a opens a PR to both after the MCP server is built, option b asks superpowers only.","agree":false}},{"id":"G8","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Toolchains","weight":"high","q":"Do we publish, by name, the finding that spec-kit's agent ticks its own checklist, and at what sample size?","lede":"The sharpest sentence the research produced is a public claim about somebody else's artefact, from twenty files.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"As measured, 2026-09-08","lines":[{"t":"Population 49,664 checklist files"},{"t":"20 sampled, 19 fully checked","mark":"warn"},{"t":"354 of 355 boxes ticked, 99.7%","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Anyone can run 200 and disagree in public","mark":"stop"}]},"after":{"title":"Re-measured at n of 200 or more","lines":[{"t":"Same population, defensible sample","mark":"good"},{"t":"Re-derivable by any reader","mark":"good"},{"t":"Meets our own rule for public claims","mark":"good"},{"t":"Costs one day, and delays the channel by it","mark":"warn"}]},"via":"one day","caption":"The claim is strong. The sample is what a critic attacks first."},"stakes":"A refuted marketing number refutes a product whose one differentiator is refusing rather than guessing.","state":["The plan pitches spec-kit as \"your checklist is green because the agent ticked it.\"","Population 49,664 files; 20 sampled; 19 fully ticked; 354 of 355 boxes ticked.","Our public-claims rule permits numbers anyone can measure and re-derive."],"tension":["99.7% from twenty files publishes only with its sample size in the sentence.","The target maintainer is the one we would also ask for a README line.","Three of our own numbers were withdrawn after failing a control."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The measurement as it stands","items":[["Population of checklists/requirements.md","49,664","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306"],["Files sampled","20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306"],["Fully checked files in the sample","19 of 20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306"],["Boxes ticked","354 of 355 = 99.7%","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306"],["Reviewer-owned checklist population, for contrast","4,536, 11 times rarer","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306"],["spec-kit's size in the plan's own preferred unit","11,072 public repos, about 1,660 active. The plan struck star counts as a channel-size measure","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:148 and :176"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The publication rules this would sit under: what the \"what we may say in public\" section (58) actually says","items":[["Rule that bites here","Any [SS]-tagged number, in any public asset ([SS] means \"search summary, nobody opened the page\"): verdict \"No. Verify or omit\"","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022 and :100"],["Rule on fidelity percentages","Bans our own fidelity or coverage percentages until the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug land; it does not cover third-party measurements, so it is not the rule that governs this one","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022"],["What the \"what we may say in public\" section (58) explicitly permits","Numbers that are [measured] or [fetched] and re-derivable by anyone, which this becomes at a defensible n","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022"],["Precedent in this corpus","Three published numbers were withdrawn when they failed a control","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Publish it now, with the sample size and the method in the same sentence","gains":["The strongest sentence the research produced"],"costs":["A twenty-file sample invites a 200-file rebuttal"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A post and a spec-kit integration page.","money":"None.","what":"Publish now, with the sample size and method in the sentence: 19 of 20 files fully ticked, 354 of 355 boxes. The strongest sentence the research produced; twenty files invite a 200-file rebuttal."},{"k":"b","label":"Re-measure at n of 200 or more before publishing anything","gains":["Meets our public-claims standard"],"costs":["Delays the spec-kit channel by a day","The larger sample may return a weaker number"],"system":"A counting script; nothing in the product.","screens":"Same as (a), later.","money":"One engineering day.","what":"Re-measure at 200 files or more before publishing anything. Meets our own public-claims standard; a day's delay on the spec-kit channel, and the larger sample may return a weaker number."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish the finding without naming spec-kit","gains":["Removes confrontation with a maintainer we want a link from"],"costs":["Removes the specificity that makes it land","Readers identify the target regardless"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A published post.","money":"None.","what":"Publish the finding without naming spec-kit. Removes the confrontation with a maintainer we want a link from; readers identify the target anyway and the specificity goes."},{"k":"d","label":"Do not publish; use it only with the ten and the twenty","gains":["Still works where the decision is made"],"costs":["Forgoes the one sentence likely to travel","Nothing accrues to the owned channel"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do not publish; use it only with the ten and the twenty. Still works where the decision is made; the one sentence likely to travel stays private."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["One more day turns a claim into a measurement, on the number most likely re-run.","Our pitch is refusing rather than guessing, so we cannot publish a guessed denominator."],"flip":"If a larger sample returns under 90%, the sentence goes and spec-kit needs a different pitch.","linked":["G6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:306","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:148","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:140","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:100"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Publishing a finding is a launch move.","recWhy":"The sharpest sentence the research produced, your checklist is green because the agent ticked it, is a public claim about somebody else's artefact from twenty files out of 49,664. Nineteen fully ticked and 354 of 355 boxes is 99.7 percent, and 99.7 percent from twenty files publishes only with its sample size in the sentence, the same rule that has already withdrawn three of our own numbers after they failed a control. One more day at 200 files turns a claim into a measurement on exactly the number most likely to be re-run by the maintainer we also want a README line from. Our pitch is refusing rather than guessing, so we cannot publish a guessed denominator about someone else. It sits after the pilot because the spec-kit channel waits on G6. Under 90 percent at the larger sample, the sentence goes and spec-kit needs a different pitch; if it holds, it is the best line the launch has."},{"id":"G18","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Services funnel","weight":"high","q":"Do we sell the ₹3,00,000 engagement off the docs scan before the scan has run for anybody?","lede":"Services fund the company, and this funnel is the least evidenced thing in the plan.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"Team runs the free docs scan","note":"ten engineering days, unbuilt","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Scan returns stale docs, broken links","note":"more than one person will work through"},{"label":"That list is the brief","note":"an upsell moment in the plan","tone":"accent"},{"label":"₹3,00,000 fixed scope","note":"no team has ever done this","tone":"stop"}],"note":"The repo docs scan sits behind 24 days of fix-first, at 1.21 engineering days a calendar week.","caption":"The one bridge between studio and product, both ends unbuilt."},"stakes":"The lead-generating scan is months away, and the ₹1.09L running cost is monthly.","state":["The product-family section makes the docs scan the diagnostic for a ₹3,00,000 engagement.","It calls this the one place consultancy and product are the same funnel.","The gap register calls it a design presented as a finding."],"tension":["The docs scan sits ten engineering days behind the 24-day first round of engine fixes.","At 1.21 days a week the scan is months off, against ₹1.09L monthly costs.","A hand-run scan consumes the same hours the build needs."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The funnel, and what stands behind it","items":[["Engagement price named in the plan","₹3,00,000 fixed scope","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:465"],["Monthly nut","about ₹1.09L, covered by 54 to 78 consulting hours","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"],["Evidence that a scan produces an engagement","None: untested","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:56"],["Studio's measured conversion from any productised diagnostic","No data in the record","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:56"],["When the scan ships","The repo docs scan feature, 10 engineering days, after 24 days of fix-first work","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Measured build rate","1.21 engineering days per calendar week; 0.23 in the last 30 days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Sell the engagement now, with the scan run by hand","gains":["Revenue and conversion data months before the feature","Tests the funnel where being wrong is cheapest"],"costs":["Consumes the hours the build also needs","Two or three data points a quarter"],"system":"No change. The scan is done by hand.","screens":"None. A written report.","money":"₹3,00,000 an engagement, against ₹1.09L monthly costs.","what":"Sell the ₹3,00,000 engagement now, with the docs scan run by hand. Revenue and conversion data months before the feature; it consumes the hours the build also needs."},{"k":"b","label":"Wait for the scan to ship before selling the engagement","gains":["The client sees the product's own scan"],"costs":["Postpones the only revenue instrument in the plan","Monthly running costs are met from ordinary client work"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"No product-attributable services revenue this year.","what":"Wait for the scan to ship before selling the engagement. The client sees the product's own scan; the only revenue instrument in the plan waits months."},{"k":"c","label":"Sell a smaller paid diagnostic first, then price the larger engagement off it","gains":["Lowest-friction test of whether anyone buys the diagnosis","Produces cash inside the quarter"],"costs":["Anchors the studio below the ₹3,00,000 figure","A second offer to scope and deliver"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"A smaller fee sooner, with anchoring risk.","what":"Sell a smaller paid diagnostic first and price the larger engagement off it. The lowest-friction test of whether anyone buys a diagnosis; it anchors the studio below ₹3,00,000."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop the productised funnel; keep services as ordinary client work","gains":["Removes an unevidenced claim from the plan"],"costs":["Gives up the one place product and studio connect","The product-family diagram loses its bottom half"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Services continue unchanged.","what":"Drop the productised funnel and keep services as ordinary client work. Removes an unevidenced claim from the plan; the one place product and studio connect goes."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The conversion claim is testable by hand today and untestable until the scan ships.","It is the only item in this area that produces cash rather than spending it."],"flip":"If two hand-run scans in a month produce no engagement conversation, the funnel is a design.","linked":["F7","G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:445","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:465","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:482","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:56"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Selling off a scan that has not run.","recWhy":"Services fund the company, and the product-family section makes the docs scan the diagnostic for a ₹3,00,000 engagement, calling it the one place consultancy and product are the same funnel. The gap register calls that a design presented as a finding. The scan sits ten engineering days behind a 24-day first round of engine fixes, so at 1.21 days a week it is months off against ₹1.09 lakh of monthly costs. The conversion claim is testable by hand today and untestable until the scan ships, and it is the only item in this area that produces cash rather than spending it. PR20 settles the engagement's shape, eight days at ₹2,40,000 with the eval days held back; this card says sell it now with the scan done by hand. Two hand-run scans in a month with no engagement conversation makes the funnel a design."},{"id":"G20","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Owned surfaces","weight":"high","q":"Do we build the public frontmatter validator?","lede":"Its ceiling matches how widely YAML frontmatter is parsed, and no plan includes it.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Built today","lines":[{"t":"Refuses 83% of the seven pinned vaults","mark":"stop"},{"t":"The left-edge list bug in frontmatter","mark":"warn"},{"t":"The first thing a visitor sees is our defect rate","mark":"stop"},{"t":"We may not publish that as coverage anyway","mark":"warn"}]},"after":{"title":"Built after the list and line-ending fixes","lines":[{"t":"Seven of the twenty-four fix-first days","mark":"warn"},{"t":"The page shows the engine at its best","mark":"good"},{"t":"Client-side only, verifiable in the network tab","mark":"good"},{"t":"The search intent is unchanged and waiting","mark":"good"}]},"via":"7 days","caption":"The build order matters more here than the build."},"stakes":"Skipping it loses the only lever sized to gray-matter's 35M monthly downloads.","state":["The channel table does not mention it.","The research calls it the one asymmetric move: 30k to 150k year-one visitors.","The bug where a list starting at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file hits 83% of real vaults."],"tension":["It must never send document content to a server, visibly, in the network tab.","Built today, it shows a refusal rate our public-claims rule forbids calling coverage.","The first round of engine fixes is 24 engineering days, at 1.21 days a week."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Year-one visitor contribution by channel, optimistic end of each research estimate","note":"From the channel table at docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:144-151. Everything on that table sums to perhaps 350k-500k in year one (r17-h7:153). The approximately 1,260,000 visitor figure for ₹20L/month (r17-h7:8) is a 24-to-36-month cumulative number, not a year-one target.","unit":"visitors, optimistic end","data":[["Organic, marketing site",200000],["The free validator",150000],["Launch platforms (HN, Reddit)",100000],["Content, 24 pieces",60000],["Published-page organic",0]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why the ceiling is the right order of magnitude, and the risk","items":[["gray-matter npm downloads per month","35,782,000 per r17-h7:9, and independently recorded at 35,124,859 in docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728. Same order, two counts"],["front-matter npm downloads per month","3,041,000","r17-h7:10"],["Query the validator targets","\"yaml frontmatter invalid / parse error\": problem-aware, huge latent volume, our strongest asset","r17-h7:69"],["Hard constraint","Client-side only, stated on the page, verifiable in the network tab","r17-h7:72"],["What it would currently reveal","The engine refuses 83% of real vaults on the left-edge list bug","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214"],["A second caveat on that number","It is measured on seven Obsidian vaults, one wiki being 77% of the files: a validator taking arbitrary pasted documents samples a different population","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build it after the left-edge list bug and the old line-ending bug are fixed","what":"Also waits on the bug where an old-style line ending makes the editor add a second frontmatter block instead of editing the first.","gains":["Shows the engine at its best","The search intent does not expire"],"costs":["The channel starts three to six months later"],"system":"The two fixes, then a client-side engine build.","screens":"One page: paste, verdict, the byte that broke it.","money":"Seven engineering days, already planned."},{"k":"b","label":"Build it now; the refusal is the point","gains":["Fastest route to a compounding channel","Refusing honestly is the product's own claim"],"costs":["A visitor's first sight is our defect rate","Competes with the engine fixes for days"],"system":"A client-side engine build, as it stands.","screens":"Same page, worse verdicts.","money":"The page, ahead of the fixes.","what":"Build it now; the refusal is the point. The fastest route to a compounding channel and refusing honestly is our own claim; a visitor's first sight is our defect rate."},{"k":"c","label":"Do not build it; the plan is already over capacity","gains":["No page leading with our own defect"],"costs":["Removes the only lever of the right magnitude","Nothing else in the table compounds"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do not build it; the plan is already over capacity. No page leading with our own defect; the only lever of the right magnitude goes, and nothing else in the table compounds."},{"k":"d","label":"Ship it as a CLI or npm package instead of a page","gains":["Reaches developers where they already are","No hosting, no page to maintain"],"costs":["Forfeits the search intent, the page's whole reason","A public package means unbudgeted support"],"system":"A published package with its own releases.","screens":"None.","money":"Public package upkeep.","what":"Ship it as a CLI or npm package instead of a page. Reaches developers where they are with no hosting; it forfeits the search intent, which is the page's whole reason."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Both bugs (a) waits on are the first two engine fixes, so sequencing costs nothing.","A page that opens on our own defect rate is worse than no page."],"flip":"If the fixes slip a quarter, a validator naming the refused byte still beats no page.","linked":["E9","G22","G9"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:8","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:9","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:69","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:72","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:145","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:300"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A public checker is a later surface.","recWhy":"The public validator is in no plan, and the research calls it the one asymmetric move, 30,000 to 150,000 year-one visitors, because its ceiling is how widely YAML frontmatter is parsed and that search intent does not expire. Built today, it would show a refusal rate our public-claims rule forbids calling coverage. The flush-left list bug hits 83 percent of real vaults and the old line-ending bug sits beside it, and a page that opens on our own defect rate is worse than no page. Both bugs are the first two engine fixes on Q14's list, so sequencing the validator after them costs nothing extra. It must also never send document content to a server, visibly, in the network tab, which Q9 requires of everything. It sits after the pilot because the fixes and the pilot come first. If the fixes slip a quarter, a validator that names the refused byte still beats no page."},{"id":"G26","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Allocation","weight":"high","q":"Do we write down that organic discovery contributes nothing in year one?","lede":"Two of the eight dead products we studied died with nobody noticing, and one had raised $45M.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"May 2020","label":"Almanac's \"GitHub for Docs\" Show HN","note":"6 points, no comments","tone":"warn"},{"when":"Sep 2021","label":"$34M Series A, Tiger Global","note":"$45M raised in all","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"Jan 2025","label":"Almanac switched off","note":"cause stated as capacity, not demand","tone":"stop"},{"when":"Jul 2026","label":"petergyang/human-review published","note":"a free markdown review skill","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"Sep 2026","label":"1,241 stars in six weeks","note":"attention, with payment unmeasured","tone":"accent"}],"note":"Editorially closed in 2014: \"Even if all of our users paid up, it wouldn't be enough.\"","caption":"Money did not buy attention here. Free did. Neither is a plan."},"stakes":"If the plan assumes any organic discovery, every channel number is optimistic by that amount.","state":["No section states what organic discovery contributes; published-page organic is about zero.","Organic search is back-loaded to months 9 to 18 and cannot carry a launch.","The whole table, summed optimistically, reaches 350k to 500k year-one visitors."],"tension":["Free artefacts do get attention: human-review went to 1,241 stars in six weeks.","Stars measure attention, and that repository says nothing about paying.","1.4% of recent Show HN posts reach 100 points, and decay in 96 hours."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"State that organic discovery contributes zero in year one, and budget nothing","gains":["Makes the rest of the table falsifiable","Costs a paragraph"],"costs":["Reads as pessimism to anyone who has not seen the dead products","Nothing compounding is being built"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None. Removes an implicit revenue assumption.","what":"State that organic discovery contributes zero in year one, and budget nothing. Makes every other number in the table falsifiable for a paragraph; it reads as pessimism to newcomers."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the channel table as a ranking and add no such statement","gains":["The table reads as a plan, not a warning"],"costs":["The optimism stays hidden inside eight rows","Nobody can tell which number assumes discovery"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the channel table as a ranking and add no such statement. The table reads as a plan rather than a warning; the optimism stays hidden inside eight rows."},{"k":"c","label":"Budget for organic on the research's own numbers, back-loaded to months 9 to 18","gains":["Uses the research rather than an anecdote about dead products","Gives content hours a stated return"],"costs":["Nothing arrives inside this plan's decision window","It still assumes we are found"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Commits content hours against a year-two return.","what":"Budget for organic on the research's own numbers, back-loaded to months 9 to 18. Uses the research rather than an anecdote; nothing arrives inside this plan's decision window."},{"k":"d","label":"Make a free artefact the discovery plan: publish a skill and see if it travels","gains":["The only observed mechanism producing attention from nothing","Reuses the engine we already have"],"costs":["Trains the audience that this is free","Says nothing about willingness to pay"],"system":"A published package or skill with a release process.","screens":"None.","money":"Engineering days against unmeasured payment.","what":"Make a free artefact the discovery plan: publish a skill and see if it travels. The only observed mechanism producing attention from nothing; it trains the audience that this is free."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The two dead products nobody noticed had no channel written down either.","Writing the zero down is what makes every other number in the table checkable."],"flip":"If a free artefact of ours draws a thousand stars in six weeks, the statement needs a carve-out.","linked":["G22","G4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:558","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:144","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:147","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:153","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:82","https://get.almanac.io/go-forward","https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/23339147","https://api.github.com/repos/petergyang/human-review","http://stet.editorially.com/articles/goodbye/","research briefing 2026-09-09, graveyard lens: Draft, 503 served since April 2023"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A statement about discovery belongs with the launch plan.","recWhy":"Two of the eight dead products studied died with nobody noticing, and neither had a channel written down. No section of the plan states what organic discovery contributes. Published pages sit under noindex, organic search is back-loaded to months 9 to 18, and the whole channel table summed optimistically reaches 350,000 to 500,000 year-one visitors against a milestone needing 1.26 million, which B12 deletes for the same reason. Writing the zero down is what makes every other row checkable, because a reader can see which numbers assume being found. Free artefacts do get attention, human-review went to 1,241 stars in six weeks, and stars measure attention, not paying, so (d) is a real mechanism and a poor plan. A free artefact of ours drawing a thousand stars in six weeks earns a carve-out, with the replacement number measured, not assumed."},{"id":"G4","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Launch events","weight":"medium","q":"Do we spend our one Show HN shot when the pilot ends, and does Product Hunt stay?","lede":"Show HN is the only launch event left, sized with two figures found nowhere else.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Show HN posts, \"markdown editor\", 12 months","n":187,"unit":"posts","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Cleared 50 points","n":12,"unit":"posts","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Cleared 100 points","n":7,"unit":"posts","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Every spike decays to zero in 96 hours.","caption":"What one Show HN in this category is worth, over twelve months."},"stakes":"One shot on the wrong title buys a 96-hour spike and nothing that lasts.","state":["The plan puts Show HN at the pilot's end, sized by Zed's score of 43 and Cursor's 9.","Those two figures appear nowhere else in the docs tree.","Product Hunt stays for the build after the pilot; the thesis document excludes it."],"tension":["31% of posts clearing 50 points carry free, open source or self-host.","Our individual tier is free forever with no signup, so that title is true.","A free title invites the free-alternatives thread, against seven maintained $0 editors."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Every Show HN measurement in the corpus, against the plan's sizing","cols":["Sample","n","Median points","Hit rate","Source"],"rows":[["\"markdown editor\", last 12 months","187","2","7 clear 100 = 3.7%; 12 clear 50 = 6.4%","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1195"],["\"markdown editor\", re-counted all-time","386","3","5.7% reach 100; 44% score ≤2","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728"],["\"markdown editor\", the count from that research report","609","3","none","docs/CRITIQUE.md:52"],["\"markdown editor\", 2024-08-18 → 2026-08-30","235","none","10 clear 100 = 4.3%; ~9.8 launches/month","docs/BUSINESS.md:1264"],["1,000 most recent Show HN, all topics","1,000","2","1.4% reach 100; 0.1% reach 500","r25-w4:82"],["The plan's own sizing","2","none","Zed 43, Cursor 9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:309, the only occurrence in the docs tree"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What correlates with winning in this category","items":[["Of the 42 posts that cleared 50 points in 12 months, share with \"open source\"/\"self-host\"/\"free\" in the title","31%, and the top four all do","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728"],["Of the 10 that cleared 100 in two years, how many announce free or open source in the title","Five products across six posts: OverType 471, OpenKnowledge 381, Rowboat twice at 219 and 205, Write.md 107, Ephe 143","docs/BUSINESS.md:1264"],["Spike decay","to zero in 96 hours","docs/BUSINESS.md:1082"],["Corpus rule on the category noun","\"Never say markdown editor\": every Show HN with that name becomes a thread of free alternatives","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:2533"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the corpus actually holds on Product Hunt","items":[["Record's channel list verdict","Explicitly excluded: \"Not on the list: Product Hunt, LinkedIn, paid anything\"","docs/THESIS.md:752"],["Plan's verdict","\"Product Hunt unchanged\" at the build after the pilot","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310"],["Research coverage of Product Hunt","\"NOT ATTEMPTED\"","docs/mdmax/tree/13-appendix.md:747"],["Launch-platform contribution, all platforms combined, year 1","30k to 100k visitors in spikes, decaying to zero in 96 hours","r17-h7:149"],["What the exclusion was reasoned from","A ₹599 product cannot fund paid acquisition at any CAC a two-founder Indian company can absorb (an argument about paid, not about a free launch day)","docs/THESIS.md:752"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"One shot at the pilot exit, titled on the refusals","gains":["Differentiated against everything else in the list"],"costs":["Refusals are abstract to a stranger scrolling","Two of the five refusals are disprovable today"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A launch page built on the refusal list.","money":"None.","what":"One Show HN at the pilot exit, titled on the refusals. Differentiated against everything else on the list; refusals are abstract to a stranger scrolling, and two of the five are disprovable today."},{"k":"b","label":"One shot at the pilot exit, titled on free; cut Product Hunt","gains":["Matches the only measured predictor of 50 points","Honest: the tier is free, no signup"],"costs":["Invites the free-alternatives thread","Forgoes a launch day reaching non-developers"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A launch page leading with the free tier.","money":"None. Advertises the tier, not a price.","what":"One Show HN at the pilot exit, titled on free, and Product Hunt cut. Matches the only measured predictor of 50 points and the tier is free with no sign-up; it invites the free-alternatives thread."},{"k":"c","label":"Post at the end of the two-week test instead","gains":["A signal months earlier"],"costs":["Burns the one shot on a mock","Cannot be repeated on the real product"],"system":"No change.","screens":"The mock becomes the landing page.","money":"None.","what":"Post at the end of the two-week test instead. A signal months earlier; it burns the one shot on a mock and cannot be repeated on the real product."},{"k":"d","label":"Do not post at all; put the hours into hand recruitment","gains":["Every hour goes to named humans"],"costs":["Loses a free 30k to 100k spike at 1.4 to 5.7% odds","Loses the credibility a good thread confers"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do not post; put the hours into hand recruitment. Every hour goes to named humans; it forgoes a free 30,000 to 100,000 visitor spike at 1.4 to 5.7 percent odds."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Free is the one title feature measurably correlated with clearing 50 points in this category.","Product Hunt has no evidence either way, and the one document treating it as a decision excluded it."],"flip":"If the twenty team conversations name a paying buyer who avoids Hacker News, spend the shot on a case study.","linked":["G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:309","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1195","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1728","docs/CRITIQUE.md:52","docs/BUSINESS.md:1264","docs/BUSINESS.md:1082","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:2533","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:82","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/THESIS.md:752","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:11918","merged: G5","https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/23339147"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"One launch shot, spent when the pilot ends.","recWhy":"Show HN is the only launch event left in the plan, and it was sized with two figures, Zed's 43 points and Cursor's 9, that appear nowhere else in the docs tree. The one measured predictor in this category is the title. 31 percent of posts clearing 50 points carry free, open source or self-host, and our individual tier is free forever with no sign-up, so that title is true, which matters because a title that is true survives the thread. Titling on the refusals reads as abstract to a stranger scrolling, and two of the five refusals are disprovable today. Product Hunt has no evidence either way and the one document that treated it as a decision excluded it, so it goes. One shot, at the pilot exit, on the real product, with PL17's floor written before the post goes up. If the twenty team conversations name a paying buyer who avoids Hacker News, the shot is spent on a case study instead."},{"id":"G9","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Owned surfaces","weight":"medium","q":"Do we publish the research, which slice, and at what rate?","lede":"The only channel needing nobody's permission has shipped two posts ever.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Engine measurements","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Market research","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Nothing yet","tone":"sunk"}],"rows":[{"label":"What one post costs","cells":["About 90 minutes; the build produces it","Re-derive every third-party number first","Nothing"]},{"label":"Who reads it","cells":["Engineers","Anyone tracking the category","Nobody"]},{"label":"What we may publish","cells":["Yes, as a defect report, never as coverage","Only re-derived figures","Not applicable"]},{"label":"Cadence it can hold","cells":["A by-product of the build","Six to eight hours a fortnight","None"]}],"note":"Measured density is 1.21 engineering days a calendar week, 0.23 over the last thirty.","caption":"Which slice, and whether any cadence survives the measured build rate."},"stakes":"A cadence a solo founder cannot hold fails publicly: a burst, then months of silence.","state":["The plan lists published research as available now; none is published yet.","Counted 2026-09-09: 235 report files, about 823,000 words under docs/research.","Two marketing posts have shipped, and one has carried a defect since 2026-08-10."],"tension":["Engine findings cost about 90 minutes; the build already produces them.","Content's year-one contribution is 20k to 60k visitors, back-loaded to months 9 to 18.","A post every fortnight takes six to eight hours the first round of engine fixes also needs."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What there is to publish, and what has shipped","items":[["Report files under docs/research","235 (counted 2026-09-09)"],["Words across those files","about 823,000 (counted 2026-09-09)"],["What \"157 sources\" actually refers to","The previous plan's own line, \"I checked it against 157 sources this week\": a verification count, not a dataset","docs/frontmatter-PILOT-PLAN-v12-2026-09-07-0756.print.html:86"],["Marketing posts ever shipped","2, one defective since 2026-08-10","r17-h7:7"],["Cost of a corpus post, from data the engine already produces","about 90 minutes","r17-h7:28"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Engine findings that are publishable today, and the caveat each must carry","items":[["Zero-indent-sequence refusals across foreign vaults","83% of real vaults refused","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214"],["Corpus the engine is verified on","8,513 third-party files, 7 vaults, 0 corruption, 0 throws. The \"what we may say in public\" section (58) names this as a may-say","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022"],["The caveat the plan itself attaches","Those 7 vaults are all Obsidian and one wiki is 77% of the 8,513 files: the refusal rate is a property of those vaults, not of markdown in the wild","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"],["Non-locality: same ten bytes, two meanings","Measured across four CommonMark engines, unanimous in both directions","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510"],["Carrier finding","An unclosed fence swallows the document; a callout has no closer to lose. Tested on four engines and GitHub","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:500"],["What the \"what we may say in public\" section (58) forbids here","Publishing our own fidelity or coverage percentage while the 83% refusal stands, so the refusal rate may be published as a defect report, never as a coverage claim","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Supply, shipping and capacity","items":[["Marketing posts ever shipped","2, one with a visible defect since 2026-08-10","r17-h7:7"],["Cost of a corpus post from data the engine already produces","about 90 minutes","r17-h7:28"],["Proposed cadence","1 substantial piece per 2 weeks + 1 shallow in the off-week, bank of 2","r17-h7:19"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week","1.21; last 30 days 0.23; sibling repo's best-ever regime 1.91","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227"],["Content's year-1 direct contribution at 24 pieces","20k to 60k visitors; \"its real value is link acquisition and sales-page support, not raw traffic\"","r17-h7:146"],["When organic starts to matter at all","Back-loaded to months 9 to 18","r17-h7:144"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Engine measurements only, published when the build produces one","gains":["Everything is ours and re-derivable","Supply is genuinely a build by-product"],"costs":["Reaches engineers and nobody else","Gives search nothing predictable to crawl"],"system":"No change. The engine already emits the numbers.","screens":"A research index on the site.","money":"About 90 minutes a post.","what":"Engine measurements only, published when the build produces one. Everything is ours and re-derivable, and supply is a by-product; it reaches engineers only, and gives search nothing predictable."},{"k":"b","label":"Engine measurements on the biweekly-plus-bank cadence as written","gains":["Builds an internal link graph"],"costs":["At 0.23 days a week it breaks in month one"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Same index, with dates.","money":"Six to eight founder-hours a fortnight.","what":"Engine measurements on the fortnightly cadence with a bank, as written. Builds an internal link graph; at 0.23 engineering days a week it breaks publicly in month one."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish the market research too, after re-deriving every third-party number","gains":["Draws links from category writers"],"costs":["Several figures have a single source","Publishes numbers about other people's products"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Same index.","money":"A day of re-derivation first.","what":"Publish the market research too, after re-deriving every third-party number. Draws links from category writers; several figures have a single source and they are numbers about other people's products."},{"k":"d","label":"Publish nothing until the two-week test returns","gains":["Every hour goes to the test and the build"],"costs":["Loses three months on a channel needing nine to eighteen","Nobody outside sees the 235 research reports"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish nothing until the two-week test returns. Every hour goes to the test and the build; three months lost on a channel that needs nine to eighteen, and nobody sees the 235 reports."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The engine emits these numbers whether or not anybody writes them up, so supply is free.","At 0.23 engineering days a week, a fortnightly commitment fails publicly in month one."],"flip":"If two engine posts in a quarter draw no links at all, the channel closes.","linked":["G20","G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:308","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:500","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:510","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:5022","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:7","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:28","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:19","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:144","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:146","merged: G19"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Publishing research is a launch move.","recWhy":"Published research is the only channel that needs nobody's permission, and it has shipped two posts ever, one carrying a defect since 10 August, while 235 report files and about 823,000 words sit under docs/research. Engine findings cost about 90 minutes each because the build produces them anyway, so supply is free. The market research is not, because several of its figures have a single source and all are numbers about other people's products, and three of our own have been withdrawn. A fortnightly commitment at a measured 0.23 engineering days a week fails publicly in month one, and a broken cadence reads worse than none. So engine measurements only, when the build produces one, no promised rhythm, and Q7's writing gate on every post. Two engine posts in a quarter drawing no links closes the channel."},{"id":"G23","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Loops","weight":"medium","q":"Do we ship the invite-only referral, or keep referral banned?","lede":"Referral sits on a banned list beside streaks and XP, with no written argument.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":4,"nodes":[{"label":"A second person opens the repo","note":"the moment the product can see","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Invite link to a document","note":"the inviter gets nothing"},{"label":"Invitee lands on the document","note":"no signup wall","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Shared review state","note":"in the build after the pilot","tone":"warn"}],"note":"No credits, free months, leaderboards, counts or notifications.","caption":"The only referral shape consistent with the stated positions."},"stakes":"Shipping it early points the invitee at the public route this plan may delete.","state":["Referral appears nowhere in the channel table or the upsell section.","The research allows one form: a workspace invite giving the inviter nothing.","The team surface to invite people into arrives in the build after the pilot."],"tension":["The research expects our version to underperform two-sided rewards substantially.","The invitee would land on the public route, whose fate is undecided."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What referral is worth against the visitor requirement","cols":["Scenario","k-factor","Visitors still required for ₹20L/mo"],"rows":[["No referral","0","1,260,000"],["5% of free signups refer 1 who signs up","0.05","~1,200,000"],["10% refer 1","0.10","The source gives both approximately 1,150,000 in its table and 1,134,000 in its derivation: the stated saving of approximately 126,000 belongs to the second"],["20% refer 1 (optimistic ceiling, no cash incentive)","0.20","~1,050,000"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The design and its limits","items":[["Proposed mechanic","An invite link to a document or workspace; the recipient lands on the readable document with no signup wall","r17-h7:134"],["The reward","The invitee's first five minutes are better; the inviter gets nothing","r17-h7:132"],["Explicitly excluded","Credits, free months, leaderboards, referral counts, notifications","r17-h7:132"],["Honest limitation","\"Ours will underperform two-sided rewards, probably substantially\"","r17-h7:138"],["Abandon threshold named by the research","Measured k below 0.03 after six months","r17-h7:138"],["When a team surface exists to invite into","The build after the pilot: shared review state and comments","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:200"],["Where the invitee would currently land","The public-note route, which is exactly what this decision and its companion on crawlability are deciding"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ship the invite form in the build after the pilot, when shared review state exists","gains":["Consistent with the product's stated positions","Lands on a surface that exists by then"],"costs":["Nothing happens for months","Underperforms two-sided rewards by design"],"system":"An invite token against shared review state.","screens":"An invite dialogue inside the review panel.","money":"None. It creates seats, which is the paid tier.","what":"Ship the invite form in the build after the pilot, once shared review state exists to invite someone into. Consistent with the stated positions; nothing for months, and weaker than two-sided rewards."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep referral banned","gains":["No invite surface to design or support"],"costs":["Forgoes the one loop the research endorses","The ban was inherited, not reasoned"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep referral banned. No invite surface to design or support; the ban was inherited beside streaks and XP, never reasoned, and it forgoes the one loop the research endorses."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship it in the pilot as a document share link","gains":["Something to measure this quarter","The share link already exists"],"costs":["Collides with the open public-page hosting decision","The landing surface may not survive it"],"system":"Depends on the public route surviving that decision.","screens":"A share dialogue.","money":"None.","what":"Ship it in the pilot as a document share link. Something to measure this quarter; it collides with the open public-page decision, and the landing surface may not survive."},{"k":"d","label":"Decide after the twenty team conversations","gains":["The build after the pilot is the earliest point anyway","The answer arrives before anything is built"],"costs":["One more open item carried into the build","Nothing measurable happens this quarter"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Decide after the twenty team conversations. The answer arrives before anything is built; one more open item carried into the build."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The build after the pilot is the first point with anything to invite somebody into.","The banned list exists to keep out inviter rewards; this invite has none."],"flip":"If the twenty team conversations describe the invite as how work starts, it belongs in the pilot.","linked":["G15"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:200","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:437","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:119","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:130","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:132","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:134","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r17-h7-content-seo-distribution.md:138","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:300"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Referral comes after there are users.","recWhy":"Referral sits on a banned list beside streaks and XP with no written argument, and the research allows exactly one form, a workspace invite that gives the inviter nothing, which is not what the ban was written to keep out. The invite needs something to invite a person into, and that is the team surface, who changed what and review state across a repository, which Q6 puts in Phase 1 and Q11 keeps asynchronous. The build after the pilot is the first point where the form has a destination. The research expects it to underperform two-sided rewards substantially, and that is accepted, because inviter rewards are the thing the ban rightly excludes. A share link in the pilot collides with the public-route decision in Q11 and F19. If the twenty team conversations describe the invite as how work starts on their team, it belongs in the pilot and this moves forward."},{"id":"G24","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Toolchains","weight":"medium","q":"Is plan mode really demoted, given it out-scores both named toolchains by two orders of magnitude?","lede":"The plan demotes the highest-attention population on a file location that may be a one-line setting.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":150,"cols":[{"title":"Plan mode","tone":"accent"},{"title":"superpowers","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"spec-kit","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Best Reddit score","cells":["1,776","38 or fewer","38 or fewer"]},{"label":"Where it writes","cells":["~/.claude/plans, outside the repo","Inside the repo, committed","Inside the repo"]},{"label":"File lifetime","cells":["Deleted after 30 days","Git","Git"]},{"label":"What we would ship","cells":["A plansDirectory recipe","A README line","A README line"]},{"label":"Cost to settle it","cells":["One hour, still unspent","Done","Done"]}],"note":"One table in the plan calls the file location untested; another demotes the channel on it.","caption":"The largest attention pool, demoted on a detail nobody has checked."},"stakes":"We walk away from the biggest audience on an unverified detail that costs an hour.","state":["The plan demotes plan mode to, at most, a `plansDirectory` onboarding step.","The competitor table shows plan mode out-scoring both named tools; its file location is untested.","The gap register priced the check at one hour."],"tension":["Plan mode writes outside the repository and deletes after thirty days.","A thirty-day deletion means our review record outlives the document it points to.","Two sections of the same plan disagree about the same channel."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Reddit attention: plan mode against the two named toolchains","note":"Recorded at docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:149. Points on the three highest-scoring plan-mode posts, against the best score for either named tool.","unit":"Reddit points","data":[["Plan mode, post 1",1776],["Plan mode, post 2",1409],["Plan mode, post 3",1329],["Best for superpowers or spec-kit",38]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why it was demoted, and what is unchecked","items":[["Where plan mode writes","~/.claude/plans/<slug>.md, outside the repo","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307"],["Retention of those files","Deleted after 30 days","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307"],["The setting the capture loop section (12) names","plansDirectory","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307"],["Whether it can point inside a repository","Untested: \"Where the files live is untested\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:149"],["What the gap register said before round 2","\"Plan mode out-scores the named toolchains on Reddit; where its files live decides whether they are a channel. One hour\"","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:40"],["Second unchecked leg","Even inside the repo, a 30-day deletion means the review anchor would outlive the document it anchors to"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep it demoted to an onboarding step","gains":["Consistent with the working-tree framing","No hour spent, no recipe to maintain"],"costs":["Walks away from the largest attention pool unverified","Leaves two plan sections disagreeing"],"system":"No change.","screens":"An onboarding step.","money":"None.","what":"Keep plan mode demoted to a plansDirectory onboarding step. Consistent with the working-tree framing and no hour spent; it walks away from the largest attention pool unverified."},{"k":"b","label":"Promote it to a week-2 channel and ship a plansDirectory recipe","gains":["Biggest population, smallest artefact","No maintainer's permission required"],"costs":["Fails if the setting cannot point into a repository","The 30-day deletion stays unanswered"],"system":"No change. A documented setting.","screens":"One recipe page.","money":"None.","what":"Promote it to a week-2 channel and ship a plansDirectory recipe. The biggest population and the smallest artefact; it fails if the setting cannot point into a repository."},{"k":"c","label":"Spend the hour: confirm plansDirectory and the deletion behaviour, then decide","gains":["Settles a two-section contradiction for an hour","Blocks nothing meanwhile"],"costs":["One more item on a long week-0 list","The answer may simply confirm the demotion"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None yet.","money":"One hour.","what":"Spend the hour: confirm plansDirectory and the deletion behaviour, then decide. Settles a two-section contradiction and blocks nothing; the answer may confirm the demotion."},{"k":"d","label":"Treat plan mode as a recruitment channel rather than a product channel","gains":["Uses the biggest pool for what the two-week test needs","No product claim to defend yet"],"costs":["Outbound into other people's communities","Still needs the file question answered"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Treat plan mode as a recruitment channel rather than a product channel. Uses the biggest pool for what the two-week test needs; outbound into other people's communities, and the file question stays."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Two sections of the same document disagree, and one hour resolves which is right.","The attention gap is two orders of magnitude, which is worth an hour to check."],"flip":"If plansDirectory cannot point inside a repository, the demotion stands and the row should say so.","linked":["G22","G6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:307","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:149","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:40","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:126"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Channel weighting belongs with the launch plan.","recWhy":"Claude Code's plan mode out-scores both named toolchains by two orders of magnitude in the competitor table, and the plan demotes it to an onboarding step on a file-location assumption nobody has tested. Two sections of the same document disagree about the same channel. The gap register priced the check at one hour: does plansDirectory point inside a repository, and does the 30-day deletion apply there. The second part matters, because a review record that outlives the document it points to is the failure E36 is built to avoid. An hour that resolves a contradiction and blocks nothing is worth spending before promotion or demotion is written down. If plansDirectory cannot point inside a repository, the demotion stands and the row says so; if it can, (b) is the cheapest channel in the table."},{"id":"G27","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Adjacent audiences","weight":"medium","q":"Do we spend outreach on the git-backed CMS audience, where the review-shaped hole is visible?","lede":"Pages CMS edits markdown in a repository, has no paid tier, and lists inline comments as \"Soon\".","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"Pages CMS","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"frontmatter","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"What it is","cells":["Git-backed CMS for markdown, MIT","An editor over the same files"]},{"label":"Stars","cells":["3,976","Not published"]},{"label":"Price","cells":["No paid tier at all","Free individual; $4 to $5 team, untested"]},{"label":"Review","cells":["Inline comments listed as \"Soon\"","Review state is the product"]},{"label":"What the audience expects","cells":["Free","To be told why this is not"]}],"note":"Developers deploy from repositories at scale: 443,392 root CNAME files.","caption":"The right audience with the right hole, trained to expect it free."},"stakes":"It is the most reachable adjacent audience we found, and the least likely to pay.","state":["The channel table names no adjacent audience beyond the two toolchains.","Developers deploy from repos at scale: 443,392 root CNAME files, 113,536 Astro configs.","Pages CMS is MIT, 3,976 stars, no paid tier, inline comments \"Soon\"."],"tension":["They already keep documents in a repository, so the review file needs no explaining.","No paid tier anywhere in view, so they are trained to expect this free.","Outreach hours here compete with the two toolchain maintainers in week 2."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Add it as a week-3 channel with its own integration page","gains":["The audience needs no education about repositories","The gap is visible and unfilled"],"costs":["Competes with toolchain outreach for the same hours","An audience with no paid tier in view"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One integration page.","money":"None.","what":"Add the git-backed CMS audience as a week-3 channel with its own page. No education about repositories needed and the gap is visible; it competes with toolchain outreach for hours."},{"k":"b","label":"Ignore it; the audience does not pay","gains":["Protects week 2 for the toolchains","No new audience to learn or write for"],"costs":["Gives up the adjacency where the incumbent names the hole","The hole stays open for whoever fills it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Ignore it; the audience does not pay. Protects week 2 for the toolchains; it gives up the adjacency where the incumbent itself names the hole."},{"k":"c","label":"Use it as a recruitment pool for the ten strangers, not as a channel","gains":["The two-week test needs people more than a channel","No permission and no build"],"costs":["No compounding effect at all","Their expectations are set by a free tool"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Use it as a recruitment pool for the ten strangers, not a channel. The two-week test needs people more than a channel, at no permission and no build; their expectations are set by a free tool."},{"k":"d","label":"Build the review feature Pages CMS lists as Soon, and offer it to them","gains":["Directly addresses a stated need","The engine already does the hard part"],"costs":["Builds for a project that gives everything away","Ties our roadmap to their release schedule"],"system":"An integration against their storage model.","screens":"A view inside somebody else's product.","money":"Engineering days against an audience that pays nothing.","what":"Build the review feature Pages CMS lists as Soon and offer it to them. Addresses a stated need and the engine does the hard part; it builds for a project that gives everything away."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The two-week test needs named people, and these already keep documents in repositories.","It costs no permission, no build and no channel hours."],"flip":"If a Pages CMS user in the pilot says they would pay, the audience is a channel.","linked":["G15","G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:300","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:60","https://pagescms.org/"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A new audience comes after the first one is served.","recWhy":"Pages CMS edits markdown in a repository, is MIT with 3,976 stars, has no paid tier, and lists inline comments as Soon, so the review-shaped hole is visible and named by the incumbent itself. Developers deploy from repositories at scale, 443,392 root CNAME files and 113,536 Astro configs, and these people already keep documents in git, so the review file needs no explaining to them. What the two-week test needs most is named people, and G15's cold list has to come from somewhere; this audience is a pool that costs no permission, no build and no channel hours. What it is not is a paying channel. No paid tier anywhere in view means they are trained to expect this free, and Q13 already gives the editor away, so the pool feeds the stranger test rather than the price test. If a Pages CMS user in the pilot says they would pay, the audience becomes a channel and gets its own page."},{"id":"G2","cat":"Go-to-market & channels","sub":"Obsidian","weight":"high","q":"Does any Obsidian surface ship, and do the four mentions still promising one get deleted?","lede":"The plugin was week 2's channel in every earlier plan, and the current plan still mentions it four times.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":170,"cols":[{"title":"The live-preview fix","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Additive review-state extension","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Forum demand, same population","cells":["501 likes, 117 posts, 18,331 views","0 likes, 1 post in three years"]},{"label":"Can a plugin do it","cells":["No. The tree is depth-1 by construction","Yes. Decoration over byte ranges"]},{"label":"Nearest measured launch","cells":["115 installs in 14 days, against our 200 kill line","Never sized"]},{"label":"What it costs to ship","cells":["U+200C written into the user's files","Obsidian owns the registry and the queue"]}],"note":"Both columns were measured in the same forum on the same day.","caption":"Demand sits on the surface we cannot build. The one we can has none."},"stakes":"Cutting it removes the pilot's only channel and a retention lever, replacing neither.","state":["The plan cuts the plugin: Obsidian's Live Preview syntax tree is one level deep, by construction.","The tiers table still lists it as the pilot's channel, killed under 200 installs.","The weekly schedule, the retention table and the product-family section still carry it."],"tension":["Every engine claim is measured on seven Obsidian vaults.","Obsidian core shipped nothing on AI, agents, diffs or review in twenty releases.","Agent-writing plugins there hold roughly forty times the review-shaped plugin's downloads."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The Obsidian forum, one population, one metric, 2026-09-08","items":[["Nested code block rendering bug (likes, posts, views)","501 / 117 / 18,331","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410"],["Closest review-state request (likes, views, posts in about 3 years)","0 / 251 / 1","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410"],["\"Tracking changes within notes\" (likes)","1","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410"],["\"Suggestion Mode like Google Docs/Word\" (likes)","3","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the plugin registry actually pays out","items":[["Community plugins","7,092","r25-w4:81"],["Median downloads per plugin","532","r25-w4:81"],["Plugins under 1,000 downloads","59.1% (4,194)","r25-w4:81"],["Share of all downloads held by the top 10%","91.7%","r25-w4:81"],["Relay, the exemplar the plan leaned on (rank)","128 of 7,092 (p98.4)","r25-w4:19"],["Relay's real best single release vs the 191,997 cumulative figure","21,047, a 9.1 times inflation","r25-w4:18"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The cost of conceding this audience, which the capture loop section (12) does not price","items":[["The corpus every engine claim rests on","test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json is seven Obsidian vaults; one wiki is 77% of the 8,513 files","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"],["What that makes the 83% refusal rate","\"A property of those vaults, not of markdown in the wild\"","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514"],["Consequence","We would be cutting the only population against which the product is verified"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where the cut channels still live in the current plan","cols":["Location","What it still says","Consequence if the capture loop section (12) governs"],"rows":[["§12 Exhibit 17, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","Free Obsidian plugin, CUT","none"],["§12 Exhibit 17, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","Chrome extension, CUT","none"],["§8 Exhibit 6, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","\"Free Obsidian live-preview plugin, week 2; kill: under 200 installs in 14 days\"","The pilot loses its only listed channel"],["§8 Exhibit 6, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203","\"Chrome extension; MCP server\" as surfaces for the build after the pilot","The build after the pilot loses a surface"],["§14 Exhibit 19, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","\"plugin shipped\" in weeks 1 to 2","A fortnight deliverable disappears"],["§17 Exhibit 24, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","\"The plugin … stays installed in a vault the user already opens daily\"","A retention lever disappears with no replacement"],["§19 diagram and Exhibit 26, docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:464 and :477","\"Chrome extension · Obsidian plugin — reading and distribution\"; \"Free; channels — MVP-1, week 2\"","A product-family member disappears"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No Obsidian surface; delete the four surviving mentions","gains":["The plan stops saying both things","Two unreplaced holes become visible"],"costs":["Week 2 loses its only non-outbound action","Our engine's test population stops being a market"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Removes it from the product-family diagram.","money":"None.","what":"No Obsidian surface, and the four surviving mentions come out: tiers table, weekly schedule, retention table, product family. Week 2 loses its one non-outbound action."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the additive review-state CodeMirror extension in week 2","gains":["Buildable, because it needs no nesting data","Keeps a foothold in the test-vault population"],"costs":["Ships the 0-likes feature, not the 501-likes fix","Adds build days to a build-nothing fortnight"],"system":"CodeMirror extension over `.frontmatter/review.jsonl`; no re-parse.","screens":"A highlight layer inside Obsidian.","money":"None. Free plugin.","what":"Ship the additive review-state CodeMirror extension in week 2. Buildable, because it needs no nesting data; it ships the zero-likes feature into a build-nothing fortnight."},{"k":"c","label":"Ship the live-preview fix as an appearance-only repaint","gains":["Reaches the one measured demand in that population"],"costs":["Depends on DOM classes Obsidian has already deleted once","Both working plugins write U+200C into user files"],"system":"A rendering hack outside the engine.","screens":"None of ours.","money":"None.","what":"Ship the live-preview fix as an appearance-only repaint. Reaches the one measured demand in that population; the parse was verified unreachable twice, and the working plugins write U+200C into files."},{"k":"d","label":"Delete three mentions; keep the retention lever open in writing","gains":["Honest about the one loss with no replacement"],"costs":["Leaves a lever depending on a cut channel"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Delete three mentions and keep the retention lever open in writing. Honest about the one loss with no replacement; a lever that depends on a cut channel."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The parse was verified unreachable twice, independently, so (c) is not available at any price.","If four mentions stay, someone builds the cut plugin from the tiers table next month."],"flip":"If an Obsidian user asks for review state unprompted, the additive extension is the cheapest reach available.","linked":["FF2","G22"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:410","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:127","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:81","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r25-w4-retention-and-growth.md:18","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:464","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","merged: G3"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"No Obsidian surface ships in the first build.","recWhy":"The plugin was week 2's channel in every earlier plan, and the current plan cuts it while still mentioning it four times, so someone builds the cut plugin from the tiers table next month unless the mentions go. The cut is technical and final. Obsidian's Live Preview syntax tree is one level deep by construction, verified unreachable twice, so (c) is not available at any price, and the two plugins that do fix nesting write an invisible character into user files, the thing this product refuses to do. Every engine number is measured on seven Obsidian vaults, and that population gave review state zero likes and a rendering bug 501: it is our test corpus, not our market. Q3 puts one surface first, which is why this sits at not now; MK7 carries the same cut from the market side. An Obsidian user asking for review state unprompted makes the additive extension the cheapest reach, re-costed then.","dupes":{"dropped":["PL8"],"areas":["Plan, scope & sequencing"],"why":"Plan, scope & sequencing asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option a here: no Obsidian surface, and delete every remaining mention. It adds rewriting the plan's no-go branch as services and engine. A Market & competition card that pairs an Obsidian extension with the ?src= link is separate.","agree":true}},{"id":"B1","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Capacity","weight":"critical","q":"Do we strike the two-founder assumption from every number, or sign an agreement naming the person, date and hours?","lede":"Two governing documents disagree on how many founders exist, and every number is denominated in them.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"The plan as written, two founders","lines":[{"t":"Monthly nut ₹1,08,918","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Default-alive at 501 paying users","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Client hours 54 to 78 a month","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Cumulative visitors 171,200","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Cost to the published calendar: zero","mark":"warn"}]},"after":{"title":"Re-derived at one founder","lines":[{"t":"Monthly nut ₹63,918","mark":"good"},{"t":"Default-alive at 294 paying users","mark":"good"},{"t":"Client hours 32.0 to 45.7 a month","mark":"good"},{"t":"Cumulative visitors 100,428","mark":"good"},{"t":"Cost to the calendar: a 1.46× stretch","mark":"stop"}]},"via":"strike the assumption","note":"Commit record: 80 of 80 here and 416 of 416 on the sibling, one author (the current plan).","caption":"Every downstream figure, re-derived at one founder"},"stakes":"Nothing can be quoted until this settles: each number moves 41 to 70 per cent.","state":["One author made 80 of 80 commits here and 416 of 416 on the sibling md repo.","The decisions document says two founders and a team.","No signed agreement exists in the repo; the critique's deadline for one was 2026-09-07."],"tension":["The nut moves 70 per cent, default-alive 41.","The two-founder case is the expensive one: a second draw from day one.","Documents naming a second founder add no hours; a signed agreement with hours would."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The commit record, re-counted 2026-09-08T18:27Z","items":[["frontmatter, this repo","82 of 82 commits, one author","git log --format='%an' | sort | uniq -c. The plan cites 80 of 80 at PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231; two commits have landed since, both by the same author"],["Sibling md repo","416 of 416 commits, one author","git -C ../md log; matches PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231 exactly"],["Cofounder named anywhere in the record","0","CRITIQUE.md:1437: a grep finds no cofounder in 16,189 lines"],["Signed founders' agreement in the repo","none found","CRITIQUE.md:2100 set the deadline at 2026-09-07"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the answer changes (CRITIQUE.md:1453-1461)","cols":["Line","Two founders","One founder"],"rows":[["Monthly nut","₹1,08,918","₹63,918"],["Default-alive, paying users","501","294"],["Client hours per month to fund it","54 to 78","32.0 to 45.7"],["Cumulative visitors, 24 months","171,200","100,428"],["Cost to the published calendar","zero","1.46× stretch"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Strike it. One founder is the default; re-derive every figure before publishing","gains":["Default-alive falls to 294 paying users, about 4,185 visitors a month.","Removes the largest untested assumption in the plan."],"costs":["No second person for client work, so the calendar stretches 1.46×.","The plan reads smaller to anyone you show it to."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Nut falls from ₹1,08,918 to ₹63,918.","what":"Strike the second founder. One person is the default and every figure is re-derived before it is published; default-alive falls to 294 paying users, and the calendar stretches 1.46 times."},{"k":"b","label":"Sign this week: a named person, an equity split, a start date, hours a week","gains":["Client work stops costing product velocity."],"costs":["Doubles the draw from day one, before revenue exists."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Nut ₹1,08,918; default-alive 501 users.","what":"Sign this week: a named person, an equity split, a start date, hours a week. Client work stops costing product velocity; it doubles the draw from day one, before revenue exists."},{"k":"c","label":"Name the second person as a contractor with an hours floor, not a founder","gains":["Buys hours without giving away equity."],"costs":["Cash cost against a consulting-funded nut, and an IP assignment."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹15,000 to ₹40,000 for the agreement, plus hours.","what":"Name the second person as a contractor with an hours floor, not a founder. Buys hours without giving away equity; a cash cost against a consulting-funded nut, and an IP assignment."},{"k":"d","label":"Carry both scenarios in the plan, labelled, until revenue decides","gains":["Honest about what is unknown, and costs nothing today."],"costs":["The calendar cannot be committed to at all."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Carry both scenarios in the plan, labelled, until revenue decides. Honest about what is unknown and costs nothing today; the calendar cannot be committed to at all."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The only measurement available says one person, twice, across 498 commits.","Striking it takes an afternoon, and leaving it skews every number downstream."],"flip":"A signed agreement naming hours a week, at which point re-derive rather than assume linear scaling.","linked":["B18","B2","PL2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/DECIDE.md:156","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1437","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1447","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1459","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1460","docs/CRITIQUE.md:2100","docs/BRIEF.md:116","docs/BUSINESS.md:759"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The pace decision needs to know who builds. Every date in the plan rests on the answer.","recWhy":"Two governing documents disagree on how many founders exist, and every number in the plan is denominated in that count: the nut moves 70 percent and default-alive moves 41 with the answer. The only measurement available says one person, twice: 80 of 80 commits here and 416 of 416 on the sibling md repository. No signed agreement exists in the repository, and the critique's deadline for one was 7 September. So strike it, re-derive every figure at one founder, and accept that the plan reads smaller to anyone shown it; a plan that reads larger than its commit log is the lesson three withdrawn numbers already taught. This is B1, the first of the three facts on the final set, and only the founders can answer it. A signed agreement naming hours a week flips it, and the figures are then re-derived rather than doubled.","dupes":{"dropped":["PL18"],"areas":["Plan, scope & sequencing"],"why":"Plan, scope & sequencing asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option a here: record a one-person build and re-derive every estimate, date and cap at a one-person rate.","agree":true}},{"id":"B2","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Funding","weight":"critical","q":"How is the build funded: services booked before it starts, one engagement a quarter alongside it, or a raise?","lede":"Services are the only funding line, and the plan's hour cap is nearly twice the measured ceiling.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Two engagements first","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"One a quarter","tone":"accent"},{"title":"₹4 Cr raise","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Money in","cells":["₹6,00,000 up front","₹3,00,000 every quarter","₹4 crore for 11%"]},{"label":"Months of the one-founder nut","cells":["9.4","continuous, 1.56× cover","367"]},{"label":"Founder hours it costs","cells":["180","30 a month averaged","none, but a board"]},{"label":"Build starts","cells":["after 4.5 months of client work","week 0","week 0"]},{"label":"Obligation taken on","cells":["two client SLAs","one client SLA at a time","a seven-year fund horizon"]},{"label":"Precedent in the record","cells":["none","Obsidian: 8 staff, no investors","Dendron: no fit for a venture-backed business"]}],"note":"Hours at the measured client-lane ceiling of 40 a month in one contiguous week (the services research).","caption":"Three ways to pay for the build, and what each costs in time"},"stakes":"Pick wrong and the build stops four months, or the company takes a seven-year obligation.","state":["The plan caps client work at 78 hours a month.","The 90-day calendar schedules no client hours at all.","The refusal to raise lives only in the decisions document."],"tension":["78 hours is 1.95× the measured ceiling of 40 hours.","₹4 crore is 367 months of runway against a seven-year fund.","Burn without services is ₹4,700 to ₹9,300 a month, so runway is not the constraint."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The two lanes, priced","items":[["Two-year product success case, monthly","₹1,09,135","BRIEF.md:25: 502 paying users, 171,200 cumulative visitors"],["The same money in consulting hours","78 hours a month","BRIEF.md:170, at the measured ₹1,400 to ₹2,000/hour"],["One productised engagement","₹3,00,000 / $8,000, 90 founder-hours","r23-u4:84 and r23-u4:95; 73.5% repeatable"],["Four engagements a year","₹12,00,000 = the ₹1L/mo milestone","r23-u4:62"],["Funnel equivalent of one client","63,060 visitors","r23-u4:62"],["Burn, if no services at all","₹4,700 to ₹9,300 a month","r23-u4:54: runway is not the constraint"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The cap the plan sets, against the ceiling the record measured","cols":["Source","Client hours per month"],"rows":[["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:329, the risk row","capped at 78"],["PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292, to cover the ₹1.09L two-founder nut","54 to 78"],["CRITIQUE.md:1460, against the one-founder nut","32.0 to 45.7"],["r23-u4:60, measured ceiling from active-day clustering","40, in one contiguous week"],["r23-u4:62, four engagements a year averaged","30"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The two routes, as priced","items":[["Antler India","₹4 crore for 11%, decision in 3 weeks; implied post-money ₹36.4 crore ≈ $3.81M","r22-t3-money.md:64, 70, fetched 2026-08-31"],["Y Combinator","$500K on two SAFEs, roughly 9.5% total at a $15M cap","r22-t3-money.md:65, fetched 2026-08-31"],["₹4 crore expressed in the nut","367 months of runway","DECIDE.md:150"],["Fund horizon","about 7 years","DECIDE.md:151"],["Trigger that would flip the verdict","three unsolicited inbound teams asking for multi-editor access","CRITIQUE.md:1075"],["Where that trigger appears in the plan","nowhere","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md, grep 2026-09-08"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the alternative funds, with what it costs","cols":["Route","Buys","Costs"],"rows":[["₹4 crore raise","367 months of the two-founder nut","11% and a seven-year obligation against a founder wall at 1,239 paying users (BUSINESS.md:781)"],["Four engagements a year","₹12,00,000 = the ₹1L/mo milestone","360 founder-hours a year, 16.6% of annual engineering time (r23-u4:62)"],["One engagement","₹3,00,000 and 63,060 visitors of funnel equivalent","90 founder-hours (r23-u4:84, 62)"],["No funding at all","full ownership","a burn of ₹4,700 to ₹9,300 a month, which is survivable (r23-u4:54)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Book two engagements before the week-3 go/no-go. No product code until invoiced","gains":["₹6,00,000 is 9.4 months of the one-founder nut."],"costs":["180 founder-hours, 4.5 months of client lane, engine defects unfixed."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹6,00,000 booked; zero product days.","what":"Book two engagements before the week-3 go or no-go, and write no product code until they are invoiced. ₹6,00,000 is 9.4 months of the one-founder nut; 180 founder-hours and 4.5 months of client lane."},{"k":"b","label":"Run both lanes from week 0 at the plan's 78-hour cap","gains":["Fastest to a shipped product on paper."],"costs":["1.95× the measured ceiling, so one lane absorbs the other."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹1L a month from hours that cannot be worked.","what":"Run both lanes from week 0 at the plan's 78-hour cap. Fastest to a shipped product on paper; 78 hours is 1.95 times the measured ceiling, so one lane absorbs the other."},{"k":"c","label":"One engagement a quarter, cap hours from the nut, and a dated refusal to raise","what":"₹3,00,000 a quarter at 30 hours a month, plus a twelve-month no on funding with a named trigger.","gains":["₹1,00,000 a month is 1.56× the one-founder nut, inside the ceiling.","One client is worth 63,060 funnel visitors."],"costs":["Never produces the lump that buys a year of calm.","Leaves nothing for a support hire."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹12,00,000 a year at 360 founder-hours."},{"k":"d","label":"Apply to Antler or Y Combinator now","gains":["₹4 crore is 367 months of the two-founder nut."],"costs":["A seven-year obligation, while support fills the founder's month at 1,239 paying users."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"₹4 crore for 11%, post-money $3.81M.","what":"Apply to Antler or Y Combinator now. ₹4 crore is 367 months of the two-founder nut; a seven-year obligation, while support fills the founder's month at 1,239 paying users."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Corrected for the ceiling, (a) and (c) buy the same money; only (c) leaves the build running.","Either way, send the proposals in week 0 and treat the closes as the measurement."],"flip":"Three unsolicited inbound teams asking for multi-editor access, the critique's own trigger.","linked":["B1","B12","B21"],"sources":["merged: B20","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:329","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:351","docs/DECIDE.md:150","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1075","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1411","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1766","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1878","docs/BUSINESS.md:781","docs/BRIEF.md:170","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r22-t3-money.md:1","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r22-t3-money.md:64","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:54","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:60","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:62","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:84","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r23-u4-consultation-and-services.md:95"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"How the build is funded decides how many hours a week it can have.","dependsOn":["B1"],"recWhy":"Services are the only funding line, the plan caps client work at 78 hours a month, and the measured ceiling is 40, so the cap is 1.95 times what one person has delivered. The 90-day calendar schedules no client hours at all. Burn without services is ₹4,700 to ₹9,300 a month, so runway is not the constraint, and a raise buys 367 months of runway against a seven-year obligation. Corrected for the ceiling, booking two engagements first and one a quarter alongside buy the same money; only the second leaves the build running, and PR20 gives the engagement its shape. ₹1,00,000 a month is 1.56 times the one-founder nut, inside the ceiling, and one client is worth 63,060 funnel visitors. This is B2, the second fact on the final set; send the proposals in week 0 and treat the closes as the measurement. Three unsolicited inbound teams reopen the raise."},{"id":"B14","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Compliance ops","weight":"high","q":"Which statutory work happens before the pilot: the CERT-In filing and runbook, the grievance officer, a GST opinion, or none?","lede":"The legal duties binding today have no size floor, no owner and nothing written; one has a six-hour clock.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"today","label":"CERT-In, six hours from noticing","note":"no size floor; Annexure II filing missing","tone":"stop"},{"when":"today","label":"SPDI Rules: policy, consent, grievance officer","note":"redress within one month; nobody named","tone":"warn"},{"when":"today","label":"SPDI Rule 7 transfer gate","note":"processor agreements due now, not in 2027","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-11-13","label":"DPDP sections 6(9) and 27(1)(d)","note":"first substantive tranche","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"2027-05-13","label":"DPDP sections 3 to 17 and the penalty chapter","note":"247 days out from the plan's date","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"The register had this backwards until round 2; the live regime is the IT Act and the SPDI Rules.","caption":"What binds now, against what the plan was written against"},"stakes":"A missed statutory clock is a violation rather than a delay, and it attaches to the pilot.","state":["The six-hour CERT-In duty is named; the Annexure II point-of-contact filing is missing.","A grievance officer is due now under SPDI Rule 5(9), and none is published.","None of it appears in the week-0 list."],"tension":["CGST section 24(iii) has no turnover floor; another reading says the vendor charges GST.","The company already buys foreign services, so whichever reading holds is operative.","A chartered accountant answers the tax question in an hour; no document here can."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Live today, not in 2027","items":[["CERT-In breach report","within 6 hours of noticing, to incident@cert-in.org.in","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543"],["Size floor","none","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543"],["Annexure II point-of-contact filing","missing everywhere in our record","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543"],["IT Act Section 70B(7) exposure","up to one year or ₹1 lakh; Section 70B(8) requires a CERT-In officer's complaint first","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543"],["Grievance officer under SPDI Rule 5(9)","one named person, redress within one month","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:542, 548"],["Log residency","180 days of ICT logs inside Indian jurisdiction, NTP to NIC or NPL","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:544"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What a 21-day founder absence does to the clock (r26-c9-capacity.md:149-156)","cols":["Domain","Consequence"],"rows":[["Statutory","CERT-In's 6 hours from noticing; DPDP Section 8(6); GDPR Art. 33 at 72 hours, none of it recoverable"],["On-call","zero responders; Better Stack's free tier covers one"],["Support","103 to 144 tickets queue against a 24-hour email expectation"],["Billing","about 8 involuntary churns, on a payment mechanism with one attempt and no retry"],["The nut","client retainers carry their own SLA; missing three weeks risks the funding source"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The two rows, as recorded in VERIFICATION.md","cols":["Row","Verdict","What it says"],"rows":[["8: RCM bounded by the registration threshold","REFUTED","CGST Section 24(iii): reverse-charge payers \"shall be required to be registered\", no turnover floor (VERIFICATION.md:41)"],["7: RCM on imported services","REVISED","Notification 10/2017-IGST(R) Sl.1 excludes a \"non-taxable online recipient\"; unregistered means the vendor charges GST, registered means you self-assess (VERIFICATION.md:40)"],["The plan's risk register","quotes row 8 only","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:330"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The foreign services already being bought","items":[["Model access","Claude Sonnet 5 at $2/$10 per MTok","BUSINESS.md:719, fetched 2026-08-30"],["Edge compute","Cloudflare Workers Paid, $5/month minimum per account","BUSINESS.md:719, fetched 2026-08-30"],["Object storage","R2 at $0.015/GB-month","BUSINESS.md:719"],["Payment processor","Dodo Payments, 4% + 15¢ on Indian cards","BUSINESS.md:719"],["Apple Developer Program","$99 per membership year","BUSINESS.md:719"],["CA retainer already budgeted","₹35,000 to ₹90,000 a year, quotes not yet obtained","BUSINESS.md:678"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Do the whole pack in week 0: file, runbook, grievance officer, CA opinion","gains":["Converts the live criminal-adjacent exposure into a filed form.","The CA retainer is already in the budget."],"costs":["Hours from a schedule already short of engineering days.","The runbook needs revising once the Firebase decision lands."],"system":"No change.","screens":"A grievance and privacy page.","money":"₹35,000 to ₹90,000 a year CA retainer, budgeted.","what":"The whole pack in week 0: the CERT-In point-of-contact filing, the six-hour runbook, a published grievance officer and a CA's GST opinion. Hours from a short schedule; the runbook is revised later."},{"k":"b","label":"CERT-In filing and runbook now; grievance officer now; tax at first revenue","gains":["Deals with the six-hour clock, the one not recoverable."],"costs":["The GST obligation attaches to purchases already made. Deferring accrues."],"system":"No change.","screens":"A grievance page.","money":"Nothing today; an accrued position, size unknown.","what":"CERT-In filing and runbook now, grievance officer now, tax at first revenue. Deals with the six-hour clock, the one not recoverable; GST attaches to purchases already made, so deferring accrues."},{"k":"c","label":"File everything at the first paying customer","gains":["Defers all of it, at zero cost today."],"costs":["The CERT-In directions have no size floor, so they already bind the pilot."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None today.","what":"File everything at the first paying customer. Defers all of it at zero cost today; the CERT-In directions have no size floor, so they already bind the pilot."},{"k":"d","label":"Wait for the Firebase decision, since the answer changes what data we hold","gains":["Avoids writing the runbook twice."],"costs":["The Firebase decision has no date, and the filing does not depend on it."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None today.","what":"Wait for the Firebase decision, since the answer changes what data we hold. Avoids writing the runbook twice; that decision has no date, and the filing does not depend on it."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The filing is a form and the runbook a page, and both get more urgent.","The grievance officer appears on the site, not in code, so it costs no engineering day."],"flip":"If the Firebase decision lands within days, write the runbook after it, but file the point of contact first.","linked":["B18","B8","L4"],"sources":["merged: B15","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:330","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:351","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:542","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:548","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:129","docs/VERIFICATION.md:40","docs/VERIFICATION.md:41","docs/BUSINESS.md:678","docs/BUSINESS.md:719","docs/BUSINESS.md:771","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c9-capacity.md:149"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Some statutory work is due before the first stored kit, so it is scheduled with phase zero.","dependsOn":["L7"],"recWhy":"The legal duties that bind today have no size floor, no owner and nothing written, and one has a six-hour clock: the CERT-In direction is named in the plan while the Annexure II point-of-contact filing is missing. A grievance officer is due now under SPDI Rule 5(9) and none is published. CGST section 24(iii) has no turnover floor on one reading, and the company already buys foreign services, so whichever reading holds is operative; a chartered accountant answers it in an hour. The filing is a form, the runbook a page, the officer appears on the site rather than in code, and the CA retainer is already in the budget, so the pack costs hours, not engineering days. Q9 deletes firestore.rules, so file the point of contact first and write the runbook once that lands. Deferring to the first paying customer fails because the directions already bind the pilot."},{"id":"B6","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Support","weight":"critical","q":"Do we instrument the ten-stranger pilot to measure support messages per paying user, or price with a 3× error bar on the largest cost?","lede":"Support is the cost structure of this business, and the number driving it is written two ways.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":210,"cols":[{"title":"0.10 per paid user","tone":"good"},{"title":"0.30 at 12 minutes","tone":"warn"},{"title":"0.30 at 20.72 minutes","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Where it is written","cells":["the PRD","BUSINESS.md","the support research's measured ticket mix"]},{"label":"Founder-hours per paying user","cells":["not derived","0.1445 a month","0.249 a month"]},{"label":"The founder wall","cells":["far out","1,239 paying users","well under 1,000"]},{"label":"What it does to ₹299","cells":["+₹47.43","−₹28.22","worse again"]},{"label":"Times measured on our users","cells":["0","0","0"]}],"note":"The 20.72-minute mean comes from the Obsidian forum tag histogram, weighted; 1.73× the flat 12.","caption":"One coefficient, three readings, none taken from a real user"},"stakes":"Every price, the hire trigger and the founder's support ceiling come from an unmeasured number.","state":["The plan states 90 founder-hours at 10,000 users, no denominator.","BUSINESS.md uses 0.3 tickets per paying user at 12 minutes; the PRD uses 0.10.","The pilot asks the ten strangers nothing about needing help."],"tension":["At 0.30 support fills the founder's whole month at 1,239 paying users.","At 0.10 an individual subscription flips from below cost to above it.","Ten users give no interval, but they separate two numbers differing 3×."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The coefficient, three ways, none measured on our users","items":[["BUSINESS.md:771","0.30 tickets per paying user per month, 12 minutes each"],["PRD Section 21.1, quoted at CRITIQUE.md:901","0.10 tickets per paying user per month"],["r6 measured mix, weighted","20.72 minutes per ticket","r6-support-deflection.md:68: 1.73× the flat 12"],["Times measured on our own users","0","CRITIQUE.md:901"],["No vendor publishes a per-user contact rate","confirmed against Zendesk's 2026 CX Trends and GitLab's public handbook","r6-support-deflection.md:12"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What the spread moves","cols":["Reading","Consequence"],"rows":[["0.30 at 12 minutes (BUSINESS.md:779-781)","0.1445 founder-hours per paying user; the wall at 1,239 paying users"],["0.10 (CRITIQUE.md:901)","₹299's effective contribution moves from −₹28.22 to +₹47.43"],["r6's 20.72 minutes, pre-deflection (r6:68)","80.45 founder-hours a month at 10,000 users"],["r6's deflection plan applied (r6:87)","23.97 founder-hours a month, a 70.2% cut bought with engineering"],["Support-hire trigger, without and with deflection (r6:157-159)","$14.18/hour against $82.64/hour of founder time, a 5.83× difference"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Instrument the pilot: log every message with a category and a handle time","gains":["Separates 0.10 from 0.30 for the cost of a spreadsheet.","Gives the deflection plan a target list from our users."],"costs":["Ten users yields no confidence interval.","Delays the pricing page by the pilot's length."],"system":"A log, not a feature.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Instrument the pilot: log every support message with a category and a handle time. Separates 0.10 from 0.30 for the cost of a spreadsheet; ten users give no confidence interval."},{"k":"b","label":"Ship the six deflection changes first, then measure","what":"Key validator, device-flow auth, never refuse an import, visible conflict state, self-serve billing and local history land first.","gains":["Moves the hire trigger 5.83× and removes slow-to-handle tickets."],"costs":["Real engineering days at 1.21 a week, optimising an unmeasured number."],"system":"Six changes: auth, import, billing, history.","screens":"Conflict state; billing.","money":"80.45 founder-hours to 23.97 at 10,000 users."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the pessimistic reading, 0.30 at 20.72 minutes, and price against it","gains":["Conservative, needs no measurement, and pushes towards teams."],"costs":["If the true rate is 0.10 you priced out of a market."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Pricing page.","money":"Raises modelled cost per user about 73%.","what":"Adopt the pessimistic reading, 0.30 tickets at 20.72 minutes, and price against it. Conservative, needs no measurement and pushes towards teams; if the true rate is 0.10 the price is out of a market."},{"k":"d","label":"Publish the 90-hour figure with its denominator stated, and measure later","gains":["Cheapest, and names the thing that makes the figure unauditable."],"costs":["It sits between two incompatible models; the next measurement costs customers."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish the 90-hour figure with its denominator stated and measure later. Cheapest, and it names what makes the figure unauditable; the next measurement costs customers."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["It is the largest cost in the model, and counting messages needs a spreadsheet.","The category gives no help: no vendor publishes a per-user contact rate."],"flip":"Fewer than about five messages across the fortnight, at which point the sample is unreadable.","linked":["B10","B7","PR7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:137","docs/BUSINESS.md:771","docs/BUSINESS.md:777","docs/BUSINESS.md:781","docs/CRITIQUE.md:901","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1075","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:12","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:68","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:87","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:157"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Support load is measured once there are paying users.","recWhy":"Support is the cost structure of this business, and the number that drives it is written two ways. BUSINESS.md uses 0.3 tickets per paying user a month at 12 minutes and the PRD uses 0.10, a threefold gap on the largest cost in the model. At 0.30 support fills the founder's whole month at 1,239 paying users; at 0.10 an individual subscription flips from below cost to above it. Ten strangers yield no confidence interval, and they separate two numbers that differ threefold, for the cost of a spreadsheet with a category and a handle time per message. PR7 adopts the ₹1,30,560 figure, and B7 gives the ten a 48-hour best effort so the messages arrive. The deflection changes in (b) are engineering days spent on an unmeasured number, so they follow the measurement. Under about five messages in the fortnight, the sample is unreadable and the pessimistic reading stands."},{"id":"B7","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Support","weight":"high","q":"What does the free tier get: no commitment, a public forum, or a response window, and does the pilot get an exception?","lede":"The free tier is the whole funnel and, at scale, the largest consumer of founder time, which cannot be bought.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"What the page promises","panes":[{"label":"Free, individual","w":50,"rows":[{"label":"Public forum, category forms, indexed answers","meta":"$0 a month","mark":"✓","tone":"accent"},{"label":"No private queue, no response window","meta":"stated on the page","mark":"no","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Every feature, no caps","meta":"all pilot features, own key","mark":"✓","tone":"good"},{"label":"Pilot fortnight only: 48-hour best effort","meta":"ten strangers","mark":"!","tone":"warn"}]},{"label":"Pro, team","w":50,"rows":[{"label":"One address, a published response window","meta":"per seat","mark":"✓","tone":"good"},{"label":"Same forum, same answers","meta":"shared","mark":"✓","tone":"neutral"}]}],"note":"Free users generate four of every seven tickets in the model (BUSINESS.md).","caption":"Bounding the time, not the product"},"stakes":"Promise too much and one person owes every free user an answer; promise nothing and pilot users go quiet unanswered.","state":["The operating rules give an address, a window, and no commitment on free.","The individual tier is free with no limits in the pilot.","All ten pilot users are free users; the pilot has no billing."],"tension":["A median free user costs ₹7.87 a month, 86 per cent founder time.","India to US Pacific is 12.5 hours, so a sub-24-hour response is impossible solo.","A stranger who goes quiet unanswered is indistinguishable from one who left."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Who the free tier actually is, at the pilot","items":[["Paying users in the pilot","0: billing is in the Absent row","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205"],["Pilot users","10 strangers, all free","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206"],["Share of tickets from free users in the model","4 of every 7","BUSINESS.md:787"],["Recommended channel to 1,000 users","support address plus GitHub Discussions, $0/month","r6-support-deflection.md:120"],["Sub-24-hour first response, solo, India to US Pacific","structurally impossible","r6-support-deflection.md:163"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each phase costs (r6-support-deflection.md:120-121)","cols":["Users","Tickets/month","Tooling","Cost"],"rows":[["0 to 1,000","up to 23","support address + GitHub Discussions + docs","$0/month"],["1,000 to 10,000","23 to 232","Help Scout Standard, 1 seat, + AI Answers","$94.60/month at 40% AI resolution"],["Same volume, flat-price alternative","232","Crisp Essentials","$95.00/month"],["Break-even between the two","93.3 AI resolutions/month","none","derived, r6:121"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What a free user costs","items":[["Hosted inference","₹0.00: bring-your-own key only","CRITIQUE.md:963"],["Infrastructure","₹1.07 a month","CRITIQUE.md:964"],["Support at 0.02 tickets × 12 min at ₹1,700/hour","₹6.80 a month","CRITIQUE.md:965"],["Total, of which founder time","₹7.87, 86% founder time","CRITIQUE.md:966"],["At 10,000 free users","₹68,000 a month of founder time against ₹25,930 of total cloud and AI","CRITIQUE.md:968"],["Share of tickets from free users","4 of every 7","BUSINESS.md:787"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What is already banked as deflection, before any cap","cols":["Settled decision","What it removes"],"rows":[["No plugin marketplace, no client-side code execution","22.79% of the Obsidian-equivalent tagged forum volume (r6:90)"],["File is the only source of truth","\"where is my data\" has a filesystem-path answer (r6:91)"],["Byte-preserving splice","the mangling and diff-noise class (r6:92)"],["Certificate shown on import, not only export","converts a 90-minute forensic ticket into a pre-answered artefact (r6:94)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No commitment on free, stated plainly, in the pilot too","gains":["Honest, and it bounds the founder's time absolutely."],"costs":["A pilot stranger who hits a refusal may get no answer."],"system":"No change.","screens":"One line on the pricing page.","money":"None.","what":"No commitment on free, stated plainly, in the pilot too. Honest, and it bounds the founder's time absolutely; a pilot stranger who hits a refusal may get no answer."},{"k":"b","label":"Unlimited features, bounded support: free goes to a public forum, never a queue","gains":["Bounds the only cost that scales, and answers stay indexed."],"costs":["A ten-user public forum reads as an empty room; some go quiet."],"system":"GitHub Discussions with category forms.","screens":"A support page; a forum link.","money":"$0 a month to 1,000 users.","what":"Unlimited features and bounded support: free goes to a public forum, never a queue. Bounds the only cost that scales and answers stay indexed; a ten-user forum reads as an empty room."},{"k":"c","label":"Forum standing, plus a 48-hour best effort for the ten pilot users only","gains":["Protects the one measurement the whole plan turns on.","Ten users at 48 hours is a few hours weekly."],"costs":["Sets an expectation that must be withdrawn to the same people."],"system":"No change.","screens":"Pricing page; a pilot welcome note.","money":"A few founder-hours a week, twice.","what":"Forum standing for free, plus a 48-hour best effort for the ten pilot users only. Protects the one measurement the plan turns on at a few hours a week; an expectation withdrawn later."},{"k":"d","label":"Cap the free tier by vault size or repository count instead of support","gains":["Creates a paid moment the product can see."],"costs":["Contradicts free, no limits, and cuts the funnel with the ops bill."],"system":"A metering surface where none exists.","screens":"An upgrade prompt at the ceiling.","money":"Unknown; nothing comparable meters an editor.","what":"Cap the free tier by vault size or repository count instead of support. Creates a paid moment the product can see; it contradicts free with no limits and cuts the funnel with the ops bill."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Only founder time grows with users, so limit support time and leave features alone.","In the two-week pilot support is how we learn, and an unanswered stranger wastes it."],"flip":"If the first hundred users show heavy vaults driving import and data-loss tickets, (d) becomes worth using.","linked":["B10","B6"],"sources":["merged: B13","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:196","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:287","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:537","docs/BUSINESS.md:787","docs/CRITIQUE.md:963","docs/CRITIQUE.md:968","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:89","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:120","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r6-support-deflection.md:163","docs/CRITIQUE.md:966"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"What free support means comes with the paid tier.","recWhy":"The free tier is the whole funnel and, at scale, the largest consumer of founder time, which cannot be bought: a median free user costs ₹7.87 a month and 86 percent of that is founder time. Only founder time grows with users, so the bound goes on support and never on features, as Q13's uncapped free tier requires. A public forum is the shape that scales, because answers stay indexed and the queue never forms. The pilot is different: it is a study with ten named strangers, support is how we learn what breaks, and a stranger who goes quiet unanswered is indistinguishable from one who left, which wastes a tenth of the answer. India to US Pacific is 12.5 hours, so 48 is the honest number rather than 24. The expectation is withdrawn afterwards, and that is said at recruitment. Heavy vaults driving import and data-loss tickets in the first hundred users make (d) worth using."},{"id":"B8","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Communication","weight":"high","q":"How do we reach a user we hold no address for: a ledger in our `.frontmatter/` folder, a vault folder, or an email field at first run?","lede":"With no address, a breach notice, a price change and a failed renewal all have nowhere to go.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":"the user's repository, after frontmatter","lines":[{"t":".frontmatter/review.jsonl","mark":"keep","note":"the separate file, already agreed"},{"t":".frontmatter/usage.jsonl","mark":"keep","note":"opt-in counts, the usage-file decision"},{"t":".frontmatter/notices/2026-09-15-price.md","mark":"add","note":"one vendor directory, not two"},{"t":"Notices/2026-09-15-price.md","mark":"stop","note":"as BUSINESS.md specifies; breaks the one-separate-file rule"},{"t":"README.md","mark":"keep","note":"untouched"}],"note":"The current plan: the separate file is the only thing frontmatter adds, and it is not in the markdown.","caption":"Where a required notice can live without a second vendor folder"},"stakes":"Some notices are legal duties we cannot meet without a channel, and a vault folder breaks the byte-exact promise.","state":["BUSINESS.md writes obligatory messages into the vault under `Notices/`.","The operating rules keep notices in an in-product list.","First run collects nothing; email comes only on an obligation."],"tension":["A folder at vault root appears in git status, every diff and on GitHub.","The pilot's day-14 ask needs a channel the plan does not have.","An email field at first run is the change most likely to depress conversion."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three answers in three documents","cols":["Location","Where specified","Consistent with the one-review-file rule?"],"rows":[["`Notices/` at vault root, read-only markdown","BUSINESS.md:121","No: a second vendor directory in the user's repo"],["In-product list","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:536","Yes: writes nothing"],["`.frontmatter/` sidecar, plain text","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:508","Yes, if notices go inside the existing directory"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the ledger has to carry","items":[["Obligatory message classes","10","BUSINESS.md:25-34"],["Severities","2: must-read and for-the-record","BUSINESS.md:130"],["Users with no address at all","a supported, permanent state","BUSINESS.md:53"],["Breach intimation channel named by DPDP Rule 7(1)","\"through her user account or any mode of communication registered by her\", verbatim","BUSINESS.md:26, fetched 2026-08-30"],["When the DPDP substantive sections and penalties commence","2027-05-13; Section 2 and Sections 18 to 26 are live now","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The channel the pilot needs and does not have","items":[["Collected at first run","nothing","BUSINESS.md:48"],["Sending infrastructure in the governing document","zero substantive occurrences of SPF, DKIM, DMARC or unsubscribe across the 120,539-word PRD v2","BUSINESS.md:17, measured 2026-08-30"],["Explicit feedback field, internal measurement","7 filled of 694 opportunities = 1.0%","BUSINESS.md:281"],["Day-14 ask in the measurement decision","paste the usage file into a message, or say no","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21"],["Vendor cost if we do send","Resend Pro, $20/month at 24,145 messages","BUSINESS.md:108, 115"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Obligatory classes that need a channel before the first charge","cols":["Class","Deadline","Channel available today"],"rows":[["Breach intimation to each affected user","without delay (DPDP Rule 7(1))","none: no address, no ledger built"],["Annual renewal pre-notice, California","at least 15 and not more than 45 days before renewal","email only; none collected"],["Price or terms change","before the next charge, never after","none"],["Payment failure and dunning","see BUSINESS.md:142-148","email only; none collected"],["Team invite and account recovery","immediate","email only, by definition"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ledger in `.frontmatter/notices/`, no-email rule held, consent page for the ten","gains":["One vendor directory, and the notice is a plain file in the repo.","The day-14 ask becomes a commitment rather than a hope."],"costs":["Still lands in the user's git history.","Does not solve the breach dead end at scale."],"system":"A notices writer inside the existing `.frontmatter/` folder.","screens":"A status-line affordance; a notices list.","money":"None.","what":"A notices ledger at .frontmatter/notices/, the no-email rule held, and a consent page for the ten pilot users. One vendor directory and a plain file; it still lands in the user's git history."},{"k":"b","label":"`Notices/` at vault root, as BUSINESS.md specifies","gains":["Most visible to a user who never opens `.frontmatter/`."],"costs":["Vendor files at the top of someone's repository, against our own rule."],"system":"A second vendor directory.","screens":"A notices folder in the tree.","money":"None.","what":"A Notices folder at vault root, as BUSINESS.md specifies. The most visible to a user who never opens .frontmatter; vendor files at the top of someone's repository, against our own rule."},{"k":"c","label":"In-app list only, plus an optional email field at first run, purpose stated","gains":["Solves breach, renewal and price-change delivery at once."],"costs":["A field on the most protected screen, and decliners still get nothing."],"system":"An optional address; a sending vendor.","screens":"First run gains a field.","money":"Resend Pro, $20 a month at 24,145 messages.","what":"An in-app list plus an optional email field at first run, purpose stated. Solves breach, renewal and price-change delivery at once; a field on the most protected screen, decliners get nothing."},{"k":"d","label":"Require an email at first run","gains":["Every notice class has a channel from day one."],"costs":["Contradicts the category convention the plan cites as a kill condition."],"system":"Accounts before value.","screens":"A signup screen that does not exist.","money":"Unknown; the plan reads it as the largest.","what":"Require an email at first run. Every notice class has a channel from day one; it contradicts the category convention the plan cites as a kill condition."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["We already committed to one vendor directory, and notices inside it break none of our rules.","The pilot is a study with ten named participants, so a consent page secures the day-14 ask."],"flip":"Breach and price-change notices still have no channel under (a), so (c) is due before the first charge.","linked":["B10","B14"],"sources":["merged: B9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:430","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:508","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:536","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541","docs/BUSINESS.md:17","docs/BUSINESS.md:25","docs/BUSINESS.md:48","docs/BUSINESS.md:53","docs/BUSINESS.md:55","docs/BUSINESS.md:108","docs/BUSINESS.md:121","docs/BUSINESS.md:142","docs/BUSINESS.md:281","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Reaching users we hold no address for assumes users.","recWhy":"With no address, a breach notice, a price change and a failed renewal all have nowhere to go, and the pilot's day-14 ask needs a channel the plan does not have. BUSINESS.md writes obligatory messages into the vault under Notices, which puts vendor files at the top of someone's repository against our own rule; the in-app list reaches nobody who stopped opening the app. We already committed to one vendor directory, .frontmatter, and a plain file inside it breaks none of our rules while landing in git history. The pilot is a study with ten named participants, so a consent page at recruitment turns the day-14 ask into a commitment. Q3 opens the editor with no account, so an email field at first run is the change most likely to depress conversion. Breach and price-change notices still have no channel at scale under (a), so (c) is due before the first charge."},{"id":"B10","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Measurement","weight":"high","q":"Do we adopt the drafted local usage file as the exit measure, and interview every stranger whether or not they share it?","lede":"The pilot passes if six of ten strangers keep it, which we cannot observe unless the measure is designed in first.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Installed","meta":"no account, nothing sent","via":"day 0","tone":"neutral","current":true},{"label":"Daily use","meta":"usage.jsonl gains a line a day","via":"days 1-13","tone":"good"},{"label":"Day-14 ask","meta":"paste the file, or say no","via":"day 14","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Counted or a hole","meta":"a decline is a tenth of the answer","via":"after","tone":"warn"}],"note":"Counts only: days opened, documents opened, spans reviewed, proposals accepted (the week-0 drafts).","caption":"The only measurement path the operating rules allow"},"stakes":"Without it the week-12 go/no-go rests on recall, against a measured 1.0 per cent response rate.","state":["The draft puts counts in `.frontmatter/usage.jsonl`, editable and deletable.","The test is 6 of the last 10 working days used, plus one review action.","The instrument is now pwa's /pilot app, six tasks with a verdict each and offline feedback, rather than the drafted local file."],"tension":["A local file reaches us only if volunteered; one decline moves the result.","The interview gives the reason no count supplies, and costs ten conversations.","An opt-in upload keyed to an account makes the line personal data."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The drafted measure","items":[["What is counted","days opened, documents opened, spans reviewed, proposals accepted, questions answered","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19: counts only, no content"],["Where it lives","`.frontmatter/usage.jsonl` in the user's repo","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20"],["How it reaches us","it does not, by default; asked once on day 14","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21"],["The gate","6 of the last 10 working days plus one review action in week 2","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22"],["Never collected","session replay, keystrokes, content, file names, prompts, IP, device identifiers","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:23; matches PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:538"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each route can and cannot tell us","cols":["Route","Tells us","Cannot tell us"],"rows":[["Local file, pasted voluntarily","days opened and actions taken, exactly","why anyone stopped; anything about people who decline"],["Day-14 interview only","the reason, in their words","whether the reported usage happened"],["Opt-in upload from day one","the same counts, without an ask","nothing more; and once keyed to an account it is personal data, so the notice-and-consent duties come forward (WEEK0:24)"],["Nothing","none","the exit test cannot be run at all"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Adopt the draft as written: local file, one ask on day 14","gains":["Consistent with every operating rule and cheap to build."],"costs":["Anyone who declines is uncounted, and it says nothing about why."],"system":"A daily counts writer in `.frontmatter/`.","screens":"A settings line naming the file.","money":"None.","what":"Adopt the draft as written: a local usage file and one ask on day 14. Consistent with every operating rule and cheap; anyone who declines is uncounted, and it says nothing about why."},{"k":"b","label":"Adopt the draft and interview every stranger on day 14 regardless","gains":["All ten accounted for, shared file or not.","The reason is the input to re-cutting or killing it."],"costs":["Makes the pilot a two-week commitment, not a hand-off.","Only the founder can run it; it does not scale."],"system":"Same writer as (a).","screens":"Same as (a).","money":"Founder time, about a day across the fortnight.","what":"Adopt the draft and interview every stranger on day 14 regardless. All ten accounted for, shared file or not, and the reason is the input to re-cutting or killing it; only the founder can run it."},{"k":"c","label":"Self-report only. Skip the file","gains":["Nothing to build, and nothing lands in the user repository."],"costs":["Rests on recall against a 1.0 per cent baseline, defensible to nobody."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Self-report only and skip the file. Nothing to build and nothing lands in the repository; it rests on recall against a 1.0 percent baseline."},{"k":"d","label":"Opt-in upload, off by default, from day one","gains":["Clean counts with no day-14 ask, and it scales past ten."],"costs":["Keyed to an account the line is personal data, pulling consent forward."],"system":"An upload path and a consent record.","screens":"A consent toggle at first run.","money":"None material at pilot scale.","what":"Opt-in upload, off by default, from day one. Clean counts with no day-14 ask; keyed to an account the line is personal data, and consent moves forward."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["With ten users every refusal is a tenth of the answer, so collect count and reason.","It is the cheapest version that produces a defensible go/no-go."],"flip":"At 100 users interviewing everyone is impossible and (d) becomes the design, consent machinery first.","linked":["AC17","B6","B7","B8","PL5"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:324","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:351","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:417","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:538","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:28","docs/BUSINESS.md:281","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:19","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The exit measure belongs with the pilot.","recWhy":"The pilot passes if six of ten strangers keep using it, which cannot be observed unless the measure is designed in first, and a local file reaches us only if volunteered, so one decline moves the result by a tenth. The instrument has since become pwa's /pilot app, six tasks with a verdict each and offline feedback, and AC17 settles where the count lives per surface. What no count supplies is the reason, and with ten people the reason is the input to re-cutting or killing the product. Q15 sets the pass at three of ten naming the problem unprompted, two still using at day 30, and a booked call from someone who was not asked, so the day-30 call is already in the design and the interview folds into it. Booking it at recruitment turns a one percent ask into an appointment. At a hundred users interviewing everyone is impossible and (d) becomes the design, consent first.","dupes":{"dropped":["L18"],"areas":["Legal, privacy & data"],"why":"Legal, privacy & data asked only which instrument ships and recommended the local file as drafted, pasted once by each person, option a here; it did not take up interviewing everyone. Its rotating install id stays a named non-option. Where the file can live is an Access, offline & install card.","agree":false}},{"id":"B12","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Distribution","weight":"high","q":"Do we plan against ₹20 lakh a month and 1.26 million cumulative visitors, or replace the milestone with default-alive?","lede":"The channel audit sums every available channel at a third of the traffic the milestone needs.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Cumulative visitors needed for ₹20L a month","n":1260000,"unit":"visitors","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Free signups implied at 9%","n":113507,"unit":"signups","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Paying users implied","n":6689,"unit":"paying","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Paying users for default-alive, one founder","n":294,"unit":"paying","tone":"good"}],"note":"Everything on the channel table reaches perhaps 350k to 500k year-one visitors (BUSINESS.md). Marketing posts ever shipped: 2.","caption":"The milestone against the only threshold that changes anything"},"stakes":"If the target cannot be reached, every honest report will read as failure.","state":["The requirement is about 1,260,000 cumulative visitors.","The same work sums the channels at 350k to 500k year one.","The current plan carries neither figure; marketing posts ever shipped: 2."],"tension":["Default-alive at one founder is 294 paying users, about 100,428 cumulative visitors.","Search traffic to published pages is about zero by choice, under noindex.","Holding ₹20 lakh at 6 per cent monthly churn takes about 89,000 visitors a month."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Cumulative visitors: what is needed against what the channels can reach","note":"Requirement from BUSINESS.md:941 and CRITIQUE.md:1459; channel capacity from BUSINESS.md:1086, summed at the optimistic end across organic, the validator, content, referral and launch spikes.","data":[["Default-alive, one founder",100428],["Default-alive, two founders",171200],["Optimistic year-one channel sum, low",350000],["Optimistic year-one channel sum, high",500000],["₹20 lakh a month",1260000]],"unit":"cumulative visitors","threshold":500000,"thresholdLabel":"the most every measured channel can reach in year one"},{"type":"stat","title":"What holding a stock costs, forever","items":[["Replacement visitors per month at 2% churn","~29,700","BUSINESS.md:249"],["At 4%","~59,500","BUSINESS.md:250"],["At 6%","~89,200","BUSINESS.md:251"],["Marketing posts ever shipped","2","BUSINESS.md:940"],["Published-page organic under the noindex default","about zero, by choice","BUSINESS.md:1080"],["ChartMogul band for accounts under $50/month","top-quartile annual gross retention 60 to 70%; 23% for AI-native","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete ₹20 lakh a month. Publish default-alive as the only target","gains":["Every channel in the audit reaches 4,185 visitors a month.","Makes an honest weekly report readable as progress."],"costs":["Removes the number that makes this look worth years.","A small target is harder to raise against."],"system":"No change.","screens":"The milestone table is rewritten.","money":"Target falls from 6,689 paying users to 294.","what":"Delete ₹20 lakh a month; publish default-alive, 294 paying users, as the only target. Every channel in the audit reaches its 4,185 visitors a month; the number that made this look big goes."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep ₹20 lakh as a three-year figure, restated with the churn replacement flow","gains":["Honest about the flow the original figure omitted."],"costs":["Still 12× default-alive, and reads as failure for three years."],"system":"No change.","screens":"The milestone table gains a churn column.","money":"About 89,000 replacement visitors a month.","what":"Keep ₹20 lakh as a three-year figure, restated with the churn replacement flow. Honest about the flow the original omitted; still twelve times default-alive, and reads as failure for three years."},{"k":"c","label":"Size the developer-tool distribution channel before publishing any target","gains":["The only candidate whose ceiling is the right order of magnitude."],"costs":["Reaches developers, the worst-converting audience, and may lower the blended rate."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None until sized.","money":"Unknown until measured, which is the point.","what":"Size the developer-tool distribution channel before publishing any target. The only candidate of the right order of magnitude; it reaches developers, the worst-converting audience."},{"k":"d","label":"Publish no traffic target until the two-week tests return","gains":["Avoids committing before the product is confirmed."],"costs":["No way to say whether any channel works; the question returns."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish no traffic target until the two-week tests return. Avoids committing before the product is confirmed; no way to say whether any channel works, and the question returns."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["No channel mix reaches it, so every honest report would read as failure.","Default-alive is 4,185 visitors a month, and hitting it means the founder is paid."],"flip":"If the developer-tool channel is sized and comes back large, ₹20 lakh becomes arguable.","linked":["B2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/BUSINESS.md:164","docs/BUSINESS.md:243","docs/BUSINESS.md:249","docs/BUSINESS.md:253","docs/BUSINESS.md:940","docs/BUSINESS.md:941","docs/BUSINESS.md:952","docs/BUSINESS.md:1080","docs/BUSINESS.md:1084","docs/BUSINESS.md:1086","docs/BUSINESS.md:1088","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1459"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Traffic milestones belong with the launch plan.","recWhy":"The ₹20 lakh a month milestone needs about 1,260,000 cumulative visitors, and the channel audit sums every available channel at 350,000 to 500,000 in year one, a third of it. Holding the figure at 6 percent monthly churn takes about 89,000 visitors a month. Every honest weekly report against that number would read as failure. Default-alive at one founder is 294 paying users, about 100,428 cumulative visitors or 4,185 a month, which every channel in the audit can reach, and hitting it means the founder is paid. Q13's bands and MK16's denominator change move the arithmetic upward later, and the target can move with evidence; a number no channel mix reaches cannot. A small target is harder to raise against, and B2 already declines the raise. A developer-tool channel sized and found large makes ₹20 lakh arguable, with a date."},{"id":"B21","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Endgame","weight":"high","q":"What are we building toward: an independent business we keep, or a team and a codebase somebody buys?","lede":"Acquisition is the most common ending among the dead products studied, and the plan never names the ending it wants.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2014-05-30","label":"Editorially closes","note":"\"Even if all of our users paid up, it wouldn't be enough.\"","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2016-03-01","label":"Poetica bought by Condé Nast","note":"dead by 2016-06-01; the goals were at odds","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2020-03-03","label":"Atrium shuts after $75.5M","note":"raised, scaled, closed","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2025-01-31","label":"Almanac shuts after $45M","note":"cause stated as capacity, not demand","tone":"stop"},{"when":"today","label":"Obsidian: 8 staff, no investors","note":"per-seat licence made optional 2025-02-20","tone":"good"}],"note":"Four of the eight dead products in this set were bought as teams by organisations that already owned a publishing pipeline.","caption":"How this category ends, and the one that did not"},"stakes":"The ending decides the price, the ownership, the not-building list and whether the format is open.","state":["The plan has no exit section; the analysis stops at the market.","The decisions document refuses a raise: 367 months against a seven-year horizon.","The product-family section already sells services around the product."],"tension":["An acquirer with a publishing pipeline wants the engine and team, not users.","Building to be bought argues for a closed review file format; building to keep, the opposite.","Obsidian is the counter-case: 8 staff, no investors, licence made optional."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Independent, permanently. Write it down, with the separate-file format published","gains":["Matches the architecture already chosen: exit is free."],"costs":["Rules out acquisition, the most common ending, and opens the format a buyer would want."],"system":"The review file schema is published and versioned.","screens":"A format page in the docs.","money":"No acquisition premium, ever.","what":"Independent, permanently, written down, with the separate-file review format published. Matches the architecture already chosen, where exit is free; it rules out acquisition, the most common ending."},{"k":"b","label":"Build to be acquired by a docs platform, CMS or agent vendor","gains":["An acquirer with a pipeline gets distribution we cannot buy."],"costs":["Four of eight dead products were bought and the software went too."],"system":"Proprietary review file; no open format.","screens":"None.","money":"One-time, unpredictable, and it ends the product.","what":"Build to be acquired by a docs platform, CMS or agent vendor. An acquirer with a pipeline gets distribution we cannot buy; four of eight dead products were bought and the software went too."},{"k":"c","label":"Independent by default, with a written price at which we would sell","gains":["Keeps the open-format promise, which is the differentiator.","Removes the question from every future cash scare."],"costs":["A written number can leak or age badly.","Decided without ever having met an acquirer."],"system":"Review file format published; nothing else changes.","screens":"A format page.","money":"None today.","what":"Independent by default, with a written price at which we would sell. Keeps the open-format promise and removes the question from every future cash scare; a number that can leak or age badly."},{"k":"d","label":"Leave it open","gains":["Costs nothing today, and no position to defend."],"costs":["The format decision defaults to closed, and the question returns under pressure."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it open. Costs nothing today; the format decision defaults to closed, and the question returns under pressure."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The architecture already chose independence: files stay the user's and exit is free.","A written price costs a paragraph and stops the question returning at every cash scare."],"flip":"An approach from an acquirer that would keep the users and the format, which none of the four got.","linked":["B12","B2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:482","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:557","docs/DECIDE.md:150","docs/CRITIQUE.md:1766"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"What we are building toward is worth asking once the pilot reports.","recWhy":"Acquisition is the most common ending among the dead products studied, four of eight were bought and the software went with them, and the plan never names the ending it wants. The architecture has already chosen: files stay the user's, the review file is plain text readable without us, and exit is free, which is the differentiator Q9 and Q11 rest on. Building to be bought argues for a closed format, so leaving the question open lets the format default to closed under pressure. A written price costs a paragraph and stops the question returning at every cash scare, a recurring event for a services-funded company. The caveats are on the card: the number can leak or age, and it is decided without ever having met an acquirer. An approach that would keep the users and the format, which none of the four got, is the one thing that reopens it."},{"id":"B18","cat":"Business & operations","sub":"Continuity","weight":"medium","q":"Do we run a five-day laptop-off drill before the first paying user, or accept bus factor zero?","lede":"One person carries the funding, the calendar, the support queue and a six-hour clock. No absence has been tested.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Funding","items":["Client retainers carry their own SLA"],"side":"0 responders","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Statutory","items":["CERT-In 6 hours","GDPR Art. 33 at 72 hours"],"side":"not recoverable","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Billing","items":["One debit attempt, no retry","About 8 involuntary churns in 21 days"],"side":"manual, after the fact","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Support","items":["103 to 144 tickets queue"],"side":"weeks to clear","tone":"warn"}],"note":"Figures for a 21-day absence at default-alive (the capacity research).","caption":"Four layers, one person, no cover on any of them"},"stakes":"An absence hits the funding, the calendar, the support promise and the statutory clock at once.","state":["The risk register lists ten risks; founder unavailability is not one.","The operating rules name no second responder.","The capacity research proposed the drill and it has never been scheduled."],"tension":["Indian card mandates get one attempt, so a failed renewal is terminal.","Before the pilot almost nothing runs, so a drill would test very little.","Five working days is a real cost against a schedule that has few."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What a 21-day absence costs at default-alive (r26-c9-capacity.md:149-156)","cols":["Domain","Consequence","Recovery"],"rows":[["Support","103 to 144 tickets queue against a 24-hour email expectation","weeks, arriving at a founder already behind"],["On-call","zero responders; Better Stack's free tier covers one","the page is simply not answered"],["Billing","147 monthly renewals × 8% single-attempt failure ≈ 8 involuntary churns in 21 days","manual, per user, after the fact"],["Statutory","CERT-In 6 hours; DPDP Section 8(6); GDPR Art. 33 at 72 hours","not recoverable: a missed clock is a violation"],["Roadmap","19.3 points = 9.8% of the 196-point plan","pushes every downstream date"],["The nut","client retainers carry their own SLA","the retainer is 100% of the funding"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Why the payment processor makes it worse","items":[["Indian card e-mandate attempts per cycle","exactly one; no smart retries, no cascade","BUSINESS.md:140, 205"],["Assumed single-attempt failure rate","8%","BUSINESS.md:771"],["Pre-debit notice","sent by the issuer 24 hours ahead, in the bank's own wording, with a cancel affordance","BUSINESS.md:203"],["Dunning ladder length","T+0, T+3, T+7, T+10, terminal","BUSINESS.md:142-148"],["Founder-hours per paying user per month","0.1445","BUSINESS.md:779"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Run the five-day drill before the pilot starts","gains":["Cheapest possible moment, because nothing much can break."],"costs":["Tests almost nothing: no renewals, no queue, no incident surface."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Five working days.","what":"Run the five-day laptop-off drill before the pilot starts. The cheapest moment, because nothing much can break; it tests almost nothing, no renewals, no queue, no incident surface."},{"k":"b","label":"Run it before the first paying user","gains":["Tests the systems that matter, while breaking them is cheap.","Converts an argument into a measurement."],"costs":["Five working days from a schedule that has few.","Some things break in front of real users."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Five working days, plus what it surfaces.","what":"Run it before the first paying user. Tests the systems that matter while breaking them is cheap, and converts an argument into a measurement; five working days from a schedule that has few."},{"k":"c","label":"Write a continuity runbook without running a drill","gains":["An afternoon, and it captures the obvious items."],"costs":["Nobody has run it, so it records intent, not tested steps."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Half a day.","what":"Write a continuity runbook without running a drill. An afternoon, and it captures the obvious items; nobody has run it, so it records intent rather than tested steps."},{"k":"d","label":"Accept it. One founder, no drill, no runbook","gains":["Free until an absence happens, and five days stay in the schedule."],"costs":["Every compliance clock runs on, and the retainer SLA has no cover."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None until an absence.","what":"Accept bus factor zero: one founder, no drill, no runbook. Free until an absence happens; every compliance clock runs on and the retainer SLA has no cover."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Before the first paying user, a five-day outage is still a test, not an incident.","Write down what broke, and use that list as the runbook instead of guesses."],"flip":"If the founder count resolves to two people, the drill still runs but its result changes meaning.","linked":["B1","B14"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:319","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:533","docs/BUSINESS.md:140","docs/BUSINESS.md:142","docs/BUSINESS.md:203","docs/BUSINESS.md:205","docs/BUSINESS.md:771","docs/BUSINESS.md:779","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c9-capacity.md:149","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c9-capacity.md:162","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r26-c9-capacity.md:181"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A continuity drill is due before the first paying user.","recWhy":"One person carries the funding, the calendar, the support queue and a six-hour statutory clock, the risk register's ten rows do not include founder unavailability, and no absence has ever been tested. Before the pilot almost nothing runs, so a drill then would test very little. Before the first paying user, renewals, the queue and the incident surface exist and breaking them is still cheap, because a five-day outage is a test rather than an incident until someone has paid. Indian card mandates get one attempt, so a failed renewal during a real absence is terminal. Write down what broke and use that list as the runbook. B1 decides whether there is a second person at all; if the count resolves to two, the drill still runs and its result changes meaning. PR4's page sets when the first paying user arrives, so the drill gets a date the moment that does."},{"id":"L1","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Data holding","weight":"critical","q":"Do we delete the Firebase document-holding design, or adopt it and take the fiduciary and intermediary duty?","lede":"Three committed files give three answers, and every legal rule in the plan follows from this one.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Delete the design","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Adopt as written","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Keep, drop public read","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"What we hold","cells":["identity, teams, billing","note bodies and revisions","note bodies and revisions"]},{"label":"Our status","cells":["no duty over documents","fiduciary and intermediary","fiduciary only"]},{"label":"Ceiling on content","cells":["none, no content held","₹250 crore, DPDP section 8(5)","₹250 crore, DPDP section 8(5)"]},{"label":"Publishing apparatus","cells":["none","74 founder-hours","none"]},{"label":"Engineering cost today","cells":["zero, nothing writes to it","build the store","build the store"]},{"label":"Forecloses","cells":["hosted viewer, server search","nothing","public share links"]}],"note":"Ceiling from the DPDP Schedule; 74 hours itemised at PRD v2 line 4194","caption":"What each answer holds, and what duty comes with it"},"stakes":"Adopting puts document content under a ₹250 crore ceiling and makes us an intermediary before the first user.","state":["Firestore rules cap bodies at 900,000 chars, make revisions undeletable and allow anonymous reads.","The old PRD says we hold documents; the plan says we hold no bytes.","Nothing writes to Firestore; its rules call themselves a prototype."],"tension":["Holding bytes makes us an intermediary under IT Act section 2(1)(w), which costs 74 founder-hours.","The claim a rival cannot copy is that documents never reach us."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Defer past the pilot","gains":["Costs nothing this week"],"costs":["A pilot with no published policy breaches SPDI Rule 4 today"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer past the pilot. Costs nothing this week; a pilot with no published policy breaches SPDI Rule 4 today, and every other legal rule waits on this one."},{"k":"b","label":"Delete `firestore.rules`; make zero document bytes one of the plan's operating rules","gains":["Legal surface collapses to identity and billing","Zero Firestore writes exist, so no engineering cost"],"costs":["Forecloses hosted publishing, server search and a hosted viewer"],"system":"Firestore schema removed; no-bytes rule written down.","screens":"Kills the hosted-viewer screen.","money":"None.","what":"Delete firestore.rules and make zero document bytes an operating rule. The legal surface collapses to identity and billing at no cost; hosted publishing and server search are foreclosed."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the Firebase design as written","gains":["Hosted sync, server search and publishing become possible"],"costs":["Document content falls under DPDP section 8(5), ceiling ₹250 crore","Intermediary status and 74 founder-hours before the first stranger"],"system":"Firestore becomes the store of record.","screens":"Share manager, revisions, moderation console.","money":"Storage, egress, counsel.","what":"Adopt the Firebase design as written. Hosted sync, search and publishing become possible; content falls under DPDP section 8(5) ceiling ₹250 crore, and intermediary status costs 74 founder-hours."},{"k":"d","label":"Hold documents server-side but delete the unauthenticated public-read path","gains":["Intermediary duty mostly falls away","Hosted sync and server search stay available"],"costs":["Fiduciary duty and the ₹250 crore ceiling stay","Still needs processor agreements and an Indian log store"],"system":"One rule deleted; the duties remain.","screens":"Hosted viewer stays behind sign-in.","money":"Storage plus processor agreements.","what":"Hold documents server-side but delete the unauthenticated public-read path. Intermediary duty mostly falls away and hosted sync stays; the fiduciary duty and the ₹250 crore ceiling remain."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Nothing writes to Firestore across 226 source files, so deleting the design costs no engineering.","The cost model and the strongest marketing claim both rest on holding no bytes."],"flip":"If sharing lands on a hosted viewer, (c) is the honest minimum and ₹250 crore must be budgeted.","linked":["F19","L10","L16","L6","P13","PL12"],"sources":["firestore.rules:7","firestore.rules:318","firestore.rules:361","firestore.rules:393","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4204","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4295","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:367","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:142","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9056","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:10100","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:10104","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:531"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Holding document bytes is the first of the five decisions. Everything about storage hangs off it.","evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the three committed files actually say","note":"A Firestore handle exists at `src/shared/infrastructure/firebase/client.ts:38`, but no write call does. The rules are a design that shipped without the code behind it, and the design is the thing being decided.","cols":["File","What it says","Where"],"rows":[["`firestore.rules`","Caps a note body at 900,000 characters and refuses every revision delete","`firestore.rules:318`, `firestore.rules:361`"],["`firestore.rules`","Allows an unauthenticated read of any share marked public. Its own comment calls that the product feature","`firestore.rules:393`"],["`firestore.rules`, first line","Calls the whole file a prototype","`firestore.rules:7`"],["`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md`","The control plane holds identity, team membership, entitlements and billing, and no document bytes","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133`"],["`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md`","Carries the same question as open decision 7, still open","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365`"],["`docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md`","Records the contradiction as gap 5","`docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:142`"]]}],"recWhy":"Three committed files give three answers: the Firestore rules cap bodies at 900,000 characters, make revisions undeletable and allow anonymous reads; the old PRD says we hold documents; the plan says we hold no bytes. Nothing writes to Firestore across 226 source files and the rules call themselves a prototype, so deleting the design costs no engineering. Holding bytes makes us an intermediary under IT Act section 2(1)(w), 74 founder-hours before the first stranger, and puts document content under DPDP's ₹250 crore ceiling; holding none collapses the legal surface to identity and billing. The cost model and the strongest marketing claim, that documents never reach us, both rest on the same choice. Q9 carries this, with AC1 and AC14 as the code changes that make it true. If sharing ever lands on a hosted viewer, (c) is the honest minimum and ₹250 crore is budgeted.","dupes":{"dropped":["B16","P8"],"areas":["Business & operations","Product & definition"],"why":"Business & operations and Product & definition both recommended deleting the document-holding design, option b here. Business & operations would decide publishing separately, after the sharing decision; Product & definition would also delete the public route. Its idea of a permalink that renders a public GitHub URL is an option on the Features share-page card.","agree":true}},{"id":"L3","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Live exposure","weight":"critical","q":"Do we treat every legal clock as already running, or as starting at the pilot?","lede":"sgnk-md is the same codebase and is listed as production. If it has users, the clocks started.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"sgnk-md listed as production","note":"202 of 228 files shared","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Google sign-in, not allowlisted","note":"collects email at first run","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Has any non-founder signed in?","note":"nobody has asked","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Yes: policy and officer overdue","note":"due at the first signup","tone":"stop"},{"label":"No: the pilot is the trigger","note":"every deadline in the plan's operating rules stands","tone":"good"}],"perRow":3,"note":"GitHub sign-in is allowlisted at auth-options.ts line 80; Google sign-in is not","caption":"One unanswered question re-dates four other decisions"},"stakes":"If yes, the published policy, the consent step and the named officer were all due months ago.","state":["202 of 228 source files are byte-identical with sgnk-md, none unique to it.","The portfolio lists sgnk-md as production; the gap register asks for its user list.","The plan says zero users. Both cannot be true."],"tension":["The sign-in allowlist is evidence about today's code, not the July deployment.","Google sign-in is not allowlisted and takes email at first run."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Ask the founders first, then act on the answer in the same week","what":"One question: has any non-founder ever signed in, and is there a list?","gains":["Five minutes, and it settles which clocks are running","That list is the only interview pool the product has"],"costs":["A verbal answer is not a deployment log"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Assume live: file the contact, publish the policy and grievance page this week","gains":["No downside if the assumption is wrong"],"costs":["About a day of work brought forward","A policy written before the Firebase and byte-path decisions may need rewriting"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Adds /privacy, /terms, /grievance.","money":"Founder time.","what":"Assume live: file the point of contact, publish the policy and grievance page this week. No downside if the assumption is wrong; a day of work brought forward, possibly rewritten later."},{"k":"c","label":"Take the sgnk-md deployment offline until the question is settled","gains":["Removes any live exposure immediately"],"costs":["Kills the only real user population, and the interview pool"],"system":"One deployment paused.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Take the sgnk-md deployment offline until the question is settled. Removes any live exposure immediately; it kills the only real user population and the interview pool."},{"k":"d","label":"Assume dormant because the GitHub allowlist gates sign-in","gains":["Every legal deadline stands as written"],"costs":["Rests on one code path, as of now","Says nothing about Google sign-in, which is ungated"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Assume dormant because the GitHub allowlist gates sign-in. Every legal deadline stands as written; it rests on one code path as of now, and Google sign-in is ungated and takes an email."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["It is the cheapest question, and it moves the breach, policy and retention deadlines.","It needs a founder, not research: is there a user list, and may ten of them be contacted."],"flip":"A deployment log showing non-founder sign-ins makes (b) mandatory rather than prudent.","linked":["L1","L17","L4","L7"],"sources":["docs/DECIDE.md:168","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:36","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:558","src/modules/auth/infrastructure/auth-options.ts:80","src/modules/auth/infrastructure/firebase-auth-gateway.ts:30","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:531"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"If we hold kits, the clocks start at the first kit, not at the pilot.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The fork, counted","note":"If sgnk-md has users, they are the only real users this product has ever had, and the clocks in every other legal card started when they arrived.","items":[["Source files byte-identical with sgnk-md","202 of 228","`docs/DECIDE.md:168`"],["Files that exist only in sgnk-md","0","`docs/DECIDE.md:168`"],["Editor files byte-identical with sgnk-md","41 of 43","`docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141`"],["What the portfolio calls sgnk-md","production","`docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:36`"],["What the plan says our user count is","zero","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:558`"],["User interviews across the whole programme","zero","`docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35`"]]}],"recWhy":"sgnk-md is the same codebase, 202 of 228 source files byte-identical and none unique to it, and the portfolio lists it as production while the plan says zero users. Both cannot be true, and if it has users the breach, policy and retention clocks started in July. The sign-in allowlist is evidence about today's code, not the July deployment, and Google sign-in is not allowlisted and takes an email at first run. One question to the founders, has any non-founder ever signed in, is five minutes and moves every deadline in this area. It is also the question G16 asks for a different reason, because that list is the only interview pool the product has. A verbal answer is not a deployment log, so the answer is acted on the same week, with B14 and L7 already doing most of what (b) would do regardless. A deployment log showing non-founder sign-ins makes (b) mandatory rather than prudent."},{"id":"L4","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Breach response","weight":"critical","q":"Who answers the six-hour clock, and do we file the CERT-In point of contact now?","lede":"The six-hour clock has no size floor, and one person covers it at 1.21 engineering days a week.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":180,"cols":[{"title":"File, runbook, best effort","tone":"warn"},{"title":"File, then shrink","tone":"accent"},{"title":"File and pay a rota","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Who answers at 03:00","cells":["one founder, if awake","one founder","a paid second responder"]},{"label":"Reportable surface","cells":["25 routes, five AI vendors","identity and billing only","unchanged"]},{"label":"Detection today","cells":["none in package.json","little left to detect","alerting must be bought"]},{"label":"Six-hour clock","cells":["best effort, stated","rarely fires","genuinely covered"]},{"label":"Cost","cells":["one day","forecloses the web trial","money and a second credential holder"]}],"note":"Capacity from PRODUCT-BRIEF line 11; the clock from line 543","caption":"Three postures against a clock one person cannot staff"},"stakes":"A reportable incident with no filed contact, no runbook and no logs is what a buyer finds first.","state":["The Annexure II point-of-contact filing is recorded as missing everywhere.","One person made 80 of 80 commits.","No runbook, roster or pager, and `package.json` carries no Sentry, PostHog or logging."],"tension":["The clock starts on noticing, so detection gates everything, and there is no detection.","IT Act section 70B(7) carries one year or ₹1 lakh; 70B(8) needs an officer's complaint."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"File nothing and rely on section 70B(8) needing a CERT-In officer's complaint","gains":["Costs nothing"],"costs":["Fails the first security questionnaire and any insurer's form"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"File nothing and rely on section 70B(8) needing a CERT-In officer's complaint. Costs nothing; fails the first security questionnaire and any insurer's form."},{"k":"b","label":"File the point of contact now, write the runbook, and state a best-effort clock","gains":["About a day of work, and honest on its face"],"costs":["A solo founder cannot guarantee six hours","Leaves the whole server-side surface reportable"],"system":"Alerting on the deploy; runbook in the repo.","screens":"None.","money":"Free-tier alerting; founder time.","what":"File the point of contact now, write the runbook, and state a best-effort clock. About a day of work and honest; one person cannot guarantee six hours, and the whole server surface stays reportable."},{"k":"c","label":"File now, then shrink the surface until a reportable incident is near impossible","what":"No server-side documents, publishing or hosted AI, so a breach exposes identity and billing only.","gains":["Cuts the reportable surface to its smallest set","Costs nothing extra: the Firebase and byte-path decisions point here"],"costs":["Forecloses the web trial and hosted publishing","Removes the likely trigger, not the duty"],"system":"Same change as the Firebase and byte-path decisions.","screens":"Removes share and hosted-AI surfaces.","money":"Forfeits any hosted tier."},{"k":"d","label":"File now and pay a second responder onto a rota before the pilot","gains":["Real coverage of a clock we otherwise cannot meet"],"costs":["A second holder of production credentials","Widens the access-revocation runbook"],"system":"Credential issue and revocation runbook.","screens":"None.","money":"A retainer before revenue.","what":"File now and pay a second responder onto a rota before the pilot. Real coverage of a clock we otherwise cannot meet; a second holder of production credentials and a wider revocation runbook."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["One person cannot staff a six-hour clock, so the lasting answer is holding less.","Filing is an hour and is in three of four options, so the choice is what we hold."],"flip":"A paying team requiring hosted sync reopens the surface, and the team tier price has to carry it.","linked":["B14","L1","L14"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:11","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:12","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:543","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3697","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4105","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4122","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4198","docs/BUSINESS.md:25","docs/BUSINESS.md:767","package.json","merged: L5"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A six hour breach clock needs a named person before the first kit is stored.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The clock, and the person on it","note":"A six hour clock with no size floor needs somebody awake to notice. One person at 1.21 days a week is the whole roster.","items":[["Report a listed incident to CERT-In, from noticing","6 hours","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4122`"],["Keep ICT logs, inside Indian jurisdiction","rolling 180 days","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4122`"],["Acknowledge a grievance, every day, permanently","24 hours","`docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4198`"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week","1.21","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:11`"],["Commits by a single author","80 of 80","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:12`"]]}],"recWhy":"The six-hour reporting clock has no size floor, one person made 80 of 80 commits, and there is no runbook, roster, pager or logging dependency in package.json, so the clock starts on noticing and nothing notices. Filing the point of contact is an hour and appears in three of the four options, so the real choice is what we hold. One person cannot staff a six-hour clock, and paying a second responder onto a rota creates a second holder of production credentials before there is a product. The lasting answer is to hold less, so that a reportable incident is near impossible. That is what Q9 does: no server-side documents, no hosted publishing, no hosted AI, and the GitHub App with three permissions. The cost is that the web trial and hosted publishing stay closed. A paying team requiring hosted sync reopens the surface, and Q13's team price has to carry the responder that comes with it."},{"id":"L6","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Publishing","weight":"critical","q":"Do we close publishing to founder-only, and adopt noindex and system-assigned slugs as a standing rule?","lede":"A crawlable public page with user-chosen slugs makes us an intermediary, and none of the apparatus exists.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":180,"cols":[{"title":"Closed to founder","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Narrow shape","tone":"warn"},{"title":"As shipped today","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"Who can publish","cells":["nobody but us","paying accounts","any signed-in user"]},{"label":"Indexable","cells":["not served","noindex, nofollow ugc","index true, follow true"]},{"label":"Slug","cells":["not applicable","system-assigned","user-chosen, 1 to 60 chars"]},{"label":"Grievance clock","cells":["none","24-hour ack, 15-day resolve","24-hour ack, 15-day resolve"]},{"label":"Preservation store","cells":["none","180 days","180 days, unbuilt"]},{"label":"Founder-hours due first","cells":["0","74","0, and none of it exists"]}],"note":"Controls from RECORD line 4181; the narrow shape from PRD v2 line 4204","caption":"The publishing surface: who reaches it, and what it drags in"},"stakes":"Open it as configured and we take a permanent 24-hour grievance clock and conditions we cannot meet.","state":["Public pages set index true, and robots.ts allows crawlers at `/`.","Users choose slugs, and any signed-in user can publish.","Outbound links get `noopener noreferrer`, not `nofollow ugc`."],"tension":["Section 79 safe harbour is conditional; losing it makes the founder a co-defendant.","74 founder-hours is seven and a half weeks, and the plan never schedules it."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Delete the share module","gains":["Removes the exposure permanently"],"costs":["Forecloses the hosted viewer, which is still undecided","Throws away code that mostly works"],"system":"Share module removed; slug list retired.","screens":"Kills the public note page.","money":"None.","what":"Delete the share module. Removes the exposure permanently; it forecloses the hosted viewer, still undecided, and throws away code that mostly works."},{"k":"b","label":"Open it under PRD v2's narrow shape and build the 74-hour apparatus first","gains":["Cuts the abuse surface sharply and keeps the feature","Produces the artefacts a B2B buyer asks for"],"costs":["74 founder-hours does not fit before the pilot","The 24-hour clock then runs permanently"],"system":"Report form, ticket store, preservation store, log.","screens":"Report page, grievance page, moderation console.","money":"Seven and a half weeks of founder time.","what":"Open publishing under PRD v2's narrow shape and build the 74-hour takedown apparatus first. Cuts the abuse surface and produces what a B2B buyer asks for; 74 founder-hours do not fit before the pilot."},{"k":"c","label":"Open it as configured today and keep the indexable pages for distribution","gains":["Keeps the organic channel argument alive"],"costs":["The shape the abuse research ranked first, with no takedown path","Cloudflare terminates accounts for deceptive user-chosen names"],"system":"No change, which is the problem.","screens":"None.","money":"None, until a takedown arrives.","what":"Open it as configured today and keep the indexable pages for distribution. Keeps the organic argument alive; the shape the abuse research ranked first, with no takedown path."},{"k":"d","label":"Gate publishing to founder-only, with noindex and system slugs as a standing rule","gains":["One line of gating removes intermediary exposure","Buys back roughly seven weeks of apparatus work"],"costs":["Loses whatever distribution a public page brings"],"system":"One route gate, plus a rendering rule.","screens":"Public note page unreachable.","money":"None.","what":"Gate publishing to the founder only, with noindex and system slugs as the rule. One line of gating removes intermediary exposure and buys back seven weeks; a public page's distribution is lost."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Publishing is not on the pilot's critical path, and the twelve-week plan omits it.","It is the one feature that makes us an intermediary on a permanent 24-hour clock."],"flip":"If the hosted viewer becomes what Pro sells, the 74 hours enter the schedule first.","linked":["G12","L1","L16","L7"],"sources":["src/app/(public)/[slug]/page.tsx:30","src/app/robots.ts:18","src/app/api/share/route.ts:25","src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:4","src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:26","src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:43","src/modules/preview/presentation/markdown/html-policy.ts:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:43","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:367","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4204","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4105","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4115","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4181","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4187","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9392","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:9403","docs/BUSINESS.md:1046","merged: L9","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:332","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:520"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Unlisted kit links need the indexing rule settled, and the public route is indexable today.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the share surface does today, against the narrow shape the PRD asks for","note":"The apparatus the wide shape needs is costed at 74 one-time founder hours, arithmetic shown, at `docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194`.","cols":["What it does today","Where","What PRD v2 asks for instead"],"rows":[["Published pages set `index: true, follow: true`","`src/app/(public)/[slug]/page.tsx:30`","`noindex` by default"],["`robots.ts` allows crawlers at `/`","`src/app/robots.ts:18`","The same rule applied to published pages"],["The slug is user-chosen, up to 60 characters","`src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:4`, `src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:26`","System-assigned slugs"],["Any signed-in actor may publish; the only gate is authentication","`src/app/api/share/route.ts:31`","Paying accounts only, `docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4204`"],["Outbound links get `noopener noreferrer`","`src/modules/preview/presentation/markdown/html-policy.ts:214`","SEO spam is the named first risk, `docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4181`"]]}],"recWhy":"Public pages set index true, robots.ts allows crawlers at /, any signed-in user can publish under a slug they choose, and outbound links carry noopener noreferrer rather than nofollow ugc. That is the shape the abuse research ranked first, and none of the apparatus that makes it survivable exists. A crawlable page with user-chosen slugs makes us an intermediary on a permanent 24-hour clock, section 79 safe harbour is conditional, and losing it makes the founder a co-defendant. The apparatus is 74 founder-hours, seven and a half weeks the plan never schedules. Publishing is not on the pilot's critical path and the twelve-week plan omits it, so one line of gating removes the exposure and the seven weeks at once. Q11 carries this exactly. If the hosted viewer becomes what Pro sells, the 74 hours enter the schedule first."},{"id":"L7","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Policy pages","weight":"high","q":"Do we publish a privacy policy, terms and a named grievance officer before the pilot, and which window?","lede":"SPDI Rule 4 requires a published policy today. The app has four pages and none is a policy.","visual":{"kind":"screen","title":"Pages served on the live app","panes":[{"label":"Exists today","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"/login","meta":"sign-in, the first screen","mark":"live","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"/<slug>","meta":"public note, unauthenticated","mark":"live","tone":"warn"},{"label":"/p/<slug>","meta":"301 redirect","mark":"legacy","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"/ vault","meta":"the editor","mark":"live","tone":"neutral"}]},{"label":"Due now, absent","w":1,"rows":[{"label":"/privacy","meta":"SPDI Rule 4, live regime","mark":"missing","tone":"stop"},{"label":"/terms","meta":"IT Rules 3(1)(a) once publishing opens","mark":"missing","tone":"stop"},{"label":"/grievance","meta":"named officer, SPDI Rule 5(9)","mark":"missing","tone":"stop"},{"label":"consent before collection","meta":"sign-in is the first screen","mark":"missing","tone":"stop"}]}],"note":"privacy and terms are reserved slugs with no route behind them; grievance is not reserved","caption":"Four pages exist; the three the live regime requires do not"},"stakes":"Ten strangers enter an app that takes an email at first run with no notice and no page.","state":["Due now: policy, consent, a grievance officer within a month, a transfer gate.","From 2027-05-13 the ceiling is ninety days, and DPDP section 13(3) makes us the mandatory first stop.","No officer is named anywhere; `privacy` and `terms` are reserved slugs with no route."],"tension":["Publishing sets it: closed owes one month, open owes 24 hours and 15 days.","We are bound by any window we publish, and missing our own is worse."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"A written notice by hand to each of the ten, full pages in the build after the pilot","gains":["Proportionate to ten known participants, and cheap"],"costs":["Does not satisfy the published-policy duty, and anyone can reach the app"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"A written notice by hand to each of the ten, full pages later. Proportionate to ten known participants and cheap; it does not meet the published-policy duty, and anyone can reach the app."},{"k":"b","label":"Publish all three before the pilot, self-drafted, one month, founder named","gains":["The pilot runs lawfully, for under a week of capacity","The window matches the live regime exactly"],"costs":["A self-drafted policy may say something we later correct","It needs revisiting the day publishing opens"],"system":"Three static routes and a monitored address.","screens":"/privacy, /terms, /grievance, linked from sign-in.","money":"Founder time; counsel deferred.","what":"Publish all three before the pilot, self-drafted, with the one-month window and the founder named. The pilot runs lawfully for under a week of capacity; a self-drafted policy may need correcting."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish the tightest window from day one: 24-hour ack, 15-day resolution","gains":["Simplest to explain, and no revisiting if publishing opens"],"costs":["Binds one person to a daily acknowledgement duty","A missed self-imposed window is worse than a modest one"],"system":"A monitored inbox with a same-day rota.","screens":"Same pages, tighter copy.","money":"None in cash; real in attention.","what":"Publish the tightest window from day one: 24-hour acknowledgement, 15-day resolution. Simplest to explain; it binds one person to a daily duty, and a missed self-imposed window is worse."},{"k":"d","label":"Counsel drafts them first, then the pilot","gains":["A reviewed document, covering CERT-In and terms together"],"costs":["₹40,000 to ₹1,20,000 before evidence anyone wants the product"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Same three pages, later.","money":"₹40,000 to ₹1,20,000, unverified.","what":"Counsel drafts them first, then the pilot. A reviewed document covering CERT-In and terms together; ₹40,000 to ₹1,20,000 before any evidence anyone wants the product."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Twelve hours of writing is under a week of measured capacity, and it removes a live breach.","Publish the window the law sets and no tighter, because we are bound by it."],"flip":"If we hold document content, the policy is a much harder document and counsel should draft it.","linked":["L1","L17","L6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:536","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:542","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:548","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4194","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:6041","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4107","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4114","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4115","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4116","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4196","src/modules/share/domain/slug.ts:43","merged: L8"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A policy, terms and a named officer are due before a stranger stores anything.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The clocks that are already running","note":"Two of these say at launch, and the app has four pages with no policy among them.","items":[["Publish rules, privacy policy and user agreement","at launch","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4107`"],["Publish the grievance officer name and contact","at launch","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4114`"],["Acknowledge a grievance","within 24 hours","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4115`"],["Resolve a grievance","within 15 days","`docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4116`"],["Grievance window under SPDI Rule 5(9), today","one month","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:548`"],["Published window from 2027-05-13","not exceeding ninety days","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:548`"]]}],"recWhy":"SPDI Rule 4 requires a published privacy policy today, the app has four pages and none is a policy, and privacy and terms are reserved slugs with no route behind them. Due now: the policy, consent, a grievance officer within a month, and a transfer gate; from 13 May 2027 the ceiling is ninety days and DPDP section 13(3) makes us the mandatory first stop. The window is set by what we publish, closed publishing owes one month and open publishing owes 24 hours and 15 days, and we are bound by whatever we print, so publish the window the law sets and no tighter. Twelve hours of writing is under a week of measured capacity and removes a live breach; counsel's fee comes before any evidence anyone wants the product. Q9 keeps the policy short, and L17 supplies its retention table. If we ever hold document content, the policy is a much harder document and counsel drafts it."},{"id":"L12","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"EU","weight":"high","q":"Do we geo-exclude the EU for the pilot, or accept EU users and budget an Art 27 representative?","lede":"GDPR Art 3(2)(a) catches a free tool, and the plan admits its targeting posture covers only that.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Geo-exclude for the pilot","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Appoint now","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Appoint at first EU seat","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Annual cost","cells":["nil","€1,920 at EDPO's under-50 band","€468 at Prighter's €39 tier"]},{"label":"Art 27 representative","cells":["not engaged","in place before any user","in place on the trigger"]},{"label":"Art 30 record","cells":["not due","written now","written on the trigger"]},{"label":"DSA Art 13","cells":["falls away if publishing is closed","a second appointment","a second appointment"]},{"label":"Who spots the first EU seat","cells":["nobody needs to","nobody needs to","no measure exists, see the measurement decision"]}],"note":"Prices read live 2026-08-31; €200 to €500 is the 51 to 500 employee band, not ours","caption":"Three EU postures, priced at one-founder scale"},"stakes":"If any of the ten pilot strangers is in the EU, a representative is due before the test.","state":["An Art 27 representative and Art 30 record are due from the first EU seat.","The plan names our rotating install ID as the Recital 24 monitoring pattern.","A representative for a one-founder company costs €39 to €160 a month, not €200 to €500."],"tension":["The Art 27(2)(a) occasional-processing escape fails for a subscription editor with sync.","DSA Art 13 bites only for an intermediary service, so it follows publishing."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Accept EU users and appoint at the first EU signup rather than pre-emptively","gains":["Matches spend to the obligation, with €39 as the floor"],"costs":["Requires detecting the first EU seat, which our telemetry cannot do"],"system":"Needs a signup-country signal we lack.","screens":"None until the trigger.","money":"€468 a year from the trigger.","what":"Accept EU users and appoint a representative at the first EU signup. Matches spend to the obligation with €39 as the floor; it needs detecting the first EU seat, which we cannot do."},{"k":"b","label":"Accept EU users and appoint an Art 27 representative now at €160 a month","gains":["No trigger to detect and no gap to explain"],"costs":["€1,920 a year before any evidence of demand","Does not remove DSA Art 13 if publishing opens"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A representative named in the policy.","money":"€1,920 a year.","what":"Accept EU users and appoint an Article 27 representative now at €160 a month. No trigger to detect and no gap to explain; €1,920 a year before any evidence of demand."},{"k":"c","label":"Geo-exclude the EU for the pilot; revisit at the first paying EU team","gains":["Removes Art 27, Art 30 and DSA Art 13 from the pilot","Removes the Art 3(2)(b) telemetry question"],"costs":["Narrows the pool for a ten-stranger test","The exclusion has to be enforced, not written"],"system":"A recruitment rule and a sign-up check.","screens":"None during the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"Geo-exclude the EU for the pilot and revisit at the first paying EU team. Removes Articles 27 and 30 and DSA Article 13 from the pilot; the pool narrows, and the exclusion has to be enforced."},{"k":"d","label":"Accept EU users and appoint nobody, arguing occasional processing","gains":["Costs nothing"],"costs":["The research tested this; it fails for a subscription editor"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Accept EU users and appoint nobody, arguing occasional processing. Costs nothing; the research tested the argument and it fails for a subscription editor with sync."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The pilot is ten people we choose, so excluding the EU costs a sentence in the recruitment.","It defers a real annual commitment past the point where we learn whether the product survives."],"flip":"If the pilot cannot be filled without EU participants, the representative must be contracted that day.","linked":["L6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:550","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:5","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:6","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:29","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:33","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:34","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4137","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:6041"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"European users reach a free web tool on day one. Accept or exclude, and budget it.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What an Article 27 representative costs, after the August re-check","note":"Prighter and EDPO price pages were read live on 2026-08-31. Budget the top of the range, because the cheap tiers are usage capped.","cols":["What the record said","Verdict","What was measured","Where"],"rows":[["A representative costs €200 to €500 a month","Revised","That band is the 51 to 500 employee price. A one person company pays €39 to €160 a month","`docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:5`"],["Article 27 is often cited as Article 13","Refuted","Two different laws, two different obligations","`docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:6`"],["No exemption was noted","Added","Article 27(2)(a) removes the duty where processing is occasional and unlikely to risk rights","`docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r18-v2-compliance-legal.md:33`"]]}],"recWhy":"GDPR Article 3(2)(a) catches a free tool, the Article 27(2)(a) occasional-processing escape fails for a subscription editor with sync, and a representative plus an Article 30 record are due from the first EU seat. The plan names our rotating install ID as the Recital 24 monitoring pattern. A representative costs a one-founder company €39 to €160 a month, not the €200 to €500 the plan assumed, and it is still an annual commitment made before we learn whether the product survives. The pilot is ten people we choose, so excluding the EU costs a sentence in the recruitment and has to be enforced there rather than merely written. DSA Article 13 follows publishing, which Q11 keeps closed, so it falls away with L6. If the pilot cannot be filled without EU participants, the representative is contracted that day."},{"id":"L17","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Retention","weight":"high","q":"What does the privacy policy promise about deletion, now that retention has a floor as well as a ceiling?","lede":"We cannot promise to delete what the law requires us to keep, and the refuted rule is still in the record.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"today","label":"CERT-In ICT logs, 180 days","note":"floor, inside India","tone":"warn"},{"when":"on publishing","label":"IT Rules 3(1)(g) and 3(1)(h), 180 days","note":"removed content, cancelled registrations","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2027-05-13","label":"Access logs plus the data they concern, 1 year","note":"Rule 6(1)(e)","tone":"stop"},{"when":"2027-05-13","label":"Processing logs survive deletion, 1 year","note":"Rule 8(3)","tone":"stop"},{"when":"superseded","label":"\"No row-level retention beyond 90 days\"","note":"still at RECORD line 4508","tone":"sunk"}],"note":"Floors from PRODUCT-BRIEF line 545; the superseded ceiling from RECORD line 4508","caption":"Every retention rule that binds today is a floor, not a ceiling"},"stakes":"A policy saying we delete what you delete is false on publication, and the user finds out late.","state":["The plan sets the floors and says to delete the record's 90-day rule.","It is still in the record, so someone will quote it.","The inactivity-erasure timer is dropped; the Third Schedule thresholds do not reach us."],"tension":["The floors binding today are CERT-In's and the IT Rules', not DPDP's.","A books-of-account floor also applies and its period is unverified."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Promise deletion on request with an except-where-law-requires clause","gains":["Short, conventional and fast to write"],"costs":["The user discovers the exceptions when they matter","Gives a rights request nothing to answer from"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One clause.","money":"None.","what":"Promise deletion on request with an except-where-law-requires clause. Short and fast to write; the user discovers the exceptions when they matter, and a rights request has nothing to answer from."},{"k":"b","label":"Defer the retention schedule until DPDP commences","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Wrong direction: CERT-In and the IT Rules bind today"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer the retention schedule until DPDP commences. No work now; the wrong direction, because CERT-In and the IT Rules bind today."},{"k":"c","label":"Publish a retention table naming the floors and what survives an account closure","gains":["One document serves the policy page and the internal schedule","The artefact a rights request is answered from"],"costs":["Every row must be true, including the Companies Act floor"],"system":"Forces a written record inventory first.","screens":"A table inside /privacy.","money":"Founder time; possibly one CA question.","what":"Publish a retention table naming the floors and what survives an account closure. One document serves the policy page and the internal schedule; every row must be true."},{"k":"d","label":"Hold nothing that needs retaining, so the retention schedule is genuinely near-empty","gains":["The shortest honest policy available"],"costs":["Only reachable if we hold no documents and no logs","CERT-In's floor and the billing records still apply"],"system":"Follows the Firebase, byte-path and log decisions.","screens":"A very short retention section.","money":"None.","what":"Hold nothing that needs retaining, so the schedule is near-empty. The shortest honest policy; only reachable with no documents and no logs, and the CERT-In floor and billing records still apply."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["A retention table doubles as the internal schedule, so it is written once.","Delete the superseded 90-day rule in the same edit, before someone quotes it in six months."],"flip":"If we hold no documents and no logs, the table gets shorter and the claim stronger.","linked":["L1","L14","L7","PR2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:546","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:3064","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4123","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4508","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:8983"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Deletion promises have to match the retention floor before the policy is written.","dependsOn":["L1"],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The floor, the ceiling, and the rule the notified text refuted","note":"Rule 3(1)(g) contradicts a promise to delete what the user deletes. The record says to name that conflict in the policy rather than hide it, `docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4123`.","items":[["Never delete below, today","180 days, CERT-In","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545`"],["Access logs and the personal data they concern, from 2027-05-13","one year, Rule 6(1)(e)","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545`"],["Processing logs surviving account deletion","one year, Rule 8(3)","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545`"],["The record rule of no row-level retention beyond 90 days","refuted, delete it","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545`"],["Does the Third Schedule inactivity timer reach us","no","`docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:546`"]]}],"recWhy":"Retention now has a floor as well as a ceiling, and we cannot promise to delete what the law requires us to keep. The CERT-In and IT Rules floors bind today, a books-of-account floor applies with a period still unverified, and the record's refuted 90-day rule is still in the record for someone to quote. A retention table names each floor and what survives an account closure, and it doubles as the internal schedule, so it is written once and it is the artefact a rights request is answered from. Q9 keeps it short, and L14 decides the row most likely to change. The 90-day rule is deleted in the same edit, before it is quoted in six months. The cost is that every row must be true, including the Companies Act period, which the CA in B14 confirms. If we hold no documents and no logs, the table gets shorter and the claim stronger, and (d) is where this converges."},{"id":"L10","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Processors","weight":"high","q":"Do we cut the vendor chain to bring-your-own key and bundled assets, or paper every processor?","lede":"Six AI routes send up to 200,000 characters to whichever of five foreign providers an env var selects.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"User's document","note":"up to 200,000 characters","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"POST /api/ai/*","note":"our server, six routes","tone":"warn"},{"label":"gateway-client reads env vars","note":"the user cannot see the choice","tone":"stop"},{"label":"One of five foreign providers","note":"Google, Groq, Cerebras, Mistral, OpenRouter","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Vercel AI Gateway if none set","note":"the silent default","tone":"stop"},{"label":"jsdelivr in the export renderer","note":"cut in the plan, still in the code","tone":"stop"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Providers listed at gateway-client.ts line 34; the CDN at pdf-doc.ts line 132 and export-doc.ts line 98","caption":"Where a stranger's document goes on the path as shipped"},"stakes":"An undisclosed sub-processor chain under both regimes, invisible in the product, with the operator paying.","state":["The AI gateway picks providers from five API-key environment variables.","A call can carry 200,000 characters, and no bring-your-own key exists.","The plan names three vendors; the code has eight parties and no agreement."],"tension":["SPDI Rule 7 names password and payment details, the two we have not papered.","A CDN was cut for the no-bytes rule; export renderers still load jsdelivr."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the five-provider fallback and publish a sub-processor list","gains":["Truthful at the moment of writing, at no cost"],"costs":["The list drifts whenever an env var changes","Five sub-processor relationships to paper"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Adds a sub-processor page.","money":"Unchanged.","what":"Keep the five-provider fallback and publish a sub-processor list. Truthful at the moment of writing, at no cost; the list drifts whenever an env var changes, and five relationships need papering."},{"k":"b","label":"Cut the chain: bring-your-own key, bundled CDN assets, then paper what is left","gains":["Removes five sub-processors and one CDN from the path","Removes the operator's AI bill"],"costs":["Loses the first-run demo of idea mode and the decision flow"],"system":"Six routes moved client-side; CDN vendored.","screens":"A key field in settings.","money":"Operator AI spend goes to zero.","what":"Cut the chain: bring-your-own key, bundled CDN assets, then paper what is left. Removes five sub-processors, one CDN and the operator's AI bill in one change; the zero-configuration demo goes."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the server path, pin one provider, sign standard DPAs, publish the list","gains":["Keeps the zero-configuration demo; one relationship, not five"],"costs":["The operator still pays for every call","Fiduciary exposure over document content stays"],"system":"Provider pinned; fallback chain removed.","screens":"A sub-processor page and an AI-panel disclosure.","money":"AI spend scales with use.","what":"Keep the server path, pin one provider, sign standard DPAs, publish the list. Keeps the zero-configuration demo with one relationship; the operator pays for every call, and fiduciary exposure stays."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer the agreements to 2027 when DPDP commences","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Backwards: SPDI Rule 7 binds today and DPDP waits","Nothing to hand the first B2B buyer"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer the agreements to 2027 when DPDP commences. No work now; backwards, because SPDI Rule 7 binds today, and there is nothing to hand the first B2B buyer."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Bring-your-own key is already the plan's position, due before any stranger.","It collapses five sub-processor relationships and the operator's AI bill in one change."],"flip":"If the pilot needs a zero-configuration demo of idea mode and decision flow, the provider must be named.","linked":["F10","L1","L19"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:133","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:171","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:328","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:542","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:549","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:551","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:558","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:83","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4540","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:22","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:34","src/app/api/ai/refine/route.ts:35","src/modules/export/presentation/pdf-doc.ts:132","src/modules/export/presentation/export-doc.ts:98","package.json","merged: L11"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The vendor chain matters once there are paying users and real volume.","recWhy":"Six AI routes send up to 200,000 characters to whichever of five foreign providers an environment variable selects, no bring-your-own key exists, the plan names three vendors while the code has eight parties and no agreement, and the export renderers still load from jsdelivr. SPDI Rule 7 binds today and names password and payment details, the two we have not papered. Bring-your-own key is already the plan's position and Q10 makes it a hard gate before any stranger reaches an AI route. With the key the user's own and the assets bundled, five sub-processor relationships and the operator's AI bill disappear in one change, and what is left to paper is identity and billing. The cost is the first-run demo of idea mode and decision flow with no configuration, which R17 already puts in question. If the pilot needs that zero-configuration demo, the provider must be named and papered first."},{"id":"L13","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"The review file","weight":"high","q":"Does the review file carry a person's real identity into the user's repository, or a pseudonymous id?","lede":"The review file is the product's main output, and nobody has asked what personal data it carries.","visual":{"kind":"file","name":".frontmatter/review.jsonl","lines":[{"t":"{\"path\":\"docs/spec.md\",\"start\":1204,","mark":"neutral"},{"t":" \"end\":1631,\"hash\":\"9f2c…\",","mark":"neutral","note":"not personal"},{"t":" \"reviewed_by\":\"…\",","mark":"stop","note":"a natural person"},{"t":" \"reviewed_at\":\"2026-09-09T11:02Z\",","mark":"warn","note":"reading history"},{"t":" \"author\":\"…\",\"model\":\"…\",","mark":"warn"},{"t":" \"prompt\":\"rewrite the retention section\"}","mark":"stop","note":"the user's own text"}],"note":"Fields from PRODUCT-BRIEF line 135; the file is committed beside the documents","caption":"The one file the product writes, and what of it is personal data"},"stakes":"An erasure request against a review file in a repository we do not control has no answer.","state":["`.frontmatter/review.jsonl` holds one JSON line per span: reviewed-by, reviewed-at, author, model and prompt.","It is committed alongside the documents, by the same path.","`reviewed-by` appears once in the whole plan and never in a legal section."],"tension":["A public repo publishes identity and timestamps; a private one shares them with cloners.","DPDP section 12(3) and GDPR Art 17 give erasure, and we control none of it.","The prompt field is the user's own instruction and may contain anything."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the separate file out of the repo, machine-local and synced separately","gains":["Removes the exposure entirely"],"costs":["Destroys the central claim: review state (who has read what) must travel"],"system":"A separate store and a sync path.","screens":"No shared review state.","money":"Sync infrastructure, two builds after the pilot.","what":"Keep the review file out of the repository, machine-local and synced separately. Removes the exposure entirely; it destroys the central claim that review state must travel with the documents."},{"k":"b","label":"Store real identity and say so plainly in the docs and at first write","gains":["Most legible to a team that wants names"],"costs":["Publishes a reading history forever, with no erasure route","Makes every rights request unanswerable"],"system":"No change to the format.","screens":"A prominent first-write notice.","money":"None.","what":"Store real identity and say so plainly in the docs and at first write. Most legible to a team that wants names; it publishes a reading history forever with no erasure route."},{"k":"c","label":"Store a per-repo pseudonymous id by default, with a local map the user owns","gains":["Removes the identifier from the artefact that leaves our control","Cross-tool review state still works"],"costs":["A team buyer wanting names in the file loses them","Needs a local map and a missing-map rule"],"system":"An id-to-name map outside the repo.","screens":"Panel shows names; the file shows ids.","money":"None.","what":"Store a per-repo pseudonymous id by default, with a local map the user owns. Removes the identifier from the artefact that leaves our control and review still works; needs a missing-map rule."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop the prompt field, keep identity, document the rest","gains":["Removes the field most likely to carry something private"],"costs":["Identity and timestamps stay, and stay unerasable","Loses the prompt evidence attribution rests on"],"system":"One field removed from the writer.","screens":"The panel drops the prompt.","money":"None.","what":"Drop the prompt field, keep identity, document the rest. Removes the field most likely to carry something private; identity and timestamps stay, and stay unerasable."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The product needs a stable per-span identity, not a name, so a pseudonymous id costs nothing.","It removes the only irreversible personal-data flow the architecture creates."],"flip":"If team buyers need human-readable attribution in the file, real identity plus a prominent first-write notice.","linked":["E37","L17"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:135","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:152","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:43","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:8888"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Identity in the review file is deferred with review state.","recWhy":"The review file is the product's main output and nobody has asked what personal data it carries. One JSON line per span with reviewed-by, reviewed-at, author, model and prompt, committed alongside the documents by the same path, into a repository whose clones we never control. A public repository publishes identity and timestamps forever; a private one shares them with every cloner; DPDP section 12(3) and GDPR Article 17 give erasure rights we cannot honour on either. The product needs a stable per-span identity, not a name, so a pseudonymous id costs nothing and removes the only irreversible personal-data flow the architecture creates. The cost is a missing-map rule and a team buyer who wants names in the file itself. If team buyers need human-readable attribution in the file, real identity with a prominent first-write notice is the fallback."},{"id":"L14","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Log residency","weight":"high","q":"Do we build the India-resident 180-day log store now, or run with no logs and say so?","lede":"The plan says log residency is already priced, and the store it was priced against is not our stack.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"What the plan's operating rules price","lines":[{"t":"R2, IN jurisdiction, object-lock","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"180 days rolling, NTP to NIC or NPL","mark":"neutral"},{"t":"no bytes, no filenames, no cleartext IP","mark":"neutral"}]},"after":{"title":"What the repo runs","lines":[{"t":"Vercel and Firebase, no region pinned","mark":"stop"},{"t":"no logging dependency in package.json","mark":"stop"},{"t":"zero logs, so nothing to capture","mark":"stop"}]},"via":"stack changed","note":"The R2 design is at PRD v2 line 3689; the residency conflict was recorded at RECORD line 4122","caption":"The log store was priced on infrastructure the product does not use"},"stakes":"A breach investigation with no log slice has no evidence, and the 180 days is a floor.","state":["The plan states 180 days in Indian jurisdiction, priced as R2 with object-lock.","We run Vercel plus Firebase, no region pinned, and `package.json` has no logging dependency.","The PRD specifies timestamp, request id, opaque user id, verb, no bytes."],"tension":["This is not a residency problem but an absence: there are no logs.","180 days is a floor, and one-year access logs follow from 2027-05-13."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Turn on the host's own logs and accept whatever region they land in","gains":["The cheapest way to have logs at all"],"costs":["Fails the residency clause"],"system":"A configuration change, no new code.","screens":"None.","money":"Platform retention charges.","what":"Turn on the host's own logs and accept whatever region they land in. The cheapest way to have logs at all; it fails the residency clause."},{"k":"b","label":"Run with no logs through the pilot and state that plainly in the runbook","gains":["Zero cost and zero personal data at rest"],"costs":["Leaves the 180-day floor unmet and incidents un-investigable","The first security questionnaire finds it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Run with no logs through the pilot and state that plainly in the runbook. Zero cost and zero personal data at rest; the 180-day floor stays unmet, and a questionnaire finds it."},{"k":"c","label":"Fix the plan to name the real stack and defer the store to the build after the pilot","gains":["Honest and free; stops a wrong price being quoted"],"costs":["Fixes the document, not the duty"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fix the plan to name the real stack and defer the store to the build after the pilot. Honest and free, and stops a wrong price being quoted; it fixes the document, not the duty."},{"k":"d","label":"Build the minimal India-resident append-only security log now","what":"Timestamp, request id, opaque user id, verb and outcome, and nothing else.","gains":["Satisfies the floor and gives breach response something to capture"],"costs":["Adds a vendor and a region decision to a one-person stack","Needs NTP sync and an object-lock equivalent"],"system":"A write-only store and request-id middleware.","screens":"None.","money":"Small storage; one more processor agreement."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Five opaque append-only fields, and the one artefact a breach response cannot reconstruct afterwards.","The price assumes R2 on Cloudflare, which is not our stack, so the plan is wrong."],"flip":"If we hold nothing server-side, no logs plus a written statement is defensible.","linked":["L1","L10","L4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:544","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:545","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:3689","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4122","vercel.json","package.json","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:531"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A log store is due with real traffic.","recWhy":"The plan says log residency is already priced, as R2 with object-lock, and we run Vercel plus Firebase with no region pinned and no logging dependency at all. The price is for a stack we do not have, and the problem is not residency but absence: there are no logs. CERT-In's 180 days is a floor, one-year access logs follow from 13 May 2027, and a security log is the one artefact a breach response cannot reconstruct afterwards. The PRD specifies five opaque append-only fields, timestamp, request id, opaque user id, verb, outcome, and no bytes, which is small enough to build now. Q9's zero document bytes shrinks what the log captures to identity and billing events, so the store is minimal. The cost is a vendor and a region decision on a one-person stack, plus NTP sync and an object-lock equivalent. If we hold nothing server-side at all, no logs plus a written statement is defensible."},{"id":"L16","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Security review","weight":"high","q":"Do we buy an outside security review before the first non-founder user, and does it cover shipped code?","lede":"We assert our own safety, and the shipped code carries a defect two files revealed in one pass.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"Anonymous visitor","note":"no account needed","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"GET /<slug>","note":"attacker-authored content","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Tight CSP bound to /p/:slug*","note":"that route is now a 301","tone":"stop","dashed":true},{"label":"Report-only policy applies","note":"permits unsafe-eval","tone":"stop"}],"perRow":4,"note":"Binding at next.config.ts line 48; the report-only policy at proxy.ts lines 112 and 117","caption":"The defect two files revealed, with no outside review bought"},"stakes":"The people who wrote the code judge whether it is safe, and the first buyer asks for the artefact.","state":["An outside review is listed as untapped, triggered at the first team customer.","Firestore rules call themselves a prototype, to be hardened before paid signups.","The enforced CSP binds to `/p/:slug*`, now a redirect, so public pages are report-only."],"tension":["The policy that applies to untrusted content permits `unsafe-eval`.","Both files' comments describe the old arrangement, which is how the defect survived."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Buy a scoped external review of the shipped code and Firestore design pre-pilot","gains":["Finds the class of defect two files revealed in one pass","Produces the artefact the first B2B buyer asks for"],"costs":["A fee and a week, before any revenue","Reviews server code the Firebase and byte-path decisions may delete"],"system":"No change until findings land.","screens":"None.","money":"A review fee, unbudgeted.","what":"Buy an external review of the shipped code and Firestore design before the pilot. Finds the class of defect two files revealed in one pass; a fee and a week before revenue, on code that may go."},{"k":"b","label":"Fix the known defects internally and defer the review to the first team customer","gains":["Free now"],"costs":["Keeps the pattern that produced the defect","Ten strangers use it before anyone outside looks"],"system":"A two-line CSP fix.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Fix the known defects internally and defer the review to the first team customer. Free now; it keeps the pattern that produced the defect, and ten strangers use it before anyone outside looks."},{"k":"c","label":"Review only `firestore.rules`, the named concrete subject","gains":["Cheapest scoping"],"costs":["Reviews a file nothing runs while running code goes unreviewed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"A small fee, little coverage.","what":"Review only firestore.rules, the named concrete subject. The cheapest scoping; it reviews a file nothing runs while running code goes unreviewed."},{"k":"d","label":"Settle Firestore and the server path first, then buy a scoped review of what is left","gains":["The smallest subject is the cheapest real review"],"costs":["Pushes the review past the pilot","Depends on two other decisions going the same way"],"system":"Follows the Firebase and byte-path decisions; no new work.","screens":"None.","money":"A smaller fee, later.","what":"Settle Firestore and the server path first, then buy a scoped review of what is left. The smallest subject is the cheapest real review; it pushes the review past the pilot."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The cheapest real review has the smallest subject, and the Firebase and byte-path decisions point there.","Fix the CSP binding this week regardless: two lines, and untrusted content is report-only."],"flip":"If we hold documents server-side, the review moves ahead of the pilot and stops being optional.","linked":["L1","L6"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:44","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","firestore.rules:7","firestore.rules:393","next.config.ts:48","src/proxy.ts:112","src/proxy.ts:117","src/proxy.ts:138"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"An outside review is due before paying users, not before twenty hand made kits.","recWhy":"We assert our own safety, and the shipped code carries a defect two files revealed in one pass. The enforced content security policy binds to /p/:slug*, which is now a redirect, so public pages run report-only, and the policy that applies to untrusted content permits unsafe-eval. Both files' comments describe the old arrangement, which is how the defect survived. Q9 deletes firestore.rules and the server PDF route and Q11 keeps publishing closed, so most of what a review bought this week would review code that is about to go. The cheapest real review has the smallest subject, and that subject exists once those decisions land. Fix the policy binding this week regardless: two lines. The cost is that the review sits past the pilot. If we ever hold documents server-side, the review moves ahead of the pilot and stops being optional.","dupes":{"dropped":["R12"],"areas":["Research & evidence"],"why":"Research & evidence recommended the same as option d here: settle whether we hold user documents and make the repo say one thing, then buy the review. Its question also asked whether the review comes before the pilot or before the first team customer.","agree":true}},{"id":"L19","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Tax","weight":"medium","q":"Do we register for GST now, stop buying foreign AI services, or put the position to a CA first?","lede":"Buying API access from a foreign supplier is importing a service, and registration starts at the first rupee.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"Company buys a foreign API key","note":"Google, Groq, Mistral, OpenRouter","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Import of services","note":"supplier outside India","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Reverse charge applies","note":"CGST section 24(iii)","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Registration due at the first rupee","note":"the ₹20 lakh floor does not apply","tone":"stop"}],"perRow":4,"note":"Stated as a fact about us at DECIDE line 213","caption":"Why the obligation attaches to buying, not to selling"},"stakes":"A known obligation accrues interest and penalty, and surfaces during diligence rather than on our timetable.","state":["Our decision memo states it as fact, not a hypothetical.","The plan's risk list carries it with a one-line response and no owner.","Whether registration has happened is not stated in any document, either way."],"tension":["The code reads five foreign suppliers' keys, and hosting is foreign too.","The export-of-services and LUT positions are inference-only."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Stop buying foreign AI services on the company account until registration is settled","gains":["Removes the trigger immediately, and matches the processor decision"],"costs":["Conflicts with running server-side AI at all","Hosting is foreign too, so imports remain"],"system":"Server-side AI off; keys removed.","screens":"The AI panel needs the user's key.","money":"Operator AI spend to zero.","what":"Stop buying foreign AI services on the company account until registration is settled. Removes the trigger immediately and matches L10; hosting is foreign too, so imports remain."},{"k":"b","label":"Register now and start filing, before any revenue","gains":["Compliant from the first import"],"costs":["Monthly filing overhead on a company with no revenue","Permanent, not reversible when convenient"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Monthly filing overhead.","what":"Register for GST now and start filing, before any revenue. Compliant from the first import; monthly filing overhead on a company with no revenue, and it is permanent."},{"k":"c","label":"Ask a CA to confirm the position and the exposure to date, then act","gains":["Settles a factual question about our own filings","Covers the inference-only tax items too"],"costs":["A short delay, and one fee"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"One consultation fee.","what":"Ask a CA to confirm the position and the exposure to date, then act. Settles a factual question about our own filings and covers the export and LUT positions; a short delay and one fee."},{"k":"d","label":"Treat it as immaterial at current spend and revisit at revenue","gains":["Cheapest today"],"costs":["Interest and penalty accrue on whatever is owed"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Unquantified exposure.","what":"Treat it as immaterial at current spend and revisit at revenue. Cheapest today; interest and penalty accrue on whatever is owed."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["A factual question about our own filings that nobody has answered, and it should not be guessed.","One consultation settles it along with the export and LUT positions."],"flip":"If the CA confirms registration is already due, stopping the foreign purchases follows immediately.","linked":["L10","PR16"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:330","docs/DECIDE.md:212","docs/DECIDE.md:213","docs/BUSINESS.md:680","docs/BUSINESS.md:698","src/modules/ai/infrastructure/gateway-client.ts:34"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Tax registration follows revenue.","recWhy":"Buying API access from a foreign supplier is importing a service, and on one reading of CGST section 24(iii) registration starts at the first rupee with no turnover floor. Our decision memo states this as fact, the risk list carries it with a one-line response and no owner, and whether registration has already happened is stated nowhere. The code reads five foreign suppliers' keys and the hosting is foreign too, so the trigger exists whatever the AI route does. This is a factual question about our own filings that should not be guessed, and one consultation settles it along with the export-of-services and LUT positions, which are inference only. B14 puts the CA opinion in week 0 and the retainer is already in the budget, so the cost is a short delay and no new fee. If the CA confirms registration is already due, stopping the foreign purchases follows the same day."},{"id":"L20","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Licensing","weight":"medium","q":"Do we split the licence, Apache-2.0 for the engine and proprietary for the app, or license the repository one way?","lede":"The repo states no licence, so the default is all rights reserved and nobody may lawfully run the engine.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Splice engine, OffsetMap, refusal logic","items":["Apache-2.0","NOTICE file","patent grant, trademark reserved"],"side":"engine","tone":"accent"},{"label":"CLI and the written spec","items":["Apache-2.0","per-directory LICENSE"],"side":"tools","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Editor, sync, billing","items":["proprietary, closed","data-portability promise"],"side":"app","tone":"neutral"}],"note":"Repo today: no LICENSE, no license field, 47 MIT runtime dependencies","caption":"The split the plan reasoned twice and never wrote to disk"},"stakes":"A certificate strategy that gains value from third-party verification currently forbids third parties from running it.","state":["No `LICENSE` or `NOTICE` file, no `license` field, and `package.json` declares `private: true`.","The PRD calls all rights reserved the worst position for a spec.","The PRD sets out the split, and the engine document repeats it."],"tension":["202 of 228 files are shared with sgnk-md, and the sibling's declaration is unchecked.","A root licence will not cover the spec prose, so per-path declarations are needed."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Apache-2.0 with NOTICE for the engine, proprietary for the app, per-directory files","gains":["Matches reasoning already written down twice","The trademark reservation is what the certificate needs"],"costs":["Requires separating the engine into its own package","Per-path declarations to keep in step"],"system":"Engine extraction, per-directory LICENSE, a license field.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE for the engine, proprietary for the app, per directory. Reasoned twice already, and it reserves the trademark the certificate needs; the engine becomes its own package."},{"k":"b","label":"Leave it unlicensed until the engine is extracted","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Every day nobody may lawfully verify the engine","The worst position for a spec others should implement"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it unlicensed until the engine is extracted. No work now; every day nobody may lawfully verify the engine, the worst position for a spec others should implement."},{"k":"c","label":"One proprietary licence for the whole repository","gains":["Simplest, and true to the current code layout"],"costs":["Forecloses the third-party verification the certificate needs","Contradicts publishing a spec for others to implement"],"system":"One root LICENSE and a license field.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"One proprietary licence for the whole repository. Simplest and true to the current layout; it forecloses the third-party verification the certificate needs."},{"k":"d","label":"Apache-2.0 for everything","gains":["Maximum adoption and the easiest verification story"],"costs":["Hands the whole editor to the incumbent we may be colliding with"],"system":"One root LICENSE.","screens":"None.","money":"Removes licence revenue.","what":"Apache-2.0 for everything. Maximum adoption and the easiest verification story; it hands the whole editor to the incumbent we may be colliding with."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The reasoning is written down twice, but nobody added the file.","Apache-2.0's trademark reservation lets the engine be verified without letting a fork issue certificates."],"flip":"If the engine will not be extracted this quarter, written intent to open it later is more honest.","linked":["E24"],"sources":["docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4636","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4640","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4641","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4645","docs/ENGINE.md:2237","docs/DECIDE.md:168","package.json"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Licence split follows publishing the engine.","recWhy":"The repository states no licence, has no LICENSE or NOTICE file and no license field, and package.json declares private true, so the default is all rights reserved and nobody may lawfully run the engine, which the PRD itself calls the worst position for a spec. The split has been reasoned twice, in the PRD and the engine document, and nobody added the file. Apache-2.0 with a NOTICE for the engine, because its section 6 withholds trademark rights, so the engine can be verified by anyone without a fork issuing certificates under the mark. Proprietary for the app, because Apache for everything hands the editor to the incumbent in the same store. E24 settles the package split and N4 the mark. Per-directory declarations are needed because a root licence cannot cover the spec prose. If the engine is not extracted this quarter, written intent to open it later is the honest interim."},{"id":"L22","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Children","weight":"medium","q":"Do we state 18-plus in the terms, build an age gate, or write down why the question is out of scope?","lede":"DPDP section 9 carries a ₹200 crore ceiling, the plan never mentions children, and the code has no age field.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":190,"cols":[{"title":"Say 18-plus in the terms","tone":"accent"},{"title":"Build age assurance","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Stay silent","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Cost","cells":["one sentence","a field, a check and friction","nothing"]},{"label":"First-run friction","cells":["none","on the first screen","none"]},{"label":"Section 9(3) monitoring ban","cells":["satisfied, nothing is tracked","satisfied","satisfied in fact, unstated"]},{"label":"If a buyer asks","cells":["point at the clause","point at the gate","nothing to point at"]}],"note":"Ceiling from RECORD line 4517; the prohibition from RECORD line 4507","caption":"Three positions on an obligation the plan never mentions"},"stakes":"The current position is silence, the only one of the three that cannot be explained to anyone.","state":["Section 9(3) bans tracking or behavioural monitoring of children.","The ceiling for the section 9 obligations is ₹200 crore.","No age field, date of birth or terms acceptance in `src/`; the plan is silent."],"tension":["A markdown editor for repositories has few children, but nobody wrote that assumption down.","The obligation does not scale with the number of children affected.","Section 9 commences 2027-05-13, so the clause is cheap now and awkward later."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Leave it unaddressed until DPDP commences","gains":["No work now"],"costs":["Defers a clause that costs one sentence","Leaves a visible hole at a ₹200 crore penalty tier"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Leave it unaddressed until DPDP commences. No work now; it defers a clause that costs one sentence and leaves a visible hole at a ₹200 crore penalty tier."},{"k":"b","label":"State 18-plus in the terms and do no behavioural monitoring of anyone","gains":["One sentence trivially satisfies section 9(3)","Pairs with the no-telemetry answer at no cost"],"costs":["A clause nobody reads is weak evidence"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One clause in /terms.","money":"None.","what":"State 18-plus in the terms and do no behavioural monitoring of anyone. One sentence satisfies section 9(3) and pairs with the no-telemetry answer at no cost; a clause nobody reads is weak evidence."},{"k":"c","label":"Build verifiable age assurance at sign-up","gains":["The strongest position if the audience assumption is wrong"],"costs":["Real friction on the first screen","Disproportionate to a developer-tool audience"],"system":"An age field, storage and a verification path.","screens":"A step before the editor.","money":"A vendor, if assurance is real.","what":"Build verifiable age assurance at sign-up. The strongest position if the audience assumption is wrong; real friction on the first screen, disproportionate to a developer-tool audience."},{"k":"d","label":"Write down that the product is not directed at children, and publish nothing","gains":["Documents the position internally at no cost"],"costs":["The argument is not available to anyone who asks"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Write down that the product is not directed at children, and publish nothing. Documents the position at no cost; the argument is not available to anyone who asks."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["It is one sentence, and an architecture that monitors nobody already meets the ban.","The only one of the three that can be explained to a buyer or regulator."],"flip":"If publishing ever aims at note-takers rather than developers, the audience assumption must be re-argued.","linked":["L7"],"sources":["docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4507","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4517","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:538","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:531"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Age terms come with the full terms of service.","recWhy":"DPDP section 9 carries a ₹200 crore ceiling and bans tracking or behavioural monitoring of children, the plan never mentions children, and there is no age field, date of birth or terms acceptance anywhere in src. A markdown editor for repositories has few children, and nobody wrote that assumption down; the obligation does not scale with the number of children affected, so the size of the audience is no defence. One sentence in the terms plus an architecture that monitors nobody, which AC17 and Q9 already commit to, meets section 9(3) at no cost and is the only version that can be explained to a buyer or a regulator when asked. It ships with L7's terms. The cost is that a clause nobody reads is weak evidence on its own. If publishing ever aims at note-takers rather than developers, the audience assumption is re-argued."},{"id":"L23","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Claims","weight":"medium","q":"Do we cite regulators at all, and do we say plainly that none requires per-span review?","lede":"Nothing opened on 2026-09-09 mandates review below the whole document, and a compliance buyer will check.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"What the source says","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"What it does not say","tone":"stop"}],"rows":[{"label":"21 CFR 11.10(e)","cells":["changes must not obscure previously recorded information","nothing about per-span human review"]},{"label":"MHRA GxP data integrity","cells":["audit trails may be reviewed by exception reporting","no granularity, no mandate"]},{"label":"EMA reflection paper, 2024","cells":["model-generated text needs a quality review mechanism","non-binding, says should, names no granularity"]},{"label":"Pharma practice","cells":["whole-document approval","not per span"]},{"label":"Aviation practice","cells":["a per-page revision date","not per span"]}],"note":"All five opened in the 2026-09-09 round; none mandates per-span review","caption":"What regulators require, against what a pitch might imply"},"stakes":"One implied mandate a compliance buyer can refute kills the credibility of every other claim.","state":["The 2026-09-09 round opened 21 CFR 11.10(e), the MHRA GxP guide and the EMA paper.","None mandates per-span review: pharma approves whole documents, aviation dates each page.","The plan already says: claim neither compliance nor exposure early."],"tension":["MHRA permits exception reporting, which supports the design and undercuts an enumerate-everything pitch.","The EMA paper says should, is non-binding, and names no granularity."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Cite them as pressure without the caveat","gains":["The strongest-sounding version"],"costs":["A compliance buyer refutes it from the source in one reading","One refuted claim discredits the rest of the deck"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Cite the regulators as pressure without the caveat. The strongest-sounding version; a compliance buyer refutes it from the source in one reading, and one refuted claim discredits the deck."},{"k":"b","label":"Defer until five regulated-document buyers have been asked what they need","gains":["Evidence before wording, which is the right order"],"costs":["Leaves the pitch without a regulated story; the conversations have not started"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer until five regulated-document buyers have been asked what they need. Evidence before wording, the right order; it leaves the pitch without a regulated story."},{"k":"c","label":"Cite regulators only with the caveat that none mandates per-span review","gains":["Compliance buyers reward the caveat and punish its absence","Keeps MHRA's exception-reporting line, which supports the design"],"costs":["Every quote must be re-fetched and string-matched first"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Wording on the regulated-buyer page.","money":"None.","what":"Cite regulators only with the caveat that none mandates per-span review. Compliance buyers reward the caveat and punish its absence; every quote is re-fetched and string-matched first."},{"k":"d","label":"Do not cite regulators at all; sell on the working-tree case alone","gains":["Nothing to refute and nothing to re-fetch"],"costs":["Gives up the one adjacency where document review is a habit"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Forgoes a buyer segment.","what":"Do not cite regulators at all; sell on the working-tree case alone. Nothing to refute and nothing to re-fetch; it gives up the one adjacency where document review is already a habit."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The caveat costs a clause and it is the sentence a compliance buyer is testing for.","MHRA's exception-reporting line already argues for the design, so the honest citation is the useful one."],"flip":"If regulated buyers report a written internal requirement for span-level review, the claim becomes buyers-require.","linked":["L21"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:41","https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-01-01/title-21.xml?part=11","https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5aa2b9ede5274a3e391e37f3/MHRA_GxP_data_integrity_guide_March_edited_Final.pdf","https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/reflection-paper-use-artificial-intelligence-ai-medicinal-product-lifecycle_en.pdf","added: L23"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"How we talk about regulators is positioning.","recWhy":"The 9 September round opened 21 CFR 11.10(e), the MHRA GxP guide and the EMA reflection paper, and none mandates review below the whole document: pharma approves whole documents, aviation dates each page, the EMA paper says should and names no granularity. A compliance buyer will check, and one refuted claim discredits the rest of the deck, which is the same rule that withdrew three of our own numbers. MHRA permits exception reporting, which supports the design and undercuts an enumerate-everything pitch, so the honest citation is also the useful one. MK31 designs for these teams without chasing them. The cost is that every quote is re-fetched and string-matched first, because a research pass here has fabricated quotations before. If regulated buyers report a written internal requirement for span-level review, the claim becomes buyers require, with the buyer named."},{"id":"L21","cat":"Legal, privacy & data","sub":"Citations","weight":"medium","q":"Do we adopt the rule that an unverifiable citation is dropped rather than repeated, starting with the gazette number?","lede":"Four governing documents cite one instrument four ways, and every legal deadline derives from it.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"PIB release, marked [SS]","note":"the registries were unreachable","tone":"warn"},{"label":"PRD v2: 846(E), 14 Nov 2025","note":"derives 14 May 2027","tone":"warn"},{"label":"BUSINESS and RECORD: 846(E), 13 Nov","note":"the marker was dropped","tone":"stop"},{"label":"The current plan: 843(E), 13 Nov","note":"a third number appears","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Every deadline in the plan's operating rules","note":"all of them derive from this date","tone":"stop"}],"perRow":3,"note":"The instruction to open the Gazette or drop the number is at PRD v2 line 5982","caption":"One unopened source, four citations, every legal deadline downstream"},"stakes":"A visible inconsistency in the documents shown to counsel, on the date that fixes every legal deadline.","state":["The plan cites G.S.R. 843(E); two other documents cite 846(E).","A fourth dates it 14 November 2025 and derives commencement a day later.","The instruction was already recorded: open the Gazette or drop the number."],"tension":["62% of load-bearing claims needed correction once a primary source was opened.","13 versus 14 November shifts the eighteen-month tranche, and the 247-day headline with it."],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"No rule; treat each citation on its merits","gains":["No process to maintain"],"costs":["And a 62% correction rate on load-bearing claims"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Standardise on 846(E) because three documents use it","gains":["Fastest to apply"],"costs":["Resolves by majority rather than by source","That is how a wrong number becomes a settled one"],"system":"A search and replace.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt the rule but keep unverified details behind an explicit marker","what":"The number stays behind the `[SS]` search-summary marker, which travels with every copy.","gains":["Preserves the lead for a later verification pass"],"costs":["The marker is only as good as the reader's attention","Three of four documents already dropped it while copying"],"system":"A marker convention and a check.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Adopt the rule and apply it now: one attempt at the Gazette, then one number or none","gains":["Fixes the date every legal rule derives from","Gives every future citation a default"],"costs":["Loses the lead for anyone verifying later","About an hour, plus edits in four documents"],"system":"A citation rule in the docs conventions.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The rule matters more than the number, because it covers every unverified claim we cite.","The correction rate says there are many, and the gazette number is the test case."],"flip":"If unverified leads are worth preserving, keep the marker, but only under a rule that it travels.","linked":["L23","L7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:541","docs/BUSINESS.md:26","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:8357","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:4516","docs/FRONTMATTER-PRD-v2-2026-08-29.md:5982","docs/DECIDE.md:212","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:531"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"A citation rule is a working practice to adopt, not a product choice."},{"id":"N1","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"The word","weight":"critical","q":"Do we keep the word \"frontmatter\", qualify it, or leave it?","lede":"The name is taken in the VS Code store our buyer uses, and every week-1 asset spends it.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Keep bare","tone":"stop"},{"title":"Qualify","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Coin","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Whoever used the name first, from 2019","cells":["Collision left unmanaged","Still adjacent to theirs","Collision gone"]},{"label":"Bare-word search","cells":["Owned by the concept","Brand-modified only","Ownable from day one"]},{"label":"The two best qualifiers","cells":["n/a","CMS and Studio both taken","n/a"]},{"label":"npm and GitHub","cells":["Both taken","Handles free 2026-08-29","stetfile 404 on both"]},{"label":"Teaching cost","cells":["None","A sentence per conversation","A sentence per asset"]},{"label":"What the naming section says","cells":["Highest risk, extra steps","Rank 1 and 2","Rank 1 until clearance"]}],"note":"\"Frontmatter Studio\" was registered by somebody else on 2026-09-07.","caption":"The three routes, on the axes the naming reports actually measured"},"stakes":"Ship bare and we carry all the confusion and trademark risk of a live 2019 extension, for nothing.","state":["The plan uses the word bare and quotes Front Matter CMS's 82,265 installs up front.","That extension is alive: v10.12.0 shipped 2026-08-21, and it owns the `@frontmatter` npm scope.","The plan has no search channel, and its seven founder decisions omit the name."],"tension":["The best spoken qualifier is gone: somebody registered \"Frontmatter Studio\" on 2026-09-07.","What remains, the bare name on a qualified handle, is what the naming report itself calls highest-risk.","The SEO argument serves a channel we do not use."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The incumbent, re-measured","items":[["VS Code installs","82,265","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132, round 2 on 2026-09-08; was 80,605 read 2026-08-29"],["Latest release","v10.12.0, 2026-08-21","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132 (alive, not abandoned)"],["Marketplace rating","5.0 from 20 ratings","22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:185"],["GitHub stars","2,539","e6-naming-risk.md:2 (estruyf/vscode-front-matter, created 2019-08-23)"],["\"Frontmatter Studio\"","taken 2026-09-07","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132 (the qualifier §52.3 ranked best in the spoken name)"],["Marketplace listings the incumbent holds","3: \"Front Matter CMS\", a \"(BETA)\" listing, \"Front Matter CMS - Theme\"","e6-naming-risk.md:87; named at COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:12496"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Where the name already lives","cols":["Surface","Holder","Source"],"rows":[["npm `frontmatter`","dead 2022 parser, v0.0.3, 11,212 dl/mo of transitive noise","22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:188"],["npm `@frontmatter` scope","the incumbent's","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132"],["GitHub org `frontmatter`","Front Matter CMS, created 2021-09-21","22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:187"],["frontmatter.codes","the incumbent's homepage","e6-naming-risk.md:2"],["frontmatter.io","live company, Fremont CA, 11-50 staff","e6-naming-risk.md:7"],["frontmatter.cc","AI agent-skills / MCP config builder","e6-naming-risk.md:7"],["frontmatter.com","no product surfaced; registration unverified","COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:7755"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How crowded the word is as a search term","items":[["gray-matter, npm downloads/month","35,782,970","COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:6386, api.npmjs.org 2026-07-29 to 08-27"],["front-matter, npm downloads/month","18,413,979","same source"],["The two combined","54,196,949","[derived] 35,782,970 + 18,413,979, computed 2026-09-09"],["GitHub repos with \"frontmatter\" in the name","564","e6-naming-risk.md:69"],["GitHub repos with \"front-matter\" in the name","319","e6-naming-risk.md:69"],["Incumbent's rank for the bare word after 7 years","not in the top results","e6-naming-risk.md:97"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the funnel would need if search ever mattered","items":[["Cumulative visitors for the plan's revenue target","1,260,000","BUSINESS.md:353 (a settled figure)"],["Free signups implied","113,507","BUSINESS.md:245"],["Channels in the plan that are search","0","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305-310"],["Marketing-site keyword map","none written","r14-g1-final-gap-sweep.md:14 (marked missing)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep \"Frontmatter\" bare","gains":["No teaching cost; the word already names what we edit","No week-1 asset has to be reworked or reprinted before the pages already scheduled go up."],"costs":["The one configuration all three naming reports argue against"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Nothing now; a worse-priced rename later.","what":"Keep frontmatter bare. No teaching cost, because the word already names what we edit; the one configuration all three naming reports argue against, in the store our buyer uses."},{"k":"b","label":"Qualify the spoken name, and not with \"CMS\" or \"Studio\"","gains":["Keeps the word's teaching value; handles measured free 2026-08-29","Costs nothing to try first, since a qualifier is a copy change, not a rename across the codebase."],"costs":["Both best qualifiers gone: CMS years ago, Studio 2026-09-07","A disambiguation sentence in every conversation, permanently"],"system":"Package and org handles change; the engine is untouched.","screens":"Wordmark, launcher title, desktop window title.","money":"One domain and a handle set.","what":"Qualify the spoken name, and not with CMS or Studio. Keeps the word's teaching value; both best qualifiers are gone, and a disambiguation sentence follows every conversation permanently."},{"k":"c","label":"Coin a name; keep \"frontmatter\" as the word for the block we edit","gains":["Kills the collision and the search problem outright","`stetfile` was 404 on npm and GitHub, 2026-08-29"],"costs":["One sentence of category teaching in every asset","`stetmark` shipped 2026-08-05 on the same metaphor; re-check npm and GitHub"],"system":"Rename two published packages and 169 src strings.","screens":"Every wordmark, the launcher, the desktop title.","money":"A domain, handles, a second clearance search.","what":"Coin a name and keep frontmatter as the word for the block we edit. Kills the collision and the search problem outright; one sentence of category teaching in every asset."},{"k":"d","label":"Flip the hierarchy: MDMAX becomes the brand","gains":["No new clearance: npm `mdmax` was 404 on 2026-08-30","Also settles the two-names question"],"costs":["\"md\" reads as a medical degree to non-developers","The research rates it a library name, not a product"],"system":"One package name survives; product strings change.","screens":"Wordmark everywhere.","money":"The cheapest of the three changes.","what":"Flip the hierarchy so MDMAX becomes the brand. No new clearance and it settles the two-names question; md reads as a medical degree, and the research rates it a library name."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Both documented same-market precedents ended in a rename: Firefox twice while small, Riot.im after launch.","Obsidian's namesakes were in other markets; ours is in the same store, and renaming costs 169 strings today."],"flip":"If an attorney's knock-out is clean and a qualifier is free, the naming report says a coined name costs more.","linked":["FF1","N2","N3","N4","N6"],"sources":["merged: N8","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:13","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305-310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:359-369","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:58","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:50","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132","docs/BUSINESS.md:245","docs/BUSINESS.md:353","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:185","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:187","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:188","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:219","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:223","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:225","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:10","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:69","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:87","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:97","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:110-111","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:29","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:63","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:112","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:117","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r14-g1-final-gap-sweep.md:14","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:6386","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:69","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:106","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:113","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:110"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The landing page, the launcher and the badge all carry the name. It cannot wait.","recWhy":"The name is taken in the VS Code store our buyer uses: Front Matter CMS has 82,265 installs, shipped v10.12.0 on 21 August, and owns the @frontmatter npm scope, and every week-1 asset spends our word against it. The best spoken qualifier went on 7 September when somebody registered Frontmatter Studio, and the bare name on a qualified handle is what the naming report calls highest-risk. Both documented same-market precedents ended in a rename, Firefox twice while small and Riot.im after launch; Obsidian's namesakes were in other markets, ours is in the same store. Renaming costs 169 strings today and a migration later, which is why Q16 puts the name before Phase 0 ships. Coining kills the collision and the search problem for one sentence of teaching per asset, and frontmatter stays the word for the block we edit. A clean attorney knock-out plus a free qualifier makes (b) cheaper.","dupes":{"dropped":["MK3"],"areas":["Market & competition"],"why":"Market & competition asked the same question and recommended the same answer, option c here: take a clean coined name. Its fourth option, approaching the incumbent's author first, is a separate card in this area.","agree":true}},{"id":"N2","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"Timing and order","weight":"critical","q":"When does the name freeze, and is it decided before or after the form factor?","lede":"The name is spent one asset at a time, and the form-factor decision scores a cost a rename would erase.","visual":{"kind":"state","states":[{"label":"Free to rename","meta":"169 strings in 44 of 248 src files","via":"","tone":"good","current":true},{"label":"Search seeded","meta":"three landing pages, weeks 1-2","via":"week 1","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Installed","meta":"a signed build on a stranger's machine","via":"the pilot","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Breaking","meta":"a customer CNAME to pages.frontmatter.app","via":"after pilot","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Only the last two are strings somebody other than us is holding.","caption":"What a rename costs at each step, and where it stops being an edit"},"stakes":"After the first install, a rename migrates preferences, keychains and an update channel on other people's machines.","state":["A public prototype carries the name; weeks 1-2 add three landing pages.","One string is declared permanent in writing: the API error-type URIs.","No rename tripwires exist; the form-factor decision counts the name against the VS Code extension."],"tension":["It is circular: the form-factor decision counts the name, and the name waits for it.","Rename cost is not flat; the plan treats the name as an agenda item.","Three further strings are widely assumed permanent and the plan asserts it nowhere."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What a rename costs, by artefact, and how well each verdict is evidenced","cols":["Artifact","Cost of changing it later","Source and evidence class"],"rows":[["Source files carrying the word","Low: 169 occurrences in 44 of 248 src files","re-measured in src/ on 2026-09-09 [measured]"],["Public prototype URL","Low: one Vercel deployment","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:237"],["Three landing pages, weeks 1-2","Low, but they seed search results we would abandon","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352 [inference on the seeding]"],["API error-type URIs at frontmatter.dev/errors/","Breaking, in the plan's own words","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:627 [quoted: the only permanence the plan states]"],["`*.frontmatter.app` published pages","Breaking for customers who have CNAMEd to pages.frontmatter.app","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1679, 1683 [the CNAME flow is specified; the breakage is inference]"],["Desktop bundle identifier `ai.sgnk.md`","Assumed permanent: it is the OS's key for preferences, keychain and the signed update channel","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:5; the plan says only that it \"must be re-identified\" (PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104) [unverified against platform docs]"],["GitHub App name \"Frontmatter\"","Assumed permanent: the plan registers 3 distinct App names across 4 environments, which implies a shared namespace","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1104, 1610 [inference: the plan never says the App namespace is global, and it was not checked]"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Precedent on rename timing","items":[["Firefox renames before dominance","2 (Phoenix, Firebird)","e6-naming-risk.md:110 (\"survivable and cheap\")"],["Riot.im rename, post-launch","1, community reaction divided","e6-naming-risk.md:111"],["Strings the plan declares permanent in writing","1","DEV-PLAN.md:627"],["Rename tripwires written into the plan","0","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md (no gate in any exhibit)"]]},{"type":"table","title":"The circle, in five lines","cols":["Document","Says","Line"],"rows":[["GAPS §G item 2","decide the form factor, with the name collision as one input","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:100"],["GAPS §G item 3","then resolve the name: keep, rename, or scope it to the desktop product","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:101"],["Plan, decision 6","the name collision argues against the extension","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"],["Plan, Exhibit 20","seven decisions listed; the name is not one of them","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:359-369"],["GAPS, founder decisions","\"the name, now a legal question and not only a marketing one\"","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:150"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What decision 6 weighs","items":[["Arguments for the extension","4: byte-exact writes 94/94 and 156/156; portable engine, 3,614 lines; free model picker and MCP; empty niche","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"],["Arguments against","4: save pipeline rewrites; no view-zone API; untested data-loss path; the name","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"],["Of those, ones a rename removes","1","the name"],["Review-state install ceiling in that store","400","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Freeze the name this week, before the landing pages go up","gains":["Ends the circle, so the form-factor decision scores honestly","Nothing is spent under a name we later abandon"],"costs":["Forces the name choice before the register searches run","Decides the worst-evidenced question in this area in one sitting"],"system":"Build-plan and desktop-config strings all settle this week.","screens":"Wordmark on all nine prototype screens.","money":"Domains and handles bought once.","what":"Freeze the name this week, before the landing pages go up. Ends the circle so the surface decision scores honestly; it forces the choice before the register searches run."},{"k":"b","label":"Freeze at the first artefact a third party holds","what":"The gate binds at the first signed build and the first customer CNAME, not before.","gains":["Draws the gate on strings we do not hold","Both artefacts already have dates in the build plan"],"costs":["The landing pages seed search results we may abandon","Tripwires work only if somebody owns watching them"],"system":"The bundle identifier and publishing wildcard become the gate.","screens":"None until the gate fires.","money":"None now."},{"k":"c","label":"Write the naming section's tripwire list, rename when one fires, no date","what":"Counsel clean, no objection, listings accepted, handles secured; any one firing triggers the rename.","gains":["Keeps the option open while facts are still arriving","The list is already drafted"],"costs":["A name gets spent by accident when nobody watches","No date means no forcing function"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Form factor first; the name inherits whatever the surface requires","gains":["Follows the ordering already written down","Costs nothing today; no name work happens until the surface itself is chosen."],"costs":["Scores the extension with a cost that may not exist","Lets a surface choice set a company asset"],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"None.","what":"Form factor first, and the name inherits whatever the surface requires. Follows the written ordering; it scores the extension with a cost that may not exist and lets a surface set a company asset."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Before the first install a rename is search-and-replace: 169 occurrences, 44 of 248 files, measured 2026-09-09.","What cannot be undone is a string somebody else holds: a notarised app, a customer CNAME."],"flip":"If the weeks-1-2 landing pages are meant to generate press and backlinks rather than recruit ten people, freeze this week.","linked":["N1","N3","N6"],"sources":["merged: N10","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:237","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:309","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:359-369","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:100","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:101","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:150","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:627","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1104","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1610","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1679","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1683","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:5","docs/FRONTMATTER-MASTER-PLAN-2026-08-28.md:182","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:12541","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:110-111","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:130","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:100-101"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A freeze date stops the name being reopened mid build.","dependsOn":["N1"],"recWhy":"The name is spent one asset at a time, and the surface decision was scoring a rename cost against the VS Code extension while the name waited on the surface, which is circular. Before the first install a rename is search-and-replace, 169 occurrences in 44 of 248 files measured on 9 September. What cannot be undone is a string somebody else holds, a notarised app, a customer CNAME, a bookmark a stranger keeps. Under Q3 the first artefact a third party holds is the open editor at / that Phase 0 ships to strangers, so the freeze lands before Phase 0 ships. That is exactly where Q16 puts it, and it is the same rule as this card rather than a different one. N3's register read happens first, and N6 keeps the identifier name-free. If the landing pages are meant to earn press and backlinks rather than recruit ten people, freeze this week."},{"id":"N3","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"Clearance","weight":"critical","q":"Do we read the registers ourselves this week, buy an attorney search, or neither before the pilot?","lede":"One EU word mark has been reported, nobody has opened it, and every register is still unchecked.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"Five registries blocked","note":"the research machine's allowlist","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Read them from an open network","note":"an hour, no money","tone":"accent","edge":"then"},{"label":"Does the EU mark exist?","note":"the only mark anyone has reported","tone":"neutral","edge":"then"},{"label":"Yes: attorney the same day","note":"no longer a cost question","tone":"warn"},{"label":"No: attorney on the shortlist","note":"the money follows a fact","tone":"good"},{"label":"Then anything public","note":"landing pages, listings, domains","tone":"neutral"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Nothing here substitutes for counsel, and none of it may be reported as clearance.","caption":"The blocker was a network allowlist, not a budget"},"stakes":"Choosing between a qualified word and a coinage without clearance risks paying for the rename twice.","state":["The naming report lists TESS, EUIPO, IP India, WIPO and every domain unchecked.","Nobody reproduced the reported FRONTMATTER EU word mark; five registries were blocked.","US registration is doubtful: the word may be refused as descriptive, an inference, not counsel."],"tension":["The blocker was the network, not the budget. Five registries were unreachable.","So \"not cleared\" is a tooling fact being carried as a cost decision.","If the word is too descriptive to register, we cannot defend it either."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What is known about the mark, and what is not","items":[["US registration","none surfaced","e6-naming-risk.md:84 (search-summary only, \"snippets cannot prove a negative\")"],["EU registration","one reported, never reproduced","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146 (five registries blocked)"],["Benelux (the incumbent is Belgian)","unchecked","e6-naming-risk.md:85 (named as the relevant home registry)"],["India / IP India TMR","unchecked","22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:229"],["Cost of a knock-out search","no figure anywhere in the corpus","grepped 2026-09-09 (every hit is the recommendation, none a price)"],["US registrability of \"frontmatter\"","Section 2(e)(1) descriptiveness refusal risk","e6-naming-risk.md:86 (tagged [inference], not counsel)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Pay an attorney for a knock-out search now, on two names","gains":["The only thing that actually settles the question","Unblocks the naming, freeze and identifier decisions at once"],"costs":["Spends before any demand signal, on a name we may drop","No price in the research to budget against"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Unbudgeted; the research has no figure.","what":"Pay an attorney for a knock-out search now, on two names. The only thing that settles the question and unblocks three decisions at once; spends before any demand signal, with no price budgeted."},{"k":"b","label":"Read the registers ourselves first, then buy counsel on what survives","gains":["The cheapest item on this entire list","The money then follows a fact, not a rumour"],"costs":["Not clearance, and must never be reported as clearance","A free search cannot prove a negative"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Nothing this week.","what":"Read the registers ourselves first, then buy counsel on what survives. The cheapest item on this list, and the money then follows a fact; not clearance, and never to be reported as clearance."},{"k":"c","label":"Skip clearance; rely on descriptiveness and no enforcement posture","gains":["The incumbent shows no mark symbol and no enforcement","The only option that spends no attorney fee before any demand signal exists."],"costs":["The evidence we have is that nobody could open a register","Implies we cannot register either, which forced Riot.im's rename"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None, until a rename.","what":"Skip clearance and rely on descriptiveness and the incumbent's no-enforcement posture. Nothing spent; the evidence is that nobody could open a register, and unregistrable also means undefendable."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer until after the two-week tests","gains":["Spends nothing before evidence anyone wants the product","The two-week tests do not need a cleared name"],"costs":["Public assets go up under a name of unknown legal status","The one thing all three reports told us not to do"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None now, more later.","what":"Defer until after the two-week tests. Nothing spent before evidence anyone wants the product; public assets go up under a name of unknown legal status, the one thing all three reports said not to do."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["A network allowlist stopped the research, not money, and one blocked registry holds the only reported mark.","An hour on an open connection produces that record or kills it. Buy counsel on what survives."],"flip":"A live mark in an overlapping class turns this from a cost question into an attorney engagement the same day.","linked":["N1","N2","N4","N6","N9"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:229","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:84","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:85","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:86","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:89","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:111","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:154","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r13-s6-remaining-unknowns.md:149","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r13-s6-remaining-unknowns.md:177"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"A register check is cheap and it is due before the name goes on a public page.","dependsOn":["N1"],"recWhy":"One EU word mark for FRONTMATTER has been reported and nobody has reproduced it, because five registries were unreachable from the research network; TESS, EUIPO, IP India, WIPO and every domain are still listed unchecked. That is a tooling fact being carried as a cost decision. An hour on an open connection either produces that record or kills it, and the money for counsel then follows a fact rather than a rumour, on the two names that survive: the coined one Q16 prefers and, per N4, MDMAX. A free search is not clearance and cannot prove a negative, so it is reported as a read, never as cleared. If the word is too descriptive to register, we cannot defend it either, which is what forced Riot.im's rename. N9's email to the incumbent's author waits on this read by a week. A live mark in an overlapping class turns this into an attorney engagement the same day.","dupes":{"dropped":["L15","R10"],"areas":["Legal, privacy & data","Research & evidence"],"why":"Legal, privacy & data and Research & evidence both recommended paying an attorney for a knock-out search on two names now, option a here, rather than reading the registers ourselves first. The legal card's rename options are covered by the name decision in this area.","agree":false}},{"id":"N4","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"Two names","weight":"high","q":"Do we keep two names, MDMAX for the engine and certificate and another for the product, or collapse to one?","lede":"The licence chosen for the engine only works if MDMAX is a mark we can defend, and nobody checked.","visual":{"kind":"arch","layers":[{"label":"Engine","items":["mdmax, 13 files, 3,614 lines","npm 404 on 2026-08-30"],"side":"the word the licence withholds","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Packages","items":["mdmax","mdmax-cli"],"side":"two published, one repo","tone":"sunk"},{"label":"Certificate","items":["mdmax cert","7 engines, 15 targets"],"side":"the published claim Apache's trademark clause protects","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Product","items":["the editor"],"side":"the name under review in the keep, qualify or coin decision","tone":"warn"}],"note":"Product code reaches exactly one symbol of the engine today.","caption":"Which layer owns a name a fork cannot use"},"stakes":"Without a defensible engine mark, a fork can issue rendering certificates under a name we cannot contest.","state":["Two packages already ship: `mdmax` and `mdmax-cli`, from one repo.","`mdmax` was 404 on npm on 2026-08-30; `frontmatter` returned 200.","Apache-2.0 was chosen for one reason: it withholds trademark rights from a fork."],"tension":["MDMAX has had no more clearance than the product name.","Two names double the teaching, and the one carrying legal weight is never spoken.","The certificate may not survive: its one customer-facing verdict, DESTROY, occurs zero times in 10,176 blocks."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"The two names, as measured","cols":["Name","npm","GitHub","Role today","Source"],"rows":[["mdmax","free (404), confirmed 2026-08-30","user taken: 2 repos, joined 2012-11-28; 18 repos named, none a product","engine, certificate, CLI, MCP server","ENGINE.md:2235; r4-naming-candidates.md:69"],["frontmatter","taken (HTTP 200): dead 2022 v0.0.3 parser","org taken by the incumbent, created 2021-09-21","the product","ENGINE.md:2235; 22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:187-188"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How much product depends on the engine name today","items":[["Files in src/modules/mdmax/","13, 3,614 lines","ENGINE.md:444"],["Symbols product code imports from it","1 (decodeStrict), in 2 files","ENGINE.md:444 (measured)"],["Engine tests passing","851 across 23 files, 1.63s","ENGINE.md:444 (\"tested and unreached\")"],["DESTROY verdicts on the pinned corpus","0 in 10,176 blocks","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:512 (the certificate's own falsifier)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep both; register MDMAX as the house mark","gains":["The certificate gets a mark a fork cannot use","Costs nothing extra: the packages are already named that way"],"costs":["Two clearance searches instead of one, and MDMAX has had none","Both names now need their own clearance search, doubling the attorney work this area's N3 already prices."],"system":"No change; the packages already exist.","screens":"A NOTICE and a mark line on the certificate.","money":"A second knock-out search.","what":"Keep both names and register MDMAX as the house mark. The certificate gets a mark a fork cannot use, at no extra cost because the packages already exist; two clearance searches instead of one."},{"k":"b","label":"Collapse to one name: the product's name is also the engine's","gains":["One mark, one story, one clearance search","Removes the double-teaching cost entirely: one word to learn, one word to search for."],"costs":["Loses the trademark hook the licence rests on","A fork could then certify under a name we cannot contest"],"system":"Rename two published packages and the CLI.","screens":"Certificate output and the CLI banner.","money":"One search instead of two.","what":"Collapse to one name: the product's name is also the engine's. One mark, one story, one search; it loses the trademark hook the licence rests on, and a fork could certify under it."},{"k":"c","label":"Flip: MDMAX is the product, \"frontmatter\" stays the descriptive word","gains":["Settles the name with one we already own","The legally cleanest option in the research"],"costs":["\"md\" reads as a medical degree to non-developers","The research rates it a library name, not a product"],"system":"The engine keeps its packages; product strings change.","screens":"Wordmark everywhere.","money":"No new clearance beyond the one search.","what":"Flip: MDMAX is the product and frontmatter stays the descriptive word. Settles the name with one we own; md reads as a medical degree, and the research rates it a library name."},{"k":"d","label":"Drop MDMAX; publish the engine under the product's scope","gains":["One npm scope to hold, one account to secure","The certificate's own customer-facing verdict has never fired once across 10,176 blocks tested."],"costs":["Concedes the certificate mark entirely","Contradicts a licence decision already written down"],"system":"Republish under a scope; every import path changes.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Drop MDMAX and publish the engine under the product's scope. One npm scope to hold; it concedes the certificate mark entirely and contradicts a licence decision already written down."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The certificate claims things about seven rendering engines, and the licence works only if a mark exists.","It costs nothing extra: the packages exist. Add MDMAX to the attorney's shortlist rather than assuming it is clean."],"flip":"Cut the certificate and the second name is not needed. The deciding test costs a day and is specified.","linked":["N1","N3"],"sources":["docs/ENGINE.md:444","docs/ENGINE.md:2231","docs/ENGINE.md:2235","docs/ENGINE.md:2237","docs/mdmax/PLAN.md:17","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:512","docs/FRONTMATTER-MASTER-PLAN-2026-08-28.md:182","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:229","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:9","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:69"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Two names or one matters when the engine is published.","recWhy":"Two packages already ship from one repository, mdmax and mdmax-cli, and Apache-2.0 was chosen for the engine for one reason: its section 6 withholds trademark rights, so a fork cannot issue certificates under the mark. That only works if MDMAX is a mark we can defend, and it has had no more clearance than the product name; mdmax was 404 on npm on 30 August, which is availability, not clearance. Two names double the teaching, an accepted cost because the engine name is for the certificate and the CLI, not the pitch. E24 and L20 both rest on this. The certificate itself may not survive, since its one customer-facing verdict occurs zero times in 10,176 blocks, and the deciding test costs a day. Add MDMAX to the attorney's shortlist in N3 rather than assuming it is clean. If the certificate is cut, the second name is not needed and (b) follows."},{"id":"N5","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"Who decides","weight":"high","q":"Does the name need a second founder's signature, or does the commit author decide and register to the entity?","lede":"The name is a founder decision, and the founder-count question is past its own 2026-09-07 deadline.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"Today","lines":[{"t":"The name is a founder decision","mark":"warn"},{"t":"Founder count open since 2026-08-31","mark":"stop"},{"t":"Its own deadline, 2026-09-07, passed","mark":"stop"},{"t":"Domain, npm scope and mark all wait","mark":"stop"}]},"after":{"title":"Registered to the entity","lines":[{"t":"The commit author decides","mark":"good"},{"t":"The company holds every string","mark":"good"},{"t":"An equity split can still change","mark":"good"},{"t":"No assignment to unwind later","mark":"good"}]},"via":"register to the company, never to a person","note":"The registrant and the equity split are different questions.","caption":"Why the founder count stops blocking the name"},"stakes":"A mark and a domain held personally do not fit a two-founder story later without an assignment.","state":["One author on all 80 commits here and all 416 on sgnk-md.","The project record says: \"One person. This is the binding constraint on everything above.\"","The gap list puts the name among four items needing the founders."],"tension":["Waiting on an overdue signature blocks the domain, the identifier and the clearance search.","An assignment is cheaper before there is a mark than after.","Only the registrant, not the equity split, has to be settled this week."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Who is on the record","items":[["Commits on this repo","80 of 80, one author","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231"],["Commits on the sibling","416 of 416, one author","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231"],["Total, one author","496","[derived] 80 + 416, computed 2026-09-09"],["Cofounder named anywhere in the record","0: grepped across 16,189 lines when that audit ran; the file is 18,344 lines today and the claim has not been re-run","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:13652"],["Deadline set for the founder count","2026-09-07","CRITIQUE.md:2100 (passed)"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week","1.21","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132 (a one-person rate)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"The commit author decides; every registration goes to the entity","gains":["Unblocks the name, the domains and the clearance search this week","The split can change; the registrant need not"],"costs":["Needs the entity to exist before the first registration","Decides a founder-listed item without the second founder"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"Registrations billed to the company, not reimbursed.","what":"The commit author decides, and every registration goes to the entity. Unblocks the name, the domains and the clearance search this week, and the split can change later; the entity has to exist first."},{"k":"b","label":"Hold the name until a signed founders' agreement exists","gains":["No decision a second founder could object to","Costs nothing today, and an assignment made later is still cheaper than one made after a mark exists."],"costs":["Blocks four downstream decisions on a signature overdue since 2026-09-07","Asked for since 2026-08-31"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None, and no progress.","what":"Hold the name until a signed founders' agreement exists. Nothing a second founder could object to; four downstream decisions wait on a signature overdue since 7 September."},{"k":"c","label":"Decide the name now, record it as reversible until the agreement exists","gains":["Progress on the name without foreclosing the founder question","Unblocks the domain and identifier work in N6 without waiting on the same signature."],"costs":["Contradicts the freeze, which exists so the name stops being reversible","A reversible name gets spent anyway"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Decide the name now and record it as reversible until the agreement exists. Progress without foreclosing the founder question; it contradicts the freeze, which exists to end reversibility."},{"k":"d","label":"Register personally, assign to the entity later","gains":["Cheapest thing to do today","The entity need not exist before the first registration"],"costs":["Most expensive if a second founder is real","Assignments of marks are a filing, not an email"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"A later assignment cost.","what":"Register personally and assign to the entity later. Cheapest today, and the entity need not exist first; most expensive if a second founder is real, and mark assignments are a filing, not an email."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One author on 496 of 496 commits; the project record's own audit found no second name.","Registering to the entity makes the founder count irrelevant: the company owns the mark whatever the split."],"flip":"A signed agreement naming a person with a start date and an hours commitment; registering to the entity still stands.","linked":["N1","N2","N6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:4854","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:13652","docs/CRITIQUE.md:2100","docs/BRIEF.md:115","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:112","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:132","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:150"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Who signs off follows the naming decision.","recWhy":"One author on all 80 commits here and all 416 on sgnk-md, 496 of 496, and the project record's own audit found no second name. The gap list still puts the name among four items needing the founders, and the founder agreement is overdue since 7 September. Waiting on that signature blocks the domain, the identifier and the clearance search. Registering every name, domain and mark to the entity makes the founder count irrelevant to ownership. The company owns the mark whatever the split turns out to be, and only the registrant, not the equity, has to be settled this week. B1 carries the founder question itself; this card refuses to let the name wait on it. The cost is that the entity has to exist before the first registration. A signed agreement naming a person, a start date and hours changes nothing here, because registering to the entity still stands."},{"id":"N6","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"The permanent strings","weight":"high","q":"Do the bundle identifier and the permanent URIs carry the name, or a string that survives a rename?","lede":"The pilot's app calls itself another product, and the one permanent URI space sits on an unchecked domain.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Carries the brand","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Name-free","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Bundle identifier","cells":["A rename migrates preferences, keychain, updates","Survives every rename; odd in a crash report"]},{"label":"API error-type URIs","cells":["Declared stable forever; changing one is a breaking change","Never moves, whatever the brand becomes"]},{"label":"Publishing wildcard","cells":["Customers CNAME to it; breaking to move","Plainer, and nobody types it"]},{"label":"Marketing site","cells":["The one string a brand should own","Loses the point of having a brand"]},{"label":"GitHub App name","cells":["3 names across 4 environments","Namespace never checked either way"]}],"note":"Only the first three end up held by somebody other than us.","caption":"Which strings other people end up holding"},"stakes":"Bind the brand into the identifier or the error URIs and a rename becomes a migration on other machines.","state":["The desktop config still names the app `sgnk-md`, with identifier `ai.sgnk.md` and frontend `md.sgnk.ai`.","The build plan carries `frontmatter.app` on 17 lines and `frontmatter.dev` on 6, measured 2026-09-09.","The naming report records every domain unchecked: no domain lookup was reachable."],"tension":["The bundle identifier keys preferences, keychain entries and the signed update channel.","The one string declared permanent points at a domain nobody has checked.","The marketing name and the identifier do not have to be the same string."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the desktop app calls itself today","cols":["Field","Value","File:line"],"rows":[["productName","sgnk-md","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3"],["identifier","ai.sgnk.md","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:5"],["frontendDist","https://md.sgnk.ai","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:9"],["window title","sgnk-md","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:18"],["longDescription","\"sgnk-md is a markdown workspace…\"","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:59"],["crate name","sgnk-md","src-tauri/Cargo.toml:2"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What signing this identity will cost, and how deep the fork goes","items":[["Apple Developer ID","$99/year (budgeted, not yet spent)","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104, :231 (required to notarise the .dmg)"],["Windows code signing","$150-400/year plus a hardware token (budgeted, not yet spent)","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104, :231"],["Auto-updater","must be signed and configured before the first public build","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104"],["Editor files identical to the sibling","41 of 43","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141"],["Files unique to frontmatter in the editor","0","GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Who holds the neighbouring domains","cols":["Domain","Holder","Source"],"rows":[["frontmatter.codes","Front Matter CMS, the incumbent","e6-naming-risk.md:2"],["frontmatter.io","live creative-services company, Fremont CA, 11-50 employees; appears among the top results for the bare word","e6-naming-risk.md:7, :97"],["frontmatter.cc","\"Visual Builder for AI Agent Skills, Prompts & MCP Configs\"","e6-naming-risk.md:7"],["frontmatter.com","no product surfaced; registration status unverified","COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:7755"],["frontmatter.app / frontmatter.dev","assumed by the build plan; never checked","DEV-PLAN.md (17 + 6 lines); 22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:194"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"How deep the assumption goes","items":[["frontmatter.app: lines in DEV-PLAN","17 lines, 19 occurrences","measured 2026-09-09"],["frontmatter.dev: lines in DEV-PLAN","6 lines, 6 occurrences","measured 2026-09-09"],["GitHub App names planned","3 distinct, across 4 environments","DEV-PLAN.md:1610; manifest name \"Frontmatter\" at :1104"],["URI spaces the plan declares unchangeable","1: the error-type URIs, on frontmatter.dev","DEV-PLAN.md:627"],["Domains actually checked","0","22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:194"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Bind everything to the product name now","what":"The identifier, both domains and the App name carry the chosen name.","gains":["Clean and conventional; one string to remember","Every build-plan reference becomes true at once"],"costs":["Forces the name choice before the register searches run","A later rename migrates an installed app and customer DNS"],"system":"Desktop config, 23 build-plan domain lines and the App manifest.","screens":"Desktop window title, About panel, error pages.","money":"Two domains for a name we may replace."},{"k":"b","label":"Name-free identifier, and the permanent URIs on a domain we hold regardless","gains":["Separates what cannot change from what most likely will","Can be set before the first signed build, without the name"],"costs":["One convention decision, and it looks odd in a crash report","A second domain to hold and renew"],"system":"One line in tauri.conf.json; the error-type base URI moves.","screens":"Only the error URI in API responses.","money":"One extra domain a year.","what":"A name-free identifier, and the permanent URIs on a domain we hold regardless. Separates what cannot change from what will, and can be set before the first signed build; a second domain to renew."},{"k":"c","label":"Leave `ai.sgnk.md` and both domains until the name is settled","gains":["No work now, and no decision taken twice","Matches N2's own freeze logic: nothing is spent on a name that has not yet been chosen."],"costs":["Ten strangers install an app naming itself another product","A notarised build carries that identity into the update channel"],"system":"No change.","screens":"The desktop app keeps sgnk-md's title.","money":"None now.","what":"Leave ai.sgnk.md and both domains until the name is settled. No work, no decision taken twice; ten strangers install an app naming itself another product, and a notarised build carries it forward."},{"k":"d","label":"Buy both domains today and collapse the dev plan's 23 lines onto one","gains":["Removes the unchecked assumption immediately","Ends the disagreement about which domain is home"],"costs":["Spends on two domains for a name we may replace","Still leaves the permanent URIs on the brand"],"system":"An hour of editing across 23 lines.","screens":"None.","money":"Two domain registrations.","what":"Buy both domains today and collapse the dev plan's 23 lines onto one. Removes the unchecked assumption and ends the which-domain-is-home disagreement; spends on a name we may replace."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Making the identifier match the marketing name is what turns a naming decision into a migration.","One line fixes the string the plan itself calls breaking; the publishing wildcard is second."],"flip":"If the name freezes this week, the identifier and the brand can be one string and binding everything is simpler.","linked":["N1","N2","N3","N5"],"sources":["merged: N7","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:3","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:5","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:9","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:18","src-tauri/tauri.conf.json:59","src-tauri/Cargo.toml:2","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:104","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:231","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:627","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1104","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1610","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1679","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:1683","docs/DEV-PLAN.md:2381","docs/BUSINESS.md:650","docs/THESIS.md:956","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:141","docs/research/prd-v2-sections/22-segments-disclosure-naming.md:194","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:2","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:7","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:97","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:7755"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The desktop app is not in the pilot. Renaming the bundle id orphans a user’s local drafts, so it moves only behind a migration.","recWhy":"The desktop config still names the app sgnk-md with identifier ai.sgnk.md and frontend md.sgnk.ai, the build plan carries frontmatter.app on 17 lines and frontmatter.dev on 6, and no domain lookup was reachable. The one string the plan declares permanent points at a domain nobody has checked. The bundle identifier keys preferences, keychain entries and the signed update channel, so making it match the marketing name is what turns a naming decision into a migration. A name-free identifier and permanent URIs on a domain we hold regardless separate what cannot change from what most likely will, and can be set before the first signed build without the name. AC10 removes the updater for v1, which makes this cheaper still. If the name freezes this week, the identifier and the brand can be one string and binding everything is simpler.","dupes":{"dropped":["AC9"],"areas":["Access, offline & install"],"why":"Access, offline & install asked the narrower question of the app identifier and recommended the same as option b here: a vendor-scoped identifier now, with the display name free to change. What that means for signing and updates is on the Access, offline & install signing card.","agree":true}},{"id":"N9","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"The incumbent","weight":"high","q":"Do we contact the incumbent's author before deciding the name, and when?","lede":"Elio Struyf has publicly asked for company collaboration, and emailing him has been an open item since 2026-08-28.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2019-08-23","label":"Front Matter CMS created","note":"senior use in the same trade channel","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"2026","label":"Open Source Priorities Update","note":"invites a company commitment","tone":"good"},{"when":"2026-08-21","label":"v10.12.0 shipped","note":"alive, and 82,265 installs","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"2026-08-28","label":"Detente email logged as an open item","note":"in the master plan","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-09-09","label":"Still unsent","note":"twelve days later","tone":"stop"}],"note":"He holds three \"Front Matter\" listings in the store an extension would ship into.","caption":"An open door, logged and not walked through"},"stakes":"An email cannot be unsent, and if a mark exists it changes what that email means.","state":["Nobody has approached him. His README carries a \"2026 Open Source Priorities Update\".","It says open source is not sustainable income, and major changes need \"a company commitment\".","The plan's rule is explicit: \"A maintainer PR is outbound; approval first\"."],"tension":["Contacting creates dated proof we knew before shipping, which a passing-off claim needs.","Not contacting leaves a 2019 extension with three store listings unmanaged.","The 2026-09-09 research brings him closer: git-backed markdown CMS is a distribution channel being weighed."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The incumbent, as a person","items":[["Author","Elio Struyf, Antwerp, Belgium","e6-naming-risk.md:3 (GitHub Star, Microsoft MVP, Google Developer Expert)"],["Project created","2019-08-23","e6-naming-risk.md:2 (senior use in the same trade channel)"],["Marketplace listings held","3: CMS, a (BETA) listing, a Theme","e6-naming-risk.md:87; named at COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:12496"],["™ or ® anywhere in the README","none observed","e6-naming-risk.md:89"],["Enforcement posture observed","none (\"confusion cost, not legal cost, is the binding constraint\")","e6-naming-risk.md:89, tagged [inference]"],["The detente email as an open item","logged 2026-08-28, never sent","FRONTMATTER-MASTER-PLAN-2026-08-28.md:182"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Send a friendly heads-up now, before any public asset carries the name","gains":["The cheapest goodwill move available, and he is sympathetic","Removes the surprise if he sees a listing beside his"],"costs":["Creates a dated record that we knew before we shipped","Cannot be unsent if a mark later turns up"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Send a friendly heads-up now, before any public asset carries the name. The cheapest goodwill move available and he is sympathetic; it creates a dated record that we knew before we shipped."},{"k":"b","label":"Open a sponsorship or brand-licence conversation on his own invitation","gains":["Could settle the whole question at freelance-budget scale","Uses an invitation open since his 2026 update"],"costs":["Commits us to the name before it is chosen or cleared","A recurring cost with no revenue behind it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"A sponsorship, sized by him.","what":"Open a sponsorship or brand-licence conversation on his own invitation. Could settle the whole question at freelance-budget scale; it commits us to the name before it is chosen or cleared."},{"k":"c","label":"Do nothing and avoid creating the record","gains":["No dated admission of prior knowledge","Matches the plan's own rule that a maintainer approach is outbound and needs approval first."],"costs":["Leaves a live name clash in that store unmanaged","Forfeits an invitation open since his 2026 update"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do nothing and avoid creating the record. No dated admission of prior knowledge; it leaves a live name clash in that store unmanaged and forfeits an open invitation."},{"k":"d","label":"Contact only after the registers have been read","gains":["Delays a friendly email by a week and loses nothing","He has been asking since his 2026 update"],"costs":["One more week of a live collision unmanaged","Depends on the registers being read"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Contact him only after the registers have been read. Delays a friendly email by a week and loses nothing; one more week of a live collision unmanaged."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["The view that confusion, not litigation, is the real risk is inference from searches that opened no register.","One EU mark is reported and unreproduced. Checking registers takes an hour, so the delay is one week."],"flip":"A clean clearance makes sponsorship attractive: he is asking for exactly the collaboration that would settle this permanently.","linked":["N1","N3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/FRONTMATTER-MASTER-PLAN-2026-08-28.md:182","docs/FRONTMATTER-COMPLETE-RECORD-2026-08-30.md:12496","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:2","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:3","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:52","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:84","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:87","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-28/e6-naming-risk.md:89","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r7/x4-external-frontier-importer-naming.md:141","docs/research/2026-09-09/BRIEFING.md:291"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Contacting the incumbent follows the naming decision.","recWhy":"Elio Struyf's README carries a 2026 Open Source Priorities Update that says open source is not sustainable income and that major changes need a company commitment. He has publicly asked for the kind of collaboration that would settle this, and emailing him has been an open item since 28 August. The tension is real: contacting him creates dated proof we knew before shipping, which a passing-off claim needs, and not contacting leaves a 2019 extension with three store listings unmanaged. The view that confusion rather than litigation is the real risk is an inference from searches that opened no register, and one EU mark is reported and unreproduced. N3's read takes an hour, so the delay is one week and it changes what the email says. A clean clearance makes sponsorship attractive, because he is asking for exactly the collaboration that would settle this permanently."},{"id":"N11","cat":"Name & identity","sub":"Testing","weight":"medium","q":"Is the name a variable in the weeks-1-2 tests, or do we decide it without asking anyone?","lede":"Fifteen people are already being recruited, and not one will be asked what the name means.","visual":{"kind":"funnel","steps":[{"label":"Candidates generated","n":41,"unit":"names","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Probed on npm and GitHub","n":15,"unit":"names","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Scored against criteria","n":2,"unit":"names","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Shown to anyone outside this project","n":0,"unit":"names","tone":"stop"}],"note":"The zero is a search of the corpus, done 2026-09-09.","caption":"Forty-one names, and nobody outside the project has seen one"},"stakes":"The name gets settled on registry probes and taste, with no evidence from anyone outside the project.","state":["Weeks 1-2 run four tests across fifteen recruits plus 20 team conversations.","The tested variable is three headline variants. The name is in no test.","A five-second screenshot test is already in the script."],"tension":["Testing the name on landing pages is clean, but those pages settle the headline.","If the name freezes before week 1, asking becomes post-hoc justification."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Naming evidence in the corpus","items":[["Names scored against criteria","2: a coinage and a qualified form","r13-s4-positioning-messaging.md:129-136 (4 of 6 criteria are pure judgment, 2 rest on registry probes)"],["Candidate names generated","41","r4-naming-candidates.md:29 (\"41 names, with strategy\"); an earlier draft of this card said 61, which the file does not support"],["Candidates probed for npm/GitHub availability","15","r4-naming-candidates.md:63 (\"Availability table — top 15\", measured 2026-08-29)"],["Strangers asked what any candidate name means","0","no record found anywhere in the corpus, searched 2026-09-09"],["People already being recruited for weeks 1-2","10 developers, 5 who bill for documents, 20 team conversations","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352"],["Existing five-second test in the script","1: what the blue highlight means","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Add one question to the script already running","what":"Ask what a tool called X would do, and whether they have seen it before.","gains":["No extra recruiting, ten seconds a person","It would be the only naming evidence anyone has"],"costs":["A weak signal from a small n, reported that way","Adds to a script already carrying four tests"],"system":"No change.","screens":"One line in the interview script.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Run one landing page per candidate name and compare","gains":["A cleaner read than any interview gives","Would give a numeric read on each candidate name rather than an opinion in an interview."],"costs":["Spends a headline test, the reason the two weeks exist","Confounds the name with the headline it carries"],"system":"No change.","screens":"Three landing pages re-cut.","money":"None beyond the pages already planned.","what":"Run one landing page per candidate name and compare. A cleaner read than any interview; it spends a headline test, the reason the two weeks exist, and confounds the name with the headline it carries."},{"k":"c","label":"Do not test the name; decide on clearance and collision alone","gains":["Defensible: those are the arguments that carry weight","Keeps the two weeks pointed at the headline"],"costs":["Leaves a founder decision with zero evidence behind it","N3's clearance search and N9's maintainer question are the only two inputs this ever gets."],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Do not test the name; decide on clearance and collision alone. Defensible, since those are the arguments that carry weight; a founder decision with zero evidence behind it."},{"k":"d","label":"Test only the confusion: show his listing and ask if it is the same product","gains":["Sizes the confusion cost the reports call the binding constraint","Cheapest of the three test options: one mock listing, not three separate landing pages."],"costs":["Answers nothing about whether an alternative name would land","Needs a mock listing that does not exist"],"system":"No change.","screens":"A mock store listing for the test.","money":"None.","what":"Test only the confusion: show his listing and ask if it is the same product. Sizes the confusion cost the reports call the binding constraint; it needs a mock listing that does not exist."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["Fifteen people are already recruited and the question takes ten seconds: the cheapest evidence available.","Separate pages would use up a headline test, and those tests are why the two weeks exist."],"flip":"If the name is frozen before week 1, drop the question rather than run it for comfort.","linked":["N1","N2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:411","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:124","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r13-s4-positioning-messaging.md:129-136","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:29","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r4-naming-candidates.md:63"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Testing the name with people comes with the pilot.","recWhy":"Fifteen people are already being recruited for the weeks 1 and 2 tests and twenty teams for the conversations, and not one will be asked what the name means. The tested variable is three headline variants, and a five-second screenshot test is already in the script. One question, what would a tool called X do, and is it the same product as the listing beside it, takes ten seconds a person, needs no extra recruiting, and would be the only naming evidence anyone has. It is a weak signal from a small n and is reported that way. Separate landing pages per name would spend a headline test, and those tests are why the two weeks exist, so (b) is out. Q16 asks the founders to choose between keeping, qualifying and coining, and this is the one cheap piece of evidence that can arrive before they do. If the name is frozen before week 1, the question is dropped rather than run for comfort."},{"id":"PL2","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Schedule","weight":"critical","q":"Which schedule does the plan publish: twelve calendar weeks, or the measured 1.21 engineering days a week, which makes it 58?","lede":"One document carries both. They differ fivefold, and four competitors shipped this surface in nine weeks.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2026-07-03","label":"VS Code merges review/unreview","note":"PR #324218, milestone 1.128.0","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-24","label":"Antigravity ships Agent Edits pane","note":"working-tree diff, free","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-08-28","label":"Notion ships per-change approval","note":"agent edits, per change","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-09-03","label":"Claude Code v2.1.260 diff panel","note":"opens beside the conversation","tone":"warn"},{"when":"2026-09-08","label":"Our rate measured at 1.21 days a week","note":"10 of 58 calendar days touched src/","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"Week 12","label":"Twelve weeks buys 14.5 days","note":"the fix-first list alone is 25.5","tone":"stop"},{"when":"Week 58","label":"The pilot at the measured rate","note":"37 weeks at the best rate ever seen","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Competitor dates from the research briefing, all opened; rates from the plan's technical section.","caption":"Nine weeks of shipping from the field, against a 58-week build."},"stakes":"An impossible date turns every gate and kill line into a number nobody enforces.","state":["The technical plan measures 1.21 engineering days a week, so 37 to 58 weeks.","The twelve-week calendar still puts the ten strangers in weeks 10 to 12.","At that rate twelve weeks buys 14.5 days; the engine fixes alone are 25.5."],"tension":["At the best-ever 1.91 a week, twelve weeks buys 22.9 days.","Four competitors shipped working-tree agent diffs between 2026-07-03 and 2026-09-03.","A dateless plan drifts, and on 8 September the code was 29 days untouched."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Engineering days per calendar week","note":"Re-measured here on 2026-09-08 by git log: 10 distinct calendar days between 2026-07-13 and 2026-09-08 touched src/, src-tauri/ or test/, over a 58-day window, which is 1.21 a week. The sibling's best is the md repo's best-ever regime, in the plan's Technical-plan section.","data":[["Last 30 days here",0.23],["Measured, whole project",1.21],["Sibling md, best ever",1.91],["Needed for 12 weeks",5.83],["Old withdrawn plan",7]],"unit":"eng days / week","threshold":1.21,"thresholdLabel":"measured rate"},{"type":"stat","title":"What twelve weeks actually buys","items":[["Engineering days in 12 weeks at 1.21","14.5","12 × 1.21"],["Engineering days in 12 weeks at 1.91","22.9","12 × 1.91, the best rate ever seen"],["Fix-first list alone","25.5 days","§9 table, rows summed"],["The full pilot at 1.21 a week","58 weeks","70 ÷ 1.21, plan's own figure"],["The full pilot at 1.91 a week","37 weeks","70 ÷ 1.91, plan's own figure"],["Independent estimate multiple on the earlier 41 days","1.6× central, 2.8×, 3.2× worst","CRITIQUE.md:791"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep twelve weeks and commit in writing to 5.8 engineering days a week","gains":["Every existing gate date survives","No new decision to make; the plan keeps every commitment already made to the founders."],"costs":["Nothing supports 5.8 days; the last 30 ran at 0.23","The independent estimate multiple on the last 41 days ran 1.6 to 3.2 times over, not under."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Honest only if client hours go to zero.","what":"Keep twelve weeks and commit in writing to 5.8 engineering days a week. Every existing gate date survives; nothing supports 5.8, and the last thirty days ran at 0.23."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep the scope and publish 37 to 58 weeks","gains":["Truthful, and checkable against the commit log","Matches the plan's own two figures exactly: 37 weeks at the best rate, 58 at the measured one."],"costs":["The pilot lands in 2027 and dated kill lines lose meaning","A dated kill line meant to bite in week 3 loses its meaning against a year-long schedule."],"system":"No change.","screens":"No change.","money":"Consulting covers running costs for a year.","what":"Keep the scope and publish 37 to 58 weeks. Truthful and checkable against the commit log; the pilot lands in 2027 and dated kill lines lose meaning."},{"k":"c","label":"Re-cut the pilot to what 1.91 a week delivers in twelve weeks: 22.9 days","gains":["Forces a hard cut now rather than a slip later","Fits inside the best rate ever measured on the sibling repo, 1.91 days a week, not an assumed one."],"costs":["22.9 days is under the 25.5-day fix-first list","No pilot left to show ten strangers"],"system":"The cut falls below even the engine fixes.","screens":"No feature screens ship.","money":"None.","what":"Re-cut the pilot to what 1.91 days a week delivers in twelve weeks: 22.9 days. Forces a hard cut now; that is under the 25.5-day fix-first list, so no pilot is left to show."},{"k":"d","label":"Publish engineering days and a weekly rate; re-forecast the date at week 4","gains":["Checkable on day 7, not at week 12","A real date from four weeks of data"],"costs":["No external commitment for four weeks","A dateless plan is easy to let drift"],"system":"The plan already prescribes the weekly report.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Publish engineering days and a weekly rate, and re-forecast the date at week 4. Checkable on day 7 and a real date from four weeks of data; no external commitment for a month."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Twelve weeks needs 4.8 times the measured rate, so nobody can plan against it.","The week-4 re-forecast turns a weekly number into a date somebody owns."],"flip":"A named second engineer with a commit record makes 1.21 obsolete; re-derive it rather than doubling it.","linked":["B1","FF1","PL3"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:210","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:229","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:22","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:130","docs/CRITIQUE.md:779","docs/CRITIQUE.md:791","docs/CRITIQUE.md:793"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The pace is one of the five decisions. Every date in the plan rests on which rate is committed.","dependsOn":["B1"],"recWhy":"One document carries a twelve-week calendar and a measured rate of 1.21 engineering days a week, which makes the same scope 37 to 58 weeks; they differ fivefold. Twelve weeks at the measured rate buys 14.5 days, and the engine fixes alone are 25.5, so nobody can plan against the calendar, and at the best-ever 1.91 a week it still buys only 22.9. Four competitors shipped a working-tree agent diff between 3 July and 3 September, and on 8 September this code had been untouched for 29 days, so a dateless plan is a real risk too. Publishing the rate and re-forecasting at week 4 is the only version that is checkable on day 7 and produces a date somebody owns from data rather than hope. Q3's one-surface order and Q14's six fix-first items are what the rate is spent on. A named second engineer with a commit record makes 1.21 obsolete, and the rate is re-derived, not doubled.","dupes":{"dropped":["B3","P18"],"areas":["Business & operations","Product & definition"],"why":"Business & operations recommended much the same as option d here: commit to a weekly density, count achieved days every Friday, re-cut at week 4. Product & definition recommended cutting scope instead, which is option c: fix-first plus review state in the pilot, six features moved to the build after it.","agree":false}},{"id":"PL5","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Exit test","weight":"critical","q":"What does \"the pilot passed\" mean, and how is it observed?","lede":"Three live documents set three different bars, and the proposed instrument measured effectively zero here.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":180,"cols":[{"title":"6 of 10 still using","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"3 ask unprompted","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"3 name it, 2 at day 30","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Measures","cells":["Retention only","Demand only","Demand and retention"]},{"label":"Passable by being polite","cells":["Partly","No","No"]},{"label":"Observed how","cells":["Local file, pasted","Session transcript","Transcript plus day-30 counts"]},{"label":"Needs the paste to work","cells":["Yes","No","For one half"]},{"label":"Where it is written","cells":["The plan's tier table","The product doc's pilot section","the record's own rewrite"]}],"note":"Each bar carries the instrument it needs; the two cannot be chosen separately.","caption":"Three bars, and what each one can actually be read from."},"stakes":"The one gate allowed to stop the product is softer than the bar it replaced, and unmeasurable.","state":["The plan's exit test is six of ten strangers still using it after two weeks.","Usage counts sit in a local file, `.frontmatter/usage.jsonl`, and reach us only if pasted.","Zero interviews have been run across the programme."],"tension":["Hand-filled feedback on our own tools got 7 entries in 694 chances.","Across three measurements that 7 never grew, so new entries ran at zero.","A silent pilot reads as a failed one, and false negatives are expensive."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Three bars, three documents, all current","cols":["Bar","Source","What it measures"],"rows":[["6 of 10 still using after two weeks","plan Exhibit 6","Retention. Observable only via the week-0 measurement decision"],["6 of 10 run it once, and 3 ask unprompted","PRODUCT §95.2 (DECIDE §3.1 carries only the six-of-ten half)","Trial plus unprompted pull. The unprompted clause was dropped"],["3 of 10 name the review-cost problem before it is named, and 2 still using on day 30","the record's own rewrite, RECORD:12352","Demand plus retention. The bar written after attacking the other two"],["40 people, 40% 'very disappointed' and 5 of 40 naming whole-file rewrites, by 2026-12-31","record, Ellis wording, RECORD:12517","Product-market fit at n=40. Needs four times the recruitment"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Where the current bar stands","items":[["Interviews run across the whole programme","0","gap register §C row 1"],["Strangers in the pilot","10","plan Exhibit 19, weeks 10 to 12"],["Measurement decision status","drafted, not accepted","WEEK0-DECISIONS §1"],["Draft's own definition of 'keeps it'","opened on 6 of the last 10 working days, plus one review action in week two","WEEK0-DECISIONS §1, exit measure row"],["Rated by the gap register","unobservable under the architecture","gap register §B row 8"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What voluntary reporting measured on this workspace","note":"The 7 of 694 row is tagged [brief] in the source, supplied not re-measured; the 1 of 99 replication and the machine-row counts are tagged [measured] on that machine.","items":[["Explicit feedback field, fills","7 of 694","1.0086%"],["Marginal fill rate across three measurements","0","numerator frozen while the denominator grew"],["Machine-written rows in the same period","42,514","gate-tier 21,707 + turn-meta 15,786 + traces 5,021"],["Ratio, machine to human","6,073 : 1","42,514 ÷ 7"],["Preference-log routing_path filled","1 of 99","1.01%, the same number independently"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each method can and cannot tell you","cols":["Method","Gives you","Does not give you"],"rows":[["Local file, pasted on request","Exact day counts, if they paste","Anything from the ones who do not reply"],["Local file plus a booked day-14 call","The counts read together, plus why","Ten calls of founder time"],["Opt-in upload toggle","Continuous data for those who opt in","Compliance with the no-vendor-database rule at scale, without a notice-and-consent path"],["Interview only","Why someone stopped","A number anyone can audit later"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Six of ten still using after two weeks, local file pasted on request","gains":["Cheapest to build, and it measures retention","The cheapest instrument to build: a local file with one paste-on-request ask."],"costs":["The paste method measured effectively zero here","Nothing catches a stranger being polite"],"system":"The local usage file, nothing else.","screens":"A settings line explaining the file.","money":"None.","what":"Six of ten still using after two weeks, with the local usage file pasted on request. Cheapest to build and it measures retention; the paste method measured effectively zero here."},{"k":"b","label":"Six of ten run it once, three ask unprompted; read from the session","gains":["Restores the unprompted-demand test","Observable with no file and no paste"],"costs":["Says nothing about whether anyone returns"],"system":"No local usage file needed for the gate.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Six of ten run it once and three ask for it unprompted, read from the session. Restores the unprompted-demand test with no file and no paste; says nothing about whether anyone returns."},{"k":"c","label":"Three of ten name the problem unprompted, two still using at day 30, booked call","gains":["Neither half passes on politeness","Both halves are cheap to observe"],"costs":["Ten calls of founder time","Day 30 extends the pilot two weeks"],"system":"The usage file stays; the call is scheduling.","screens":"The settings line only.","money":"None.","what":"Three of ten name the problem unprompted, two still using at day 30, and a booked call from someone not asked. Neither half passes on politeness; ten calls of founder time and two extra weeks."},{"k":"d","label":"Ellis survey, forty people, 40% very disappointed, by year end","gains":["The strongest signal available"],"costs":["Forty strangers from zero interviews is another project","Nothing decided until year end"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"Unbudgeted recruitment cost.","what":"An Ellis survey of forty people, 40 percent very disappointed, by year end. The strongest signal available; forty strangers from zero interviews is another project, and nothing is decided until then."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["It is the only bar written after somebody attacked the other two.","Booking at recruitment turns a 1% ask into an appointment, and the call finds out why."],"flip":"If strangers will not take a call, the opt-in upload toggle is the fallback.","linked":["B10","PL17","PL7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:206","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:313","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:417","docs/DECIDE.md:92","docs/PRODUCT.md:965","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:12321","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:12352","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:12517","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:28","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:20","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:21","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:22","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:27","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r5-measurement-plan.md:9","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r5-measurement-plan.md:14","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-29-r11/r5-measurement-plan.md:17","merged: PL6"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan proposes what passing means. Agree it before phase zero starts, not after.","dependsOn":["PL2"],"recWhy":"Three live documents set three different bars, and the instrument the plan proposed, a local usage file pasted on request, has been measured on our own tools. hand-filled feedback got 7 entries in 694 chances, and across three measurements that 7 never grew, so new entries ran at zero. A silent pilot reads as a failed one, and a false negative here is the most expensive result available. The bar in (c) is the only one written after somebody attacked the other two. Three of ten naming the problem unprompted cannot be politeness, two still using at day 30 cannot be a first-day courtesy, and a booked call from someone who was not asked is demand. Booking the call at recruitment turns a one percent ask into an appointment, and the call finds out why. Q15 carries this word for word, agreed before the pilot starts. If strangers will not take a call, the opt-in upload toggle is the fallback."},{"id":"PL3","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Fix-first","weight":"high","q":"When does the fix-first list run, and in what order: the table's, the calendar's, or split into demo-blockers and ship-blockers?","lede":"A table headed \"these block everything else\" schedules its largest row five weeks after the work it blocks.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":200,"cols":[{"title":"Table order","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Fortnightly table","tone":"warn"},{"title":"Two named gates","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Left-edge list bug, 4 days","cells":["First","Weeks 3 to 4","Weeks 3 to 5, demo-blocker"]},{"label":"Engine wiring, 8 days","cells":["Fourth","Weeks 8 to 9","Weeks 3 to 5, demo-blocker"]},{"label":"Byte budget, cert, entities, Tauri id","cells":["In the list","In no week","Before the strangers"]},{"label":"Decision-independent rows","cells":["11.5 days","Not distinguished","Start now, in parallel"]},{"label":"First feature week","cells":["After 25.5 days","Week 5","Week 5"]},{"label":"Stated total","cells":["25.5","24, in the sequence line","25.5"]}],"note":"Rows and days from the plan's fix-first table; weeks from its fortnightly table.","caption":"Three artefacts in one document give three orders."},"stakes":"The day-24 refusal test waits on the bug where a list starting at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file.","state":["The plan heads twelve rows \"Fix first. These block everything else\".","The calendar fixes that bug in weeks 3 to 4 and wires the engine in weeks 8 to 9.","Weeks 1 to 2 say \"build nothing\"; on 8 September the code was 29 days untouched."],"tension":["Six rows depend on no open decision and total 11.5 days.","At 1.21 a week, those six are 9.5 calendar weeks.","The day-24 test checks the 83% refusal rate."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Fix-first rows against the open decisions","cols":["Row","Days","Depends on decision 1, 6 or 7?"],"rows":[["1: the left-edge list bug, column-zero list item","4","No: engine, correct in any shell"],["2: the old line-ending bug, bare-CR fence","3","No"],["3 No CI","1","No"],["5 Byte budget is an echo","2","No"],["10 mdmax cert never runnable","1","No"],["11 entities undeclared dependency","0.5","No"],["4 Engine unwired (one symbol of thirteen files)","8","Yes: wiring target depends on the shell (decision 6)"],["6 No bring-your-own key","3","Yes: deleted if VS Code (decision 6)"],["7 LLM port returns a bare string","2","Yes: only needed if idea mode, decision flow and repo docs scan stay (see the pilot's generation-and-AI features decision above)"],["9 src-tauri carries the sibling's identity","1","Yes: moot if no desktop app"],["0 The shell is a fork · 8 Web byte path","decision","Yes"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Measured on this repo, 2026-09-08","items":[["Documentation file-changes, last 14 days","396","git log --since='14 days ago' --name-only -- docs"],["src/ file-changes, last 14 days","0","same command against src"],["Days since src/ last changed","29","last src commit cc1d451, 2026-08-10"],["Days since any engineering commit","8","last test commit 484f579, 2026-08-31"],["Decision-independent fix-first work","11.5 days","rows 1,2,3,5,10,11"],["That work at the measured rate","9.5 calendar weeks","11.5 ÷ 1.21"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Fix-first rows against the plan's 90-day calendar","cols":["Row","Days","Week in the plan's 90-day calendar"],"rows":[["3 CI, deliberate red run","1","3 to 4"],["2: the old line-ending bug","3","3 to 4"],["1: the left-edge list bug","4","3 to 4"],["6 BYO key","3","5 to 7"],["7 LLM port structured output","2","5 to 7"],["4 Engine unwired","8","8 to 9"],["5 Byte budget is an echo","2","not scheduled"],["9 src-tauri identity","1","not scheduled"],["10 mdmax cert never runnable","1","not scheduled"],["11 entities undeclared","0.5","not scheduled"],["Sum of rows","25.5","sequence line still says 24"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"Verified against the code, 2026-09-08","items":[["Files in .github/workflows","0","the directory does not exist"],["package.json budget script","the `budget` npm script prints: `echo 'No bundle budget configured yet — skipping'`","literal string at package.json:20, 26 scripts total"],["cert among the 26 scripts","absent","node scripts/mdmax-cert.mjs has no script entry"],["Engine symbols reaching product code","1 of 13 files","decodeStrict from shape-gate, imported in exactly 2 files"],["entities in package.json","absent from dependencies and devDependencies","imported at fold.ts:43 and verdict.ts:53"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Build nothing until week 3 and keep the calendar as written","gains":["Keeps the discipline that refuted three headlines","Matches the discipline that already refuted three earlier headline claims before they shipped."],"costs":["Extends a 29-day code freeze to 43","The day-24 refusal test cannot run"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Build nothing until week 3 and keep the calendar as written. Keeps the discipline that refuted three headlines; extends a 29-day code freeze to 43, and the day-24 refusal test cannot run."},{"k":"b","label":"Start the six decision-independent rows now, in parallel with the tests","gains":["Engine fixes land whatever the meeting decides","Six rows totalling 11.5 days move regardless of which surface or which features the meeting picks."],"costs":["Splits founder attention across two jobs"],"system":"Left-edge bug, old-line-ending bug that adds a second frontmatter block, CI, bundle budget, certification command, `entities`.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Start the six decision-independent rows now, alongside the tests. Engine fixes land whatever the meeting decides; founder attention splits across two jobs."},{"k":"c","label":"Start the six now; split the rest into demo-blockers and ship-blockers","gains":["Two named gates instead of one vague header","Forces the 4.5 unscheduled days onto a week"],"costs":["The same attention split as option b","The calendar must be rewritten in the same edit"],"system":"Demo-blockers 18 days, ship-blockers 7.5.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Start the six now, and split the rest into demo-blockers and ship-blockers. Two named gates instead of one vague header; the same attention split, and the calendar is rewritten in the same edit."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the calendar and demote the table to a defect list with no ordering claim","gains":["Removes the contradiction in one edit","The smallest possible edit: nothing in the schedule moves, only the table's own heading."],"costs":["Drops the argument that the engine comes first","The 8-day engine wiring still lands late"],"system":"No change to the code.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the calendar and demote the table to a defect list with no ordering claim. Removes the contradiction in one edit; drops the argument that the engine comes first."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["Those six hold under every answer; refusing 83% of vaults is wrong in any product.","The split dates the 4.5 unscheduled days: bundle budget, certification command, `entities`, desktop app name."],"flip":"If the meeting drops the engine, option a is right.","linked":["PL2"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:210","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:212","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:223","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:224","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:225","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:327","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:336","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:356","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:83","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:124","package.json:20","merged: PL4"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Phase zero has a short fix list. The order it runs in is a first week answer.","recWhy":"A table headed these block everything else schedules its largest row five weeks after the work it blocks, and the calendar says build nothing in weeks 1 and 2 while the code sat untouched for 29 days. Six rows depend on no open decision and total 11.5 days: the flush-left list refusal that rejects 83 percent of real vaults, the line-ending bug, and their neighbours. Refusing most vaults is wrong in any product, so those six start now and land whatever else is decided. The remaining rows split into demo-blockers, what has to be true before a stranger sees a file, and ship-blockers, what has to be true before one is paid for. The split also forces the 4.5 unscheduled days, bundle budget, certification command, entities, desktop app name, onto a week. Q14 carries this exactly. The cost is the same attention split as (b) plus a calendar rewrite. If the meeting drops the engine, (a) is right."},{"id":"PL10","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Kill conditions","weight":"high","q":"Which kill conditions are live: the plan's four on the market, or the record's seven dated switches, one tripped now?","lede":"Not one condition in the current plan can ever fire on the founder.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":180,"cols":[{"title":"The plan's four","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"The three time checks","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Fires on","cells":["The market or the engine","The founder's own capacity"]},{"label":"Checked how","cells":["Interviews and corpus runs","Two git commands, no judgment"]},{"label":"Reading today","cells":["Not yet started","Writing check at 396 to 0 over 14 days"]},{"label":"Named action","cells":["Stop, or re-cut and retest","Freeze writing 14 days; cancel the month"]},{"label":"Attached to a live asset","cells":["One is attached to a cut plugin","Yes"]}],"note":"The writing check, re-run here with the two commands the switch itself names.","caption":"Which kill set can fire on the person doing the work."},"stakes":"If one kill switch is ignored, people start ignoring the rest.","state":["The plan's risk table has four numeric kill conditions, all on the market or engine.","A fifth, the 200-install line, names the cut Obsidian plugin.","The record's seven dated kill switches were never carried across."],"tension":["The writing switch last read 396 docs changes to zero code in fourteen days.","Its action, a fourteen-day writing freeze, would stall the week-0 exit-measure write-up.","One of the seven assumes a paid pre-order, so the full set forces one into weeks 1 to 2."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"the writing switch, measured on this repo 2026-09-08","note":"The writing switch fires above 3:1 for two consecutive weeks. Re-run here with the two commands the switch itself names: git log --since='14 days ago' --name-only --pretty=format: -- docs, and the same for src.","data":[["docs/ file-changes, 14 days",396],["src/ file-changes, 14 days",0],["the writing switch's trip line",3]],"unit":"file changes","threshold":3,"thresholdLabel":"3:1 trip line"},{"type":"table","title":"The two kill sets","cols":["ID","Condition","Can it fire on the founder?"],"rows":[["the Risks section, row 1","under 4 of 10 want review state","No: fires on the market"],["the Risks section, row 7","patched corpus still refusing >10 of 7,969 by day 24","No: fires on the engine"],["the Tiers section and the tiers table","plugin under 200 installs in 14 days","No, and the plugin is cut (see the channels question above)"],["the writing switch","docs:src file-changes >3:1 for two weeks → freeze writing 14 days","Yes, reading 396:0 today"],["the product-hours switch","logged product hours under 300 of 430 budgeted → drop client work or drop the product","Yes"],["the client-hours switch","a client month demands >60 client hours → the product month is cancelled in writing","Yes"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Keep the plan's conditions and treat the record's seven as superseded","gains":["Simplest, and no new bookkeeping","No new bookkeeping and no dates to re-derive against a schedule that keeps moving."],"costs":["Nothing can fire on the founder","One condition names a channel that no longer exists"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the plan's four market conditions and treat the record's seven switches as superseded. Simplest, no new bookkeeping; nothing can fire on the founder, and one row names a cut channel."},{"k":"b","label":"Add the record's three time checks to the plan's four, starting on a named Monday","gains":["The only conditions that fire on the person building","The writing switch is checkable with two commands"],"costs":["A fourteen-day writing freeze at an unchosen moment","The start date must be named or it drifts"],"system":"None; a weekly check.","screens":"None.","money":"The client-hours switch cancels any month over sixty client hours.","what":"The three watch your own time. They fire on writing outpacing building 3 to 1 for two weeks, product hours under 300 of 430, or a client month over 60 hours."},{"k":"c","label":"Adopt all seven of the record's kill switches with new dates","gains":["A complete set with dates and actions"],"costs":["The pre-order switch assumes a paid test nobody has chosen yet","Every date needs re-deriving against the schedule"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"Ties the plan to a paid gate.","what":"Adopt all seven of the record's kill switches with new dates. A complete set with dates and actions; the pre-order switch assumes a paid test nobody chose yet, and every date needs re-deriving."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep the plan's four and replace the cut plugin line with a live channel condition","gains":["Fixes the one row attached to nothing","Fixes the one row in the risk table that currently points at a channel already cut elsewhere."],"costs":["Still nothing measures the founder","Leaves the capacity failure mode unwatched"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the plan's four and replace the cut-plugin row with a live channel condition. Fixes the one row attached to nothing; still nothing measures the founder."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["The plan's four fire on the market. The record's last three fire on the founder: writing, product hours, client hours.","The writing switch needs two commands and no judgment, and reads its most extreme value now."],"flip":"If the two-week tests count as the work, the writing switch's window must exclude that fortnight.","linked":["PL2","PL3","PL5","PL7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:321","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:327","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:328","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:329","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14295","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14296","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14297","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14299","docs/CRITIQUE.md:793"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Kill lines only work if they are set before the work starts. Pick which ones are live.","recWhy":"Not one condition in the current plan can fire on the founder. The risk table's four numeric kills are all on the market or the engine, a fifth names the Obsidian plugin that was cut, and the record's seven dated switches were never carried across. The record's last three watch the person building, writing, product hours and client hours, and the writing switch is checkable with two commands and reads its most extreme value now, 396 docs changes to zero code in fourteen days. Adding those three to the plan's four gives a set that can say no to the founder as well as to the market, and it starts on a named Monday, because a start date that drifts is no start date. The fourteen-day writing freeze it triggers is the honest cost. PL2 supplies the rate it reads. If the two-week tests count as the work, the writing switch's window excludes that fortnight."},{"id":"PL12","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"First run","weight":"high","q":"The pilot plan has no sign-up, but the app shows a login screen before a file. Which one changes?","lede":"The only non-engine work this repo owns is what the pilot plan says must not exist.","visual":{"kind":"flow","nodes":[{"label":"User opens frontmatter","note":"no actor, no file yet","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Login screen","note":"the app layout, before any file","tone":"stop","edge":"today"},{"label":"Open a local folder","note":"needs no identity at all","tone":"accent","edge":"proposed"},{"label":"Drag files in","note":"read-only session","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Connect a GitHub repo","note":"needs the user's token","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Sign in","note":"only on the repo path","tone":"warn"}],"perRow":3,"note":"Paths from the plan's platform table; the login gate from the shipped code.","caption":"What each first-run path physically needs."},"stakes":"Removing the gate deletes shipped work; keeping it breaks the thirty-second first run.","state":["The pilot scope table lists sign-up among the things the pilot leaves out.","The retention section wants a folder opened and edited in thirty seconds, with no account.","The app's main layout shows a login screen whenever nobody is signed in."],"tension":["A local folder needs no identity. A GitHub repo needs a token.","Zero Firestore document writes exist across 226 source files.","Three committed files contradict each other on whether we hold documents."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"Verified in the code, 2026-09-08","items":[["Vault layout without an actor","returns <LoginScreen />","src/app/(vault)/layout.tsx:12-13"],["Non-engine files unique to this repo","7","5 are the Firebase auth stack"],["firestore.rules","419 lines, committed","note bodies bounded at 900,000 bytes"],["Revision history in those rules","allow delete: if false","append-only, with public content snapshots"],["Firestore document writes across 226 source files","0","the only Firestore code is an unused accessor"],["What the tiers table says the pilot has","no sign-up","and §17 says no account"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Remove the gate for the pilot and keep the auth code behind a flag","gains":["Restores the thirty-second first run","Nothing is deleted"],"costs":["Shelves the only non-engine work this repo owns"],"system":"A flag around the main layout.","screens":"The launcher becomes the first screen.","money":"None.","what":"Remove the gate for the pilot and keep the auth code behind a flag. Restores the thirty-second first run and nothing is deleted; the only non-engine work this repo owns is shelved."},{"k":"b","label":"Keep sign-in and make the tier and retention sections admit an account is required","gains":["Honest about what ships today, and no code changes","No code changes at all; the fix is entirely in what two sections of the plan say."],"costs":["Loses the no-account first run retention depends on","Asking for an account before value is a known failure"],"system":"None.","screens":"The login screen stays first.","money":"None.","what":"Keep sign-in and make the tier and retention sections admit an account is required. Honest about what ships today and no code changes; asking for an account before value is a known failure."},{"k":"c","label":"Optional: local folder and drag-in with no account; a repo requires one","gains":["Matches what each path physically needs","Preserves the thirty-second first run"],"costs":["Two first-run paths to design and test","The sign-in code stays half-used until the Firebase decision"],"system":"The layout branches on storage, not on sign-in.","screens":"The launcher gains a no-account path.","money":"None.","what":"Optional: a local folder and drag-in with no account, and a repository requires one. Matches what each path needs and keeps the thirty-second first run; two first-run paths to design and test."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer until it is decided whether the Firebase design ships","gains":["One decision instead of two","Removes the appearance of choosing between two things that are actually the same underlying question."],"costs":["The first screen stays undecided as the pilot nears","Every legal rule waits on it too"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Defer until it is decided whether the Firebase design ships. One decision instead of two; the first screen stays undecided as the pilot nears, and every legal rule waits on it too."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["It asks for an account only where a repo needs one.","It deletes no sign-in work and does not wait on the Firebase decision."],"flip":"If Firebase is removed entirely, a is right and the login screen goes.","linked":["L1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:205","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:365","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:429","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:142","src/app/(vault)/layout.tsx:12","firestore.rules:221","firestore.rules:318","firestore.rules:361"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Sign in for strangers is in the first build, and today the app shows a login before a file.","recWhy":"The pilot scope table lists sign-up among the things the pilot leaves out, the retention section wants a folder opened and edited in thirty seconds with no account, and the app's main layout shows a login screen whenever nobody is signed in. The plan and the code contradict each other on the first screen. A local folder and a dragged-in file need no identity; a GitHub repository needs a token, and that is the only place an account is physically required. Asking for one where it is required, and nowhere else, deletes no sign-in work and waits on nothing: zero Firestore document writes exist across 226 source files, so the Firebase question does not gate this. Q3 and AC20 carry the same rule. The cost is two first-run paths to design and test. If Firebase is removed entirely, (a) is right and the login screen goes."},{"id":"PL7","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Weeks 1 to 2","weight":"high","q":"What runs in weeks 1 to 2: forty free contacts as written, or fewer tests and one that charges a card?","lede":"After two adversarial rounds nothing has tested whether anyone pays, and the fortnight is already booked solid.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"Week 0","label":"Buyer, byte path and exit measure written","note":"the plan's fortnightly table, week 0","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"Weeks 1 to 2","label":"Forty contacts and three landing pages","note":"four a day from a standing start","tone":"warn"},{"when":"Day 14","label":"Kill signals read","note":"under 4 of 10 stops the build","tone":"stop"},{"when":"Week 3","label":"Go or no-go","note":"needs only the developer sessions","tone":"accent"},{"when":"Weeks 1 to 4","label":"Team conversations and the priced arm","note":"the Pro price gates the build after the pilot, not week 3","tone":"accent"}],"note":"Weeks and contents from the plan's fortnightly table; the priced arm from the record's Gate 1.","caption":"Which tests the week-3 gate needs, and which can run longer."},"stakes":"Whether anyone will pay stays untested into the build, where being wrong gets expensive.","state":["Weeks 1 to 2 need ten developer sessions, five people who bill for documents, and twenty team conversations.","No checkout, no price and no card appear in the fortnight.","Whether anyone pays is still open after eleven desk-research investigations."],"tension":["Zero interviews so far, and the plan answers that with forty in ten days.","Only the developer sessions and the screenshot test gate week 3.","Almanac raised $45M on this same pitch and shut down on 2025-01-31."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"What the scheduled tests can and cannot falsify","cols":["Test","Falsifies","Does not falsify"],"rows":[["Ten developers shown the mock, both framings","That the problem is recognised","That anyone would buy the answer"],["Five people who bill for documents","That approval is a felt cost","Price, seat count, or who signs"],["Three landing pages","Which headline reads best","Whether any headline converts to money"],["Twenty team conversations","What teams say they are gated on","What a team does when asked for a card"],["Five-second screenshot test","Whether the highlight reads as a highlight","Anything commercial"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The priced test that was designed and then dropped","items":[["Gate 1 threshold","12 paid pre-orders","record's 90-day plan, RECORD:14193"],["Traffic required","1,200 visitors, 400 per arm","≥1.00% capture"],["Price point","$19, fully refundable on request","real card charged, RECORD:14169"],["Paid ads ceiling","₹20,000","₹16.67 per click at 1,200"],["What Gate 1 was allowed to kill","the entire product","stated in the plan's own words, RECORD:14198"],["Payment evidence gathered to date","none","gap register, still open"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The fortnight, counted","items":[["Developers shown the mock","10","decision 1"],["People who bill for documents","5","decision 2"],["Team conversations","20","the Pro price"],["Five-second screenshot viewings","5","whether the tint reads"],["Total contacts","40","4 per working day"],["Builds inside 'build nothing'","3 landing pages, plus a plugin that no longer exists","Exhibit 19, weeks 1 to 2"],["Interviews run to date","0","across the whole programme"]]},{"type":"table","title":"What each test gates, and when that gate is actually needed","cols":["Test","Gates","Needed by"],"rows":[["Ten developer sessions","the week-3 go/no-go","week 3"],["Five-second screenshot","whether the highlight design is wrong before building","week 3"],["Five billers","decision 2, first buyer","week 1, per the plan's decision list"],["Twenty team conversations","the Pro price and what the build after the pilot offers","the build after the pilot, months later"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Run all four tests as written, forty contacts in two weeks, free","gains":["Every question closes at once if recruitment holds","If recruitment genuinely holds across all forty contacts, every open question closes in one fortnight."],"costs":["Partial data at the week-3 gate if it slips","Nothing in the fortnight can disprove the price"],"system":"None.","screens":"Three landing pages.","money":"No revenue signal at all.","what":"Run all four tests as written: forty free contacts in two weeks. Every question closes at once if recruitment holds; partial data at the week-3 gate if it slips, and nothing can disprove the price."},{"k":"b","label":"Sequence: developers and the screenshot test first, teams over four weeks","gains":["The go/no-go runs on time on what it needs","The two tests that actually gate week 3 run first, so the go or no-go date never moves."],"costs":["The team-pricing answer arrives later","Still no test that returns a refusal to pay"],"system":"None.","screens":"Three landing pages.","money":"None.","what":"Sequence: developers and the screenshot test first, the twenty teams over four weeks. The go or no-go runs on time on what it needs; still no test that returns a refusal to pay."},{"k":"c","label":"Sequence as in (b) and add a refundable pre-order arm to the landing pages","gains":["The only test that can return a hard no","Numbers already exist in the record"],"costs":["Needs a checkout and 1,200 unbudgeted visitors","Sells an artefact the form-factor decision may change"],"system":"A checkout and a refund path.","screens":"Pricing and checkout, outside the product.","money":"₹20,000 ad ceiling; refunds on request.","what":"Sequence as in (b) and add a refundable pre-order arm to the landing pages. The only test that can return a hard no, with its numbers already in the record; needs a checkout and unbudgeted visitors."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep all four at lower n; ask teams for signed intent at a stated price","gains":["Twenty-one contacts is deliverable","Twenty-one contacts across the same fortnight is a schedule that is actually deliverable."],"costs":["Every 'x of 10' kill threshold must be restated","Signed intent has no measured conversion here"],"system":"None.","screens":"Three landing pages.","money":"None.","what":"Keep all four at lower n and ask teams for signed intent at a stated price. Twenty-one contacts is deliverable; every kill threshold must be restated, and signed intent has no measured conversion."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["It is the only test that can return a no, and the record already sets its numbers.","Two rounds measured installs and attention, and neither tells us who will pay."],"flip":"If the form-factor decision picks a VS Code extension, the pre-order page sells a different artefact.","linked":["PL17","PL5","PR17"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:336","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:366","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14169","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14177","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14193","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14198","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","merged: PL9"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"The old two week test shape is replaced by the twenty hand made kits.","recWhy":"After two adversarial rounds nothing has tested whether anyone pays, and the fortnight as written books ten developer sessions, five people who bill for documents and twenty team conversations. That is forty free contacts in ten days from a programme with zero interviews, with no checkout, no price and no card anywhere in it. Only the developer sessions and the screenshot test gate week 3, so they run first and the teams spread over four weeks, which G15 makes bookable. The pre-order arm is the one test that can return a hard no, its numbers already exist in the record, and PR4 and PR16 put the page and the rail in place. The costs are a checkout before the product exists and about 1,200 unbudgeted visitors. If the surface decision changes what is sold, the page sells the new artefact."},{"id":"PL17","cat":"Plan, scope & sequencing","sub":"Ninety days","weight":"high","q":"Does the quarter end with ten strangers using a pilot, or with a launch on a bar written in advance?","lede":"As written, the quarter produces no distribution and no revenue, and its one channel needs permission nobody has asked for.","visual":{"kind":"timeline","items":[{"when":"2026-08-12","label":"Zed Delta launch: 679 HN points","note":"the visible bar in this category now","tone":"stop"},{"when":"Week 2","label":"superpowers README line","note":"outbound; maintainer not approached","tone":"warn"},{"when":"Weeks 10 to 12","label":"Polish; ten strangers","note":"the quarter's only outcome","tone":"neutral"},{"when":"After the exit","label":"Show HN, one shot","note":"outside the quarter, per the go-to-market section","tone":"warn"},{"when":"Record's week 12","label":"Gate 3: 30 paid, or 50 for two","note":"the ending the plan dropped","tone":"accent"}],"note":"HN points from the research briefing, opened; weeks from the plan's fortnightly table and the record.","caption":"What the quarter ends with, against what it used to."},"stakes":"Ninety days could end with the only testable result being whether ten people liked it.","state":["The twelve-week calendar ends with polish and ten strangers in weeks 10 to 12.","The channel plan puts Show HN at the end of the pilot, one shot only.","The one scheduled channel is a README line in the superpowers toolchain, in week 2."],"tension":["Nobody has asked its maintainer yet, and the answer may be no.","A launch bar chosen after the pilot result is chosen under relief.","Zed Delta got 679 Hacker News points on launch, which gives the floor a reference."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Two ninety-day endings","cols":["Week","Current plan (the plan's 90-day calendar)","Record's 90-day plan"],"rows":[["1 to 2","four tests, build nothing","landing pages, checkout wired, ten stranger sessions, rename probe"],["3","go or no-go","GATE 1: 12 paid pre-orders on 1,200 visitors, or stop"],["8 to 9","revert, panel, answer in place, scan, engine wired","GATE 2: plugin live and 25 installs in 14 days; first non-founder paid purchase"],["10 to 12","polish; ten strangers; 6 of 10 by the week-0 measure","launch across five surfaces; GATE 3 at 30 paid (one founder) or 50 (two)"],["End state","a pilot result","a launch, a price, and a number"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the quarter currently commits to","items":[["Distribution events scheduled","1","superpowers README line, week 2"],["Maintainer approached about it","no","gap register D6"],["Channels cut in round 2","2","Obsidian plugin, Chrome extension"],["Revenue events scheduled","0","§14 Exhibit 19"],["Show HN, per the Go-to-market section","after the exit test","one shot, outside the quarter"],["Monthly nut","about ₹1.09L","covered by 54 to 78 consulting hours, §11"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"End at the pilot as written; launch is a separate quarter","gains":["Focused, and it matches the build capacity","Matches what 1.21 measured engineering days a week can actually deliver inside ninety days."],"costs":["No distribution evidence in ninety days","The one channel still needs an unasked permission"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"No revenue this quarter.","what":"End the quarter at the pilot as written, with launch a separate quarter. Focused and matched to capacity; no distribution evidence in ninety days, and the one channel needs an unasked permission."},{"k":"b","label":"Add a week 13: Show HN and one community post, with a stated floor","gains":["Puts a distribution number inside the quarter","Puts one real distribution number inside the quarter instead of deferring it past the pilot entirely."],"costs":["Spends a one-shot channel on a pilot-grade build","Adds a week the schedule cannot hold"],"system":"None.","screens":"A public landing page.","money":"None.","what":"Add a week 13: Show HN and one community post with a stated floor. A distribution number inside the quarter; spends a one-shot channel on a pilot-grade build, and adds a week the schedule cannot hold."},{"k":"c","label":"Add a payment event: the first paid team seat inside the ninety days","gains":["The strongest possible ending","The strongest possible signal a quarter could end on: money from a stranger who was not asked."],"costs":["Billing and a merchant account are outside the pilot","Redundant if weeks 1 to 2 already include a paid pre-order"],"system":"Billing and entitlements, none in the pilot.","screens":"Checkout and account screens.","money":"Merchant fees and a compliance surface.","what":"Add a payment event: the first paid team seat inside the ninety days. The strongest ending; billing and a merchant account are outside the pilot, and redundant beside a week-2 pre-order."},{"k":"d","label":"End at the pilot; pre-register the launch date and floor, and ask the maintainer","gains":["Removes a bar chosen under relief","Turns an uncertain channel into an answered question"],"costs":["No revenue evidence this quarter","A pre-registered date can still slip"],"system":"None.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"End at the pilot, pre-register the launch date and its floor now, and ask the superpowers maintainer this week. Removes a bar chosen under relief; no revenue evidence this quarter."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Show HN is one shot, so the bar has to be written before we see the result.","Asking the superpowers maintainer this week decides whether we have one channel or none."],"flip":"If the weeks 1 to 2 tests include a paid pre-order, the quarter already carries commercial evidence.","linked":["PL2","PL7"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:305","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:309","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:357","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14208","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14217","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14219","docs/FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md:14226","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:58","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:134"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"How the quarter ends depends on what phase zero returns.","recWhy":"As written the quarter ends with polish and ten strangers, produces no distribution and no revenue, and its one scheduled channel is a README line in a toolchain whose maintainer nobody has asked. Show HN is one shot, so its floor has to be written before the result is seen, because a bar chosen after the pilot is chosen under relief. Zed Delta's 679 points on launch give the floor a reference, and G4 settles the title. Asking the superpowers maintainer this week, which G6 specifies as an issue, decides whether the quarter has one channel or none, for the cost of an email. The revenue event in (c) needs billing and a merchant account the pilot does not have; PL7 and PR4 put a refundable pre-order in weeks 1 and 2 instead, which is the commercial evidence this quarter can honestly carry. If that pre-order ships, the quarter already has its number and this card's flip has fired."},{"id":"R1","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Programme","weight":"critical","q":"Stop the research rounds and run the two weeks of human tests, or run a third round on the eleven open items?","lede":"Every round has changed the headline and none has produced a user. A third costs the two weeks the tests need.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":164,"cols":[{"title":"Two desk rounds, done","tone":"sunk"},{"title":"Weeks 1-2 tests, unstarted","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Findings","cells":["280 before 2026-09-08, plus 152 in round 2","None yet"]},{"label":"People asked anything","cells":["0","40 scheduled"]},{"label":"Effect on the headline","cells":["Four changes in two days","One go/no-go at week 3"]},{"label":"Source code touched","cells":["0 of 16 commits since 2026-08-31","None planned; build nothing"]},{"label":"Cost of one more","cells":["Two weeks, on the observed rate","Two weeks, and the first human contact"]}],"note":"Round 2 ran 23 agents over 11 gaps and sent the headline itself back to hypothesis.","caption":"What another desk round buys, against what the tests buy."},"stakes":"The week-3 go/no-go stays decided by nobody who has used the thing.","state":["Round 2 left eleven items open, and seven of them need no founder.","Week 0 schedules five other items and none of the eleven.","`src/` untouched since 2026-08-10. Zero of sixteen commits after 2026-08-31 touch it."],"tension":["Two of the eleven gate the build: review marks surviving an agent's rewrite, and Cursor offering the model API.","The other nine change nothing about whether ten developers want this.","Stopping the rounds need not stop the monthly competitor check. Three claims moved in nine weeks."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"The research programme, measured 2026-09-09","items":[["Agent reports under docs/research/agent-reports-*","214","699,018 words; find + wc, re-measured 2026-09-09"],["User interviews across the whole programme","0","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35"],["Findings before 2026-09-08, two adversarial rounds","280","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13"],["Findings, round 2 (2026-09-08)","152","75 confirmed / 60 revised / 16 refuted, GAPS:116"],["Last commit touching src/","2026-08-10","git log -1 -- src/, thirty days ago"],["Commits dated after 2026-08-31 touching src/","0 of 16","git log; 13 docs, 1 prototype, 1 deploy config, 1 decisions site"],["Measured engineering days per calendar week","1.21, and 0.23 over the last 30 days","the withdrawn schedule needed 7.0 (PRODUCT-BRIEF:11, :227)"]]},{"type":"bars","title":"Words of tracked markdown per line of source","note":"Tracked .md excluding the pinned test corpus, summed per commit. The denominator, 25,407 lines of .ts/.tsx across 226 files, was verified identical at all three commits. The plan's own published series (87 at DECIDE:217, 92.2 at CRITIQUE:135) counts a wider file set that could not be reproduced here: the direction is the same, the absolute is not, so these are the reproducible figures.","data":[["2026-08-31, DECIDE commit 8fd0bf9",80.9],["2026-08-31, CRITIQUE commit a8ac822",84.8],["2026-09-08, HEAD f495376",85.7]],"unit":"words per line"}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Stop the rounds. Run the weeks 1-2 tests next","gains":["First human contact in week 1, not week 5","Ends a programme that has never stopped early"],"costs":["A fatal review-mark result arrives after the tests","The competitor check stops too, unless named separately"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Stop the rounds and run the weeks 1 and 2 tests next. First human contact in week 1 and a programme that finally stops; a fatal review-mark result arrives after the tests."},{"k":"b","label":"One bounded round 3 on the founder-independent items, capped at two days","gains":["Closes the cheap items with a stated end date","Tests still start on day three"],"costs":["The cap must be enforced against a programme that never stops","Two days of the two weeks"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"One bounded round 3 on the founder-independent items, capped at two days. Closes the cheap items with an end date; the cap has to be enforced against a programme that never stops."},{"k":"c","label":"Run round 3 unbounded, as rounds 1 and 2 were","gains":["Every round has caught real errors","Nothing is committed on an unattacked claim"],"costs":["On the observed rate: 150-plus findings, another headline change","First human contact still unscheduled"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Run round 3 unbounded, as rounds 1 and 2 were. Every round has caught real errors; on the observed rate it returns 150-plus findings and another headline change, with human contact still unscheduled."},{"k":"d","label":"Split: two code experiments in week 0, the other nine accepted as risk","what":"The review-mark and Cursor tests run in week 0; a named person signs off the other nine.","gains":["Cheapest: each experiment is one person for one day","Tests and gating evidence arrive together"],"costs":["Somebody has to sign nine open risks","Worthless unless the signature is written down"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"d","recCase":["Each test is one person for one day, and both move a number the meeting uses.","Keep the monthly competitor re-check. It caught Notion, Antigravity and Claude Code."],"flip":"If the meeting picks the VS Code extension, the Cursor test stops being optional and the split becomes three gating experiments.","linked":["R14"],"sources":["docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:13","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:22","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:35","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:112","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:116","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:130","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:11","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:22","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:351","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:560","docs/CRITIQUE.md:135","docs/DECIDE.md:217","https://www.notion.com/releases","https://antigravity.google/blog/vcs-and-terminal","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anthropics/claude-code/main/CHANGELOG.md"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"The plan stops the rounds and runs twenty kits by hand. This card is that decision.","recWhy":"Every research round has changed the headline and none has produced a user, and src has been untouched since 10 August; zero of sixteen commits after 31 August touch it. Round 2 left eleven items open, and only two of them gate the build: whether review marks survive an agent's rewrite, and whether Cursor offers the model API. Each of those is one person for one day, and each moves a number the meeting uses, so they run in week 0 while the other nine are accepted as risk, in writing, with a signature, because an unsigned accepted risk is worth nothing. The monthly competitor check stays, named separately, since it caught Notion, Antigravity and Claude Code and three headline claims moved in nine weeks. If the surface decision picks a VS Code extension, the Cursor test stops being optional and the split becomes three gating experiments."},{"id":"R17","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Evidence class","weight":"high","q":"Do the founder tests of idea mode, decision flow and docs scan get notes attached this week, or get relabelled untested?","lede":"Three features carrying eighteen engineering days rest on evidence that is one sentence in a chat log.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"The evidence table today","lines":[{"t":"Idea mode, decision flow, docs scan: founder test","mark":"class"},{"t":"Participants recorded: none","mark":"gap"},{"t":"Protocol recorded: none","mark":"gap"},{"t":"18 of about 70 pilot days ride on it","mark":"cost"}]},"after":{"title":"After one document","lines":[{"t":"Who, when, what they did, what they said","mark":"attached"},{"t":"The plan's strongest exhibit","mark":"if it exists"},{"t":"Or all three relabelled untested","mark":"if not"},{"t":"18 days move behind the week-10 pilot","mark":"if not"}]},"via":"one document","note":"The plan's risk table already describes the fallback.","caption":"The strongest label in the plan, or its weakest, decided by one file."},"stakes":"Eighteen of the pilot's roughly seventy engineering days rest on an evidence class with no artefact.","state":["The plan's evidence table gives idea mode, decision flow and the docs scan their own class.","Week 0 schedules attaching the founder notes for idea mode and decision flow.","The gap register rates it High: a chat statement with no notes or protocol."],"tension":["Standalone PRD generators number 512 repos, and the top one has 57 stars.","`/speckit.clarify`, Kiro and Claude Code plan mode ship the same step free.","The gap register finds these features point at a buyer who runs no agent."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What the founder-test class carries","items":[["Features in the class","3 of 13","F5, F6, F7 (PRODUCT-BRIEF:187)"],["Engineering days those three carry","18 of about 70","F5+F6 8d, F7 10d (PRODUCT-BRIEF:227)"],["Participants recorded","none","GAPS:23"],["Protocol recorded","none","GAPS:23"],["Notes attached to the plan","none","still a week-0 task, PRODUCT-BRIEF:351"],["Public evidence pointing the other way","512 PRD-generator repos, top has 57 stars","PRODUCT-BRIEF:155, and the same figure in the F5 row at :59"],["Free tools shipping the same step","/speckit.clarify, Kiro, Claude Code plan mode","PRODUCT-BRIEF:60"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Attach the notes this week: who, when, what they did, what they said","gains":["A day of writing decides eighteen engineering days","The only evidence from a person using it"],"costs":["The write-up may show the test was informal","Founder time in a week carrying four decisions"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Attach the notes this week: who, when, what they did, what they said. A day of writing decides eighteen engineering days; it may show the test was informal."},{"k":"b","label":"Relabel all three untested and move them into the ten-stranger pilot","gains":["Matches what the risk table already anticipates","No claim rests on an unattachable artefact"],"costs":["Moves 18 pilot days onto week-10 evidence","Discards a real test for not being written down"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Relabel all three untested and move them into the ten-stranger pilot. Matches what the risk table anticipates; it moves 18 pilot days onto week-10 evidence and discards a real test for lack of notes."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the label and attach nothing","gains":["Free","Nobody's time goes on a write-up"],"costs":["No artefact behind a class, in a document built on openable claims","The first reader who asks finds nothing"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the label and attach nothing. Free; no artefact behind a class, in a document built on openable claims, and the first reader who asks finds nothing."},{"k":"d","label":"Cut idea mode and decision flow from the pilot; keep repo docs scan (needs no model)","gains":["Removes 8 days and the gap register's buyer contradiction","The docs scan needs no model and no key"],"costs":["Removes the two features a person reportedly used and liked","Cuts on the label, not the feature"],"system":"Structured output leaves the pilot build.","screens":"Idea-mode and decision-flow screens leave the pilot.","money":"None.","what":"Cut idea mode and decision flow from the pilot and keep the docs scan, which needs no model. Removes 8 days and the buyer contradiction; cuts on the label, not the feature."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One document either exists or does not, and the answer moves eighteen engineering days inside the pilot.","If it exists, it is the plan's only evidence from a person using the thing."],"flip":"No research changes this; only the founders can say whether the notes exist.","linked":["MK4"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:59","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:60","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:69","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:155","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:187","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:325","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:351","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:23","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:52"],"when":"mvp","whenWhy":"Generation ships as the funnel on founder tests whose notes were never attached.","recWhy":"Three features carrying eighteen engineering days rest on evidence that is one sentence in a chat log. The plan's evidence table gives idea mode, decision flow and the docs scan their own class, and the gap register rates that High, a chat statement with no notes and no protocol. Standalone PRD generators number 512 repositories with the top one at 57 stars, and speckit.clarify, Kiro and Claude Code plan mode ship the same step free, so the market points against these features and the founder test for them. One document either exists or it does not, and if it exists it is the plan's only evidence from a person using the thing. Q12 makes the kit wait on exactly this: generation ships in Phase 2, after the founder test notes are attached and twenty kits have been made by hand. No research changes this; only the founders can say whether the notes exist."},{"id":"R7","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Corpus","weight":"critical","q":"Do we pin a second corpus of agent-written repositories before the engine fixes, or keep the seven Obsidian vaults?","lede":"Every engine number is measured on note-takers. The pitch is documents an agent wrote into a repository.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":164,"cols":[{"title":"Seven Obsidian vaults, pinned","tone":"sunk"},{"title":"Agent-written repos, unpinned","tone":"accent"}],"rows":[{"label":"Files","cells":["8,513, pinned 2026-08-29","None pinned"]},{"label":"Concentration","cells":["One wiki is 77.4% of the files","Unknown"]},{"label":"Files named spec.md","cells":["0","The population the pitch names"]},{"label":"AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md","cells":["2 of 8,513","Unknown"]},{"label":"What it sizes","cells":["83% refusal, four days on the left-edge list bug","Nothing yet"]},{"label":"What it proves","cells":["Byte-exactness on real files","Nothing yet"]}],"note":"The weeks 3-4 row already names one spec-kit and one superpowers repo.","caption":"The corpus the engine is measured on, against the one in the pitch."},"stakes":"The bug where a list starting at the left edge makes the editor refuse the file gets four days, sized on one wiki.","state":["`test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json` pins seven Obsidian vaults, 8,513 files, 19,047,891 bytes, pinned 2026-08-29.","The plan states the caveat and does not act on it.","In those 8,513 files: zero `spec.md`, zero `specs` directories, two agent-instruction files."],"tension":["One vault is 77.4% of the file count, so the 83% mostly describes that wiki.","AIDev catalogues the population: 932,791 agent-authored pull requests across 116,211 repositories.","Discarding the vaults loses byte-exactness proven on 8,513 real files."],"evidence":[{"type":"bars","title":"Share of the 8,513-file corpus, from SOURCES.json","note":"Computed from md_files per vault; the seven sum exactly to 8,513, verified 2026-09-09","data":[["community-archive/obsidian-hub",77.4],["oldwinter/knowledge-garden",11.3],["quanru/obsidian-example-lifeos",6.3],["s-blu/dataview-example-vault",3.1],["kepano/kepano-obsidian",1.2],["erazlogo/obsidian-history-vault",0.5],["CyanVoxel/Obsidian-Vault-Template",0.2]],"unit":"% of corpus files"},{"type":"stat","title":"What the corpus does not contain","items":[["Files named spec.md","0","find over test/corpus/foreign/_vendor, 2026-09-09"],["Directories named specs","0","same"],["AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md files","2 of 8,513","both in oldwinter/knowledge-garden"],["Repositories that are Obsidian vaults","7 of 7","SOURCES.json"],["Engine claims measured on it","refusal rate, byte-exactness, the engine bugs numbered 1 through 4","PRODUCT-BRIEF:514"],["Days budgeted for the left-edge list bug, sized on its refusal rate","4","83% of real vaults refused (PRODUCT-BRIEF:214)"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Pin a second corpus of agent-written repos before the engine fixes","gains":["Sizes the fixes against the population the pitch names","Every refusal figure gains its denominator"],"costs":["Delays the fixes by the clone-and-pin time","Two corpora to keep pinned and re-run"],"system":"A second SOURCES.json and a second gate run in CI.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Pin a second corpus of agent-written repositories before the engine fixes. Sizes the fixes against the population the pitch names; delays the fixes by the clone-and-pin time."},{"k":"b","label":"Replace the vault corpus with the agent corpus","gains":["One corpus to maintain, matching the pitch","No stale refusal figures left to quote"],"costs":["Throws away byte-exactness proven on 8,513 files","Loses the standing regression gate"],"system":"The existing gate is repointed; historical comparisons break.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Replace the vault corpus with the agent corpus. One corpus matching the pitch; it throws away byte-exactness proven on 8,513 files and the standing regression gate."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the vaults as the gate; add the instruction-file corpus at the start of Phase 1","gains":["Costs nothing extra; weeks 3-4 name those repos","Keeps the byte-exactness proof intact"],"costs":["Refusal figures stay vault-derived through the go/no-go","The list bug's four days stay sized on the wrong population"],"system":"One extra pinned source at weeks 3-4.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep the vaults as the gate; add the instruction-file corpus at the start of Phase 1. Nothing extra, because weeks 3 and 4 already name those repos; refusal figures stay vault-derived for now."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep as is; markdown is markdown","gains":["No work","The gate is already green and understood"],"costs":["Every fidelity number describes a population the pitch ignores","The plan's corpus caveat stays unacted on"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Keep as is; markdown is markdown. No work, and the gate is green and understood; every fidelity number describes a population the pitch ignores."}],"rec":"c","recCase":["The vault corpus is a good regression gate, and it is the one thing the engine can prove.","Weeks 3-4 already prototype on a spec-kit and a superpowers repo, so pinning costs only that clone."],"flip":"If the list bug's four-day estimate is defended with the 83% figure, the second corpus has to come first.","linked":["E18"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:214","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:354","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:514","test/corpus/foreign/SOURCES.json","https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09185"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"A second corpus protects engine work that is deferred.","recWhy":"Every engine number is measured on seven Obsidian vaults, 8,513 files and 19,047,891 bytes pinned on 29 August, and one vault is 77.4 percent of the file count, so the 83 percent refusal figure mostly describes one wiki. In those files there are zero spec.md, zero specs directories and two agent-instruction files, while the pitch is documents an agent wrote into a repository. The vault corpus is still the one thing the engine can prove, byte-exactness on 8,513 real files, so it stays as the gate. Weeks 3 and 4 already prototype on a spec-kit and a superpowers repository, and Q6 makes instruction files the point. The second corpus is the AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md files those repositories hold, pinned at the start of Phase 1 for the cost of a clone. If the list bug's four-day estimate is defended with the 83 percent figure, the second corpus comes first."},{"id":"R11","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Measurement","weight":"medium","q":"Do we buy the two competitor benchmarks, screens and speed, before week 3?","lede":"Ten screen queries are written and unrun. The application has four named measures and no values.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":144,"cols":[{"title":"Mobbin screen benchmark","tone":"neutral"},{"title":"Speed against Obsidian","tone":"neutral"}],"rows":[{"label":"Cost","cells":["One fresh session","Two days, per the gap register"]},{"label":"Already answered by","cells":["The incumbents' docs, four of six groups","Nothing; the app is unmeasured"]},{"label":"Left open","cells":["Launcher, onboarding, pricing, upsell","Cold start, typing at 10,000 words"]},{"label":"Needed before","cells":["The first screen a stranger sees","Only if a headline claims speed"]},{"label":"Blocked by","cells":["The injection-taint gate; a fresh session clears it","A 41,527 ms regex on an unguarded path"]}],"note":"Neither has been run. The incumbents' docs are vendor text, which beats a screenshot of the same behaviour.","caption":"Two unbought measurements, and what each would actually settle."},"stakes":"Either the launcher and pricing page get no external reference, or two days go on an unclaimed number.","state":["The screen benchmark never ran: our session's prompt-injection guard refused every call to Mobbin's screen library.","The plan's table of the incumbents' own documentation already answers four of the six screen groups.","The technical plan names four measures to publish and gives a value for none."],"tension":["Vendor text beats a screenshot, but it cannot answer the launcher or the pricing page.","The engine benchmark found a quadratic: 41,527.3 ms on 320 KB of `[[`, slope 1.98.","None of the three headline variants mentions speed."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Measured and unmeasured, engine versus application","cols":["Thing","Value","Source"],"rows":[["shapeGate over the corpus","8,513 files in 63.5 ms = 286 MB/s, none refused","r15-e4 §70.2, executed 2026-08-31"],["OffsetMap.toByte","465 ns over 20,000 random lookups","r15-e4 §70.2"],["Wikilink regex on 320 KB of \"[[\"","41,527.3 ms, slope k = 1.98, no guard on the path","r15-e4 §70.3 (shapeGate has no call site)"],["Vault parsed per cold start","77 MB","PRODUCT-BRIEF:233"],["Time to first keystroke, cold","unmeasured","named as a measure to publish, :233"],["Typing latency at 10,000 words","unmeasured",":233"],["Anything compared with Obsidian","unmeasured","GAPS:43, priced at two days"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"The screen benchmark, and what already exists without it","items":[["Mobbin queries prepared","10, across 6 screen groups","exhibit 22, PRODUCT-BRIEF:387-396"],["Mobbin screens opened","0","refused by the injection-taint gate, 2026-09-08"],["Placement findings from primary docs","5 rows","exhibit 21, PRODUCT-BRIEF:379-385"],["Query groups the interface-placement exhibit already answers","4 of 6","AI at rest :381, off switch :382, review placement :383, context scoping :384"],["Query groups it does not answer","2: launcher/onboarding, pricing/upsell","4 of the 10 queries, :391 and :396"],["Global AI off-switch precedent","Zed after 412 reactions, VS Code after 30, Telegram after 181","exhibit 21, :382"],["Prototype screens built","9","seen by nobody outside this session, GAPS:27"],["Cost to run","one fresh session","GAPS:39"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Buy both in week 0: our four numbers, the Obsidian comparison, all ten queries","gains":["Produces a speed number nobody currently claims","No screen is designed without a reference"],"costs":["Two days plus a session out of two weeks","Six of the ten queries re-derive the vendor-documentation table"],"system":"A benchmark harness over the application, which does not exist.","screens":"Launcher, review panel, settings and the pricing page.","money":"None beyond time.","what":"Buy both benchmarks now: our four numbers, the Obsidian comparison, all ten screen queries. A speed number nobody claims and a reference for every screen; two days and a session out of two weeks."},{"k":"b","label":"Our four numbers, plus the four queries the incumbents' docs cannot answer","gains":["Hours, not days, aimed where no primary source answers","Answers whether ten strangers can use the build"],"costs":["No competitor speed claim if a headline needs one","Review panel and settings keep vendor-text references"],"system":"A small app benchmark; no new corpus.","screens":"The launcher and the pricing page.","money":"None.","what":"Our four numbers, plus the four screen queries the incumbents' docs cannot answer. Hours rather than days, aimed where no primary source answers; no competitor speed claim if a headline needs one."},{"k":"c","label":"Neither: the incumbents' docs stand and five real people beat both benchmarks","gains":["Frees the session and the two days","Real reactions outrank a reference library"],"costs":["Launcher and pricing page get no external reference","No falsifiable performance commitment survives in the technical plan"],"system":"The four published measures are dropped.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Neither: the incumbents' docs stand and five real people beat both benchmarks. Frees the session and the two days; the launcher and pricing page get no external reference."},{"k":"d","label":"Both, after the two-week tests, so the borrows answer what confused real people","gains":["Best-targeted use of both","Avoids buying references for screens that get cut"],"costs":["The screens the test judges are built first","Pushes measurement past the go/no-go"],"system":"No change now.","screens":"None yet.","money":"None.","what":"Both, after the two-week tests, so the borrows answer what confused real people. Best-targeted; the screens the test judges are built first, and measurement moves past the go or no-go."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Six of the ten queries re-derive conventions the incumbents' own documentation already establishes.","Guard the wikilink regex regardless, because 41 seconds on a keystroke path is a defect to fix."],"flip":"If any tested headline claims speed, the Obsidian comparison becomes mandatory before that headline ships.","linked":["R1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:296","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:375","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:379","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:381","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:382","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:383","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:387","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:391","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:396","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:27","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:39","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:43","docs/research/agent-reports-2026-08-30/r15-e4-performance.md","merged: R13"],"when":"after","whenWhy":"Competitor benchmarks come before a launch, not before phase zero.","recWhy":"Ten screen queries are written and unrun because a prompt-injection guard refused every call to the screen library, and the technical plan names four performance measures to publish and gives a value for none. Six of the ten queries re-derive conventions the incumbents' own documentation already establishes, so buying all ten spends a session on what a table already answers. The four that vendor text cannot answer, the launcher and the pricing page among them, are hours rather than days. Our own four numbers are owed regardless. The engine benchmark also found a quadratic, 41,527.3 milliseconds on 320 KB of wikilink openers with slope 1.98, and that regex gets guarded whatever this card decides, since 41 seconds on a keystroke path is a defect. Q5 fixes the chrome this references. If any tested headline claims speed, the Obsidian comparison becomes mandatory before that headline ships."},{"id":"R14","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Form factor evidence","weight":"high","q":"Do we test whether Cursor and the other forks honour the model APIs before the form-factor decision?","lede":"Two of the savings that make the extension route attractive are proven on VS Code and nowhere else.","visual":{"kind":"compare","labelWidth":164,"cols":[{"title":"VS Code","tone":"good"},{"title":"Cursor and other forks","tone":"warn"}],"rows":[{"label":"Byte-exact write","cells":["94/94 and 156/156, two harnesses","Untested"]},{"label":"Language-model API","cells":["Supplied by the host","Unknown"]},{"label":"Population in the plan","cells":["78.06M Copilot Chat, 24.89M Claude Code","No install count anywhere"]},{"label":"If the API holds","cells":["Bring-your-own-key fix (3 days) and file-scoped AI's key UI deleted","Same saving, wider base"]},{"label":"If it does not","cells":["No effect","The key UI comes back for that share"]}],"note":"Live mode is standalone-only either way: there is no view-zone API.","caption":"The extension route's savings, and the host they were measured on."},"stakes":"The meeting decides the form factor on a saving that may cover only part of the population.","state":["The form-factor decision records byte-exact extension writes on VS Code 1.135.0.","Whether the forks honour those APIs is still on the open list.","The plan carries no install count for Cursor or any fork."],"tension":["Bring-your-own-key support is a three-day fix, and only a host-supplied model picker deletes it.","The size of the affected group is unmeasured, which is itself the finding.","VS Code merged review/unreview and a markdown feedback editor, both experimental and undocumented."],"evidence":[{"type":"stat","title":"What is proven and what is assumed about the extension route","items":[["Byte-exact writes proven on","VS Code 1.135.0","94/94 and 156/156 bytes, two harnesses, PRODUCT-BRIEF:364"],["Forks tested","0","GAPS:146"],["Install count for Cursor or any fork, anywhere in the plan","none","grep: 5 Cursor mentions at :149, :157, :309, :326, :381 (none an install count)"],["VS Code population measured","78.06M Copilot Chat, 24.89M Claude Code extension, 14.45M markdown-all-in-one","GAPS:48"],["Days deleted if the model API holds","3 (fix #6) plus file-scoped AI's key UI","PRODUCT-BRIEF:219, :364"],["Live mode in an extension","not possible: no view-zone API","PRODUCT-BRIEF:364"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Test before the meeting: a stub extension in Cursor calling the model API","gains":["Moves a number the form-factor decision rests on","Counts the fork population in the same sitting"],"costs":["An afternoon in a week already full of decisions","A negative result weakens the extension case"],"system":"A throwaway stub extension; no product code.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Test before the meeting: a stub extension in Cursor calling the model API, and count the fork population in the same sitting. Moves a number the surface decision rests on; an afternoon in a full week."},{"k":"b","label":"Decide on the VS Code numbers alone and treat the forks as upside","gains":["Defensible: VS Code is the population with real numbers","No delay"],"costs":["The saving covers a smaller base than the argument implies","The gap is discovered during the build"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None.","what":"Decide on the VS Code numbers alone and treat the forks as upside. Defensible, since VS Code is the population with real numbers; the saving covers a smaller base than the argument implies."},{"k":"c","label":"Build the key UI regardless, so the extension works everywhere","gains":["Removes the dependency entirely","One code path across every host"],"costs":["Costs the three days back","The extension case gets weaker by that much"],"system":"The bring-your-own-key fix and the AI panel's key screen stay in the sequence.","screens":"Settings keeps the key field.","money":"Three engineering days.","what":"Build the key UI regardless, so the extension works everywhere. Removes the dependency entirely; costs the three days back, and the extension case gets weaker by that much."},{"k":"d","label":"Defer the form factor until the two-week tests say which shell people want","gains":["Decides on demand rather than on savings","Both mocks already exist"],"costs":["Delays a decision that gates the build sequence","Ten people's preference is not a population measurement"],"system":"No change until week 3.","screens":"Both mocks stay live.","money":"None.","what":"Defer the form factor until the two-week tests say which shell people want. Decides on demand rather than savings; ten people's preference is not a population measurement."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["One afternoon moves an input to a decision the meeting takes, and reversing it later costs far more.","Count the fork population in the same sitting. The plan has no number for it at all."],"flip":"If the meeting chooses the standalone editor, this question no longer matters.","linked":["FF1","R1"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:219","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:364","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:48","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:125","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:146","microsoft/vscode#324218, merged 2026-07-03; issues #326539 and #326540, milestone 1.130.0"],"when":"notnow","whenWhy":"Testing editor forks matters only if an extension ships.","recWhy":"Two of the savings that made the extension route attractive, byte-exact writes and a host-supplied model picker that deletes three days of key UI, are proven on VS Code 1.135.0 and nowhere else. The plan carries no install count for Cursor or any fork, which is itself the finding. One afternoon moves an input to a decision that is expensive to reverse, and counts the fork population in the same sitting. This sits at not now because Q3 chose the web editor at / as the one surface and the Mac app later from the same tree, so the extension is not the form factor being built and the test has nothing to inform yet. The finding is kept rather than deleted because FF12's later extension keeps the question alive. If the surface decision ever moves to an extension, this test runs before that meeting, not after it."},{"id":"R5","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Method","weight":"high","q":"Does the plan restore the evidence tags, or keep the three-word labels?","lede":"The document people actually read has no way to tell a fetched number from a recalled one.","visual":{"kind":"ba","before":{"title":"The plan today","lines":[{"t":"83% of real vaults refused","mark":"unmarked"},{"t":"About 1,500 live markers","mark":"unmarked"},{"t":"at exactly our intended tier","mark":"unmarked"},{"t":"1.21 engineering days per week","mark":"unmarked"}]},"after":{"title":"Weak classes marked only","lines":[{"t":"83% of real vaults refused","mark":"silent = opened"},{"t":"About 1,500 live markers","mark":"silent = opened"},{"t":"at exactly our intended tier [SS]","mark":"marked"},{"t":"1.21 engineering days per week","mark":"silent = opened"}]},"via":"mark weak","note":"Silence has to be defined once at the top, or a bare number means nothing.","caption":"Tag where the errors are, not everywhere, so the marks stay visible."},"stakes":"An untagged number gets quoted as fact. That is how the 62,976 figure was published.","state":["The plan labels features public evidence, founder user test or untested.","It carries zero `[fetched]`, `[measured]`, `[derived]`, `[inference]` or `[SS]` (search summary or recall) tags, grepped 2026-09-09.","The research record carries 2,741, and the project's evidence rule makes them mandatory on every claim."],"tension":["Numbers quoted from prose were wrong about half the time.","Thirteen of twenty-one headline claims needed an edit before publication.","The 2026-09-09 briefing ran on two marks. The unmarked 18% figure is the unsourceable one."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Evidence tags, counted with grep -o on 2026-09-09","cols":["Tag","FRONTMATTER-RECORD.md","PRODUCT-BRIEF.md (the current plan)"],"rows":[["[fetched]: primary source opened","1,097","0"],["[measured]: executed here","863","0"],["[derived]: arithmetic shown","369","0"],["[inference]: reasoning","247","0"],["[SS]: may never be published as fact","165","0"],["Total","2,741","0"]]},{"type":"stat","title":"What the verification pass found the tags were catching","items":[["Load-bearing claims sent to six verification agents","21","VERIFICATION.md:17"],["Verifiable sub-claims they decomposed into","34","VERIFICATION.md:17"],["CONFIRMED","13 (38.2%)","VERIFICATION.md:21"],["REVISED","13 (38.2%)","directionally right, numerically or scopally wrong, :22"],["REFUTED","5 (14.7%)","false, must be deleted, :23"],["UNVERIFIABLE","3 (8.8%)","no openable source exists, :24"],["Headline claims needing an edit before publication","13 of 21","VERIFICATION.md:26"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Restore all five tags in the plan","gains":["No number is ever bare","One rule, no judgement about which class is weak"],"costs":["A fifty-page document dense enough that readers stop seeing tags","Retro-fitting 27 exhibits is a rewrite"],"system":"A writing rule.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Restore [SS] and [inference] only: mark the weak, leave the strong bare","gains":["Marks where the verification pass found the errors","One legend line, and it stays readable"],"costs":["Silence carries meaning, so the legend must be stated","A missed mark reads as a strong claim"],"system":"A writing rule plus one legend line of front matter.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Keep the labels and add a source column to every exhibit","gains":["More readable than inline tags","Puts the source beside the number"],"costs":["A source column on twenty-seven exhibits is a rewrite","Wide tables break in the PDF"],"system":"The exhibit renderer takes another column.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Keep as is; the plan is a summary and the record carries the tags","gains":["Free","The record is genuinely complete"],"costs":["Anyone quoting the plan quotes untagged numbers","Nobody opens a 325-page record to check"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"b","recCase":["Tagging every number makes the plan unreadable, and readers stop seeing marks that are everywhere.","Two marks put the mark where the error rate is: prose-quoted numbers and reasoning."],"flip":"If the plan starts going outside the company, restore all five. An external reader has no record to fall back on.","linked":["R18","R6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:4","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:186","docs/PRODUCT.md:1299","docs/VERIFICATION.md:17","docs/VERIFICATION.md:21","docs/VERIFICATION.md:26","docs/VERIFICATION.md:165"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"Restoring evidence tags is a formatting job on the plan."},{"id":"R6","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Method","weight":"high","q":"Do we gate the plan on a checker built from a withdrawals ledger, or keep correcting it by hand?","lede":"Round 2 applied twenty-two changes and left five decisions stated both ways inside one file.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Number published","note":"62,976, in a commit title","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Corrected in one paragraph","note":"Now about 1,400 live markers","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Old PDFs stay on disk","note":"Plan versions 10 to 14, all under docs/","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Quoted again","note":"Nothing checks the rest of the file","tone":"stop"},{"label":"Ledger row written","note":"The vocabulary audit already does this for two","tone":"accent","edge":"instead"},{"label":"Grep gate on the PDF build","note":"Fails at the moment the plan leaves","tone":"good"}],"note":"The ledger is the input list the checker consumes; neither works alone.","caption":"How a withdrawn number comes back, and where to stop it."},"stakes":"The retention section still sells a channel that was cut, in a founder-meeting document.","state":["The plan's retired-terms table lists ten terms and carries two of the six withdrawn numbers.","No npm script names `docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md`, so nothing checks it.","The Obsidian plugin is struck in one place and still live in four others."],"tension":["Every plan PDF from v10 to v14 is still on disk under `docs/`, quotable.","The marker count is about 1,400 in one place and about 1,500 in another.","A hand re-read after every round is what produced these five contradictions."],"evidence":[{"type":"table","title":"Numbers this programme published and then withdrew","cols":["As published","Where it went out","What it is now","In the terms-checked table?"],"rows":[["62,976 public spec files with an unanswered marker","commit 94edca6 title, plan v10.1, 2026-09-06","3,368 files carry the marker; 42% of a read-every-hit sample of 50 were live, ≈1,400 (PRODUCT-BRIEF:58)","Yes, at :178, but it says about 1,500"],["\"496,000 stars\" as channel size","earlier plans","replaced by active-repo counts; stars measure attention to a CLI (:176)","Yes, at :176"],["1.5M browser readers","earlier channel sizing","about 647,000; the figure summed a 500,000-user reader with a 1,000,000-user clipper (:310)","No"],["114 seats at $8 covers the nut","earlier pricing","withdrawn; the feature is GitHub's at $4 (:292)","No"],["Ten weeks to the pilot","the previous plan's schedule","withdrawn as arithmetically impossible; 70 engineering days is 37 weeks at the best rate ever observed, 58 at the measured one (:227, :229)","No"],["The 2.5x optimistic correction","the audits","withdrawn; the 41-day figure already had 25% active-day density baked in (:229)","No"]]},{"type":"table","title":"Round-2 decisions that survive in both states, same file","cols":["What round 2 decided","Struck at","Old position still stands at"],"rows":[["Obsidian plugin CUT","the channels table, line 304","the Tiers channels row :204; flowchart stop-branch :338; weeks 1-2 \"plugin shipped\" :352; retention lever :428; family diagram :464; the family table :477; the rendering-rules modes row :524"],["Chrome extension CUT","the channels table, line 310","the Tiers surfaces row :203; family diagram :464; the family table :477"],["Diff-review issue at 266 reactions, re-checked 2026-09-08","the Definition section :23 and review state :56","262 reactions at :150, :322, :383"],["Web app indexes per open file, not server full-text","WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:50","\"move to server full-text search in the web app\" at :233"],["Live markers ≈1,400","answer in place :58","\"About 1,500 live markers\" at :178"]]}],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"A withdrawals ledger, and a grep gate that fails the PDF build","gains":["Half a day; it fires when the plan PDF builds","Shows the six withdrawals in three days openly"],"costs":["The list needs maintaining after every round","A gate that fires falsely trains you to skip it"],"system":"A script beside `build-plan-pdf.mjs`, and one npm script.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Extend the ledger; keep correcting by hand","gains":["Free today, minutes of work","The withdrawal count becomes visible"],"costs":["A hand re-read produced the five live contradictions","Nothing catches the sixth"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Inline [withdrawn] markers wherever an old number might still be quoted","gains":["Catches the number at the point of use","No build step to maintain"],"costs":["Scatters the record, leaves no index to check against","Cannot mark a number inside a shipped PDF"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Generate the plan from the record, as the Tier-1 docs are generated","gains":["One correction propagates into every document","Removes the error class, not instances"],"costs":["A build-tree rewrite for a document that changes shape weekly","The plan's structure is not the record's"],"system":"The plan joins the pipeline that generates the core docs.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["`npm run record` already checks the assembled record line for line; a retired-terms check is a smaller version.","The ledger is the checker's input, so four rows and half a day covers both."],"flip":"If the plan is rewritten from scratch once more, do option d and stop hand-maintaining it.","linked":["R18","R5"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:58","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:165","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:176","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:178","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:203","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:204","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:227","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:229","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:233","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:292","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:304","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:310","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:352","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:428","docs/PRODUCT-BRIEF.md:477","docs/GAPS-2026-09-08.md:118","docs/WEEK0-DECISIONS-2026-09-08.md:50","docs/MAP.md:108","merged: R9"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"Building a checker from a ledger is tooling work."},{"id":"R18","cat":"Research & evidence","sub":"Method","weight":"high","q":"Do we adopt a verbatim-only rule: nothing in quotation marks that has not been re-fetched and string-matched?","lede":"The 2026-09-09 research got the substance right and invented the wording. About one quotation in three was a paraphrase.","visual":{"kind":"flow","perRow":3,"nodes":[{"label":"Finding written from a source","note":"Substance is usually right","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Quotation marks used?","note":"The only form that cannot be checked by reading","tone":"warn"},{"label":"Re-fetch and string-match","note":"One request, in the same pass","tone":"accent"},{"label":"Exact match: keep the marks","note":"Publishable on a slide","tone":"good"},{"label":"No match: drop the marks","note":"Say it in our own words, cite the URL","tone":"neutral"},{"label":"Cannot re-open the source","note":"Not on a slide, at any confidence","tone":"stop"}],"note":"Two quotations attributed to vendor blogs today were on neither page.","caption":"The gate a quotation passes before it reaches a founder meeting."},"stakes":"One fabricated quotation read aloud undoes the plan's method: every claim can be opened and checked.","state":["Two quotations attributed to vendor blogs are on neither page. The findings survived.","Strings attributed to VS Code's review documentation are absent; that page documents no such capability.","The plan's method is that every claim points at something openable."],"tension":["A paraphrase inside quotation marks looks exactly like a quotation, and usually reads better.","Four strings survived the 2026-09-09 check, including \"Agent Edits\" and \"Uncommitted\".","Numbers fail the same way. The 18% unreviewed figure has no source at all."],"evidence":[],"options":[{"k":"a","label":"Verbatim-only: re-fetch and string-match every quotation, or the marks come off","gains":["Kills the only error class a reader cannot catch","One request per quotation, and nothing else"],"costs":["Slows any pass that quotes heavily","A changed page forces the sentence to be rewritten"],"system":"A quote-check list beside the plan's retired-terms check.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"b","label":"Drop quotations: state every finding in our own words with the URL beside it","gains":["Removes the failure entirely","Nothing to re-check later"],"costs":["Loses a vendor stating its own behaviour, the vendor-documentation table's method","Paraphrase invites its own drift"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"c","label":"Mark each quotation with its verification class and let the reader judge","gains":["Consistent with the evidence-tag decision","Keeps un-reopenable quotations, honestly marked"],"costs":["An unverified quotation still gets read aloud","Pushes the judgement onto the reader"],"system":"A writing rule.","screens":"None.","money":"None."},{"k":"d","label":"Nothing; the tag discipline already covers it","gains":["Free","The record's tags have caught real errors"],"costs":["A tag never says whether the words are the source's own","The 2026-09-09 round shows the tags missed it"],"system":"No change.","screens":"None.","money":"None."}],"rec":"a","recCase":["The failure was not laziness. Every fabricated quotation had the finding right, so reading never caught it.","One request per quotation, and the retired-terms check is already being built over the plan."],"flip":"If a source cannot be re-fetched, because of a login or a deletion, the rule falls back to option b.","linked":["R5","R6"],"sources":["docs/PRODUCT.md:1299","docs/VERIFICATION.md:165","docs/MAP.md:108","https://zed.dev/blog/agentic-xanadu","https://antigravity.google/blog/vcs-and-terminal"],"when":"task","whenWhy":"A quoting rule is a working practice to adopt."}];
window.FINAL={"note": "The final set. Sixteen product questions folding the MVP cards. Each carries a position and the evidence in one line; the folded cards stay answerable underneath. Written 2026-09-14.", "groups": [{"id": "Q1", "area": "What it is", "title": "The sentence", "position": "frontmatter is where you write, review and keep current the files your agents obey.", "evidence": "60,000 projects use AGENTS.md, 77 commits to one file in openai/codex, two LibreChat files drifted 3x, and your own CLAUDE.md is 10,033 words. Spec Kit and Kiro generate and leave. The vendor's /memory shows what loaded this session, not which file wins across a repo.", "cards": ["P1", "P2"]}, {"id": "Q2", "area": "What it is", "title": "The buyer", "position": "The team running coding agents, then the organisation above it. Not the individual, who has one file and will not pay.", "evidence": "The main OpenAI repo holds 88 instruction files. Claude Code documents an org-wide CLAUDE.md managed by IT for all users, with no tooling around it.", "cards": ["MK4"]}, {"id": "Q3", "area": "What ships first", "title": "Where it opens, and on what surface", "position": "The web editor at /, no account until the first save or share. One core. The shell frozen; new work into the engine and one surface. The Mac app, built 27 May, becomes the desktop surface later from the same tree.", "evidence": "dillinger, StackEdit and Excalidraw show zero sign-in words and an editor on arrival; our home page is the login screen; the gate is three lines and the editor never checks it.", "cards": ["FF1", "FF3", "FF8", "AC20", "PL12"]}, {"id": "Q4", "area": "What ships first", "title": "The first thirty seconds", "position": "A paste target and one sample document that turns into a kit. Folder first, sandbox second, read path split from write path. Four templates as files.", "evidence": "An empty editor demonstrates nothing a text area does not. The sample shows the part that is different and costs a document, not a feature.", "cards": ["FL21", "FL12", "F27"]}, {"id": "Q5", "area": "What ships first", "title": "The editor chrome, as one block", "position": "Three modes and cut Split. AI prompt at the cursor. Tab indents, Escape then Tab leaves. Dark mode with dark renders. Tokens reconciled and gated in CI. Three AI verbs on the rail. One switch hides all AI. Bookmarks and graph to the palette. Table editor and ghost text deleted. Four concepts, nine controls.", "evidence": "Eleven craft calls, each with a recommendation on its card. Confirm as a block and name any exception.", "cards": ["D8", "D7", "D15", "D21", "D11", "FL3", "F8", "F9", "F18", "F21", "P15"]}, {"id": "Q6", "area": "What makes it different", "title": "The instruction-file view is Phase 1, and the point", "position": "Every AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and rules file in a repo. Which one wins for a path. Size against the vendor's limit. What an agent changed that no person read, by path, read-only. Comments in a separate file, CriticMarkup on demand. Body writes wait until after the pilot.", "evidence": "The strongest signal found all week and the one thing none of the four competitors does.", "cards": ["F35", "F26", "F13", "E21"]}, {"id": "Q7", "area": "What makes it different", "title": "Does this read like you, as a feature", "position": "The writing gate runs on a document and names the tell.", "evidence": "Measured against 2,710 dictation entries and 196,522 of your own words. It exists. No editor has it.", "cards": ["F39"]}, {"id": "Q8", "area": "What makes it different", "title": "What ships from the AIOS", "position": "Three things as templates: the learned-rule format, the file gate, the skill file shape. The orchestrator, 128 skills and 156 scripts stay yours.", "evidence": "43 of 128 skills run the system itself; the preference log it learns from holds zero pairs. The best thing you built, and not installable in a day.", "cards": ["P26"]}, {"id": "Q9", "area": "What we promise", "title": "Nothing leaves the machine in Phase 0", "position": "Browser fetches the tarball and unzips in the page. Server PDF route deleted, browser print wired. Icons inline, fonts embedded, build stops fetching Google Sans. Client-side search index. Three GitHub permissions, never an org one. firestore.rules deleted; zero bytes as an operating rule.", "evidence": "Two of five exports send the document to us today; the build failed inside a network sandbox on fonts.googleapis.com; the print function at export-doc.ts:130 is imported by nothing.", "cards": ["AC1", "AC14", "D13", "F25", "AC3", "L1"]}, {"id": "Q10", "area": "What we promise", "title": "Whose key", "position": "The user's own key, a hard gate before any stranger reaches an AI route. Session memory on the web, OS keychain on desktop. No local model.", "evidence": "Nothing in the tools checked offers generation on a key the user holds. Keeps the privacy claim exact with no metering to build.", "cards": ["F10", "AC13"]}, {"id": "Q11", "area": "What we promise", "title": "Sharing", "position": "A link somebody reads and comments on, with suggestions the owner accepts or refuses. Not live editing. Public share route disabled, decided in week 2. Publishing gated to founder with noindex and system slugs. Wordmark on exports off by default.", "evidence": "Live editing reopens the sync decision settled against CRDTs and competes with Google Docs on its strongest axis. A kit is reviewed asynchronously.", "cards": ["F36", "F19", "L6", "G12"]}, {"id": "Q12", "area": "The kit", "title": "The kit, and its check", "position": "Generation ships in Phase 2 as start from here, never as the claim, after founder notes are attached and twenty kits are made by hand. A names-and-claims pass runs before the link, and the user sees the report.", "evidence": "Any model writes 33 files. A kit whose files disagree is worse than none. The check is the half a competitor cannot copy from a prompt.", "cards": ["F34", "F5"]}, {"id": "Q13", "area": "What it costs", "title": "Free and paid", "position": "Free: editor, paste, export, local, uncapped. Pro, $6 to $8: storage, sync, share, own key, kit, templates. Team, $15 to $20 a seat: the instruction-file view across a repo, who changed what, review state. Enterprise unpriced until asked. Hosted AI metered in actions with a daily breaker. Two tiers at launch.", "evidence": "Obsidian gives the editor away and charges for Sync and Publish. Kiro's Pro row is $20 per user. Nobody charges for an editor.", "cards": ["PR24", "PR1", "PR21", "PR7", "PR10"]}, {"id": "Q14", "area": "The floor", "title": "What has to be true before a stranger opens a file", "position": "Patch the flush-left refusal, red-proof first, pre-commit to the structural fix above a residual of ten. CI in week one with a deliberate red run. Non-English and text scaling in scope. Six fix-first items start now.", "evidence": "The editor refuses most real vaults today, and no automated check runs on any push.", "cards": ["E1", "E12", "E33", "PL3", "E5", "E17"]}, {"id": "Q15", "area": "The floor", "title": "What the pilot passed means", "position": "Three of ten name the problem unprompted. Two still using at day 30. One booked call from someone not asked. Agreed before it starts.", "evidence": "A kill line set after the fact is not a kill line.", "cards": ["PL5"]}, {"id": "Q16", "area": "The name", "title": "The name", "position": "Coin a new one and keep frontmatter as the word for the block we edit. This is the one I would not decide for you.", "evidence": "frontmatter is taken in the VS Code store your buyer uses. It goes on every screen and export, so it is due before Phase 0 ships.", "cards": ["N1"]}], "facts": {"title": "Not product, still needed", "note": "These set every date in the plan and no research can answer them.", "cards": ["B1", "B2", "G15"]}, "still": {"title": "Still needed before the pilot, not product", "note": "Legal clocks, the name registers, the research process. On the site, not in the way.", "cards": ["G16", "B14", "L3", "L4", "L7", "L12", "L17", "N2", "N3", "PL2", "PL10", "R1", "R17"]}};
